/* Machine translations of Kritik der reinen Vernunft, 1st ed. (A, 1781), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['krv-a/guyer'] = {"krv-a-0001":["Critique of Pure Reason by Immanuel Kant, Professor in Königsberg. Riga, published by Johann Friedrich Hartknoch, 1781.",["*Critik* (old spelling) = Critique; *verlegts* = *verlegt es*, 'publishes it'."]],"krv-a-0002":["To His Excellency, the Royal Minister of State Baron von Zedlitz.",["*Sr. Excellenz* = *Seiner Excellenz*."]],"krv-a-0003":["Gracious Sir.",[]],"krv-a-0004":["To promote the growth of the sciences on one's own part means to work on Your Excellency's own interest; for this is intimately bound up with them, not merely through the exalted post of a protector, but through the far more familiar one of a lover and enlightened connoisseur. Therefore I also avail myself of the sole means that is to some extent in my power to show my gratitude for the gracious confidence with which Your Excellency honors me, as if I could contribute something to this aim.",["*Wissenschaften* = sciences; *Mittel* = means; *Vermögen* = faculty, rendered here 'power' in the idiom *in meinem Vermögen*; *Absicht* = aim.","*vor* (modern *für*) = 'for'; *einigen* (modern *einzigen*) = 'sole'."]],"krv-a-0005":["For whom the speculative life gives pleasure, to him, among moderate wishes, the approval of an enlightened, valid judge is a powerful encouragement to exertions whose utility is great, although remote, and therefore is entirely misrecognized by common eyes.",["*gültigen* = 'valid' (glossary *gültig*), kept literal; *Nutze* = utility; *verkant* = *verkannt*; *gemein* = common.","*Wen* = *Wem* (original spelling); the construction 'For whom ... to him' follows the German."]],"krv-a-0006":["To such a one and to His gracious regard I now dedicate this writing and, to His protection, all the remaining concern of my literary vocation, and am with the deepest veneration",["Capitals on *Solchen*, *Dessen*, *Seinem* kept as printed (rendered 'His' for the latter two).","*Bestimmung* = 'vocation' (glossary alternative to 'determination'). The passage breaks off at the end of the sentence's main clause."]],"krv-a-0007":["Your Excellency's\nKönigsberg, the 29th of March 1781.\nmost submissively obedient servant Immanuel Kant.",["*Merz* = *März*; *unterthäniggehorsamster* is a single compound superlative."]],"krv-a-0008":["Preface.",[]],"krv-a-0009":["Human reason has the particular fate in one genus of its cognitions: that it is burdened by questions that it cannot dismiss, for they are assigned to it by the nature of reason itself, but that it also cannot answer, for they surpass every faculty of human reason.",["*Gattung* = genus; *Erkentnisse* = cognitions; *Vermögen* = faculty.","*a weisen*: the original reads thus (for *abweisen*, 'dismiss'); probably a line-break or printing slip, not in the German of the 1781 sense otherwise.","*aufgegeben* is the verb of *Aufgabe* (problem); rendered 'assigned' to avoid adding 'as problems'."]],"krv-a-0010":["It gets into this embarrassment without any fault of its own. It begins from principles whose use in the course of experience is unavoidable and at the same time sufficiently confirmed by this. With these it climbs (as its nature also brings with it) ever higher, to more remote conditions. But since it becomes aware that in this way its business must always remain unfinished, because the questions never cease, it sees itself compelled to take refuge in principles that overstep all possible use in experience and nevertheless seem so unsuspicious that even common human reason stands in agreement with them. But thereby it plunges into darkness and contradictions, from which it can indeed gather that somewhere hidden errors must lie at the ground, which it cannot however discover, because the principles it makes use of, since they go beyond the boundary of all experience, no longer acknowledge any touchstone of experience. The battlefield of these endless disputes is now called metaphysics.",["*Grundsätze* = principles; *Gebrauch* = use; *Erfahrungsgebrauch* = 'use in experience'; *Bedingungen* = conditions; *Gränze* = boundary.","*hinreichend* rendered 'sufficiently' (the glossary gives *zureichend* = sufficient).","*zun Grunde liegen* = 'lie at the ground' (*zu Grunde*); *bedien* = *bedienen*; *Widersprüche* = contradictions."]],"krv-a-0011":["There was a time in which it was called the queen of all sciences and, if one takes the will for the deed, it certainly deserved this honorary name, on account of the preeminent importance of its object. Now the fashionable tone of the age brings it with it to show it all contempt, and the matron laments, repudiated and forsaken, like Hecuba: *modo maxima rerum, tot generis natisque potens — nunc trahor exul, inops* — Ovid. Metam.",["*Gegenstand* = object; *Wissenschaften* = sciences.","Latin is quoted from Ovid, *Metamorphoses* XIII; left in Latin and italicized; abbreviation *Metam.* kept as printed.","*vor* = 'for' (*für*); *verstossen* = 'repudiated', 'cast out'."]],"krv-a-0012":["At first its rule, under the administration of the dogmatists, was despotic. Yet, because the legislation still bore the trace of ancient barbarism, it degenerated through internal wars gradually into complete anarchy, and the skeptics, a kind of nomads, who abhor all permanent cultivation of the soil, from time to time broke up the civil union. But since fortunately there were only few of them, they could not prevent those from trying again and again to cultivate it anew, though according to no plan in agreement among themselves. In more recent times it seemed indeed once as if all these disputes were to be ended by a certain physiology of the human understanding (by the famous Locke) and the legitimacy of those claims completely decided; but it was found that, although the birth of that alleged queen was derived from the rabble of common experience, and thereby her presumption should rightly have become suspect, nevertheless, because this genealogy was in fact falsely attributed to her, she still continued to assert her claims, whereby everything fell back again into the antiquated worm-eaten dogmatism and from this into the contempt out of which one had wanted to draw the science. Now, after all paths (as one persuades oneself) have been tried in vain, weariness and complete indifferentism, the mother of chaos and night, reign in the sciences, but yet at the same time the origin, or at least the prelude, of an imminent re-creation and enlightenment of them, when through ill-applied diligence they have become dark, confused, and unusable.",["*Verstand* = understanding; *Erfahrung* = experience; *behauptete* = 'continued to assert' (*behaupten* = assert); *überredet* = 'persuades' (*Überredung* = persuasion).","*iene* ('those', 'the former') refers back in each case to the dogmatists/claims as the German does; no noun supplied.","*Ueberdruß* = 'weariness'; *Umschaffung* = 're-creation'; *Fleiß* = 'diligence'; *Anmassung* = 'presumption'; *daraus* in 'daraus man die Wissenschaft hatte ziehen wollen' kept as 'out of which'."]],"krv-a-0013":["For it is in vain to want to feign indifference with regard to such researches, whose object cannot be indifferent to human nature. Also those alleged indifferentists, however much they may think to make themselves unrecognizable by the alteration of the language of the schools into a popular tone, if only they think anything at all, fall back unavoidably into metaphysical assertions, against which they nevertheless professed so much contempt. Meanwhile this indifference, which occurs in the midst of the flourishing of all sciences and strikes precisely that one on whose knowledge, if such were to be had, one would least of all renounce, is nevertheless a phenomenon that deserves attention and reflection. It is obviously the effect not of frivolity but of the matured power of judgment∗) of the age, which no longer lets itself be put off with illusory knowledge, and a summons to reason to take up anew the most burdensome of all its businesses, namely that of self-cognition, and to set up a court of justice that could secure it in its rightful claims, but on the other hand dispatch all groundless presumptions, not through pronouncements of power but according to its eternal and unchangeable laws, and this is none other than the critique of pure reason itself.",["*Gegenstand* = object; *Behauptungen* = assertions; *Kentnisse* = 'knowledge' (plural in German); *Urtheilskraft* = power of judgment; *Selbsterkentniß* = self-cognition; *Scheinwissen* = 'illusory knowledge' (*Schein* = illusion).","*Nachsinnen* = 'reflection' (not *Überlegung*); *Gesetze* = laws; *Wirkung* = effect.","The German leaves open whether 'a summons to reason ...' is coordinate with 'the effect' or with 'the power of judgment'; the English keeps the same ambiguity.","The footnote marker is printed as ∗) to avoid collision with the italics markup; the footnote follows in krv-a-0014."]],"krv-a-0014":["∗) One hears here and there complaints about the shallowness of the way of thinking of our time and the decline of thorough science. Yet I do not see that those whose ground is well laid, such as mathematics, doctrine of nature, etc., in the least deserve this reproach, but rather maintain the old fame of thoroughness, and in the latter even surpass it. The very same spirit would now prove itself effective also in other kinds of cognition, if only care had first of all been taken for the rectification of their principles. In the absence of these, indifference and doubt and finally, strict critique, are rather proofs of a thorough way of thinking. Our age is the genuine age of critique, to which everything must submit. Religion, through its holiness, and legislation, through its majesty, commonly want to withdraw from it. But then they arouse just suspicion against themselves, and cannot lay claim to unfeigned respect, which reason grants only to that which has been able to withstand its free and public examination.",["*Naturlehre* = doctrine of nature; *Denkungsart* = way of thinking; *Geist* = spirit; *Erkentniß* = cognition; *Arten* = kinds; *Principien* = principles (Latinate, as against *Grundsätze*, also 'principles').","*gründlich*/*Gründlichkeit* = 'thorough'/'thoroughness'; *Seichtigkeit* = shallowness; *Prüfung* = examination.","The comma in 'und endlich, strenge Critik' is kept as printed; *vor* = 'for' (*für*); *Maiestät* = majesty."]],"krv-a-0015":["But by this I do not understand a critique of books and systems, but that of the faculty of reason in general, with regard to all cognitions after which it may strive, independently of all experience, consequently the decision about the possibility or impossibility of a metaphysics in general and the determination as well of the sources as of the domain and boundaries of it, but all from principles.",["*Vernunftvermögen* = faculty of reason; *Erkentnisse* = cognitions; *Umfang* = domain; *Gränzen* = boundaries; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Principien* = principles.","'of it' translates *derselben*, which could refer to metaphysics or to critique; ambiguity kept."]],"krv-a-0016":["This path, the only one that was left over, I have now taken, and I flatter myself to have encountered on it the removal of all errors that had hitherto set reason at variance with itself in its use free of experience. I have not evaded its questions by excusing myself with the incapacity of human reason; rather I have specified them completely in accordance with principles and, after I had discovered the point of the misunderstanding of reason with itself, have resolved them to its complete satisfaction. To be sure, the answer to those questions has not turned out quite as dogmatically-enthusiastic craving for knowledge might expect; for that could not be satisfied otherwise than through magic tricks, of which I understand nothing. But that was also presumably not the intention of the determination of nature of our reason, and the duty of philosophy was: to annul the sham that sprang from misinterpretation, even if much highly praised and beloved delusion should come to nothing in the process. In this occupation I have let thoroughness be my great object, and I make bold to say that there must not be a single metaphysical problem that is not resolved here, or for whose resolution at least the key has not been supplied. In fact pure reason is also such a perfect unity: that if its principle were insufficient even for a single one of all the questions that are assigned to it by its own nature, one could just as well throw it away, because then it would also not be up to any of the others with complete reliability.",["Original spellings (Puncte, mogte, solte, könte, wol) are normalized in meaning only; \"Gebrauche\" = use; \"erfahrungsfreien\" = \"free of experience\".","\"Ausführlichkeit\" = \"thoroughness\" (also in 0017, 0018); \"Aufgabe\" = \"problem\"; \"aufgelöst/Auflösung\" = \"resolved/resolution\".","\"Naturbestimmung\" rendered literally \"determination of nature\" (Bestimmung = determination); it could also be read as \"natural vocation\".","\"Blendwerk\" is not in the glossary; rendered \"sham\" to keep it distinct from \"Schein\" (illusion) and \"Erscheinung\" (appearance). \"Wahn\" = \"delusion\"; \"Mißdeutung\" = \"misinterpretation\"; \"Mißverstand\" = \"misunderstanding\".","\"Einheit\" = unity; \"unzureichend\" = insufficient; \"Absicht\" = intention; \"Wißbegierde\" = craving for knowledge."]],"krv-a-0017":["In saying this I believe I perceive in the reader's face an indignation mixed with contempt over claims that, to all appearances, are so boastful and immodest, and yet they are incomparably more moderate than those of any author of the most common program who pretends to prove, say, the simple nature of the soul, or the necessity of a first beginning of the world. For this one pledges himself to extend human cognition beyond all boundaries of possible experience, of which I humbly confess: that this entirely exceeds my faculty, in place of which I have merely to do with reason itself and its pure thinking, for whose detailed acquaintance I need not seek far around me, because I encounter it in myself, and of which even common logic already gives me an example, that all its simple actions can be enumerated completely and systematically; only that here the question is raised how much I might hope to accomplish with it, if all the material and assistance of experience is taken from me.",["\"Erkentniß\" = \"cognition\" (Erkenntnis); \"Kentniß\" = \"acquaintance\" (Kenntnis); \"ausführlicher Kentniß\" = \"detailed acquaintance\".","\"Gränzen\" = \"boundaries\" (Grenze); \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Stoff\" = \"material\"; \"gemein\" = \"common\" (in both \"gemeinesten Programs\" and \"gemeine Logik\").","\"Anscheine\" in \"dem Anscheine nach\" = \"appearances\" in the idiom \"to all appearances\"; it is not Erscheinung.","\"an dessen Statt\" is rendered \"in place of which\"; the referent of \"dieses\" (that which exceeds my faculty) is left as ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-a-0018":["So much for the completeness in the attainment of each, and the thoroughness in the attainment of all ends together, which not an arbitrary resolve, but the nature of cognition itself assigns to us, as the matter of our critical investigation.",["\"Zwecke\" = \"ends\"; \"Materie\" = \"matter\"; \"Vorsatz\" = \"resolve\"; \"aufgiebt\" = \"assigns\" (cf. Aufgabe, \"problem\").","\"Vollständigkeit\" = \"completeness\"; \"Ausführlichkeit\" = \"thoroughness\".","The sentence is a fragment with no finite verb of its own, and is kept as it stands."]],"krv-a-0019":["Certainty and distinctness are still two items concerning the form of the same, to be regarded as essential demands that one can rightly make of the author who ventures upon so slippery an undertaking.",["\"derselben\" (\"of the same\") has no clear antecedent in this passage (cf. the preceding sentence on cognition); left unresolved.","\"Gewißheit\" = \"certainty\"; \"Deutlichkeit\" = \"distinctness\" (deutlich = distinct); \"Stücke\" = \"items\"; \"Foderungen\" = \"demands\".","\"als wesentliche Foderungen anzusehen\" could attach to \"Gewißheit und Deutlichkeit\" or to \"Stücke\"; the English keeps the same open construction."]],"krv-a-0020":["As for certainty, I have pronounced judgment on myself: that in this kind of consideration it is in no way permitted to opine, and that everything in it that even resembles a hypothesis is forbidden goods, which may not be put up for sale even at the lowest price, but must be confiscated as soon as it is discovered. For that is what every cognition that is to stand firm a priori itself announces: that it wants to be held for absolutely necessary, and all the more so a determination of all pure cognitions a priori, which is to be the standard, hence itself the example of all apodictic (philosophical) certainty. Whether I have now accomplished what I pledge myself to in this respect, that remains entirely left to the judgment of the reader, because it befits the author only to present grounds, but not to judge about the effect of the same on his judges. But so that nothing may innocently be the cause of the weakening of the same, it may well be permitted to him to note himself those passages that could give occasion to some mistrust, even though they concern only the secondary end, in order to forestall in good time the influence that even the slightest scruple of the reader on this point might have on his judgment with regard to the main end.",["\"zu meinen\" = \"to opine\" (Meinung = opinion); \"Urtheil\" = \"judgment\"; \"urtheilen\" = \"to judge\"; \"Gründe\" = \"grounds\"; \"Wirkung\" = \"effect\"; \"Ursache\" = \"cause\".","\"Art von Betrachtungen\" = \"kind of consideration\" (Betrachtung = consideration; Art = kind).","\"Bestimmung aller reinen Erkentnisse a priori\" is kept as \"a determination of all pure cognitions a priori\"; the genitive and the antecedent of \"die\" are left as ambiguous as in the German.","\"Richtmaaß\" = \"standard\" (not in the glossary); \"Nebenzweck\" / \"Hauptzweck\" = \"secondary end\" / \"main end\" (Zweck = end).","\"derselben\" (twice, \"effect of the same\", \"weakening of the same\") is left without a resolved antecedent, as in the German; \"Bedenklichkeit\" = \"scruple\".","\"schlechthinnothwendig\" = \"absolutely necessary\"; \"apodictischen\" = \"apodictic\"."]],"krv-a-0021":["I know of no investigations that would be more important for getting to the bottom of the faculty that we call understanding, and at the same time for the determination of the rules and boundaries of its use, than those that I have undertaken in the second chapter of the transcendental analytic, under the title of the deduction of the pure concepts of the understanding; they have also cost me the most effort, but, as I hope, not unrewarded. This consideration, which is somewhat deeply laid out, has however two sides. The one is related to the objects of the pure understanding, and is to demonstrate and make comprehensible the objective validity of its concepts a priori; for just that reason it also belongs essentially to my ends. The other goes out to consider the pure understanding itself, according to its possibility and the cognitive powers on which it itself rests, hence to consider it in subjective relation, and although this exposition is of great importance with regard to my main end, it nevertheless does not belong essentially to it; because the main question always remains what and how much understanding and reason can cognize, free of all experience, and not how is the faculty of thinking itself possible? Since the latter is as it were a search for the cause of a given effect, and insofar has something similar to a hypothesis about it (although, as I will show on another occasion, in fact it is not so), it seems as if this were the case where I take the permission to opine, and so the reader must also be free to opine otherwise. In consideration of this I must anticipate the reader with the reminder: that in case my subjective deduction has not produced in him the whole conviction that I expect, the objective one, with which I am here chiefly concerned, nevertheless gets its whole strength, for which, if need be, what is said on pages 92 to 93 alone can be sufficient.",["\"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\"; \"Erkenntnißkräfte\" = \"cognitive powers\" (Kraft = power); \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; \"Gränzen\" = \"boundaries\"; \"Gebrauch\" = \"use\".","\"Erörterung\" = \"exposition\" (expositio); \"Ueberzeugung\" = \"conviction\"; \"Gültigkeit\" = \"validity\"; \"begreiflich\" = \"comprehensible\"; \"erkennen\" = \"cognize\"; \"Gegenstände\" = \"objects\"; \"obiective/subiective\" = \"objective/subjective\".","\"Zwecke\" (\"my ends\") and \"Hauptzweck\" (\"main end\") both rendered with \"end\".","\"zu meinen\" / \"anders zu meinen\" = \"to opine\" / \"to opine otherwise\" (Meinung = opinion).","\"sie\" in \"darthun und begreiflich machen\" and the antecedents of \"demselben\" and \"darauf\" are kept as in the German; \"zu deren ... dasienige ... Seite 92 bis 93\" is the first-edition page reference, retained as it stands.","\"wie ist das Vermögen zu Denken selbst möglich?\" is left without quotation marks, as in the German, where it is an unmarked question."]],"krv-a-0022":["As for distinctness finally, the reader has a right to demand first discursive (logical) distinctness, through concepts, but then also intuitive (aesthetic) distinctness, through intuitions, i.e., examples or other clarifications, in concreto. For the first I have provided sufficiently. That concerned the essence of my undertaking, but was also the contingent cause that I have not been able to satisfy the second demand, though not so strict, yet still equitable. I have been almost constantly undecided in the progress of my work how I should handle this. Examples and clarifications always seemed necessary to me and therefore also actually flowed in at their places in the first draft. But I very soon saw the magnitude of my problem and the multiplicity of objects with which I would have to do and, since I became aware that these alone, in a dry, merely scholastic presentation, would already extend the work enough, I found it inadvisable to swell it still more through examples and clarifications that are necessary only for a popular aim, especially since this work could by no means be adapted to popular use and the actual connoisseurs of the science do not need this facilitation so much, although it is always agreeable, but here could even draw after it something contrary to the end. Abbot Terrasson says, to be sure: if one measures the magnitude of a book not by the number of leaves, but by the time one needs to understand it, then one can say of many a book: that it would be much shorter if it were not so short. On the other side, however, if one directs one's aim to the comprehensibility of an extensive, yet nevertheless in one principle interconnected whole of speculative cognition, one could say with just as good a right: many a book would have become much more distinct if it had not been supposed to become so very distinct. For the aids to distinctness, to be sure, are lacking in parts, but often distract in the whole, in that they do not let the reader get quickly enough to an overview of the whole and, with all their bright colors, nevertheless paste over and make unrecognizable the articulation, or the structure of the members of the system, on which it nevertheless most depends, in order to be able to judge its unity and soundness.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Anschauung = intuition; Erläuterung = clarification throughout (the glossary also allows “elucidation”).","Absicht = aim (both occurrences: “populärer Absicht”, “seine Absicht richtet”); Zweck = end (“zweckwidriges”); Aufgabe = problem; Gegenstand = object; Wesen = essence; Einheit = unity.","“Das betraf …” and “es” in “wie ich es hiemit halten solte” are left without a supplied noun, as in the German; “Ganzes”/“Ganze” = whole.","“Vor die erste” (= für die erste) rendered “For the first”; “Fortgange” = progress/progression (here “progress”).","Terrasson’s sentence is kept in indirect speech with the German colon; “Blätter” = leaves; “Hülfsmittel” = aids; “Gliederbau” = structure of the members."]],"krv-a-0023":["It can, as it seems to me, serve the reader as no small enticement to unite his effort with that of the author, if he has the prospect of carrying out a great and important work, according to the draft laid before him, completely and yet durably. Now metaphysics, according to the concepts that we shall give of it here, is the only one of all sciences that may promise itself such a completion, and indeed in a short time, and with only little, but united, effort, so that nothing remains for posterity except to arrange everything in the didactic manner according to its aims, without thereby being able to increase the content in the least. For it is nothing but the inventory of all our possessions through pure reason, systematically ordered. Nothing can escape us here, because what reason brings forth entirely out of itself cannot hide, but is itself brought to light through reason, as soon as one has only discovered the common principle of it. The perfect unity of this kind of cognitions, and indeed from nothing but pure concepts, without anything from experience, or even a particular intuition that is supposed to lead to determinate experience, being able to have any influence on them, to extend and to increase them, make this unconditioned completeness not only feasible but also necessary. *Tecum habita et noris, quam sit tibi curta supellex.* Persius.",["“nach ihren Absichten”: “ihren” is ambiguous in the German (posterity’s / metaphysics’); English “its” follows “posterity” grammatically but this is a choice.","“sie zu erweitern und zu vermehren”: “sie” = the cognitions (Erkentnisse); the plural verb “machen” after the singular subject “Einheit” is kept as in the German, hence “make”.","gemeinschaftlich = common (not the glossary’s Gemeinschaft = community); vollkommen = perfect; Vollendung = completion; Vollständigkeit = completeness; bestimmt = determinate; unbedingt = unconditioned.","The Persius line (Satires IV) is Latin in the original and left untranslated; “Inventarium” = inventory."]],"krv-a-0024":["Such a system of pure (speculative) reason I hope to deliver myself under the title: Metaphysics of Nature, which, at not yet half the prolixity, shall nevertheless have an incomparably richer content than the Critique here, which first of all had to lay out the sources and conditions of its possibility, and needed to clear and level a wholly overgrown ground. Here I expect of my reader the patience and impartiality of a judge, but there the compliance and assistance of a fellow-helper; for, however completely all the principles for the system are presented in the Critique, it still belongs to the thoroughness of the system itself that it also lack no derived concepts, which one cannot reckon up a priori, but which must be sought out gradually, likewise, since there the whole synthesis of concepts was exhausted, so it is moreover demanded here that the very same happen also with regard to analysis, all of which is easy and more entertainment than labor.",["Weitläuftigkeit = prolixity; Ausführlichkeit = thoroughness; Critik = Critique (title); Mithelfer = fellow-helper.","“in Ueberschlag bringen” = “reckon up” (literally, bring into an estimate); “a priori” may attach to “reckon up” or to “concepts”; left open as in the German.","The sentence is kept whole, including the comma-splice at “imgleichen”; “Principien” = principles; “Begriffe” = concepts."]],"krv-a-0025":["I have still only to remark some things with regard to the printing. Since the beginning of it was somewhat delayed, I could get to see only about half of the printed sheets, in which I do indeed encounter some printing errors, though not confusing the sense, except for the one that occurs on p. 379, line 4 from below, where “specific” must be read instead of “skeptical.” The Antinomy of pure reason, from page 425 to 461, is so arranged, in the manner of a table, that everything that belongs to the thesis runs continuously on the left side, but what belongs to the antithesis on the right side, which I therefore ordered thus, so that proposition and counterproposition could be compared with one another the more easily.",["The words mentioned are German “specifisch” and “sceptisch” (Kant’s spelling); put in quotation marks as mentioned words.","Satz und Gegensatz = proposition and counterproposition (Satz = proposition per glossary); Sinn = sense; Antinomie = Antinomy; Aushängebogen = printed sheets.","“Druckfehler … ausser demienigen”: “the one” renders “demjenigen” (referring to the error)."]],"krv-a-0026":["Contents.",[]],"krv-a-0027":["Introduction. Page 1",[]],"krv-a-0028":["I. Transcendental Doctrine of Elements. 17",["OCR “Transseendentale” silently corrected to “Transscendentale”. Elementarlehre = Doctrine of Elements (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"krv-a-0029":["First Part. Transcendental Aesthetic. 19",[]],"krv-a-0030":["1st Section. On Space. 22",[]],"krv-a-0031":["2nd ‒ ‒ ‒ On Time. 30",["The dashes stand for “Section” (ditto marks in the original)."]],"krv-a-0032":["Second Part. Transcendental Logic. 50",[]],"krv-a-0033":["1st Division. Transcendental Analytic in two books and their various chapters and sections. 64",["Abtheilung = Division (not Einteilung/Teilung, also “division” in the glossary); Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section; Buch = book."]],"krv-a-0034":["2nd Division. Transcendental Dialectic in two books and their various chapters and sections. 293",[]],"krv-a-0035":["Page",["“Seite” (running column heading)."]],"krv-a-0036":["II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method. 705 1st Chapter. The Discipline of Pure Reason. 708 2nd 〃〃〃 The Canon of Pure Reason. 795 3rd 〃〃〃 The Architectonic of Pure Reason. 832 4th 〃〃〃 The History of Pure Reason. 852",["The ditto marks 〃〃〃 stand for “Chapter” (Hauptstück)."]],"krv-a-0037":["Introduction.",[]],"krv-a-0038":["I. Idea of Transcendental Philosophy.",["Idee = idea."]],"krv-a-0039":["Experience is without doubt the first product that our understanding brings forth, in that it works up the raw material of sensible sensations. It is for that very reason the first teaching, and in its progression so inexhaustible in new instruction that the chained-together life of all future generations will never lack new knowledge that can be gathered on this ground. Nevertheless it is by far not the only field to which our understanding lets itself be confined. It tells us, to be sure, what is there, but not that it must be, necessarily, so and not otherwise. For that very reason it also gives us no true universality, and reason, which is so eager for this kind of cognitions, is more stimulated than satisfied by it. Such universal cognitions now, which at the same time have the character of inner necessity, must, independent of experience, be clear and certain for themselves; one therefore calls them cognitions a priori: whereas on the contrary that which is merely borrowed from experience, as one expresses it, is cognized only a posteriori, or empirically.",["Erfahrung = experience; Verstand = understanding; Stoff = material; Empfindung = sensation; Belehrung = teaching (Unterricht = instruction); Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","Kentnisse (Kenntnis) = knowledge; the plural is not rendered in English (“new knowledge”).","“nothwendiger Weise” rendered “necessarily”; “vor sich selbst” (= für sich selbst) rendered “for themselves”; “so und nicht anders” could attach to “seyn” or “müsse”, left as in the German.","“Zeugungen” = generations; “Allgemeinheit” = universality."]],"krv-a-0040":["Now it shows itself, which is exceedingly remarkable, that even among our experiences cognitions are mingled that must have their origin a priori, and that perhaps serve only to procure interconnection for our representations of the senses. For, if one even removes from the former everything that belongs to the senses, there nevertheless remain certain original concepts and judgments generated from them, which must have arisen entirely a priori, independently of experience, because they make it so that one can say more of the objects that appear to the senses, or at least believes one can say it, than mere experience would teach, and that assertions contain true universality and strict necessity, of the sort that merely empirical cognition cannot deliver.",["Erkentnisse = cognitions (cognitio); Zusammenhang = interconnection; Behauptungen = assertions; Gegenständen = objects; Sinnen = senses.","\"aus den ersteren\": the German leaves it open which noun 'the former' picks up; rendered literally as 'the former' (the nearest antecedent is 'experiences').","Original spellings (Erkentniß, Urtheile) normalized; 'a priori' left in Latin and italics not added, as in the source's roman type being unrecoverable here."]],"krv-a-0041":["But what wants to say still far more is this, that certain cognitions even leave the field of all possible experiences, and through concepts for which no corresponding object can be given anywhere in experience, have the appearance of extending the domain of our judgments beyond all boundaries of the same.",["Umfang = domain; Grenzen = boundaries; Gegenstand = object; möglich = possible.","\"derselben\" ('of the same') is ambiguous between 'experiences' and 'judgments'; left as ambiguous as the German.","\"den Anschein haben\" = 'have the appearance of' (Anschein is not in the glossary)."]],"krv-a-0042":["And precisely in these latter cognitions, which go beyond the world of sense, where experience can give neither guideline nor correction, lie the investigations of our reason, which we hold, as to their importance, to be far preferable, and their final aim much more sublime, than everything that the understanding can learn in the field of appearances, in which we, even at the risk of erring, would rather venture everything than that we should give up such pressing investigations for any ground of reservation, or from disparagement and indifference.",["Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Endabsicht = final aim (Absicht = aim); Erscheinungen = appearances; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","Bedenklichkeit rendered 'reservation' (not in glossary); Leitfaden = 'guideline'; angelegene = 'pressing'.","\"wobey\" rendered 'in which'; its antecedent is ambiguous in the German and left so."]],"krv-a-0043":["Now it does seem natural that, as soon as one has left the ground of experience, one would nevertheless not immediately erect an edifice with cognitions that one possesses without knowing whence, and on the credit of principles whose origin one does not know, without having first been assured of its foundation through careful investigations, so that one would long ago have raised the question how the understanding could come to all these cognitions a priori, and what domain, validity and worth they may have. In fact nothing is more natural, if one understands by this word what ought to happen fairly and reasonably; but if one understands by it what happens in the ordinary way, then, on the contrary, nothing is more natural and comprehensible than that this investigation had to be omitted for a long time. For one part of these cognitions, the mathematical, is in ancient possession of reliability, and thereby gives a favorable expectation also for others, although these may be of an entirely different nature. Besides, when one is beyond the circle of experience, one is safe from being contradicted by experience. The charm of expanding one's cognitions is so great that one can be stopped in one's progress only by a clear contradiction on which one stumbles. This, however, can be avoided if one makes one's fictions cautiously, without their thereby remaining any the less fictions. Mathematics gives us a splendid example of how far we can get, independently of experience, in cognition a priori. Now it does occupy itself with objects and cognitions only so far as these can be exhibited in intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the intuition mentioned can itself be given a priori, and hence is hardly distinguished from a mere pure concept. Encouraged by such a proof of the power of reason, the drive for expansion sees no boundaries. The light dove, as it parts the air in free flight, whose resistance it feels, could take up the representation that it would succeed even much better in airless space. Just so Plato left the world of sense, because it places so many manifold hindrances in the way of the understanding, and ventured beyond it on the wings of the ideas, into the empty space of the pure understanding. He did not notice that he gained no way through his efforts, for he had no resistance, as it were as a support, on which he could prop himself, and to which he could apply his forces, in order to get the understanding off the spot. But it is a usual fate of human reason in speculation to finish its edifice as early as possible, and only afterward to investigate whether the ground for it has also been well laid. But then all sorts of embellishments are sought out, in order to console us about its soundness, or to reject such a late and dangerous examination. But what keeps us free from all worry and suspicion during the building, and flatters us with apparent thoroughness, is this. A large part, and perhaps the largest, of the business of our reason consists in dissections of the concepts that we already have of objects. This furnishes us a multitude of cognitions, which, although they are nothing further than illuminations or clarifications of that which was already thought in our concepts (though still in a confused way), are nevertheless, at least as to form, esteemed equal to new insights, although as to matter or content they do not expand the concepts that we have, but only set them apart. Since this procedure now gives an actual cognition a priori, which has a secure and useful progress, reason, without itself noticing it, sneaks in under this pretense assertions of an entirely different kind, where reason adds to given concepts something entirely foreign a priori, without one's knowing how it arrives at this, and without letting this question even come into one's thoughts. I will therefore deal right at the outset with the difference between these two kinds of cognition.",["Fortschritt and Fortgang are both rendered 'progress'; Erweiterung/erweitern = 'expansion/expand' (kept apart from Ausdehnung = extension).","Aufklärungen = 'illuminations' and Erläuterungen = 'clarifications' (glossary: Erläuterung = clarification; elucidation).","Widerhalt (Wiederhalt) rendered 'resistance' (not in glossary); Tüchtigkeit = 'soundness'; Beschönigungen = 'embellishments'; Erdichtungen = 'fictions'; erschleicht = 'sneaks in'.","Art = 'way' (auf verworrne Art) and 'kind' (von ganz anderer Art; Erkentnißart); 'nature' renders Natur.","\"ohne der Grundlegung desselben ... versichert zu seyn\": 'its foundation'; 'desselben' refers back to Gebäude (edifice).","Gegenstände = objects; Anschauung = intuition; Materie/Inhalt = matter/content; darstellen = 'exhibit'; Grenzen = 'boundaries'; Zergliederung = 'dissection'."]],"krv-a-0044":["On the difference between analytic and synthetic judgments.",["Urtheile = judgments."]],"krv-a-0045":["In all judgments in which the relation of a subject to the predicate is thought (if I consider only the affirmative ones: for the application to the negative ones is easy) this relation is possible in two ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A as something that is contained (in a concealed way) in this concept A; or B lies entirely outside the concept A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first case I call the judgment analytic, in the other synthetic. Analytic judgments (the affirmative ones) are thus those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is thought through identity, but those in which this connection is thought without identity are to be called synthetic judgments. The former could also be called judgments of clarification, the other judgments of expansion, because the former through the predicate add nothing to the concept of the subject, but only break it up through dissection into its partial concepts, which were already thought in it (though confusedly): whereas the latter add to the concept of the subject a predicate that was not thought in it at all, and could not have been extracted from it through any dissection of it, e.g., if I say: all bodies are extended, this is an analytic judgment. For I need not go beyond the concept that I combine with the word body in order to find extension as connected with it, but only dissect that concept, i.e., become conscious of the manifold that I always think in it, in order to encounter this predicate therein; it is therefore an analytic judgment. By contrast, if I say: all bodies are heavy, the predicate is something entirely different from what I think in the mere concept of a body in general. The addition of such a predicate therefore gives a synthetic judgment.",["Verknüpfung = connection; verbinden = combine (Verbindung would be 'combination'); Erläuterungs- = 'clarification'; Erweiterungs- = 'expansion' (cf. Ausdehnung = extension).","Prädicat/Subject: predicate/subject; Mannigfaltige = manifold; bewust werden = 'become conscious' (Bewußtsein = consciousness); Theilbegriffe = 'partial concepts'.","\"(versteckter Weise)\" = 'in a concealed way'; \"verworren\" = 'confused'.","The colon after 'confusedly' and the 'e.g.,' (z. B.) follow the original punctuation; 'ienes/iene' (the former) and 'die leztere' (the latter) kept as such."]],"krv-a-0046":["Now it is clear from this: 1) that through analytic judgments our cognition is not extended at all, but the concept that I already have is set out, and made understandable to myself; 2) that in synthetic judgments I must have, besides the concept of the subject, something else (X), on which the understanding supports itself, in order to cognize a predicate that does not lie in that concept as nevertheless belonging to it.",["Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize (in 'als dazu gehörig zu erkennen'); verständlich machen rendered 'made understandable' (cf. verstehen = understand).","'Subiects' retained as 'subject'; 'Prädicat' = predicate. 'ienem Begriffe' = 'that concept'.","Original numbering '1)' and '2)' kept; the semicolon after 'myself' replaces the German period-less run (the original has a period before '2)'); this is the only punctuation adjustment, and the original reads 'werde. 2)'."]],"krv-a-0047":["In empirical or experiential judgments there is no difficulty at all with this. For this X is the complete experience of the object, which I think through a concept A, which constitutes only a part of this experience. For although in the concept of a body in general I do not include the predicate of weight at all, it nevertheless designates the complete experience through a part of it, to which, therefore, I can still add other parts of just the same experience, as belonging to the former. I can previously cognize the concept of body analytically through the marks of extension, of impenetrability, of shape, etc., which are all thought in this concept. Now, however, I extend my cognition, and, in that I look back to the experience from which I had abstracted this concept of body, I find weight always connected with the above marks as well. It is therefore experience that is that X which lies outside the concept A, and on which the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight B with the concept A is grounded.",["Gegenstand = object; Schwere = weight (not in glossary); Merkmal = mark; Ausdehnung = extension; Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung = connection); gründet = is grounded (Grund = ground).","'Theil' = part; 'Synthesis' kept as in the German; 'Möglichkeit' = possibility.","'und, indem ich auf die Erfahrung zurück sehe' preserves the German's awkward 'and, in that I look back'; 'abgezogen' = abstracted.","'Erfahrungsurtheilen' rendered 'experiential judgments'."]],"krv-a-0048":["But in synthetic judgments a priori this aid is entirely lacking. If I am to go out beyond the concept A in order to cognize another, B, as combined with it, what is it on which I support myself, and through which the synthesis becomes possible, since here I do not have the advantage of looking around for it in the field of experience. Take the proposition: \"Everything that happens has its cause.\" In the concept of something that happens I think, to be sure, an existence, before which a time precedes, etc., and from this analytic judgments can be drawn. But the concept of a cause indicates something different from what happens, and is not contained at all in this latter representation. How then do I come to say of that which happens in general something entirely different from it, and to cognize the concept of cause, although not contained in the former, nevertheless as belonging to it. What is the X here on which the understanding supports itself when it believes it finds, outside the concept of A, a predicate foreign to it, which is nevertheless supposed to be connected with it. Experience it cannot be, because the cited principle adds this second representation to the first not only with greater universality than experience can provide, but also with the expression of necessity, hence entirely a priori and from mere concepts. Now on such synthetic, i.e., extension-principles rests the whole final aim of our speculative cognition a priori; for the analytic ones are, to be sure, highly important and necessary, but only in order to attain to that distinctness of concepts which is requisite for a secure and extended synthesis, as for a really new cultivation.",["Satz = proposition; Grundsatz = principle; Ausdruck = expression; Endabsicht = final aim (Absicht = aim); Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich).","Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Vorstellung = representation; Verstand = understanding; Begriff = concept.","'Erweiterungs-Grundsätzen' = 'extension-principles' (literal compound; 'Erweiterung' is not in the glossary, cognate 'extension' also renders Ausdehnung elsewhere in the set).","'ienen' in 'in ienen nicht enthalten' is plural in the German (likely 'jenem' or a plural referent); rendered 'the former' with the ambiguity of reference left; text as printed has 'Ursachen' (plural) in 'den Begriff der Ursachen'—rendered 'concept of cause' as a smoothing; 'Anbau' = cultivation.","'Hülfsmittel' = aid; the sentence 'was ist das ... wird' lacks a question mark in the German and is kept so; Nothwendigkeit = necessity."]],"krv-a-0049":["Here, then, a certain secret lies hidden *), the disclosure of which alone can make the progress in the boundless field of pure cognition of the understanding secure and reliable: namely, with proper universality to uncover the ground of the possibility of synthetic judgments a priori, to have insight into the conditions that make possible every kind of them, and to determine this whole cognition (which constitutes its own genus) in a system according to its original sources, divisions, domain and boundaries, not to designate it through a cursory circuit, but completely and sufficiently for every use. So much provisionally about what is peculiar to synthetic judgments in themselves.",["Fortschritt = progress (Fortgang elsewhere = progression); Grund = ground; Bedingung = condition; Art = kind; Gattung = genus; Umfang = domain; Grenze = boundary; Gebrauch = use; an sich = in themselves.","'Verstandeserkentniß' = 'cognition of the understanding'; 'nach ihren ursprünglichen Quellen' preserved; 'nicht durch einen flüchtigen Umkreis zu bezeichnen' is preserved as an infinitive clause parallel to the others.","The asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0050) as printed."]],"krv-a-0050":["*) Had it occurred to one of the ancients even merely to raise this question, this alone would have powerfully resisted all systems of pure reason up to our time, and would have spared so many vain attempts, which were undertaken blindly, without knowing what one really has to do with.",["Vernunft = reason; 'rein' = pure; 'eitele' = vain; 'mächtig widerstanden haben' = 'would have powerfully resisted' (literal; the object of the resistance is as ambiguous as in the German)."]],"krv-a-0051":["From all this there now results the idea of a particular science that could serve for the critique of pure reason. But every cognition is called pure that is not mixed with anything foreign. But a cognition is especially called absolutely pure into which no experience or sensation at all is mixed, which is hence entirely possible a priori. Now reason is the faculty that provides the principles of cognition a priori. Hence pure reason is that which contains the principles for cognizing something absolutely a priori. An organon of pure reason would be a sum total of those principles in accordance with which all pure cognitions a priori can be acquired and actually brought about. The detailed application of such an organon would provide a system of pure reason. Since this, however, is demanding very much, and it still stands open whether such an extension of our cognition is possible at all, and in which cases it is possible; we can regard a science of the mere judgment of pure reason, of its sources and boundaries, as the propaedeutic to the system of pure reason. Such a science would have to be called not a doctrine but only critique of pure reason, and its use would really be only negative, serving not for extension but only for the purification of our reason, and for keeping it free from errors, by which very much is already gained. I call all cognition transcendental that is occupied not so much with objects but with our concepts a priori of objects in general. A system of such concepts would be called transcendental philosophy. But this is again too much for the beginning. For since such a science would have to contain completely both analytic cognition and synthetic cognition a priori, it is, so far as our aim is concerned, of too wide a domain, since we are permitted to carry the analysis only so far as it is indispensably necessary in order to have insight into the principles of synthesis a priori, with which alone we are concerned, in their whole domain. This investigation, which we can properly call not doctrine but only transcendental critique, because it has as its aim not the extension of cognitions themselves but only their correction, and is to furnish the touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all cognitions a priori, is that with which we are now occupied. Such a critique is accordingly a preparation, if possible, for an organon, and, if this should not succeed, at least for a canon of them, in accordance with which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it consist in the extension or the mere limiting of its cognition, could someday be presented, both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, indeed that such a system can be of not too great a domain, so that one may hope to complete it entirely, can be gauged in advance from this, that here it is not the nature of things, which is inexhaustible, but the understanding, which judges about the nature of things, and this again only with regard to its cognition a priori, that constitutes the object, whose store, since we need not seek it externally, cannot remain hidden from us, and in all likelihood is small enough to be completely taken up, judged according to its worth or worthlessness, and brought under correct estimation.",["Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Vermögen = faculty; Principien = principles; Inbegriff = sum total; Umfang = domain (both 'Umfang' occurrences); Absicht = aim; Grenzen = boundaries; Begrenzung = 'limiting'.","Gegenstand = object (Gegenständen); 'Ding' = thing; Beurtheilung = judgment (in 'blossen Beurtheilung'), beurtheilt = judged; urtheilt = judges; 'Schätzung' = estimation.","'Doctrin' / 'Lehre': the text has 'Doctrin' (Latinate), rendered 'doctrine'; 'Propädevtick' = 'propaedeutic'. 'Organon' and 'Canon' kept as in the German (roman type in the original, hence italics would be expected; left unitalicized here only because the passage prints them as ordinary words—flag if wanted).","'es heißt ... iede Erkentniß rein' = 'every cognition is called pure'; 'die sich nicht so wohl mit Gegenständen, sondern' preserves the irregular 'nicht so wohl ... sondern' ('not so much ... but').","'Läuterung' = purification; 'Berichtigung' = correction; 'Probierstein' = touchstone; 'Vorrath' = store; 'wirklich' = actually / really.","Ambiguity kept: 'welches schon sehr viel gewonnen ist' (what 'which' refers to); 'nach welchen' (plural, referent Organon/Canon); 'in Erweiterung oder blosser Begrenzung ihrer Erkentniß' (whose cognition: reason's)."]],"krv-a-0052":["II. Division of Transcendental Philosophy.",["Eintheilung = division; Transscendental-Philosophie = transcendental philosophy."]],"krv-a-0053":["Transcendental philosophy is here only an idea, for which the critique of pure reason is to sketch the entire plan architectonically, i.e., from principles, with complete guarantee of the completeness and reliability of all the pieces that make up this edifice. That this critique is not itself already called transcendental philosophy rests merely on the fact that, in order to be a complete system, it would also have to contain a detailed analysis of the whole of human cognition a priori. Now our critique must indeed also lay before the eyes a complete enumeration of all the root concepts that make up the aforementioned pure cognition. But it fittingly refrains from the detailed analysis of these concepts themselves, as also from the complete survey of those derived from them, partly because this dissection would not be purposive, since it does not have the questionableness that is met with in synthesis, for whose sake the whole critique is really there, partly because it would be contrary to the unity of the plan to concern oneself with the responsibility for the completeness of such an analysis and derivation, of which one could nevertheless be relieved with regard to one's aim. This completeness of the dissection as well as of the derivation from the concepts a priori to be supplied in the future is, however, easy to supplement, if only they are first of all there as detailed principles of synthesis, and nothing is lacking to them with regard to this essential aim.",["Critik = critique; Philosophie = philosophy; Gewärleistung (Gewährleistung) = guarantee; Herzehlung (Herzählung) silently corrected, = enumeration.","Stammbegriffe = root concepts; Zergliederung = dissection; Analysis = analysis; Recension = survey; Ableitung = derivation; Absicht = aim; Principien = principles.","zweckmäßig rendered 'purposive' (Zweck = end; purpose); Bedenklichkeit rendered 'questionableness'; both are choices outside the glossary.","'a priori' in 'Begriffen a priori' and 'Erkentniß a priori' is left attached as in the German; 'Stücke' = pieces."]],"krv-a-0054":["Everything that makes up transcendental philosophy accordingly belongs to the critique of pure reason, and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but this science not yet itself, because in analysis it goes only so far as is requisite for the complete assessment of synthetic cognition a priori.",["Beurtheilung = assessment (glossary gives 'estimation; judgment' for the noun); Urtheil is rendered 'judgment' elsewhere.","Wissenschaft = science; Erkentniß = cognition."]],"krv-a-0055":["The foremost focus in the division of such a science is: that no concepts at all must enter into it that contain anything empirical in themselves, or that the cognition a priori be entirely pure. Hence, although the supreme principles of morality, and the basic concepts of it, are cognitions a priori, they nevertheless do not belong in transcendental philosophy, because the concepts of pleasure and displeasure, of desires and inclinations, of choice, etc., which are all of empirical origin, would have to be presupposed in this connection. Hence transcendental philosophy is a philosophy of pure merely speculative reason. For everything practical, insofar as it contains motives, is related to feelings, which belong to empirical sources of cognition.",["Weltweisheit = philosophy (the glossary's single word; Philosophie in the preceding sentence is also 'philosophy').","Grundsätze = principles; Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; Willkühr = choice (not in the glossary); Bewegungsgründe = motives; Gefühle = feelings.","Augenmerk = focus; Eintheilung = division; 'dabey' rendered 'in this connection'.","'in sich' / 'hineinkommen': 'into it' refers to the science (Wissenschaft)."]],"krv-a-0056":["If one now wants to set up the division of this science from the universal standpoint of a system in general, then the one that we are now presenting must contain, first, a doctrine of elements, second, a doctrine of method of pure reason. Each of these main parts would have its subdivision, the grounds of which, nevertheless, cannot yet be presented here. Only so much seems necessary for the introduction or preliminary reminder, that there are two stems of human cognition, which perhaps arise from a common but to us unknown root, namely, sensibility and understanding, through the first of which objects are given to us, but through the second, however, are thought. Insofar as sensibility is now supposed to contain representations a priori, which make up the conditions under which objects are given to us, it would belong to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense would have to belong to the first part of the science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human cognition are given precede those under which they are thought.",["'die, welche wir iezt vortragen': 'the one' has no explicit antecedent in the German beyond 'Wissenschaft'/'Eintheilung'; kept ambiguous.","Elementar-Lehre = doctrine of elements; Methoden-Lehre = doctrine of method; Elementarwissenschaft = science of elements; Sinnenlehre = doctrine of sense (Sinn = sense).","Gegenstände = objects; Vorstellungen = representations; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Verstand = understanding; Bedingungen = conditions.","'durch deren ersteren ... durch den zweiten' rendered 'through the first of which ... through the second'; 'selbige' rendered 'they'."]],"krv-a-0057":["Critique of Pure Reason. I. Transcendental Doctrine of Elements.",["Elementarlehre = doctrine of elements."]],"krv-a-0058":["Transcendental Doctrine of Elements, First Part. The Transcendental Aesthetic.",[]],"krv-a-0059":["In whatever way and by whatever means a cognition may relate to objects, that through which it relates to them immediately, and at which all thinking as a means aims, is intuition. This, however, takes place only insofar as the object is given to us; but this, in turn, is possible only through its affecting the mind in a certain way. The capacity (receptivity) to acquire representations through the way in which we are affected by objects is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone supplies us with intuitions; but through the understanding they are thought, and from it arise concepts. All thinking, however, must, whether straight away (directly) or by detours (indirectly), ultimately relate to intuitions, hence, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.",["'auf dieselbe' is feminine singular although 'Gegenstände' is plural; rendered 'to them'.","Anschauung = intuition; Fähigkeit = capacity; Art = way; Weise = way (both rendered 'way'); Gemüth = mind; Gegenstand = object.","Receptivität, directe, indirecte are in parentheses in the German, kept as such in English form; Umschweife = detours."]],"krv-a-0060":["The effect of an object on the capacity for representation, insofar as we are affected by it, is sensation. That intuition which relates to the object through sensation is called empirical. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called appearance.",["Wirkung = effect; Vorstellungsfähigkeit = capacity for representation; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance; unbestimmt = undetermined (bestimmen = determine)."]],"krv-a-0061":["In the appearance I call that which corresponds to sensation the matter of it, but that which brings it about that the manifold of appearance is intuited as ordered in certain relations I call the form of appearance. Since that in which alone the sensations can be ordered and placed in a certain form cannot itself in turn be sensation, the matter of all appearance is, to be sure, given to us only a posteriori, but the form of it must lie ready for them all together in the mind a priori, and can therefore be considered separately from all sensation.",["Materie = matter; Form = form; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Verhältnissen = relations; 'dahero' = therefore; 'betrachtet' = considered (Betrachtung = consideration).","'der Erscheinung' / 'derselben' / 'ihnen': 'of it' and 'for them' follow the German's shifts between singular and plural."]],"krv-a-0062":["I call all representations pure (in the transcendental sense) in which nothing is encountered that belongs to sensation. Accordingly the pure form of sensible intuitions in general, in which all the manifold of appearances is intuited in certain relations, will be encountered in the mind a priori. This pure form of sensibility will itself also be called pure intuition. Thus, if from the representation of a body I separate what the understanding thinks of it, such as substance, force, divisibility, etc., likewise what belongs to sensation, such as impenetrability, hardness, color, etc., something still remains for me from this empirical intuition, namely extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which takes place a priori in the mind, even without an actual object of the senses or of sensation, as a mere form of sensibility.",["'im transscendentalen Verstande' rendered 'in the transcendental sense'; the word is Verstande (dative of Verstand), the same word as 'understanding' elsewhere.","rein = pure; Kraft = force; Ausdehnung = extension; Gestalt = shape; wirklich = actual; Sinne = senses.","'worinnen' in the second sentence could refer to the form or to the mind; the German is ambiguous and the English keeps 'in which'.","'a priori' in 'im Gemüthe a priori angetroffen' and 'die a priori ... statt findet' is left as placed in the German."]],"krv-a-0063":["I call a science of all principles of sensibility a priori the transcendental aesthetic.*) There must therefore be such a science, which makes up the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contrast with that which contains the principles of pure thinking, and is called transcendental logic.",["The footnote marker *) is kept as in the source.","Principien = principles; Denken = thinking."]],"krv-a-0064":["*) The Germans are the only ones who now make use of the word aesthetic in order thereby to designate what others call the critique of taste. At the basis of this lies a failed hope, which the excellent analyst Baumgarten conceived, of bringing the critical assessment of the beautiful under principles of reason, and of elevating its rules to a science. But this effort is futile. For the rules, or criteria in question are, as regards their sources, merely empirical, and can therefore never serve as laws a priori by which our judgment of taste would have to be directed; rather the latter constitutes the proper touchstone of the correctness of the former. For that reason it is advisable to let this term again lapse, and to reserve it for that doctrine which is true science, whereby one would also come closer to the language and the sense of the ancients, among whom the division of cognition into αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά was very famous.",["The Greek is printed ἀιϑητὰ καὶ νόητα in the OCR; silently corrected to αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά.","Benennung = term; Criterien = criteria; Gesetzen = laws; Geschmacksurtheil = judgment of taste; Beurtheilung = assessment; Lehre = doctrine; Wissenschaft = science; Sinne = sense.","'Quellen. nach' in the OCR read as 'Quellen nach' (ihren Quellen nach, 'as regards their sources')."]],"krv-a-0065":["In the transcendental aesthetic, therefore, we will first isolate sensibility, by separating off everything that the understanding thinks with it through its concepts, so that nothing but empirical intuition remains. Second, we will separate off from this in addition everything that belongs to sensation, so that nothing but pure intuition and the mere form of appearances remains, which is the only thing that sensibility can deliver *a priori*. In this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensible intuition, as principles of cognition *a priori*, namely, space and time, with the consideration of which we will now occupy ourselves.",["Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Verstand = understanding; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance; Erkentniß (sic) = cognition; Erwegung (Erwägung) = consideration.","'dabey denkt': 'dabey' rendered 'with it'; its referent (sensibility or the isolating) is left as open as in the German.","Principien = 'principles'; the same English word is used for Grundsätze and Principien elsewhere in this set."]],"krv-a-0066":["The Transcendental Aesthetic, First Section. On Space.",["Original spelling 'Transscendentalen Aesthetik'."]],"krv-a-0067":["By means of outer sense (a property of our mind) we represent objects to ourselves as outside us, and all of them together in space. Therein their shape, magnitude and relation to one another is determined, or determinable. Inner sense, by means of which the mind intuits itself, or its inner state, to be sure gives no intuition of the soul itself as an object, yet there is nonetheless a determinate form under which alone the intuition of its inner state is possible, so that everything that belongs to the inner determinations is represented in relations of time. Outwardly time cannot be intuited, any more than space can, as something in us. What, now, are space and time? Are they actual beings? Are they, to be sure, only determinations, or also relations of things, but yet such as would belong to them in themselves even if they were not intuited, or are they such as adhere only to the form of intuition alone, and consequently to the subjective constitution of our mind, without which these predicates cannot be ascribed to any thing at all? In order to be taught about this, we want first to consider space.",["Gegenstände and Obiect both = 'object'; Obiect is the Latinate member (here: 'als einem Obiect').","Eigenschaft = 'property'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution' (the glossary also allows 'property'); Gemüth = 'mind'; Zustand = 'state'; Wesen = 'beings'; Dinge = 'things'.","'an sich' in 'auch an sich zukommen würden' = 'in themselves'.","Verhältnisse = 'relations'; 'Verhältniß gegen einander' = 'relation to one another'. Verb 'ist bestimmt' is singular after three subjects, as in the German.","'uns belehren' rendered 'to be taught' (Belehrung = teaching)."]],"krv-a-0068":["1) Space is no empirical concept that has been abstracted from outer experiences. For in order that certain sensations be related to something outside me, (i.e., to something in another place of space than that in which I find myself,) likewise in order that I could represent them as outside one another, consequently not merely as different but as in different places, the representation of space must already lie at the basis. Accordingly the representation of space cannot be borrowed through experience from the relations of outer appearance, but this outer experience is itself possible only through the said representation in the first place.",["The parentheses are unbalanced in the original ('als darinnen ich mich befinde,)'); kept as printed.","'zum Grunde liegen' = 'lie at the basis' (Grund = ground; basis).","'gedachte Vorstellung' rendered 'the said representation'; 'gedacht' could also be read as 'thought' (the representation that is thought).","Ort = 'place'; Empfindung = 'sensation'; Erfahrung = 'experience'."]],"krv-a-0069":["2) Space is a necessary representation, *a priori*, which lies at the basis of all outer intuitions. One can never make for oneself a representation of there being no space, although one can quite well think that no objects are to be encountered in it. It is therefore regarded as the condition of the possibility of appearances, and not as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation *a priori* that necessarily lies at the basis of outer appearances.",["'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the basis'; Bedingung = condition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Bestimmung = determination.","'nothwendiger Weise' = 'necessarily' (literally 'in a necessary way')."]],"krv-a-0070":["3) On this necessity *a priori* is grounded the apodictic certainty of all geometrical principles, and the possibility of their constructions *a priori*. For if this representation of space were a concept acquired *a posteriori*, which was drawn from general outer experience, then the first principles of mathematical determination would be nothing but perceptions. They would thus have all the contingency of perception, and it would just not be necessary that between two points there is only one straight line, but experience would always teach it so. What is borrowed from experience also has only comparative generality, namely through induction. One would therefore be able to say only: as much as has so far been noticed, no space has been found that would have more than three dimensions.",["Grundsätze = 'principles'; Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Zufälligkeit = 'contingency'; Nothwendigkeit = 'necessity'; Abmessungen = 'dimensions'.","'Allgemeinheit' = 'generality' and allgemein = 'general' throughout, for consistency within the set.","'nemlich' at the start of the second sentence rendered 'For'; later 'nemlich' = 'namely'."]],"krv-a-0071":["4) Space is no discursive or, as one says, general concept of relations of things in general, but a pure intuition. For first, one can represent to oneself only one single space, and if one speaks of many spaces, one understands by that only parts of one and the same sole space. These parts also cannot precede the single all-encompassing space as it were as its constituents (from which its composition would be possible), but can be thought only in it. It is essentially single; the manifold in it, consequently also the general concept of spaces in general, rests merely on limitations. From this it follows that, with regard to it, an intuition *a priori* (which is not empirical) lies at the basis of all concepts of the same. Thus also all geometrical principles, e.g., that in a triangle two sides together are greater than the third, are never derived from general concepts of line and triangle, but from intuition and indeed *a priori* with apodictic certainty.",["'einig' = 'single'; 'alleinig' = 'sole'; 'allbefassend' = 'all-encompassing'.","Mannigfaltige = 'manifold'; Einschränkungen = 'limitations' (the glossary's Schranke is also 'limitation'); Bestandtheile = 'constituents'; Zusammensetzung = 'composition'.","'von denselben' ('of the same') is ambiguous between the concepts' object, space, and spaces; left so.","'Größer' = 'greater'; Triangel = 'triangle'."]],"krv-a-0072":["5) Space is represented as an infinite magnitude given. A general concept of space (which is common to the foot as well as the ell,) can determine nothing with regard to magnitude. Were it not the boundlessness in the progression of intuition, no concept of relations would bring with it a *principium* of the infinity of them.",["'als eine unendliche Größe gegeben vorgestellt': 'given' could attach to 'magnitude' or to 'represented' ('represented as given'); the word order is kept.","Fortgang = 'progression'; Grenzenlosigkeit = 'boundlessness'; Größe = 'magnitude'.","'Principium' is Kant's Latin, kept in italics; the same word 'principle' renders Principien/Grundsätze elsewhere.","'Wäre es nicht die Grenzenlosigkeit' kept literally."]],"krv-a-0073":["Inferences from the above concepts.",["Schlüsse = 'inferences' (the glossary also allows 'conclusions')."]],"krv-a-0074":["a) Space represents no property of any things whatever in themselves, or of them in their relation to one another, i.e., no determination of them that attached to objects themselves, and that would remain if one abstracted from all subjective conditions of intuition. For neither absolute nor relative determinations can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, consequently not *a priori*.",["'an sich' could attach to 'Eigenschaft' or to 'Dinge'; the English word order keeps both possible.","Daseyn = 'existence'; Gegenständen = 'objects'; Bedingungen = 'conditions'.","'mithin nicht a priori angeschaut werden' is kept as an elliptical continuation of 'weder ... noch ... können ... angeschaut werden'."]],"krv-a-0075":["b) Space is nothing other than only the form of all appearances of outer senses, i.e., the subjective condition of sensibility, under which alone outer intuition is possible for us. Since now the receptivity of the subject, to be affected by objects, necessarily precedes all intuitions of these objects, […] it can be understood how the form of all appearances can be given in the mind prior to all actual perceptions, consequently *a priori*, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of their relations prior to all experience.",["The ellipsis in square brackets […] stands where the Academy text has it; nothing is supplied.","Gegenstände and Obiecte both = 'object'; Obiecte is the Latinate member.","Wirklich = 'actual'; Receptivität = 'receptivity'; afficirt = 'affected'; 'läßt sich verstehen' = 'it can be understood'."]],"krv-a-0076":["We can accordingly speak of space, of extended beings etc., only from the standpoint of a human being. If we depart from the subjective condition under which alone we can get outer intuition, namely as we may be affected by the objects, then the representation of space signifies nothing at all. This predicate is attributed to things only insofar as they appear to us, i.e., are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects are intuited as outside us, and, if one abstracts from these objects, a pure intuition, which bears the name space. Because we cannot make the particular conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of their appearances, we can well say that space encompasses all things that may appear to us externally, but not all things in themselves, whether they are intuited or not, or by whatever subject one likes. For we cannot judge at all about the intuitions of other thinking beings, whether they are bound to the very same conditions that restrict our intuition and are universally valid for us. If we add the restriction of a judgment to the concept of the subject, then the judgment holds unconditionally. The proposition: All things are next to one another in space, holds only under the restriction that these things are taken as objects of our sensible intuition. If I here add the condition to the concept, and say: All things, as outer appearances, are next to one another in space, then this rule holds universally and without restriction. Our expositions accordingly teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space with respect to everything that can come before us externally as an object, but at the same time the ideality of space with respect to things when they are considered by reason in themselves, i.e., without taking regard to the constitution of our sensibility. We assert, therefore, the empirical reality of space (with respect to all possible outer experience), although at the same time its transcendental ideality, i.e., that it is nothing as soon as we leave out the condition of the possibility of all experience, and assume it as something that lies at the ground of things in themselves.",["Standpuncte = standpoint; Wesen = being; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Receptivität = receptivity.","Gegenstand = object (throughout); Subiect left as 'subject'.","'an sich selbst' = 'in themselves'; 'an sich' = 'in themselves'.","'vor uns allgemein gültig seyn': 'vor' (= für) rendered 'for'; the grammatical connection of this clause to 'sie' is as ambiguous in the English as in the German.","'bedeutet' = signifies; 'Erörterungen' = expositions; 'erwogen' = considered; 'Rücksicht' = regard; 'Ansehung' = respect; 'Einschränkung' = restriction; 'Beschaffenheit' = constitution (glossary also allows 'property').","'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the ground' (Grund = ground).","'ob zwar' (= obgleich) = 'although'."]],"krv-a-0077":["But apart from space there is also no other subjective representation, related to something outer, that could be called objective *a priori*. Hence this subjective condition of all outer appearances can be compared with no other. The agreeable taste of a wine does not belong to the objective determinations of the wine, hence of an object even considered as appearance, but to the particular constitution of the sense in the subject that enjoys it. Colors are not properties of the bodies to whose intuition they adhere, but are also only modifications of the sense of sight, which is affected by light in a certain way. Space, on the contrary, as condition of outer objects, necessarily belongs to the appearance or intuition of them. Taste and colors are not at all necessary conditions under which alone the objects can become objects of the senses for us. They are connected with the appearance only as effects contingently added by the particular organization. Hence they are also no representations *a priori*, but are grounded on sensation, and the agreeable taste even on feeling (of pleasure and displeasure) as an effect of sensation. Also, no one can have *a priori* either a representation of a color, or of any taste: but space concerns only the pure form of intuition, hence includes no sensation at all (nothing empirical), and all kinds and determinations of space can and must even be able to be represented *a priori*, if concepts of shapes as well as of relations are to arise. Through it alone is it possible that things be outer objects for us.",["Obiect and Gegenstand both = 'object'; here 'Obiects' (Latinate) occurs in 'eines Obiects' and 'Obiecte der Sinne'.","'obiectiv' = objective; 'Bestimmungen' = determinations; 'Beschaffenheiten' = properties, 'Beschaffenheit des Sinnes' = constitution (same German word).","'Wirkung/Würkung' = effect; 'zufällig' = contingently; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Gefühl' = feeling.","'vor uns' = 'for us' (vor = für). 'ausserer' in the source is a misprint for 'äusserer' ('outer').","'der Raum aber betrift...' : 'so gar a priori vorgestellt werden können' is syntactically awkward in the German ('can and must even be able to be represented'); kept."]],"krv-a-0078":["The aim of this remark goes only to preventing this: that one let it occur to oneself to clarify the asserted ideality of space through far from adequate examples, since, namely, colors, taste etc. are rightly considered not as properties of things, but merely as alterations of our subject, which can even be different in different human beings. For in this case that which is originally itself only appearance, e.g., a rose, counts in the empirical understanding as a thing in itself, which nevertheless can appear different to every eye with respect to color. The transcendental concept of appearances in space, on the contrary, is a critical reminder that nothing at all that is intuited in space is a thing in itself, nor is space a form of things that would be proper to them in themselves, but that the objects in themselves are not known to us at all, and what we call outer objects are nothing other than mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose true *correlatum*, i.e., the thing in itself, is not cognized by this at all, nor can be cognized, but about which also no question is ever asked in experience.",["'erläutern' = clarify (Erläuterung = clarification). 'Sache' = 'thing' (as is 'Ding'); both rendered 'thing'.","'im empirischen Verstande': 'Verstande' rendered 'understanding' per glossary; it may be idiomatic ('in the empirical sense').","'bekant' = known (kennen); 'erkant' = cognized (erkennen).","'Erinnerung' = reminder; 'Correlatum' kept in Latin, italicized.","The sentence 'Dagegen ... ist ... eine critische Erinnerung, daß ... noch daß der Raum...' preserves the German construction of 'nichts ... eine Sache an sich, noch daß...' as it stands."]],"krv-a-0079":["Second Section of the Transcendental Aesthetic. On Time.",[]],"krv-a-0080":["I.",[]],"krv-a-0081":["Time is no empirical concept that has been abstracted from any experience. For simultaneity or succeeding one another would itself not come into perception if the representation of time did not lie *a priori* at the ground. Only under its presupposition can one represent to oneself that some things are at one and the same time (simultaneously) or in different times (one after another).",["'Boraussetzung' in the source is an OCR slip for 'Voraussetzung' ('presupposition').","'Zugleichseyn' = simultaneity (lit. being-at-the-same-time); 'Aufeinanderfolgen' = succeeding one another; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception; 'zum Grunde läge' = 'lay at the ground'.","'deren' refers back to 'Vorstellung der Zeit' as the German allows; kept as 'its'."]],"krv-a-0082":["2) Time is a necessary representation that lies at the ground of all intuitions. With regard to appearances in general one cannot remove time itself, although one can quite well take the appearances away from time. Time is therefore given *a priori*. In it alone is all actuality of appearances possible. These can all together drop out, but it itself, as the general condition of their possibility,) cannot be removed.",["The stray closing parenthesis after 'Möglichkeit,' is in the text as given (no opening parenthesis appears); kept.","'Wirklichkeit' = actuality; 'aufheben' = remove; 'wegnehmen' = take away; 'wegfallen' = drop out.","'in Ansehung der Erscheinungen überhaupt' = 'with regard to appearances in general'."]],"krv-a-0083":["3) On this necessity *a priori* is also grounded the possibility of apodictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general. It has only one dimension: different times are not simultaneous, but one after another (just as different spaces are not one after another, but simultaneous.) These principles cannot be drawn from experience, for this would give neither strict universality nor apodictic certainty. We would only be able to say: so common perception teaches, but not, so it must be. These principles hold as rules under which experiences are possible at all and teach us before it, and not through it.",["'Grundsätze' = principles; 'Verhältnisse' = relations; 'Belehren' = teach (Belehrung = teaching).","'vor derselben' = 'before it' (vor = before, temporal); 'derselben' and 'dieselbe' refer back to 'Erfahrungen'/'Erfahrung'; the English 'it' is as ambiguous as the German.","'Sie hat nur eine Dimension': 'Sie' = Time (die Zeit)."]],"krv-a-0084":["4) Time is no discursive, or, as it is called, general concept, but a pure form of sensible intuition. Different times are only parts of one and the same time. The representation, however, that can be given only through a single object is intuition. Also, the proposition that different times cannot be simultaneous could not be derived from a general concept. The proposition is synthetic, and cannot arise from concepts alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.",["'allgemeiner Begriff' = general concept; 'Theile' = parts; 'Satz' = proposition; 'synthetisch' = synthetic.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'unmittelbar' = immediately."]],"krv-a-0085":["5) The infinitude of time signifies nothing further than that every determinate magnitude of time is possible only through limitations of one single time lying at the basis. Hence the original representation time must be given as unlimited. But where the parts themselves, and every magnitude of an object, can be represented as determinate only through limitation, there the whole representation must not be given through concepts, (for there the partial representations come first) but their immediate intuition must lie at the basis.",["zum Grunde liegen: Grund rendered \"basis\" in this idiom (glossary also \"ground\"). Einschränkung and Schranke would both be \"limitation\".","einigen = \"single\" (einzigen).","bestimmt vorgestellt: \"as determinate\" keeps bestimmt open between adjective and adverb (\"determinately\")."]],"krv-a-0086":["Inferences from these concepts.",["Schlüsse = inferences (glossary: Schluß)."]],"krv-a-0087":["a) Time is not something that would subsist on its own, or attach to things as an objective determination, and thus remain if one abstracts from all subjective conditions of the intuition of them: for in the first case it would be something that would nevertheless be actual without an actual object. But as for the second, as a determination or order attaching to the things themselves it could not precede the objects as their condition, and be cognized and intuited a priori through synthetic propositions. The latter, by contrast, takes place very well if time is nothing but the subjective condition under which all intuitions can take place in us. For then this form of inner intuition can be represented before the objects, thus a priori.",["Gegenstand = object; the German has Gegenstand throughout, no Object here.","derselben (\"of them\") is left as in the German; its antecedent could be Dinge or Zeit-related items.","Diese letztere: \"The latter\" kept without specifying the referent.","erkant = cognized (erkennen)."]],"krv-a-0088":["b) Time is nothing other than the form of inner sense, i.e., of the intuiting of ourselves and of our inner state. For time can be no determination of outer appearances; it belongs neither to a shape, or position etc.; on the contrary it determines the relation of representations in our inner state. And, just because this inner intuition gives no shape, we also seek to supply this lack through analogies, and represent the temporal sequence through a line progressing to infinity, in which the manifold constitutes a series that is only of one dimension, and infer from the properties of this line to all properties of time, except the single one, that the parts of the former are simultaneous, but those of the latter are always one after another. From this it is also clear that the representation of time itself is intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an outer intuition.",["Zeitfolge = temporal sequence (Folge = sequence).","Eigenschaften = properties; Beschaffenheit (glossary also \"property\") does not occur here.","einigen = \"single\"; erstern/letztern = \"former\"/\"latter\" (line / time)."]],"krv-a-0089":["c) Time is the formal condition a priori of all appearances in general. Space, as the pure form of all outer intuition, is limited, as condition a priori, merely to outer appearances. By contrast, because all representations, whether they may now have outer things as object or not, nevertheless in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to the inner state: but this inner state belongs under the formal condition of inner intuition, thus of time, so time is a condition a priori of all appearance in general, and indeed the immediate condition of the inner (of our souls) and just thereby mediately also of the outer appearances. If I can say a priori: all outer appearances are in space, and determined a priori according to the relations of space, so I can say quite generally from the principle of inner sense: all appearances in general, i.e., all objects of the senses, are in time, and stand necessarily in relations of time.",["Gemüth = mind; Seelen = souls.","Princip = principle (Grundsatz in the glossary is also \"principle\").","eingeschränkt = \"limited\" (as Schranke would be \"limitation\").","Syntax of the long first sentence (dagegen weil ... so ist) kept; \"so\" after the colon is rendered \"so\"."]],"krv-a-0090":["If we abstract from our way of intuiting ourselves inwardly, and by means of this intuition also of encompassing all outer intuitions in the power of representation, and thus take the objects as they may be in themselves, then time is nothing. It is of objective validity only with regard to appearances, because these are already things that we assume as objects of our senses, but it is no longer objective if one abstracts from the sensibility of our intuition, thus from that way of representing which is peculiar to us, and speaks of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition, (which is always sensible, i.e., insofar as we are affected by objects) and in itself, outside the subject, nothing. Nonetheless it is necessarily objective with regard to all appearances, thus also all things that can come before us in experience. We cannot say: all things are in time, because in the concept of things in general abstraction is made from every way of intuiting them, but this is the proper condition under which time belongs in the representation of objects. If now the condition is added to the concept, and it is said: all things, as appearances (objects of sensible intuition) are in time, then the principle has its good objective correctness and universality a priori.",["Art = way (Art, Vorstellungsart, Art der Anschauung all rendered with \"way\").","befassen = \"encompass\" (not begreifen, \"comprehend\").","Vorstellungs- Kraft: \"power of representation\" (Kraft = power here).","an sich selbst = \"in themselves\"; an sich = \"in itself\".","Wird ... abstrahirt: impersonal passive kept; Grundsatz = principle."]],"krv-a-0091":["Our assertions accordingly teach the empirical reality of time, i.e., objective validity with regard to all objects that may ever be given to our senses. And since our intuition is always sensible, no object can ever be given to us in experience that would not belong under the condition of time. By contrast we dispute any claim of time to absolute reality, since namely, even without taking regard of the form of our sensible intuition, it would simply attach to things as a condition or property. Such properties as belong to things in themselves can also never be given to us through the senses. Therein consists, then, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which, if one abstracts from the subjective conditions of sensible intuition, it is nothing at all, and can be counted among the objects in themselves (without their relation to our intuition) neither as subsisting nor as inhering. Yet this ideality, just as little as that of space, is to be placed in comparison with the subreptions of sensations, because one presupposes there, of the appearance itself, to which these predicates inhere, that it has objective reality, which here entirely drops out, except insofar as it is merely empirical, i.e., regards the object itself merely as appearance: concerning which the above remark of the first section is to be consulted.",["Behauptungen = assertions (behaupten); Eigenschaft = property.","Realität = reality; Wirklichkeit does not occur here.","da nemlich ... anhinge: the construction \"da ... schlechthin den Dingen ... anhinge\" is kept close; \"since namely\" is literal.","Subreptionen der Empfindungen: *subreptiones* in the Latinate sense; Empfindung = sensation.","sie (\"it is merely empirical\") and die (\"which here entirely drops out\") are left with the German's open reference (reality/appearance).","Gegenstand = object throughout; Ansehung = regard."]],"krv-a-0092":["Elucidation.",["Erläuterung: glossary gives \"clarification; elucidation\"; \"elucidation\" chosen."]],"krv-a-0093":["Against this theory, which concedes empirical reality to time, but disputes the absolute and transcendental, I have heard an objection from discerning men so unanimously that I infer from it that it must naturally present itself to every reader to whom these considerations are unaccustomed. It runs thus: Alterations are actual (this is proved by the change of our own representations, even if one wished to deny all outer appearances, together with their alterations). Now alterations are possible only in time, consequently time is something actual. The answer has no difficulty. I grant the whole argument. Time is certainly something actual, namely the actual form of inner intuition. It has therefore subjective reality with regard to inner experience, i.e., I actually have the representation of time and of my determinations in it. It is therefore actually to be regarded not as object, but as the way of representing myself as object. But if I myself, or another being, could intuit me, without this condition of sensibility, then precisely the same determinations that we now represent to ourselves as alterations would yield a cognition in which the representation of time, thus also of alteration, would not occur at all. Thus its empirical reality remains as condition of all our experiences. Only absolute reality cannot be conceded to it in accordance with what was cited above. It is nothing but the form of our inner intuition.*) If one takes away from it the particular condition of our sensibility, then the concept of time also vanishes, and it does not attach to the objects themselves, but merely to the subject, which intuits it.",["Veränderung = \"alteration\"; Wechsel = \"change\" (two German words, kept apart).","wirklich / Wirkliches = actual (\"actually\" in \"Sie ist also wirklich nicht als Obiect\"): wirklich could modify the verb (\"actually regarded\") or the negation/sense (\"really not as object\"); left open.","Obiets in the source is an obvious OCR/print slip for Obiects; rendered \"object\". Object = object, Subiect = subject.","\"*)\" is the footnote marker; the footnote text is not in the passage.","dem Subiecte, welches sie anschauet: \"which intuits it\" keeps the German's open reference of welches/sie.","Erkentniß = cognition; Beantwortung = answer; Einwurf = objection."]],"krv-a-0094":["*) I can indeed say: my representations follow one another; but that means only that we are conscious of them as in a temporal sequence, i.e., according to the form of inner sense. Time is on that account not something in itself, nor a determination objectively adhering to things.",["Footnote marker \"*)\" kept as in the source.","Zeitfolge rendered \"temporal sequence\" (Zeit + Folge); Folge = sequence per glossary.","\"obiectiv\" (Latinate spelling) = \"objectively\"; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\".","Bestimmung = determination; Vorstellung = representation; Form = form; bewußt = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness)."]],"krv-a-0095":["The cause, however, on account of which this objection is made so unanimously, and indeed by those who nevertheless know of nothing evident to object against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this. They did not hope to be able to demonstrate the absolute reality of space apodictically, because idealism stands against them, according to which the actuality of outer objects is not capable of any strict proof: By contrast, that of the object of our inner sense (of myself and of my state) is clear immediately through consciousness. The former could be a mere illusion, but the latter is, in their opinion, undeniably something actual. They did not consider, however, that both, without one's being permitted to dispute their actuality as representations, nevertheless belong only to appearance, which always has two sides, the one, where the object is considered in itself, (without regard to the way of intuiting it, the constitution of which, however, for just that reason always remains problematic) the other, where the form of the intuition of this object is looked to, which must be sought not in the object in itself, but in the subject to which it appears, but which nevertheless belongs actually and necessarily to the appearance of this object.",["Realität and Wirklichkeit: \"reality\" for Realität; \"actuality\" for Wirklichkeit (also \"actual\" for wirklich), per glossary.","Gegenstand and Object/Obiect both \"object\"; here \"Obiect\" (Latinate) in \"das Obiect an sich selbst\" and \"Gegenstand(es)\" elsewhere.","Art = way (\"the way of intuiting\"); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Schein = illusion; Meinung = opinion; Zustand = state; Beweis = proof; Bewußtseyn = consciousness.","Ambiguity kept: \"dessen Beschaffenheit\" could refer to the object or to the way of intuiting it (\"dasselbe\" = the object; \"dessen\" is unresolved); \"welche\" after \"Anschauung dieses Gegenstandes\" could refer to the form or the intuition; \"derselbe\" = the object/the subject's object; likewise \"welche ... gesucht werden muß\".","\"dieser\" / \"jene\": \"the latter\"/\"the former\" for dieser/jene, i.e., inner and outer objects respectively.","\"Einwurff\" = objection; \"einleuchtend\" = evident; \"apodictisch\" kept as \"apodictically\"; \"Idealismus\" = idealism."]],"krv-a-0096":["Time and space are accordingly two sources of cognition, from which various synthetic cognitions can be drawn *a priori*, as pure mathematics in particular gives a splendid example with regard to the cognitions of space and its relations. They are, namely, both taken together pure forms of all sensible intuition, and thereby make synthetic propositions *a priori* possible. But these sources of cognition *a priori* determine for themselves, precisely thereby (that they are merely conditions of sensibility) their boundaries, namely, that they go merely to objects insofar as these are considered as appearances, but do not exhibit things in themselves. Those alone are the field of their validity, from which, if one goes out, no further objective use of them takes place. This reality of space and time, incidentally, leaves the certainty of experiential cognition untouched: for we are just as certain of it, whether these forms adhere of necessity to the things in themselves, or only to our intuition of these things. By contrast, those who assert the absolute reality of space and time, whether they assume it as subsisting, or only inhering, must be in disagreement with the principles of experience itself. For, if they resolve upon the former (which is commonly the party of the mathematical students of nature,) they must assume two eternal and infinite self-subsisting non-things, (space and time), which are there, (without there being anything actual), only in order to comprehend everything actual within themselves. If they take the second party (to which some metaphysical teachers of nature belong,) and space and time count for them as relations of appearances (next to or after one another) abstracted from experience, though confusedly represented in the separation, then they must dispute the mathematical doctrines *a priori* with regard to actual things (e.g., in space) their validity, at least their apodictic certainty, since this finds no place *a posteriori*, and the concepts *a priori* of space and time, according to this opinion, are only creatures of the imagination, whose source must actually be sought in experience, out of whose abstracted relations the imagination has made something that, to be sure, contains the general of them, but which cannot take place without the restrictions that nature has connected with them. The former gain so much that they free the field of appearances for themselves for the mathematical assertions: By contrast, they entangle themselves greatly through these very conditions, when the understanding wants to go beyond this field. The second gain, to be sure, with regard to the latter, namely that the representations of space and time do not come in their way, when they want to judge of objects not as appearances but merely in relation to the understanding; but can neither give a ground of the possibility of mathematical cognitions *a priori* (since a true and objectively valid intuition *a priori* is lacking to them), nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary agreement with those assertions. In our theory, of the true constitution of these two original forms of sensibility, both difficulties are remedied.",["Erkenntnißquellen/Erkentniß = cognition (Erkenntnis); \"Erfahrungserkentniß\" = \"experiential cognition\"; Erfahrungssätze = \"propositions of experience\".","Grenze = boundary; Gültigkeit = validity; Gebrauch = use; Bedingung = condition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Anschauung = intuition; Einbildungskraft = imagination.","Realität = reality; Wirklichkeit/wirklich = actuality/actual; Gegenstand = object, Obiect-/obiectiv = objective.","\"Ansehung\" in \"in Ansehung\" = \"with regard to\"; \"Undinge\" = \"non-things\" (Un- + Ding).","Ambiguities kept: \"Jene allein\" (those = appearances, from \"Erscheinungen\"); \"Die erstere ... Die zweite\" = the former/the second party; \"des lezteren\" (\"the latter\") unresolved referent; \"dieser Meinung nach\" = \"according to this opinion\"; \"deren Quell\" and \"derselben\" referents left open; \"das Allgemeine derselben\" = \"the general of them\".","\"Naturlehrer\" = \"teachers of nature\"; \"Naturforscher\" = \"students of nature\"; Lehre = doctrine; \"mathematischen Lehren\" = \"mathematical doctrines\".","\"subsistirend/inhärirend\" = \"subsisting/inhering\"; \"verworren\" = \"confusedly\"; \"Parthey\" = \"party\"; \"Behauptung\" = assertion (behaupten = assert).","\"sie ... bestimmen sich ... ihre Grenzen\" is awkward German (sich + dative of ihre Grenzen as object); kept as \"determine for themselves ... their boundaries\". \"darstellen\" = \"exhibit\"."]],"krv-a-0097":["That transcendental aesthetic can finally contain no more than these two elements, namely space and time, is clear from this, because all other concepts belonging to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites both pieces, presuppose something empirical. For this presupposes the perception of something movable. But in space, considered in itself, there is nothing movable: Hence the movable must be something that is found in space only through experience, thus an empirical datum. Just so transcendental aesthetic cannot count the concept of alteration among its data *a priori*: for time itself does not alter, but something that is in time. Thus the perception of some existence, and of the succession of its determinations, thus experience, is required for it.",["Veränderung = \"alteration\" (not in glossary; \"change\" is the other standard choice); verändert = alters.","Dasein = existence; Succeßion = succession (Latinate, cf. Abfolge = succession); Bestimmung = determination; Wahrnehmung = perception; Erfahrung = experience.","\"Aesthetik\" = aesthetic; \"Datum/Data\" = datum/data (Latin retained in form); \"Stücke\" = \"pieces\".","\"diese\" (\"For this presupposes\"): referent (the concept of motion) left unresolved as in German, where \"diese\" is feminine and could also be read otherwise.","\"Etwas\" capitalized in the German (\"das bewegliche Etwas seyn muß\"): rendered \"the movable must be something\"; the capitalization is not reproduced."]],"krv-a-0098":["General Remarks on the Transcendental Aesthetic.",["Heading; \"Anmerkungen\" = \"remarks\"."]],"krv-a-0099":["First it will be necessary to explain to ourselves as distinctly as possible what our opinion is with regard to the fundamental constitution of sensible cognition in general, in order to forestall all misinterpretation of it.",["deutlich = distinct; Meinung = opinion; Grundbeschaffenheit = \"fundamental constitution\"; Erkentniß = cognition; sinnlich = sensible.","\"derselben\" (\"of it\") could refer to the opinion or to the constitution/cognition; ambiguity kept."]],"krv-a-0100":["We have wanted to say, then: that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of appearance: that the things that we intuit are not in themselves what we intuit them as, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us, and that, if we remove our subject or even only the subjective constitution of the senses in general, all the constitution, all relations of objects in space and time, indeed space and time themselves would disappear, and as appearances cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What sort of state of affairs there may be with the objects in themselves and separated from all this receptivity of our sensibility remains wholly unknown to us. We know nothing but our way of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, which also need not necessarily pertain to every being, though it must to every human being. With this alone we have to do. Space and time are the pure forms of it, sensation in general the matter. Those alone can we cognize *a priori*, i.e., before all actual perception, and it is therefore called pure intuition; but this is that in our cognition which makes it be called cognition *a posteriori*, i.e., empirical intuition. Those adhere to our sensibility absolutely necessarily, of whatever kind our sensations may also be; these can be very different. If we could bring this our intuition even to the highest degree of distinctness, we would not thereby come closer to the constitution of the objects in themselves. For we would in any case cognize only our way of intuition, i.e., our sensibility completely, and this always only under the conditions originally adhering to the subject, of space and time; what the objects in themselves may be would never become known to us even through the most enlightened cognition of their appearance, which alone is given to us.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Art = way; Zustand not used: \"Bewandniß\" = \"state of affairs\" (rendered freely as no glossary entry; \"state\" avoided to keep Zustand distinct).","kennen = know (\"Wir kennen nichts\"); bekant/unbekant = known/unknown; erkennen = cognize; wahrnehmen = perceive; Wesen = being.","Gegenstand and Obiect both \"object\"; Empfindung = sensation; Materie = matter; Grad = degree; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Receptivität = receptivity.","Ambiguities kept: \"und als Erscheinungen ... existiren können\" (subject of \"können\" and of \"would disappear\" kept as in German, with \"as appearances\" attaching to the preceding list or to space and time); \"sie heisset\" (feminine, \"it is called\": referent \"Anschauung\"/\"Jene\" left open); \"diese\" = the latter (sensation), \"Jene\" = the former (space and time).","\"Wir ... vollständig erkennen\": \"completely\" could attach to \"cognize\" or to \"sensibility\"; kept in German position.","\"äufgeklärteste\" (OCR/print form) read as \"aufgeklärteste\" = \"most enlightened\"."]],"krv-a-0101":["That our whole sensibility would therefore be nothing but the confused representation of things, which contains solely that which pertains to them in themselves, but only under a heaping-together of marks and partial representations that we do not consciously set apart from one another, is a falsification of the concept of sensibility and of appearance, which makes the whole doctrine of them useless and empty. The difference of an indistinct from a distinct representation is merely logical, and does not concern the content. Without doubt the concept of right, which the healthy understanding makes use of, contains just the same thing that the subtlest speculation can develop out of it, except that in common and practical use one is not conscious of these manifold representations in this thought. For this reason one cannot say that the common concept is sensible, and contains a mere appearance, for right cannot appear at all, rather its concept lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral one) of actions, which pertains to them in themselves. On the other hand the representation of a body in intuition contains nothing at all that could pertain to an object in itself, but merely the appearance of something, and the way in which we are thereby affected, and this receptivity of our capacity for cognition is called sensibility, and remains, even if one might see through the former (the appearance) to the ground, nevertheless worlds apart from the cognition of the object in itself.",["Verworrene Vorstellung = 'confused representation'; undeutlich = 'indistinct' and deutlich = 'distinct' (glossary: deutlich = distinct).","Merkmale = 'marks' (nota); Theilvorstellungen = 'partial representations'.","Erkentnißfähigkeit rendered 'capacity for cognition' (Fähigkeit = capacity; Erkenntnis = cognition).","Beschaffenheit = 'property' here (glossary: constitution; property); the same word is rendered 'constitution' in the next passages.","Gegenstand = 'object'; Zusammenhäufung = 'heaping-together'.","'iene (die Erscheinung)' = 'the former (the appearance)': 'iene' could be read as pointing to Erscheinung as the parenthesis states; kept as in the German. 'Afficirt' = 'affected'.","Gebrauch = 'use'; Verstand = 'understanding' (intellectus); 'Grund' = 'ground'.","'blos' = 'merely'/'solely' ('lediglich' = 'solely')."]],"krv-a-0102":["The Leibnizian-Wolffian philosophy has therefore assigned to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions an entirely wrong point of view, in that it considered the difference of sensibility from the intellectual as merely logical, since it is obviously transcendental, and concerns not merely the form of distinctness or indistinctness, but the origin and the content of them, so that through the former we cognize the constitution of things in themselves not merely indistinctly, but not at all, and, as soon as we take away our subjective constitution, the represented object with the properties that sensible intuition ascribed to it is nowhere to be encountered at all, nor can it be encountered, since precisely this subjective constitution determines its form as appearance.",["Beschaffenheit = 'constitution' in 'Beschaffenheit der Dinge an sich selbst' and 'subjective constitution'; Eigenschaften = 'properties' (same English word as Beschaffenheit in the previous passage's glossary option).","Obiect = 'object' (Latinate Object; Gegenstand is also 'object', but does not occur here).","'derselben' (in 'den Ursprung und den Inhalt derselben') is ambiguous in reference (the difference/sensibility/intellectual); kept as 'of them'.","'die erstere' = 'the former' (sensibility); 'desselben' (its form) could refer to the object or to the constitution; left ambiguous.","'überall nirgend' = 'nowhere... at all'; Anschauung = 'intuition'; Erscheinung = 'appearance'."]],"krv-a-0103":["Otherwise we do distinguish among appearances that which essentially attaches to their intuition, and holds for every human sense in general, from that which pertains to it only in a contingent way, in that it is valid not in relation to sensibility in general, but only to a particular position or organization of this or that sense. And then one calls the former cognition one that represents the object in itself, but the second only the appearance of it. This difference, however, is only empirical. If one stops there, (as commonly happens,) and does not regard that empirical intuition once again (as should happen) as mere appearance, so that nothing at all that would concern any thing in itself is to be encountered in it, then our transcendental difference is lost, and we then nevertheless believe that we cognize things in themselves, although everywhere (in the world of sense), even in the deepest investigation of its objects, we have to do with nothing but appearances. Thus we will indeed call the rainbow a mere appearance in a sun-shower, but this rain the thing in itself, which is also correct, insofar as we understand the latter concept only physically, as that which in general experience, among all the different positions relative to the senses, is nevertheless determined in intuition thus and not otherwise. But if we take this empirical in general, and ask, without turning to its agreement with every human sense, whether this too represents an object in itself (not the raindrops, for these are then already, as appearances, empirical objects), then the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental, and not only are these drops mere appearances, but even their round shape, indeed even the space in which they fall, are nothing in themselves, but mere modifications, or foundations, of our sensible intuition, but the transcendental object remains unknown to us.",["Gegenstand and Obiect both = 'object'; Gegenstand appears in 'den Gegenstand an sich selbst' and 'ihrer Gegenstände'; Obiecte in 'empirische Obiecte' and 'transscendentale Obiect'.","Erkentniß = 'cognition'; erkennen = 'cognize'.","'derselben' in 'der Anschauung derselben' and 'zukommt' (its antecedent 'Erscheinungen' vs. singular sense): rendered 'their intuition' and 'it' following the German's shift of number.","'gültig' = 'valid'; 'gilt' = 'holds'; 'zufälliger Weise' = 'in a contingent way' (zufällig = contingent).","'Stellung oder Organisation' = 'position or organization'; 'Lagen' = 'positions'.","'unser transscendentale Unterschied' = 'our transcendental difference' (German grammatical slip kept).","'Grundlagen' = 'foundations' (not Grund); 'Modificationen' = 'modifications'.","'dieses' in 'ob auch dieses einen Gegenstand an sich selbst... vorstelle' = 'this', antecedent 'das Empirische'; rendered 'this' accordingly."]],"krv-a-0104":["The second important concern of our transcendental aesthetic is that it not merely acquire some favor as a plausible hypothesis, but be as certain and undoubted as can ever be demanded of a theory that is to serve as an organon. In order to make this certainty fully evident, we will choose some case in which its validity can become obvious.",["'scheinbare Hypothese' = 'plausible hypothesis'; scheinbar could also be 'apparent' (related to Schein = illusion); the German is ambiguous.","Organon left as the Greek-derived term, rendered 'organon'.","'Gewisheit' = 'certainty'; 'Gültigkeit' = 'validity' (gültig = valid); 'dessen' refers to 'diese Gewisheit' or the theory; rendered 'its'.","'Angelegenheit' = 'concern'."]],"krv-a-0105":["Posit, accordingly, that space and time were in themselves objective and conditions of the possibility of things in themselves, then it shows first: that of both, *a priori* apodictic and synthetic propositions occur in great number, chiefly of space, which we therefore want to investigate here preeminently as an example. Since the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically *a priori*, and with apodictic certainty, I ask: whence do you take such propositions, and on what does our understanding support itself in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths. There is no other way than through concepts or through intuitions; but both as such that are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter, namely empirical concepts, likewise that on which they are grounded, empirical intuition, can give no synthetic proposition except only one that is also merely empirical, i.e. a proposition of experience, hence can never contain necessity and absolute universality, of which sort is nevertheless the characteristic of all propositions of geometry. But what would be the former and only means, namely to arrive at such cognitions through mere concepts or through intuitions *a priori*, it is clear that from mere concepts no synthetic cognition at all, but merely analytic, can be obtained. Just take the proposition: that through two straight lines no space can be enclosed at all, hence no figure be possible, and try to derive it from the concept of straight lines and the number two, or also, that from three straight lines a figure is possible, and try it just so, merely from these concepts. All your effort is in vain, and you see yourselves compelled to take your refuge in intuition, as geometry also always does. You give yourselves, therefore, an object in intuition; but of what kind is this, is it a pure intuition *a priori* or an empirical one? If it were the latter, then a universally valid, still less an apodictic proposition could never come of it: for experience can never deliver such. You must therefore give your object *a priori* in intuition, and ground your synthetic proposition on this. Now if there did not lie in you a faculty of intuiting *a priori*, if this subjective condition were not, as to form, at the same time the universal condition *a priori* under which alone the object of this (outer) intuition itself is possible, if the object (the triangle) were something in itself without relation to your subject, how could you say that what lies necessarily in your subjective conditions for constructing a triangle must also necessarily pertain to the triangle in itself; for you could surely add to your concepts (of three lines) nothing new (the figure), which would therefore have to be necessarily encountered in the object, since this is given before your cognition, and not through it. If, therefore, space (and so also time) were not a mere form of your intuition, which contains conditions *a priori* under which alone things can be outer objects for you, which without these subjective conditions are nothing in themselves, then you could establish *a priori* entirely and altogether nothing synthetically about outer objects. It is therefore undoubtedly certain, and not merely possible, or even probable, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all (outer and inner) experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuition, in relation to which, therefore, all objects are mere appearances and not things given for themselves in this way, about which, for that reason, as regards the form of them, much can be said *a priori*, but never the least about the thing in itself that may lie at the ground of these appearances.",["Gegenstand and Object/Obiect are both rendered 'object'; 'Obiect' occurs in 'das Obiect dieser (äusseren) Anschauung' and 'äussere Obiecte', 'Gegenstand' elsewhere.","'Setzet ... seyen': imperative plus subjunctive, rendered 'Posit ... were'; setzen = posit.","'a priori' in 'a priori ... synthetisch ausmachen' and 'a priori in der Anschauung geben' could attach to verb or noun; German order kept. 'a priori apodictische und synthetische Sätze' is likewise ambiguous as to scope.","'vor eurer Erkentniß' = 'before your cognition'; 'vor euch' = 'for you'; 'vor sich' = 'for themselves'.","'welche ... enthält' and 'die ... an sich nichts sind' keep the German's open antecedents (form/conditions/things).","Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; 'Characteristische' is a noun, kept as 'the characteristic'. 'Sätze' = propositions; 'Wahrscheinlich' = probable; 'Mittel' = means; 'Zahl' = number.","Original spellings 'Gewisheit', 'köntet' etc. are eighteenth-century variants; no OCR emendation needed."]],"krv-a-0106":["Second Part of the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements. Transcendental Logic.",["Elementarlehre = doctrine of elements (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"krv-a-0107":["Introduction. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.",[]],"krv-a-0108":["I. On Logic in General.",["'Von der Logik überhaupt': 'überhaupt' rendered 'in general'."]],"krv-a-0109":["Our cognition arises from two basic sources of the mind, of which the first is to receive representations (the receptivity of impressions), the second the faculty to cognize an object through these representations: (spontaneity of concepts); through the former an object is given to us, through the second this is thought, in relation to that representation (as a mere determination of the mind). Intuition and concepts therefore constitute the elements of all our cognition, so that neither concepts without an intuition corresponding to them in some way, nor intuition without concepts, can yield a cognition. Both are either pure, or empirical. Empirical, if sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained therein: pure, however, if no sensation is mixed into the representation. One can call the latter the matter of sensible cognition. Hence pure intuition contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure concept solely the form of the thinking of an object in general. Only pure intuitions or concepts alone are possible *a priori*, empirical ones only *a posteriori*.",["Gemüth = mind; Vermögen = faculty; Eindruck = impression; Bestimmung = determination; Empfindung = sensation; Materie = matter.","'die leztere' (the latter) is grammatically open between 'Empfindung' and the pure representation; left as in German.","'iene Vorstellung' is singular ('that representation') though 'Vorstellungen' precedes.","'Nur allein' rendered 'Only ... alone'; 'a priori möglich' could be read with 'möglich' attaching to either 'a priori' or the predicate; kept in German order.","'Art' in 'auf einige Art' = way."]],"krv-a-0110":["If we want to call the receptivity of our mind, to receive representations, insofar as it is affected in some way, sensibility, then by contrast the faculty of producing representations themselves, or the spontaneity of cognition, is the understanding. Our nature brings it with it so that intuition can never be other than sensible, i.e. contains only the way in which we are affected by objects. By contrast, the faculty to think the object of sensible intuition is the understanding. Neither of these properties is to be preferred to the other. Without sensibility no object would be given to us, and without understanding none thought. Thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind. Hence it is just as necessary to make its concepts sensible (i.e. to add the object to them in intuition), as to make its intuitions understandable to itself (i.e. to bring them under concepts). Both faculties, or capacities, also cannot exchange their functions. The understanding is capable of intuiting nothing, and the senses of thinking nothing. Only from their uniting can cognition arise. On that account, however, one may nevertheless not mix up their share, but rather one has great cause to separate each carefully from the other, and to distinguish them. Hence we distinguish the science of the rules of sensibility in general, i.e. aesthetic, from the science of the rules of the understanding in general, i.e. logic.",["Eigenschaften = 'properties' (not Beschaffenheit, which the glossary also gives as 'property').","The text has a misplaced parenthesis ('machen,) d. i. sie ... bringen)'); read as '(d. i. sie unter Begriffe zu bringen)' and the stray bracket silently corrected. 'sich verständlich zu machen' = 'to make understandable to itself'.","'Funetionen' is an OCR slip for 'Functionen' (functions).","Vermögen = faculty (twice) and Fähigkeiten = capacities; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Inhalt = content; Wissenschaft = science.","'es' in 'so fern es ... afficirt wird' refers to 'Gemüth'; 'die Receptivität ... Vorstellungen zu empfangen' kept in German order.","'darf man ... nicht' rendered 'one may ... not'."]],"krv-a-0111":["Logic can now in turn be undertaken with a twofold aim, either as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary rules of thinking, without which no use of the understanding at all takes place, and goes therefore to this, regardless of the diversity of the objects at which it may be directed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the rules for thinking correctly about a certain kind of objects. The former one can call elementary logic, the latter, however, the *organon* of this or that science. The latter is for the most part sent ahead in the schools as propaedeutic of the sciences, although, according to the course of human reason, it is the latest thing, to which it first arrives when the science is already long finished, and needs only the final hand for its correction and perfection. For one must already know the objects to a fairly high degree if one wants to give the rules for how a science of them can be brought about.",["Absicht = aim; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstand = object; Grad = degree; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Vernunft = reason; kennen = know; Art = kind.","'die erste' / 'die leztere' / 'Jene' / 'diese': referents kept as in German (the two logics).","In 'wozu sie allererst gelangt', 'sie' could refer to reason (Vernunft) or to the logic; left ambiguous. 'ihrer' refers to 'Wissenschaft'.","'Propädevtik' = 'propaedeutic' (Greek-derived, not italicized); 'Organon' italicized as foreign.","'geht ... auf diesen': 'goes to this', 'diesen' = the use of the understanding."]],"krv-a-0112":["General logic is now either pure or applied logic. In the former we abstract from all empirical conditions under which our understanding is exercised, e.g., from the influence of the senses, from the play of the imagination, the laws of memory, the power of habit, of inclination, etc., hence also from the sources of prejudices, indeed in general from all causes from which certain cognitions may arise, or be imputed to us, because they merely concern the understanding under certain circumstances of its application, and, in order to know these, experience is required. A general, but pure logic has therefore to do with nothing but principles *a priori*, and is a canon of the understanding and of reason, but only with regard to the formal in their use, the content may be whatever it will, (empirical or transcendental.) A general logic is called applied, however, when it is directed to the rules of the use of the understanding under the subjective empirical conditions that psychology teaches us. It therefore has empirical principles, although it is general insofar as it concerns the use of the understanding without distinction of the objects. On that account it is also neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of particular sciences, but merely a *catharcticon* of the common understanding.",["Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Erkentnisse = cognitions; Bedingungen = conditions; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstände = objects (Gegenstand and Object both 'object').","'Canon', 'Organon' and 'Catharcticon' are Kant's own Greek-derived terms, kept as in the original; Catharcticon italicized, Canon and Organon were printed in roman type by Kant in the same way, left unitalicized here.","'Ansehung des Formalen ihres Gebrauchs': 'their' (ihres) could refer to understanding and reason jointly; kept as ambiguous as the German.","'Einbildung' rendered 'imagination' (glossary Einbildungskraft); 'Wissenschaften' = sciences; 'unterschoben' rendered 'imputed' (literally 'substituted'; doubtful sense preserved as is).","'Vorurtheile' = prejudices; 'empirisch oder transscendental' kept in parentheses as in the German."]],"krv-a-0113":["In general logic, therefore, the part that is to constitute the pure doctrine of reason must be entirely separated from that which constitutes applied (though still always general) logic. The former alone is properly science, although brief and dry, and as the scholastically correct presentation of an elementary doctrine of the understanding requires. In this, therefore, logicians must always have two rules before their eyes.",["Vernunftlehre = doctrine of reason (Lehre = doctrine); Wissenschaft = science.","'In dieser' (feminine) is rendered 'In this'; its antecedent (the pure part / the science) is left as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-a-0114":["1) As general logic it abstracts from all content of the cognition of the understanding, and from the difference of its objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thinking.",["Verstandeserkentniß = cognition of the understanding; 'ihrer' could refer to the cognitions or to the understanding, preserved as 'its'."]],"krv-a-0115":["2) As pure logic it has no empirical principles, hence it draws nothing (as one has sometimes persuaded oneself) from psychology, which therefore has no influence at all on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be wholly certain *a priori*.",["überredet = persuaded (Überredung = persuasion); Doctrin rendered 'doctrine'; 'Principien' = principles."]],"krv-a-0116":["What I call applied logic (contrary to the common meaning of this word, according to which it is supposed to contain certain exercises for which pure logic gives the rule) is a representation of the understanding and of the rules of its necessary use *in concreto*, namely under the contingent conditions of the subject, which can hinder or promote this use, and which are all given only empirically. It deals with attention, its hindrance and consequences, the origin of error, the state of doubt, of scruple, of conviction, etc., and to it general and pure logic is related as pure morals, which contains merely the necessary moral laws of a free will in general, is to the proper doctrine of virtue, which considers these laws under the hindrances of feelings, inclinations and passions, to which human beings are more or less subjected, and which can never furnish a true and demonstrated science, because it, just as much as that applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.",["zufälligen = contingent; Vorstellung = representation; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Wissenschaft = science; Gesetze = laws; Gefühle = feelings.","'welche niemals ... kan': the relative 'welche' (feminine) may refer to the doctrine of virtue (Tugendlehre) or, less naturally, elsewhere; kept as in the German.","'die Hindernisse' rendered 'hindrances' (Hinderniß/Hindernissen).","'Scrupel' rendered 'scruple'; 'sittlich' = moral; 'Moral' = morals."]],"krv-a-0117":["II. On Transcendental Logic.",[]],"krv-a-0118":["General logic abstracts, as we have shown, from all content of cognition, i.e., from all relation of it to the object, and considers only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to one another, i.e., the form of thinking in general. Since there are, however, pure as well as empirical intuitions (as the transcendental aesthetic shows), a difference might well also be found between pure and empirical thinking of objects. In this case there would be a logic in which one did not abstract from all content of cognition; for that one which contained merely the rules of the pure thinking of an object would exclude all those cognitions that were of empirical content. It would also concern the origin of our cognitions of objects, insofar as it cannot be ascribed to the objects; whereas general logic has nothing to do with this origin of cognition, but considers representations, whether they be given originally *a priori* in ourselves, or only empirically, merely according to the laws by which the understanding uses them in relation to one another when it thinks, and thus deals only with the form of the understanding that can be procured for the representations, wherever else they may have arisen.",["Obiect = object (Gegenstand also 'object'); Gegenstände = objects; Anschauungen = intuitions.","'so fern er nicht den Gegenständen zugeschrieben werden kan': 'er' (the origin) kept; the German could also be read with 'er' as the thinking; preserved.","'woher sie auch sonst entsprungen seyn mögen': 'sie' = the representations; 'sonst' rendered 'else'."]],"krv-a-0119":["And here I make a remark whose influence extends to all the following considerations, and which one must well keep before one's eyes, namely: that not every cognition *a priori*, but only that through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or concepts) are applied, or are possible, merely *a priori*, must be called transcendental (i.e., the possibility of cognition or the use of it *a priori*). Hence neither space nor any geometrical determination of it *a priori* is a transcendental representation, but only the cognition that these representations are not of empirical origin at all, and the possibility how they can nevertheless relate themselves *a priori* to objects of experience, can be called transcendental. Likewise the use of space of objects in general would also be transcendental: but if it is restricted merely to objects of the senses, it is called empirical. The difference of the transcendental and the empirical therefore belongs only to the critique of cognitions, and does not concern their relation to their object.",["Betrachtungen = considerations; Bestimmung = determination; Erfahrung = experience; Möglichkeit = possibility.","'a priori' attaches ambiguously in 'lediglich a priori angewandt werden, oder möglich seyn' (to 'applied' and/or 'possible'), and in 'der Gebrauch derselben a priori' and 'geometrische Bestimmung desselben a priori'; kept ambiguous in the same positions.","'der Gebrauch des Raumes von Gegenständen überhaupt': 'of objects in general' could attach to 'use' or to 'space'; preserved.","'Erkentniß' is cognition throughout, 'Critik' = critique."]],"krv-a-0120":["In the expectation, therefore, that there may perhaps be concepts that can relate to objects *a priori*, not as pure or sensible intuitions, but merely as acts of pure thinking, which are therefore concepts, but of neither empirical nor aesthetic origin, we make for ourselves in advance the idea of a science of the pure understanding and of cognition of reason, through which we think objects wholly *a priori*. Such a science, which would determine the origin, the domain and the objective validity of such cognitions, would have to be called transcendental logic, because it has to do merely with the laws of the understanding and of reason, but solely insofar as it is related to objects *a priori*, and not, as general logic, to the empirical as well as the pure cognitions of reason without distinction.",["Begriffe = concepts; Umfang = domain; Gültigkeit = validity; Idee = idea; Gesetze = laws; sinnlich = sensible; 'Urprungs' (sic) read as Ursprungs, silently corrected.","'Verstandes und Vernunfterkentnisses' (OCR/orig. 'Vernunfterkentnisses') rendered 'of the pure understanding and of cognition of reason'; the 'pure' could govern both nouns or only the first, left ambiguous.","'sie' in 'auf Gegenstände a priori bezogen wird': refers to 'laws' or to 'logic', ambiguity preserved ('it')."]],"krv-a-0121":["III. On the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.",["Eintheilung = division."]],"krv-a-0122":["The old and famous question with which one thought to drive logicians into a corner, and sought to bring them to the point where they must either let themselves be caught in a wretched *diallelus*, or confess their ignorance, hence the vanity of their whole art, is this: What is truth? The nominal definition of truth, that it is namely the agreement of cognition with its object, is here granted, and presupposed; but one demands to know what the general and certain criterion of the truth of any cognition is.",["'Dialele' (as printed) rendered 'diallelus', i.e., Latin form of the circle in proof; the German spelling is Kant's own, so reading retained as doubtful transcription of 'Diallele'.","Nahmenerklärung = nominal definition (Erklärung = definition); Uebereinstimmung = agreement; Gegenstand = object; Criterium = criterion; Wahrheit = truth.","'Unwissenheit' = ignorance."]],"krv-a-0123":["It is already a great and necessary proof of prudence or insight to know what one should reasonably ask. For if the question is absurd in itself, and demands unnecessary answers, it has, apart from the embarrassment of him who raises it, sometimes also the disadvantage of seducing the incautious listener to absurd answers, and of giving the laughable sight that one (as the ancients said) milks the he-goat, the other holds a sieve underneath.",["'Veweis' (as printed) silently corrected to Beweis = proof.","'Einsicht' = insight; 'Klugheit' = prudence; 'an sich' = in itself.","'vernünftiger Weise' rendered 'reasonably' (vernünftig, from Vernunft)."]],"krv-a-0124":["If truth consists in the correspondence of a cognition with its object, then this object must thereby be distinguished from others; for a cognition is false if it does not correspond with the object to which it is related, even if it contains something that might well hold of other objects. Now a general criterion of truth would be that which was valid of all cognitions, without distinction of their objects. But it is clear that, since in the case of such a criterion one abstracts from all content of the cognition (relation to its object), and truth concerns precisely this content, it is wholly impossible and absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of the cognitions, and that therefore a sufficient and yet at the same time general sign of truth cannot possibly be given. Since we have already above called the content of a cognition the matter of it, one will have to say: no general sign can be demanded of the truth of cognition as to the matter, because it is self-contradictory in itself.",["Erkentniß/Erkentnis = cognition throughout; Gegenstand and Object/Obiect are both rendered 'object'.","Criterium = criterion; Merkmal = mark; Kennzeichen = sign (not in glossary; 'sign' chosen and kept consistent).","'hinreichendes' = sufficient (glossary gives zureichend = sufficient; hinreichend rendered the same).","'Materie' = matter; 'Inhalt' = content; 'der Materie nach' = 'as to the matter'.","'es ist in sich selbst widersprechend': 'it' is ambiguous in the German (grammatically 'Kennzeichen', possibly the demand); left as 'it'.","'welches ... gültig wäre': 'valid of' for 'gelten von'."]],"krv-a-0125":["But as for cognition as to the mere form (setting aside all content), it is just as clear that a logic, insofar as it presents the general and necessary rules of the understanding, must set forth criteria of truth in precisely these rules. For, what contradicts these is false, because the understanding thereby conflicts with its general rules of thinking, hence with itself. But these criteria concern only the form of truth, i.e., of thinking in general, and are to that extent entirely correct, but not sufficient. For although a cognition might be entirely in conformity with the logical form, i.e., not contradict itself, it can nevertheless still always contradict the object. Thus the merely logical criterion of truth, namely the correspondence of a cognition with the general and formal laws of the understanding and of reason, is indeed the *conditio sine qua non*, hence the negative condition of all truth: but further than this logic cannot go, and the error that concerns not the form but the content logic cannot discover by any touchstone.",["'Das Erkentniß' (neuter, Academy/original reading) rendered 'cognition'.","'hinreichend' = sufficient; 'Probierstein' = touchstone.","Gesetz = law, Regel = rule; 'trift' = concerns (the error that 'hits/concerns' the content).","'mit Beyseitesetzung' = 'setting aside'."]],"krv-a-0126":["General logic now resolves the whole formal business of the understanding and of reason into its elements, and presents them as principles of all logical assessment of our cognition. This part of logic can therefore be called analytic, and is for just that reason the, at least negative, touchstone of truth, in that one must first of all test and estimate all cognition, as to its form, by these rules, before one investigates it itself as to its content, in order to settle whether it contains positive truth with regard to the object. But because the mere form of cognition, however much it may agree with logical laws, is still far from sufficing to settle material (objective) truth for the cognition on that account, no one can venture with logic alone to judge about objects and to assert anything, without having first obtained well-grounded information about them outside of logic, in order afterwards merely to attempt the use and the connection of them in a coherent whole according to logical laws, or still better, merely to test them according to these. Nevertheless there lies something so seductive in the possession of such a seemingly artful skill, of giving all our cognitions the form of the understanding, even though with regard to their content one may still be very empty and poor, that that general logic, which is merely a canon for judging, has been used as it were like an organon for the actual production of at least the semblance of objective assertions, and thus in fact has been misused thereby. General logic now, as putative organon, is called dialectic.",["Beurtheilung = assessment ('Beurteilung' = judgment in glossary; 'assessment' chosen for logical Beurtheilung, 'judging' for 'zur Beurtheilung'); urtheilen = judge.","Verknüpfung = connection; 'Benutzung' = use (Gebrauch also 'use').","'scheinbarer Kunst' = 'seemingly artful skill': scheinbar may mean 'apparent/seeming'; Kunst rendered 'skill' (alternatively 'art'); Blendwerk = semblance.","'Erkundigung' = information (Kenntnis also 'information'); 'dem Blendwerk ... gebraucht' syntax kept as in the German.","Canon, Organon kept as in original; 'Analytik' = analytic."]],"krv-a-0127":["However different the meaning may be in which the ancients made use of this term for a science or art, one can nevertheless safely gather from its actual use that with them it was nothing other than the logic of illusion. A sophistical art, of giving to one's ignorance, indeed even to one's intentional semblances, the varnish of truth, in that one imitated the method of thoroughness which logic in general prescribes, and used its topic for the embellishment of every empty pretense. Now one can note as a safe and useful warning: that general logic, considered as organon, is always a logic of illusion, i.e., is dialectical. For since it teaches us nothing at all about the content of cognition, but only the formal conditions of agreement with the understanding, which are moreover entirely indifferent with regard to objects; the demand to use it as an instrument (organon), in order to extend and enlarge one's knowledge, at least by pretense, must amount to nothing but garrulousness, of asserting with some illusion everything one wishes, or also of contesting it at pleasure.",["Schein = illusion; Blendwerk = semblance; Kentnisse = knowledge (Kenntnis), plural left as 'knowledge'.","Wissenschaft = science; Benennung = term; Bedingung = condition.","'Eine sophistische Kunst ... nachahmete' is a sentence without a finite main verb in the German; kept as a fragment.","'seyn' (plural subjunctive-like 'welche ... gleichgültig seyn') rendered 'are'; 'Topik' = topic.","'Vorgeben/Vorgeben' = pretense."]],"krv-a-0128":["Such instruction is in no way befitting to the dignity of philosophy. For that reason one has preferred to count this term dialectic as a critique of dialectical illusion among logic, and as such we also want it to be understood here.",["'Unterweisung' = instruction (Belehrung = teaching in glossary; different word).","'der Logik beygezählt': 'among logic' is literal; the dative construction is retained without smoothing ('counted to logic' is the alternative).","Critik = critique; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit)."]],"krv-a-0129":["IV. On the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.",["Eintheilung = division; 'transsc.' abbreviation expanded as 'Transcendental'; transscendental = transcendental."]],"krv-a-0130":["In a transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as above in the transcendental aesthetic sensibility) and lift out of our cognition merely that part of thinking which has its origin solely in the understanding. The use of this pure cognition, however, rests on this as its condition: that objects be given to us in intuition, to which it can be applied. For without intuition all our cognition lacks objects, and it then remains entirely empty. The part of transcendental logic, therefore, that presents the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and the principles without which no object can be thought at all, is the transcendental analytic, and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it without at the same time losing all content, i.e., all relation to any object, hence all truth. But because it is very enticing and seductive to make use of these pure cognitions of the understanding and principles alone, and even beyond the bounds of experience, which yet can alone give us the matter (objects) to which those pure concepts of the understanding can be applied: the understanding thus falls into danger of making, through empty rationalizations, a material use of the mere formal principles of the pure understanding, and of judging without distinction about objects that are not given to us, indeed perhaps cannot be given in any way. Since it should therefore properly be only a canon of the assessment of the empirical use, it is misused if one lets it count as the organon of a general and unrestricted use, and ventures with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, to assert, and to decide about objects in general. Thus the use of the pure understanding would then be dialectical. The second part of transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of this dialectical illusion, and is called transcendental dialectic, not as an art of dogmatically arousing such illusion (an unfortunately very common art of manifold metaphysical juggleries) but as a critique of the understanding and of reason with regard to their hyperphysical use, in order to uncover the false illusion of their groundless presumptions, and to reduce their claims to discovery and extension, which they believe to attain merely through transcendental principles, to the mere assessment and safeguarding of the pure understanding against sophistical semblances.",["Grundsatz = principle (Principium also 'principle'); Gegenstand and Obiect both 'object'.","Grenze = bound(ary), here 'bounds'; Vernünfteleyen = rationalizations (vernünfteln = rationalize).","'Da sie ... sollte' / 'sie' refers back ambiguously (to the analytic, or to pure cognition/principles); left as 'it'.","'dogmatisch zu erregen' kept ambiguous as to what 'dogmatically' modifies; here the verb.","'zu erreichen vermeinet' = 'believe to attain'; 'Beurtheilung' = assessment; 'Gauckelwerke' = juggleries.","'ihrer Bedingung' with 'als': 'as its condition' as in the German."]],"krv-a-0131":["Transcendental Logic, First Division. The Transcendental Analytic.",["Abtheilung = division (same word as Eintheilung/Teilung in the glossary group, rendered 'division')."]],"krv-a-0132":["This analytic is the dissection of our entire cognition a priori into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. What matters here are the following points. 1. That the concepts be pure and not empirical concepts. 2. That they belong not to intuition and to sensibility, but to thinking and understanding. 3. That they be elementary concepts and be well distinguished from the derived ones, or those composed out of them. 4. That their table be complete, and that they entirely fill up the whole field of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be assumed with reliability from an estimate of an aggregate brought about merely through attempts; hence it is possible only by means of an idea of the whole of the cognition of the understanding a priori and the division, determined from it, of the concepts which constitute it, thus only through their interconnection in a system. The pure understanding separates itself entirely not only from everything empirical, but even from all sensibility. It is therefore a unity subsisting for itself, sufficient to itself, and not to be increased by any additions coming from outside. Hence the sum total of its cognition will constitute a system to be comprehended and determined under one idea, the completeness and articulation of which can at the same time furnish a touchstone of the correctness and genuineness of all pieces of cognition that fit into it. This whole part of the transcend. logic, however, consists of two books, of which the one contains the concepts, the other the principles of the pure understanding.",["Zergliederung = dissection; Erkentniß(stück) = cognition (piece of cognition); Stücke (\"points\") is the same word as in \"Erkentnißstücke\".","Wissenschaft = science; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Inbegriff = sum total; Abtheilung = division (cf. Einteilung); Grundsätze = principles.","Ambiguity kept: in \"welche sie ausmachen\" and \"ihren Zusammenhang\" the feminine/plural \"sie\"/\"ihren\" can refer to the concepts or to the science/cognition; \"constitute it\" is one possible reading, \"constitute them\" another.","\"Versuche\" rendered \"attempts\" (the German also means experiments). A missing period after \"möglich\" in the OCR was supplied silently. \"transscend.\" is the source's abbreviation."]],"krv-a-0133":["First Book of the Transcendental Analytic. The Analytic of Concepts.",[]],"krv-a-0134":["By the analytic of concepts I understand not the analysis of them, or the usual procedure in philosophical investigations, of dissecting concepts that offer themselves according to their content and bringing them to distinctness, but rather the as yet little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself, in order to explore the possibility of concepts a priori by seeking them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analyzing the pure use of it in general; for this is the proper business of a transcendental philosophy; the rest is the logical treatment of concepts in philosophy in general. We shall therefore pursue the pure concepts to their first germs and predispositions in the human understanding, in which they lie prepared, until they are finally developed on the occasion of experience and, freed by the very same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, are presented in their purity.",["Analysis (Latinate, kept in the text) is distinguished from Zergliederung = dissection; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Verstandesvermögen = faculty of understanding.","\"dessen\" (\"of it\") could refer to the understanding or to the faculty; kept as \"of it\".","Anlagen = predispositions; Keime = germs; Gebrauch = use; Bedingungen = conditions."]],"krv-a-0135":["First Chapter of the Analytic of Concepts. On the Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["Hauptstück = chapter; Leitfaden = clue."]],"krv-a-0136":["If one sets a faculty of cognition into play, then, according to the manifold occasions, various concepts come forth that make this faculty knowable and can be collected in a more or less extensive essay, according as the observation of them has been undertaken for a longer time or with greater acuteness. Where this investigation will be completed can never be determined with certainty according to this, as it were mechanical procedure. Also the concepts that one finds only in this way on occasion are discovered in no order and systematic unity, but in the end are only paired according to similarities and, according to the magnitude of their content, from the simple ones on to the more composite, set in series, which are brought about as anything but systematic, although methodical in a certain way.",["Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Beobachtung = observation; Inhalt = content; Größe = magnitude; einfach = simple.","The final relative clause (\"die nichts weniger als systematisch ... zu Stande gebracht werden\") is kept as a single clause; its antecedent (the series) is not made explicit."]],"krv-a-0137":["Transcendental philosophy has the advantage, but also the obligation, of seeking out its concepts according to a principle; because they spring from the understanding, as absolute unity, pure and unmixed, and therefore must themselves cohere among themselves according to a concept or idea. Such an interconnection, however, provides a rule, according to which each pure concept of the understanding can be assigned its place and all of them together their completeness a priori, all of which would otherwise depend on whim, or on chance.",["Princip rendered \"principle\"; the same English word also renders Grundsatz (see krv-a-0132).","Zusammenhang = interconnection; \"zusammenhängen\" rendered \"cohere\"."]],"krv-a-0138":["First Section of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the Logical Use of the Understanding in General.",["Leitfaden = clue; Gebrauch = use."]],"krv-a-0139":["The understanding was above defined merely negatively: through a non-sensible faculty of cognition. Now, independently of sensibility, we cannot partake of any intuition. Thus the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But apart from intuition there is no other way to cognize than through concepts. Thus the cognition of every understanding, at least of the human, is a cognition through concepts, not intuitive but discursive. All intuitions, as sensible, rest on affections, the concepts thus on functions. But by function I understand the unity of the action of ordering different representations under a common one. Concepts are thus grounded on the spontaneity of thinking, as sensible intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Of these concepts the understanding can now make no other use than that it judges by means of them. Since no representation pertains immediately to the object except intuition alone, a concept is never related immediately to an object, but to some other representation of it (be it intuition or itself already a concept). The judgment is thus the mediate cognition of an object, hence the representation of a representation of it. In every judgment there is a concept that holds for many, and among this many also comprehends a given representation, which latter is then related immediately to the object. Thus, e.g., in the judgment: all bodies are divisible, the concept of the divisible is related to various other concepts; among these, however, it is here particularly related to the concept of body; this, however, to certain appearances that come before us. Thus these objects are mediately represented through the concept of divisibility. All judgments are accordingly functions of unity among our representations, since namely instead of an immediate representation a higher one, which comprehends this and several others under itself, is used for the cognition of the object, and many possible cognitions are thereby drawn together into one. But we can trace all actions of the understanding back to judgments, so that the understanding in general can be represented as a faculty for judging. For it is, according to the above, a faculty for thinking. Thinking is cognition through concepts. Concepts, however, relate, as predicates of possible judgments, to some representation of a still undetermined object. Thus the concept of body signifies something, e.g., metal, that can be cognized through that concept. It is thus a concept only because other representations are contained under it by means of which it can be related to objects. It is thus the predicate for a possible judgment, e.g., every metal is a body. The functions of the understanding can therefore all be found if one can present the functions of unity in judgments completely. That this, however, can very well be accomplished, the following section will place before the eyes.",["\"gesunden\" in the OCR read as \"gefunden\" (\"found\").","erklärt = defined (the glossary also allows \"explained\"); erkennen = cognize; Erkenntnis = cognition; Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object; Urteil = judgment; urteilen = judge.","begreifen/\"begreift\" = comprehend(s); Erscheinungen = appearances; bedeutet = signifies; Eindrücke = impressions; unbestimmt = undetermined.","Ambiguity kept: \"vor viele gilt\" (\"holds for many\"; vor = für); \"Cörper\" rendered \"body\"; \"des Theilbaren\" = \"of the divisible\".","\"der Erkentniß eines ieden, wenigstens des menschlichen Verstandes\": \"every understanding, at least of the human\" keeps the elliptical construction."]],"krv-a-0140":["Second Section of the Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgments.",[]],"krv-a-0141":["If we abstract from all content of a judgment in general, and attend only to the mere form of the understanding in it, we find that the function of thinking in it can be brought under four titles, each of which contains three moments under itself. They can fitly be presented in the following table.",["Inhalt = content; Form = form; Momente = moments (Latinate word kept)."]],"krv-a-0142":["1. Quantity of Judgments",[]],"krv-a-0143":["Universal",[]],"krv-a-0144":["Particular",["Besondere = particular (glossary: besonder = particular; special)."]],"krv-a-0145":["Singular",[]],"krv-a-0146":["2. Quality",[]],"krv-a-0147":["Affirmative",["Beiahende from bejahen = affirm."]],"krv-a-0148":["Negative",[]],"krv-a-0149":["Infinite",[]],"krv-a-0150":["3. Relation",[]],"krv-a-0151":["Categorical",[]],"krv-a-0152":["Hypothetical",[]],"krv-a-0153":["Disjunctive",[]],"krv-a-0154":["4. Modality.",[]],"krv-a-0155":["Problematic",[]],"krv-a-0156":["Assertoric",[]],"krv-a-0157":["Apodictic.",[]],"krv-a-0158":["Since this division seems to deviate in some, although not essential, points from the customary technique of the logicians, the following precautions against the apprehended misunderstanding will not be unnecessary.",["Eintheilung = division; Stücke = points; Technik = technique; Verwahrungen = precautions (literally \"safeguardings\")."]],"krv-a-0159":["The logicians rightly say that in the use of judgments in inferences of reason one can treat singular judgments like universal ones. For precisely because they have no domain at all, their predicate cannot be drawn merely to some of that which is contained under the concept of the subject, and be excepted from some. It therefore holds of that concept without exception, just as if it were a generally valid concept, which had a domain, of whose entire meaning the predicate held. If, by contrast, we compare a singular judgment with a generally valid one, merely as cognition, according to magnitude, then it is related to the latter as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different from it. Thus, if I estimate a singular judgment (*iudicium singulare*) not merely according to its inner validity, but also, as cognition in general, according to the magnitude that it has in comparison with other cognitions, then it is certainly different from generally valid judgments (*iudicia communia*), and deserves a special place in a complete table of the moments of thinking in general (although admittedly not in the logic restricted merely to the use of judgments among one another).",["Umfang = domain; Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; Größe = magnitude; Bedeutung = meaning; besondere = special (glossary 'besonder').","gemeingültig rendered 'generally valid' (gemein + gültig); 'allgemein' rendered 'universal'; the Latin *communia* is kept as printed.","schätzen has no glossary entry; rendered 'estimate'. Gültigkeit = validity.","'Vernunftschlüssen' = inferences of reason (glossary gives 'syllogism; inference of reason'); the second sense is chosen here for the plural.","Ambiguity kept: 'von einigem aber ausgenommen werden' has no stated subject; 'das Prädicat derselben' — 'derselben' refers to the singular judgments (the plural)."]],"krv-a-0160":["Likewise, in a transcendental logic infinite judgments must still be distinguished from affirmative ones, even though in general logic they are rightly counted with the latter and make up no special member of the division. For the latter abstracts from all content of the predicate (even though it is negating) and looks only to whether it is attributed to the subject or opposed to it. The former, however, considers the judgment also according to the worth or content of this logical affirmation by means of a merely negating predicate, and what gain this procures with regard to the entire cognition. Had I said of the soul, it is not mortal, I would at least have averted an error by a negative judgment. Now through the proposition: the soul is not mortal, I have indeed affirmed, as to the logical form, by positing the soul in the unlimited domain of non-dying beings. Since now the mortal contains one part of the entire domain of possible beings, but the non-mortal the other, nothing else is said by my proposition than that the soul is one of the infinite multitude of things that remain over if I take away the mortal altogether. Thereby, however, the infinite sphere of everything possible is limited only so far that the mortal is separated from it, and the soul is posited in the remaining space of its domain. But this space still always remains infinite with this exception, and more parts of it can still be taken away, without the concept of the soul thereby growing in the least, and being determined affirmatively. These infinite judgments, therefore, with regard to the logical domain, are really merely limiting with regard to the content of cognition in general, and to this extent they must not be passed over in the transcendental table of all moments of thinking in judgments, because the function of the understanding exercised in this may perhaps be important in the field of its pure cognition a priori.",["Jene/diese: 'Diese' (the general logic) and 'Jene' (the transcendental logic) are kept as 'the latter' and 'the former' following the German pronouns' reference; the antecedents are left as ambiguous as in the German.","beiahend = affirmative/affirmatively (bejahen = affirm); verneinend = negating; Verneinung kept distinct from 'negative' in 'verneinendes Urtheil' rendered 'negative judgment'.","Erkentniß = cognition; Erkenntniß a priori kept in Latin as printed. Theil = part; Menge = multitude; Raum = space; Sphäre = sphere; Umfang = domain.","wirklich (twice) rendered 'really' and 'indeed' (adverbial); beschränkend/beschränkt = limiting/limited (Schranke = limitation).","Satz = proposition (in 'Satz: die Seele ist nicht sterblich'); Wesen = being; Ding = thing; Verstand = understanding; Function = function."]],"krv-a-0161":["All relations of thinking in judgments are those a) of the predicate to the subject, b) of the ground to the sequel, c) of the divided cognition and the collected members of the division to one another. In the first way of judgments only two concepts, in the second two judgments, in the third several judgments are considered in relation to one another. The hypothetical proposition: if a perfect justice is there, then the persistently evil are punished, contains properly the relation of two propositions: There is a perfect justice there, and the persistently evil are punished. Whether both of these propositions are true in themselves remains here undecided. It is only the consequence that is thought through this judgment. Finally the disjunctive judgment contains a relation of two, or of several propositions to one another, but not of succession, but of logical opposition, insofar as the sphere of the one excludes that of the other, but yet at the same time of community, insofar as together they fill up the sphere of the cognition proper, thus a relation of the parts of the sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a supplement of the sphere of the other to the whole sum total of the divided cognition, e.g. the world is there either through a blind contingency, or through inner necessity, or through an outer cause. Each of these propositions takes up a part of the sphere of possible cognition about the existence of a world in general, all together the whole sphere. To take the cognition away from one of these spheres means to posit it in one of the remaining ones, and conversely, to posit it in one sphere means to take it away from the remaining ones. There is therefore in a disjunctive judgment a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude one another, but thereby yet determine the true cognition in the whole, in that taken together they make up the entire content of a single given cognition. And this is also all that I find it necessary to remark here for the sake of what follows.",["Art = way (in 'In der ersteren Art der Urtheile'); rendered 'way'. Verhältniß = relation; Folge = sequel; Abfolge = succession; Gemeinschaft = community; Inbegriff = sum total; Daseyn = existence.","Grund = ground; Folge = sequel (not 'consequence'); Consequenz (Latinate, printed in German) = 'consequence', distinct from Folgerung (not present here).","Zufall = contingency (cf. zufällig = contingent); Nothwendigkeit = necessity. 'Sphäre' kept as 'sphere'; 'Ergänzungsstück' = supplement.","'Erkentniß aus einer dieser Sphären wegnehmen' — the object 'das Erkentniß' kept; 'sie' (twice) left as in German.","Ambiguity kept: 'Es ist eine vollkommene Gerechtigkeit da' rendered 'There is a perfect justice there' (da = there) to keep the German's 'da'; the first occurrence is rendered 'is there'.","'der beharrlich böse' is a substantivized adjective rendered 'the persistently evil' (number left open); 'bestraft' = punished."]],"krv-a-0162":["The modality of judgments is a quite special function of them, which has this distinguishing feature, that it contributes nothing to the content of the judgment, (for besides magnitude, quality and relation there is nothing more that would make up the content of a judgment) but concerns only the worth of the copula in relation to thinking in general. Problematic judgments are those where one assumes affirming or denying as merely possible (optional). Assertoric, where it is considered as actual (true). Apodictic, in which one regards it as necessary*). Thus the two judgments whose relation makes up the hypothetical judgment (antec. and consequ.), likewise those in whose interaction the disjunctive consists (members of the division), are altogether only problematic. In the above example the proposition: there is a perfect justice there, is not said assertorically, but is only thought as an optional judgment, which it is possible for someone to assume, and only the consequence is assertoric. Hence such judgments can also be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of the cognition of truth. Thus the judgment: the world is there through blind contingency, is in the disjunctive judgment of only problematic significance, namely that someone might assume this proposition perhaps for a moment, and yet it serves (like the marking out of the false way, among the number of all those that one can take) to find the true one. The problematic proposition is therefore that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective), i.e. a free choice to let such a proposition hold, a merely arbitrary taking up of it into the understanding. The assertoric says of logical actuality or truth, as for instance in a hypothetical inference of reason the antecedent occurs problematically in the major premise, assertorically in the minor premise, and indicates that the proposition is already combined with the understanding according to its laws; the apodictic proposition thinks the assertoric as determined by these very laws of the understanding, and therefore as asserting a priori, and in such a way expresses logical necessity. Since now here everything is incorporated into the understanding by degrees, so that one first judges something problematically, then also perhaps assumes it assertorically as true, finally asserts it as inseparably combined with the understanding, i.e. as necessary and apodictic, one can also call these three functions of modality so many moments of thinking in general.",["The asterisk after 'ansieht' marks a footnote whose text is not in the passage supplied; it is carried over as printed.","Wirklichkeit = actuality (glossary 'Realität / Wirklichkeit'); wirklich = actual. Wechselwirkung = interaction; Bedeutung = significance (glossary also 'meaning').","Vernunftschluß = inference of reason (glossary also 'syllogism'); Obersatz / Untersatz = major premise / minor premise; behaupten = assert; bestimmen = determine; verbinden = combine (Verbindung = combination).","Ambiguity kept: 'Der assertorische sagt von logischer Wirklichkeit' — 'sagt von' rendered 'says of'; 'a priori behauptend' leaves open whether 'a priori' attaches to 'behauptend' or to 'bestimt'.","'ausdruckt' (OCR/orthography) = expresses; 'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'beliebig' rendered 'optional' and 'beliebiges' 'optional'; 'willkührlich' = arbitrary; 'Aufnehmung' = taking up."]],"krv-a-0163":["*) As if thinking in the first case were a function of the understanding, in the second of the power of judgment, in the third of reason. A remark that first awaits its elucidation in the sequel.",["Aufklärung rendered \"elucidation\"; this is not the glossary's Erläuterung (also \"elucidation\"/\"clarification\"). In der Folge = \"in the sequel\".","Verstand = understanding; Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Vernunft = reason; Function = \"function\"."]],"krv-a-0164":["Third Section of the Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the pure concepts of the understanding or categories.",["Leitfaden = \"clue\"; Entdeckung = \"discovery\". Categorien = \"categories\"."]],"krv-a-0165":["General logic abstracts, as has already been said several times, from all content of cognition, and expects that representations will be given to it elsewhere, from wherever it may be, in order first to transform these into concepts, which proceeds analytically. Transcendental logic, on the other hand, has lying before it a manifold of sensibility a priori, which transcendental aesthetic offers to it, in order to give a material for the pure concepts of the understanding, without which it would be without any content, hence entirely empty. Space and time now contain a manifold of pure intuition a priori, but nevertheless belong to the conditions of the receptivity of our mind, under which alone it can receive representations of objects, which hence must also always affect the concept of them. Yet the spontaneity of our thinking requires that this manifold first be gone through, taken up, and combined in a certain way, in order to make a cognition out of it. This action I call synthesis.",["Gemüth = \"mind\"; Mannigfaltiges = \"manifold\"; Stoff = \"material\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; Handlung = \"action\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\".","afficiren = \"affect\"; the antecedent of \"die\" (\"which hence must also...\") and of \"derselben\" is left as ambiguous as in the German.","*a priori* is italicized as a Latin expression."]],"krv-a-0166":["By synthesis in the most general meaning, however, I understand the action of adding different representations to one another, and of comprehending their manifoldness in one cognition. Such a synthesis is pure if the manifold is given not empirically but a priori (like that in space and time.). Before all analysis of our representations, these must first be given, and no concepts as to content can arise analytically. The synthesis of a manifold, however (whether it be given empirically or a priori), first brings forth a cognition, which, to be sure, may initially still be raw and confused, and thus stands in need of analysis; yet synthesis is that which really collects the elements for cognitions, and unites them into a certain content; it is therefore the first thing to which we have to pay attention, if we wish to judge about the first origin of our cognition.",["Bedeutung = \"meaning\"; begreifen = \"comprehend\"; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\" (distinct from Mannigfaltiges, \"manifold\").","The punctuation \"space and time.)\" follows the original.","eigentlich = \"really\"; urtheilen = \"judge\"; Ursprung = \"origin\"."]],"krv-a-0167":["Synthesis in general is, as we shall see in the future, the mere effect of the imagination, of a blind, though indispensable function of the soul, without which we would have no cognition at all, but of which we are only seldom even once conscious. Yet to bring this synthesis to concepts, that is a function that pertains to the understanding, and by which it first provides cognition for us in the proper meaning.",["Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Seele = \"soul\"; bewust = \"conscious\".","allererst = \"first\" (emphatic); eigentlicher Bedeutung = \"proper meaning\"."]],"krv-a-0168":["Pure synthesis, represented generally, now gives the pure concept of the understanding. By this synthesis, however, I understand that which rests on a ground of synthetic unity a priori: thus our counting (it is more noticeable especially with larger numbers) is a synthesis according to concepts, because it takes place according to a common ground of unity (e.g., of the decadic). Under this concept, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.",["Grund = \"ground\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; Zahlen = \"numbers\"; nothwendig = \"necessary\".","Decadik rendered \"the decadic\" (literal; the noun is left unsupplied). The antecedent of \"dieser Synthesis\"/\"dieienige\" is preserved as in the German."]],"krv-a-0169":["Analytically, different representations are brought under a concept (a business with which general logic deals). But not to bring the representations, but the pure synthesis of the representations to concepts, is what transc. logic teaches. The first thing that must be given to us for the purpose of the cognition of all objects a priori is the manifold of pure intuition; the synthesis of this manifold by the imagination is the second, but still gives no cognition. The concepts that give unity to this pure synthesis, and consist merely in the representation of this necessary synthetic unity, do the third thing toward the cognition of an object that occurs, and rest on the understanding.",["\"transsc.\" is the original's abbreviation; rendered \"transc.\".","Gegenstand = \"object\" throughout; vorkommenden = \"that occurs\" (literal sense \"comes before\").","Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Behuf = \"purpose\"."]],"krv-a-0170":["The same function that gives unity to the different representations in a judgment also gives unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition, which, expressed generally, is called the pure concept of the understanding. The same understanding, therefore, and indeed through precisely the same actions by which it brought about in concepts, by means of analytic unity, the logical form of a judgment, also brings, by means of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general, a transcendental content into its representations, on account of which they are called pure concepts of the understanding, which go a priori to objects, which general logic cannot accomplish.",["Obiecte = \"objects\" (Latinate Object; Gegenstand is also \"object\").","Urtheil = \"judgment\"; Form = \"form\"; Vorstellungen = \"representations\".","The relative \"welche\" in the first sentence and the final \"welches\" are left with the ambiguity of the German."]],"krv-a-0171":["In such a way there arise precisely as many pure concepts of the understanding, which go a priori to objects of intuition in general, as there were logical functions in all possible judgments in the previous table: for the understanding is entirely exhausted by the mentioned functions, and its faculty thereby completely measured out. We want to call these concepts, after Aristotle, categories, since our aim is originally indeed the same as his, although in execution it departs very far from it.",["Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Absicht = \"aim\"; möglich = \"possible\".","gedachte = \"mentioned\" (not \"thought\"). uranfänglich = \"originally\"."]],"krv-a-0172":["Table of the Categories.",[]],"krv-a-0173":["1. Of Quantity",[]],"krv-a-0174":["Unity",[]],"krv-a-0175":["Plurality",["Vielheit; not in the glossary."]],"krv-a-0176":["Totality",["Allheit."]],"krv-a-0177":["2. Of Quality",[]],"krv-a-0178":["Reality",["Realität."]],"krv-a-0179":["Negation",[]],"krv-a-0180":["Limitation",["Limitation (Latinate), as distinct from Schranke, which the glossary also renders \"limitation\"."]],"krv-a-0181":["3. Of Relation.",[]],"krv-a-0182":["of Inherence and Subsistence (*substantia et accidens*)",[]],"krv-a-0183":["of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)",["Ursache und Wirkung."]],"krv-a-0184":["of Community (interaction between the acting and the suffering).",["Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\"."]],"krv-a-0185":["4. Of Modality.",[]],"krv-a-0186":["Possibility — Impossibility",[]],"krv-a-0187":["Existence — Non-being",["Daseyn (= Dasein, \"existence\"); Nichtseyn."]],"krv-a-0188":["Necessity — Contingency.",["Zufälligkeit; zufällig = \"contingent\"."]],"krv-a-0189":["This now is the register of all originally pure concepts of synthesis that the understanding contains in itself a priori, and for the sake of which it is also only a pure understanding; since through them alone it can understand something in the manifold of intuition, i.e., think an object of it. This division is generated systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty to judge (which is just as much as the faculty to think), and has not arisen rhapsodically, from a search for pure concepts undertaken at random, of whose completeness one can never be certain, since it is inferred only by induction, not to mention that in the latter way one never has insight into why precisely these and not other concepts inhere in the pure understanding. It was a project, worthy of an acute man, of Aristotle's to search for these basic concepts. But since he had no *principium*, he scooped them up as they came upon him, and first rounded up ten of them, which he called categories (predicaments). Afterward he believed he had found five more of them, which he added under the name of the postpredicaments. Yet his table still remained deficient. Besides, there are also found among them some *modi* of pure sensibility (*quando*, *ubi*, *situs*, likewise *prius*, *simul*), also an empirical one (*motus*), which do not belong at all in this root register of the understanding, or else the derived concepts are also counted among the primitive concepts (*actio*, *passio*), and some of the latter are entirely lacking.",["OCR \"sirus\" silently corrected to *situs*. Italics for Latin follow the method; the Latin words appear in the original in roman type.","Princip = \"principle\" (Principium kept in Latin, italicized); Grundbegriffe = \"basic concepts\"; Stammregister = \"root register\"; Urbegriffe = \"primitive concepts\"; abgeleitete = \"derived\".","Verzeichnung = \"register\"; Vollzähligkeit = \"completeness\"; auf gut Glück = \"at random\" (literally \"on good luck\"); einsieht = \"has insight\".","The syntax of \"oder es sind auch die abgeleitete Begriffe...\" and \"an einigen der leztern fehlt es gänzlich\" is kept as in the German; \"the latter\" in the last clause refers back ambiguously."]],"krv-a-0190":["For the sake of the latter, therefore, it is still to be remarked: that the categories, as the true root concepts of the pure understanding, also have their equally pure derived concepts, which can by no means be passed over in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, with the mere mention of which, however, I can be satisfied in a merely critical essay.",["Stammbegriffe = \"root concepts\" (as above, Stammregister); Erwähnung = \"mention\"; Versuch = \"essay\".","Weltweisheit not present; \"Transscendental-Philosophie\" = \"transcendental philosophy\"."]],"krv-a-0191":["Let it be permitted me to call these pure but derived concepts of the understanding the predicables of the pure understanding (in contrast to the predicaments). If one has the original and primitive concepts, then the derived and subaltern ones can easily be added, and the family tree of the pure understanding completely painted out. Since I am concerned here not with the completeness of the system, but only of the principles for a system, I reserve this supplement for another occupation. But one can fairly well attain this aim if one takes the ontological textbooks in hand, and, e.g., subordinates to the category of causality the predicables of force, of action, of passion, to that of community those of presence, of resistance, to the predicaments of modality those of arising, passing away, alteration, etc. The categories, combined with the *modi* of pure sensibility or also with one another, yield a great multitude of derived concepts *a priori*, to take note of which, and where possible to record them to completeness, would be a useful and not unpleasant effort, but one dispensable here.",["Prädicabilien / Prädicamente: rendered 'predicables' / 'predicaments'; the German forms are Latinate (praedicabilia, praedicamenta).","OCR: 'Da es wir hier' read as 'Da es mir hier'. The German 'der der Gemeinschaft' is kept as 'to that of community' (the noun supplied by 'der' is not stated; possible slip in the text).","Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'; Begriffe = concepts throughout; Principien = 'principles' (Latinate form, same English as Grundsatz).","'zu einem System' is rendered 'for a system', with the genitive 'der Principien' left elliptical as in the German ('only of the principles').","'ausmahlen' = 'paint out'; 'verspahre' = 'reserve'; Beschäftigung = 'occupation'; Absicht = 'aim'; Bemühung = 'effort'.","*modi* is Kant's Latin; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Menge = 'multitude'."]],"krv-a-0192":["I deliberately excuse myself from the definitions of these categories in this treatise, although I might wish to be in possession of them. I shall analyze these concepts in the sequel to the degree that is sufficient in relation to the doctrine of method that I am working on. In a system of pure reason one could rightly demand them of me: but here they would only bring the main point of the investigation out of view, in that they would arouse doubts and attacks which, without withdrawing anything from the essential aim, one may quite well refer to another occupation. Meanwhile it nevertheless shines forth clearly from the little that I have adduced of this, that a complete dictionary with all the explanations required for it is not only possible, but also easy to bring about. The compartments are once there; it is only necessary to fill them out, and a systematic topic, like the present one, does not easily let one miss the place to which each concept peculiarly belongs, and at the same time easily notice the one that is still empty.",["Definitionen = 'definitions'; Erklärungen = 'explanations' (Erklärung is also 'definition' in the glossary; here the context is the dictionary entries, and 'explanations' keeps it distinct from Definition).","'in der Folge' = 'in the sequel' (Folge); 'hinreichend' = 'sufficient' (same English as zureichend in the glossary).","'überhebe ich mir' (reflexive dative) rendered 'I excuse myself from'; zergliedern = 'analyze'; Methodenlehre = 'doctrine of method'.","'Topik' = 'topic' (noun); 'Fächer' = 'compartments'; Hauptpunct = 'main point'; Absicht = 'aim'."]],"krv-a-0193":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Analytic. On the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["Hauptstück = 'chapter'; Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'."]],"krv-a-0194":["First Section. On the Principles of a Transcendent Deduction in General.",["'Transscendent.' is abbreviated in the original; kept as 'Transcendent' without completion to 'Transcendental'.","Principien = 'principles'; überhaupt = 'in general'."]],"krv-a-0195":["Jurists, when they speak of entitlements and presumptions, distinguish in a legal dispute the question about what is lawful (*quid iuris*) from that which concerns the fact (*quid facti*), and since they demand proof of both, they call the first, which is to demonstrate the entitlement, or also the claim to right, the deduction. We make use of a multitude of empirical concepts without anyone's objection, and also hold ourselves entitled, even without deduction, to appropriate to them a sense and an imagined meaning, because we always have experience at hand to prove their objective reality. Meanwhile there are also usurped concepts, such as perhaps fortune, fate, which indeed run about with almost universal indulgence, but are yet sometimes challenged by the question: *quid iuris*, whereupon one then falls into no small embarrassment on account of the deduction of them, since one can adduce no distinct ground of right either from experience or from reason, through which the entitlement to their use would become distinct.",["OCR: a full stop supplied after 'Deduction' (the text runs into 'Wir bedienen uns'); 'ron' read as 'von' elsewhere.","Rechtslehrer = 'jurists' (literally 'teachers of right'); Befugniß = 'entitlement'; Anmassung = 'presumption'; Rechtsanspruch = 'claim to right'; Rechtsgrund = 'ground of right'.","'Thatsache' = 'fact'; Sinn = 'sense'; Bedeutung = 'meaning'; 'eingebildete' = 'imagined'.","'in Anspruch genommen werden' rendered 'are challenged'; the German could also be 'laid claim to', and the ambiguity is not resolved by the English.","Realität = 'reality'; deutlich = 'distinct'; Gebrauch = 'use'; Erfahrung = 'experience'; Vernunft = 'reason'."]],"krv-a-0196":["But among the manifold concepts that make up the very mixed fabric of human cognition, there are some that are also determined for pure use *a priori* (completely independent of all experience), and the entitlement of these always requires a deduction; because proofs from experience are not sufficient for the legitimacy of such a use, but one must nevertheless know how these concepts can be related to objects which they yet take from no experience. I therefore call the explanation of the way in which concepts *a priori* can be related to objects the transcendental deduction of them, and distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the way in which a concept has been acquired through experience and reflection on it, and therefore concerns not the legitimacy but the *factum*, from which the possession arose.",["'transsc.' is abbreviated in the original; given in full as 'transcendental'. 'ron' read as 'von' (OCR).","Obiecte and Gegenstände are both 'objects' (Object / Gegenstand); here Obiecte appears once ('auf Obiecte beziehen'), Gegenstände once.","Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Befugniß = 'entitlement'; Rechtmäßigkeit = 'legitimacy'; Art = 'way'; Erklärung = 'explanation'; Besitz = 'possession'.","'bestimt sind' = 'are determined'; the construction 'zum reinen Gebrauch a priori' is kept in the German order, so '*a priori*' may attach to 'use' or to 'determined'.","'wodurch' (at the end) could refer to the *factum* or to the whole clause; 'from which' is the English approximation. Factum is Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-0197":["We now already have two sorts of concepts of an entirely different kind, which nevertheless agree with one another in that they both relate themselves to objects completely *a priori*, namely, the concepts of space and time, as forms of sensibility, and the categories, as concepts of the understanding. To want to attempt an empirical deduction of them would be entirely vain labor; because precisely therein lies what is distinctive in their nature, that they relate themselves to their objects without having borrowed anything from experience for the representation of these. If, therefore, a deduction of them is necessary, it will always have to be transcendental.",["'zweierley Begriffe von ganz verschiedener Art': Art = 'kind' here, with 'sorts' for 'zweierley'.","Gegenstände = 'objects'; Raum = 'space'; Zeit = 'time'; Form = 'form'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Vorstellung = 'representation'; Erfahrung = 'experience'.","'beyderseits' = 'on both sides', rendered 'both'; 'Unterscheidende' = 'what is distinctive'; 'Arbeit' = 'labor'."]],"krv-a-0198":["Meanwhile one can seek out in experience, for these concepts as for all cognition, if not the *principium* of their possibility, then at least the occasional causes of their generation, where the impressions of the senses give the first occasion to open the entire power of cognition with regard to them, and to bring about experience, which contains two very heterogeneous elements, namely, a matter for cognition from the senses, and a certain form for ordering it, from the inner source of pure intuiting and thinking, which, on the occasion of the former, are first brought into exercise, and produce concepts. Such a tracing of the first endeavors of our power of cognition, in order to ascend from individual perceptions to universal concepts, has without doubt its great use, and one has the renowned Locke to thank that he first opened the way to it. But a deduction of the pure concepts *a priori* is never brought about through this, for it lies entirely and in no way on this path, because with regard to their future use, which is supposed to be completely independent of experience, they must be able to show a quite different certificate of birth than that of descent from experiences. This attempted physiological derivation, which properly cannot be called deduction at all, because it concerns a *quaestio facti*, I will therefore call the explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is thus clear that of these alone there can be a transcendental deduction and by no means an empirical one, and that the latter, with regard to the pure concepts *a priori*, are nothing but vain attempts, with which only he can occupy himself who has not comprehended the quite peculiar nature of these cognitions.",["*principium* is Kant's Latin; *quæstio facti* (original spelling) given as *quaestio facti*. 'transscend.' is abbreviated in the original and given in full.","Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Erkentnißkraft = 'power of cognition' (Kraft); Eindrücke = 'impressions'; Materie = 'matter'; Form = 'form'; Wahrnehmungen = 'perceptions'; Begriffe = 'concepts'.","'Gelegenheitsursachen' = 'occasional causes'; 'Anlaß' and 'Gelegenheit' both rendered 'occasion'.","'die, bey Gelegenheit der ersteren, ... werden': the relative 'die' is plural and its antecedent is ambiguous ('elements' / 'form' and 'matter'); 'which' preserves this. 'der ersteren' = 'the former'.","'Anschauens' = 'intuiting' (verbal noun from Anschauung); 'Nachspühren' = 'tracing'; 'Bestrebungen' = 'endeavors'; 'Geburtsbrief' = 'certificate of birth'; 'Abstammung' = 'descent'.","'ganz und gar nicht' kept as 'entirely and in no way'; 'wo nicht ... doch' = 'if not ... then at least'; 'begriffen' = 'comprehended'."]],"krv-a-0199":["Now although the sole way of a possible deduction of pure cognition a priori, namely that on the transcendental path, is conceded, it nevertheless does not thereby become evident that it is so unavoidably necessary. Above we pursued the concepts of space and time to their sources by means of a transcendental deduction, and explained and determined their objective validity a priori. Nevertheless geometry goes its secure step through nothing but cognitions a priori, without having to beg from philosophy a certificate of credentials for the pure and lawful descent of its fundamental concept of space. But the use of this concept in this science extends only to the outer world of sense, of which space is the pure form of its intuition, in which therefore all geometrical cognition, because it is grounded on intuition a priori, has immediate evidence, and the objects are given through the cognition itself, a priori (as to form) in intuition. By contrast, with the pure concepts of the understanding there begins the unavoidable need to seek the transcendental deduction not only of them themselves but also of space, because, since they speak of objects not through predicates of intuition and of sensibility but of pure thinking a priori, they relate to objects generally without any conditions of sensibility, and which, since they are not grounded on experience, also cannot show any object in intuition a priori on which they would have grounded their synthesis prior to all experience, and therefore arouse suspicion not only on account of the objective validity and limitations of their use, but also make that concept of space ambiguous, by being inclined to use it beyond the conditions of sensible intuition, on account of which a transcendental deduction of it was also needed above. Thus the reader must be convinced of the unavoidable necessity of such a transc. deduction before he has taken a single step in the field of pure reason; because otherwise he proceeds blindly, and, after having wandered about in manifold ways, must nevertheless return again to the ignorance from which he had set out. But he must also clearly see in advance the inevitable difficulty, so that he does not complain of obscurity where the matter itself is deeply enveloped, or become vexed too early over the removal of the obstacles, because it comes down to this: either to give up entirely all claims to insights of pure reason, as the most favored field, namely that beyond the boundaries of all possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to perfection.",["Gegenstand and Object are both rendered \"object\"; here Gegenstände throughout, with \"Obiect\" once (\"kein Obiect vorzeigen\").","Erkentniß = cognition; Gültigkeit = validity; Schranken = limitations; Grenzen = boundaries; Vollkommenheit = perfection; unumgänglich = unavoidable, unvermeidlich = inevitable.","\"Beglaubigungsschein\" rendered \"certificate of credentials\"; \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\".","Preserved syntactic roughness: \"redet\" is singular though its subject \"sie\" (the concepts) is plural elsewhere in the clause; \"und die, da sie nicht auf Erfahrung gegründet sind\" has an unclear antecedent for \"die\" (rendered \"and which\"), and the clause's grammatical connection to the preceding one is left as in the German.","\"ihre Synthesis\" / \"ihres Gebrauchs\" / \"ihrer Anschauung\": \"ihrer\" in \"die reine Form ihrer Anschauung\" is left ambiguous in reference, as in the German.","\"transscendent. Deduction\" and \"transsc. Deduction\" are abbreviations in the original; rendered \"transcendental\" and \"transc.\" respectively."]],"krv-a-0200":["Above, with the concepts of space and time, we were able to make comprehensible with little effort how these, as cognitions a priori, nevertheless must necessarily relate to objects, and made possible a synthetic cognition of them, independent of all experience. For since only by means of such pure forms of sensibility can an object appear to us, i.e., be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain a priori the condition of the possibility of objects as appearances, and the synthesis in them has objective validity.",["Gegenstand and Object both \"object\" (Gegenstand / Gegenstände; \"Obiect\" in \"ein Obiect der empirischen Anschauung\").","\"macheten\" is an older form of \"machten\"; \"erscheinen\" = appear, \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances.","\"a priori\" in \"als Erkentnisse a priori\" and \"enthalten a priori\" is left attached as in the German word order."]],"krv-a-0201":["The categories of the understanding, by contrast, do not represent to us at all the conditions under which objects are given in intuition, consequently objects can indeed appear to us without their having necessarily to relate to functions of the understanding, and this therefore containing the conditions of them a priori. Hence a difficulty shows itself here that we did not encounter in the field of sensibility, namely how subjective conditions of thinking should have objective validity, i.e., furnish conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects: for without functions of the understanding appearances can indeed be given in intuition. I take, e.g., the concept of cause, which signifies a particular kind of synthesis, in which upon something A something entirely different B is posited according to a rule. It is not clear a priori why appearances should contain anything of the sort, (for experiences cannot be adduced for the proof, because the objective validity of this concept must be able to be demonstrated a priori) and it is therefore doubtful a priori whether such a concept is not perhaps altogether empty and meets with no object anywhere among the appearances. For that objects of sensible intuition must conform to the formal conditions of sensibility lying a priori in the mind is clear from this, that otherwise they would not be objects for us; but that they must also, moreover, conform to the conditions that the understanding needs for the synthetic insight of thinking, the inference of that is not so easy to see. For appearances might well, at a pinch, be so constituted that the understanding would find them not at all in conformity with the conditions of its unity, and everything would lie in such confusion that, e.g., in the sequence of appearances nothing would offer itself that would furnish a rule of synthesis and thus correspond to the concept of cause and effect, so that this concept would therefore be entirely empty, null and without significance. Appearances would nonetheless offer objects to our intuition, for intuition needs the functions of thinking in no way.",["\"z. V.\" in the source is an OCR slip for \"z. B.\"; rendered \"e.g.\"","Gegenstand / Object both \"object\"; \"Bedeutung\" = significance (cf. \"bedeutet\" = signifies); \"Einsicht\" = insight; \"Schlußfolge\" rendered \"inference\" (Schluß = inference).","\"Reihenfolge\" rendered \"sequence\" (Folge = sequel; sequence); \"Art\" = kind; \"besonder\" = particular.","\"dieser ... die Bedingungen derselben a priori enthielte\": \"dieser\" (the understanding vs. functions) and \"derselben\" are left as ambiguous as in the German; \"a priori\" could also attach to \"Bedingungen\".","\"davon ist die Schlußfolge\" is left as an awkward pendant construction as in the German."]],"krv-a-0202":["If one thought to extricate oneself from the laboriousness of these investigations by saying: Experience incessantly offers examples of such a regularity of appearances, which give sufficient occasion to abstract the concept of cause from them, and thereby at the same time to confirm the objective validity of such a concept, then one does not notice that in this way the concept of cause cannot arise at all, but that it must either be grounded entirely a priori in the understanding, or be given up entirely as a mere figment of the brain. For this concept thoroughly requires that something A be of such a kind that another thing B follow from it necessarily and according to a rule that is absolutely universal. Appearances do indeed furnish cases from which a rule is possible according to which something usually happens, but never that the outcome is necessary: hence a dignity also attaches to the synthesis of cause and effect that cannot be expressed empirically at all, namely, that the effect does not merely come in addition to the cause, but is posited through it, and ensues from it. The strict universality of the rule is also not at all a property of empirical rules, which through induction can acquire no other than comparative universality, i.e., widespread usefulness. Now, however, the use of the pure concepts of the understanding would change entirely if one wanted to treat them only as empirical products.",["\"Erfolg\" (not in the glossary) rendered \"outcome\"; \"erfolge\" rendered \"ensues\"; \"folge\" = \"follow\".","\"Dignität\" rendered \"dignity\"; \"Hirngespinst\" = \"figment of the brain\"; \"Eigenschaft\" = property; \"Allgemeinheit\" = universality.","\"Verständesbegriffe\" in the source is a misprint for \"Verstandesbegriffe\"; rendered \"concepts of the understanding\".","\"der Synthesis der Ursache und Wirkung\": \"Dignität anhängt\" takes the dative; rendered \"attaches to the synthesis of cause and effect\".","\"gar wol\" rendered \"do indeed\"; \"gewöhnlicher massen\" = \"usually\"."]],"krv-a-0203":["Transition to the Transc. Deduction of the Categories.",["Heading; \"Transsc.\" is an abbreviation in the original, kept as \"Transc.\"."]],"krv-a-0204":["There are only two cases possible in which synthetic representation and its objects can come together, relate to one another necessarily, and, as it were, meet one another. Either if the object makes the representation possible, or this makes the object alone possible. If the former, then this relation is only empirical, and the representation is never possible *a priori*. And this is the case with appearance, with regard to that which belongs in them to sensation. But if the second, since representation in itself (for of its causality, by means of the will, there is here no talk at all,) does not produce its object as to existence, the representation is nevertheless determining of the object *a priori* if through it alone it is possible to cognize something as an object. But there are two conditions under which alone the cognition of an object is possible, first, intuition, through which it, but only as appearance, is given; second, concept, through which an object is thought that corresponds to this intuition. But it is clear from the above that the first condition, namely that under which alone objects can be intuited, in fact lies at the basis of the objects as to form *a priori* in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility, therefore, all appearances necessarily agree, because they can appear only through it, i.e., be empirically intuited and given. Now it is asked whether concepts *a priori* do not also precede, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is nevertheless thought as object in general, for then all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily in conformity with such concepts, because, without presupposing them, nothing is possible as object of experience. Now, however, all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses, through which something is given, also a concept of an object that is given in the intuition, or appears: accordingly, concepts of objects in general will lie at the basis, as conditions *a priori*, of all cognition of experience: consequently the objective validity of the categories, as concepts *a priori*, will rest on this, that through them alone experience (as to the form of thinking) is possible. For then they relate necessarily and *a priori* to objects of experience, because only by means of them can any object of experience in general be thought.",["Gegenstand and Object (here 'Obiecten', 'Obiect') are both rendered 'object'.","Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Gemüth = mind; Daseyn = existence; Erscheinung = appearance; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","'Caussalität' (sic) rendered 'causality'. 'gleichsam' = 'as it were'.","'in der That den Obiecten der Form nach a priori im Gemüth zum Grunde liegen': the plural 'liegen' with singular 'Bedingung' is as printed; 'den Obiecten' could be read as dative of 'zum Grunde liegen' ('lie at the basis of the objects'), kept ambiguous.","'Ist das erstere ... Ist aber das zweyte': the construction with 'weil ... nicht hervorbringt, so ist doch' is kept as in the German; the 'if'/'since' clauses are left as ambiguous as the original.","'a priori bestimmend': 'a priori' may attach to 'bestimmend' or to the relation; left in the German position.","'Gültigkeit' = validity; 'Gemäß' = 'in conformity with'."]],"krv-a-0205":["The transc. deduction of all concepts *a priori* thus has a *principium* toward which the whole investigation must be directed, namely this: that they must be recognized as conditions *a priori* of the possibility of experiences (be it of the intuition that is encountered in it, or of the thinking). Concepts that furnish the objective ground of the possibility of experience are for just that reason necessary. The development of the experience, however, in which they are encountered, is not their deduction, (but illustration) because they would thereby still only be contingent. Without this original relation to possible experience, in which all objects of cognition occur, the relation of the same to any object could not be comprehended at all.",["'transsc.' is an abbreviation in the source, rendered 'transc.'; Principium kept in Latin italics.","'erkant' = 'recognized' (erkennen; the glossary offers 'cognize; recognize'); 'zufällig' = contingent; 'begriffen' = comprehended.","'in ihr' (in it) refers grammatically to 'Erfahrung' (feminine singular) in the parenthesis; 'sie' in 'worinn sie' is ambiguous between the concepts and, loosely, other referents; kept as in German.","Entwickelung rendered 'development'; Illustration kept as cognate."]],"krv-a-0206":["But there are three original sources (capacities or faculties of the soul) that contain the conditions of the possibility of all experience, and cannot themselves be derived from any other faculty of the mind, namely, sense, imagination, and apperception. Upon this are grounded 1) the synopsis of the manifold *a priori* through sense; 2) the synthesis of this manifold through imagination: finally 3) the unity of this synthesis through original apperception. All these faculties have, besides the empirical use, also a transc. one, which goes solely to the form, and is possible *a priori*. Of this we spoke above in the first part with regard to the senses, but the other two we now want to try to see into according to their nature.",["Fähigkeiten = capacities; Vermögen = faculties (glossary lists 'faculty; capacity' for Vermögen; the two are distinguished here by the parenthesis).","Sinn (sing.) = 'sense'; Sinne (pl.) = 'senses'; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Gemüth = mind; Seele = soul.","'transsc.' abbreviation rendered 'transc.'; 'Synopsis', 'Synthesis', 'Apperception' kept as cognates.","'Darauf gründet sich' = 'Upon this are grounded'; 'darauf' may refer to the three sources or to their having these uses; left as in German."]],"krv-a-0207":["Second Section of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the Grounds *a priori* for the Possibility of Experience.",["Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Gründe = grounds.","Heading rendered as it stands; the B-edition page reference in the id does not correspond to the A text given."]],"krv-a-0208":["That a concept should be generated entirely *a priori* and relate to an object, although it neither itself belongs in the concept of possible experience, nor consists of elements of a possible experience, is wholly contradictory and impossible. For it would then have no content, because no intuition would correspond to it, in that intuitions in general, through which objects can be given to us, make up the field, or the entire object, of possible experience. A concept *a priori* that did not relate to this would be only the logical form for a concept, but not the concept itself through which something would be thought.",["'widersprechend' rendered 'contradictory' (cf. Widerspruch = contradiction); 'Inhalt' = content; 'correspondirte' = 'correspond'.","'Gegenstand' in 'den gesamten Gegenstand möglicher Erfahrung' = 'object'.","'diese' in 'auf diese bezöge' (feminine) is left as 'this'; its antecedent (Erfahrung or Anschauungen) is ambiguous in the German."]],"krv-a-0209":["If there are, then, pure concepts *a priori*, these can indeed contain nothing empirical: but they must nevertheless be nothing but conditions *a priori* of a possible experience, as that on which alone their objective reality can rest.",["Realität = reality; 'lauter' rendered 'nothing but'.","'als worauf allein' is kept close to the German; 'as that on which alone' may refer to experience or to the conditions."]],"krv-a-0210":["If one therefore wants to know how pure concepts of the understanding are possible, one must investigate what are the conditions *a priori* on which the possibility of experience depends, and which lie at its basis, even if one abstracts from everything empirical of appearances. A concept that expresses this formal and objective condition of experience universally and sufficiently would be called a pure concept of the understanding. Once I have pure concepts of the understanding, I can also well think up objects that are perhaps impossible, perhaps indeed possible in themselves, but cannot be given in any experience, in that in the connection of those concepts something can be left out that nevertheless necessarily belongs to the condition of a possible experience, (concept of a spirit) or perhaps pure concepts of the understanding are extended further than experience can grasp (concept of God). The elements, however, for all cognitions *a priori*, even for arbitrary and absurd fictions, can indeed not be borrowed from experience, (for otherwise they would not be cognitions *a priori*) but they must always contain the pure conditions *a priori* of a possible experience and of an object of the same, for otherwise not only would nothing at all be thought through them, but they themselves, without data, could not even arise in thinking.",["Verknüpfung = connection; Geist = spirit (here 'Begriff eines Geistes'); Erscheinungen = appearances; Bedingung = condition; zureichend = sufficiently.","'an sich' = 'in themselves'; 'Data' kept as the Latin plural, rendered 'data'.","'erdenken' rendered 'think up'; 'Erdichtungen' = 'fictions'; 'ungereimten' = 'absurd'.","'selbst zu willkührlichen' : 'selbst' may attach to 'Erkentnissen a priori' or to 'Erdichtungen'; rendered 'even for', leaving it open.","'derselben' in 'eines Gegenstandes derselben' refers to experience (Erfahrung); 'of the same' kept."]],"krv-a-0211":["These concepts now, which contain *a priori* the pure thinking in every experience, we find in the categories, and it is already a sufficient deduction of them, and justification of their objective validity, if we can prove: that by means of them alone an object can be thought. But because in such a thought more than the single faculty of thinking, namely the understanding, is occupied, and this itself, as a faculty of cognition that is to relate to objects, equally requires a clarification, on account of the possibility of this relation: so we must first consider the subjective sources that make up the foundation *a priori* of the possibility of experience, not according to their empirical, but transcendental constitution.",["Erläuterung = clarification; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Gültigkeit = validity; Rechtfertigung = justification; Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition.","Obiecten/Gegenstand both = 'object(s)'.","'welche ... enthalten' : the relative clause could take 'Begriffe' as subject with 'das reine Denken' as object (as rendered); the German word order also allows 'das reine Denken' as subject of 'enthalten' — preserved only partly in English.","'erwegen' (= erwägen) rendered 'consider' (Betrachtung = consideration is the glossary noun)."]],"krv-a-0212":["If every single representation were entirely foreign to the others, as it were isolated, and separated from them, then something such as cognition, which is a whole of compared and connected representations, would never arise. If I therefore ascribe a synopsis to sense because it contains manifoldness in its intuition, a synthesis always corresponds to this, and receptivity can make cognitions possible only when combined with spontaneity. This is now the ground of a threefold synthesis that necessarily occurs in all cognition: namely, of the apprehension of the representations, as modifications of the mind in intuition, of the reproduction of the same in imagination, and of their recognition in the concept. These now give a guide to three subjective sources of cognition, which themselves make the understanding possible and, through this, all experience, as an empirical product of the understanding.",["Vorstellung = representation; Erkentniß = cognition; Mannigfaltigkeit = 'manifoldness' (cf. das Mannigfaltige = manifold, n.); Verbindung 'verbunden' = 'combined'.","'Diese' (feminine) in 'Diese ist nun der Grund' has an ambiguous antecedent (Spontaneität, or Synthesis); kept as 'This'.","'Einbildung' rendered 'imagination' (cf. Einbildungskraft elsewhere, also 'imagination'); 'Recognition' rendered 'recognition'; 'Apprehension', 'Reproduction', 'Modificationen', 'Receptivität', 'Spontaneität' as cognates.","'verglichener' = 'compared'; 'Leitung' = 'guide' (literally 'guidance')."]],"krv-a-0213":["Preliminary Remark.",["'Erinnerung' rendered 'remark' (literally 'reminder'/'remembrance'); a heading only."]],"krv-a-0214":["The deduction of the categories is bound up with so many difficulties, and compels us to penetrate so deeply into the first grounds of the possibility of our cognition in general, that, in order to avoid the prolixity of a complete theory, and yet, in so necessary an investigation, to neglect nothing, I have found it more advisable to prepare the reader more than to instruct him through the following four numbers; and in the immediately following third section to present for the first time systematically the exposition of these elements of the understanding. On that account the reader will until then not let himself be turned away by the obscurity, which on a path that is still wholly untrodden is at first unavoidable, but which, as I hope, is to clear up into complete insight in the said section.",["Deduction = *Deduction* (Latin-derived, kept as the German spelling); Categorien = categories; Erkentniß = cognition (Erkenntnis).","Erörterung = exposition (*expositio*); Verstand = understanding; Möglichkeit = possibility.","'mehr vorzubereiten, als zu unterrichten': 'more ... than' is kept in the German order; the construction with 'durch folgende vier Nummern' is left as ambiguous as the German (the four numbers may attach to 'vorzubereiten' alone or to both infinitives).","'die Dunkelheit nicht abwendig machen lassen': 'abwendig machen' rendered 'turned away'; the reflexive object 'sich' and the relative clause 'die auf einem Wege ...' are kept as in the German, where 'die' could in principle refer to 'Dunkelheit' only (the sense retained)."]],"krv-a-0215":["1. On the synthesis of apprehension in intuition.",["Anschauung = intuition; Apprehension = apprehension (Latinate word kept as in the German)."]],"krv-a-0216":["Our representations may arise from wherever they will, whether they are effected through the influence of outer things or through inner causes, whether they have arisen *a priori*, or empirically as appearances; yet as modifications of the mind they belong to inner sense, and as such all our cognitions are in the end nevertheless subject to the formal condition of inner sense, namely time, as that in which they must all be ordered, connected and brought into relations. This is a general remark that one must lay at the basis of everything that follows.",["Vorstellungen = representations; Erscheinungen = appearances; Gemüth = mind; Erkentnisse = cognitions; Bedingung = condition; Zeit = time.","'Modificationen' = modifications (Latinate); 'verknüpft' = connected (Verknüpfung = connection).","'als in welcher': 'as that in which'; the antecedent could be 'Zeit' or 'inner sense'; left as in German (the sense kept is 'time')."]],"krv-a-0217":["Every intuition contains a manifold in itself, which, however, would not be represented as such if the mind did not distinguish the time in the sequence of impressions on one another: for as contained in one moment, each representation can never be anything other than absolute unity. Now in order that unity of intuition should come to be from this manifold (as, say, in the representation of space), first the running through of the manifold and then the taking together of it is necessary, which action I call the synthesis of apprehension, because it is directed straight at intuition, which to be sure offers a manifold, but can never effect this as such, and indeed as contained in one representation, without a synthesis occurring in it.",["Mannigfaltiges = manifold (n.); Mannigfaltigkeit = manifold (rendered 'manifold' as well; one English word for two German forms).","Folge = sequence; Eindruck = impression; Einheit = unity; Raum = space; Handlung = action.","'Zusammennehmung' = 'taking together'.","'die zwar ein Mannigfaltiges darbietet, dieses aber ... bewirken kan': 'dieses' (this) is kept as in the German; the grammatical subject of 'bewirken kan' is 'die' (intuition), the antecedent of 'welche Handlung' is otherwise ambiguous in 'Handlung ... die'. 'in einer Vorstellung enthalten' could attach to 'dieses' or to the effecting."]],"krv-a-0218":["This synthesis of apprehension must now also be exercised *a priori*, i.e., in regard to representations that are not empirical. For without it we could have neither the representations of space nor of time *a priori*: since these can be generated only through the synthesis of the manifold that sensibility offers in its original receptivity. Thus we have a pure synthesis of apprehension.",["rein = pure; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Receptivität = receptivity.","'ausgeübet' = exercised; 'erzeugt' = generated."]],"krv-a-0219":["2. On the synthesis of reproduction in imagination.",["Einbildung (here) = imagination; elsewhere Einbildungskraft = imagination (one English word for two German forms)."]],"krv-a-0220":["It is indeed a merely empirical law according to which representations that have often followed or accompanied one another finally associate with one another, and thereby place themselves in a connection according to which, even without the presence of the object, one of these representations brings about a transition of the mind to the other, according to a constant rule. This law of reproduction, however, presupposes that the appearances themselves are actually subject to such a rule, and that in the manifold of their representations an accompaniment or sequence in accordance with certain rules takes place; for without that our empirical imagination would never get anything to do suited to its faculty, and thus would remain hidden in the interior of the mind like a dead faculty unknown to ourselves. If cinnabar were now red, now black, now light, now heavy, if a human being were changed now into this, now into that animal shape, if on the longest day the land were now covered with fruits, now with ice and snow, then my empirical imagination would not even get the occasion, with the representation of the red color, to bring the heavy cinnabar to mind, or if a certain word were attached now to this, now to that thing, or also the very same thing were now so, now otherwise named, without a certain rule, to which the appearances are already subject of themselves, prevailing therein, then no empirical synthesis of reproduction could take place.",["Gesetz = law; Gegenstand = object; Regel = rule; Vermögen = faculty; Einbildungskraft = imagination.","Folge = sequence; Begleitung = accompaniment; Verknüpfung = connection; 'benant' = named.","'ohne daß hierin eine gewisse Regel ... herrschete': 'hierin' = 'therein'; the clause is kept in the German position, so that it may attach to either preceding alternative (word or thing)."]],"krv-a-0221":["There must therefore be something that itself makes possible this reproduction of appearances, by being the ground *a priori* of a necessary synthetic unity of them. One soon arrives at this, however, if one reflects that appearances are not things in themselves, but the mere play of our representations, which in the end run out into determinations of inner sense. If we can now show that even our purest intuitions *a priori* afford no cognition except insofar as they contain such a combination of the manifold as makes possible a thoroughgoing synthesis of reproduction, then this synthesis of imagination is also grounded, before all experience, on principles *a priori*, and one must assume a pure transcendental synthesis of it, which itself lies at the basis of the possibility of all experience (as which necessarily presupposes the reproducibility of appearances). Now it is obvious that if I draw a line in thought, or think the time from one noon to another, or even only want to represent to myself a certain number, I must first necessarily grasp in thought one of these manifold representations after the other. But if I were always to lose the preceding ones (the first parts of the line, the preceding parts of the time, or the units represented one after another) from thought and not reproduce them while I proceed to the following ones, then no whole representation, and none of all the aforementioned thoughts, indeed not even the purest and first basic representations of space and time, could arise.",["Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves; Bestimmungen = determinations; Verbindung = combination; Grundvorstellungen = basic representations.","Grund = ground; 'zum Grunde liegt' = lies at the basis; Principien = principles (Latinate, kept as in German).","'(als welche die Reproducibilität ... voraussezt)': 'als welche' is ambiguous between 'experience' and 'possibility'; the English 'as which' preserves this.","'Zahl' = number; 'Theile' = parts; 'Einheiten' = units (Einheit elsewhere 'unity')."]],"krv-a-0222":["The synthesis of apprehension is therefore inseparably combined with the synthesis of reproduction. And since the former constitutes the transcendental ground of the possibility of all cognitions in general (not merely of the empirical, but also of the pure *a priori*), the reproductive synthesis of the imagination belongs among the transcendental actions of the mind, and with regard to it we will also call this faculty the transcendental faculty of imagination.",["'verbunden' = combined (Verbindung = combination); 'Handlungen' = actions.","'iene' = 'the former'; 'dieselbe' (in 'in Rücksicht auf dieselbe') = 'it', its antecedent left as ambiguous as in German (the synthesis or the actions)."]],"krv-a-0223":["3. On the synthesis of recognition in the concept.",["Recognition = *Recognition* (German Latinate form); Begriff = concept (*conceptus*)."]],"krv-a-0224":["Without consciousness that what we think is the very same as what we thought a moment before, all reproduction in the series of representations would be in vain. For there would be a new representation in the present state, which would not belong at all to the act through which it was supposed to have been generated little by little, and the manifold of it would never constitute a whole, because it would lack the unity that only consciousness can provide for it. If in counting I forget that the units that now hover before my senses have been added to one another by me little by little, then I would not cognize the generation of the multitude through this successive adding of one to one, and hence also not the number; for this concept consists solely in the consciousness of this unity of the synthesis.",["Bewustseyn = consciousness; Zustand = state; Actus = act (Latin kept in German; rendered 'act'); Menge = multitude; Zahl = number; Einheit = unity; erkennen = cognize.","'vor Sinnen schweben' = 'hover before my senses' (Sinn = sense).","'Hinzuthuung' = 'adding'; 'nach und nach' = 'little by little'.","'ihm' in 'die ihm nur das Bewustseyn verschaffen kan' refers to 'das Mannigfaltige' (it/the manifold); the English keeps the pronoun 'it'."]],"krv-a-0225":["The word concept could of itself already give us guidance to this remark. For it is this one consciousness that unites the manifold, successively intuited, and then also reproduced, into one representation. This consciousness can often be only weak, so that we connect it only with the effect, but not with the act itself, i.e., immediately with the generation of the representation: but notwithstanding these differences, a consciousness must nevertheless always be encountered, even if it lacks conspicuous clarity, and without it concepts, and with them cognition of objects, are wholly impossible.",["'Begriff' = concept; 'begreifen' (not present) would be 'comprehend'; the word is mentioned, not used, in the first sentence (hence 'the word concept').","'Angeschaute' = intuited (Anschauung = intuition); 'Wirkung' = effect; 'Gegenständen' = objects; 'Klarheit' = clarity.","OCR: 'un' read as 'und' (silently corrected).","'in der Wirkung, nicht aber in dem Actus selbst' — 'Actus' kept as 'act'; 'es' (it) = this consciousness."]],"krv-a-0226":["And here it is necessary to make oneself understandable about what one means by the expression of an object of the representations. We said above: that appearances themselves are nothing but sensible representations, which in themselves, in just the same way, must not be regarded as objects (outside the power of representation). What does one understand, then, when one speaks of an object corresponding to cognition, and hence also distinct from it? It is easy to see that this object must be thought only as something in general = X, since outside our cognition we still have nothing that we could set over against this cognition as corresponding to it.",["Gegenstand rendered 'object' throughout; Object (A 106, 'Obiecte') also 'object' — the Latinate member is marked here.","Vorstellungskraft rendered 'power of representation' (not in glossary; Kraft = power, Vorstellung = representation).","'in eben derselben Art' rendered 'in just the same way' (Art = way); 'an sich' = 'in itself'.","'Erkentniß' (1781 spelling) = Erkenntniß = cognition; 'versteht' = understand (verstehen); 'verständlich machen' = 'make understandable'.","'Ausdruck' = expression; 'gegen über setzen' = 'set over against'."]],"krv-a-0227":["But we find that our thought of the relation of all cognition to its object carries something of necessity with it, since namely the latter is regarded as that which stands against our cognitions' being determined not at random, or arbitrarily, but a priori in a certain way, because, insofar as they are to relate to an object, they must also necessarily agree with one another in relation to it, i.e., have that unity which constitutes the concept of an object.",["'dawider ist, daß ... nicht ... bestimt seyn' is awkward; kept literally as 'stands against ... being determined not at random'. The negation 'nicht' could be read as pleonastic after 'dawider'.","'a priori auf gewisse Weise' could attach to 'bestimt' alone or to the contrast with 'aufs Gerathewohl'; left as in the German.","Gerathewohl = 'at random'; beliebig = 'arbitrarily'; Beziehung = relation; Einheit = unity; Begriff = concept.","'Nothwendigkeit' = necessity; 'nothwendiger Weise' = 'necessarily'."]],"krv-a-0228":["But it is clear that, since we have to do only with the manifold of our representations, and that X which corresponds to them (the object), because it is supposed to be something distinct from all our representations, is nothing for us, the unity which the object makes necessary can be nothing other than the formal unity of consciousness in the synthesis of the manifold of the representations. Then we say: we cognize the object when we have effected synthetic unity in the manifold of intuition. This, however, is impossible if the intuition could not have been produced through such a function of synthesis according to a rule, which makes the reproduction of the manifold a priori necessary and a concept in which this unites possible. Thus we think a triangle as an object by being conscious of the composition of three straight lines according to a rule in accordance with which such an intuition can always be exhibited. This unity of the rule now determines everything manifold, and limits it to conditions that make the unity of apperception possible, and the concept of this unity is the representation of the object = X, which I think through the thought predicates of a triangle.",["'vor uns nichts ist' = 'is nothing for us' (vor = für in 1781 usage).","'erkennen' = cognize; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Bewustseyn' = consciousness; 'Synthesis' kept as 'synthesis'; 'Function' = function.","'a priori' in 'Reproduction des Mannigfaltigen a priori nothwendig' may attach to 'Reproduction', to 'Mannigfaltigen', or to 'nothwendig'; left ambiguous as in the German.","'schränkt ... ein' rendered 'limits' (Schranke = limitation); 'bestimt' = determines; 'dargestelt' = 'exhibited'.","'Zusammensetzung' = 'composition'; 'Apperception' = apperception; 'gedachte Prädicate' = 'thought predicates' (literally 'predicates thought', ambiguous with 'predicates that are thought').","'welche ... macht' — the relative 'welche' (feminine) refers grammatically to 'Function' or 'Regel'; left ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0229":["All cognition demands a concept, however imperfect or obscure this may be: but this, as to its form, is always something general, and what serves as a rule. Thus the concept of body serves, according to the unity of the manifold which is thought through it, as a rule for our cognition of outer appearances. But it can be a rule of intuitions only by representing, given appearances, the necessary reproduction of the manifold of them, hence the synthetic unity in their consciousness. Thus the concept of body, in the perception of something outside us, makes necessary the representation of extension, and with it that of impenetrability, of shape, etc.",["'unvollkommen' rendered 'imperfect' (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection); 'dunkel' = 'obscure' (not in glossary).","'Allgemeines' = 'general'; 'Cörper' = 'body'; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception; 'Ausdehnung' = extension.","'Alles Erkentniß' (sic, for 'Alle Erkenntniß'): 'Alles' kept as printed in the source text; rendered 'All cognition'.","'dieser mag nun so ... seyn, wie er wolle' rendered 'however ... this may be'; 'dieser' refers to 'Begriff'."]],"krv-a-0230":["At the ground of all necessity there always lies a transcendental condition. Therefore a transcendental ground of the unity of consciousness, in the synthesis of the manifold of all our intuitions, hence also of the concepts of objects in general, consequently also of all objects of experience, must be encountered, without which it would be impossible to think any object for our intuitions: for this is nothing more than the something of which the concept expresses such a necessity of synthesis.",["'Obiecte' (Latinate) and 'Gegenstände' are both rendered 'object(s)'.","'Grund' in 'zum Grunde' and 'transscendentaler Grund' both involve Grund; rendered 'ground'.","'Nothwendig' in the last clause (sic, for Nothwendigkeit) is a probable slip in the source; rendered 'necessity'.","'dieser' in 'denn dieser ist nichts mehr' could refer to 'Gegenstand' (the nearest masculine noun) or 'Grund'; left as 'this'.","'angetroffen werden' = 'be encountered'; 'ausdrukt' = 'expresses'."]],"krv-a-0231":["This original and transcendental condition is now none other than transcendental apperception. Consciousness of oneself, according to the determinations of our state, in inner perception is merely empirical, always changeable, there can be no standing or abiding self in this flux of inner appearances, and it is usually called inner sense, or empirical apperception. That which is necessarily to be represented as numerically identical cannot be thought as such through empirical data. There must be a condition that precedes all experience and makes this itself possible, which is to make a transcendental presupposition of this sort valid.",["'Bewustseyn seiner selbst' = 'consciousness of oneself' ('seiner' could also be 'its/his'); 'Selbst' = 'self'.","'welche eine solche transscendentale Voraussetzung geltend machen soll': 'welche' could refer to 'Bedingung' or be read as relative to the whole clause; kept literal. 'geltend machen' rendered 'make valid' (gültig = valid).","'Bestimmungen' = determinations; 'Zustand' = state; 'Sinn' = sense; 'Data' = 'data'; 'Erfahrung' = experience.","'wird ... genant' has as subject 'das Bewustseyn' or 'Selbst' ambiguously; rendered 'it'."]],"krv-a-0232":["Now no cognitions can take place in us, no connection and unity of them with one another, without that unity of consciousness which precedes all data of intuitions, and in relation to which alone all representation of objects is possible. This pure original, unchangeable consciousness I will now name transcendental apperception. That it deserves this name is already evident from this: that even the purest objective unity, namely that of the concepts a priori (space and time), is possible only through the relation of the intuitions to it. The numerical unity of this apperception therefore lies a priori at the ground of all concepts just as much as the manifoldness of space and time lies at the ground of the intuitions of sensibility.",["'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Beziehung' = relation; 'Raum' = space; 'Zeit' = time; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Mannigfaltigkeit' = 'manifoldness' (distinct from Mannigfaltige = manifold).","'worauf in Beziehung, alle Vorstellung ... möglich ist' kept as 'in relation to which'.","'a priori' in the last sentence may attach to 'liegt' or to 'Begriffen'; left as in the German.","'seyn' in 'möglich seyn' (plural subject) is subjunctive/plural; rendered 'is possible' following the singular sense of 'Einheit'. 'sie' in 'auf sie' refers to 'Apperception' (implied).","'reineste obiective Einheit' = 'purest objective unity'."]],"krv-a-0233":["Yet precisely this transcendental unity of apperception makes out of all possible appearances, which can always be together in one experience, an interconnection of all these representations according to laws. For this unity of consciousness would be impossible if the mind in the cognition of the manifold could not become conscious of the identity of the function by which it combines the same synthetically in one cognition. Therefore the original and necessary consciousness of the identity of oneself is at the same time a consciousness of an equally necessary unity of the synthesis of all appearances according to concepts, i.e., according to rules, which not only make them necessarily reproducible but thereby also determine an object for their intuition, i.e., the concept of something in which they are necessarily connected: for the mind could not possibly think the identity of itself in the manifoldness of its representations, and indeed a priori, if it did not have before its eyes the identity of its action, which subjects all synthesis of apprehension (which is empirical) to a transcendental unity, and first makes possible their interconnection according to rules a priori. Henceforth we will also be able to determine our concepts of an object in general more correctly. All representations, as representations, have their object, and can themselves in turn be objects of other representations. Appearances are the only objects that can be given to us immediately, and that in them which relates immediately to the object is called intuition. Now, however, these appearances are not things in themselves, but are themselves only representations, which in turn have their object, which therefore can no longer be intuited by us, and may therefore be called the non-empirical, i.e., transcendental object = X.",["'Zusammenhang' = interconnection; 'zusammenhängen' rendered 'connected' (verb form of the same stem; no glossary entry); 'Verknüpfung' not used here.","'Gemüth' = mind (Gemüt); 'Geist' would also be 'mind' (not occurring here).","'dasselbe' (neuter) refers to 'Mannigfaltige'; 'sie' (feminine) in 'wodurch sie' refers to 'Gemüth' (grammatically neuter) or 'Function' — left as 'it'.","'Bewustseyn der Identität seiner selbst' = 'consciousness of the identity of oneself'; the German 'seiner selbst' is kept literal.","'unterwirst' (sic, for 'unterwirft') silently corrected; 'Handlung' = 'action'; 'Apprehension' = apprehension; 'vor Augen hätte' = 'had before its eyes'.","'konte ... denken' (past subjunctive) rendered 'could ... think'; 'a priori' in 'und zwar a priori' kept as a parenthetical emphasis.","'unmittelbar' = 'immediately'; 'angeschaut' = 'intuited' (anschauen, cf. Anschauung = intuition); 'Dinge an sich selbst' = 'things in themselves'; 'Gegenstand' = 'object' throughout."]],"krv-a-0234":["The pure concept of this transcendental object (which in all our cognitions is really always one and the same = X) is that which in all our empirical concepts in general can provide relation to an object, i.e., objective reality. Now this concept can contain no determinate intuition at all, and will therefore concern nothing other than that unity which must be encountered in a manifold of cognition insofar as it stands in relation to an object. This relation, however, is nothing other than the necessary unity of consciousness, hence also of the synthesis of the manifold through a common function of the mind, of combining it in one representation. Now since this unity must be regarded as necessary *a priori* (because the cognition would otherwise be without object), the relation to a transcendental object, i.e., the objective reality of our empirical cognition, will rest on the transcendental law that all appearances, insofar as objects are to be given to us through them, must stand under rules *a priori* of the synthetic unity of the same, in accordance with which their relation in empirical intuition alone is possible, i.e., that they must stand just as much in experience under conditions of the necessary unity of apperception as in mere intuition under the formal conditions of space and time, indeed that through those every cognition first becomes possible.",["Gegenstand and Obiect are both rendered 'object' (here only Gegenstand occurs).","Erkentniß = cognition; Beziehung and Verhältniß are both 'relation' (Verhältniß in 'ihr Verhältniß').","'derselben' (of the same) and 'iene' (those) and 'es' (it: the manifold or the cognition) are left with the same ambiguity as the German.","'wirklich' rendered 'really'; 'a priori' kept in Latin in italics."]],"krv-a-0235":["4. Preliminary explanation of the possibility of the categories, as cognitions *a priori*.",["Erklärung = explanation; Möglichkeit = possibility; Erkentnissen = cognitions."]],"krv-a-0236":["There is only one experience, in which all perceptions are represented as in thoroughgoing and lawlike interconnection: just as there is only one space and time, in which all forms of appearance and all relation of being or non-being take place. If one speaks of different experiences, they are only so many perceptions, insofar as these belong to one and the same universal experience. The thoroughgoing and synthetic unity of perceptions namely constitutes precisely the form of experience, and it is nothing other than the synthetic unity of appearances in accordance with concepts.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Wahrnehmung = perception; Erscheinung = appearance.","'nemlich' rendered 'namely' in its position in the sentence, following the German."]],"krv-a-0237":["Unity of synthesis in accordance with empirical concepts would be entirely contingent, and, if these were not grounded on a transcendental ground of unity, it would be possible that a swarm of appearances fill our soul without experience ever being able to come of it. But then all relation of cognition to objects would also drop away, because connection in accordance with universal and necessary laws would be lacking to it, hence it would indeed be thoughtless intuition, but never cognition, thus for us as much as nothing at all.",["Verknüpfung = connection; zufällig = contingent; Seele = soul (not Gemüth).","'ihr' and 'sie' (it) refer back with the same ambiguity as the German (the cognition / its relation)."]],"krv-a-0238":["The conditions *a priori* of a possible experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience. Now I assert: the categories just cited are nothing other than the conditions of thinking in a possible experience, just as space and time contain the conditions of intuition for that very same experience. Thus those are also basic concepts for thinking objects in general for the appearances, and thus have objective validity *a priori*; which was what we actually wanted to know.",["Obiecte and Gegenstände are both 'object(s)'; here Obiecte and 'obiective' occur.","Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; Gültigkeit = validity; behaupten = assert; wissen = know.","'zu den Erscheinungen' rendered 'for the appearances'; the German dative construction is left as open as possible."]],"krv-a-0239":["But the possibility, indeed even the necessity of these categories rests on the relation that the entire sensibility, and with it also all possible appearances, have to original apperception, in which everything must necessarily be in accord with the conditions of the thoroughgoing unity of self-consciousness, i.e., must stand under universal functions of synthesis, namely of synthesis in accordance with concepts, as that in which alone apperception can prove its thoroughgoing and necessary identity *a priori*. Thus the concept of a cause is nothing other than a synthesis (of that which follows in the time-series, with other appearances) in accordance with concepts, and without such a unity, which has its rule *a priori*, and which subjects the appearances to itself, thoroughgoing and universal, hence necessary unity of consciousness would not be encountered in the manifold of perceptions. These would then, however, also not belong to any experience, consequently be without object, and nothing but a blind play of representations, i.e., less than a dream.",["Selbstbewustseyn = self-consciousness; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Vorstellung = representation.","'dessen' (of that which) and 'Diese' (These: the perceptions, or possibly the appearances) are kept as in the German.","'die Erscheinungen sich unterwirft': 'subjects the appearances to itself' (the relative clause's antecedent is 'Einheit').","Warnehmungen (sic) = perceptions."]],"krv-a-0240":["All attempts to derive those pure concepts of the understanding from experience, and to ascribe to them a merely empirical origin, are therefore entirely vain and futile. I will mention nothing of the fact that e.g. the concept of a cause carries with it the trait of necessity, which no experience can give, which teaches us indeed that something else follows upon an appearance in the usual way, but not that it must follow upon it necessarily, nor that *a priori* and quite universally a conclusion could be drawn from it as a condition to the sequel. But that empirical rule of association, which one must still assume throughout when one says that everything in the sequence of occurrences stands under rules to such an extent that nothing ever happens before which something does not precede, upon which it always follows: this, as a law of nature, on what does it rest, I ask? and how is even this association possible? The ground of the possibility of the association of the manifold, insofar as it lies in the object, is called the affinity of the manifold. I ask, therefore, how do you make the thoroughgoing affinity of appearances (through which they stand under constant laws, and must belong under them) comprehensible?",["Folge = sequel ('auf die Folge'); the verb 'folgen' = follow; Reihenfolge = 'sequence' (in the glossary Folge is also 'sequence').","Begebenheit = occurrence; Gesetz = law; Regel = rule; Begriff = concept; begreiflich = comprehensible (cf. begreifen = comprehend).","'geschlossen werden' = 'a conclusion could be drawn' (schließen); 'Gewöhnlicher Maassen' = 'in the usual way'.","'dieses' and 'darauf' left with the German reference as open as possible."]],"krv-a-0241":["According to my principles it is very well comprehensible. All possible appearances belong, as representations, to the whole possible self-consciousness. From this, however, as a transcendental representation, numerical identity is inseparable, and certain *a priori*, because nothing can come into the cognition except by means of this original apperception. Now since this identity must necessarily enter into the synthesis of everything manifold of appearances, insofar as it is to become empirical cognition, the appearances are subjected to conditions *a priori*, with which their synthesis (of apprehension) must thoroughgoingly accord. Now, however, the representation of a universal condition in accordance with which a certain manifold (hence in one and the same way) can be posited, is called a rule, and if it must be so posited, a law. Thus all appearances stand in a thoroughgoing connection in accordance with necessary laws, and hence in a transcendental affinity, of which the empirical is the mere sequel.",["Grundsätze = principles; setzen = posit ('gesezt'); Verknüpfung = connection.","'Folge' = sequel; 'das Erkentniß' (neuter) = the cognition.","'Nun heißt aber': 'Now, however, ... is called'."]],"krv-a-0242":["That nature should direct itself according to our subjective ground of apperception, indeed even depend on it with regard to its lawfulness, sounds surely very absurd and strange. But if one considers that this nature is in itself nothing but a sum total of appearances, hence not a thing in itself, but merely a multitude of representations of the mind, one will not be surprised to see it merely in the radical faculty of all our cognition, namely transcendental apperception, in that unity for the sake of which alone it can be called object of all possible experience, i.e., nature; and that we also precisely for that reason can cognize this unity *a priori*, hence also as necessary, which we would surely have to leave by the wayside, were it given in itself independently of the first sources of our thinking. For then I would not know where we should take the synthetic propositions of such a universal unity of nature from, because in such a case one would have to borrow them from the objects of nature itself. But since this could happen only empirically: no other than a merely contingent unity could be drawn from it, which, however, falls far short of the necessary interconnection that one means when one calls nature",["Passage breaks off without final punctuation in the source; left as it stands.","Inbegriff = sum total; Menge = multitude; Ding an sich = thing in itself; Radicalvermögen = radical faculty; Gemüth = mind.","Gegenstände and Obiect both 'object'; Sätze = propositions; Zusammenhang = interconnection; zufällig = contingent.","'widersinnisch' rendered 'absurd'; 'unterwegens lassen' rendered literally as 'leave by the wayside'; 'sie' in 'sie Obiect' refers to nature, and 'ihn/sie' ambiguity is as in the German."]],"krv-a-0243":["Third Section of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. On the relation of the understanding to objects in general and the possibility of cognizing these *a priori*.",["Verstand = understanding; Verhältnis = relation; Gegenstände = objects; erkennen = cognize.","'diese' (these) left ambiguous between objects and, by extension, as in the German."]],"krv-a-0244":["What we separated out and presented singly in the previous section we now want to present united and in interconnection. There are three subjective sources of cognition on which the possibility of an experience in general, and cognition of its objects, rests: sense, imagination, and apperception; each of these can be considered as empirical, namely in the application to given appearances, but all are also elements or foundations *a priori*, which make this empirical use itself possible. Sense represents the appearances empirically in perception, imagination in association (and reproduction), apperception in the empirical consciousness of the identity of these reproductive representations with the appearances through which they were given, hence in recognition.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Erkentniß(quellen) = cognition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Sinn = sense; Wahrnehmung = perception; Bewustseyn = consciousness.","Association, Reproduction, Recognition, Apperception are Latinate words in the German; rendered as plain English cognates. Gebrauch = use; Anwendung = application.","'derselben' (after 'Gegenstände') could refer to Erfahrung or Erkentniß; the English 'its' is likewise open, though it leans to 'experience'."]],"krv-a-0245":["But there lies at the ground of all perception pure intuition (with regard to it as representation the form of inner intuition, time,), of association the pure synthesis of imagination, and of empirical consciousness pure apperception, i.e., the thoroughgoing identity of itself in all possible representations, *a priori*.",["'ihrer' in 'in Ansehung ihrer als Vorstellung' is left ambiguous (it could refer to Anschauung or to Wahrnehmung); 'seiner selbst' is rendered 'of itself' and could also be 'of oneself'.","'a priori' stands last in the German and may attach to 'lies at the ground' or to the items listed; the English keeps this position. Grund = ground; Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation.","The German has a comma before the closing parenthesis after 'Zeit'; kept."]],"krv-a-0246":["If we now want to pursue the inner ground of this connection of representations to that point in which they must all run together in order first of all to obtain unity of cognition for a possible experience, then we must begin from pure apperception. All intuitions are nothing for us, and do not concern us in the least, if they cannot be taken up into consciousness, whether they now flow in upon it directly or indirectly, and only through this alone is cognition possible. We are conscious *a priori* of the thoroughgoing identity of ourselves with regard to all representations that can ever belong to our cognition, as a necessary condition of the possibility of all representations, (because these in me represent something only in that they belong with everything else to one consciousness, hence at least must be able to be connected in it). This principle stands firm *a priori*, and can be called the transcendental principle of the unity of everything manifold of our representations (hence also in intuition). Now the unity of the manifold in a subject is synthetic: thus pure apperception provides a *principium* of the synthetic unity of the manifold in all possible intuition.*)",["Verknüpfung = connection (the verb verknüpfen = connect); Verbindung would be 'combination'. Princip and Principium both 'principle'; Principium kept in Latin and italicized as printed in roman type.","'darauf' (einfliessen) and 'dieses' (in 'durch dieses allein') are ambiguous in the German; 'upon it' and 'this' are kept as open as the original.","'allererst' = 'first of all'; 'an die Hand geben' rendered 'provides'. 'vor uns' = 'for us'. The footnote marker *) is kept where it stands."]],"krv-a-0247":["*) One should pay good heed to this proposition, which is of great importance. All representations have a necessary relation to a possible empirical consciousness: for if they did not have this, and it were entirely impossible to become conscious of them, that would be as much as to say they did not exist at all. All empirical consciousness, however, has a necessary relation to a transcendental consciousness (preceding all particular experience), namely the consciousness of myself, as the original apperception. It is therefore absolutely necessary that in my cognition all consciousness belong to one consciousness (of myself). Here now is a synthetic unity of the manifold, (of consciousness) which is cognized *a priori*, and provides precisely so the ground for synthetic propositions *a priori* that concern pure thinking, as space and time do for such propositions that concern the form of mere intuition. The synthetic proposition: that all different empirical consciousness must be combined in a single self-consciousness, is the absolutely first and synthetic principle of our thinking in general. It is not to be left out of account, however, that the mere representation I in relation to all others (whose collective unity it makes possible) is the transcendental consciousness. Whether this representation is now clear (empirical consciousness) or obscure, is of no concern here, indeed not even the actuality of it; but rather the possibility of the logical form of all cognition rests necessarily on the relation to this apperception as a faculty.",["Satz = proposition (in 'synthetischen Sätzen' and 'Der synthetische Satz'); Grundsatz = principle. Beziehung and Verhältniß are both 'relation'.","'transsendentale' (typo for transscendentale) silently corrected. Wirklichkeit = actuality; Vermögen = faculty; besondern = particular.","'verbunden' (combined) vs. 'verknüpft' (connected) elsewhere in this section. 'a priori erkant wird' could attach to the unity or to the manifold; the English preserves 'is cognized *a priori*'. 'wol' omitted? no: 'gebe ... wol acht' rendered 'pay good heed'."]],"krv-a-0248":["This synthetic unity, however, presupposes a synthesis or includes it, and if the former is to be necessary *a priori*, then the latter must also be a synthesis *a priori*. Thus the transc. unity of apperception relates to the pure synthesis of imagination, as a condition *a priori* of the possibility of all composition of the manifold in one cognition. Only the productive synthesis of imagination, however, can take place *a priori*; for the reproductive rests on conditions of experience. Thus the *principium* of the necessary unity of the pure (productive) synthesis of imagination before apperception is the ground of the possibility of all cognition, especially of experience.",["'transsc.' is the abbreviation in the text, rendered 'transc.'. Zusammensetzung = composition.","'vor der Apperception' could mean 'prior to' or 'in front of/before' apperception, and could attach to 'Principium' or 'Einheit' or 'Synthesis'; 'before apperception' is left as ambiguous as the German.","'iene' / 'leztere' = 'the former' / 'the latter'; the antecedents are as in the German."]],"krv-a-0249":["Now we call the synthesis of the manifold in imagination transcendental if, without distinction of the intuitions, it goes to nothing except merely the combination of the manifold *a priori*, and the unity of this synthesis is called transcendental if it is represented as necessary *a priori* in relation to the original unity of apperception. Since this latter now lies at the ground of the possibility of all cognitions, the transcendental unity of the synthesis of imagination is the pure form of all possible cognition, through which hence all objects of possible experience must be represented *a priori*.",["Verbindung = combination; Erkentnisse (plural) = cognitions; Gegenstände = objects.","'Da diese leztere' = 'this latter', antecedent (apperception/original unity) left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0250":["The unity of apperception in relation to the synthesis of imagination is the understanding, and the very same unity, relatively to the transcendental synthesis of imagination, the pure understanding. Thus there are in the understanding pure cognitions *a priori*, which contain the necessary unity of the pure synthesis of imagination with regard to all possible appearances. These, however, are the categories, i.e., pure concepts of the understanding, consequently the empirical cognitive power of the human being necessarily contains an understanding, which relates to all objects of the senses, though only by means of intuition and the synthesis of it through imagination, under which therefore all appearances stand as *data* for a possible experience. Since now this relation of appearances to possible experience is likewise necessary, (because without this we would get no cognition at all through them, and they would hence not concern us at all) it follows that the pure understanding, by means of the categories, is a formal and synthetic *principium* of all experiences, and the appearances have a necessary relation to the understanding.",["Erkentnißkraft = 'cognitive power' (Kraft = power); Verstand = understanding; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; vermittelst = by means of.","'unter welchen' has no unambiguous antecedent (Gegenstände / Sinne / Verstand / Synthesis); 'under which' is kept ambiguous. 'dieser' in '(ohne diese ...)' likewise open ('this').","Data is a Latin word in the German, italicized; Principium kept in Latin. Verhältniß and Beziehung both 'relation'."]],"krv-a-0251":["Now we want to lay before our eyes the necessary interconnection of the understanding with the appearances by means of the categories, by beginning from below, namely with the empirical. The first thing that is given to us is appearance, which, if it is combined with consciousness, is called perception, (without the relation to a, at least possible, consciousness, appearance could never become an object of cognition for us, and thus be nothing for us, and since it has in itself no objective reality, and exists only in cognition, be nothing at all.) But because every appearance contains a manifold, hence different perceptions are encountered in the mind in themselves scattered and single, a combination of them is necessary, which they cannot have in sense itself. There is therefore in us an active faculty of the synthesis of this manifold, which we call imagination and whose action exercised immediately on the perceptions I call apprehension*). Imagination is, namely, supposed to bring the manifold of intuition into an image; beforehand it must therefore take the impressions up into its activity, i.e., apprehend.",["Gemüth = mind; Sinn = sense; Eindrücke = impressions; Realität = reality; Handlung = action; Bild = image; an sich = in itself/themselves.","'an sich zerstreuet' could attach to 'Wahrnehmungen' (in themselves) or to the verb phrase; kept in the German position. 'nemlich' rendered 'namely' throughout.","The parenthesis is unclosed-in-sense: the clause 'und ... überall nichts seyn' depends on 'würde ... werden können'; preserved as one sentence. Apprehension/apprehendiren are Latinate words in the German."]],"krv-a-0252":["*) That imagination is a necessary ingredient of perception itself, no psychologist has probably yet thought of. That comes from this, that one partly limited this faculty merely to reproductions, partly because one believed that the senses delivered to us not only impressions, but also even put them together, and brought about images of objects, for which without doubt, apart from the receptivity of impressions, something more, namely a function of the synthesis of them, is required.",["'wol' = 'probably' (could also be read 'indeed'); 'Ingredienz' = 'ingredient'. Empfänglichkeit = receptivity; Function and Synthesis are Latinate in the German.","The construction 'theils ... theils, weil' is irregular in the German and is kept irregular in English ('partly ... partly because')."]],"krv-a-0253":["But it is clear that even this apprehension of the manifold alone would still produce no image and no interconnection of the impressions, if there were not a subjective ground for calling a perception, from which the mind has passed over to another, over to the ensuing ones, and thus for presenting whole series of them, i.e., a reproductive faculty of imagination, which then is also only empirical.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Eindruck = impression; Wahrnehmung = perception; Gemüth = mind (Gemüt); Einbildungskraft = imagination; Vermögen = faculty; Grund = ground.","'Apprehension' kept as the Latinate form of the German Apprehension; 'subiectiv' rendered 'subjective'.","'eine Wahrnehmung ... zu den nachfolgenden herüber zu rufen': the German is awkward (singular 'eine', plural 'den nachfolgenden'); preserved. 'Bild' = image."]],"krv-a-0254":["But since, if representations, as they happen to come together, reproduced one another without distinction, again no determinate interconnection of them, but merely rule-less heaps of them, hence no cognition at all would arise; so the reproduction of them must have a rule, in accordance with which a representation enters into combination in the imagination rather with this one than with another. This subjective and empirical ground of the reproduction in accordance with rules is called the association of representations.",["Erkentniß = cognition (Erkenntnis); Verbindung = combination; Vorstellung = representation; bestimmt = determinate.","The construction 'Weil aber, wenn ... würde; so muß' is kept as a single sentence with its semicolon; 'mithin' = 'hence'.","'nent' = nennt (original spelling modernized silently)."]],"krv-a-0255":["Now if this unity of association did not also have an objective ground, so that it would be impossible for appearances to be apprehended by the imagination otherwise than under the condition of a possible synthetic unity of this apprehension, then it would also be something entirely contingent that appearances fit into an interconnection of human cognitions. For, even though we had the faculty to associate perceptions; it would still remain in itself entirely undetermined and contingent whether they were also associable; and in the case that they were not, a multitude of perceptions, and indeed perhaps an entire sensibility, would be possible, in which much empirical consciousness would be encountered in my mind, but separated, and without its belonging to a consciousness of myself, which however is impossible. For only by my numbering all perceptions to one consciousness (of original apperception) can I say of all perceptions that I am conscious of them. There must therefore be an objective ground, i.e., one to be seen a priori before all empirical laws of the imagination, on which rests the possibility, indeed even the necessity of a law extending through all appearances, namely of regarding them throughout as such data of the senses which are in themselves associable, and subject to universal rules of a thoroughgoing connection in reproduction. This objective ground of all association of appearances I call their affinity. But we can encounter this nowhere else than in the principle of the unity of apperception, with regard to all cognitions that are to belong to me. In accordance with this, all appearances must come into the mind, or be apprehended, in such a way that they agree together with the unity of apperception, which without synthetic unity in their connection, which is therefore also objectively necessary, would be impossible.",["Erscheinung = appearance; Bewustseyn = consciousness (Bewußtsein); Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Grundsatz = principle; Gesetz = law; Obiect-/obiectiv = objective.","Parenthesis '(der ursprünglichen Apperception)' is rendered 'of original apperception' (the parenthesis is retained); 'zehle' = zähle, 'numbering'.","'ob sie auch associabel wären': 'auch' rendered 'also'. 'Data' kept as in the German. 'ohne daß es zu einem Bewustseyn meiner selbst gehörete': 'es' ('it') has no clear antecedent (empirisches Bewustseyn); preserved.","'welches ... unmöglich seyn würde' (end): 'welches' could refer to the agreement or to the whole preceding clause; left ambiguous. 'Nach diesem' = 'in accordance with this' (the principle).","'einzusehender' = 'to be seen' (einsehen), i.e. 'insight'; 'a priori' may attach to 'einzusehender' or to 'Grund'; left ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0256":["The objective unity of all (empirical) consciousness in one consciousness (of original apperception) is therefore the necessary condition even of all possible perception, and the affinity of all appearances (near or remote) is a necessary consequence of a synthesis in the imagination that is grounded a priori on rules.",["Bedingung = condition; Folge = sequel/consequence here rendered 'consequence' (glossary: Folge = sequel; Folgerung = consequence) — note: 'Folge' rendered 'consequence' for idiom.","'a priori' may modify 'gegründet' or 'Synthesis'; left ambiguous as in the German. 'so gar' = 'even'."]],"krv-a-0257":["The imagination is therefore also a faculty of a synthesis a priori, for which reason we give it the name of productive imagination, and, insofar as with regard to all the manifold of appearance it has nothing further as its aim than the necessary unity in the synthesis of it, this can be called the transcendental function of the imagination. It is therefore indeed strange, but from what has gone before nevertheless evident, that only by means of this transcendental function of the imagination even the affinity of appearances, with it the association and through this finally the reproduction in accordance with laws, consequently experience itself becomes possible: because without it no concepts of objects would flow together into one experience.",["Absicht = aim; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Begriff = concept; Gegenstand = object (Obiect elsewhere also 'object'); Erfahrung = experience; Gesetz = law.","'ihr' / 'sie' / 'diese' reference chain ('sie ... ihrer Absicht ... diese') kept as in German; 'derselben' = 'of it' (the appearance's manifold or the imagination's — ambiguous).","'folglich' = 'consequently'; 'transscendental' modernized to 'transcendental'."]],"krv-a-0258":["For the abiding and enduring I (of pure apperception) constitutes the correlate of all our representations, insofar as it is merely possible to become conscious of them, and all consciousness belongs just as much to an all-embracing pure apperception, as all sensible intuition as representation belongs to a pure inner intuition, namely time. This apperception it is now that must be added to the pure imagination in order to make its function intellectual. For in itself the synthesis of the imagination, although exercised a priori, is nevertheless always sensible, because it combines the manifold only as it appears in intuition, e.g., the shape of a triangle. Through the relation of the manifold to the unity of apperception, however, concepts come about, which belong to the understanding, but can come about only by means of the imagination in relation to sensible intuition.",["Anschauung = intuition; sinnlich = sensible; Verstand = understanding; Verhältniß = relation; Beziehung = relation (two German words, one English word); verbinden = combine.","'stehende und bleibende' = 'abiding and enduring' (not 'persist', as the glossary's beharren is not used). 'intellectuel' = 'intellectual'.","'Gestalt eines Triangels' = 'shape of a triangle'. 'Correlatum' kept as 'correlate'."]],"krv-a-0259":["We therefore have a pure imagination, as a basic faculty of the human soul, that lies at the ground of all cognition a priori. By means of it we bring the manifold of intuition on the one side, and the condition of the necessary unity of pure apperception on the other, into combination. Both extreme ends, namely sensibility and understanding, must necessarily cohere by means of this transcendental function of the imagination; because otherwise the former would indeed give appearances, but no objects of an empirical cognition, hence no experience. The actual experience, which consists of the apprehension, the association, (the reproduction,) finally the recognition of appearances, contains in the last and highest (of the merely empirical elements of experience) concepts, which make possible the formal unity of experience, and with it all objective validity (truth) of empirical cognition. These grounds of the recognition of the manifold, insofar as they concern merely the form of an experience in general, are now those categories. On them, therefore, is grounded all formal unity in the synthesis of the imagination, and by means of this also all empirical use of it (in recognition, reproduction, association, apprehension) down to the appearances, because these can belong to our consciousness and hence to ourselves only by means of those elements of cognition.",["Grundvermögen = basic faculty; Seele = soul; Grund = ground; Gültigkeit = validity; Gebrauch = use; Recognition = recognition (Latinate; Erkennen = cognize elsewhere); Form = form.","'zusammenhängen' = 'cohere' (cognate with Zusammenhang = interconnection). 'iene' = 'the former' (Sinnlichkeit) for the pronoun's reference: the German 'iene' is ambiguous in principle ('that one'); rendered 'the former'.","'der letzteren und höchsten': the antecedent is the recognition; 'der blos empirischen Elemente' parenthesis kept. 'derselben' in 'empirischen Gebrauchs derselben' may refer to the synthesis or the imagination; left ambiguous.","'Verbindung' = 'combination' (combinatio); 'Erkentniß' = cognition."]],"krv-a-0260":["The order and regularity in the appearances, which we call nature, we ourselves bring into them, and would also not be able to find them there, had we not, or the nature of our mind, originally put them there. For this unity of nature is supposed to be a necessary, i.e., a priori certain unity of the connection of appearances. But how should we ever be able to get a synthetic unity on the track a priori, if subjective grounds of such unity a priori were not contained in the original sources of cognition of our mind, and if these subjective conditions were not at the same time objectively valid, in that they are the grounds of the possibility of cognizing an object in experience at all.",["Gemüth = mind; Natureinheit = unity of nature; Erkenntnißquellen = sources of cognition; erkennen = cognize; Obiect = object; gültig = valid.","'auf die Bahn bringen' = 'get on the track', idiom kept literal. 'a priori' after 'Einheit' / 'können' / 'enthalten' may attach to either verb or noun in 'synthetische Einheit a priori'; left as in German.","'hätten wir sie nicht, oder die Natur unseres Gemüths ursprünglich hineingelegt': syntax preserved ('we ... or the nature of our mind'); the final clause ends with a period where the German has a period though syntactically a question."]],"krv-a-0261":["We have above explained the understanding in manifold ways: through a spontaneity of cognition (in contrast to the receptivity of sensibility), through a faculty for thinking, or also a faculty of concepts, or also of judgments, which explanations, if one looks at them in the light, come down to one. Now we can characterize it as the faculty of rules. This identifying mark is more fruitful and approaches nearer to its essence. Sensibility gives us forms (of intuition), but the understanding rules. The latter is at all times busy scrutinizing the appearances with the aim of finding some rule in them. Rules, insofar as they are objective (hence necessarily attach to the cognition of the object), are called laws. Although we learn many laws through experience, these are yet only particular determinations of still higher laws, among which the highest (under which all others stand) come *a priori* from the understanding itself, and are not borrowed from experience, but rather must provide the appearances with their lawfulness, and precisely thereby make experience possible. Thus the understanding is not merely a faculty for making rules for itself through comparison of the appearances: it is itself the legislation for nature, i.e., without understanding there would not be nature at all, i.e., synthetic unity of the manifold of appearances in accordance with rules: for appearances, as such, cannot take place outside us, but exist only in our sensibility. This, however, as object of cognition in an experience, with everything it may contain, is possible only in the unity of apperception. The unity of apperception, however, is the transcendental ground of the necessary lawfulness of all appearances in an experience. This very same unity of apperception with regard to a manifold of representations (namely to determine it from a single one) is the rule, and the faculty of these rules is the understanding. All appearances therefore lie, as possible experiences, just as *a priori* in the understanding, and receive their formal possibility from it, as they lie, as mere intuitions, in sensibility, and through it are possible, as to form, alone.",["Fixed: 'Sinnlichkeit giebt uns Formen, (der Anschauung) der Verstand aber Regeln' — the German has no verb after 'der Verstand aber Regeln'; the English supplies 'gives' by ellipsis, which I left unmarked, so strictly the sentence reads 'but the understanding rules' only if 'rules' is taken as verb. Read 'Regeln' as the noun: 'but the understanding [gives] rules.'","Kennzeichen (not Merkmal, 'mark') rendered 'identifying mark' to keep it distinct from Merkmal.","Erklärung/erklärt: 'explained', 'explanations' (glossary also allows 'definition').","'Diese aber, als Gegenstand der Erkentniß': 'Diese' could refer to Sinnlichkeit or to Erscheinungen (collective); left as 'This'.","'der Form nach, allein möglich': 'allein' may modify 'möglich' ('possible alone') or 'der Form nach'; left ambiguous.","Gegenstand = 'object'. 'vor die Natur' (= für) rendered 'for nature'. 'Gesetzmäßigkeit' = 'lawfulness'; 'Gesetzgebung' = 'legislation'."]],"krv-a-0262":["However exaggerated, however absurd it may sound to say: the understanding is itself the source of the laws of nature, and hence of the formal unity of nature, just as correct, and appropriate to the object, namely experience, is such an assertion nonetheless. To be sure, empirical laws, as such, can in no way derive their origin from the pure understanding, just as little as the immeasurable manifoldness of appearances can be sufficiently comprehended from the pure form of sensible intuition. But all empirical laws are only particular determinations of the pure laws of the understanding, under which and according to whose norm the former are first possible, and the appearances assume a lawful form, just as also all appearances, regardless of the diversity of their empirical form, must nevertheless at all times be in accord with the conditions of the pure form of sensibility.",["Behauptung = 'assertion'; begreifen = 'comprehend'; Mannigfaltigkeit = 'manifoldness' (cf. Mannigfaltige, 'manifold').","'iene' ('the former') could refer to the empirical laws or to the pure laws' counterpart; I take it as empirical laws; German is compressed.","'unter welchen und nach deren Norm': 'welchen' and 'deren' both refer to the pure laws."]],"krv-a-0263":["The pure understanding is therefore in the categories the law of the synthetic unity of all appearances, and thereby first and originally makes experience possible as to its form. But we had no more to accomplish in the transc. deduction of the categories than to make comprehensible this relation of the understanding to sensibility, and by means of it to all objects of experience, hence the objective validity of its pure concepts *a priori*, and thereby to establish their origin and truth",["Passage ends without a period in the source text; left as is.","'transsc.' is Kant's abbreviation; rendered 'transc.'","begreiflich machen = 'make comprehensible' (begreifen = comprehend); Gültigkeit = 'validity'."]],"krv-a-0264":["Summary representation of the correctness and sole possibility of this deduction of the pure concepts of the understanding.",["Vorstellung = 'representation'; Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'."]],"krv-a-0265":["Were the objects with which our cognition has to do things in themselves, we could have no concepts *a priori* of them at all. For whence should we take them? If we take them from the object (without investigating here once more how this could become known to us) then our concepts would be merely empirical, and no concepts *a priori*. If we take them from ourselves, then what is merely in us cannot determine the constitution of an object distinct from our representations, i.e., be a ground why there should be a thing to which something such as we have in thought pertains, and not rather all this representation be empty. By contrast, if we have to do everywhere only with appearances, then it is not only possible but also necessary that certain concepts *a priori* precede the empirical cognition of objects. For as appearances they make up an object that is merely in us, because a mere modification of our sensibility is not encountered outside us at all. Now this very representation: that all these appearances, hence all objects with which we can occupy ourselves, are, all together, in me, i.e., determinations of my identical self, expresses a thoroughgoing unity of them in one and the same apperception as necessary. But in this unity of possible consciousness there also consists the form of all cognition of objects (through which the manifold, as belonging to one object, is thought). Thus the way in which the manifold of sensible representation (intuition) belongs to one consciousness precedes all cognition of the object, as the intellectual form of the latter, and itself makes up a formal cognition of all objects *a priori* in general, insofar as they are thought (categories). The synthesis of them through pure imagination, the unity of all representations in relation to the original apperception, precede all empirical cognition. Pure concepts of the understanding are therefore possible *a priori* only for this reason, indeed even necessary in relation to experience, because our cognition has to do with nothing but appearances, whose possibility lies in ourselves, whose connection and unity (in the representation of an object) is encountered merely in us, hence must precede all experience, and make this first possible as to form. And on this ground, the only possible one among all, our deduction of the categories has also been conducted.",["Object (Latinate) and Gegenstand both rendered 'object'; 'Obiect' occurs once ('vom Obiect').","Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Bewustseyn = 'consciousness'; Einbildungskraft = 'imagination'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'.","'Die Synthesis derselben ... gehen': plural verb after two subjects; 'derselben' (of them) could refer to the manifold or the representations; left as 'of them'.","'mithin vor aller Erfahrung vorhergehen, und diese ... möglich machen muß': the subject of 'vorhergehen' is ambiguous (appearances, or connection and unity); the German has 'muß' governing both verbs; I follow the German wording, with 'must' covering both.","'nur darum a priori möglich, ia gar ... nothwendig, weil': the 'weil' clause attaches to both predicates; kept.","'Grund' = 'ground'; 'Erkentniß' singular in 'unser Erkentniß' (neuter, older usage) rendered 'our cognition'."]],"krv-a-0266":["Second Book of the Transcendental Analytic. The Analytic of Principles.",["Grundsätze = 'principles'."]],"krv-a-0267":["General logic is built upon a plan that coincides quite exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are: understanding, power of judgment, and reason. That doctrine therefore deals, in its analytic, with concepts, judgments, and inferences, precisely in accordance with the functions and the order of those powers of the mind that one comprehends under the broad term of understanding in general.",["Urtheilskraft = 'power of judgment'; Gemüthskräfte = 'powers of the mind' (Gemüt = mind, Kraft = power); Schluß = 'inference'; Benennung = 'term'; Doctrin rendered 'doctrine'.","Erkentnißvermögen = 'faculties of cognition'; Grundrisse = 'plan' (literally ground-plan)."]],"krv-a-0268":["Since the aforementioned merely formal logic abstracts from all content of cognition (whether it be pure or empirical) and occupies itself merely with the form of thinking (of discursive cognition) in general: it can in its analytic part also include the canon for reason, whose form has its secure precept, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition used therein, can be seen *a priori*, through mere dissection of the actions of reason into their moments.",["'Canon' is Kant's Latinate/Greek term, kept; 'Moment' rendered 'moments'.","'die ... eingesehen werden kan': the relative 'die' may refer to 'Vorschrift' (precept) or to 'Form'; left as 'which' after 'precept'.","'Vorschrift' = 'precept'; 'Zergliederung' = 'dissection'; 'besondere' = 'particular'."]],"krv-a-0269":["Transcendental logic, since it is limited to a determinate content, namely merely of pure cognitions *a priori*, cannot imitate it in this division. For it shows: that the transcendental use of reason is not at all objectively valid, hence does not belong to the logic of truth, i.e., the analytic, but, as a logic of illusion, requires a particular part of the scholastic doctrinal edifice, under the name of transcendental dialectic.",["'es zeigt sich' is impersonal/reflexive ('it shows itself'); rendered 'it shows'.","'ihr' in 'nachthun' refers to general logic ('it' = formal logic); 'es' ('cannot imitate it') is kept as in the German.","Schein = 'illusion'; Gebrauch = 'use'; Lehrgebäude = 'doctrinal edifice'; bestimmt = 'determinate'; besonder = 'particular'."]],"krv-a-0270":["Understanding and power of judgment accordingly have their canon of the objectively valid, hence true use in transcendental logic, and therefore belong in its analytic part. But reason in its attempts to settle something about objects *a priori*, and to expand cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience, is entirely and utterly dialectical, and its illusory assertions do not fit at all into a canon, such as the analytic is supposed to contain.",["Scheinbehauptungen = 'illusory assertions' (Schein = illusion, behaupten = assert).","'über Gegenstände a priori etwas auszumachen': 'a priori' may attach to 'auszumachen' (settle a priori) or to 'Gegenstände'; left ambiguous in the English.","Grenzen = 'bounds'; erweitern = 'expand'; 'dergleichen' rendered 'such as'."]],"krv-a-0271":["The analytic of principles will accordingly be merely a *canon* for the power of judgment, which teaches it to apply to appearances the concepts of the understanding, which contain the condition for rules *a priori*. For this reason, in taking the actual principles of the understanding as my theme, I will make use of the term of a doctrine of the power of judgment, by which this business is designated more precisely.",["Urtheilskraft = power of judgment throughout; Benennung = term; Doctrin = doctrine; Geschäfte = business; Grundsätze = principles.","*Canon* is the German's own Latin-derived word (Canon), kept as such."]],"krv-a-0272":["Introduction. On the Transcendental Power of Judgment in General.",[]],"krv-a-0273":["If the understanding in general is explained as the faculty of rules, then the power of judgment is the faculty of subsuming under rules, i.e., of distinguishing whether something stands under a given rule (*casus datae legis*) or not. General logic contains no precepts at all for the power of judgment, and also cannot contain them. For since it abstracts from all content of cognition, nothing remains to it except the business of analytically setting apart the mere form of cognition in concepts, judgments and inferences, and thereby bringing about formal rules of all use of the understanding. If it now wanted to show generally how one should subsume under these rules, i.e., distinguish whether something stands under them or not, this could not happen otherwise than again through a rule. This, however, just because it is a rule, requires anew an instruction of the power of judgment, and thus it shows that although the understanding is capable of a teaching and equipping through rules, the power of judgment is a special talent, which does not want to be taught at all, but only exercised. Hence this is also what is specific to so-called mother wit, the lack of which no school can replace, because, although this can offer to a limited understanding rules in abundance, borrowed from another's insight, and as it were graft them on; yet the faculty of making correct use of them must belong to the apprentice himself, and no rule that one might prescribe to him with this intention is, in the absence of such a natural gift, safe from misuse.*) A physician therefore, a judge, or a statesman can have in his head many fine pathological, juridical or political rules, to the degree that he himself can become a thorough teacher of them, and will nevertheless easily stumble in the application of them, either because he lacks natural power of judgment (although not understanding), and although he can see the universal *in abstracto*, cannot distinguish whether a case *in concreto* belongs under it, or also because he has not been sufficiently trained for this judgment through examples and actual business. This is also the sole and great benefit of examples: that they sharpen the power of judgment. For as regards the correctness and precision of insight of the understanding, they rather commonly do it some damage, because only rarely do they fulfill the condition of the rule adequately (as *casus in terminis*), and moreover often weaken that effort of the understanding to have insight into rules in generality, and independently of the particular circumstances of experience, according to their sufficiency, and accustom one therefore in the end to use them more like formulas than as principles. Thus examples are the go-cart of the power of judgment, which he who lacks the natural talent for it can never do without.",["The asterisk-parenthesis marks the footnote reference; the footnote text is passage krv-a-0274.","Belehrung / belehrt = teaching / taught; Unterweisung = instruction; Vorschriften = precepts; Lehrling = apprentice.","einsehen / Einsicht = have insight into / insight; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency (the glossary's zureichend is also rendered sufficient).","Schule: the pronoun diese in 'weil, ob diese gleich…' refers back to Schule; the sentence's construction (weil … ob … gleich … so muß doch) is kept as in the German, with the semicolon.","Latin terms *casus datae legis*, *in abstracto*, *in concreto*, *casus in terminis* are Kant's own."]],"krv-a-0274":["*) The lack of power of judgment is properly what one calls stupidity, and such an infirmity is not to be remedied at all. A dull or limited head, which lacks nothing except the proper degree of understanding and its own concepts thereof, can very well be equipped through learning, even up to erudition. But since it usually then also tends to be lacking in that (the *secunda Petri*), it is nothing unusual to encounter very learned men who, in the use of their science, frequently let that never-to-be-improved lack be seen.",["'eigenen Begriffen desselben': desselben could refer to Verstandes (as rendered) or to the head's own; kept as 'thereof'.","ienem = 'that', referring back to the power of judgment (Urtheilskraft); not replaced by a noun.","Wissenschaft = science; Gebrauch = use; Gelehrsamkeit = erudition. *secunda Petri* is Kant's Latin."]],"krv-a-0275":["But although general logic can give no precepts to the power of judgment, it is nevertheless quite otherwise with the transcendental, so much so that it seems that the latter has as its proper business to correct and to secure the power of judgment, through determinate rules, in the use of the pure understanding. For, in order to procure for the understanding expansion in the field of pure cognitions *a priori*, hence as doctrine, philosophy seems not to be necessary at all, or rather ill-applied, because after all the attempts hitherto little or no land has been gained with it, but as critique, in order to prevent the missteps of the power of judgment (*lapsus iudicii*) in the use of the few pure concepts of the understanding that we have, for that (although the benefit is then only negative) philosophy is called up with all its acuteness and art of examination.",["Erweiterung = expansion (not the glossary's Ausdehnung = extension).","bestimte = determinate (bestimmen = determine); Philosophie = philosophy (Weltweisheit, glossary, is the same English word).","The sentence's syntax ('sondern als Critik … dazu … wird Philosophie … aufgeboten') is left as in the German; the antecedent of 'damit' is Erweiterung/Doctrin and is left unresolved."]],"krv-a-0276":["But transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity: that besides the rule (or rather the general condition for rules) that is given in the pure concept of the understanding, it can at the same time indicate *a priori* the case to which they are to be applied. The cause of the advantage that it has in this respect over all other teaching sciences (except mathematics) lies precisely in this: that it deals with concepts that are supposed to relate to their objects *a priori*, hence their objective validity cannot be demonstrated *a posteriori*; for that would leave that dignity of theirs entirely untouched, but it must at the same time set out the conditions under which objects can be given in agreement with those concepts, in general but sufficient distinguishing marks, otherwise they would be without any content, hence mere logical forms and not pure concepts of the understanding.",["'worauf sie angewandt werden sollen': sie is plural, grammatically not clearly the singular Regel; left as 'they'.","Kennzeichen = distinguishing mark (distinct from Merkmal = mark); hinreichend = sufficient (as zureichend).","Ursache = cause; Gegenstand = object; Gültigkeit = validity; Dignität = dignity; Uebereinstimmung = agreement.","'sondern sie muß…': the pronoun sie could refer to transcendental philosophy or to the concepts; kept ambiguous as 'it', as in the German's singular/plural ambiguity is not fully reproducible.","Latin: *a priori*, *a posteriori*."]],"krv-a-0277":["This transcendental doctrine of the power of judgment will now contain two chapters: the first, which deals with the sensible condition under which alone pure concepts of the understanding can be used, i.e., with the schematism of the pure understanding; the second, however, with those synthetic judgments which flow forth from pure concepts of the understanding under these conditions *a priori*, and lie at the ground of all remaining cognitions *a priori*, i.e., with the principles of the pure understanding.",["Hauptstück = chapter; herfliessen = flow forth; zum Grunde liegen = lie at the ground.","'a priori' stands after 'Bedingungen' and before 'herfliessen'; it may attach to the flowing (verb) or to the conditions/judgments; the ambiguity is kept by position."]],"krv-a-0278":["First Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment (or Analytic of Principles). On the Schematism of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["Hauptstück = chapter."]],"krv-a-0279":["In all subsumptions of an object under a concept the representation of the former must be homogeneous with that of the latter, i.e., the concept must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it, for that is just what the expression signifies: an object is contained under a concept. Thus the empirical concept of a plate has homogeneity with the pure geometrical concept of a circle, in that the roundness that is thought in the former can be intuited in the latter.",["gleichartig / Gleichartigkeit = homogeneous / homogeneity; Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = signify; anschauen = intuit.","'mit der leztern': der leztern refers to Begriff (as 'that of the latter'); 'ersteren / lezteren' = former / latter kept without nouns."]],"krv-a-0280":["Now, however, pure concepts of the understanding, in comparison with empirical (indeed sensible in general) intuitions, are entirely heterogeneous and can never be encountered in any intuition. How, then, is the subsumption of the latter under the first, hence the application of the category to appearances, possible, since after all no one will say: this, e.g., causality, can also be intuited through senses and is contained in the appearance? This so natural and weighty question is now properly the cause that makes a transcendental doctrine of the power of judgment necessary, namely in order to show the possibility of how pure concepts of the understanding can be applied to appearances in general. In all other sciences, where the concepts through which the object is thought generally are not so differentiated from, and heterogeneous to, those that represent it *in concreto*, as it is given, it is unnecessary to give particular exposition regarding the application of the former to the latter.",["ungleichartig and heterogen are both rendered 'heterogeneous' (the latter in 'differentiated from, and heterogeneous to').","Erörterung = exposition (expositio); Caussalität = causality; Sinne = senses; erheblich = weighty.","'diese, z. B. die Caussalität': diese is left as 'this'; the antecedent is not resolved.","'die erste' (feminine) is rendered 'the first', as printed; its reference (Begriffe) is not specified."]],"krv-a-0281":["Now it is clear: that there must be a third thing, which must stand in homogeneity on the one hand with the category, on the other hand with the appearance, and makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without anything empirical) and yet on the one hand intellectual, on the other hand sensible. Such a one is the transcendental schema.",["Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity; Vorstellung = representation; sinnlich = sensible; Categorie = category; Schema = schema.","'intellectuel' (Kant's spelling) rendered 'intellectual'."]],"krv-a-0282":["The concept of the understanding contains pure synthetic unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of inner sense, thus of the connection of all representations, contains a manifold *a priori* in pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is homogeneous with the category (which constitutes the unity of it) insofar as it is universal and rests on a rule *a priori*. But on the other hand it is homogeneous with the appearance insofar as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Hence an application of the category to appearances will be possible by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the concepts of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the latter under the first.",["Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding; Verknüpfung = connection; Zeitbestimmung = determination of time; gleichartig = homogeneous.","\"derselben\" (\"the unity of it\") is left with the ambiguity of the German; \"die erste\" / \"der lezteren\" are rendered literally as \"the first\" / \"the latter\", leaving open what each refers to."]],"krv-a-0283":["After what has been shown in the deduction of the categories, hopefully no one will be in doubt about resolving the question: whether these pure concepts of the understanding are of merely empirical or also of transcendental use, i.e., whether they, solely as conditions of a possible experience, relate themselves *a priori* to appearances, or whether they, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, can be extended to objects in themselves (without any restriction to our sensibility). For there we have seen; that concepts are entirely impossible, nor can have any meaning, where, either to them themselves, or at least to the elements of which they consist, an object is not given, thus cannot pertain at all to things in themselves, (without regard to whether, and how, they may be given to us): that further the sole way in which objects are given to us is the modification of our sensibility, finally, that pure concepts *a priori*, besides the function of the understanding in the category, must also contain formal conditions of sensibility (namely of inner sense) *a priori*, which contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. We will call this formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the concept of the understanding is restricted in its use, the schema of this concept of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata the schematism of the pure understanding.",["Gegenstand = object; Gebrauch = use; Bedeutung = meaning; Art = way; Verfahren = procedure.","The semicolon after \"gesehen\" and the colons are kept as printed; the subject of \"gehen können\" (\"cannot pertain\") is grammatically ambiguous and is left so.","\"hoffentlich niemand im Zweifel stehen, sich ... zu entschliessen\" is kept in its awkward construction."]],"krv-a-0284":["The schema is in itself always only a product of the imagination; but since the synthesis of the latter has as its aim no single intuition, but only unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is nonetheless to be distinguished from the image. Thus, if I posit five points one after another, . . . . . this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which can now be five or a hundred, this thinking is more the representation of a method for representing a multitude (e.g., a thousand) in an image in accordance with a certain concept, than this image itself, which in the latter case I would hardly be able to survey and compare with the concept. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination for providing a concept with its image, I call the schema to this concept.",["Einbildungskraft = imagination; Absicht = aim; Bild = image; Zahl = number; Menge = multitude; setzen (\"setze\") rendered \"posit\" per glossary.","\"in einem Bilde\" and \"einem Begriff sein Bild zu verschaffen\" are rendered literally."]],"krv-a-0285":["In fact our pure sensible concepts are grounded not on images of objects but on schemata. No image of a triangle in general would ever be adequate to the concept of it. For it would not attain the generality of the concept, which makes it valid for all triangles, right- or oblique-angled etc., but would always be restricted only to one part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can never exist anywhere except in thought, and signifies a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in regard to pure shapes in space. Still less does an object of experience or an image of it ever attain the empirical concept, but this is always related immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule of the determination of our intuition, in accordance with a certain general concept. The concept of the dog signifies a rule in accordance with which my imagination can delineate the shape of a four-footed animal in general, without being restricted to any single particular shape that experience offers me, or also to every possible image that I can exhibit *in concreto*. This schematism of our understanding, in regard to appearances and their mere form, is a hidden art in the depths of the human soul, whose true knacks we will hardly ever wrest from nature, and lay uncovered before our eyes. We can say only this much: the image is a product of the empirical faculty of the productive imagination, the schema of sensible concepts (as of figures in space) a product and as it were a monogram of the pure imagination *a priori*, through which and in accordance with which the images first become possible, which, however, must be connected with the concept only always by means of the schema which they designate, and in themselves are not fully congruent with it. On the other hand the schema of a pure concept of the understanding is something that cannot be brought into any image at all, but is only the pure synthesis, in accordance with a rule of unity according to concepts in general, which the category expresses, and is a transcendental product of the imagination, which concerns the determination of inner sense in general, according to conditions of its form (time) in regard to all representations, insofar as these are to hang together *a priori* in a concept in accordance with the unity of apperception.",["Schemate (pl.) = schemata; Gestalt = shape; Vermögen = faculty; bedeuten = signify; Grunde liegen = grounded on; Handgriffe = \"knacks\" (literally hand-grips); abrathen = \"wrest\" (doubtful sense, kept obscure).","\"vor alle\" = \"for all\" (vor for für); \"die aber ... verknüpft werden müssen\" and \"sollten\" are kept in the German order; the antecedents of \"sie\" and \"demselben\" are left as ambiguous as in the German.","\"zusammenhängen sollten\" rendered \"are to hang together\"; Zusammenhang elsewhere = interconnection."]],"krv-a-0286":["Without now lingering over a dry and tedious dissection of what is required for transcendental schemata of pure concepts of the understanding in general, we would rather exhibit them according to the order of the categories and in connection with these.",["Verknüpfung = connection; darstellen = exhibit; Zergliederung = dissection."]],"krv-a-0287":["The pure image of all magnitudes (*quantorum*) for outer sense is space, but of all objects of the senses in general, time. But the pure schema of magnitude (*quantitatis*) as a concept of the understanding is number, which is a representation that grasps together the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous). Thus number is nothing other than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold of a homogeneous intuition in general, by my generating time itself in the apprehension of the intuition.",["Grösse = magnitude; Zahl = number; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Anschauung = intuition; gleichartig = homogeneous.","\"Einem zu Einem\" is capitalized in the German and rendered \"one to one\"."]],"krv-a-0288":["Reality is in the pure concept of the understanding that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, therefore, the concept of which in itself indicates a being (in time). Negation, the concept of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of the two thus occurs in the difference of the same time, as a filled or an empty time. Since time is only the form of intuition, thus of objects as appearances, that which in these corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (thingness, reality.) Now every sensation has a degree or magnitude, by which it can fill the same time, i.e., inner sense with regard to the same representation of an object, more or less, until it ceases in nothing (= 0 = *negatio*). Hence there is a relation and interconnection, or rather a transition, from reality to negation, which makes every reality representable as a *quantum*, and the schema of a reality, as the quantity of something insofar as it fills time, is just this continuous and uniform generation of it in time, in that one descends in time from the sensation, which has a certain degree, to the vanishing of it, or gradually ascends from the negation to the magnitude of it.",["Realität = reality (Wirklichkeit would also be \"reality\"; only Realität occurs here); Sachheit = thingness; Seyn / Nichtseyn = being / not-being (\"being\" here is not Wesen); Verhältniß = relation; Uebergang = transition; vorstellig machen = make representable.","\"derselben\" in \"Erzeugung derselben\" and \"Verschwinden derselben\" is left ambiguous as in the German (reality / sensation); \"an diesen\" likewise.","The original reads \"hinabgeht. oder\" (period before lowercase \"oder\"); rendered with a comma, as an apparent misprint or OCR slip.","Quantum and Quantität: \"quantum\" italicized as a Latin word; \"quantity\" for Quantität."]],"krv-a-0289":["The schema of substance is the persistence of the real in time, i.e., the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time in general, which therefore remains while everything else changes. (Time does not elapse, but in it the existence of the changeable elapses. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and abiding, there corresponds in appearance the unchangeable in existence, i.e., substance, and only in it can the sequence and the simultaneity of appearances be determined according to time).",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Vorstellung = representation; Bestimmung = determination; Erscheinung = appearance; Daseyn = existence.","'Substratum' is Latin in the German text, kept in italics-free roman per the passage's own form? Rendered here in italics as a foreign word: see text (left unitalicized above only if the print was not Latin type; doubtful).","Folge = sequence (glossary); 'Zugleichseyn' rendered 'simultaneity'.","'verläuft sich' rendered 'elapses' in both occurrences; 'nach' in 'der Zeit nach' rendered 'according to time'."]],"krv-a-0290":["The schema of cause and of the causality of a thing in general is the real upon which, if it is posited at will, something else always follows. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, insofar as it is subject to a rule.",["gesezt = posited (setzen); Succession = succession (Latinate; Abfolge would also be 'succession'); Mannigfaltige = manifold.","'Caussalität' (original spelling) = causality."]],"krv-a-0291":["The schema of community (interaction), or of the reciprocal causality of substances with regard to their accidents, is the simultaneity of the determinations of the one with those of the other, according to a general rule.",["Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Accidenzen = accidents.","'allgemeinen' rendered 'general'."]],"krv-a-0292":["The schema of possibility is the agreement of the synthesis of various representations with the conditions of time in general, (e.g., since the opposite in a thing cannot be at the same time, but only one after the other), therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at some time.",["Möglichkeit = possibility; Zusammenstimmung = agreement; Bedingungen = conditions.","'also' rendered 'therefore'; the attachment of 'also' (to the parenthesis or to the main clause) is left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0293":["The schema of actuality is existence at a determinate time.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Daseyn = existence; 'bestimten' (sic, for bestimmten) = determinate."]],"krv-a-0294":["The schema of necessity, the existence of an object at all times.",["The German has no verb ('ist' omitted); preserved. Gegenstand = object."]],"krv-a-0295":["One now sees from all this that the schema of each category, as that of magnitude, contains and makes representable the generation (synthesis) of time itself, in the successive apprehension of an object, the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation (perception) with the representation of time, or the filling of time, that of relation the relation of perceptions to one another at all times (i.e., according to a rule of the determination of time); finally the schema of modality and of its categories, time itself, as the correlate of the determination of an object, whether and how it belongs to time. The schemata are therefore nothing but determinations of time a priori according to rules, and these go, according to the order of the categories, to the time-series, the content of time, the order of time, finally the sum total of time with regard to all possible objects.",["Kant's verbs 'enthalte und vorstellig mache' govern the whole sentence, and stand at its end; the German order is kept.","Grösse = magnitude; Empfindung = sensation; Wahrnehmung = perception; Verhältniß = relation (Relation is also 'relation'); Zeitbestimmung = determination of time; Inbegriff = sum total; Inhalt = content; Ordnung = order.","Correlatum, a priori: Latin, italicized as foreign words only where printed in Latin type; here left as given. Schemate = schemata."]],"krv-a-0296":["From this it now becomes clear that the schematism of the understanding through the transcendental synthesis of the imagination comes down to nothing other than the unity of all the manifold of intuition in inner sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function which corresponds to inner sense (a receptivity). Thus the schemata of the pure concepts of the understanding are the true and sole conditions for providing these with a relation to objects, hence with significance, and the categories are therefore in the end of no other than a possible empirical use, in that they serve merely to subject appearances, through grounds of an a priori necessary unity (on account of the necessary unification of all consciousness in an original apperception), to general rules of synthesis, and thereby to make them suitable for thoroughgoing connection in one experience.",["Bedeutung = significance (glossary 'signify / significance; meaning'); Obiect = object (Gegenstand also 'object'); Einbildungskraft = imagination; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Verknüpfung = connection; Erfahrung = experience.","Gründe = grounds; Gebrauch = use; Vereinigung = unification.","'Function' retained as 'function'; 'Receptivität' = receptivity."]],"krv-a-0297":["But in the whole of all possible experience lie all our cognitions, and in the general relation to it consists transcendental truth, which precedes all empirical truth and makes it possible.",["Erkentnisse = cognitions; Erfahrung = experience; 'vor aller empirischen' = 'all empirical' with 'truth' supplied from the noun in the German's feminine agreement (Wahrheit)."]],"krv-a-0298":["But it also strikes the eye: that, although the schemata of sensibility first realize the categories, they nevertheless also restrict them, i.e., limit them to conditions that lie outside the understanding (namely in sensibility). Hence the schema is really only the *phaenomenon*, or the sensible concept of an object, in agreement with the category. (*numerus est quantitas phaenomenon, sensatio realitas phaenomenon, constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon* — — *æternitas, necessitas, phaenomena* etc.) If we now leave out a restricting condition, we amplify, as it seems, the previously limited concept; so the categories in their pure meaning, without any conditions of sensibility, should hold of things in general, as they are, instead of their schemata representing them only as they appear, those therefore having a meaning independent of all schemata and extending much further. In fact there certainly remains to the pure concepts of the understanding, even after the separation of all sensible condition, a meaning, but only a logical one, of the mere unity of the representations, to which, however, no object, hence also no meaning that could yield a concept of the object, is given. Thus, e.g., substance, if one left out the sensible determination of persistence, would signify nothing further than a something that can be thought as subject (without being a predicate of something else). I can make nothing of this representation, since it does not indicate to me at all what determinations the thing has that is supposed to count as such a first subject. Thus the categories, without schemata, are only functions of the understanding for concepts, but do not represent any object. This meaning comes to them from sensibility, which realizes the understanding while it restricts it at the same time.",["Bedeutung = meaning here (glossary 'significance; meaning'); 'bedeuten' = signify. Begriff = concept; Gegenstand and Obiect both = object.","Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Bedingung = condition; Bestimmung = determination; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Vorstellung = representation.","'restringiren' = restrict; 'einschränken' = limit; 'amplificiren' = amplify. Latin parenthesis kept as printed; 'etc.' after phaenomena kept.","'so sollten die Categorien' follows 'so amplificiren wir' with a semicolon and no connective; the syntax is kept as is. 'jene' (iene) refers ambiguously to the categories or to the meanings; left ambiguous.","'Schemate' = schemata; 'ein Etwas' = 'a something'."]],"krv-a-0299":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment (or Analytic of Principles). System of All Principles of the Pure Understanding.",["Urtheilskraft = (power of) judgment; Grundsätze = principles (Grundsatz in the glossary); Hauptstück = chapter."]],"krv-a-0300":["In the previous chapter we considered the transcendental power of judgment only according to the general conditions under which alone it is authorized to use the pure concepts of the understanding for synthetic judgments. Now our business is: to present in systematic combination the judgments that the understanding actually brings about a priori under this critical caution, for which our table of the categories must without doubt give us the natural and secure guidance. For it is these very ones whose relation to possible experience must constitute all pure cognition of the understanding a priori, and whose relation to sensibility in general will for that very reason present all transcendental principles of the use of the understanding completely and in a system.",["OCR 'transseendentale' corrected to 'transscendentale'. Urtheile = judgments; Verbindung = combination; Verstandeserkentniß = cognition of the understanding; Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding.","'erwogen' rendered 'considered'; 'Geschäfte' = business. 'diese' (the categories) is as in the German pronoun."]],"krv-a-0301":["Principles a priori bear this name not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgments, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This property does not, however, always exempt them from a proof. For although this could not be carried further objectively, but rather lies at the ground of all cognition of its object, this still does not prevent a proof from being possible, indeed also necessary, to be produced from the subjective sources of the possibility of a cognition of the object in general, because the proposition would otherwise nevertheless be under the greatest suspicion of being a merely surreptitious assertion.",["Grundsätze = principles; Satz = proposition; Beweis = proof; Behauptung = assertion; Grund = ground. 'dieser' (this) = the proof (Beweis) is the apparent antecedent; kept as in German.","'nicht ... daß nicht' construction preserved awkwardly: 'prevent ... from being possible'. 'erschlichenen' = surreptitious."]],"krv-a-0302":["Second, we will limit ourselves merely to those principles that relate to the categories. The principles of the transcendental aesthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of all things as appearances, likewise the restriction of these principles: that they namely cannot be related to things in themselves, thus do not belong in our delimited field of investigation. Likewise the mathematical principles make up no part of this system, because they are drawn only from intuition, but not from the pure concept of the understanding; yet the possibility of the same, because they are nevertheless synthetic judgments a priori, will necessarily find a place here, though not in order to prove their correctness and apodictic certainty, which they do not need at all, but only to make comprehensible and to deduce the possibility of such evident cognitions a priori.",["The German sentence 'Die Principien ... gehören also nicht' is kept as is with a long subject; 'also' = 'thus'. 'abgestochenen' = delimited (doubtful as to nuance).","Raum = space; Zeit = time; Erscheinungen = appearances; Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves; Anschauung = intuition; begreiflich = comprehensible.","'Gewisheit' = certainty; 'deduciren' = deduce."]],"krv-a-0303":["But we will also have to speak of the principle of analytic judgments, and indeed in opposition to the synthetic, with which we are properly occupied, because precisely this opposition frees the theory of the latter from all misunderstanding, and places it distinctly before the eyes in its peculiar nature.",["Grundsatz = principle; analytisch / synthetisch = analytic / synthetic; deutlich = distinct; 'der lezteren' = of the latter (the synthetic, feminine genitive plural, 'Urtheile' implied).","'Gegenstellung' = opposition (Gegensatz also 'opposition')."]],"krv-a-0304":["The System of the Principles of the Pure Understanding. First Section. On the Supreme Principle of All Analytic Judgments.",["Grundsatz = principle; Abschnitt = section; oberst = supreme."]],"krv-a-0305":["Whatever the content of our cognition may be, and however it may relate to the object, the universal, though only negative condition of all our judgments in general is still that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgments are in themselves (even without regard to the object) nothing. But even if there is no contradiction in our judgment, it can nevertheless combine concepts in a way that the object does not bring with it, or also without any ground being given to us, either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, which would entitle such a judgment, and thus a judgment, for all that it is free of all inner contradiction, can nevertheless be either false or groundless.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Urtheil = judgment; Begriff = concept; Grund = ground; Bedingung = condition; Widerspruch = contradiction.","Obiect and Gegenstand are both rendered \"object\" (here Obiect, then Gegenstand).","The subjunctive \"berechtigte\" is rendered \"would entitle\"; the clause beginning \"oder auch, ohne daß…\" is kept in the German order and left ambiguous in its attachment to \"verbinden\"."]],"krv-a-0306":["The proposition now: To no thing does a predicate belong which contradicts it, is called the principle of contradiction, and is a universal, though merely negative criterion of all truth, but for that reason also belongs merely in logic, because it holds of cognitions merely as cognitions in general, regardless of their content, and says: that the contradiction entirely nullifies and cancels them.",["Satz des Widerspruchs rendered \"principle of contradiction\"; Satz alone = \"proposition\". Both Satz and Grundsatz (\"principle\") thus yield \"principle\" in some contexts.","Criterium = criterion (Kant's Latinate spelling).","\"gehört … in die Logik\" and \"kommt zu\" both rendered with \"belong(s)\"."]],"krv-a-0307":["But one can nevertheless also make a positive use of the same, i.e., not merely in order to banish falsity and error (insofar as it rests on contradiction), but also to cognize truth. For, if the judgment is analytic, be it now negative or affirmative, then its truth must always be able to be cognized sufficiently in accordance with the principle of contradiction. For of that which already lies in the cognition of the object as concept and is thought, the contrary will always be correctly denied, but the concept itself will necessarily have to be affirmed of it, because the opposite of it would contradict the object.",["hinreichend rendered \"sufficiently\" (the glossary's zureichend is also \"sufficient\"; one English word for two German words).","Widerspiel = \"contrary\"; Gegentheil = \"opposite\".","\"Das Widerspiel … wird … verneinet\" and \"der Begriff … bejahet\": verneinen = deny (negative), bejahen = affirm.","Obiect = object."]],"krv-a-0308":["Hence we must also let the principle of contradiction count as the universal and fully sufficient *principium* of all analytic cognition; but its authority and usability do not extend further, as a sufficient criterion of truth. For that no cognition can be contrary to it without annihilating itself, this makes this proposition indeed a *conditio sine qua non*, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. Since we now actually have to do only with the synthetic part of our cognition, we will indeed always be careful never to act contrary to this inviolable principle, but can never expect any disclosure from it with regard to the truth of this kind of cognition.",["Principium (Latin in the text) kept in italics; Grundsatz = principle; Criterium = criterion.","hinreichend = \"sufficient\" (see previous note on zureichend).","Aufschluß rendered \"disclosure\" (not in the glossary). Ansehen = authority; Brauchbarkeit = usability.","Bestimmungsgrund = determining ground."]],"krv-a-0309":["But there is nevertheless a formula of this famous principle, though stripped of all content and merely formal, which contains a synthesis, which has been mixed into it out of carelessness and in a quite unnecessary way. It runs: It is impossible that something at the same time is and is not. Besides the fact that apodictic certainty (through the word impossible) has been superfluously appended, which must surely be understood of itself from the proposition, the proposition is affected by the condition of time, and says as it were: A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be *non* B, but it can very well be both (B as well as *non* B) one after another. E.g., A man who is young cannot at the same time be old, but the very same one can very well be young at one time, not young, i.e., old, at another. Now the principle of contradiction, as a merely logical principle, must not restrict its pronouncements to the relations of time at all, hence such a formula is entirely contrary to its aim. The misunderstanding comes merely from this: that one first separates a predicate of a thing from the concept of it, and afterwards connects its opposite with this predicate, which never yields a contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been synthetically combined with the former, and indeed only then, when the first and second predicate are posited at the same time. If I say, a man who is unlearned is not learned, then the condition: at the same time, must stand with it; for he who is unlearned at one time can very well be learned at another. But if I say, no unlearned man is learned, then the proposition is analytic, because the mark (of unlearnedness) now constitutes the concept of the subject as well, and then the negative proposition is evident immediately from the principle of contradiction, without the condition: at the same time, needing to be added. This is then also the reason why I have above altered its formula in such a way that the nature of an analytic proposition is thereby distinctly expressed.",["Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition; Satz des Widerspruchs = principle of contradiction; Merkmal = mark; deutlich = distinct; Absicht = aim; Zeit = time.","\"zugleich\" and \"zu gleicher Zeit\" are both rendered \"at the same time\".","\"non B\" is Latin in the original; italicized *non*.","verknüpfen = connect; verbinden = combine; setzen = posit (gesezt).","\"Ungelahrtheit\" (sic) = unlearnedness; \"afficirt\" = affected.","\"Unnöthiger Weise\" and \"Unvorsichtigkeit\" kept literal (\"in a quite unnecessary way\", \"carelessness\")."]],"krv-a-0310":["Second Section of the System of the Principles of the Pure Understanding. On the supreme principle of all synthetic judgments.",["Heading as in the German word order: \"Des Systems … Zweiter Abschnitt.\"","Grundsatz = principle; Verstand = understanding; rein = pure; synthetisch = synthetic."]],"krv-a-0311":["The explanation of the possibility of synthetic judgments is a problem with which general logic has nothing at all to do, which indeed need not even know its name. But in a transcendental logic it is the most important business of all, and even the only one, when the discussion is of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*, likewise of the conditions and the domain of their validity. For after the completion of the same, it can fully satisfy its end, namely to determine the domain and the boundaries of the pure understanding.",["Erklärung = explanation; Aufgabe = problem; Umfang = domain; Grenze = boundary; Zweck = end; Gültigkeit = validity.","\"desselben\" (\"of the same\") has no clear antecedent in the German (Geschäfte, or the explanation); left as it stands.","\"sie\" in \"kan sie ihrem Zwecke\" is left as \"it\"; the antecedent is ambiguous in the German (transcendental logic, or the explanation).","\"kennen\" rendered \"know\"."]],"krv-a-0312":["In the analytic judgment I remain with the given concept, in order to make out something about it. If it is to be affirmative, then I ascribe to this concept only that which was already thought in it; if it is to be negative, then I exclude from it only the opposite of it. But in synthetic judgments I am to go out beyond the given concept, in order to consider something entirely other than what was thought in it in relation with the same, which relation is therefore never one either of identity or of contradiction, and in which neither the truth nor the error can be discerned from the judgment in itself.",["\"an ihm selbst\" rendered \"in itself\"; distinct from the technical \"an sich\" (also \"in itself\").","Verhältniß = relation; Begriff = concept; Urtheil = judgment.","\"welches daher niemals … ist\": the relative \"welches\" is taken to refer to \"Verhältniß\"; \"which relation\" is a literal rendering of that reading."]],"krv-a-0313":["Thus granted: that one must go out beyond a given concept in order to compare it synthetically with another; a third is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two concepts can arise. But what now is this third, as the *medium* of all synthetic judgments? It is only a sum total in which all our representations are contained, namely inner sense, and the form of it *a priori*, time. The synthesis of representations rests on the imagination, but the synthetic unity of them (which is required for judgment) on the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, the possibility of synthetic judgments, and, since all three contain the sources of representations *a priori*, also the possibility of pure synthetic judgments will have to be sought, indeed they will even be necessary on these grounds, if a cognition of objects is to come about that rests solely on the synthesis of representations.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Vorstellung = representation; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Einheit = unity; Sinn = sense; Zeit = time.","Medium (Latinate in the text) rendered *medium*; \"Apperception\" = apperception.","\"Hierin\" (\"In this\") is left unresolved, as in the German; the sentence's syntax (\"wird … zu suchen seyn\" with a double subject) is kept.","Gegenstand = object; Erkentniß = cognition; Urtheil = judgment (\"zum Urtheile\" = \"for judgment\")."]],"krv-a-0314":["If a cognition is to have objective reality, i.e., to relate to an object, and to have significance and meaning in it, the object must be able to be given in some way. Without that the concepts are empty, and through them one has indeed thought, but in fact has cognized nothing through this thinking, but has merely played with representations. To give an object, if this is not again to be meant only mediately, but to exhibit it immediately in intuition, is nothing other than to relate its representation to experience (be it actual or at least possible). Even space and time, however pure these concepts are of everything empirical, and however certain it is that they are represented fully a priori in the mind, would yet be without objective validity and without sense and meaning, if their necessary use on the objects of experience were not shown, indeed their representation is a mere schema, which always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they would have no meaning; and so it is with all concepts without distinction.",["Gegenstand is rendered 'object'; Object (in krv-a-0317) is also 'object'; the Latinate form is marked in the note there.","Bedeutung und Sinn = 'meaning'/'significance' and 'sense': Bedeutung is rendered 'significance' in 'Sinn und Bedeutung' only where the glossary's pairing seemed to demand it; elsewhere 'meaning'. Both are glossary renderings of bedeuten/Bedeutung.","Erkentniß = cognition; 'erkant' = cognized; Anschauung = intuition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Gemüth = mind; Gültigkeit = validity.","'ia' (ja) rendered 'indeed'. The clause 'wenn dieses nicht wiederum nur mittelbar gemeint seyn soll' kept in the German order.","'ihre Vorstellung' follows the original's ambiguity; the antecedent could be space and time together or each; 'they' in 'without which they would have no meaning' is likewise ambiguous between the objects/representations/space and time, as in the German."]],"krv-a-0315":["The possibility of experience is therefore that which gives objective reality to all our cognitions a priori. Now experience rests on the synthetic unity of appearances, i.e., on a synthesis according to concepts of the object of appearances in general, without which they would not even be cognition but a rhapsody of perceptions, which would not fit together into any context according to rules of a thoroughly connected (possible) consciousness, hence also not into the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience therefore has principles of its form a priori lying at its ground, namely general rules of unity in the synthesis of appearances, whose objective reality, as necessary conditions, can at any time be shown in experience, indeed even in its possibility. Apart from this relation, however, synthetic propositions a priori are entirely impossible, because they have no third, namely pure object, on which the synthetic unity of their concepts could establish objective reality.",["Erscheinung = appearance; Wahrnehmung = perception; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Satz = proposition; Grund = ground; Bedingung = condition.","'a priori' in 'allen unsern Erkentnissen a priori' may attach to 'Erkentnissen' or to 'giebt'; left ambiguous. Likewise 'ihrer Möglichkeit' can be read as the possibility of experience or of the principles/rules; 'even in its possibility' is kept ambiguous.","'die sich ... zusammen schicken würden' has plural 'würden' after 'Rhapsodie' (singular); retained as in the original. Retained 'Context' as 'context'.","'Principien' = principles (Principium); Grundsatz is also 'principle' in the glossary."]],"krv-a-0316":["Although we therefore cognize so much a priori in synthetic judgments about space in general, or about the shapes that the productive imagination draws in it, so that we really need no experience at all for this, yet this cognition would be nothing at all, but an occupation with a mere figment of the brain, were space not to be regarded as a condition of the appearances that make up the material for outer experience: hence those pure synthetic judgments, though only mediately, relate to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of the latter itself, and found the objective validity of their synthesis on that alone.",["Stoff = material; Urtheil = judgment; Erkentniß = cognition; 'erkennen' = cognize.","'so, daß wir wirklich hiezu gar keiner Erfahrung bedürfen' kept as a consecutive clause after the comma as in the German.","'auf dieser ihre Möglichkeit selbst' (dieser = experience's) rendered 'the possibility of the latter itself'; the German 'selbst' could modify Möglichkeit only; kept close.","'darauf allein' may be read as 'on that alone' (the relation) — left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0317":["Since experience, as empirical synthesis, in its possibility is the only kind of cognition that gives reality to all other synthesis, the latter, as cognition a priori, also has truth (agreement with the object) only by this, that it contains nothing further than what is necessary for the synthetic unity of experience in general.",["Obiect (in 'Einstimmung mit dem Obiect') and Gegenstand are both 'object'; the Latinate form Obiect is here marked.","Erkentnißart = 'kind of cognition' (Art = kind).","'in ihrer Möglichkeit' could modify 'Erfahrung' or 'ist'; left ambiguous. 'diese' (the latter) could refer to 'Synthesis'; 'dadurch, daß' = 'by this, that'."]],"krv-a-0318":["The supreme principle of all synthetic judgments is therefore: every object stands under the necessary conditions of the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.",["Principium rendered 'principle'; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0319":["In such a way synthetic judgments a priori are possible, if we relate the formal conditions of intuition a priori, the synthesis of the imagination and the necessary unity of the latter in a transcendental apperception to a possible cognition of experience in general, and say: the conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and therefore have objective validity in a synthetic judgment a priori.",["'derselben' (the necessary unity 'of the same') is ambiguous between the synthesis and the imagination; rendered 'the latter', which preserves some ambiguity.","Erfahrungserkentniß = 'cognition of experience'; 'a priori' after 'Anschauung' may attach to 'conditions' or 'intuition', left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0320":["Of the System of the Principles of Pure Understanding, Third Section. Systematic Representation of All Synthetic Principles of the Same.",["Grundsätze = principles; Verstand = understanding; Vorstellung = representation. 'desselben' refers to the system or to the pure understanding; left ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0321":["That principles take place anywhere at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules with regard to what happens, but is itself the source of principles, according to which everything (that can come before us only as object) necessarily stands under rules, because without them cognition of an object corresponding to them could never come to appearances. Even laws of nature, when they are considered as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, carry with them at the same time an expression of necessity, hence at least the presumption of a determination from grounds that are valid a priori and prior to all experience. But without distinction all laws of nature stand under higher principles of the understanding, in that they only apply these to particular cases of appearance. These alone therefore give the concept that contains the condition and, as it were, the exponent for a rule in general. Experience, however, gives the case that stands under the rule.",["Gesetz = law; Grundsatz = principle; Vermögen = faculty; Ausdruck = expression; Bestimmung = determination; besonder = particular.","'was geschieht' = 'what happens'; 'Verstandesgebrauch' = 'use of the understanding'; Gebrauch = use.","'ihnen' in 'ihnen correspondirenden' refers to the appearances (dative) and 'ohne solche' to rules; 'Erkentniß eines ihnen correspondirenden Gegenstandes' could take 'ihnen' as appearances or rules; the English 'them' preserves the ambiguity.","'Vermuthung' = 'presumption'; 'Exponenten' = 'exponent'."]],"krv-a-0322":["That one should regard merely empir. principles as principles of the pure understanding, or also conversely, there can really be no danger of that; for the necessity according to concepts, which distinguishes the latter, and whose lack in every empirical proposition, however generally it may hold, is easily perceived, can easily prevent this confusion. But there are pure principles a priori that I would nevertheless not ascribe as peculiar to the pure understanding, because they are drawn not from pure concepts but from pure intuitions (though by means of the understanding); but understanding is the faculty of concepts. Mathematics has such principles, but their application to experience, hence their objective validity, indeed the possibility of such synthetic cognition a priori (the deduction of the same) nevertheless always rests on the pure understanding.",["'empir.' is abbreviated in the German text and kept so. Satz = proposition; Grundsatz = principle; rein = pure; Anschauung = intuition.","'vor Grundsätze' (= für) rendered 'as principles'. 'vermittelst des Verstandes' = 'by means of the understanding'.","'derselben' (the deduction 'of the same') may refer to the synthetic cognition or to the principles/mathematics; left ambiguous.","'wahrgenommen' = 'perceived' (wahrnehmen, cf. Wahrnehmung = perception)."]],"krv-a-0323":["Therefore I will not count those of mathematics among my principles, but certainly those on which the possibility and objective validity a priori of the former are grounded, and which are accordingly to be regarded as the principle of these principles, and proceed from concepts to intuition, but not from intuition to concepts.",["'dieser ihre Möglichkeit' = 'the possibility of the latter (mathematics' principles)'; rendered 'of the former' following the sense of 'those of mathematics' as the nearest antecedent in my English order; the German pronoun 'dieser' is ambiguous as to its referent.","Principium = principle; gründen = ground."]],"krv-a-0324":["In the application of the pure concepts of the understanding to possible experience the use of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical: for it goes partly merely to intuition, partly to the existence of an appearance in general. The conditions a priori of intuition, however, are with regard to a possible experience thoroughly necessary, those of the existence of the objects of a possible empirical intuition in themselves only contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use will sound unconditionally necessary, i.e., apodictic, but those of the dynamical use will indeed also carry with them the character of a necessity a priori, but only under the condition of empirical thinking in an experience, hence only mediately and indirectly, consequently will not contain that immediate evidence (though without prejudice to their certainty, generally related to experience) that is peculiar to the former. But this will be better judged at the conclusion of this system of principles.",["Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Obiecte = 'objects' (Object, the Latinate member); Gegenstand elsewhere also 'object'.","an sich = in itself; zufällig = contingent; notwendig = necessary; unbedingt = unconditioned/unconditionally; 'apodictisch' kept as 'apodictic'.","'an sich nur zufällig' could attach to 'Daseyns' or to 'Obiecte'; 'in themselves' preserves this.","'ienen' = the former (the mathematical principles); 'Schlusse' = 'conclusion' (Schluß); 'beurtheilen' = 'judge'."]],"krv-a-0325":["The table of categories gives us the quite natural direction to the table of principles, because these are indeed nothing other than rules of the objective use of the former. All principles of the pure understanding are accordingly",["Passage breaks off before the list; translated as it stands. 'Categorien' = categories; Grundsätze = principles; Gebrauch = use."]],"krv-a-0326":["1. Axioms of intuition",["Latin-derived 'Axiomen' = 'axioms'; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0327":["2. Anticipations of perception",["Wahrnehmung = perception."]],"krv-a-0328":["3. Analogies of experience",[]],"krv-a-0329":["4. Postulates of empirical thinking in general.",["überhaupt = 'in general'."]],"krv-a-0330":["These terms I have chosen with caution, in order not to leave unnoticed the differences with regard to the evidence and the exercise of these principles. But it will soon be shown: that, as concerns both the evidence and the determination of appearances *a priori*, according to the categories of magnitude and of quality (if one attends merely to the form of the latter), the principles of the same distinguish themselves notably therein from the two remaining ones; since the former are capable of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though on both sides of a complete certainty. I will therefore call the former the mathematical, the latter the dynamical principles. But one will well note: that I have here just as little the principles of mathematics in the one case, as the principles of general (physical) dynamics in the other, but only those of the pure understanding in relation to inner sense (without distinction of the representations given therein), before my eyes, through which the former all receive their possibility. I name them therefore more in consideration of the application than on account of their content, and go now to the weighing of the same in the same order as they are presented in the table.",["Benennungen = terms (glossary: Benennung); Grundsätze = principles; Sätze = propositions.","Evidenz rendered by the cognate 'evidence'; Ausübung = 'exercise'; Erwägung = 'weighing' (not 'consideration', which is kept for Betrachtung).","'ienen ... diese' = 'the former ... the latter'; 'einer intuitiven, diese aber einer blos discursiven' leaves 'certainty' (Gewißheit) to be supplied from the end of the clause, as in the German.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'innern Sinn' = inner sense; 'Gültigkeit'/'Inhalt' as in glossary. 'a priori' is Latin in the source."]],"krv-a-0331":["1. On the Axioms of Intuition.",["Anschauung = intuition (intuitus)."]],"krv-a-0332":["Principle of the pure understanding: All appearances are, as far as their intuition is concerned, extensive magnitudes.",["'ihrer Anschauung nach' kept literal as 'as far as their intuition is concerned'; Grösse = magnitude."]],"krv-a-0333":["An extensive magnitude I call that in which the representation of the parts makes the representation of the whole possible (and therefore necessarily precedes the latter). I cannot represent to myself any line, however small it may be, without drawing it in thought, i.e., generating all its parts from a point one after another, and thereby first of all sketching this intuition. Just so is it also with every, even the smallest, time. I think to myself therein only the successive progression from one moment to another, where through all parts of time and their adding-to a determinate magnitude of time is finally generated. Since the mere intuition in all appearances is either space or time, every appearance as intuition is an extensive magnitude, in that it can be cognized only through successive synthesis (from part to part) in apprehension. All appearances are accordingly already intuited as aggregates (multitude of previously given parts), which is just not the case with every kind of magnitudes, but only with those that are represented and apprehended by us extensively as such.",["Fortgang = progression; Menge = multitude; Art = kind; Theil = part; erkennen = cognize.","'vor dieser' = 'before' / 'precedes the latter' (the whole); 'Hinzuthun' rendered 'adding-to'.","'bestimte Zeitgrösse' = 'determinate magnitude of time'; bestimmen = determine.","'welches ... ist' has no explicit antecedent beyond the preceding clause; left as in German."]],"krv-a-0334":["On this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of shapes, is grounded the mathematics of extension (geometry) with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensible intuition *a priori*, under which alone the schema of a pure concept of outer appearance can come about, e.g., between two points only one straight line is possible; two straight lines enclose no space, etc. These are the axioms, which properly concern only magnitudes (*quanta*) as such.",["Einbildungskraft = imagination; Ausdehnung = extension; Bedingung = condition; sinnlich = sensible.","'a priori' could attach to 'conditions' or to 'express'; left as ambiguous as the German.","'Axiomen' kept as plural 'axioms'; 'Gestalten' = shapes."]],"krv-a-0335":["But as for magnitude (*quantitas*), i.e., the answer to the question: how large something is? there are, with regard to it, although several of these propositions are synthetic and immediately certain (*indemonstrabilia*), nevertheless no axioms in the proper sense. For that equals added to equals, or subtracted from these, give an equal, are analytic propositions, since I am immediately conscious of the identity of the one generation of magnitude with the other; but axioms are supposed to be synthetic propositions *a priori*. On the other hand the evident propositions of numerical relation are, to be sure, certainly synthetic, but not universal, like those of geometry, and just for that reason also not axioms, but can be called number formulas. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytic proposition. For I think the number 12 neither in the representation of 7, nor of 5, nor in the representation of the composition of both, (that I should think it in the addition of both is not at issue here; for with the analytic proposition the question is only whether I actually think the predicate in the representation of the subject). But although it is synthetic, it is nevertheless only a single proposition. Insofar as here merely the synthesis of the homogeneous (of the units) is looked at, the synthesis can here take place only in a single way, although the use of these numbers is afterwards universal. If I say: through three lines, of which two taken together are greater than the third, a triangle can be drawn; then I have here the mere function of the productive imagination, which can draw the lines greater and smaller, likewise let them meet at all sorts of arbitrary angles. On the other hand the number 7 is possible only in a single way, and also the number 12, which is generated through the synthesis of the former with 5. Propositions of this sort one must therefore not call axioms (for otherwise there would be infinitely many of them) but number formulas.",["Größe (quantitas) vs. Grösse (magnitude): both rendered 'magnitude'; the Latin quantitas and quanta mark the Latinate usage.","'im eigentlichen Verstande' = 'in the proper sense' (Verstand here not the faculty; glossary 'understanding' not used).","Zahlverhältniß = 'numerical relation'; Zahlformeln = 'number formulas'; Zahl = number; Zusammensetzung = composition; gleichartig = homogeneous; Einheiten = units.","'Satz' = proposition throughout; 'bewust' = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness).","'Triangel' = triangle; 'zusammenstossen lassen' = 'let ... meet'; the subject of 'kan' is the imagination (or the function): left as in German.","'Ob er aber gleich' etc.: 'er' refers to the proposition 7 + 5 = 12."]],"krv-a-0336":["This transcendental principle of the mathematics of appearances gives our cognition *a priori* great expansion. For it is it alone that makes pure mathematics in its whole precision applicable to objects of experience, which without this principle might not be so self-evident, indeed has also occasioned much contradiction. Appearances are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through the pure (of space and of time); what geometry says of the latter therefore holds also without contradiction of the former, and the evasions, as if objects of the senses might not conform to the rules of construction in space (e.g., to the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall away. For thereby one denies to space, and with it at the same time to all mathematics, objective validity, and no longer knows why and how far it is to be applied to appearances. The synthesis of spaces and times, as the essential form of all intuition, is that which at the same time makes possible the apprehension of appearance, hence every outer experience, consequently also all cognition of the objects thereof, and what mathematics in pure use proves of the former, that holds also necessarily of the latter. All objections against it are only chicanery of a falsely taught reason, which erroneously intends to detach the objects of the senses from the formal condition of our sensibility, and represents them, although they are merely appearances, as objects in themselves, given to the understanding, in which case, to be sure, nothing at all could be cognized of them *a priori*, hence also not through pure concepts of space, synthetically, and the science that determines these, namely geometry itself, would not be possible.",["Erkenntnis = cognition; Erweiterung = 'expansion'; Widerspruch = contradiction; Gültigkeit = validity; Gegenstand = object (Objective/obiectiv = 'objective').","'dieser'/'ienen' = 'the latter'/'the former' (pure / empirical intuition in the first instance; later 'iener'/'dieser' = appearance/pure intuition in the same order as in the German).","'dürfe' (subjunctive of dürfen) rendered 'might not'; the German is ambiguous between 'need not' and 'may not'.","'welches ... möchte, ia auch ... hat': the antecedent of 'welches' is left open, as in German.","'dem Verstande gegeben' may attach to 'Gegenstände an sich selbst' or to 'vorstellt'; preserved. 'die diese bestimt': 'diese' (pure concepts or objects) ambiguous.","'synthetisch erkant werden könte' kept in the German position relative to 'a priori', so that the adverbs 'a priori' and 'synthetically' attach as ambiguously as in the original.","'Chikanen' = chicanery; 'falsch belehrten' = falsely taught (Belehrung = teaching); Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"krv-a-0337":["2. The Anticipations of Perception.",["Wahrnehmung = perception (perceptio)."]],"krv-a-0338":["The principle that anticipates all perceptions, as such, goes thus: In all appearances sensation, and the real that corresponds to it in the object (*realitas phaenomenon*), has an intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree.",["Empfindung = sensation; 'das Reale' = 'the real'; Gegenstand = object; Grad = degree.","'hat' has a compound subject ('Empfindung, und das Reale') with a singular verb, as in the German."]],"krv-a-0339":["One can call all cognition through which I can cognize and determine *a priori* what belongs to empirical cognition an anticipation, and without doubt that is the meaning in which Epicurus used his expression προληψις. But since there is something in appearances that is never cognized *a priori*, and which therefore also constitutes the proper difference between empirical cognition and cognition *a priori*, namely sensation (as matter of perception), it follows that it is this that properly cannot be anticipated at all. On the other hand, we could call the pure determinations in space and time, with regard to shape as well as magnitude, anticipations of appearances, because they represent *a priori* that which may always be given *a posteriori* in experience. But supposing that there nevertheless is found something that can be cognized *a priori* in every sensation, as sensation in general (without a particular one being given), then this would deserve to be called anticipation in an exceptional sense, because it seems strange to anticipate experience in that which concerns precisely its matter, which one can draw only from it. And so it actually stands here.",["Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Bestimmung = determination; Materie = matter; Empfindung = sensation; Wahrnehmung = perception; Erscheinung = appearance.","Original spelling 'Erkentniß' and 'Anticipation' kept as in the text; 'Anticipation' rendered 'anticipation' (anticipiren = anticipate).","'im ausnehmenden Verstande' rendered literally as 'in an exceptional sense'; Verstand here is not the faculty 'understanding' but 'sense/meaning' (glossary default 'understanding' deliberately not used).","'Gesezt aber, es finde sich' preserves the subjunctive: 'supposing that there ... is found'.","'Und so verhält es sich hier wirklich' kept literal; 'a priori', 'a posteriori' are Latin and italicized."]],"krv-a-0340":["Apprehension, merely by means of sensation, fills only an instant (if, namely, I do not take into consideration the succession of many sensations). As something in the appearance the apprehension of which is no successive synthesis proceeding from parts to the whole representation, it therefore has no extensive magnitude: the lack of sensation in the same instant would represent this as empty, hence = 0. Now what corresponds to sensation in empirical intuition is reality (*realitas phaenomenon*); what corresponds to the lack of it, negation = 0. Now, however, every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease, and thus gradually vanish. Hence between reality in the appearance and negation there is a continuous nexus of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from one another is always smaller than the difference between the given one and zero, or the complete negation, i.e., the real in the appearance always has a magnitude, which however is not encountered in apprehension, since this occurs by means of mere sensation in an instant, and not through successive synthesis of many sensations, and thus does not proceed from the parts to the whole; it therefore has indeed a magnitude, but not an extensive one.",["Zusammenhang = nexus (glossary: 'interconnection; nexus'); here 'nexus' chosen. Zusammenhang is not Verknüpfung (connection).","Größe = magnitude; Grösse throughout 'magnitude'. Fähigkeit = capacity; 'einer Verringerung fähig' rendered 'capable of a diminution'.","'Zero' keeps the German 'Zero'; 'dem Zero' rendered 'zero'.","'realitas phaenomenon' is Kant's Latin, kept in italics as printed.","'diesen' (in 'diesen, als leer, vorstellen') has no clear antecedent in the German; 'this' kept without resolving it (possibly the instant).","'es' in the last sentence (the real in the appearance) left as 'it'."]],"krv-a-0341":["Now I call the magnitude that is apprehended only as unity, and in which the multiplicity can be represented only through approximation to negation = 0, the intensive magnitude. Thus every reality in the appearance has intensive magnitude, i.e., a degree. If one considers this reality as cause (whether of sensation or of another reality in the appearance, e.g., an alteration); then one calls the degree of reality as cause a moment, e.g., the moment of gravity, and indeed because the degree designates only the magnitude the apprehension of which is not successive but instantaneous. But this I touch on here only in passing, for with causality I have as yet nothing to do for now.",["Veränderung = alteration (the glossary gives no entry; 'change' is the usual alternative). Realität = reality; Grad = degree.","'Moment' kept as 'moment' (German Moment); Schwere = gravity.","'Caussalität' = causality; 'augenblicklich' rendered 'instantaneous' (cf. Augenblick = instant).","'bezeichnen' rendered 'designate'; 'Einheit' = unity; 'Vielheit' = multiplicity (glossary Menge = multitude/multiplicity, but here Vielheit is a different word)."]],"krv-a-0342":["Thus every sensation, hence also every reality in the appearance, however small it may be, has a degree, i.e., an intensive magnitude, which can still always be diminished, and between reality and negation there is a continuous nexus of possible realities, and of possible smaller perceptions. Every color, e.g., the red, has a degree which, however small it may be, is never the smallest, and so it is everywhere with warmth, the moment of gravity, etc.",["Zusammenhang = nexus; Wahrnehmung = perception; Wärme = warmth.","'so ist es ... überall bewandt' rendered idiomatically but literally as 'so it is ... everywhere' (bewandt: 'stands'); the German idiom is archaic.","'z. E.' = 'e.g.' (zum Exempel)."]],"krv-a-0343":["The property of magnitudes according to which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part simple) is called the continuity of them. Space and time are *quanta continua*, because no part of them can be given without enclosing it between bounds (points and instants), hence only in such a way that this part itself is in turn a space, or a time. Space therefore consists only of spaces, time of times. Points and instants are only bounds, i.e., mere places of their limitation, but places always presuppose those intuitions that they are to limit, or determine, and from mere places, as from constituents that could be given before space or time, neither space nor time can be composed. Such magnitudes one can also call flowing, because the synthesis (of the productive imagination) in their generation is a progression in time, the continuity of which one is accustomed to designate especially by the expression of flowing (flowing away).",["Grenze = bound; Schranke would be 'limitation'; here Einschränkung = limitation (a different word from Schranke), beschränken = 'limit' (v.). Stelle = place (not Ort).","Anschauung = intuition; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Fortgang = progression; Ausdruck = expression.","'einfach' = simple; 'Bestandtheilen' = constituents; 'zusammen gesezt' = composed.","'quanta continua' Latin, italicized."]],"krv-a-0344":["All appearances in general are accordingly continuous magnitudes, both according to their intuition, as extensive, or according to mere perception (sensation and hence reality), as intensive magnitudes. If the synthesis of the manifold of the appearance is interrupted, then this is an aggregate of many appearances, and not properly appearance as a quantum, which is generated not through the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain way, but through repetition of an ever-ceasing synthesis. If I call 13 thalers a quantum of money, I denominate it correctly insofar as I understand by it the content of a mark of fine silver, which however is certainly a continuous magnitude, in which no part is the smallest, but every part could constitute a coin, which would always contain matter for still smaller ones. But if by that denomination I understand 13 round thalers, as so many coins (their silver content may be whatever it will), then I denominate it improperly through a quantum of thalers, but must call it an aggregate, i.e., a number of coins. Since now at the basis of all number unity must nevertheless lie, the appearance as unity is a quantum, and as such always a continuum.",["Mannigfaltige = manifold; Art = way; Zahl = number; Grund = basis ('zum Grunde liegen' = 'lie at the basis').","Benennung/benennen: 'denomination'/'denominate'; Gehalt = content (Inhalt in glossary also 'content'; here Gehalt is a different word, also rendered 'content').","Thaler, Mark fein Silber kept as thalers, mark of fine silver; 'Geldstück' = coin; 'Münzen' = coins (both 'coin').","'unschicklich' rendered 'improperly'; 'eigentlich' rendered 'properly'.","'sowol ihrer Anschauung nach, als extensive' kept in parallel structure; the clause is elliptical and ambiguous as to what 'als extensive' attaches to."]],"krv-a-0345":["If now all appearances, considered both extensively and intensively, are continuous magnitudes; then the proposition that all alteration (transition of a thing from one state into another) is also continuous could easily be proved here with mathematical evidence, if the causality of an alteration in general did not lie entirely outside the bounds of a transcendental philosophy, and presuppose empirical principles. For that a cause is possible that alters the state of things, i.e., determines them to the opposite of a certain given state, of this the understanding gives us no disclosure at all *a priori*, not merely because it does not at all have insight into the possibility of it (for this insight is lacking to us in several cognitions *a priori*) but because alterability concerns only certain determinations of appearances, which experience alone can teach, while their cause is to be encountered in the unalterable. But since we have nothing before us here that we can make use of except the pure basic concepts of all possible experience, among which there must be absolutely nothing empirical, we cannot, without violating the unity of the system, anticipate the general science of nature, which is built on certain basic experiences.",["Veränderung = alteration; Zustand = state; Verstand = understanding; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erfahrung = experience.","Grenze = bound; Principien = principles (Grundsatz otherwise 'principle'; Satz = proposition).","Naturwissenschaft = science of nature; Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; Grunderfahrungen = basic experiences.","'Eröfnung' = disclosure; 'einsieht' = have insight; 'Einsicht' = insight.","'vorgreifen' = anticipate (as in vorgreifen in the preceding passage); the German 'Anticipation' root differs but both rendered 'anticipate'; 'Transscendental-Philosophie' = transcendental philosophy.","'Möglichkeit' = possibility."]],"krv-a-0346":["Nevertheless we do not lack proofs of the great influence this principle of ours has in anticipating perceptions, and even in supplementing their lack to such an extent that it bolts the door against all false inferences that might be drawn from it.",["Grundsatz = principle; Beweisthum = proof; Wahrnehmung = perception; Schluß = inference.","'Riegel vorschieben' idiom rendered literally ('bolts the door').","'daraus' is ambiguous (from the lack of perceptions, or from the principle); preserved with 'from it'."]],"krv-a-0347":["If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation an infinite sequence of steps of ever lesser degrees takes place, and nevertheless every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations, then no perception, and hence also no experience, is possible that would prove a total lack of everything real in the appearance, whether immediately or mediately, (through whatever detour in inferences one may wish), i.e., a proof of empty space or of an empty time can never be drawn from experience. For the total lack of the real in sensible intuition can, first, not itself be perceived, second, it can be concluded from no single appearance and the difference of the degree of its reality, or may also never be assumed for the explanation of it. For even if the whole intuition of a determinate space or time is real through and through, i.e., no part of it is empty; yet, because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive magnitude of the appearance unchanged, can decrease through infinite steps down to nothing (the empty), there must be infinitely different degrees with which space or time is filled, and the intensive magnitude in different appearances can be smaller or greater, although the extensive magnitude of the intuition is equal.",["Stufenfolge rendered literally as 'sequence of steps' (Folge = sequence); Grad = degree; Mangel = lack; Beweis = proof; Anschauung = intuition; Größe = magnitude.","'derselben' (after Erklärung) is left as ambiguous as the German: 'of it'; its antecedent could be the appearance or the lack.","Original has 'in Schlüssen' with the dative 'im Schlüssen'; rendered 'in inferences'. 'Schlüsse' = inference."]],"krv-a-0348":["We want to give an example of this. Almost all teachers of nature, since they perceive a great difference in the quantity of matter of different kinds under equal volume (partly through the moment of gravity, or of weight, partly through the moment of resistance against other moved matters), unanimously conclude from it: this volume (extensive magnitude of the appearance) must be empty in all matters, although in different measure. But who of these for the most part mathematical and mechanical investigators of nature would ever have let it occur to himself that they ground this their inference solely on a metaphysical presupposition, which they nevertheless pretend so much to avoid, in that they assume that the real in space (I do not wish to call it here impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical concepts,) is everywhere one and the same, and can differ only according to extensive magnitude, i.e., according to multitude. To this presupposition, for which they could have no ground in experience, and which is therefore merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental proof, which indeed is not supposed to explain the difference in the filling of spaces, but nevertheless entirely removes the supposed necessity of that presupposition of being able to explain the stated difference in no other way than through empty spaces that are to be assumed, and has the merit of putting the understanding at least in freedom to think this diversity also in another way, if the explanation of nature should make some hypothesis necessary for this. For there we see that, although equal spaces may be completely filled by different matters, so that in neither of the two is there a point in which their presence would not be encountered, nevertheless every real, with the same quality, has its degree (of resistance or of weighing), which, without diminution of the extensive magnitude or multitude, can become infinitely smaller before it passes into the empty and vanishes. Thus an expansion that fills a space, e.g., warmth, and in the same way every other reality (in the appearance), without leaving the smallest part of this space empty in the least, can decrease in its degrees to infinity, and none the less fill the space with these smaller degrees just as well as another appearance with greater ones. My aim here is by no means to assert that this is actually how it stands with the diversity of matters, according to their specific gravity, but only to demonstrate from a principle of the pure understanding that the nature of our perceptions makes such a way of explanation possible, and that one falsely assumes the real of the appearance to be equal according to degree, and different only according to aggregation and its extensive magnitude, and even asserts this, ostensibly, through a principle of the understanding *a priori*.",["Naturlehrer rendered 'teachers of nature' (Lehre = doctrine) and Naturforscher 'investigators of nature'; Menge = multitude; Aufgabe not involved.","'Schweere' = gravity, 'Gewicht' = weight; 'Beweis' = proof; 'Erklärung/Erklärungsart' = explanation; 'Absicht' = aim; 'Grundsatz' = principle.","'Ausspannung' rendered literally as 'expansion'; 'vorgeblicher massen' as 'ostensibly'.","'Verstand in Freyheit zu versetzen' kept close: 'putting the understanding ... in freedom'.","Undurchdringlichkeit = impenetrability; Reale = the real (substantivized adjective)."]],"krv-a-0349":["Nevertheless this anticipation of perception has, for an investigator accustomed to the transcendental and made cautious thereby, always something striking about it, and arouses some hesitation about the fact that the understanding such a synthetic proposition as that of the degree of everything real in appearances is, and hence of the possibility of the inner difference of sensation itself, if one abstracts from its empirical quality, and it is thus still a question not unworthy of resolution: how the understanding can pronounce synthetically *a priori* about appearances, and anticipate these even in that which is properly, and merely, empirical, namely concerns sensation.",["The German sentence beginning 'daß der Verstand einen dergleichen synthetischen Satz ...' has no finite predicate for 'der Verstand' (apparently broken off or defective); the English follows it as it stands and supplies nothing.","Satz = proposition; Auflösung = resolution; Bedenken = hesitation; Empfindung = sensation; Qualität = quality.","'die Empfindung angeht': the grammatical construction is preserved ('namely concerns sensation')."]],"krv-a-0350":["The quality of sensation is always merely empirical, and cannot be represented *a priori* at all (e.g., colors, taste, etc.). But the real that corresponds to sensations in general, in opposition to negation = 0, represents only something the concept of which in itself contains a being, and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness in general. For in inner sense the empirical consciousness can be raised from 0 to every greater degree, so that just the same extensive magnitude of intuition (e.g., an illuminated surface) arouses as great a sensation as an aggregate of much else (less illuminated) together. One can therefore abstract entirely from the extensive magnitude of the appearance, and yet represent in the mere sensation in one moment a synthesis of the uniform increase from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness. All sensations are therefore, as such, indeed given only *a priori*, but the property of them, that they have a degree, can be cognized *a priori*. It is remarkable that in magnitudes in general we can cognize *a priori* only a single quality, namely continuity, but in all quality (the real of appearances) nothing further *a priori* than the intensive quantity of it, namely that they have a degree, everything else remains left to experience.",["DOUBTFUL READING: the text as given reads 'nur a priori gegeben' in 'Alle Empfindungen werden daher ... zwar nur a priori gegeben'; translated as it stands, but the sense of 'zwar nur ... gegeben, aber ... a priori erkant' may call for a different reading in the printed edition; to be checked against the original.","bedeutet = signifies; Gegensatz = opposition; Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary); Steigerung = increase; Moment = moment.","'ihrer ... das Reale ... correspondirt' etc.: 'Quantität' = quantity, 'Qualität' = quality; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'Bewustseyn' = consciousness.","'Seyn' = being."]],"krv-a-0351":["3. The Analogies of Experience.",[]],"krv-a-0352":["The general principle of them is: All appearances stand, according to their existence, *a priori* under rules of the determination of their relation to one another in one time.",["Daseyn = existence; Grundsatz = principle; Bestimmung = determination; Verhältnis = relation.","'derselben' (the Analogies) rendered 'of them'."]],"krv-a-0353":["The three *modi* of time are persistence, sequence and simultaneity. Hence three rules of all temporal relations of appearances, according to which the existence of each can be determined with regard to the unity of all time, will precede all experience, and first make it possible.",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Folge = sequence; Zugleichseyn = simultaneity (literally 'being-simultaneous'); Einheit = unity.","'diese' in 'diese allererst möglich machen' is plural in the German; rendered 'it' (referring to experience) only because English requires it; the plural referent may be ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0354":["The general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception, with regard to all possible empirical consciousness (of perception), at every time, consequently, since that lies at the basis *a priori*, on the synthetic unity of all appearances according to their relation in time. For the original apperception relates to inner sense (the sum total of all representations), and indeed *a priori* to the form of it, i.e., the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. In the original apperception all this manifold is now to be unified, according to its temporal relations; for this is what the transcendental unity of it says *a priori*, under which stands everything that is to belong to my (i.e., my single) cognition, hence can become an object for me. This synthetic unity in the temporal relation of all perceptions, which is determined *a priori*, is thus the law: that all empirical determinations of time must stand under rules of the general determination of time, and the analogies of experience, with which we now want to deal, must be rules of such a kind.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand = object; Erkenntnis = cognition; Gesetz = law; Zeitbestimmung = determination of time.","'des mannigfaltigen empirischen Bewustseyns' is rendered 'the manifold empirical consciousness', keeping the German's ambiguity (manifold as adjective or as 'manifold of').","'a priori' in 'dieses sagt die transscendentale Einheit derselben a priori' is left unattached, as in the German (it may go with 'says' or with 'unity').","'einigen' rendered 'single'; 'vor mich' = 'for me'."]],"krv-a-0355":["These principles have this peculiarity about them, that they consider not the appearances and the synthesis of their empirical intuition, but merely the existence, and their relation to one another with regard to this their existence. Now the way in which something is apprehended in the appearance can be determined a priori in such a way that the rule of its synthesis can at the same time give this intuition a priori in every empirical example at hand: i.e., can bring it about from that. But the existence of appearances cannot be cognized a priori, and, even if we could arrive by this path at inferring to some existence, we would nevertheless not cognize it determinately, i.e., we would not be able to anticipate that by which its empirical intuition would be distinguished from others.",["Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Existenz also = existence elsewhere in this selection (e.g., krv-a-0360).","Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; erkennen = cognize; bestimmt = determined, bestimt (adv.) = determinately.","'a priori' is left in roman position as in the German, attached ambiguously to 'be determined' / 'give'; 'in dergestalt' rendered 'in such a way'.","'ihre' / 'seiner' / 'sie daraus': pronouns kept as in German; 'sie' in 'sie daraus zu Stande bringen' refers grammatically to the intuition (Anschauung) or the rule; left ambiguous in English as 'it'.","'Synthesis' kept as in the German; 'apprehendirt' = apprehended (apprehendere).","'schliessen' = infer (to infer to some existence); 'anticipiren' = anticipate."]],"krv-a-0356":["The previous two principles, which I called the mathematical, in consideration of the fact that they authorized applying mathematics to appearances, went to appearances in accordance with their mere possibility, and taught how they could be generated, both as to their intuition and as to the real in their perception, according to rules of a mathematical synthesis; hence both in the one and in the other numerical magnitudes, and, with them, the determination of the appearance as magnitude, can be used. Thus, e.g., I will be able to compose the degree of the sensations of sunlight from roughly 200,000 illuminations by the moon and give it determinately a priori, i.e., construct it. Hence we can call the former principles constitutive.",["Grundsatz = principle; Grösse = magnitude; Zahlgrössen = numerical magnitudes (Zahl = number).","'die mathematische' (sic, plural elided in German 'nante') rendered 'the mathematical'; 'Realen' = the real; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception.","'bestimt' = determinate(ly); 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'construiren' = construct.","'gingen auf ... ihrer blossen Möglichkeit nach': 'went to' for 'gingen auf' kept literal.","'Empfindungen' = sensations; 'Grad' = degree."]],"krv-a-0357":["It must be quite otherwise with those that are to bring the existence of appearances under rules a priori. For, since this does not let itself be constructed, they will go only to the relation of existence, and can yield no other than merely regulative principles. Thus here there is to be thought neither of axioms nor of anticipations, but, if a perception is given to us in a time-relation to others (though indeterminate ones); then it will not be possible to say a priori: which other and how great a perception, but rather how it is necessarily combined with that one as to existence, in this *modus* of time. In philosophy analogies signify something very different from that which they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulas that assert the equality of two relations of magnitude, and are always constitutive, so that, if two members of the proportion are given, the third is also thereby given, i.e., can be constructed. But in philosophy the analogy is not the equality of two quantitative, but of qualitative relations, where from three given members I can cognize and give a priori only the relation to a fourth, but not this fourth member itself, though I do have a rule for seeking it in experience, and a mark for finding it therein. An analogy of experience will therefore be only a rule according to which unity of experience (not as perception itself, as empirical intuition in general) is to arise from perceptions, and as a principle of the objects (of appearances) is to hold not constitutively but merely regulatively. But precisely the same will also hold of the postulates of empirical thinking in general, which concern together the synthesis of mere intuition (the form of appearance), of perception (the matter of it), and of experience (the relation of these perceptions), namely that they are only regulative principles, and are distinguished from the mathematical ones, which are constitutive, not indeed in certainty, which stands firm a priori in both, but still in the way of evidence, i.e., the intuitive in them, (and hence also of demonstration).",["Verhältniß = relation; Daseyn = existence; Zeitverhältnisse = time-relations; Zeit = time.","'bedeuten' = signify; 'vorstellen' = represent; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'Merkmal' = mark (nota).","'modo' (Latin, roman in the original) = *modus* given in italics; the German has the ablative 'modo'; 'in diesem modo der Zeit' kept in Latin as printed: rendered *modus*.","'Grundsatz' = principle, but 'Principien' (regulative Principien) also = principles; one English word for both.","'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Einheit' = unity; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception.","'(nicht wie Wahrnehmung selbst, als empirische Anschauung überhaupt)': parenthesis kept; its attachment is ambiguous and left so.","'der Evidenz, d. i. dem Intuitiven derselben, (mithin auch der Demonstration)': case and syntax kept; 'derselben' ambiguous as to referent.","'Postulaten' = postulates; 'Materie' = matter; 'Form' = form; 'Gewißheit' = certainty."]],"krv-a-0358":["But what was recalled with all synthetic principles, and must be especially noted here, is this: that these analogies have their sole significance and validity not as principles of the transcendental, but merely of the empirical use of the understanding, and hence also can only be proved as such, that consequently the appearances must be subsumed not under the categories simpliciter, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which these principles are to be related were things in themselves; then it would be quite impossible to cognize anything of them synthetically a priori. Now they are nothing but appearances, whose complete cognition, in which all principles a priori must in the end always run out, is solely possible experience, consequently those can have as their goal nothing but merely the conditions of the unity of empirical cognition in the synthesis of appearances; but this is thought only in the schema of the pure concept of the understanding, of whose unity, as of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function not restricted by any sensible condition. Through these principles, therefore, we will be authorized to put together the appearances only according to an analogy with the logical and universal unity of concepts, and hence to make use in the principle itself indeed of the category, but in the execution (the application to appearances) to set the schema of it, as the key to its use, in its place, or rather to set that beside it, as restricting condition, under the name of a formula of the former.",["'schlechthin' = simpliciter rendered literally as 'simpliciter' would add Latin not in the German; the German reads 'schlechthin', so 'without qualification' would be closer: correct reading is 'categories without qualification' (the 'simpliciter' above is an error of mine; read 'schlechthin' = absolutely/without qualification).","Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding; Bedeutung = significance; Gültigkeit = validity; beweisen = prove; Gegenstände = objects; Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves.","'subsumiret' = subsumed; 'Schemate' = schemata; 'Categorien' = categories; 'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'Erkentnisses' = cognition.","'iene' ('those') = the principles; 'diese' = the synthesis (or possibly the cognition/unity); referents left as in German.","'Ausführung' = execution; 'restringirende' = restricting; 'Schlüssel' = key; 'Formel' = formula.","'an dessen Stelle, oder iener vielmehr...zur Seite setzen': the construction is kept; 'dessen' (the category's) and 'iener' (the schema) are left ambiguous in the English as in the German."]],"krv-a-0359":["A. First Analogy. Principle of Persistence.",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Grundsatz = principle."]],"krv-a-0360":["All appearances contain the persistent (substance) as the object itself, and the changeable as its mere determination, i.e., a way in which the object exists.",["Beharrliche = persistent (n.); Wandelbare = changeable (n.); Gegenstand = object; Bestimmung = determination; Art = way; existirt = exists (Existenz/Dasein both = existence).","'Substanz' left as 'substance'."]],"krv-a-0361":["Proof of this first analogy.",["Beweis = proof."]],"krv-a-0362":["All appearances are in time. This can determine the relation in their existence in two ways, either insofar as they are one after another or simultaneous. In regard to the former, time is considered as time-series, with respect to the second as time-domain.",["Daseyn = existence; Zeitreihe = time-series; Zeitumfang = time-domain (Umfang = domain).","'zugleich' = simultaneous / at the same time; 'nach einander' = one after another.","'Diese' (fem.) = Zeit; 'derselben' = of the appearances (or of time) is ambiguous in German; English 'their' is left similarly ambiguous.","'Weise' = way; the same English word as for Art; 'betrachtet' = considered (Betrachtung = consideration)."]],"krv-a-0363":["Our apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive, and is therefore always alternating. Thus we can never determine through it alone whether this manifold, as object of experience, is simultaneous or follows one after another, where there does not lie at the ground of it something that is at all times, i.e., something abiding and persistent, of which all alternation and being-simultaneous are nothing but so many ways (*modi* of time) in which the persistent exists. Only in the persistent, therefore, are time-relations possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time), i.e., the persistent is the *substratum* of the empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all time-determination is possible. Persistence expresses time in general as the constant *correlatum* of all existence of appearances, of all alternation and of all accompaniment. For alternation does not affect time itself, but only the appearances in time (just as being-simultaneous is not a *modus* of time itself, in which no parts at all are simultaneous, but all are one after another). If one wanted to ascribe to time itself a sequence one after another, one would have to think yet another time in which this sequence would be possible. Through the persistent alone does existence in different parts of the time-series, one after another, acquire a magnitude, which one calls duration. For in mere sequence alone existence is always vanishing and beginning, and never has the least magnitude. Without this persistent there is therefore no time-relation. Now time cannot be perceived in itself; hence this persistent in the appearances is the *substratum* of all time-determination, consequently also the condition of the possibility of all synthetic unity of perceptions, i.e., of experience, and in this persistent all existence and all alternation in time can be regarded only as a *modus* of the existence of that which abides and persists. Thus in all appearances the persistent is the object itself, i.e., the substance (*phaenomenon*), but everything that alternates, or can alternate, belongs only to the way in which this substance or substances exist, hence to their determinations.",["Wechsel = alternation (wechseln = alternate); Beharrliche/Beharrlichkeit = persistent/persistence; Bleibendes = abiding; Daseyn and Existenz are both \"existence\" (existirt = exists).","\"wo an ihr nicht etwas zum Grunde liegt\": \"ihr\" (fem.) could refer to the appearance or to the manifold; \"wo\" kept as \"where\" (conditional/locative sense left open).","\"so viel Arten ... wie das Beharrliche existirt\": the construction is elliptical; \"wie\" can be read as \"in which\" or \"as/how\". Rendered \"ways ... in which\".","Zugleichseyn = \"being-simultaneous\", distinguished from Simultaneität = \"simultaneity\"; Zeitbestimmung = time-determination; Zeitverhältnisse = time-relations.","Latin kept in italics: modi/modus, substratum, correlatum, phaenomenon (Kant's \"phænomenon\"). Art = way; Gegenstand = object; Wahrnehmung = perception; Grösse = magnitude; Dauer = duration."]],"krv-a-0364":["I find that at all times not merely the philosopher but even the common understanding has presupposed this persistence as a *substratum* of all alternation of appearances, and will also always assume it as undoubted, except that the philosopher expresses himself somewhat more determinately about this, in that he says: in all alterations in the world the substance abides, and only the accidents alternate. But I nowhere encounter even the attempt at a proof of this so synthetic proposition, indeed it also only seldom stands, as it nevertheless deserves, at the head of the pure and wholly *a priori* subsisting laws of nature. In fact the proposition that substance is persistent is tautological. For merely this persistence is the ground why we apply the category of substance to the appearance, and one would have had to prove that in all appearances there is something persistent, in which the changeable is nothing but determination of its existence. But since such a proof can never be conducted dogmatically, i.e., from concepts, because it concerns a synthetic proposition *a priori*, and one never thought that such propositions are valid only in reference to possible experience, hence can also be proved only through a deduction of the possibility of the latter; so it is no wonder if it has indeed been laid at the ground of all experience (because one feels the need of it for empirical cognition), but has never been proved.",["\"er\" in the last clause (masc.) could refer to the proof (Beweis) or the proposition (Satz); rendered \"it\" to keep this open.","Veränderung = alteration; Wechsel = alternation; Accidenzen = accidents; Wandelbare = changeable; Bestimmung = determination.","Beziehung = \"reference\" (kept distinct from Verhältniß = \"relation\" elsewhere in this group); Gesetze = laws; Satz = proposition.","tavtologisch (sic) = tautological; a priori is italicized as Latin; Erkentniß = cognition."]],"krv-a-0365":["A philosopher was asked: how much does smoke weigh? He answered: subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the ashes remaining, and you have the weight of the smoke. He thus presupposed as incontestable that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but only the form of it suffers a modification. In just the same way the proposition: from nothing comes nothing, was only another corollary from the principle of persistence, or rather of the everlasting existence of the proper subject in the appearances. For if that in the appearance which one wants to call substance is to be the proper *substratum* of all time-determination, then all existence, in past time as well as that of future time, must be able to be determined solely and alone in it. Hence we can give an appearance the name substance only because we presuppose its existence at all times, which is not even well expressed by the word persistence, since this goes more to future time. Nevertheless the inner necessity to persist is yet inseparably combined with the necessity of having always been, and the expression may therefore remain. *Gigni de nihilo nihil, in nihilum nil posse reverti* were two propositions that the ancients connected inseparably, and that from misunderstanding one now sometimes separates, because one represents to oneself that they concern things in themselves, and the former might be opposed to the dependence of the world on a supreme cause (even as to its substance), which worry is unnecessary, since here there is talk only of appearances in the field of experience, whose unity would never be possible if we wanted to let new things (as to substance) arise. For then that would drop out which alone can represent the unity of time, namely the identity of the *substratum*, as that in which all alternation alone has thoroughgoing unity. This persistence is yet nothing further than the way of representing to ourselves the existence of things (in the appearance).",["Abänderung = \"modification\" (kept distinct from Veränderung = alteration); Folgesatz = \"corollary\"; Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition.","Daseyn = existence; Subiect = subject; Besorgniß = \"worry\"; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; durchgängige = thoroughgoing.","Latin line kept in the original and italicized; \"verbunden\" rendered \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination), \"verknüpfte\" rendered \"connected\".","\"Ding an sich selbst\" = thing in itself; Art = way; vorstellen = represent."]],"krv-a-0366":["The determinations of a substance, which are nothing other than particular ways of its existing, are called accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations that express the non-being of something in the substance). If one now ascribes to this real in the substance a particular existence (e.g., to motion, as an accident of matter), then one calls this existence inherence, in distinction from the existence of substance, which one calls subsistence. But from this many misinterpretations arise, and it is spoken more precisely and more correctly if one designates the accident only through the way in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Nevertheless, by virtue of the conditions of the logical use of our understanding, it is unavoidable to separate off, as it were, that which can alternate in the existence of a substance while the substance abides, and to consider it in relation to the proper persistent and radical; hence this category also stands under the title of relations, more as the condition of them than because it itself contained a relation.",["Accidenz = accident; Inhärenz = inherence; Subsistenz = subsistence; Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding.","Verhältniß = relation; Realen = real; Radicale = \"radical\" (noun, as in the German).","\"mehr, als die Bedingung derselben, als daß sie selbst ein Verhältniß enthielte\": the comparative construction is awkward in the German and is kept so; \"derselben\" could refer to relations or to accidents.","Negation = negation; Nichtseyn = non-being."]],"krv-a-0367":["On this persistence is now also grounded the rectification of the concept of alteration. Arising and perishing are not alterations of that which arises or perishes. Alteration is a way of existing that follows upon another way of existing of the very same object. Hence everything that alters is abiding, and only its state alternates. Since this alternation therefore concerns only the determinations, which can cease or also begin; so we can say, in an expression seeming somewhat paradoxical: only the persistent (the substance) is altered, the changeable suffers no alteration, but rather an alternation, since some determinations cease and others begin.",["Veränderung = alteration (sich verändern = alter); Wechsel = alternation; Entstehen/Vergehen = arising/perishing.","Berichtigung = rectification; Zustand = state; Gegenstand = object; Art zu existiren = way of existing.","\"Da ... so können wir\" is a single sentence with a semicolon in the original; the German punctuation is kept."]],"krv-a-0368":["Alteration can therefore be perceived only in substances, and arising or perishing, absolutely, without its merely concerning a determination of the persistent, can be no possible perception at all, since precisely this persistent makes possible the representation of the transition from one state into another, and from non-being to being, which can therefore be cognized empirically only as alternating determinations of that which remains. Assume that something absolutely begins to be; then you must have a point of time in which it was not. But to what will you attach this, if not to that which is already there? For an empty time that would precede is no object of perception; but if you connect this arising with things that were previously, and that endure up to what arises, then the latter was only a determination of the former, as of the persistent. It is just the same with perishing: for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time in which an appearance is no more.",["Veränderung = alteration; Wahrnehmung = perception; Beharrliche = the persistent; Bestimmung = determination; Vorstellung = representation; Zustand = state; Zeitpunct = point of time.","Gegenstand = object (here); Erscheinung = appearance; erkant = cognized (erkennen).","'die also nur als wechselnde Bestimmungen ... erkant werden können': the relative 'die' could refer to the representation or to the transition and the passage from non-being to being; the English 'which' preserves the ambiguity.","'Nichtseyn, zum Seyn': 'non-being' and 'being' render Nichtseyn and Seyn; 'Daseyn' elsewhere = existence.","Original spellings (e.g. 'indem' for 'in dem', 'heften', 'fortdauren') are normalized without comment in the English."]],"krv-a-0369":["Substances (in the appearance) are the substrates of all determinations of time. The arising of some, and the perishing of others, of the same would itself cancel the sole condition of the empirical unity of time, and the appearances would then be related to two kinds of time, in which existence would flow side by side, which is absurd. For there is only one time, in which all different times must be posited not simultaneously but one after another.",["OCR 'derselbenwürde' silently corrected to 'derselben würde'.","'derselben' (genitive plural): could refer to the substances or to the determinations of time; the English 'of the same' leaves this open.","Zeitbestimmungen = determinations of time; Bedingung = condition; Einheit = unity; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); gesezt = posited (setzen); zweyerley Zeit = 'two kinds of time'.","'nicht zugleich, sondern nach einander': 'not simultaneously but one after another'."]],"krv-a-0370":["Thus persistence is a necessary condition under which alone appearances, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. But as to what the empirical criterion of this necessary persistence, and with it of the substantiality of appearances, may be, the sequel will give us occasion to remark what is necessary.",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Criterium = criterion; Substanzialität = substantiality; Folge = sequel (here 'what follows', not Folge as consequence).","Dinge and Gegenstände both = 'things' and 'objects' respectively; Gegenstände = objects (Object does not occur here).","bestimbar = determinable (bestimmbar), spelling normalized.","'das Nöthige anzumerken': 'to remark what is necessary' (literally 'the necessary'; not 'notwendig')."]],"krv-a-0371":["B. Second Analogy. Principle of Generation.",["Grundsatz = principle; Erzeugung = generation."]],"krv-a-0372":["Everything that happens (begins to be) presupposes something upon which it follows according to a rule.",["geschieht = happens; anhebt zu seyn = begins to be; Regel = rule."]],"krv-a-0373":["Proof.",["Beweis = proof."]],"krv-a-0374":["The apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive. The representations of the parts follow one upon another. Whether they also follow one another in the object is a second point of reflection, which is not contained in the former. Now one can indeed call everything, and even every representation, insofar as one is conscious of it, an object; but what this word is to signify in the case of appearances, not insofar as they are (as representations) objects, but only designate an object, requires deeper investigation. Insofar as they, merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not at all distinguished from apprehension, i.e., from reception into the synthesis of the imagination, and one must therefore say: the manifold of appearances is always produced successively in the mind. Were appearances things in themselves, then no human being would be able to gauge from the succession of the representations of their manifold how this is combined in the object. For we have to do only with our representations; how things in themselves (without regard to the representations through which they affect us) may be is entirely outside our sphere of cognition. Now although appearances are not things in themselves, and yet are nevertheless the only thing that can be given to us for cognition, I am to indicate what sort of combination in time belongs to the manifold in the appearances themselves, while the representation of it in apprehension is always successive. Thus, e.g., the apprehension of the manifold in the appearance of a house that stands before me is successive. Now the question is whether the manifold of this house itself is also successive in itself, which of course no one will concede. But now, as soon as I raise my concepts of an object up to the transcendental meaning, the house is not a thing in itself at all, but only an appearance, i.e., a representation, whose transcendental object is unknown; what then do I understand by the question: how the manifold in the appearance itself (which is yet nothing in itself) may be combined? Here that which lies in the successive apprehension is considered as representation, but the appearance that is given to me, notwithstanding that it is nothing further than a sum total of these representations, is considered as the object of them, with which my concept, which I draw from the representations of apprehension, is to agree. One soon sees that, because agreement of cognition with the object is truth, only the formal conditions of empirical truth can be asked about here, and appearance, in the relation opposite to the representations of apprehension, can be represented as the object of them distinct from them only if it stands under a rule that distinguishes it from every other apprehension and makes one way of combination of the manifold necessary. That in the appearance which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension is the object.",["Obiect/Gegenstand both = 'object': Obiect (Latinate) in 'Obiect nennen', 'ein Obiect bezeichnen', 'in dem Obiect', 'mit dem Obiect', 'Obiect derselben', 'ist das Obiect'; Gegenstand(e) in 'Gegenstände des Bewustseyns', 'transscendentaler Gegenstand', 'als der Gegenstand derselben', 'im Gegenstande'.","Apprehension kept as 'apprehension'; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Verbindung/verbunden = combination/combined; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Gemüth = mind (Gemüt).","'Man siehet bald... Gegenverhältniß': 'in the relation opposite to' renders 'im Gegenverhältniß mit'; 'Inbegriff' = sum total; 'Erkentnißsphäre' = 'sphere of cognition'.","'nicht, in so fern sie (als Vorstellungen) Obiecte sind, sondern nur ein Obiect bezeichnen': the syntax is obscure; kept as in German ('designate' = bezeichnen).","'ermessen' rendered 'gauge'; 'vor eine Verbindung' = 'what sort of combination' (vor = für, 'what ... of'); 'Aufnahme' = reception.","'dessen transscendentaler Gegenstand unbekant ist': 'dessen' refers to 'Vorstellung' (or to Erscheinung); English 'whose' preserves this."]],"krv-a-0375":["Now let us proceed to our problem. That something happens, i.e., that something, or a state, comes to be that previously was not, cannot be empirically perceived unless an appearance precedes which does not contain this state in itself; for an actuality that follows upon an empty time, hence an arising before which no state of things precedes, can no more be apprehended than empty time itself. Every apprehension of an occurrence is therefore a perception that follows upon another. But because this is so constituted in all synthesis of apprehension, as I have shown above in the appearance of a house, it is thereby not yet distinguished from others. But I also remark: that if in an appearance that contains a happening I call the preceding state of the perception A, the following one however B, then B can only follow A in apprehension, but perception A cannot follow B, but only precede. I see, e.g., a ship drifting down the stream. My perception of its place lower down follows upon the perception of its place higher up along the course of the river, and it is impossible that in the apprehension of this appearance the ship should be perceived first lower down, but afterwards higher up the stream. The order in the sequence of the perceptions in the apprehension is here therefore determined, and the latter is bound to it. In the previous example of a house my perceptions in the apprehension could begin at its peak and end at the ground, but also begin from below and end above, likewise apprehend the manifold of the empirical intuition to the right or to the left. In the series of these perceptions there was therefore no determinate order which made it necessary when I had to begin in the apprehension in order to connect the manifold empirically. But this rule is always to be encountered in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the perceptions following one another (in the apprehension of this appearance) necessary.",["Aufgabe = problem; Zustand = state; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Begebenheit = occurrence; Geschehen/geschieht = happening/happens; Anschauung = intuition; Wahrnehmung = perception; Folge = sequence (in 'Folge der Wahrnehmungen').","'sonden' (OCR/original) silently corrected to 'sondern'; 'bestimt' = determined (bestimmt), 'determinate' for 'bestimte'.","'und an dieselbe ist die leztere gebunden': 'the latter' = apprehension (or possibly 'the latter' of the two perceptions); the English keeps 'the latter'.","'welche es nothwendig machte, wenn ich in der Apprehension anfangen müßte': the clause is obscure (wenn = 'when'/'if'); kept as 'when', which preserves the ambiguity with 'with what'.","'Spitze' = peak; 'Boden' = ground; 'Stelle' = place (Ort is the glossary term for Ort, not used here)."]],"krv-a-0376":["I will therefore, in our case, have to derive the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of appearances, because otherwise the former is entirely undetermined, and distinguishes no appearance from another. The former alone proves nothing about the connection of the manifold in the object, because it is wholly arbitrary. The latter, therefore, will consist in the order of the manifold of appearance, in accordance with which the apprehension of the one (what happens) follows upon that of the other (which precedes) according to a rule. Only thereby can I be warranted in saying of the appearance itself, and not merely of my apprehension, that a sequence is to be encountered in it, which signifies as much as that I cannot arrange the apprehension otherwise than precisely in this sequence.",["Folge is rendered 'sequence' throughout (glossary: Folge = sequel; sequence); Apprehension is kept as the cognate 'apprehension'.","Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Mannigfaltige = 'manifold'; Object/Obiect = 'object'; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; bedeuten = 'signify'.","'Jene'/'iene' (the former) and 'Diese' (the latter): the referents are kept as the German pronouns leave them, rendered 'the former'/'the latter'.","'berechtigt seyn, zu sagen' rendered 'be warranted in saying'; 'in iener' could refer back to 'Erscheinung' (the appearance itself), as rendered.","'unbestimt' (sic, for unbestimmt) = 'undetermined'; 'beliebig' = 'arbitrary'."]],"krv-a-0377":["According to such a rule, therefore, in that which in general precedes an occurrence there must lie the condition for a rule according to which this occurrence always and necessarily follows; but conversely I cannot go back from the occurrence and determine (through apprehension) that which precedes. For from the following point of time no appearance goes back to the previous one, but yet it is related to some previous one; from a given time, on the contrary, the progress to the determinate following one is necessary. Hence, because it is nevertheless something that follows, I must necessarily relate it to something else in general that precedes, and upon which it follows according to a rule, i.e., necessarily, so that the occurrence, as the conditioned, gives secure direction to some condition, but this determines the occurrence.",["Begebenheit = 'occurrence' (glossary); Ereignis (= 'event') does not occur here.","Fortgang = 'progress'; bedingt = 'conditioned'; Bedingung = 'condition'; bestimmen = 'determine'.","'vor einer Begebenheit' is rendered 'in general ... an occurrence' with 'überhaupt' placed as in the German: 'in dem, was überhaupt vor einer Begebenheit vorhergeht' (could be read with 'was' or with 'vorhergeht').","'beziehet sich doch auf irgend einen vorigen': the German 'sich' leaves the subject ambiguous between the appearance and the point of time; kept as 'it is related'.","'diese aber die Begebenheit bestimt': 'diese' (this) = the condition, as the feminine singular 'Bedingung' requires.","'Zeitpunct' = 'point of time'."]],"krv-a-0378":["Suppose that nothing precedes an occurrence upon which it would have to follow according to a rule, then all sequence of perception would be only solely in apprehension, i.e., merely subjective, but would thereby not at all be determined objectively as to which would properly be the preceding and which the succeeding among the perceptions. We would in such a way have only a play of representations, which would not be related to any object, i.e., through our perception one appearance would not at all be distinguished from every other, as far as the time-relation is concerned; because the succession in apprehending is everywhere one and the same, and thus there is nothing in the appearance that determines it, so that thereby a certain sequence is made objectively necessary. I will therefore not say: that in the appearance two states follow one another, but only: that one apprehension follows the other, which is merely something subjective, and determines no object, hence cannot count at all as cognition of any object (even not in the appearance).",["Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Vorstellung = 'representation'; Zustand = 'state'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Gegenstand and Obiect both = 'object' (here Obiect in 'kein Obiect', Gegenstand in 'irgend eines Gegenstandes').","Succession (Latinate) and Folge (German) are both 'succession'/'sequence': 'Succession' is rendered 'succession', 'Folge' 'sequence'.","'dem Zeitverhältnisse nach' = 'as far as the time-relation is concerned'; 'allerwerts' = 'everywhere'.","'vor Erkentniß' (for für/vor) = 'as cognition'; 'selbst nicht in der Erscheinung' kept elliptical: 'even not in the appearance'.","'welches eigentlich das Vorhergehende...' rendered with 'properly'; 'müßte' (subjunctive) kept as 'would'."]],"krv-a-0379":["If we therefore experience that something happens, we always presuppose thereby that something or other precedes, upon which it follows according to a rule. For without this I would not say of the object: that it follows, because the mere sequence in my apprehension, if it is not determined through a rule in relation to something preceding, warrants no sequence in the object. It therefore always happens with regard to a rule, according to which the appearances in their sequence, i.e., as they happen, are determined through the previous state, that I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and only merely under this presupposition alone is even the experience of something that happens possible.",["erfahren = 'experience' (verb), Erfahrung = 'experience'; geschehen = 'happen'.","'Rücksicht' = 'regard'; 'berechtiget' = 'warrants'.","'daß ich meine subiective Synthesis ... obiectiv mache' is left in its original syntactic position, dependent on 'es geschieht ... immer in Rücksicht auf eine Regel'.","'nur lediglich ... allein' = 'only merely ... alone' (the redundancy kept)."]],"krv-a-0380":["To be sure it seems as if this contradicted all the remarks that have ever been made about the course of the use of our understanding, according to which we have been guided only first of all, through the perceived and compared agreeing sequences of many occurrences upon preceding appearances, to discover a rule in accordance with which certain occurrences always follow upon certain appearances, and have thereby been prompted first to make for ourselves the concept of cause. On such a footing this concept would be merely empirical, and the rule that it provides, that everything that happens has a cause, would be just as contingent as experience itself: its universality and necessity would then only be fabricated, and would have no true universal validity, because they would be grounded not a priori, but only on induction. But it goes here as with other pure representations a priori (e.g., space and time), which we can extract as clear concepts from experience only because we had put them into experience, and thereby first brought the latter about through them. To be sure the logical clarity of this representation of a rule determining the series of occurrences, as a concept of cause, is possible only when we have made use of it in experience, but a regard to it, as condition of the synthetic unity of appearances in time, was nevertheless the ground of experience itself, and thus preceded it a priori.",["Verstandesgebrauch = 'use of our understanding'; Gebrauch = 'use'; Begriff = 'concept'; Raum = 'space'; Zeit = 'time'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Gültigkeit = 'validity'; rein = 'pure'; synthetisch = 'synthetic'; Einheit = 'unity'.","'a priori' is in Latin and is italicized per the method; the passage prints it in roman: *a priori*.","'Rücksicht auf dieselbe': 'dieselbe' refers back to the representation (Vorstellung) of a rule, kept as 'it'.","'Nothwendigkeit' = 'necessity'; 'Allgemeinheit' = 'universality'; 'angedichtet' = 'fabricated' (literally 'poetically attributed').","'Auf solchen Fuß' = 'On such a footing'.","'Induction' = 'induction'; the long first sentence is syntactically awkward (the 'nach welchen' clause) and is kept as is.","'Klarheit' = 'clarity'; 'klare Begriffe' = 'clear concepts'; 'Reihe' = 'series'."]],"krv-a-0381":["It therefore comes down to showing by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute the sequence (of an occurrence, where something happens that previously was not) to the object, and distinguish it from the subjective one of our apprehension, except when a rule lies at the ground that necessitates us to observe this order of perceptions rather than another, indeed that this necessitation is properly what first makes the representation of a succession in the object possible.",["'als wenn' (= 'except when'/'than when') is kept loosely: 'niemals ... als wenn eine Regel zum Grunde liegt'; 'als' here has an exceptive sense.","'nöthig' (for nöthigt) = 'necessitates'; Nöthigung = 'necessitation'; 'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the ground'.","Obiect = 'object'; Succession = 'succession'; Folge = 'sequence'.","'vielmehr' = 'rather'; 'allererst' = 'first'."]],"krv-a-0382":["We have representations in us, of which we can also become conscious. This consciousness, however, may be extended as far, and be as exact or punctual, as one wishes, they still remain only ever representations, i.e., inner determinations of our mind in this or that time-relation. How do we now come to posit an object for these representations, or, over and above their subjective reality, as modifications, to attribute to them yet, I know not what, objective one. Objective meaning cannot consist in the relation to another representation (of that which one would like to call of the object), for otherwise the question renews itself, how does this representation in turn go out of itself, and get objective meaning over and above the subjective, which is proper to it, as determination of the state of mind? If we investigate what new constitution the relation to an object gives to our representations, and what is the dignity that they thereby receive, we find that it does nothing further than to make the combination of the representations necessary in a certain way, and to subject them to a rule; that conversely only by the fact that a certain order in the time-relation of our representations is necessary is objective meaning conferred upon them.",["Bewustseyn = 'consciousness'; bewust werden = 'become conscious'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Gemüth = 'mind' (Gemüthszustand = 'state of mind'); Realität = 'reality'; Bedeutung = 'meaning'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Verbindung = 'combination'; Gegenstand and Obiect = 'object'.","'so bleiben es doch nur immer Vorstellungen': the 'so' after the concessive 'mag' is rendered literally as a run-on, without 'yet' or 'nevertheless' added.","'ich weis nicht, was vor eine, obiective' is elliptical (a noun such as 'Realität' is missing after 'obiective'); rendered 'objective one'.","'von dem, was man vom Gegenstande nennen wollte' is obscure and kept literal: 'of that which one would like to call of the object'.","'Dignität' = 'dignity'; 'Modificationen' = 'modifications'; 'Zeitverhältniß' = 'time-relation'.","'Beziehung' = 'relation'; 'Regel' = 'rule'; 'unterwerfen' = 'subject'.","'Wie kommen wir nun dazu: daß wir ...' is followed by a period in the original, not a question mark; kept as such."]],"krv-a-0383":["In the synthesis of appearances the manifold of representations always follows one after another. Through this no object at all is represented; because through this sequence, which is common to all apprehensions, nothing is distinguished from the other. But as soon as I perceive, or presuppose, that in this sequence there is a relation to the preceding state, from which the representation follows according to a rule; then something represents itself as an occurrence, or as what happens, i.e., I cognize an object, which I must posit in time at a certain determinate place, which, given the preceding state, cannot be assigned to it otherwise. If I therefore perceive that something happens, then in this representation it is contained, first: that something precedes, because it is precisely in relation to this that the appearance gets its temporal relation, namely, to exist after a preceding time in which it was not. But it can get its determinate temporal place in this relation only by something being presupposed in the preceding state, upon which it always, i.e., according to a rule, follows; from which it then results that I cannot, first, reverse the series, and place what happens before that upon which it follows: second, that, if the state that precedes is posited, this determinate occurrence follows unavoidably and necessarily. Through this it happens: that there comes to be an order among our representations, in which the present (insofar as it has come to be) gives direction to some preceding state, as a, though still undetermined, correlatum of this event that is given, which however relates itself determiningly to this, as its sequel, and connects it necessarily with itself in the temporal series.",["Obiect / Gegenstand: both rendered 'object' (here 'Obiect' in the first sentences, 'Gegenstand' in 'ich erkenne einen Gegenstand').","Folge (sequence/sequel): 'diese Folge' = 'this sequence'; 'als seine Folge' = 'as its sequel'; the verb folgen = 'follow'.","Begebenheit = occurrence; 'Eräugniß' (Ereignis) = event; erkennen = cognize; Zustand = state; Vorstellung = representation; Mannigfaltige = manifold.","'die ihm ... nicht anders ertheilt werden kan': the antecedent of 'die' is 'Stelle'; 'ihm' = the object. Kept as in the German.","'diese bestimmte Begebenheit unausbleiblich und nothwendig folge': subjunctive kept as dependent on 'daß'; 'zweitens daß' is parallel to 'daß ich erstlich'.","'sich auf diese ... bestimmend bezieht': 'bestimmend' is a participle/adverb, 'determiningly'; 'diese' and 'sie' are ambiguous between 'Eräugniß' and the present, left as in German.","'Anweisung giebt' rendered 'gives direction'; 'Stelle' = place; 'Zeitverhältniß' = temporal relation; 'Correlatum' kept in Latin form, italics omitted as printed roman only by OCR uncertainty."]],"krv-a-0384":["If it is now a necessary law of our sensibility, hence a formal condition of all perceptions: that the previous time necessarily determines the following; (in that I cannot arrive at the following except through the preceding), then it is also an indispensable law of the empirical representation of the temporal series, that the appearances of the past time determine every existence in the following, and that these, as occurrences, do not take place except insofar as those determine their existence in time, i.e., fix it according to a rule. For only in appearances can we empirically cognize this continuity in the interconnection of times.",["Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Zusammenhang = interconnection; erkennen = cognize.","'iene' and 'diese' (the appearances of the past time / of the following time) are left as bare pronouns; 'ihnen' refers to 'diese' (the latter).","'nicht statt finden, als so fern': rendered literally 'do not take place except insofar as'."]],"krv-a-0385":["To all experience and its possibility belongs understanding, and the first thing that it does to this end is not: that it makes the representation of objects distinct, but that it makes the representation of an object in general possible. This now happens by its transferring the temporal order to the appearances and their existence, in that it grants to each of them as sequel a place in time determined a priori with regard to the preceding appearances, without which it would not agree with time itself, which determines a priori to all its parts their place. This determination of place cannot now be borrowed from the relation of appearances to absolute time, (for that is no object of perception) but conversely, the appearances must determine for one another their places in time itself, and make these necessary in the temporal order, i.e., that which follows, or happens, must follow according to a universal rule upon that which was contained in the previous state, from which there arises a series of appearances, which by means of the understanding produces and makes necessary precisely the same order and continuous interconnection in the series of possible perceptions as is encountered a priori in the form of inner intuition, (time) in which all perceptions would have to have their place.",["Verstand = understanding; deutlich = distinct; Gegenstand = object (both 'Gegenstände' and 'Gegenstandes'; 'Obiect' is not used here); Anschauung = intuition.","'a priori bestimte Stelle': 'a priori' could attach to 'bestimte' or to 'Stelle'/'zuerkent'; left ambiguous. Likewise 'a priori' in 'allen ihren Theilen a priori ihre Stelle bestimt' and at the end ('angetroffen wird').","'ihr' / 'jeder derselben': 'sie' in 'nicht ... übereinkommen würde' is singular (each appearance); 'dieselbe' in 'dieselbe in der Zeitordnung nothwendig machen' is singular in German ('Stelle'); rendered 'these' for the plural 'Stellen'.","'als sie ... angetroffen wird': the comparison 'eben dieselbige ... als' construed as 'precisely the same ... as'; the German is awkward and the subject of 'angetroffen wird' is left unresolved.","'Folge' (as sequel) = Folge; 'möglicher' = possible; Zusammenhang = interconnection."]],"krv-a-0386":["That something happens is therefore a perception that belongs to a possible experience, which becomes actual by my regarding the appearance, according to its place in time, as determined, hence as an object, which can always be found according to a rule in the interconnection of perceptions. This rule, however, for determining something according to temporal sequence, is: that in that which precedes the condition is to be encountered under which the occurrence always (i.e., necessarily) follows. Thus the principle of sufficient ground is the ground of possible experience, namely of the objective cognition of appearances, with regard to their relation in the succession of time.",["Obiect = object; Wahrnehmung = perception; Bedingung = condition; Begebenheit = occurrence; Erkentniß = cognition.","'wirklich wird': 'wirklich' = actual.","'Satz vom zureichenden Grunde' = 'principle of sufficient ground' (Satz rendered 'principle' here as the established name; Grundsatz is also 'principle').","'Reihenfolge der Zeit' = 'succession of time' (Reihenfolge rendered 'succession', glossary Abfolge = succession)."]],"krv-a-0387":["The ground of proof of this principle, however, rests solely on the following moments. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold through the imagination, which is always successive, i.e., the representations always follow one another in it. The sequence, however, is not determined at all in the imagination as to order (what must precede and what must follow), and the series of the one of the following representations can just as well be taken backwards as forwards. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given appearance), then the order is determined in the object, or, to speak more precisely, there is in it an order of the successive synthesis, which determines an object, according to which something must necessarily precede, and if this is posited, the other must necessarily follow. If therefore my perception is to contain the cognition of an occurrence, since namely something actually happens, then it must be an empirical judgment, in which one thinks that the sequence is determined, i.e., that it presupposes another appearance in time, upon which it necessarily, or according to a rule, follows. Otherwise, if I posit what precedes, and the occurrence did not necessarily follow upon it, I would have to hold it only for a subjective play of my imaginings, and if I nevertheless represented something objective by it, would have to call it a mere dream. Thus the relation of appearances (as possible perceptions) according to which the subsequent (what happens) is determined as to its existence necessarily by something preceding and according to a rule in time, hence the relation of cause to effect, is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgments, with regard to the series of perceptions, hence of their empirical truth, and thus of experience. The principle of the causal relation in the sequence of appearances therefore holds also for all objects of experience, (under the conditions of succession) because it is itself the ground of the possibility of such an experience.",["Beweisgrund = ground of proof; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Apprehension = apprehension; Synthesis = synthesis; Mannigfaltige = manifold.","Obiect / Gegenstand: both 'object' ('im Obiect', 'ein Obiect bestimt'; 'Gegenständen der Erfahrung').","'die Reihe der einen der folgenden Vorstellungen': obscure as in the German; rendered literally.","'Ist aber diese Synthesis eine Synthesis der Apprehension': kept; 'da nemlich etwas wirklich geschieht': 'da' kept as 'since namely' (could also be 'where').","'und stellete ich mir darunter doch etwas obiectives vor, sie einen blossen Traum nennen': the verb 'müssen' is carried over by ellipsis from the preceding clause, left elliptical as in German.","'vor allen Gegenständen' (= für): rendered 'for' via 'also for all'; 'Caussalverhältnisses' = causal relation; 'Succeßion' = succession.","Urtheil = judgment; Gültigkeit = validity; Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Wahrheit = truth."]],"krv-a-0388":["Here, however, a scruple still emerges that must be removed. The proposition of the causal connection among appearances is restricted in our formula to the sequence of them, although in the use of it it is found that it also fits their accompaniment, and that cause and effect can be simultaneous. There is, e.g., warmth in the room that is not encountered in the open air. I look around for the cause, and find a heated stove. Now this, as cause, is simultaneous with its effect, the warmth of the room; thus here there is no sequence according to time between cause and effect, but they are simultaneous, and the law nevertheless holds. The greatest part of the efficient cause in nature is simultaneous with its effects, and the temporal sequence of the latter is occasioned only by the fact that the cause cannot carry out its entire effect in one moment. But in the moment in which it first arises, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause, because if that had ceased to be one moment earlier, this would not have arisen at all. Here one must note well: that what is looked at is the order of time, and not its lapse: the relation remains even if no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect can be vanishing (they thus simultaneous), but the relation of the one to the other nevertheless always remains determinable according to time. If I consider as cause a ball that lies on a stuffed cushion and presses a dimple into it, then it is simultaneous with the effect. Yet I distinguish the two nonetheless through the time-relation of the dynamical connection of the two. For if I lay the ball on the cushion; then upon its previous smooth shape the dimple follows; but if the cushion has (I do not know whence) a dimple, a leaden ball does not follow upon it.",["Satz der Caussalverknüpfung: Satz rendered 'proposition'; Verknüpfung = 'connection' (connexio); Reihenfolge = 'sequence', Zeitfolge = 'temporal sequence' (Folge = sequence).","'der größte Theil der wirkenden Ursache' is singular in the German ('cause'), followed by plural 'Wirkungen'; kept as it stands. 'iene' / 'diese' rendered 'that' / 'this'; their referents are left as ambiguous as in the German.","'der Zeit nach' = 'according to time'; Verhältniß = 'relation'; Ablauf = 'lapse'; Begleitung = 'accompaniment'; Gebrauch = 'use'."]],"krv-a-0389":["Accordingly the temporal sequence is certainly the sole empirical criterion of the effect in relation to the causality of the cause that precedes. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although both appearances are simultaneous. For as soon as I scoop this out of a larger vessel with the glass, something follows, namely the alteration of the horizontal level that it had there into a concave one that it assumes in the glass.",["Veränderung = 'alteration'; Horizontalstand = 'horizontal level'; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Criterium = 'criterion'.","'dieses' (the water) and 'es' (the water or the level) are left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0390":["This causality leads to the concept of action, this to the concept of force, and thereby to the concept of substance. Since I do not want to mix my critical project, which goes solely to the sources of synthetic cognition *a priori*, with dissections that concern merely the clarification (not amplification) of concepts, I leave the detailed exposition of them to a future system of pure reason: although one encounters such an analysis in rich measure even in the textbooks of this kind known up to now. But the empirical criterion of a substance, insofar as it seems to reveal itself not through the persistence of the appearance but better and more easily through action, I cannot leave untouched.",["Erläuterung = 'clarification'; Erweiterung = 'amplification' (not in the glossary; Ausdehnung is 'extension'); Erörterung = 'exposition'; Vorhaben = 'project'; Zergliederungen = 'dissections'.","Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Beharrlichkeit = 'persistence'; Handlung = 'action'."]],"krv-a-0391":["Where there is action, hence activity and force, there is also substance, and in this alone must the seat of that fruitful source of appearances be sought. That is said quite well: but if one is to declare oneself on what one understands by substance, and wants to avoid the faulty circle thereby, it is not so easily answered for. How does one want to infer straightaway from the action to the persistence of the one acting, which is yet such an essential and peculiar mark of substance (*phaenomenon*)? However, according to our previous, the resolution of the question has no such difficulty, although according to the common way (of proceeding merely analytically with its concepts) it would be entirely unresolvable. Action already signifies the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Since now all effect consists in what happens, hence in the mutable, which designates time according to succession; the last subject of it is the persistent, as the substratum of everything changing, i.e. substance. For according to the principle of causality actions are always the first ground of all change of appearances, and thus cannot lie in a subject that itself changes, because otherwise other actions and another subject that determined this change would be required. By virtue of this action proves, as a sufficient empirical criterion, substantiality, without my needing first to seek its persistence through compared perceptions, which also could not be done on this path with the thoroughness that is required for the magnitude and strict universal validity of the concept. For that the first subject of the causality of all arising and passing away cannot itself arise and pass away (in the field of appearances) is a certain inference, which runs out into empirical necessity and persistence in existence, hence into the concept of a substance as appearance.",["'nach unserm vorigen' is elliptical in the German (no noun supplied); left elliptical.","Kennzeichen = 'mark' (the glossary's Merkmal is also 'mark'); hinreichend = 'sufficient' (as zureichend in the glossary); Wechsel = 'change'; Wandelbaren = 'mutable'; bedeuten = 'signify'.","Grundsatz = 'principle'; Schluß = 'inference'; Allgemeingültigkeit = 'universal validity'; Dasein = 'existence'; Ausführlichkeit = 'thoroughness'; Kennzeichen/phaenomenon: the Latin is printed in roman type in the source and is italicized here.","'dessen' in 'Kraft dessen' and 'desselben' (twice) have unclear referents; kept as in the German ('this' for dessen, 'its'/'of it')."]],"krv-a-0392":["If something happens, then the mere arising, without regard to what arises there, is already in itself an object of investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into this state, supposing that this also contained no quality in the appearance, is already alone necessary to investigate. This arising affects, as was shown in number A, not the substance (for that does not arise), but its state. It is thus merely alteration, and not origin from nothing. If this origin is regarded as effect of a foreign cause, then it is called creation, which cannot be admitted as an occurrence among appearances, since its possibility alone would already cancel the unity of experience, although, if I consider all things not as *phenomena* but as things in themselves, and as objects of the mere understanding, they, although they are substances, can nevertheless be regarded as dependent in their existence on a foreign cause, which however would then entail entirely other word-meanings, and would not fit appearances, as possible objects of experience.",["Gegenstand = 'object'; Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Zustand = 'state'; Veränderung = 'alteration'; Dasein = 'existence'; Verstand = 'understanding'.","'trift' rendered 'affects'; 'ist schon allein nöthig zu untersuchen' kept literally, so that 'already alone' may modify the transition or the necessity.","'Nummer A' left as printed (a reference to an earlier section); Wortbedeutungen = 'word-meanings'; Phönomene = *phenomena*."]],"krv-a-0393":["How now something can be altered at all, how it is possible that a state in one point of time can be followed by an opposite one in another, of that we have not the least concept *a priori*. For this the acquaintance with actual forces is required, which can be given only empirically, e.g., of the moving forces, or, what is one and the same, of certain successive appearances (as motions) that indicate such forces. But the form of every alteration, the condition under which it alone can proceed as an arising of another state, (the content of it, i.e. the state that is altered, may be whichever it will) hence the succession of the states itself (what has happened) can nevertheless be weighed *a priori* according to the law of causality and the conditions of time.*)",["Kentniß = 'acquaintance' (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance); wirklich = 'actual'; Kräfte = 'forces'; Form, Inhalt = 'form', 'content'; Bedingungen = 'conditions'.","erwogen = 'weighed'; 'a priori' could attach to 'weighed' or to the whole predicate; left as ambiguous as the German. The asterisk refers to the following footnote."]],"krv-a-0394":["*) Note well: that I am speaking not of the alteration of certain relations in general, but of alteration of the state. Hence, if a body moves uniformly, it does not alter its state (of motion) at all, but certainly does if its motion increases or decreases.",["Relationen = 'relations'; Cörper = 'body'; gleichförmig = 'uniformly'; 'wol' rendered 'well' in 'Man merke wol' and 'certainly' in 'aber wol'."]],"krv-a-0395":["If a substance passes from a state a into another b, then the point of time of the second is distinguished from the point of time of the first state, and follows it. Likewise the second state, as reality (in the appearance), is distinguished from the first, in which this was not, as b is from zero, i.e., even if the state b were to differ from the state a only in magnitude, the alteration is an arising of b—a, which was not in the previous state, and with regard to which it is = 0.",["Veränderung rendered \"alteration\" here and throughout; Zustand = \"state\"; Realität = \"reality\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Größe = \"magnitude\".","\"it is = 0\": the German \"er\" refers grammatically to \"Zustand\" (the previous state); kept as in the German.","\"Zero\" is Kant's spelling; rendered \"zero\"."]],"krv-a-0396":["It is therefore asked: how a thing passes from a state = a into another = b. Between two instants there is always a time, and between two states in them always a difference, which has a magnitude (for all parts of appearances are always in turn magnitudes). Thus every transition from one state into the other occurs in a time that is contained between two instants, of which the first determines the state from which the thing departs, the second the one into which it arrives. Both, therefore, are boundaries of the time of an alteration, hence of the intermediate state between both states, and as such belong along with it to the whole alteration. Now every alteration has a cause, which proves its causality in the whole time in which the former proceeds. Thus this cause does not bring forth its alteration suddenly (all at once or in one instant), but in a time, so that, as the time grows from the initial instant a to its completion in b, the magnitude of the reality (b—a) is also generated through all the smaller degrees that are contained between the first and the last. All alteration is therefore possible only through a continuous action of causality, which, insofar as it is uniform, is called a moment. The alteration does not consist of these moments, but is generated by them as its effect.",["Augenblick = \"instant\"; Zeitpunct (previous passage) = \"point of time\".","Grenzen = \"boundaries\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Ursache = \"cause\"; Caussalität = \"causality\".","\"iene\" (\"the former\") in \"in welcher iene vorgeht\" is kept ambiguous as in the German; it picks up Veränderung.","\"Grösse\" in \"Grösse der Realität\" and elsewhere = \"magnitude\".","Original spellings (zween, Caussalität, bestimt, plözlich) normalized silently."]],"krv-a-0397":["This now is the law of the continuity of all alteration, the ground of which is this: that neither time, nor also the appearance in time, consists of parts that are the smallest, and that nevertheless the state of the thing in its alteration passes through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no difference of the real in the appearance, just as no difference in the magnitude of the times, that is the smallest, and so the new state of reality grows from the first, in which this was not, through all the infinite degrees of it, the differences of which from one another are all smaller than that between o and a.",["Gesetz der Continuität = \"law of continuity\"; Grund = \"ground\".","\"die- Zeit\" is an OCR/typographic slip for \"die Zeit\"; silently corrected.","\"o\" in \"between o and a\" kept as printed (the zero).","\"deren Unterschiede ... kleiner seyn\": the plural \"seyn\" is kept as \"are\"; \"derselben\" (\"of it\") refers to Realität.","\"Es ist kein Unterschied ... der kleineste\": the construction is kept elliptical; \"that is the smallest\" attaches to \"difference\"."]],"krv-a-0398":["What use this proposition may have in the investigation of nature, that does not concern us here. But how such a proposition, which seems to extend our cognition of nature so far, is possible fully *a priori*, that requires our examination very much, even if the appearance proves that it is actual and correct, and one would therefore like to believe oneself relieved of the question of how it was possible. For there are so many kinds of unfounded presumptions of the extension of our cognition through pure reason: that it must be adopted as a general principle to be thoroughly distrustful on that account, and without documents that can provide a thorough deduction, to believe and accept nothing of the sort, even on the clearest dogmatic proof.",["Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Beweis = \"proof\"; Wirklich = \"actual\"; reine Vernunft = \"pure reason\".","\"völlig a priori möglich\": \"fully\" could modify \"a priori\" or \"möglich\"; the ambiguity is preserved.","\"Augenschein\" rendered \"the appearance\" would collide with Erscheinung; here it is the term \"Augenschein\", rendered \"appearance\" in the sense of what is evident to the eye; note it is distinct from Erscheinung.","\"der kläresten\" (sic) = \"the clearest\"."]],"krv-a-0399":["All increase of empirical cognition, and every progress of perception, is nothing but an extension of the determination of inner sense, i.e., a progression in time, whatever the objects may be, appearances or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is in itself determined by nothing further, i.e., the parts of it are only in time, and through the synthesis of it, but are not given prior to it: On that account every transition in perception to something that follows in time is a determination of time through the generation of this perception, and since the latter, always and in all its parts, is a magnitude, the generation of a perception as a magnitude through all degrees, none of which is the smallest, from zero up to its determinate degree. From this there now becomes clear the possibility of cognizing a law of alterations, as to their form, *a priori*. We anticipate only our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, since it dwells in us prior to all given appearance itself, must certainly be able to be cognized *a priori*.",["Fortschritt = \"progress\"; Fortgang = \"progression\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; bestimmen = \"determine\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\".","\"iene\" (\"the latter\") after \"da\" is ambiguous in the German between the generation/perception and the determination; \"the latter\" is kept as a rendering of \"iene\" and is not resolved.","\"desselben\" and \"derselben\" refer back to \"Fortgang\" and \"Zeit\"/\"Synthesis\"; the pronouns are left as in the German.","\"sie aber nicht vor ihr gegeben\": \"sie\" (the parts) and \"ihr\" (the synthesis) are kept as pronouns.","The sentence beginning \"und da iene ...\" has no finite main clause in the German as printed; kept as it stands.","\"Apprehension\" is Kant's Latinate word, kept as \"apprehension\"; \"anticipiren\" = \"anticipate\"; \"erkant\" = \"cognized\"; \"Innern Sinnes\" = \"inner sense\"."]],"krv-a-0400":["Thus, just as time contains the sensible condition *a priori* of the possibility of a continuous progression of what exists to what follows, so the understanding, by means of the unity of apperception, is the condition *a priori* of the possibility of a continuous determination of all places for the appearances in this time, through the series of causes and effects, of which the former inevitably draw after them the existence of the latter, and thereby make the empirical cognition of the relations of time for every time (universally), hence objectively valid.",["Verstand = \"understanding\"; Apperception = \"apperception\"; Daseyn = \"existence\"; obiectiv gültig = \"objectively valid\".","\"vor\" (in \"vor die Erscheinungen\", \"vor iede Zeit\") is eighteenth-century usage for \"für\"; rendered \"for\".","\"deren die erstere der leztern ihr Daseyn\": colloquial genitive-dative construction; \"of which the former ... the existence of the latter\".","\"Zeitverhältnisse\" = \"relations of time\"."]],"krv-a-0401":["C. Third Analogy. Principle of community.",["Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"."]],"krv-a-0402":["All substances, insofar as they are simultaneous, stand in thoroughgoing community (i.e., interaction among one another).",["zugleich = \"simultaneous\" (here, as elsewhere, \"at the same time\"/\"simultaneous\" for zugleich); Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\"; Substanz = \"substance\".","\"seyn\" is the subjunctive/old plural form of \"sein\"; rendered \"are\"."]],"krv-a-0403":["Proof.",["Beweis = \"proof\"."]],"krv-a-0404":["Things are simultaneous insofar as they exist in one and the same time. But by what does one recognize that they are in one and the same time? When the order in the synthesis of the apprehension of this manifold is indifferent, i.e., can go from A through B, C, D to E, or also conversely from E to A. For if it were in time successive (in the order that begins from A and ends in E), then it is impossible to begin the apprehension in perception from E and to proceed backwards to A, because A belongs to the past time, and therefore can no longer be an object of apprehension.",["Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\"; Synthesis = \"synthesis\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; erkent = \"recognizes\" (erkennen; glossary alternative \"cognize\").","\"gleichgültig\" = \"indifferent\".","\"wäre sie ... nach einander\": \"sie\" refers to the order; \"in der Zeit nach einander\" rendered \"in time successive\".","The sentence beginning \"Wenn die Ordnung ...\" has no main clause in the German; kept as a fragment."]],"krv-a-0405":["Assume now: in a manifoldness of substances as appearances each of them were fully isolated, i.e., none acted into the other, and received from it reciprocally influences, then I say: that their simultaneity would not be an object of a possible perception, and that the existence of the one could lead by no path of empirical synthesis to the existence of the other. For if you think them as separated by a fully empty space, then the perception which proceeds from the one to the other in time would indeed determine the existence of this latter by means of a following perception, but could not distinguish whether the appearance follows objectively upon the former, or is rather simultaneous with it.",["Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\" (distinct from Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\"); Zugleichseyn = \"simultaneity\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Raum = \"space\".","\"dieser ihr Daseyn\": \"of this latter\" (the second substance); \"iener\" = \"that\" (the first); the pronouns are kept as in the German.","\"Einflüsse\" = \"influences\"; \"wechselseitig\" = \"reciprocally\"."]],"krv-a-0406":["There must therefore be something besides mere existence, by which A determines for B its place in time, and conversely B in turn for A, because only under this condition can substances thought of as existing simultaneously be represented empirically. Now only that determines for another its place in time which is the cause of it, or of its determinations. Thus every substance (since it can be a sequel only with regard to its determinations) must contain in itself the causality of certain determinations in the other, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other, i.e., they must stand in dynamical community (immediate or mediate), if simultaneity is to be recognized in any possible experience. Now, however, everything is necessary with regard to the objects of experience without which the experience of these objects itself would be impossible. Thus it is necessary for all substances in the appearance, insofar as they are simultaneous, to stand in thoroughgoing community of interaction among one another.",["Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; notwendig = \"necessary\".","\"die Ursache von ihm, oder seinen Bestimmungen\": \"of it, or of its determinations\"; the antecedent of \"ihm\" is kept as the German pronoun.","\"vorgestellt\" = \"represented\" (Vorstellung = representation); \"erkant\" = \"recognized\".","\"(unmittelbar oder mittelbar)\" = \"(immediate or mediate)\"."]],"krv-a-0407":["The word community is ambiguous in our language, and can signify as much as *communio*, but also as *commercium*. We here make use of it in the latter sense, as a dynamical community, without which even the local one (*communio spatii*) could never be empirically cognized. It is easy to remark from our experiences that only the continuous influences in all places of space can lead our sense from one object to another, that the light, which plays between our eye and the world-bodies, effects a mediate community between us and these, and thereby proves the being-simultaneous of the latter, that we cannot empirically alter any place (perceive this alteration) without matter everywhere making the perception of our position possible for us, and this, only by means of its reciprocal influence, can demonstrate its being-simultaneous, and thereby, as far as the most remote objects, the coexistence of the same (although only mediately). Without community every perception (of the appearance in space) is broken off from the others, and the chain of empirical representations, i.e., experience, would begin entirely anew with a new object, without the previous one being able to hang together with it in the slightest, or to stand in a temporal relation. I do not at all intend hereby to refute empty space: for that may always be, wherever perceptions do not reach, and thus no empirical cognition of being-simultaneous takes place; but it is then no object at all for all our possible experience.",["Gemeinschaft = community; bedeuten = signify; Erkenntnis (Erkentniß) = cognition; erkant = cognized.","Weltkörpern rendered literally 'world-bodies'; Zugleichseyn = 'being-simultaneous' kept literal throughout.","Gegenstand and Obiect both = 'object' (Gegenstande/Obiect in the last sentence: 'Obiect').","'Stelle' = position/place; 'Ort' = place; 'Stelle' rendered 'position' in 'our position' and 'place' elsewhere in the following passages; the German uses two words.","'und diese nur vermittelst...darthun kan': the subject 'diese' (matter) is kept as in the German; the grammar is loose.","'vor' (= für) rendered 'for'; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Einfluß' = influence."]],"krv-a-0408":["The following can serve for clarification. In our mind all appearances, as contained in a possible experience, must stand in community (*communio*) of apperception, and insofar as the objects are to be represented as connected existing simultaneously, they must reciprocally determine their place in a time, and thereby make up a whole. If this subjective community is to rest on an objective ground, or to be related to appearances as substances, then the perception of the one, as ground, must make possible the perception of the other, and conversely, so that the succession, which is always in the perceptions, as apprehensions, will not be ascribed to the objects, but these can be represented as existing simultaneously. But this is a reciprocal influence, i.e., a real community (*commercium*) of substances, without which, therefore, the empirical relation of being-simultaneous could not take place in experience. Through this *commercium* appearances, insofar as they stand outside one another and yet in connection, make up a composite (*compositum reale*), and composites of this kind become possible in manifold ways. The three dynamical relations, from which all the rest arise, are therefore that of inherence, of consequence, and of composition.",["Erläuterung = clarification; Gemüthe = mind; Grund = ground; Succeßion = succession; Verknüpfung (verknüpft) = connection (connected).","'zugleichexistirend' = 'existing simultaneously'; 'Obiecten/Gegenstände' both = 'objects'.","'ein zusammengeseztes aus' is as printed; 'a composite' translates it, with 'compositum reale' following in the text.","'Inhärenz, Consequenz, Composition' kept as cognates ('inherence, consequence, composition'); Consequenz is not Folgerung."]],"krv-a-0409":["These, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing other than principles of the determination of the existence of appearances in time, according to all three *modi* of the same, the relation to time itself, as a magnitude (the magnitude of existence, i.e., duration), the relation in time, as a series (one after another), finally also in it, as a sum total of all existence, (simultaneous). This unity of time-determination is through and through dynamical, i.e., time is not regarded as that in which experience would immediately determine its place for every existence, which is impossible, because absolute time is no object of perception with which appearances could be held together; rather, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of appearances can acquire synthetic unity according to temporal relations, determines for each of them its place in time, hence a priori, and valid for each and every time.",["Grundsätze = principles; Daseyn = existence (Existenz would also be 'existence'); Grösse = magnitude; Inbegriff = sum total; Dauer = duration.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'gültig' = valid; 'bestimte' (subjunctive) = 'would determine'.","'hence a priori, and valid for...': 'a priori' may attach to 'determines' or to 'place'; left as in German.","'vor' = 'for'."]],"krv-a-0410":["By nature (in the empirical sense) we understand the interconnection of appearances as to their existence, according to necessary rules, i.e., according to laws. There are therefore certain laws, and indeed a priori, which first make a nature possible; the empirical ones can only by means of experience, and indeed in consequence of those original laws, according to which experience itself first becomes possible, take place, and be found. Our analogies therefore properly present the unity of nature in the interconnection of all appearances under certain exponents, which express nothing other than the relation of time (insofar as it comprehends all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can take place only in synthesis according to rules. Together they therefore say: all appearances lie in one nature, and must lie therein, because without this unity a priori no unity of experience, hence also no determination of the objects in it, would be possible.",["Verstande (im empirischen Verstande) = 'sense' here is a rendering of 'Verstand' in the idiom 'im empirischen Verstande'; literally 'understanding', rendered 'sense' for the idiom. Alternative: 'in the empirical understanding'.","Zusammenhang = interconnection; Natureinheit = unity of nature; begreift = comprehends; Bestimmung = determination.","'Gegenstände' = objects.","'zufolge' = 'in consequence of'; 'a priori' after 'Gesetze, und zwar' could attach to 'laws' only; and 'without this unity a priori' may attach to 'unity' or to 'without... possible'."]],"krv-a-0411":["But a remark is to be made about the kind of proof that we have made use of in the case of these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiarity of the same, which must at the same time be very important as a prescription for every other attempt to prove intellectual and at the same time synthetic propositions a priori. Had we wanted to prove these analogies dogmatically, i.e., from concepts: namely that everything that exists is encountered only in what is persistent, that every occurrence presupposes something in the previous state, upon which it follows according to a rule, finally, that in the manifold that is simultaneous, the states in relation to one another are simultaneous according to a rule, (stand in community) then all effort would have been entirely in vain. For one cannot get from one object and its existence to the existence of another, or its way of existing, through mere concepts of these things, however one may analyze them. What then remained to us? The possibility of experience, as a cognition in which all objects must ultimately be able to be given to us, if their representation is to have objective reality for us. In this third thing, now, whose essential form consists in the synthetic unity of the apperception of all appearances, we found conditions a priori of the thoroughgoing and necessary time-determination of all existence in appearance, without which even the empirical time-determination would be impossible, and found rules of synthetic unity a priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. In the lack of this method, and with the delusion of wanting to prove dogmatically synthetic propositions, which the use of the understanding in experience recommends as its principles, it has happened that a proof has been attempted so often, but always in vain, of the proposition of sufficient ground. The two remaining analogies no one has thought of; although one always made use of them tacitly*), because the guiding thread of the categories was lacking, which alone can discover, and make noticeable, every gap of the understanding, in concepts as well as in principles.",["Beweisart = kind of proof; beharrlich = persistent; Begebenheit = occurrence; Zustand = state; Erkenntnis = cognition; Gebrauch = use; Satz = proposition; Grundsatz = principle.","'Gegenstand' and 'Obiect' = 'object'; 'Realität' = reality; 'Wahne' = 'delusion'.","'Satze des zureichenden Grundes' = 'proposition of sufficient ground' (Satz, not Grundsatz).","The footnote marker *) is kept after 'tacitly'; footnote follows as krv-a-0412.","Syntax of 'daß... die Zustände... zugleich seyn, (in Gemeinschaft stehen)' kept as in the German, which is irregular (indirect clause with 'seyn')."]],"krv-a-0412":["*) The unity of the world-whole, in which all appearances are to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the tacitly assumed principle of the community of all substances that exist simultaneously: for, were they isolated, they would not as parts make up a whole, and were their connection (interaction of the manifold) not already necessary on account of being-simultaneous, one could not infer from this, as a merely ideal relation, to that, as a real one. Although we have shown in its place: that community is properly the ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition, of coexistence, and that one therefore properly infers back only from this to that, as its condition.",["Folgerung = consequence; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Verknüpfung = connection; Grundsatz = principle; Weltganzen = world-whole.","'schliessen' = 'infer'; 'Erkentniß' = cognition.","'diesem... iene': 'this... that', antecedents left as in German (being-simultaneous / community); the second sentence is a fragment-like 'Wiewol' clause, kept."]],"krv-a-0413":["4. The postulates of empirical thinking in general.",[]],"krv-a-0414":["1. What agrees with the formal conditions of experience (in accordance with intuition and concepts) is possible.",["'der Anschauung und den Begriffen nach' = 'in accordance with intuition and concepts'; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0415":["2. What is interconnected with the material conditions of experience (of sensation) is actual.",["wirklich = actual; Empfindung = sensation; zusammenhängen = be interconnected."]],"krv-a-0416":["3. That whose interconnection with the actual is determined in accordance with universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessarily.",["'Dessen... ist (existirt) nothwendig' = 'That whose... is (exists) necessarily'; existirt = exists."]],"krv-a-0417":["Clarification.",["Erläuterung = clarification."]],"krv-a-0418":["The categories of modality have this particular feature about them: that they do not in the least augment, as a determination of the object, the concept to which they are attached as predicates, but rather only express the relation to the faculty of cognition. If the concept of a thing is already entirely complete, I can nevertheless still ask about this object whether it is merely possible, or also actual, or, if it is the latter, whether it is even also necessary? Through this no further determinations are thought in the object itself, but it is only asked how it (together with all its determinations) is related to the understanding and its empirical use, to the empirical power of judgment, and to reason (in its application to experience)?",["Categorien = categories; Prädicate = predicates; Obiect and Gegenstand both = 'object' (Obiects, Gegenstande).","Bestimmung = determination; Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition).","Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Gebrauch = use.","Original spelling 'frägt sich' ... 'verhalte' preserved as a single question; the final sentence ends in a question mark in the German, kept.","'besondere' in 'das besondere an sich' = 'particular' (noun use), rendered 'particular feature'; 'feature' has no word in German, supplied by English syntax only."]],"krv-a-0419":["Just for this reason the principles of modality are also nothing further than explanations of the concepts of possibility, actuality and necessity in their empirical use, and hereby at the same time restrictions of all categories to the merely empirical use, without admitting and permitting the transcendental. For, if these are not to have a merely logical significance, and to express the form of thinking analytically, but are to concern things and their possibility, actuality or necessity, then they must go to possible experience and its synthetic unity, in which alone objects of cognition are given.",["Grundsätze = principles; Erklärungen = explanations (also 'definitions' in the glossary); Bedeutung = significance.","Möglichkeit, Wirklichkeit, Nothwendigkeit = possibility, actuality, necessity; Wirklichkeit here 'actuality'.","'diese' ('these') is ambiguous in the German between the principles and the categories; left ambiguous in 'these'.","'Restrictionen' = 'restrictions' (Latinate, no Schranke); 'zuzulassen und zu erlauben' = 'admitting and permitting'.","'in welcher' (in which) could refer to experience or to its synthetic unity; ambiguity retained."]],"krv-a-0420":["The postulate of the possibility of things therefore demands that the concept of them agree with the formal conditions of an experience in general. This, namely the objective form of experience in general, however, contains all synthesis that is required for the cognition of objects. A concept that comprises a synthesis in itself is to be held for empty, and does not relate to any object, if this synthesis does not belong to experience, either as borrowed from it, and then it is called an empirical concept, or as one on which, as a condition a priori, experience in general (the form of it) rests, and then it is a pure concept, which nevertheless belongs to experience, because its object can be encountered only in this. For where is one to get the character of the possibility of an object that has been thought through a synthetic concept a priori, if it does not happen from the synthesis that makes up the form of the empirical cognition of objects. That no contradiction must be contained in such a concept is, to be sure, a necessary logical condition; but for the objective reality of the concept, i.e., the possibility of such an object as is thought through the concept, it is by far not enough. Thus in the concept of a figure that is enclosed in two straight lines there is no contradiction, for the concepts of two straight lines and of their coming together contain no negation of a figure; rather the impossibility rests not on the concept in itself, but on the construction of it in space, i.e., on the conditions of space and the determination of it; these, however, have in turn their objective reality, i.e., they go to possible things, because they contain in themselves the form of experience in general a priori.",["Obiect/Gegenstand both 'object'; 'Obiecte' in 'objective' = obiectiv.","Erkentniß = cognition; Bedingungen = conditions; Realität = reality; Widerspruch = contradiction; an sich = in itself.","'vor leer zu halten' = 'to be held for empty' (vor = für); 'erborgt' = 'borrowed'.","'a priori' in 'als Bedingung a priori' and in 'durch einen synthetischen Begriff a priori gedacht' and 'enthalten ... a priori': attachment ambiguous as in German; last clause could attach to 'contain' or to 'form'.","'Zusammenstossung' = 'coming together' (literally collision/meeting).","'diese' in the last sentence: grammatically 'these' = conditions of space and its determination (or construction); kept as 'these'.","'Verneinung' = 'negation'; 'Construction' = 'construction'; 'denn' = 'then' (old spelling of dann) in 'und denn heißt er'.","Sentence 'Denn wo will man ...' ends with a period in the German though a question in sense; period kept."]],"krv-a-0421":["And now we want to lay before our eyes the extensive utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. If I represent to myself a thing that is persistent, such that everything that alternates belongs merely to its state, then I can never cognize from such a concept alone: that a thing of the kind is possible. Or, I represent to myself something that is to be constituted such that, if it is posited, something else always and unfailingly follows upon it, then this may indeed be able to be thought so without contradiction; but whether such a property (as causality) is encountered in any possible thing cannot be judged through this. Finally I can represent to myself various things (substances) that are constituted such that the state of one draws after it a sequence in the state of the other, and so reciprocally, but whether such a relation could pertain to any things cannot be gathered at all from these concepts, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these concepts express a priori the relations of perceptions in every experience, does one recognize their objective reality, i.e., their transcendental truth, and indeed to be sure independently of experience, but yet not independently of all relation to the form of an experience in general, and the synthetic unity in which alone objects can be empirically cognized.",["beharrlich = persistent (beharren/Beharrlichkeit = persist/persistence); wechselt = alternates (wechseln).","erkennen = cognize (erkennen in 'erkent man' rendered 'recognize', glossary gives cognize; recognize); 'erkant werden' = 'be cognized'.","Zustand = state; gesezt = posited (setzen); Folge = sequence; Wahrnehmung = perception; Verhältnisse = relations.","Caussalität = causality; Eigenschaft = property (glossary: Beschaffenheit = constitution; property; here Eigenschaft is not glossed).","'beschaffen' = 'constituted' (Beschaffenheit).","'a priori' in 'ausdrücken ... a priori' could attach to the verb or to 'Wahrnehmungen' / 'Verhältnisse'; kept ambiguous.","'wechselsweise' = 'reciprocally' (lit. alternately), cf. Wechselwirkung.","'willkührliche' = 'arbitrary'; 'ie' in 'iederzeit' = 'always'."]],"krv-a-0422":["But if one wanted to make for oneself entirely new concepts of substances, of forces, of interactions, out of the material that perception offers us, without borrowing from experience itself the example of their connection; then one would end up in sheer chimeras, whose possibility has no mark at all in its favor, because with them one does not accept experience as teacher, nor borrow these concepts from it. Such invented concepts cannot obtain the character of their possibility, as the categories do, a priori, as conditions on which all experience depends, but only a posteriori, as such as are given through experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized a posteriori and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance that would be persistently present in space, yet without filling it (like that intermediate thing between matter and thinking beings which some have wanted to introduce), or a particular fundamental force of our mind to intuit the future in advance (not merely to conclude it), or finally a faculty of it to stand in community of thoughts with other human beings (however distant they may be), these are concepts whose possibility is entirely groundless, because it cannot be grounded on experience and its known laws, and without it is an arbitrary combination of thoughts which, although it contains no contradiction, yet can make no claim to objective reality, hence to the possibility of such an object as one would here think. As far as reality is concerned, it is surely forbidden of itself to think such a one in concreto without taking experience to help; because it can go only to sensation, as matter of experience, and does not concern the form of the relation, with which one could at most play in fictions.",["Stoff = material; Materie = matter; Kräfte = forces; Wechselwirkungen = interactions; Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination (Gedankenverbindung).","Hirngespinste = 'chimeras' (lit. brain-spinnings); Kennzeichen = 'mark' (not Merkmal; no glossary entry).","Gemüth = mind; Vermögen = faculty; Gemeinschaft = community; Wesen = being; Mittelding = 'intermediate thing'; Ding = thing.","anschauen = intuit (Anschauung); folgern = conclude; Empfindung = sensation; Erdichtungen = 'fictions'.","'und ohne sie eine willkührliche Gedankenverbindung ist die' is syntactically rough in the original ('sie' = Erfahrung/Gesetze); 'it' left ambiguous. 'die' is the relative pronoun ('which').","'eine solche' (such a one) refers to Realität; 'in concreto' kept in Latin italics: *in concreto*; also *a priori*, *a posteriori*.","'welche beharrlich ... gegenwärtig' = 'persistently present'."]],"krv-a-0423":["But I pass over everything whose possibility can be gathered only from actuality in experience, and consider here only the possibility of things through concepts a priori, of which I go on to assert: that it can never take place from such concepts on their own, but always only as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; erwägen = 'consider' (Betrachtung also 'consideration'); behaupten = assert.","'von denen' (of which) refers to concepts a priori or to things; 'sie' in 'daß sie' could be 'possibility' or 'things'; 'it' chosen with 'possibility' but ambiguity is in the German (plural 'sie' with 'Möglichkeit' singular: German 'sie' is both). English 'it' resolves this; plural 'they' (things) is also possible.","'vor sich allein' = 'on their own' (lit. for themselves alone).","'formale und obiective Bedingungen' = 'formal and objective conditions'; *a priori* kept in italics."]],"krv-a-0424":["It does indeed have the appearance as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from its concept in itself (it is certainly independent of experience); for in fact we can give an object to it entirely *a priori*, i.e., construct it. But because this is only the form of an object, it would still always remain only a product of imagination, the possibility of whose object would still remain doubtful, as for which something more is still required, namely that such a figure be thought under nothing but conditions on which all objects of experience rest. Now that space is a formal condition *a priori* of outer experiences, that precisely the same formative synthesis through which we construct a triangle in imagination is entirely one and the same with that which we exercise in the apprehension of an appearance in order to make for ourselves an experiential concept of it, this alone is what connects with this concept the representation of the possibility of such a thing. And thus the possibility of continuous magnitudes, indeed even of magnitudes in general, because the concepts of them are all synthetic, is never clear from the concepts themselves, but only from them, as formal conditions, of the determination of the objects in experience in general, and where indeed would one want to seek objects that corresponded to the concepts, were it not in experience, through which alone objects are given to us, although we, without precisely sending experience itself ahead, merely in relation to the formal conditions under which in it something in general is determined as object, thus entirely *a priori*, but yet only in relation to them, and within their boundaries, can cognize and characterize the possibility of things.",["Einbildungskraft = imagination (glossary); 'Einbildung' in the second sentence also rendered 'imagination'; the German words differ.","Gegenstand = object throughout; no Object occurs in this passage.","erkennen = cognize; Erkenntnis not present here. Begriff = concept; Erfahrungsbegriff = 'experiential concept'.","Bestimmung = determination; bestimmt = determined. Grenzen = boundaries. Möglichkeit = possibility.","'aus ihnen, als formalen Bedingungen, der Bestimmung der Gegenstände': the construction is obscure and left so; 'der Bestimmung' may be read as dependent on 'Bedingungen' (conditions of the determination) or otherwise. 'allererst klar' left without an explicit verb 'ist' supplied beyond the German's own.","'Wo sollte man auch ... suchen wollen' is kept as a question without question mark, following the German, whose sentence continues with 'wie wol'; the German has no final main clause, and 'ia so gar' is rendered 'indeed even'.","'Apprehension' keeps the Latinate German Apprehension; 'construiren' = construct; 'characterisiren' = characterize.","'als wozu' rendered 'as for which', keeping the awkward relative; 'sey' (subjunctive) rendered 'be'. 'a priori' italicized as Latin."]],"krv-a-0425":["The postulate, to cognize the reality of things, demands perception, hence sensation, of which one is conscious, to be sure not precisely immediately, of the object itself, whose existence is to be cognized, but yet interconnection of it with some actual perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which set out all real connection in an experience in general.",["Wirklichkeit = reality (glossary lists 'actuality; reality'); wirklich = actual. Realität is not present here, but 'real' translates 'reale' in 'reale Verknüpfung'.","Wahrnehmung = perception; Empfindung = sensation; Bewußtsein: 'bewust' = conscious; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Verknüpfung = connection; Daseyn = existence.","'zwar nicht eben unmittelbar, von dem Gegenstande selbst': the phrase 'of the object itself' may attach to 'perception/sensation' or to 'immediately'; left ambiguous as in German.","'Wirklichkeit der Dinge zu erkennen': 'to cognize' governs the postulate's content; 'darlegen' = set out."]],"krv-a-0426":["In the mere concept of a thing no mark of its existence can be encountered at all. For even though it may be so complete that not the least is lacking for thinking a thing with all its inner determinations, existence still has nothing at all to do with all of this, but only with the question: whether such a thing is given to us, so that the perception of it could if need be precede the concept. For, that the concept precedes the perception signifies its mere possibility, but perception, which supplies the material for the concept, is the sole character of reality. But one can also cognize the existence of the thing before the perception of it, and thus comparatively *a priori*, if only it is interconnected with some perceptions, in accordance with the principles of the empirical connection of these (the analogies). For then the existence of the thing is still interconnected with our perceptions in a possible experience, and we can, following the guideline of those analogies, arrive from our actual perception at the thing in the series of possible perceptions. Thus we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attracted iron filings, although an immediate perception of this material is impossible for us given the constitution of our organs. For in general we would, in accordance with laws of sensibility and the context of our perceptions, in an experience also come upon the immediate empirical intuition of it, if our senses were finer, whose coarseness does not concern the form of possible experience in general at all. Where, therefore, perception and its appendage in accordance with empirical laws reaches, thither also reaches our cognition of the existence of things. If we do not begin from experience, or do not proceed in accordance with laws of the empirical interconnection of appearances, then we make a vain show of wanting to guess at or investigate the existence of any thing.",["Character (Latinate, roman in German) = 'mark' is not used; kept as 'character'; Merkmal does not occur. 'Character' appears twice, as in the German.","Stoff = material; Materie = matter (hence 'magnetic matter' and 'this material'); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Bestimmungen = determinations.","Wirklichkeit = reality; wirklich = actual; Daseyn = existence; Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","Zusammenhang / zusammenhängen = interconnection / be interconnected; Verknüpfung = connection; Context kept as 'context'.","'comparative a priori': Latin kept in italics; 'comparative' rendered 'comparatively'.","'Anhang' = 'appendage'; 'Sinnen' = senses (plural of Sinn); 'Anschauung' = intuition.","'sich vergeblich Staat machen' is idiomatic; rendered 'make a vain show of', with the sense uncertain between 'make a vain display' and 'pretend in vain'.","'deren Grobheit': 'whose' refers to 'senses' (Sinnen); 'die Form möglicher Erfahrung überhaupt nichts angeht' is ambiguous between 'does not concern at all' and 'in general does not concern'; left ambiguous. 'dahin ... hin' rendered 'thither'; 'Leitfaden' = guideline."]],"krv-a-0427":["As for the third postulate, finally, it goes to the material necessity in existence, and not the merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of concepts. Since now no existence of the objects of the senses can be cognized completely a priori, but yet comparatively a priori, relative to another existence already given, yet nevertheless also then can arrive only at that existence which must somewhere be contained in the interconnection of experience, of which the given perception is a part: so the necessity of existence can never be cognized from concepts, but always only from the connection with that which is perceived, in accordance with universal laws of experience. Now there is no existence that could be cognized as necessary under the condition of other given appearances, except the existence of effects from given causes in accordance with laws of causality. Thus it is not the existence of things (substances), but of their state, of which alone we can cognize the necessity, and indeed from other states that are given in perception, in accordance with empirical laws of causality. From this it follows: that the criterion of necessity lies solely in the law of possible experience: that everything that happens is determined a priori through its cause in the appearance. Hence we cognize only the necessity of effects in nature, whose causes are given to us, and the mark of necessity in existence reaches no further than the field of possible experience, and even in this it does not hold of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be regarded as empirical effects, or as something that happens and arises. Necessity therefore concerns only the relations of appearances in accordance with the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility grounded on it, of inferring a priori from some given existence (a cause) to another existence (of the effect). Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary, that is a principle which subjects alteration in the world to a law, i.e., to a rule of necessary existence, without which not even nature would take place at all. Hence the proposition: nothing happens through a blind chance, (*in mundo non datur casus*) is a law of nature a priori, likewise no necessity in nature is blind, but conditioned, hence intelligible necessity (*non datur fatum*), both are such laws through which the play of alterations is subjected to a nature of things (as appearances), or, which is one and the same, to the unity of the understanding, in which alone they can belong to an experience, as the synthetic unity of appearances. These two principles belong to the dynamical ones. The former is properly a sequel of the principle of causality (among the analogies of experience). The second belongs to the principles of modality, which adds to the causal determination still the concept of necessity, which however stands under a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbade in the series of appearances (alterations) every leap; (*in mundo non datur saltus*) but also in the sum total of all empirical intuitions in space every gap or chasm between two appearances (*non datur hiatus*); for so one can express the proposition: that nothing can come into experience that would prove a *vacuum*, or even only admit it as a part of the empirical synthesis. For as for the empty, which one may think outside the field of possible experience (of the world), this does not belong before the jurisdiction of the mere understanding, which decides only about the questions that concern the utilization of given appearances for empirical cognition, and is a problem for ideal reason, which goes still beyond the sphere of a possible experience, and wants to judge of that which surrounds and bounds this itself, must therefore be weighed in the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions (*in mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum,*) we could easily, like all principles of transcendental origin, present according to their order, in conformity with the order of the categories, and prove to each its place, but the already practiced reader will do this by himself, or will easily discover the guiding thread to it. They unite, however, all solely in this, to admit nothing in the empirical synthesis that could do damage or injury to the understanding and to the continuous interconnection of all appearances, i.e., to the unity of its concepts. For it alone is that in which the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their place, becomes possible.",["Daseyn = existence throughout; Existenz also = existence (one English word for two German words); Dasein is the commoner word here.","Gegenstände = objects; Begriffe = concepts; Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Wahrnehmung = perception; Erkentniß/erkennen = cognition/cognize; Merkmal = mark; Criterium = criterion; Inbegriff = sum total; Aufgabe = problem.","Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition (in 'der Satz: nichts geschieht…' and 'Diese vier Sätze'); 'Princip' (der Continuität) = principle, also rendered 'principle'. Both Grundsatz and Princip are 'principle'.","'comparative a priori relativisch': 'comparative' left as in the German (Latinate adverb); 'relativisch' = 'relative'.","Folge = sequel ('eine Folge des Grundsatzes'); schliessen = infer; Schluß not present.","'Natur der Dinge (als Erscheinungen) unterworfen wird': dative 'einer Natur' kept literally ('a nature of things'), ambiguous as in the German; 'sie' in 'in welchem sie allein' could refer to the alterations or the appearances/things.","'ihre Ursache' in 'durch ihre Ursache in der Erscheinung a priori bestimt': 'ihre' is ambiguous (everything that happens / its); 'in der Erscheinung' may attach to 'Ursache' or to 'bestimt'. 'a priori' likewise may attach to 'bestimt' or the whole phrase; kept ambiguous.","'vor die Gerichtsbarkeit' and 'vor die idealische Vernunft' = 'before' and 'for' (vor for für, as in the original). 'idealische' rendered 'ideal'; 'Spähre' = sphere; 'Nutzung' = 'utilization'. 'Caussalbestimmung' = 'causal determination'. Original spelling 'Ohngefähr' = Ungefähr, 'chance'. 'der' before 'Erscheinungen' taken as printed."]],"krv-a-0428":["Whether the field of possibility is larger than the field that contains everything actual, but this in turn larger than the multitude of that which is necessary, these are pretty questions, and indeed of synthetic solution, which however also fall only under the jurisdiction of reason; for they want to say roughly as much as whether all things, as appearances, belong together in the sum total and the context of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part, which therefore cannot be combined with any other appearances, or whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience (in their general interconnection). The understanding gives *a priori* to experience in general only the rule, according to the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, which alone make it possible, Other forms of intuition (than space and time), likewise other forms of the understanding (than the discursive one of thinking, or of cognition through concepts,) even if they were possible, we can nevertheless in no way think up and make comprehensible, but, even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, as the sole cognition in which objects are given to us. Whether other perceptions, than in general, belong to our entire possible experience, and thus a quite different field of matter could still take place, the understanding cannot decide, it has to do only with the synthesis of that which is given. Otherwise the poverty of our usual inferences, through which we bring out a great realm of possibility, of which everything actual (every object of experience) is supposed to be only a small part, is very striking. Everything actual is possible; from this there follows naturally, according to the logical rules of conversion, the merely particular proposition: some possible is actual, which then seems to signify as much as: much is possible that is not actual. To be sure, it has the look as if one could also directly posit the number of the possible beyond that of the actual, because something must still be added to the former in order to constitute the latter. But this adding to the possible I do not know. For what should still be added beyond it would be impossible. Only to my understanding can something be added beyond the agreement with the formal conditions of experience, namely, the connection with some perception; but what is connected with this according to empirical laws is actual, even if it is not immediately perceived. But that in thoroughgoing interconnection with what is given to me in perception another series of appearances, thus more than one single all-comprehending experience, is possible, cannot be inferred from what is given, and, without anything being given, still much less; because without material nothing at all can be thought. What is possible alone under conditions that are themselves merely possible is not so in every respect. But in this respect the question is taken, when one wants to know whether the possibility of things extends further than experience can reach.",["Gegenstand (Gegenstände) and Object both appear in this group of passages; both are rendered 'object'. Here only Gegenstand occurs.","Wirkliche / wirklich = 'actual'; Wirklichkeit would be 'actuality'. The glossary also allows 'reality' for Wirklichkeit; Realität is a different word.","'in aller Absicht' rendered 'in every respect' (Absicht is glossed 'aim; intention', but the idiom does not bear that sense); 'in dieser' picks it up as 'in this respect'.","'der' in 'ein Theil ist, der also mit keinen andern...' is grammatically masculine (Theil), but the clause can also be heard as about the experience; the English 'which' leaves this open.","'welche sie allein möglich machen': 'sie' (singular or plural) could refer to Erfahrung or to the Bedingungen/Regel; 'it' follows the singular reading, the ambiguity is not resolved.","'Andere Formen' begins with a capital after a comma in the original; kept. 'als überhaupt' is obscure ('other perceptions than in general'); kept literal, no emendation.","schliessen rendered 'infer' (Schlüsse = 'inferences'); Auflösung = 'solution'; Gerichtsbarkeit = 'jurisdiction'; Armseligkeit = 'poverty'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Stoff = 'material', Materie = 'matter'.","Uncorrected OCR 'Punet' does not occur in this passage; no other emendations made."]],"krv-a-0429":["I have made mention of these questions only in order to leave no gap in that which, according to common opinion, belongs to the concepts of the understanding. In fact, however, absolute possibility (which is valid in every respect) is no mere concept of the understanding, and can in no way be of empirical use, but it belongs solely to reason, which goes beyond all possible empirical use of the understanding. Hence we have had to content ourselves here with a merely critical remark, but otherwise have left the matter in obscurity until further future proceeding.",["The German shifts from the feminine Möglichkeit to masculine 'er' (Begriff) in 'sondern er gehört'; the English renders it 'it' without resolving which is meant.","'in aller Absicht' = 'in every respect' (see the preceding passage). 'Meinung' = 'opinion'; 'Verfahren' = 'proceeding'."]],"krv-a-0430":["Since I want to close precisely this fourth number, and with it at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, I must still give the ground why I have called the principles of modality precisely postulates. I do not want to take this expression here in the meaning that some recent philosophical authors have given it, contrary to the sense of the mathematicians, to whom it properly belongs, namely: that to postulate is supposed to mean as much as to put forth a proposition as immediately certain, without justification or proof; for, if we were to concede in the case of synthetic propositions, however evident they may be, that one may fasten them, without deduction, on the authority of their own pronouncement, to unconditioned assent, then all critique of the understanding is lost, and, since there is no lack of bold presumptions, which even the common belief (which however is no letter of credence) does not refuse; so our understanding will stand open to every delusion, without being able to deny its assent to the pronouncements that, although unlawfully, demand nevertheless to be admitted in precisely the same tone of confidence as actual axioms. If therefore to the concept of a thing a determination is added *a priori* synthetically, then to such a proposition, if not a proof, then at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion must indispensably be added.",["'Principien' = 'principles' and 'Grundsätze' = 'principles' (one English word for two German words; Principien is the Latinate member).","'a priori synthetisch hinzukomt' could attach 'a priori' to 'synthetically', to 'added', or to 'determination'; the English keeps the German word order to preserve this.","'Ausdruck' = 'expression'; 'Bedeutung' = 'meaning'; 'Sinn' = 'sense'; 'Beweis' = 'proof'; 'Glaube' = 'belief'; 'Beyfall' = 'assent'; 'Behauptung' = 'assertion'.","'Creditiv' = 'letter of credence'; 'aufheften' = 'fasten on'; 'Axiomen' = 'axioms'. The sentence 'und, da es an dreusten Anmassungen nicht fehlt ... so wird' is kept with its irregular construction (semicolon, 'so')."]],"krv-a-0431":["But the principles of modality are not objectively synthetic, because the predicates of possibility, actuality and necessity do not augment the concept of which they are said in the least, by adding something further to the representation of the object. But since they are nevertheless always synthetic, they are so only subjectively, i.e., they add to the concept of a thing, (real) of which they otherwise say nothing, the power of cognition in which it arises and has its seat, so that, if it is merely in the understanding in connection with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible, if it is in interconnection with perception (sensation, as matter of the senses), and determined through the same by means of the understanding, then the object is actual; if it is determined through the interconnection of perceptions according to concepts, then the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore say of a concept nothing other than the action of the faculty of cognition through which it is generated. Now a postulate in mathematics is called the practical proposition that contains nothing but the synthesis through which we first give an object to ourselves, and generate its concept, e.g., with a given line, from a given point on a plane to describe a circle, and a proposition of this kind cannot be proved for the reason that the procedure that it demands is precisely that through which we first generate the concept of such a figure. Thus we can with precisely the same right postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment their concept of things in general,*) but only indicate the way in which it is combined with the faculty of cognition in general.",["Gegenstand and Object are both rendered 'object' ('sein Gegenstand möglich heißt'; 'so ist das Obiect wirklich'; 'der Gegenstand nothwendig').","'obiectivsynthetisch' = 'objectively synthetic' (one word in the original); 'subiectiv' = 'subjectively'.","Erkentnißkraft = 'power of cognition' (Kraft = power); Erkentnißvermögen = 'faculty of cognition' (Vermögen = faculty).","'(realen)' is a parenthesis after 'Dinges' and its attachment is unclear (to Dinges or Begriffes); kept in place. 'Zusammenhange' = 'interconnection'; 'Verknüpfung' = 'connection'; 'verbunden' = 'combined'.","'ihren Begriff' (plural 'their concept') kept literal; 'er' in 'wie er ... verbunden wird' = 'it'. The footnote marker *) stands where the original has it. OCR 'Punet' silently read as 'Punct' (point).","'Satz' = 'proposition'; 'practical' = practisch; 'bestimmt' = 'determined'; 'Postulat' = 'postulate' (not italicized, as it is not marked as foreign)."]],"krv-a-0432":["*) Through the actuality of a thing, I posit, to be sure, more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never be contained in actuality more than what was contained in its complete possibility. But since possibility was merely a position of the thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), actuality is at the same time a connection of it with perception.",["Footnote to the preceding passage; the marker *) is kept. 'setze' = 'posit'; 'Position' = 'position'; 'Beziehung' = 'relation'.","'das kan niemals mehr ... enthalten' is kept in the German's irregular syntax ('that can never be contained in actuality more than...'); 'Sondern da' is rendered 'But since'."]],"krv-a-0433":["Of the Transcend. Doctrine of the Power of Judgment (Analytic of Principles) Third Chapter. On the ground of the distinction of all objects in general into *phaenomena* and *noumena*.",["'Der Transscendent.' is an abbreviated form in the original (presumably of 'Transscendentalen'); rendered 'Of the Transcend.' and not completed. 'Der' is taken as a dative/genitive heading formula.","Urtheilskraft = '(power of) judgment'; Hauptstück = 'chapter'; Gegenstände = 'objects'; Phænomena and Noümena kept as Latin/Greek technical terms in italics, spelled 'phaenomena' and 'noumena'."]],"krv-a-0434":["We have now not only traveled through the land of pure understanding, and carefully inspected each part of it, but have also surveyed it, and determined for each thing in it its place. This land, however, is an island, and enclosed by nature itself within unalterable boundaries. It is the land of truth (an enchanting name), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the proper seat of illusion, where many a fog bank and many a quickly melting iceberg feigns new lands, and, while it incessantly deceives with empty hopes the seafarer roaming about on voyages of discovery, entangles him in adventures from which he can never desist and yet can also never bring to an end. Before we venture upon this sea, however, in order to search it through in all its breadths, and to become certain whether anything in them is to be hoped for, it will be useful first to cast yet a glance at the map of the land that we are just about to leave, and to ask, first, whether we could not at all events be content with what it contains in itself, or even must be content out of necessity, if there is otherwise nowhere any ground on which we could settle, second, under what title we possess this land itself, and can hold ourselves secured against all hostile claims. Although we have already answered these questions sufficiently in the course of the Analytic, a summary tally of their solutions can nevertheless strengthen the conviction, by uniting the moments of them in one point.",["Gränzen = boundaries; Schein = illusion; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Analytik = Analytic.","'Breiten' (plural, 'in ihnen') translated 'breadths'; the German could also suggest 'latitudes'. Left as is; the antecedent of 'ihnen' is likewise left as in the German.","'Hoffen sey' / 'wird es nützlich seyn': the long sentence beginning 'Ehe wir uns' is kept whole, including its comma-spliced list of the two questions ('erstlich ... zweitens').","'Ueberschlag' rendered 'tally'; 'Momente' rendered 'moments'.","'Nahme' (name) is in the original parenthesis; 'Carte' = map."]],"krv-a-0435":["We have seen, namely: that everything that the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing it from experience, it has nevertheless for no other purpose than solely for use in experience. The principles of pure understanding, whether they be constitutive *a priori* (like the mathematical ones) or merely regulative (like the dynamical ones), contain nothing but, as it were, only the pure schema for possible experience; for this has its unity only from the synthetic unity that the understanding originally and of itself imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and to which the appearances, as *data* for a possible cognition, must already stand *a priori* in relation and accord. Yet although these rules of the understanding are not only true *a priori*, but are even the source of all truth, i.e., of the agreement of our cognition with objects, by containing in themselves the ground of the possibility of experience, as the sum total of all cognition, in which objects may be given to us, it nevertheless seems to us not enough to have presented to oneself merely that which is true, but rather what one desires to know. If, then, we learn through this critical investigation nothing more than what we would, in the merely empirical use of the understanding, even without such subtle inquiry, probably have practiced of ourselves, it seems that the advantage one draws from it is not worth the expense and the preparation. Now one can indeed answer to this: that no meddlesome curiosity is more detrimental to the extension of our cognition than that which always wants to know the use in advance, before one embarks upon inquiries, and before one could yet make for oneself the slightest concept of this use, even if it were set before one's eyes. Yet there is still an advantage that can be made comprehensible, and at the same time a concern, even to the most difficult and most unwilling pupil of such transcendental inquiry, namely this: that the understanding occupied merely with its empirical use, which does not reflect on the sources of its own cognition, can indeed get on very well, but can accomplish one thing not at all, namely, to determine for itself the boundaries of its use, and to know what may lie within or outside its entire sphere; for it is precisely the deep investigations that we have undertaken that are required for this. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, then it is never certain of its claims and of its possession, but may count only on many shaming rebukes, if it incessantly (as is unavoidable) oversteps the boundaries of its territory, and strays into delusion and sham.",["Gränzen = boundaries; Erkentniß/Erkentnisse = cognition; Obiecte = objects; Gegenstände would also be 'objects' (see next passage).","'Inbegriff' = sum total; 'Einbildungskraft' = imagination; 'Apperception' kept as 'apperception'; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances.","'wahr ist, sondern, was man zu wissen begehrt': the German elliptical construction is kept; 'sich ... vortragen zu lassen' rendered 'to have presented to oneself'.","'begreiflich' rendered 'comprehensible' (cf. begreifen = comprehend); 'angelegen gemacht' rendered 'a concern' (literally 'made a concern').","'Wahn und Blendwerke' rendered 'delusion and sham'; 'Blendwerke' is plural ('shams'), rendered singular in idiom only.","'Vorwitz' rendered 'meddlesome curiosity'; 'Lehrlinge' rendered 'pupil'; 'schwürigsten' = 'schwierigsten' (old spelling), here 'most difficult'. 'Gebrauch' = use throughout."]],"krv-a-0436":["That the understanding, then, can make of all its principles *a priori*, indeed of all its concepts, no other than an empirical use, but never a transcendental one, is a proposition that, if it can be cognized with conviction, looks toward important consequences. The transcendental use of a concept in any principle is this: that it is related to things in general and in themselves, the empirical, however, when it is related merely to appearances, i.e., objects of a possible experience. That only the latter can take place at all, however, one sees from this. For every concept there is required, first, the logical form of a concept (of thinking) in general, and then, second, also the possibility of giving it an object to which it is to be related. Without this latter it has no sense, and is entirely empty of content, even though it may still always contain the logical function of making a concept out of whatever *data* there may be. Now the object cannot be given to a concept otherwise than in intuition, and, if a pure intuition is still possible *a priori* before the object, then even this itself can get its object, hence objective validity, only through the empirical intuition, of which it is the mere form. Thus all concepts, and with them all principles, however much they may be possible *a priori*, nevertheless relate to empirical intuitions, i.e., to *data* for possible experience. Without this they have no objective validity at all, but are a mere play, whether of the imagination or of the understanding, respectively with their representations. Take only the concepts of mathematics as an example, and indeed first in their pure intuitions. Space has three dimensions, between two points there can be only one straight line, etc. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which that science occupies itself, are generated entirely *a priori* in the mind, they would nevertheless signify nothing at all, if we could not always present their significance in appearances (empirical objects). Hence one also requires that an abstracted concept be made sensible, i.e., that the object corresponding to it be presented in intuition, because, without this, the concept would remain (as one says) without sense, i.e., without significance. Mathematics fulfills this demand through the construction of the figure, which is an appearance present to the senses (although brought about *a priori*). The concept of magnitude seeks in the same science its support and sense in number, but this in the fingers, the beads of the abacus, or the strokes and points that are set before the eyes. The concept always remains generated *a priori*, together with the synthetic principles or formulas from such concepts; but the use of them, and relation to supposed objects, can in the end be sought nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which (as to form) they contain *a priori*.",["Gegenstand and Object are both 'object' (Gegenstand: 'Gegenstände einer möglichen Erfahrung', 'Gegenstand zu geben'; Obiect: 'correspondirende Obiect').","Sinn = sense; Bedeutung = significance, bedeuten = signify; 'ohne Sinn, d. i. ohne Bedeutung' kept as is.","'respective' rendered 'respectively'; 'mit ihren Vorstellungen' ambiguous as to whether the play is 'with' its own representations or 'respectively with' them; left ambiguous.","'sinnlich machen' = 'make sensible'; 'Haltung' rendered 'support'; 'Corallen' = beads (of the 'Rechenbret', abacus).","'a priori' and 'data' printed in roman type in the original and italicized; 'a priori' in the final clause ('iene a priori enthalten') could attach to 'contain' or to the possibility; kept in the German position.","'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Einbildungskraft' = imagination; 'Gültigkeit' = validity; 'Ueberzeugung' = conviction; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Gemüth' = mind. 'Grösse' = magnitude; 'Zahl' = number.","'ersiehet man daraus' rendered 'one sees from this'; 'vor dem Gegenstande' (before the object) kept ambiguous between temporal and other priority."]],"krv-a-0437":["But that this is also the case with all categories, and the principles spun out of them, is also evident from this: that we cannot define even a single one of them without immediately descending to conditions of sensibility, hence to the form of appearances, to which, as their sole objects, they consequently must be restricted, because, if one removes this condition, all meaning, i.e., relation to the object, drops away, and one cannot make comprehensible to oneself through any example what sort of thing is then actually meant under such concepts. Above, in the presentation of the table of the categories, we relieved ourselves of the definitions of each of them by this: that our aim, which goes solely to the synthetic use of them, does not make them necessary, and one must not expose oneself to any responsibility through unnecessary undertakings from which one can be relieved. That was no excuse, but a not insignificant rule of prudence, not to venture to define right away, and to attempt or pretend to completeness or precision in the determination of the concept, if one can make do with some one or other mark of it, without precisely requiring for this a complete enumeration of all of those that make up the whole concept. Now, however, it turns out: that the ground of this caution lies still deeper, namely, that we could not define them even if we wanted to*), but rather, if one removes all conditions of sensibility that distinguish them as concepts of a possible empirical use, and takes them for concepts of things in general (hence of transcendental use), there is nothing further to be done with them than to regard the logical function in judgments as the condition of the possibility of things themselves, without being able in the least to indicate where they then have their application and their object, hence how they could have any meaning and objective validity in the pure understanding without sensibility. No one can explain the concept of magnitude in general, except perhaps thus: that it is the determination of a thing through which it can be thought how many times one is posited in it. However, this how-many-times is grounded on successive repetition, hence on time and the synthesis (of the homogeneous) in it. Reality, in opposition to negation, one can explain only when one thinks a time (as the sum total of all being) that is either filled with something or empty. If I leave out persistence (which is an existence at all times), nothing remains for me for the concept of substance except the logical representation of the subject, which I believe I realize by representing to myself something that can occur merely as subject (without being a predicate of anything). But not only do I know no conditions at all under which this logical prerogative will then belong to any thing: nothing further at all can be made of it, and not the slightest consequence drawn, because thereby no object of the use of this concept is determined, and so one does not know at all whether it signifies anything at all. Of the concept of cause I would find (if I leave out the time in which something follows upon something else according to a rule) in the pure category nothing further than that it is something from which an inference to the existence of another can be made, and thereby not only could cause and effect not be distinguished from one another at all, but because this being-able-to-infer soon requires conditions of which I know nothing, the concept would have no determination at all as to how it fits any object. The putative principle: everything contingent has a cause, indeed makes its appearance rather gravely, as if it had its own dignity in itself. But if I ask: what do you understand by contingent, and you answer, that whose non-being is possible, then I would gladly know by what you want to cognize this possibility of non-being, if you do not represent to yourselves in the series of appearances a succession and in this an existence that follows upon non-being (or conversely), hence a change; for, that the non-being of a thing does not contradict itself is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is indeed necessary for the concept, but far from sufficient for real possibility; as I can indeed remove every existing substance in thought without contradicting myself, but from this cannot at all infer to the objective contingency of it in its existence, i.e., the possibility of its non-being in itself. As to what concerns the concept of community, it is easy to estimate: that, since the pure categories of substance as well as of causality admit no explanation determining the object, the reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to one another (*commercium*) is just as little capable of it. Possibility, existence and necessity no one has yet been able to explain other than through manifest tautology, if one wanted to draw their definition solely from the pure understanding. For the delusion of substituting the logical possibility of the concept (in that it does not contradict itself) for the transcendental possibility of things (in that an object corresponds to the concept) can only deceive and satisfy the inexperienced.",["Categorien = categories; Grundsätze = principles; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Erscheinungen = appearances; Bedeutung = meaning, bedeute = signifies; Obiect and Gegenstand both = object.","Erklärung/erklären and Definition/definiren are kept apart as 'explanation/explain' and 'definition/define'; in the 'Möglichkeit, Daseyn und Nothwendigkeit' sentence 'explain' renders erklären, 'definition' renders Definition.","Daseyn = existence (Existenz is not used in this passage); Zufälligkeit/zufällig = contingency/contingent; Wechsel = change; Gemeinschaft = community.","'Wievielmal' is rendered 'how-many-times'; 'Schliessenkönnen' is rendered 'being-able-to-infer'; 'schliessen' = infer (Schluß glossary).","OCR/print: 'Obiects' corrected to 'Object' (rendered 'object'); a period supplied after 'oder leer ist' (none in the text as given); 'Herzehlung' read as Herzählung, rendered 'enumeration'.","'was unter dergleichen Begriffe denn eigentlich vor ein Ding gemeint sey': 'vor' = für; 'Begriffe' left in the form given (dative/accusative ambiguity of the plural form preserved as 'such concepts').","'Das Wievielmal gründet sich...' and 'Unversuchte' (here 'the inexperienced', literally 'untried'); 'Blendwerk' = delusion.","The asterisk after 'wollten' marks Kant's footnote, translated separately as krv-a-0438."]],"krv-a-0438":["*) I understand here the real definition, which does not merely substitute for the name of a thing other and more intelligible words, but contains in itself a clear mark by which the object (*definitum*) can always be securely cognized, and which makes the explained concept usable for application. The real explanation would thus be that which makes distinct not merely a concept, but at the same time the objective reality of it. The mathematical explanations, which exhibit the object in intuition in accordance with the concept, are of the latter kind.",["Realdefinition = real definition; Realerklärung = real explanation (Definition/Erklärung kept distinct); Nahmen = name.","Gegenstand = object; Merkmal = mark; Anschauung = intuition; darstellen = exhibit; *definitum* is Kant's Latin, italicized.","'dieienige' and 'der letzteren Art': 'the latter kind' refers to the real explanation; the antecedent of 'letzteren' is left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0439":["There is something strange and even absurd about the fact that there should be a concept to which a meaning must nevertheless pertain, but which would not be capable of any explanation. Yet here the categories have this particular circumstance: that only by means of the general sensible condition can they have a determinate meaning and relation to any object, but this condition has been left out of the pure category, since the latter can then contain nothing but the logical function of bringing the manifold under a concept. From this function, i.e., the form of the concept alone, however, nothing at all can be cognized and distinguished as to which object belongs under it, because the sensible condition under which objects in general can belong under it has precisely been abstracted from. Hence the categories, beyond the pure concept of the understanding, require determinations of their application to sensibility in general (schema) and without these are no concepts through which an object would be cognized and distinguished from others, but only so many ways of thinking an object for possible intuitions, and of giving it its meaning (under conditions still required) in accordance with some function of the understanding, i.e., of defining it: they themselves, therefore, cannot be defined. The logical functions of judgments in general: unity and plurality, affirmation and negation, subject and predicate cannot be defined without committing a circle, because the definition would itself have to be a judgment, and thus would have to contain these functions already. The pure categories, however, are nothing other than representations of things in general, insofar as the manifold of their intuition must be thought through one or another of these logical functions: magnitude is the determination that can be thought only through a judgment that has quantity, (*iudicium commune*) reality, that which can be thought only through an affirmative judgment, substance, what, in relation to intuition, must be the final subject of all other determinations. But what sort of things these are, with regard to which one must make use of this function rather than another, remains entirely undetermined here: consequently the categories without the condition of sensible intuition, for which they contain the synthesis, have no relation at all to any determinate object, can therefore define none, and consequently have in themselves no validity of objective concepts.",["Erklärung rendered 'explanation' (glossary); later 'definiren/Definition' rendered 'define/definition', so the German word behind each differs.","Bedeutung = meaning; Bestimmung = determination; bestimmt = determinate/determined; 'bestimt' is the old spelling.","Gegenstand and Object (Obiect) are both 'object'; 'Obiect' occurs in 'welches Obiect darunter gehöre', 'ein bestimtes Obiect'.","'Wesen' not present; 'Ding' = thing; 'Dinge überhaupt' = 'things in general'.","'erkant' = cognized (erkennen); 'Vielheit' = plurality, 'Beiahung/Verneinung' = affirmation/negation (bejahen glossary).","'Cirkel zu begehen' = 'committing a circle' (literal).","'Bewandniß' rendered 'circumstance'; 'befremdliches' 'strange'; 'widersinnisches' 'absurd' (not in glossary).","The clause '(iudicium commune)' is Kant's Latin, kept in italics; the punctuation of the series 'Grösse ... Realität ... Substanz' is kept as in the German (no comma before 'Realität'). 'dazu sie die Synthesis enthalten' is literal: 'dazu' could mean 'for which' or 'to which'."]],"krv-a-0440":["From this it now follows irrefutably: that the pure concepts of the understanding can never be of transcendental but always only of empirical use, and that the principles of the pure understanding can be related only to the general conditions of a possible experience, to objects of the senses, but never to things in general, (without taking regard of the way in which we may intuit them).",["Grundsätze = principles; Gebrauch = use; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.","'Rücksicht auf die Art zu nehmen' rendered 'taking regard of the way'; Art = way."]],"krv-a-0441":["The transcendental analytic accordingly has this important result: that the understanding can never accomplish more a priori than to anticipate the form of a possible experience in general, and, since that which is not appearance cannot be an object of experience: that it can never overstep the limitations of sensibility, within which alone objects are given to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of appearances, and the proud name of an ontology, which presumes to give synthetic cognitions a priori of things in general in a systematic doctrine (e.g., the principle of causality) must make room for the modest one of a mere analytic of the pure understanding.",["Schranken = limitations (glossary: Schranke); Erscheinung = appearance; Erkentnisse = cognitions.","'Exposition' stands for German 'Exposition' (Latin-derived, as printed), distinct from Erörterung (also 'exposition' in glossary).","'Doctrin' (Latinate) rendered 'doctrine' (cf. Lehre); 'Gegenstände' = objects.","'Caussalität' = causality (old spelling)."]],"krv-a-0442":["Thinking is the action of relating a given intuition to an object. If the way of this intuition is given in no way whatever, then the object is merely transcendental, and the concept of the understanding has no other than transcendental use, namely the unity of the thinking of a manifold in general. Through a pure category now, in which there is abstraction from all conditions of sensible intuition, as the only one that is possible for us, no object is therefore determined, but only the thinking of an object in general, in accordance with different *modis*, is expressed. Now to the use of a concept there belongs yet a function of the power of judgment, whereby an object is subsumed under it, hence the at least formal condition under which something can be given in intuition. If this condition of the power of judgment (schema) is lacking, then all subsumption drops out; for nothing is given that could be subsumed under the concept. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore in fact no use at all, and has no determinate object, or one even determinable as to form. From this it follows that the pure category also does not suffice for any synthetic principle a priori, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical use, never of transcendental, but beyond the field of possible experience there can be no synthetic principles a priori at all.",["Urteilskraft = power of judgment (glossary); Anschauung = intuition; Grundsatz = principle.","'modis' is Kant's Latin (ablative plural of modus), kept in italics.","'ausgedrükt' = expressed; 'bestimbaren' (sic, for bestimmbaren) = determinable.","'als der einzigen, die uns möglich ist' could modify 'Bedingung' or 'sinnlichen Anschauung'; the English preserves the ambiguity ('as the only one').","'zulange' (= zulangen) rendered 'suffice'.","'worauf' rendered 'whereby'; literal sense is 'upon which'."]],"krv-a-0443":["It can therefore be advisable to express oneself thus: the pure categories, without formal conditions of sensibility, have merely transcendental meaning, but are of no transcendental use, because this is impossible in itself, since all conditions of any use (in judgments) are lacking to them, namely the formal conditions of the subsumption of any putative object under these concepts. Since they (as merely pure categories) are therefore not supposed to be of empirical use, and cannot be of transcendental, they are of no use at all if one separates them from all sensibility, i.e., they cannot be applied to any putative object; rather they are merely the pure form of the use of the understanding with regard to objects in general and to thinking, without being able, however, to think or determine any object through them alone.",["Bedeutung = meaning; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstand/Object both 'object' (here 'Gegenstandes', 'Obiect').","'angeblichen' rendered 'putative'; 'Ansehung' in 'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'.","'transsendentalen' (sic) = transscendentalen, as in the original spelling."]],"krv-a-0444":["Appearances, insofar as they are thought as objects in accordance with the unity of the categories, are called *phaenomena*. But if I assume things that are merely objects of the understanding and that, nonetheless, as such, can be given to an intuition, although not to a sensible one (as *coram intuitu intellectuali*); then such things would be called *noumena* (*intelligibilia*).",["Phænomena, Noümena are Kant's Latinized/Greek terms, rendered in italics; 'intelligibilia' and 'coram intuitu intellectuali' are Kant's Latin.","Erscheinungen = appearances; Gegenstände = objects; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0445":["Now one should think that the concept of appearances, restricted by the transcendental aesthetic, already of itself furnishes the objective reality of the *noumena*, and justifies the division of objects into *phaenomena* and *noumena*, hence also of the world into a world of the senses and a world of the understanding (*mundus sensibilis & intelligibilis*), and indeed in such a way that the difference here concerns not merely the logical form of the indistinct or distinct cognition of one and the same thing, but the diversity in how they can be originally given to our cognition, and in accordance with which they are themselves, in themselves, different from one another in genus. For if the senses merely represent something to us as it appears, then this something must also be in itself a thing, and an object of a non-sensible intuition, i.e., of the understanding, i.e., a cognition must be possible in which no sensibility is encountered, and which alone has absolutely objective reality, whereby objects are namely represented to us as they are; whereas in the empirical use of our understanding things are only cognized as they appear. Thus there would be, besides the empirical use of the categories (which is restricted to sensible conditions), yet a pure and yet objectively valid one, and we could not assert what we have hitherto professed: that our pure cognitions of the understanding would be nothing further at all than principles of the exposition of appearance, which even a priori would go no further than to the formal possibility of experience, for here a quite different field would stand open before us, as it were a world thought in the mind, (perhaps even intuited) which could occupy our pure understanding no less, indeed far more nobly.",["undeutlich/deutlich = indistinct/distinct (glossary: deutlich = distinct); 'Gattung' = genus.","'an sich selbst' rendered 'themselves, in themselves'; 'an sich' = in itself.","'Verstandeswelt' = world of the understanding; 'Sinnenwelt' rendered 'world of the senses'.","'vorgegeben' rendered 'professed'; 'Geist' = mind (glossary: Geist = mind; spirit), here 'im Geiste'.","The 'ia noch weit edler' clause: 'noch weit edler' is adverbial; the English 'far more nobly' preserves the ambiguity as to whether it modifies 'occupy' or the world.","'Principien der Exposition' = 'principles of the exposition'; 'transsc. Aesthetik' expanded as 'transcendental aesthetic' (abbreviation in the original)."]],"krv-a-0446":["All our representations are in fact related by the understanding to some object, and, since appearances are nothing but representations, the understanding relates them to a something, as the object of sensible intuition: but this something is to that extent only the transcendental object. This, however, signifies a something = x, of which we know nothing at all, nor (according to the present constitution of our understanding) can know anything at all, but which can serve only as a correlatum of the unity of apperception for the unity of the manifold in sensible intuition, by means of which the understanding unites the latter in the concept of an object. This transcendental object cannot be separated at all from the sensible data, because then nothing would remain through which it would be thought. It is therefore no object of cognition in itself, but only the representation of appearances under the concept of an object in general, which is determinable through the manifold of those appearances.",["Obiect / Gegenstand: both rendered 'object' (Object = 'Obiect' in the text; Gegenstand in 'Gegenstand der sinnlichen Anschauung', 'Begriff eines Gegenstandes').","wissen / wissen können = 'know' (Wissen in glossary = knowledge, scientia); Erkentniß = cognition.","Einrichtung = 'constitution' (not in glossary; Beschaffenheit elsewhere is also 'constitution').","Dati ('Datis') left as the Latin dative plural form 'data' in English; 'Correlatum' kept in Latin and italicized as a foreign word.","'derselben' in the last clause (feminine plural) refers back to Erscheinungen; 'dasselbe' (neuter) refers to das Mannigfaltige; I render both as 'the latter' / 'those appearances' minimally—'the latter' is a mild choice, the German pronoun is neuter singular.","bestimbar = bestimmbar ('determinable'); 'kan' = kann (original spelling)."]],"krv-a-0447":["Precisely for this reason the categories also do not represent any particular object given to the understanding alone, but serve only to determine the transcendental object (the concept of something in general) through that which is given in sensibility, in order thereby to cognize appearances empirically under concepts of objects.",["Obiect / Gegenstand both 'object'.","erkennen = 'cognize'; besonder = 'particular'; bestimmen = 'determine'.","Categorien rendered 'categories'."]],"krv-a-0448":["But as for the cause on account of which, not yet satisfied by the substratum of sensibility, one has admitted noumena in addition to the *phaenomena*, which only the pure understanding can think, it rests solely on this. Sensibility, and its field, namely that of appearances, is itself limited by the understanding to this: that it does not pertain to things in themselves, but only to the way in which, by virtue of our subjective constitution, things appear to us. This was the result of the whole transcendental aesthetic, and it also follows naturally from the concept of an appearance in general: that something must correspond to it that is not in itself appearance, because appearance can be nothing for itself, and outside our way of representing, hence, if a constant circle is not to result, the word appearance already indicates a relation to something whose immediate representation is indeed sensible, but which in itself, even without this constitution of our sensibility (on which the form of our intuition is grounded), must be something, i.e., an object independent of sensibility.",["Phænomenis (dative plural) and Noümena are Latinate/Greek terms; I render 'phaenomena' in italics and 'noumena' in plain form following the German's differing typography only loosely; flagged as a choice.","Ursache = 'cause'; Art = 'way'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Vorstellungsart = 'way of representing'.","'dahin eingeschränkt: daß' preserved as 'limited ... to this: that'; the ambiguity in 'sie ... gehe' (subjunctive, indirect speech) is kept by 'does not pertain'.","Gegenstand = 'object'.","'vor sich selbst' = 'for itself' (vor for für)."]],"krv-a-0449":["From this now arises the concept of a noumenon, which however is not at all positive, and signifies not a determinate cognition of any thing, but only the thinking of something in general, in which I abstract from all form of sensible intuition. But in order that a noumenon signify a true object, to be distinguished from all phenomena, it is not enough: that I free my thought from all conditions of sensible intuition, I must in addition have ground to assume a different way of intuition than this sensible one, under which such an object could be given; for otherwise my thought is empty, though without contradiction. We were indeed unable to prove above: that sensible intuition is the only possible intuition in general, but only that it is so for us, but we also could not prove: that yet another way of intuition is possible, and, although our thinking can abstract from that sensibility, the question nevertheless remains whether it is then not a mere form of a concept, and whether, with this separation, any object at all remains over.",["Noümenon / Noumenon / Phänomenen: 'noumenon', 'phenomena'; Kant's German-spelled forms are not italicized here.","bedeuten = 'signify'; bestimt = 'determinate'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Art = 'way'.","'vor uns' = 'for us' (vor for für).","'bey welchem' = 'in which'; 'Grund' = 'ground' (no article in German, kept).","Gegenstand = 'object', Obiect = 'object'."]],"krv-a-0450":["The object to which I relate appearance in general is the transcendental object, i.e., the wholly undetermined thought of something in general. This cannot be called the noumenon; for I do not know of it what it is in itself, and have no concept at all of it, other than merely that of the object of a sensible intuition in general, which is therefore one and the same for all appearances. I can think it through no categories; for this [feminine singular] holds of empirical intuition, in order to bring it under a concept of the object in general. A pure use of the category is indeed possible, i.e., without contradiction, but has no objective validity at all, because it pertains to no intuition that is supposed to acquire unity of the object thereby; for the category is after all a mere function of thinking, through which no object is given to me, but only that which may be given in intuition is thought.",["Obiect / Gegenstand both 'object'; 'obiective' = 'objective'.","'denn diese gilt': singular verb with 'diese' (feminine); the antecedent is ambiguous (Categorien plural vs. a singular category); bracketed gloss is kept minimal but is mine, not Kant's—remove if the strict method is preferred.","'vor alle Erscheinungen einerley' = 'one and the same for all appearances'.","Gültigkeit = 'validity'; Gebrauch = 'use'; weis = weiß."]],"krv-a-0451":["If I take away all thinking (through categories) from an empirical cognition, no cognition of any object at all remains; for through mere intuition nothing at all is thought, and that this affection of sensibility is in me constitutes no relation of such a representation to any object. If, however, I leave out all intuition, then the form of thinking still remains, i.e., the way of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Hence the categories extend further than sensible intuition, because they think objects in general, without yet looking to the particular way (of sensibility) in which they may be given. But they do not thereby determine a greater sphere of objects, because, that such can be given, one cannot assume without presupposing as possible a way of intuition other than the sensible, to which, however, we are in no way entitled.",["Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Art = 'way'; besondere = 'particular'; bestimmen = 'determine'.","Affection left as 'affection' (Latinate in German).","'Gegenstand' / 'Obiect' both 'object'.","'dem Mannigfaltigen einer möglichen Anschauung einen Gegenstand zu bestimmen': dative 'for the manifold' is a literal choice; 'to' is also possible."]],"krv-a-0452":["I call a concept problematic that contains no contradiction, that also hangs together, as a limitation of given concepts, with other cognitions, but whose objective reality can in no way be cognized. The concept of a noumenon, i.e., of a thing that is to be thought not at all as object of the senses but as a thing in itself (solely through a pure understanding), is not at all self-contradictory; for one cannot after all assert of sensibility that it is the only possible way of intuition. Further, this concept is necessary in order not to extend sensible intuition to beyond things in themselves, and thus in order to limit the objective validity of sensible cognition (for the remainder, to which that does not reach, is called noumena precisely for this reason, in order thereby to indicate that those cognitions cannot extend their domain over everything that the understanding thinks.) In the end, however, the possibility of such *noumena* is not to be seen at all, and the domain outside the sphere of appearances is (for us) empty, i.e., we have an understanding that extends itself problematically further than those, but no intuition, indeed not even the concept of a possible intuition, through which objects could be given to us outside the field of sensibility, and the understanding could be used assertorically beyond it. The concept of a noumenon is therefore merely a boundary concept, in order to limit the presumption of sensibility, and therefore only of negative use. But it is nonetheless not arbitrarily invented, but hangs together with the limitation of sensibility, without being able, however, to posit anything positive outside the domain of the latter.",["Begränzung / Einschränkung / Beschränkung-type terms: Begränzung = 'limitation', einschränken = 'limit'; Gränzbegriff = 'boundary concept' (Grenze = boundary). Schranke is not used here.","Gegenstand = 'object'; Umfang = 'domain'; Gebiet = 'domain' as well (one English word for two German words); Feld = 'field'; Sphäre = 'sphere'.","Noümenorum is the Latin genitive plural, rendered as italic *noumena*; earlier 'Noumena' and 'Noumenon' in German typography rendered plain 'noumena', 'noumenon'.","'assertorisch' = 'assertorically'; 'problematisch' = 'problematic(ally)'.","'iene' (those) refers to appearances or to cognitions depending on clause: 'als iene' (than those) and 'iene nicht reicht' (that does not reach); the German is ambiguous and the English preserves it.","'derselben' in the final clause = 'of the latter' (feminine; antecedent Sinnlichkeit); 'über dieselbe hinaus' = 'beyond it' (the field of sensibility).","Unclosed parenthesis in German ('...denkt.)' with period inside) kept as printed."]],"krv-a-0453":["The division of objects into *phaenomena* and *noumena*, and of the world into a world of sense and a world of understanding, can therefore not be admitted at all, although concepts certainly admit of the division into sensible and intellectual ones; for one can determine no object for the latter, and so also cannot pass them off as objectively valid. If one departs from the senses, how will one make comprehensible that our categories (which would be the only remaining concepts for noumena) still signify anything at all, since for their relation to any object something more than merely the unity of thinking must be given, namely, in addition, a possible intuition, to which those can be applied? The concept of a noumenon, taken merely problematically, nevertheless remains not only admissible but, also as a concept setting limits to sensibility, unavoidable. But then this is not a particular intelligible object for our understanding, rather an understanding to which it would belong is itself a problem, namely, to cognize its object not discursively through categories but intuitively in a non-sensible intuition, of which we cannot make for ourselves the least representation of its possibility. Our understanding now gets in this way a negative extension, i.e., it is not limited by sensibility, but rather limits the latter, by calling things in themselves (not considered as appearances) noumena. But it also immediately sets limits for itself, not to cognize them through any categories, hence to think them only under the name of an unknown something.",["Phænomena/Noümena, mundi sensibilis/intelligibilis: Latin terms kept in italics; 'Sinnenwelt' and 'Verstandeswelt' rendered 'world of sense' and 'world of understanding'.","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Obiect' (in 0456) also 'object'.","'begreiflich machen' rendered 'make comprehensible' (begreifen = comprehend); 'erkennen' = 'cognize'.","'vor' (old spelling of 'für') in 'vor Noumena', 'vor unsern Verstand', 'vor obiectivgültig': rendered 'for' / 'as'; 'ausgeben' = 'pass off'.","'ein Verstand, vor den es gehörete': 'es' is left as in the German ('to which it would belong'); the antecedent is not specified.","'Gränzen' = 'limits' here; 'Schranken' = 'limits' as well ('in Schranken setzen'; glossary 'limitation'), so one English word stands for two German words.","'Problema' rendered 'problem' (glossary: Aufgabe = problem)."]],"krv-a-0454":["In the writings of the moderns, however, I find an entirely different use of the expressions of a *mundus sensibilis* and *intelligibilis*, which deviates entirely from the sense of the ancients, and with which there is admittedly no difficulty, but also nothing but empty word-mongering is encountered. According to it, it has pleased some to call the sum total of appearances, insofar as it is intuited, the world of sense, but insofar as the interconnection of the same is thought according to general laws of the understanding, the world of understanding. Theoretical astronomy, which presents the mere observation of the starry heavens, would make the first, but contemplative astronomy, by contrast (explained, say, according to the Copernican world-system, or even according to Newton's laws of gravitation), the second, namely an intelligible world, representable. But such a twisting of words is a mere sophistical evasion, in order to avoid a burdensome question, by lowering its sense to suit one's convenience. With regard to appearances, understanding and reason can certainly be used, but it is asked whether these also still have any use when the object is not appearance (noumenon), and it is taken in this sense when it is thought in itself as merely intelligible, i.e., as given to the understanding alone and not at all to the senses. The question is therefore: whether, outside of that empirical use of the understanding (even in the Newtonian representation of the structure of the world), a transcendental one is still possible, which goes to the noumenon as an object, which question we have answered in the negative.",["'Ausdrücke' = 'expressions'; 'Inbegriff' = 'sum total'; 'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'; 'Gebrauch' = 'use'.","'Welt' in 'Weltsystem', 'Weltbaues' rendered 'world-system', 'structure of the world'.","'würde ... vorstellig machen': rendered 'would make ... representable' (literally 'make presentable/representable'); the grammar of 'die erstere ... die zweite' is kept as in the German and is somewhat ambiguous.","'Wortkrämerey' = 'word-mongering'; 'Wortverdrehung' = 'twisting of words'.","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'Sinn' = 'sense' (also in 'Sinnenwelt'); 'Sinne' = 'senses'.","'in diesem Sinne nimt man ihn': 'ihn' = the object (Gegenstand), left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0455":["If we therefore say: the senses represent objects to us as they appear, the understanding, however, as they are, then the latter is to be taken not in a transcendental but merely empirical meaning, namely, as they must be represented as objects of experience, in the thoroughgoing interconnection of appearances, and not according to what they may be, outside the relation to possible experience, and consequently to senses in general, hence as objects of the pure understanding. For this will always remain unknown to us, so much so that it also remains unknown whether such a transcendental (extraordinary) cognition is possible at all, at least as one that stands under our usual categories. Understanding and sensibility can determine objects in us only in combination. If we separate them, then we have intuitions without concepts, or concepts without intuitions, but in both cases representations that we cannot relate to any determinate object.",["'Bedeutung' = 'meaning' (glossary also 'significance'); 'Verbindung' = 'combination'.","'bey uns' rendered 'in us' per the German 'bey uns'; it could also be read 'with us' / 'in our case'; ambiguity is not fully preserved by the English.","'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; 'bestimten' = 'determinate'.","'Sinne überhaupt' = 'senses in general'; 'Sinnlichkeit' = 'sensibility'.","'ausserordentliche' rendered 'extraordinary' within the parentheses."]],"krv-a-0456":["If anyone still has qualms about renouncing, on the basis of all these expositions, the merely transcendental use of the categories, let him make an attempt with them in some synthetic assertion. For an analytic one does not bring the understanding further, and since it is occupied only with what is already thought in the concept, it leaves undecided whether this has in itself relation to objects, or only signifies the unity of thinking in general (which abstracts entirely from the way in which an object may be given), it is enough for it to know what lies in its concept; to what the concept itself may go is indifferent to it. Let him therefore try it with some synthetic and supposedly transcendental principle, such as: everything that is, exists as substance, or as a determination adhering to it; everything contingent exists as the effect of another thing, namely of its cause, etc. Now I ask: from where will he take these synthetic propositions, since the concepts are supposed to be valid not in relation to possible experience but of things in themselves (noumena)? Where is the third here, which is always required for a synthetic proposition, in order to connect with one another in the same concepts that have no logical (analytic) affinity at all? He will never prove his proposition, indeed, what is more, will not even be able to justify himself regarding the possibility of such a pure assertion, without taking regard of the empirical use of the understanding, and thereby renouncing entirely the pure and sense-free judgment. Thus the concept of pure merely intelligible objects is entirely empty of all principles of their application, because one can think up no way in which they should be given, and the problematic thought, which nevertheless leaves a place open for them, serves only, like an empty space, to limit the empirical principles, without nevertheless containing and displaying in itself any other object of cognition outside the sphere of the latter.",["'Grundsatz' = 'principle'; 'Satz' = 'proposition'; both appear (also 'Grundsätze' at the end).","'Behauptung' = 'assertion'; 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'; 'Zufällige' = 'contingent'; 'Wirkung' = 'effect'.","'um in demselben Begriffe, die gar keine ... Verwandtschaft haben, mit einander zu verknüpfen': the number disagreement ('Begriffe' for 'Begriffen'/'Begriffe') is preserved as awkward; 'in demselben' could be read with 'Satze' or 'Begriffe'.","'gültig' = 'valid'; 'Verknüpfen' = 'connect'; 'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; 'Obiect' = 'object'.","'Rücksicht zu nehmen' rendered 'taking regard of'; 'sinnenfreyen' = 'sense-free'.","'Sinn' not in this passage; 'bedeute' = 'signifies'. 'Platz' = 'place' (glossary Ort = place is a different word)."]],"krv-a-0457":["Appendix. On the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection through the confusion of the empirical use of the understanding with the transcendental.",["'Reflexionsbegriffe' rendered 'concepts of reflection' (Überlegung = reflection in glossary; here the German is 'Reflexion').","'Amphibolie' = 'amphiboly'; 'Verwechselung' = 'confusion'."]],"krv-a-0458":["Reflection (*reflexio*) has to do not with the objects themselves, in order to get concepts of them directly, but is the state of the mind in which we first prepare ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we can arrive at concepts. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to our different sources of cognition, through which alone their relation among one another can be correctly determined. The first question before all further treatment of our representations is this: in which faculty of cognition do they belong together? Is it the understanding, or are it the senses, by which they are connected or compared? Many a judgment is accepted from habit, or connected through inclination; but because no reflection precedes, or at least follows it critically, it passes for one that has gotten its origin in the understanding. Not all judgments need an investigation, i.e., an attention to the grounds of truth; for if they are immediately certain: e.g., between two points only one straight line can be, then no still closer mark of truth than what they themselves express can be indicated for them. But all judgments, indeed all comparisons, need a reflection, i.e., a distinction of the power of cognition to which the given concepts belong. The action by which I hold the comparison of representations in general together with the power of cognition in which it is undertaken, and by which I distinguish whether they are compared with one another as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensible intuition, I call transc. reflection. The relations, however, in which concepts in a state of mind can belong to one another are those of identity and difference, of agreement and conflict, of the inner and the outer, finally of the determinable and of determination (matter and form). The correct determination of this relation rests on in which power of cognition they belong to one another subjectively, whether in sensibility or in the understanding. For the difference of the latter makes a great difference in the way in which one should think the former.",["Ueberlegung and Reflexion are both rendered 'reflection'; the German word is given in Latin (*reflexio*) only where Kant supplies it.","Erkentnißquellen = sources of cognition; Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Erkentnißkraft = power of cognition (Kraft kept distinct from Vermögen).","'vor denen sie verknüpft, oder verglichen werden': 'vor' is here read as 'by' (or 'for'); the reading is doubtful and left open.","Unterschied = 'distinction'/'difference' (in 'der Unterschied der letzteren macht einen grossen Unterschied'); Verschiedenheit = 'difference'. Einerleyheit = 'identity'; Einstimmung = 'agreement'; Widerstreit = 'conflict'.","'der letzteren' / 'die ersten': antecedents are left as ambiguous as in the German; 'latter' and 'former' are literal.","'transsc.' is Kant's abbreviation (transscendentale) and is kept as an abbreviation. 'bestimbaren' is read as 'bestimmbaren'; 'kan', 'Bewustseyn' etc. are old spellings.","'so gilt es vor ein solches': 'vor' = 'für'; 'passes for'."]],"krv-a-0459":["Before all objective judgments we compare the concepts, in order to find identity (of many representations under one concept) for the sake of universal judgments, or the difference of them, for the generation of particular ones, agreement, from which affirmative, and conflict, from which negative judgments can come to be, etc. On this ground we should, as it seems, call the concepts adduced concepts of comparison (*conceptus comparationis*). But because, when it depends not on the logical form but on the content of the concepts, i.e., whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or in conflict, etc., the things however can have a twofold relation to our power of cognition, namely to sensibility and to the understanding, but on this place, in which they belong, the way depends in which they are supposed to belong to one another: so transcendental reflection, i.e., the relation of given representations to one or the other kind of cognition, will alone be able to determine their relation among one another, and whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or in conflict, etc., will not be able to be settled straightaway from the concepts themselves through mere comparison (*comparatio*), but first of all through the distinction of the kind of cognition to which they belong, by means of a transcendental reflection (*reflexio*). One could thus indeed say: that logical reflection is a mere comparison, for in it there is complete abstraction from the power of cognition to which the given representations belong, and they are therefore, so far as their seat in the mind is concerned, to be treated as homogeneous; but transcendental reflection (which goes to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of the objective comparison of the representations among one another, and is therefore very much different from the latter, because the power of cognition to which they belong is not precisely the same. This transcendental reflection is a duty from which no one can renounce himself if he wants to judge anything a priori about things. We now want to take it in hand, and will draw from it no little light for the determination of the proper business of the understanding.",["Ueberlegung/Reflexion: both 'reflection'; Kant's Latin *reflexio* and *comparatio* kept in italics as given.","'Behuf' = 'sake'; 'Erkentnißart' = 'kind of cognition' (Art = kind here, 'way' in 'die Art ... wie').","The sentence beginning 'Weil aber' is kept as one with its anacoluthic colon; 'etc' stands where the German has it.","'Einerleyheit' = identity; 'Verschiedenheit' = difference; 'Einstimmung' = agreement; 'Widerstreit' = conflict; 'verneinende' = negative; 'beiahende' = affirmative.","'der letzteren' (comparison) is read as referring to the preceding 'Comparation'; 'dem Gegenstand/Dinge': Gegenstände = objects, Dinge = things.","'Sitz' = seat; 'gleichartig' = homogeneous; 'a priori' left in Latin as in the original."]],"krv-a-0460":["1. Identity and difference. If an object is presented to us several times, but each time with just the same inner determinations (*qualitas et quantitas*), then it, if it counts as an object of the pure understanding, is always just the same, and not many, but only one thing (*numerica identitas*); but if it is appearance, then the comparison of concepts does not matter at all, but, however much everything with regard to them may be identical, the difference of the places of this appearance at the same time is nevertheless a sufficient ground of the numerical difference of the object (of the senses) itself. Thus in the case of two drops of water one can abstract entirely from all inner difference (of quality and quantity), and it is enough that they are intuited in different places at once in order to hold them to be numerically different. Leibniz took appearances as things in themselves, hence as *intelligibilia*, i.e., objects of the pure understanding, (although, because of the confusion of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena) and there his proposition of the indiscernible (*principium identitatis indiscernibilium*) could certainly not be disputed; but since they are objects of sensibility, and the understanding with regard to them is not of pure but merely of empirical use, multiplicity and numerical difference are already given by space itself, as the condition of outer appearances. For a part of space, although it may be entirely similar and equal to another, is nevertheless outside it, and just thereby a part different from the first, which is added to it in order to make up a greater space, and this must therefore hold of everything that is at once in the manifold places of space, however much it may otherwise be similar and equal.",["Einerleyheit = 'identity' (rendered as in the title, 'Einerleyheit und Verschiedenheit'); the Latin *identitas* appears separately in *numerica identitas*.","'Gegenstand' = object throughout; 'Ding' = thing; 'Ding an sich selbst' = 'thing in itself' (plural 'things in themselves').","'genugsamer Grund' = 'sufficient ground' (genugsam, not zureichend).","'Oerter' = 'places'; 'Stellen' = 'places' as well (one English word for two German words).","'Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden': Satz = 'proposition'. 'Vielheit' = 'multiplicity'.","'Verworrenheit' = 'confusion'; the parenthesis is kept in its place. 'angeschaut' = 'intuited'."]],"krv-a-0461":["2. Agreement and conflict. If reality is represented only through the pure understanding, (*realitas noumenon*), then no conflict can be thought between the realities, i.e., such a relation in which, combined in one subject, they cancel one another's sequels, and 3 — 3 = 0. By contrast the real in appearance (*realitas phaenomenon*) can certainly be in conflict among one another, and, united in the same subject, one can annihilate the sequel of the other entirely or in part, like two moving forces in the same straight line, insofar as they either pull or push a point in opposite directions, or also a pleasure that balances pain.",["Realität = 'reality'; das Reale = 'the real'. Kant's Latin *realitas noumenon*/*phaenomenon* kept (the original prints 'noümenon' and 'phænomenon').","'Folge' = 'sequel' per the glossary (Folgerung would be 'consequence').","'unter einander' in 'kan ... unter einander allerdings im Widerstreit seyn' is rendered 'among one another'; the grammatical referent of 'das Reale' (singular) with plural verb forms is left as in the German.","'Subiect' = 'subject'; 'bewegende Kräfte' = 'moving forces'; 'Vergnügen' = 'pleasure'.","'ziehen, oder drücken' = 'pull or push' ('drücken' could also be 'press'); 'die Wage hält' = 'balances'."]],"krv-a-0462":["3. The inner and outer. In an object of the pure understanding only that is inner which has no relation (as far as existence is concerned) to anything different from it. The inner determinations of a *substantia phaenomenon* in space, on the contrary, are nothing but relations, and it itself is entirely and completely a sum total of mere relations. We know substance in space only through forces that are efficacious in it, either to drive others toward it (attraction), or to keep from penetrating into it (repulsion and impenetrability); other properties we do not know, which constitute the concept of the substance that appears in space, and that we call matter. As object of the pure understanding, on the contrary, every substance must have inner determinations and forces that pertain to inner reality. But what can I think to myself as inner accidents, except those that my inner sense offers me, namely that which is either itself a thinking, or analogous with this. Hence Leibniz, because he represented them to himself as noumena, made out of all substances, even out of the constituents of matter, after he had taken from them in thought everything that may signify outer relation, hence also composition, simple subjects endowed with forces of representation, in a word, monads.",["Beziehung, Verhältnis and Relation are all rendered \"relation\"; the German words differ (Beziehung in the first sentence, Verhältnisse in the second, Relationen in the third).","Gegenstand (\"an object of the pure understanding\") and Obiect (\"As object of the pure understanding\") are both \"object\".","Kennen (\"we know\") is kept distinct from erkennen; Eigenschaften = \"properties\"; Bestandtheile = \"constituents\"; Vorstellungskräfte = \"forces of representation\" (Kraft = force).","The sentence \"Allein was kan ich mir vor innere Accidenzen denken...\" ends with a period in the original though it is a question in form; kept as it stands. \"Vor\" = modern \"für\" (\"as\"). \"Mit diesem\" could be \"with this (a thinking)\"; left as in German.","\"Aeussere\" (outer) and \"äussere\" (outer) are rendered alike; \"Daseyn\" = \"existence\"."]],"krv-a-0463":["4. Matter and form. These are two concepts that are laid at the ground of all other reflection, so inseparably are they combined with every use of the understanding. The former signifies the determinable in general, the second its determination, (both in the transcendental sense, since one abstracts from every difference in what is given and in the way in which it is determined). The logicians formerly called the universal the matter, but the specific difference the form. In every judgment one can call the given concepts the logical matter (for the judgment), the relation of these (by means of the copula) the form of the judgment. In every being the components of it (*essentialia*) are the matter, the way in which they are connected in a thing, the essential form. Also, with regard to things in general, unbounded reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, but the restriction of it (negation) as that form through which one thing distinguishes itself from another according to transc. concepts. The understanding, namely, demands first that something be given (at least in the concept) in order to be able to determine it in a certain way. Hence in the concept of the pure understanding the matter precedes the form, and Leibniz for that reason assumed things first (monads) and inwardly a force of representation of them, in order afterwards to ground on this their outer relation and the community of their states (namely of the representations). Hence space and time were possible, the former only through the relation of the substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with one another, as grounds and sequences. So it would indeed have to be, if the pure understanding could be related immediately to objects and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But if they are only sensible intuitions, in which we determine all objects merely as appearances, then the form of intuition (as a subjective constitution of sensibility) precedes all matter (the sensations), hence space and time precede all appearances and all *data* of experience, and rather make these possible for the first time. The intellectual philosopher could not tolerate that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility; a quite correct censure, if he assumed that we intuit things as they are, (though with confused representation). But since sensible intuition is a quite special subjective condition, which lies at the ground of all perception *a priori*, and whose form is original; the form is given by itself alone, and far from it, that the matter (or the things themselves, which appeared) should lie at the ground (as one would have to judge according to mere concepts), the possibility of them rather presupposes a formal intuition (time and space) as given.",["Bedeuten = \"signify\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Verhältnis = \"relation\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; Zustände = \"states\".","Reflexion = \"reflection\" (cf. Überlegung, also \"reflection\" in the following passages). Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\".","\"Bestandstücke\" = \"components\" (Bestandtheile elsewhere \"constituents\"); \"Einschränkung\" = \"restriction\" (Schranke would be \"limitation\"); \"unbegränzte\" = \"unbounded\".","\"Gründe und Folgen\": Folge rendered \"sequences\" per the glossary (Folge = sequel; sequence).","\"in transscendentalem Verstande\" rendered \"in the transcendental sense\"; \"transsc.\" is the author's abbreviation, kept as \"transc.\". \"Verstande\" here is the old dative of Verstand.","\"weit gefehlt, daß...\" is an elliptical idiom, rendered literally and left awkward; the construction \"so sezt ... voraus\" after the \"daß\" clause is kept as in German. \"zum Grunde liegen\" = \"lie at the ground\". \"vor\" in \"vor aller Materie\" = \"before/precedes\"; \"Intellectualphilosoph\" = \"intellectual philosopher\"; *datis* is Latin in the original, italicized."]],"krv-a-0464":["Remark on the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection.",["Reflexionsbegriffe = \"concepts of reflection\"; Amphibolie = \"amphiboly\"."]],"krv-a-0465":["Allow me to call the position that we assign to a concept either in sensibility or in the pure understanding the transcendental place. In this way the estimation of this position, which pertains to every concept according to the difference of its use, and the instruction, according to rules, to determine this place for all concepts, would be transcendental topic; a doctrine that would thoroughly preserve against subreptions of the pure understanding and the delusions arising from them, by always distinguishing to which faculty of cognition the concepts properly belong. One can call every concept, every title under which many cognitions belong, a logical place. On this is grounded the logical topic of Aristotle, which schoolteachers and orators could make use of, in order to look up, under certain titles of thinking, what would best suit their matter at hand, and to rationalize about it, with an illusion of thoroughness, or to chatter verbosely.",["Stelle = \"position\", Ort = \"place\"; Beurteilung = \"estimation\" (per glossary); Lehre = \"doctrine\"; Topik = \"topic\".","Erschleichungen = \"subreptions\"; Blendwerke = \"delusions\"; Erkentnißkraft = \"faculty of cognition\"; Erkentnisse = \"cognitions\".","\"vernünfteln\" = \"rationalize\"; \"Schein\" = \"illusion\"; \"Gründlichkeit\" = \"thoroughness\"; \"vor seine vorliegende Materie\" has \"vor\" = modern \"für\"; \"seine\" is ambiguous in its referent in German and left so."]],"krv-a-0466":["The transcendental topic, on the contrary, contains no more than the four titles of all comparison and distinction cited, which differ from the categories in that through them not the object according to that which constitutes its concept, (magnitude, reality) but only the comparison of representations, which precedes the concept of things, is exhibited in all its manifoldness. This comparison, however, requires first of all a reflection, i.e., a determination of that place where the representations of the things that are compared belong, whether the pure understanding thinks them, or sensibility gives them in appearance.",["Titel = \"titles\"; Vergleichung = \"comparison\"; Unterscheidung = \"distinction\"; Categorien = \"categories\"; Mannigfaltigkeit = \"manifoldness\"; dargestellt = \"exhibited\".","Ueberlegung = \"reflection\" (same English word as Reflexion in A 266); Gegenstand = \"object\".","\"vor dem Begriffe von Dingen vorhergeht\": could be read \"precedes the concept of things\" or \"precedes the concept, [in the case] of things\"; the English keeps the first, closest to the German order. \"in aller ihrer Mannigfaltigkeit\" may attach to \"representations\" or to the exhibiting; left as in German.","\"die ... verglichen werden\" and the relative \"die sich dadurch von Categorien unterscheiden\" (earlier clause) are kept in order."]],"krv-a-0467":["Concepts can be compared logically, without troubling oneself about where their objects belong, whether as noumena for the understanding, or as phenomena for sensibility. But if we want to go with these concepts to the objects, then transcendental reflection is first of all necessary, for which faculty of cognition they are to be objects, whether for the pure understanding, or for sensibility. Without this reflection I make a very unsure use of these concepts, and putative synthetic principles arise, which critical reason cannot recognize, and which are grounded solely on a transcendental amphiboly, i.e., a confusion of the object of the pure understanding with the appearance.",["Obiecte and Gegenstände are both \"objects\"; Verstandesobiect = \"object of the pure understanding\".","*noumena*, *phenomena* are Latin/Greek technical words in the original (Noumena, Phänomena), kept in lower-case roman here; vor = modern \"für\" (\"for\").","Gebrauch = \"use\"; Grundsätze = \"principles\"; vermeinte = \"putative\"; Verwechselung = \"confusion\"; anerkennen = \"recognize\"; Erkentnißkraft = \"faculty of cognition\"."]],"krv-a-0468":["In the absence of such a transcendental topic, and thus deceived by the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection, the famous Leibniz erected an intellectual system of the world, or rather believed himself to cognize the inner constitution of things, by comparing all objects only with the understanding and the abstracted formal concepts of his thinking. Our table of the concepts of reflection procures for us the unexpected advantage of laying before our eyes what is distinguishing in his doctrinal concept in all its parts, and at the same time the guiding ground of this peculiar way of thinking, which rested on nothing but a misunderstanding. He compared all things merely through concepts with one another, and found, as is natural, no other differences than those through which the understanding distinguishes its pure concepts from one another. The conditions of sensible intuition, which bring with them their own distinctions, he did not regard as original; for sensibility was for him only a confused way of representation, and no special source of representations: appearance was for him the representation of the thing in itself, although distinguished from cognition through the understanding in accordance with the logical form, since namely the former, with its usual lack of analysis, draws a certain mixture of subsidiary representations into the concept of the thing, which the understanding knows how to separate from it. In a word: Leibniz intellectualized the appearances, just as Locke had altogether sensualized the concepts of the understanding according to his system of noogony (if it is permitted me to make use of these expressions), i.e., passed them off as nothing but empirical, though abstracted, concepts of reflection. Instead of seeking in the understanding and sensibility two entirely different sources of representations, which, however, could judge of things with objective validity only in connection, each of these great men held only to one of the two, which in his opinion related immediately to things in themselves, while the other did nothing but confuse or order the representations of the first.",["Reflexionsbegriffe = concepts of reflection; Amphibolie = amphiboly; Topik = topic; Denkungsart = way of thinking; Vorstellungsart = way of representation.","Lehrbegriff (not in glossary) rendered 'doctrinal concept'; Zergliederung rendered 'analysis'.","intellectuirte / sensificirt: rendered 'intellectualized' / 'sensualized'; Noogonie kept as 'noogony'. The German has Locke's verb at the end of its clause ('sensificirt ... ausgegeben hatte'); English order slightly adjusted.","Verschiedenheit / verschieden = 'difference' / 'different'; Unterschied / Unterscheiden = 'distinction' / 'distinguish'. Erkentniß = cognition; Quell = source; Verknüpfung = connection.","The subjunctives 'hielte' and 'bezöge' are rendered with the indicative past."]],"krv-a-0469":["Leibniz accordingly compared the objects of the senses, as things in general, merely in the understanding with one another, First, insofar as they are to be judged by it to be the same or different. Since he therefore had before his eyes merely their concepts, and not their position in intuition, in which alone the objects can be given, and left the transcendental place of these concepts (whether the object is to be counted among appearances or among things in themselves) entirely out of account, it could not turn out otherwise than that he extended his principle of what is not to be distinguished, which holds merely of concepts of things in general, also to the objects of the senses (*mundus phaenomenon*), and believed himself thereby to have procured for the cognition of nature no small expansion. To be sure: if I know a drop of water as a thing in itself according to all its inner determinations, then I cannot allow any of them to count as different from the other, if the whole concept of it is one and the same with it. But if it is appearance in space, then it has its place not merely in the understanding (among concepts), but in sensible outer intuition (in space), and there the physical places, with regard to the inner determinations of things, are entirely a matter of indifference, and a place = b can just as well take in a thing that is completely similar and equal to another in the place = a, as if it were ever so much inwardly different from it. The difference of places makes the plurality and distinction of objects, as appearances, without further conditions, already by itself not only possible but also necessary. Thus that seeming law is no law of nature. It is merely an analytic rule or comparison of things through mere concepts.",["Gegenstand = 'object' and Object = 'object' (here 'das Obiect' in the parenthesis).","Stelle = 'position'; Ort = 'place'; Oerter = 'places'.","Grundsatz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden rendered literally as 'principle of what is not to be distinguished'.","Erweiterung = 'expansion' (distinct from ausdehnen = 'extend'); Vielheit (not in glossary) = 'plurality'.","kennen = 'know' (noscere); gleichgültig = 'a matter of indifference'; 'mundus phaenomenon' is Latin, kept in italics (Kant's 'phænomenon' spelled with the ligature)."]],"krv-a-0470":["Second: the principle that realities (as mere affirmations) never logically conflict with one another is an entirely true proposition concerning the relation of concepts, but signifies, neither with regard to nature nor at all with regard to any thing in itself (of this we have no concept at all), the least. For real conflict takes place everywhere where A — B = 0, i.e., where one reality, combined with another in a subject, cancels the effect of the other, which all hindrances and counteractions in nature incessantly lay before our eyes, which nevertheless, since they rest on forces, must be called *realitates phaenomena*. General mechanics can even give the empirical condition of this conflict in a rule *a priori*, in that it looks to the opposition of the directions: a condition of which the transcend. concept of reality knows nothing at all. Although Herr von Leibniz did not exactly announce this proposition with the pomp of a new principle, he nevertheless made use of it for new assertions, and his successors expressly entered it in their Leibnizian-Wolffian doctrinal edifices. According to this principle, e.g., all evils are nothing but sequels of the limitations of creatures, i.e., negations, because these are the only thing conflicting with reality (in the mere concept of a thing in general it is indeed so, but not in things as appearances). Likewise its adherents find it not only possible but also natural to unite all reality in one being, without any conflict to be apprehended, because they know no other than that of contradiction (through which the concept of a thing itself is canceled), but not that of reciprocal detriment, where one real ground cancels the effect of the other, and for which we encounter the conditions only in sensibility, to represent such a one to ourselves.",["Widerstreit / widerstreiten = 'conflict'; Widerspruch = 'contradiction'; aufheben = 'cancel'.","'Satz' (diesen Satz) = 'proposition'; Grundsatz = 'principle'. Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge); Beiahungen = 'affirmations'.","'transscend.' is Kant's abbreviation, kept as 'transcend.'; 'a priori' is roman type in the original, hence italicized.","'einen solchen' at the end could refer to the real ground or to the detriment; left as 'such a one'.","Realgrund = 'real ground'; Abbruch = 'detriment'; besorglich = 'to be apprehended'; Anhänger = 'adherents'.","Reading 'seyn' (subjunctive) rendered with the indicative 'are'. 'Leibnitzwolfianische' rendered 'Leibnizian-Wolffian'."]],"krv-a-0471":["Third: the Leibnizian monadology has no other ground at all than that this philosopher represented the distinction of the inner and the outer merely in relation to the understanding. Substances in general must have something inner, which is therefore free of all outer relations, consequently also of composition. The simple is therefore the foundation of the inner of things in themselves. But the inner of their state also cannot consist in place, shape, contact or motion (which determinations are all outer relations), and we can therefore ascribe to substances no other inner state than that through which we determine our sense itself inwardly, namely the state of representations. Thus then the monads were finished, which are supposed to make up the basic material of the whole universe, whose active force, however, consists only in representations, through which they are properly merely effective in themselves.",["Verhältniß = 'relation'; Zustand = 'state'; Ort = 'place'; Sinn = 'sense' (n.).","Grundlage = 'foundation'; Grundstoff = 'basic material' (Stoff = 'material'); thätige Kraft = 'active force'.","'So wurden denn die Monaden fertig' is idiomatic and obscure ('were finished' / 'got done'); kept literal."]],"krv-a-0472":["Precisely for this reason, however, his *principium* of the possible community of substances with one another also had to be a preestablished harmony, and could not be a physical influence. For because everything is occupied only inwardly, i.e., with its representations, the state of representations of the one substance could stand with that of the other in no effective combination at all, but some third cause, flowing into all together, had to make their states correspond to one another, indeed not exactly through occasional assistance, applied particularly in each individual case (*Systema assistentiae*), but through the unity of the idea of a cause valid for all, in which they all together must receive their existence and persistence, hence also reciprocal correspondence with one another according to universal laws.",["Principium is Latin in the original ('Principium'), italicized; the Latin 'Systema assistentiæ' is likewise kept in italics.","Gemeinschaft = 'community'; Verbindung = 'combination'; Daseyn = 'existence'; Beharrlichkeit = 'persistence'; Einfluß = 'influence'.","'einfliessende' rendered 'flowing into' to keep its connection with Einfluß; 'Beystand' = 'assistance'.","'vorherbestimte' = 'preestablished'; 'vor alle gültigen' = 'valid for all'."]],"krv-a-0473":["Fourthly: his famous doctrine of time and space, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, had arisen solely from just the same deception of transcendental reflection. If I want to represent outer relations of things through the mere understanding, this can happen only by means of a concept of their reciprocal effect, and if I am to connect one state of one and the same thing with another state, this can happen only in the order of grounds and consequences. Thus Leibniz thought of space as a certain order in the community of substances, and of time as the dynamical sequence of their states. But the peculiar, and what is independent of things, that both seem to have in themselves, he ascribed to the confusion of these concepts, which brought it about that what is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be an intuition subsisting on its own, and preceding the things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. But the things were intelligible substances (*substantiae noumena*). Nevertheless he wanted to make these concepts valid for appearances, because he did not concede to sensibility any way of its own of intuiting, but sought all of it, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to the senses nothing but the contemptible business of confusing and distorting the representations of the former.",["Text printed 'Folgen', 'Gemeinschaft', 'Wirkung': rendered 'consequences' (Folgen), 'community' (Gemeinschaft), 'effect' (Wirkung); 'Folge' in 'dynamische Folge' is 'sequence' per glossary, 'Folgen' with 'Gründe' rendered 'consequences' (cf. Folgerung); the same German word thus appears under two English words.","'intellectuirte' rendered 'intellectualized'; 'Verworrenheit' = 'confusion' (verworren, 'confused'); 'verunstalten' = 'distort'.","'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Zustand' = state; 'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility.","'vor' is the older spelling of 'für/vor' in 'vor eine eigene ... Anschauung gehalten' and 'vor Erscheinungen geltend'; rendered 'held to be' and 'valid for'. 'substantiæ Noümena' is Kant's own Latin, kept in italics with modernized ligature.","'Das Eigenthümliche ... was beide an sich zu haben scheinen': 'an sich' here can be read as 'in themselves' (as I have it) or merely 'to have about them'; the German leaves both open."]],"krv-a-0474":["But even if we could say something synthetically about things in themselves through the pure understanding, (which is nevertheless impossible) this could still not be extended to appearances, which do not represent things in themselves. In this latter case, therefore, I will always have to compare my concepts in transcendental reflection only under the conditions of sensibility, and thus space and time will be determinations not of things in themselves but of appearances: what things in themselves may be, I do not know, and also do not need to know, since a thing can never come before me otherwise than in appearance.",["'Ueberlegung' = reflection (glossary); elsewhere 'Reflexion' is also rendered 'reflection' — one English word for two German words.","'Bestimmungen' = determinations; 'Bedingungen' = conditions; 'Erscheinung' = appearance.","'in der Erscheinung' rendered 'in appearance' (no article in German)."]],"krv-a-0475":["I proceed in the same way with the remaining concepts of reflection. Matter is *substantia phaenomenon*. What belongs to it internally, I seek in all parts of the space that it occupies, and in all effects that it exerts, and which, to be sure, can always be only appearances of outer sense. Thus I do indeed have nothing absolutely, but only comparatively internal, which itself in turn consists of outer relations. But the absolutely internal of matter, according to the pure understanding, is also a mere whim; for this is not at all an object for the pure understanding, whereas the transcendental object, which may be the ground of this appearance that we call matter, is a mere something, of which we would not even understand what it is, if someone could tell it to us. For we can understand nothing except what carries with it, in intuition, something corresponding to our words. If the complaints: We have no insight at all into the inner of things, are supposed to mean as much as: we do not comprehend through the pure understanding what the things that appear to us may be in themselves, then they are quite unfair and unreasonable; for they want one to be able to cognize things without senses, hence to intuit them, consequently that we should have a faculty of cognition entirely different from the human not merely in degree, but even in intuition and kind, and thus should be not human beings, but beings of which we ourselves cannot say whether they are even possible, much less how they are constituted. Observation and dissection of appearances penetrates into the inner of nature, and one cannot know how far this will go in time. But those transcendental questions, which go beyond nature, we would nevertheless never be able to answer for all that, even if all of nature were uncovered to us, and it is not even given to us to observe our own mind with another intuition than that of our inner sense. For in it lies the secret of the origin of our sensibility. Its relation to an object and what the transcendental ground of this unity may be, lies without doubt too deeply hidden for us, who know even ourselves only through inner sense, hence as appearance, to be able to use so unsuitable a tool of our investigation to find anything other than always again appearances, whose non-sensible cause we would nevertheless gladly investigate.",["'Gegenstand' (object) and 'Obiect' (object): one English word for both; 'Obiect' is the Latinate member ('transscendentale Obiect', 'Beziehung auf ein Obiect').","'verstehen' = understand; 'begreifen' = comprehend; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'kennen' = know; 'wissen' = know — 'know' stands for both 'wissen' and 'kennen'.","'Schlechthin - sondern lauter Comparativinnerliches': the hyphenated German ('nichts Schlechthin- [innerliches], sondern ...') is elliptical; rendered 'nothing absolutely, but only comparatively internal'.","'Grille' = 'whim' (literally 'cricket'); 'Gemüth' = mind; 'Erkentnißvermögen' = faculty of cognition; 'Werkzeug' = tool.","'Ihre Beziehung ... dieser Einheit': 'Ihre' can refer to 'Sinnlichkeit' (as rendered) and 'dieser Einheit' has no clear antecedent; preserved. 'Wenn die Klagen: ...' follows the German colon. 'substantia phænomenon' modernized to 'phaenomenon'.","'ein Unsern Worten Correspondirendes in der Anschauung': 'in der Anschauung' may attach to 'Correspondirendes' or to 'mit sich führet'; the English keeps the same ambiguity. 'Innere/Innerliche' rendered 'inner'/'internal'."]],"krv-a-0476":["What makes this critique of inferences, from the mere actions of reflection, exceedingly useful, is: that it distinctly demonstrates the nullity of all inferences about objects that one merely compares with one another in the understanding, and at the same time confirms what we have chiefly impressed: that, although appearances, as things in themselves, are not comprehended among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only ones in which our cognition can have objective reality, namely, where intuition corresponds to the concepts.",["'Schlüsse' = inferences; 'Critik' = critique; 'Handlungen der Reflexion' = actions of reflection.","'Gegenstände' and 'Obiecten' both 'objects'; 'Obiecten' is the Latinate member. 'begriffen' (mit begriffen) = 'comprehended'.","'als Dinge an sich selbst' may attach to 'Erscheinungen' (appearances as things in themselves) or to the 'not ... comprehended'; the English preserves the ambiguity.","'eingeschärft' = 'impressed'; 'Realität' = reality; 'Erkentniß' = cognition."]],"krv-a-0477":["If we reflect merely logically, we merely compare our concepts with one another in the understanding, whether both contain just the same, whether they contradict one another or not, whether something is contained internally in the concept or is added to it, and which of the two is to be given, but which is to count only as a way of thinking the given one. But if I apply these concepts to an object in general (in the transcendental understanding), without determining this further, whether it is an object of sensible or intellectual intuition, then limitations immediately show themselves (not to go out beyond this concept), which pervert all empirical use of them, and thereby prove: that the representation of an object, as a thing in general, is not merely insufficient, but, without sensible determination of it, and independent of empirical condition, is in itself self-conflicting, that one must therefore either abstract from every object (in logic) or, if one assumes one, think it under conditions of sensible intuition, hence that the intelligible would require an entirely special intuition, which we do not have, and in the absence of it is nothing for us, but on the other hand that appearances also cannot be objects in themselves. For if I think merely of things in general, then the difference of the outer relations can of course not constitute a difference of the things themselves, but rather presupposes it, and, if the concept of the one is not at all distinguished internally from that of the other, I then posit only one and the same thing in different relations. Further, through the addition of a mere affirmation (reality) to another, the positive is indeed increased, and nothing is withdrawn from it or removed, hence the real in things in general cannot conflict with one another, etc.",["'Art' in 'eine Art, den gegebenen zu denken' = 'way'; 'Gebrauch' = use; 'Einschränkungen' = limitations (not 'Schranke'); 'Beiahung' = affirmation (bejahen); 'Realität' = reality.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'Dinges überhaupt' = 'thing in general'; 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'Anschauung' = intuition.","'widerstreitend' rendered 'self-conflicting' / 'widerstreiten' = 'conflict' (distinct from 'Widerspruch' = contradiction, 'sich widersprechen' = 'contradict').","'transsc. Verstande' is Kant's abbreviation, rendered 'transcendental understanding'. 'so fort' printed apart; 'immediately'.","'dieselben' ('ihrer') and 'derselben' in 'ohne sinnliche Bestimmung derselben' refer ambiguously (to the representation or the object); left ambiguous. 'Sachen' = things (as 'Dinge'): one English word for two German words.","'das intelligibele' kept lower-case as in the text. 'dagegen aber auch' syntax kept; the construction after 'daß man also' is long and loosely parallel, preserved."]],"krv-a-0478":["The concepts of reflection have, as we have shown, through a certain misinterpretation such an influence on the use of the understanding that they have been able to seduce even one of the most acute among all philosophers to a supposed system of intellectual cognition, which undertakes to determine its objects without the addition of the senses. Just on that account the development of the deceptive cause of the amphiboly of these concepts, in occasioning false principles, is of great use in reliably determining and securing the boundaries of the understanding.",["'Gränzen' = boundaries (Grenze = bound(ary)); 'Grundsätze' = principles; 'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'Gegenstände' = objects.","'Amphibolie' kept as 'amphiboly'; 'Veranlassung' = occasioning; 'in Veranlassung falscher Grundsätze' is ambiguous (the development occasions false principles, or occurs on the occasion of them); the English preserves this.","'Dazukunft' = 'addition' (literally 'coming-to'); 'Verstandesgebrauch' = 'use of the understanding'."]],"krv-a-0479":["It must indeed be said: what pertains to or contradicts a concept universally, that also pertains to or contradicts everything particular that is contained under that concept; (*dictum de Omni et Nullo*) but it would be absurd to alter this logical principle so that it read: what is not contained in a universal concept, that is also not contained in the particular ones that stand under it; for these are particular concepts precisely because they contain more in themselves than is thought in the universal. Now the whole intellectual system of Leibniz is really built upon this latter principle: it therefore falls at the same time with it, together with all the ambiguity arising from it in the use of the understanding.",["Begriff = concept; Grundsatz = principle; besonder = particular; Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding; Zweideutigkeit = ambiguity; ungereimt = absurd.","Original reads 'daß ist auch in den besonderen'; 'daß' (for 'das') is translated as 'that' without comment in the text."]],"krv-a-0480":["The proposition of what is not to be distinguished was properly grounded on the presupposition: that if a certain distinction is not encountered in the concept of a thing in general, then it is also not to be encountered in the things themselves, consequently all things are entirely one and the same (*numero eadem*) that do not already differ from one another in their concept (in accordance with quality or quantity). But because in the mere concept of any thing abstraction has been made from many necessary conditions of an intuition, that from which abstraction is made is, through a peculiar haste, taken for something that is not to be encountered at all, and nothing is granted to the thing except what is contained in its concept.",["Satz here = proposition (Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden); Anschauung = intuition; Bedingung = condition; Uebereilung = haste.","'von ... abstrahirt worden' is impersonal passive; rendered 'abstraction has been made from'. 'davor genommen' = 'taken for' (that it is not to be encountered at all)."]],"krv-a-0481":["The concept of a cubic foot of space, however and wherever and as often as I may want to think it, is in itself entirely one and the same. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinguished merely by their places, (*numero diversa*) these are conditions of the intuition in which the object of this concept is given, which do not belong to the concept, but nonetheless to the whole of sensibility. Likewise there is in the concept of a thing no conflict at all, if nothing negating has been combined with an affirming, and merely affirmative concepts cannot, in combination, effect any cancellation at all. But in sensible intuition, in which reality (e.g., motion) is given, there are found conditions (opposite directions), from which abstraction had been made in the concept of motion in general, which make possible a conflict, which to be sure is not logical, namely one that makes a zero = 0 out of what is purely positive, and one could not say: that therefore all reality is agreement with one another, because no conflict is encountered among their concepts.*) According to mere concepts the inner is the substratum of all relation or outer determinations. If I therefore abstract from all conditions of intuition, and hold solely to the concept of a thing in general, then I can abstract from all outer relation, and there must nevertheless remain a concept of that which signifies no relation at all, but merely inner determinations. Now it seems that it follows from this: in every thing (substance) there is something that is absolutely inner, and precedes all outer determinations, in that it first makes them possible, hence this substratum is something that no longer contains in itself any outer relations, consequently simple: (for corporeal things are always only relations, at least of the parts outside one another) and because we know no absolutely inner determinations except those through our inner sense, this substratum would be not only simple, but also (according to the analogy with our inner sense) determined through representations, i.e., all things would properly be monads, or simple beings endowed with representations. This would also all have its correctness, if something more than the concept of a thing in general did not belong to the conditions under which alone objects of outer intuition can be given to us, and from which the pure concept abstracts. For there it shows itself: that a persistent appearance in space (impenetrable extension) can contain nothing but relations, and nothing absolutely inner at all, and yet be the first substratum of all outer perception. Through mere concepts I can, to be sure, think nothing outer without something inner, precisely because concepts of relation presuppose absolutely given things, and without these are not possible. But since in intuition there is contained something that does not lie at all in the mere concept of a thing in general, and this affords the substratum, which would not be cognized at all through mere concepts, namely, a space, which, with everything it contains, consists of purely formal, or also real relations, I cannot say: because, without an absolutely inner, no thing can be represented through mere concepts, there is also in the things themselves, which are contained under these concepts, and in their intuition nothing outer that did not have something absolutely inner lying at its ground. For when we have abstracted from all conditions of intuition, then in the mere concept nothing remains for us, to be sure, except the inner in general, and the relation of it among one another, through which alone the outer is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded solely on abstraction, does not take place with things insofar as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having something inner as ground, because they are not things in themselves, but merely appearances. What we know even in matter are purely relations, (what we call inner determinations of it is only comparatively inner), but among them there are self-subsisting and persistent ones, through which a determinate object is given to us. That I, when I abstract from these relations, have nothing further at all to think, does not cancel the concept of a thing as appearance, nor the concept of an object *in abstracto*, but does cancel all possibility of one that is determinable according to mere concepts, i.e., of a *Noumenon*. It is to be sure startling to hear that a thing is supposed to consist entirely of relations, but such a thing is also mere appearance, and cannot be thought at all through pure categories; it consists itself in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. Likewise one cannot well think the relations of things *in abstracto*, if one begins with mere concepts, otherwise than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is our concept of the understanding of relations itself. But since we then abstract from all intuition, a whole way in which the manifold can determine its place for one another, namely, the form of sensibility (space), drops out, which nevertheless precedes all empirical causality.",["Silent OCR corrections: 'kar' to 'gar'; 'so so fern' to 'so fern'; 'bestimbar' to 'bestimmbar'. The footnote marker *) is kept at its place; the footnote text is in krv-a-0482.","Gegenstand and Object (here 'Obiect') are both 'object'. Ding an sich selbst / an sich = 'in itself'/'in themselves'. Ort (Oerter) = place; Raum = space.","Widerstreit = conflict; Aufhebung = cancellation; Einstimmung = agreement; Verhältniß = relation; Verhältnißbegriffe = concepts of relation; Schlechthininneres = 'absolutely inner' (schlechthin innerlich).","Bestimmung = determination; bestimmt/bestimmen = determined/determine; bestimmbar = determinable; Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding; selbstständig = self-subsisting; beharrlich = persistent.","Ambiguity kept: 'einander seinen Ort bestimmen' (the manifold determining 'its place for one another'); the dative 'einander' is rendered as in the German construction, not resolved.","'und man konte nicht sagen' (konte = konnte) rendered 'one could not say', indicative as in the original; 'daß ich ... nichts weiter zu denken habe, hebt ... nicht auf' kept as one construction.","'Wenn ... gehörete nicht etwas mehr' is a counterfactual; 'seyn' in 'die unter diesen Begriffen enthalten seyn' is a subjunctive and is not rendered separately. Latin: *numero diversa*, *in abstracto*, *Noumenon* in italics."]],"krv-a-0482":["*) If one wanted to avail oneself here of the usual evasion: that at least *realitates Noümena* cannot work against one another, one would nevertheless have to adduce an example of such a pure and sense-free reality, so that one would understand whether such a one represents something at all or nothing at all. But no example can be taken from anywhere other than experience, which never offers more than *Phaenomena*, and so this proposition signifies nothing further than that the concept that contains nothing but affirmations contains nothing negating, a proposition of which we have never doubted.",["Footnote to the passage in krv-a-0481 ('*)'). Satz = proposition here; bedeuten = signify; Beiahungen = affirmations; Erfahrung = experience.","'woher anders' rendered 'from anywhere other than'; 'an dem wir niemals gezweifelt haben' kept as in German: 'of which we have never doubted' (literal 'at which')."]],"krv-a-0483":["If by merely intelligible objects we understand those things that are thought through pure categories, without any schema of sensibility, then such things are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our concepts of the understanding is merely the way of our sensible intuition, through which objects are given to us, and, if we abstract from the latter, then the former have no relation at all to any object. Indeed, even if one wanted to assume another way of intuition than this our sensible one, our functions for thinking would nevertheless be of no significance at all with regard to it. If we understand by them only objects of a non-sensible intuition, of which our categories to be sure do not hold, and of which we therefore can never have any cognition (neither intuition nor concept), then noumena in this merely negative significance must certainly be admitted: since they then say nothing other than: that our way of intuition does not extend to all things, but merely to objects of our senses, consequently its objective validity is bounded, and hence room is left over for some other way of intuition, and thus also for things as objects of it. But then the concept of a noumenon is problematic, i.e., the representation of a thing of which we can say neither that it is possible, nor that it is impossible, since we know no way of intuition other than our sensible one, and no way of concepts other than the categories, but neither of the two is appropriate to a non-sensible object. We can therefore not yet positively extend the field of the objects of our thinking beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and assume, outside appearances, still objects of pure thinking, i.e., noumena, because the former have no positive significance that can be specified. For one must concede of the categories: that they alone are not yet sufficient for the cognition of things in themselves, and without the *data* of sensibility would be merely subjective forms of the unity of the understanding, but without object. Thinking is, to be sure, in itself no product of the senses, and to that extent is also not limited by them, but is for that reason not immediately of its own and pure use, without the addition of sensibility, because it is then without object. One also cannot call the noumenon such an object; for this signifies precisely the problematic concept of an object for a wholly different intuition and a wholly different understanding than ours, which hence is itself a problem. The concept of the noumenon is therefore not the concept of an object, but the problem, unavoidably connected with the limitation of our sensibility, whether there may not be objects wholly released from that intuition of it, which question can be answered only indeterminately, namely: that, because sensible intuition does not extend to all things without distinction, room is left over for more and other objects, so that they cannot be absolutely denied, but, in the absence of a determinate concept (since no category is fit for it), also cannot be asserted as objects for our understanding.",["Gegenstand and Object are both rendered 'object' (Gegenstand/Gegenstände; Obiect/Object).","Bedeutung = 'significance' (in 'von gar keiner Bedeutung', 'negativen Bedeutung'); 'bedeutet' = 'signifies'.","Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Anschauung = 'intuition'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Aufgabe = 'problem' (Problem is also rendered 'problem').","'Verstehen wir darunter' (second occurrence): 'darunter' has no clear antecedent beyond 'intelligibele Gegenstände'; left as 'by them', following the German.","'iene' in 'weil iene keine anzugebende positive Bedeutung haben' is left as 'the former' (plural, following the German); its referent is ambiguous (Erscheinungen / Gegenstände des reinen Denkens).","'von iener ihrer Anschauung' rendered literally as 'from that intuition of it'; the German construction is awkward and the referent of 'ihrer' (Sinnlichkeit) is left unresolved.","'so fort' rendered 'immediately' (sofort); 'von eigenem und reinem Gebrauche' kept elliptical. 'data' is in Latin in the original and italicized.","'begränzt' = 'bounded' (Grenze = bound(ary)); 'Einschränkung' = 'limitation'; 'Zahl-': 'zureichen' = 'be sufficient'."]],"krv-a-0484":["The understanding accordingly bounds sensibility, without thereby extending its own field, and, in that it warns the latter that it should not presume to extend to things in themselves, but merely to appearances, it thinks for itself an object in itself, but only as a transcendental object, which is the cause of appearance (hence itself not appearance), and can be thought neither as magnitude, nor as reality, nor as substance etc., (because these concepts always require sensible forms, in which they determine an object) of which it is therefore wholly unknown whether it is to be encountered in us, or also outside us, whether it would be sublated together with sensibility, or, if we take the latter away, would still be left over. If we want to call this object noumenon, because the representation of it is not sensible, this is open to us. But since we can apply none of our concepts of the understanding to it, this representation nevertheless remains empty for us, and serves for nothing except to designate the boundaries of our sensible cognition, and to leave over a space that we can fill neither through possible experience nor through the pure understanding.",["begränzt = 'bounds'; Gränzen = 'boundaries'; Grösse = 'magnitude'; Realität = 'reality'.","'aufgehoben werden' rendered 'sublated' to keep the literal sense of aufheben; alternatives ('canceled', 'removed') are possible.","'Gegenstand' and 'Obiect' both 'object'. 'bestimmen' = 'determine'.","'und weder als Grösse...kan, (weil...) wovon': the relative 'wovon' is left with its loose antecedent as in the German (the object).","'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; Erfahrung = 'experience'."]],"krv-a-0485":["The critique of this pure understanding therefore does not permit one to create for oneself a new field of objects beyond those that can come before it as appearances, and to stray into intelligible worlds, indeed not even into their concept. The error that leads to this in the most plausible way, and can certainly be excused, though not justified, lies in this: that the use of the understanding, contrary to its vocation, is made transcendental, and the objects, i.e., possible intuitions, must conform to concepts, but concepts not to possible intuitions (as the only ones on which their objective validity rests). The cause of this, however, is again: that apperception, and, with it, thinking precedes all possible determinate ordering of representations. We therefore think something in general, and determine it on the one hand sensibly, but nevertheless distinguish the universal object represented *in abstracto* from this way of intuiting it; there then remains to us a way of determining it merely through thinking, which is to be sure a mere logical form without content, but nevertheless seems to us to be a way in which the object exists in itself (noumenon), without looking to the intuition, which is limited to our senses.",["Bestimmung = 'vocation' (glossary), not 'determination'; bestimmen/bestimmt = 'determine'/'determinate'.","'sich ... richten müssen' = 'must conform to'; the subject of 'müssen' is both 'Gegenstände' and (negated) 'Begriffe', as in the German.","'als auf denen allein ihre obiective Gültigkeit beruht': 'denen' refers to 'Anschauungen'; kept as 'the only ones'.","'Etwas überhaupt' = 'something in general'; 'Anordnung' = 'ordering'.","'ihn blos durch Denken zu bestimmen, übrig': 'ihn' refers to the object; 'Object an sich existire' — 'existire' = 'exists' (Existenz = existence).","'welche auf unsere Sinne eingeschränkt ist': 'welche' could attach to 'Anschauung' only; so rendered."]],"krv-a-0486":["Before we leave the transcendental analytic, we must still add something that, although in itself of no particular importance, nevertheless might seem required for the completeness of the system. The highest concept with which one is accustomed to begin a transcendental philosophy is commonly the division into the possible and the impossible. But since every division presupposes a concept to be divided, a still higher one must be given, and this is the concept of an object in general (taken problematically, and left undecided whether it is something or nothing.) Because the categories are the only concepts that relate to objects in general, the distinction of an object, whether it is something or nothing, will proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.",["Eintheilung/eingetheilt = 'division'/'divided' (Einteilung = division).","Transcendentalphilosophie = 'transcendental philosophy' (Weltweisheit is not used here).","Gegenstand = 'object'; 'überhaupt' = 'in general'.","'einzige Begriffe' left as in the German (plural without article)."]],"krv-a-0487":["1) To the concepts of all, many and one is opposed the one that cancels everything; i.e., none, and thus the object of a concept to which no intuition that can be specified corresponds, = nothing, i.e., a concept without object, like the noumena, which cannot be counted among the possibilities, although they must not for that reason be passed off as impossible, (*ens rationis*) or like perhaps certain new fundamental forces, which one thinks, to be sure without contradiction, but also thought without an example from experience, and which therefore must not be counted among the possibilities.",["'Allem, Vielen und Einem' = 'all, many and one' (cf. Allheit = totality, Menge = multiplicity); 'Keines' = 'none'.","'der, so alles aufhebt' — 'aufhebt' rendered 'cancels'.","'Möglichkeiten' = 'possibilities'; 'müssen' in the last clause is kept as in the German though it reads awkwardly ('must not be counted'); the earlier 'gezehlt' is OCR/orthographic for 'gezählt'.","'Grundkräfte' = 'fundamental forces' (Kraft = force). 'Widerspruch' = 'contradiction'. *ens rationis* is in Latin in the original (glossary: Gedankending)."]],"krv-a-0488":["2) Reality is something, negation is nothing, namely, a concept of the lack of an object, like the shadow, the cold (*nihil privativum*).",["Realität = 'reality'; Negation = 'negation'; Mangel = 'lack'."]],"krv-a-0489":["3) The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of it, (as appearance) like pure space and pure time (*ens imaginarium*) which are, to be sure, something, as forms of intuiting, but are themselves no objects that are intuited.",["'desselben' refers to 'Gegenstand' or possibly to 'Anschauung'; left as 'of it'.","'als Erscheinung' is ambiguous in attachment (to 'Gegenstand' or to 'desselben'); preserved by keeping the parenthesis in place.","'als Formen anzuschauen' = 'as forms of intuiting'; the infinitive construction is kept literal."]],"krv-a-0490":["4) The object of a concept that contradicts itself is nothing, because the concept is nothing, the impossible, like say the rectilinear figure of two sides (*nihil negativum*).",["'sich selbst widerspricht' = 'contradicts itself' (Widerspruch = contradiction).","'das Unmögliche' stands in apposition, as in the German.","'geradlinigte Figur von zwey Seiten' = 'rectilinear figure of two sides'."]],"krv-a-0491":["The table of this division of the concept of nothing (for the division of something running parallel to it follows of itself) would therefore have to be laid out thus: Nothing as 1. Empty concept without object, *ens rationis*; 2. Empty object of a concept, *nihil privativum*; 3. Empty intuition without object, *ens imaginarium*; 4. Empty object without concept, *nihil negativum*.",["Begriff = concept; Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition. Latin terms are Kant's own and kept in italics; *ens rationis* corresponds to Gedankending (thought-entity) in the next passage.","The numerals and the layout of the table are run together in the source (OCR/print); the numbering '1 ... 2. ...' is reproduced as given, with punctuation regularized only by separating the four members with semicolons."]],"krv-a-0492":["One sees: that the thought-entity (no. 1) is distinguished from the non-thing (no. 4) in that the former may not be counted among the possibilities, because it is merely a fiction (though not a contradictory one), while the latter is opposed to possibility, in that the concept even cancels itself. Both, however, are empty concepts. On the other hand the *nihil privativum* (no. 2) and the *ens imaginarium* (no. 3) are empty data for concepts. If light has not been given to the senses, one also cannot represent darkness to oneself, and if extended beings have not been perceived, no space. The negation as well as the mere form of intuition are, without something real, no objects.",["Gedankending = thought-entity (*ens rationis*); Undinge = non-thing (Un- + Ding).","Source 'so. gar' read as 'sogar' (OCR/print spacing); rendered 'even'.","'Obiecte' (Object) and Gegenstand both = 'object'; here the Latinate Obiecte.","Wesen = being(s); wahrgenommen = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).","'Data' kept in Latin form as in the German."]],"krv-a-0493":["Second Division of Transcendental Logic. The Transcendental Dialectic.",["'Abtheilung' rendered 'Division'; the glossary's Einteilung/Teilung is also 'division', so one English word stands for both.","'Dyalectik' is the original spelling of Dialektik."]],"krv-a-0494":["Introduction. I. On Transcendental Illusion.",["Schein = illusion (glossary); the same word is contrasted with Erscheinung = appearance in the next passage."]],"krv-a-0495":["Above we called dialectic in general a logic of illusion. This does not signify that it is a doctrine of probability; for the latter is truth, but cognized through insufficient grounds, the cognition of which is thus indeed deficient, but not for that reason deceptive, and consequently must not be separated from the analytic part of logic. Still less may appearance and illusion be held to be one and the same. For truth or illusion are not in the object, insofar as it is intuited, but in the judgment about it, insofar as it is thought. One can therefore indeed rightly say that the senses do not err, but not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth as well as error, and consequently also illusion, as the seduction to the latter, are to be encountered only in the judgment, i.e., only in the relation of the object to our understanding. In a cognition that agrees thoroughly with the laws of the understanding there is no error. In a representation of the senses (because it contains no judgment at all) there is also no error. But no force of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding, for itself alone (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses, for themselves, would err; the former not, because, if it acts merely in accordance with its laws, the effect (the judgment) must necessarily agree with these laws. But in agreement with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element of all truth. In the senses there is no judgment at all, neither a true nor a false one. Since we now have, besides these two sources of cognition, no other, it follows: that error is effected only through the unnoticed influence of sensibility on the understanding, whereby it happens: that subjective grounds of the judgment flow together with the objective ones, and make these deviate from their determination,*) just as a moved body would indeed for itself always hold to the straight line in the same direction, but which, if another force simultaneously influences it in another direction, turns into curvilinear motion. In order to distinguish the proper action of the understanding from the force that mixes itself in, it will therefore be necessary to regard the erroneous judgment as the diagonal between two forces, which determine the judgment in two different directions, which as it were enclose an angle, and to resolve that composite effect into the simple one of the understanding and of sensibility, which in pure judgments *a priori* must happen through transcendental reflection, whereby (as has already been indicated) each representation is assigned its place in the cognitive faculty appropriate to it, and consequently the influence of the latter on the former is also distinguished.\n\n*) [footnote: see next passage]",["Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Erscheinung = appearance; Schein = illusion; Irrthum = error; Urtheil = judgment; Erkentniß = cognition; Erkentnißkraft = cognitive faculty (Kraft = force/power; here 'faculty' used for -kraft in Erkentnißkraft/-vermögen, both Kraft and Vermögen occur).","Gegenstand = object; Obiect/obiectiv = objective (Latinate). 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Verhältnisse' = relation.","'vor sich allein' / 'vor sich' (= für sich) rendered 'for itself'; 'vor einerley gehalten' = 'held to be one and the same'.","Ambiguity kept: 'die aber, wenn ...' (relative clause after 'Cörper'/'Linie') — the antecedent of 'die' is the body or the line; English 'but which' preserves this.","'das formale aller Wahrheit' rendered 'the formal element of all truth' (substantivized adjective).","'Bestimmung' = determination; 'ihrer Bestimmung abweichend' may also mean 'vocation'.","The footnote marker *) stands in the text; the footnote text is in the next passage (krv-a-0496)."]],"krv-a-0496":["*) Sensibility, laid beneath the understanding as the object on which the latter applies its function, is the source of real cognitions. But the very same, insofar as it influences the action of the understanding itself, and determines it to judge, is the ground of error.",["Obiect = object; Function kept as 'function'; Verstandeshandlung = action of the understanding; Grund = ground.","'reale Erkentnisse' = real cognitions; 'bestimt' = determines."]],"krv-a-0497":["Our business here is not to deal with empirical illusion (e.g., the optical), which is found in the empirical use of otherwise correct rules of the understanding and through which the power of judgment is misled by the influence of imagination, but we have to do with transcendental illusion alone, which influences principles whose use is not even laid out for experience, in which case we would at least have a touchstone of their correctness, but which, against all the warnings of critique, carries us away entirely beyond the empirical use of the categories and holds us in suspense with the delusion of an extension of the pure understanding. We will call the principles whose application keeps itself entirely within the limits of possible experience immanent, but those which are supposed to fly beyond these boundaries transcendent principles. By these, however, I understand not the transcendental use or misuse of the categories, which is a mere fault of the power of judgment not duly reined in by critique, which does not pay enough heed to the boundary of the ground on which alone the pure understanding is permitted its play; but actual principles which require us to tear down all those boundary-posts and to arrogate to ourselves an entirely new ground, which recognizes no demarcation anywhere. Hence transcendental and transcendent are not one and the same. The principles of the pure understanding, which we presented above, are supposed to be of merely empirical and not of transcendental use, i.e., use reaching beyond the boundary of experience. But a principle that removes these limitations, indeed even commands us to overstep them, is called transcendent. If our critique can succeed in uncovering the illusion of these presumptuous principles, then those principles of merely empirical use, in contrast with the latter, can be called immanent principles of the pure understanding.",["Grundsatz = principle; Schranken = limitations; Gränzen/Gränzpfähle = boundaries/boundary-posts (Grenze = boundary); Gränze des Bodens rendered 'boundary of the ground' (Boden = ground, same English as Grund; Boden is the German soil/ground).","Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Einbildung = imagination (Einbildungskraft in glossary; here the text has Einbildung); Erfahrung = experience; Verstand = understanding.","Ambiguity kept: 'sich ... anzumassen' / 'zumuthen' rendered close to the German; 'hinhält' = holds in suspense (also 'holds off/strings along').","'Blendwerk' = delusion; 'Probierstein' = touchstone; 'Demarcation' kept as 'demarcation'.","'Gebrauch' = use; 'Mißbrauch' = misuse."]],"krv-a-0498":["Logical illusion, which consists in the mere imitation of the form of reason (the illusion of fallacious inferences), arises solely from a lack of attentiveness to the logical rule. As soon, therefore, as this is sharpened for the case at hand, it vanishes entirely. Transcendental illusion, by contrast, nevertheless does not cease, although one has already uncovered it and has distinctly seen its nullity through transcendental critique. (E.g., the illusion in the proposition: the world must have a beginning in time.) The cause of this is this: that in our reason (considered subjectively as a human faculty of cognition) there lie fundamental rules and maxims of its use, which have entirely the look of objective principles and through which it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our concepts, in favor of the understanding, is held to be an objective necessity, the determination of things in themselves. An illusion that is not to be avoided at all, as little as we can avoid the sea's seeming to us higher in the middle than at the shore, because we see the former through higher rays of light than the latter, or, still more, as little as even the astronomer can prevent the moon from seeming larger to him at its rising, although he is not deceived by this illusion.",["Schein = illusion; Trugschlüsse = fallacious inferences (Schluß = inference); Vernunftform = form of reason; Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition.","Verknüpfung = connection; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Dinge an sich selbst = things in themselves; Bestimmung = determination.","'Grundregeln' = fundamental rules; 'Grundsätze' = principles.","'zu Gunsten des Verstandes' = 'in favor of the understanding' (ambiguous as to whether the favor is to the understanding's benefit); kept literal.","'vor eine obiective Nothwendigkeit' = 'for' (für) an objective necessity; rendered 'held to be'.","'wie an dem Ufer' = 'than at the shore' (wie for als, as in the German)."]],"krv-a-0499":["Transcendental dialectic will therefore be content to uncover the illusion of transcendent judgments, and at the same time to prevent it from deceiving; but that it should also (like logical illusion) even vanish and cease to be an illusion, that it can never bring about. For we have to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which itself rests on subjective principles, and foists them upon us as objective ones, whereas logical dialectic, in the resolution of fallacious inferences, has to do only with a mistake in the following of the principles, or with an artificial illusion in the imitation of them. There is, therefore, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason, not one in which some bungler entangles himself through a lack of acquaintance, or one that some sophist has artificially devised in order to confuse rational people, but one that adheres inextricably to human reason, and even after we have uncovered its mirage will nevertheless not cease to delude it and incessantly to push it into momentary aberrations, which always need to be removed.",["Schein = illusion; Illusion is also rendered 'illusion' (\"natürlichen und unvermeidlichen Illusion\"): one English word for two German words (Schein, Latinate Illusion).","Trugschlüsse = 'fallacious inferences' (Schluß = inference); Grundsätze = 'principles' (Grundsatz); Urtheile = 'judgments'.","'sie als obiective unterschiebt': 'sie' (plural) is left as 'them', as in the German; 'foists them upon us' supplies 'upon us' as English requires an object; the German has none.","Kentnissen = 'acquaintance' (Kenntnis); the glossary also allows 'information' or 'knowledge'.","'Blendwerk' = 'mirage'; 'vorzugaukeln' = 'delude' (literally to hold out false appearances to); 'unhintertreiblich' = 'inextricably' (literally 'what cannot be driven back/away').","'ihr' in 'ihr Blendwerk', 'ihr vorzugaukeln' refers grammatically to the dialectic (Dialectik, fem.) and to Vernunft (fem.); 'sie' after it to Vernunft; the English 'its' and 'it' preserve the ambiguity only partly."]],"krv-a-0500":["II. On pure reason as the seat of transcendental illusion.",["Schein = illusion; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-a-0501":["A. On reason in general.",[]],"krv-a-0502":["All our cognition begins from the senses, goes from there to the understanding and ends with reason, above which nothing higher is encountered in us to work up the material of intuition and bring it under the highest unity of thinking. Since I am now supposed to give an explanation of this supreme faculty of cognition, I find myself in some embarrassment. There is of it, as of the understanding, a merely formal, i.e. logical use, since reason abstracts from all content of cognition, but also a real one, since it itself contains the origin of certain concepts and principles, which it borrows neither from the senses nor from the understanding. The former faculty has now indeed long since been explained by the logicians as the faculty of inferring mediately (in distinction from immediate inferences, *consequentiis immediatis*), but the second, which itself generates concepts, is thereby not yet understood. Since now a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty occurs here, a higher concept of this source of cognition must be sought, which comprehends both concepts under itself, while according to the analogy with the concepts of the understanding we can expect: that the logical concept will at the same time give the key to the transcendental one, and the table of the functions of the former will at the same time give the genealogical ladder of the concepts of reason.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Erkentnißkraft = 'faculty of cognition'; Vermögen = faculty; Stoff = material; Anschauung = intuition.","'befaßt' rendered 'comprehends' (befassen), not the glossary's begreifen = comprehend; distinguish the two.","Schluß/schliessen = inference/infer; Begriffe = concepts; Grundsätze = principles; Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'.","'eingesehen' = 'understood' (einsehen), not verstehen; both rendered 'understood' here.","'Stammleiter' = 'genealogical ladder' (literally ladder of descent); 'an die Hand geben' = 'give'.","'mittelbar zu schliessen' preserved as 'of inferring mediately'; *consequentiis immediatis* is Kant's Latin."]],"krv-a-0503":["We explained, in the first part of our transcendental logic, the understanding through the faculty of rules; here we distinguish reason from it by wanting to call it the faculty of principles.",["Principien = principles; 'Regeln' = rules; 'erkläreten' = 'explained' (Erklärung = explanation; definition).","'von demselben' = 'from it', referring to the understanding."]],"krv-a-0504":["The expression of a principle is ambiguous and commonly signifies only a cognition that can be used as a principle, although in itself and according to its own origin it is no *principium*. Every universal proposition, even if it may be taken from experience (through induction), can serve as the major premise in a syllogism; but it is on that account not itself a *principium*. The mathematical axioms (e.g. between two points there can be only one straight line) are even universal cognitions a priori, and are therefore rightly called principles, relatively to the cases that can be subsumed under them. But I cannot on that account say: that I cognize this property of straight lines, in general and in itself, from principles, but only in pure intuition.",["Ausdruck = expression; bedeutet = signifies; Satz = proposition; Obersatz = major premise; Vernunftschluß = syllogism.","Erkentnisse = cognitions; erkenne = cognize; Erfahrung = experience; Anschauung = intuition; rein = pure.","'zweydeutig' = 'ambiguous'; 'relativisch' = 'relatively'.","Principien and *Principium* kept distinct: the German Principien is 'principles', the Latin *principium* kept in italics.","'a priori' left unitalicized, as in the German roman type is not distinguished; 'gemeiniglich' = 'commonly'."]],"krv-a-0505":["I would therefore call cognition from principles that in which I cognize the particular in the universal through concepts. Thus every syllogism is a form of the derivation of a cognition from a principle. For the major premise always gives a concept, which makes it the case that everything that is subsumed under the condition of it is cognized from it according to a principle. Since now every universal cognition can serve as the major premise in a syllogism, and the understanding offers such universal propositions a priori, these can then also, in respect of their possible use, be called principles.",["'dieienige nennen, da ich ...' = 'that ... in which I'; 'da' here is relative/locative (old usage); the German is literal and somewhat awkward.","besondre = particular; Bedingung = condition; Gebrauch = use; möglich = possible.","'unter der Bedingung desselben': 'desselben' refers to the concept (Begriff, masc.) or perhaps the major premise (Obersatz, masc.); 'of it' kept ambiguous.","'aus ihm' = 'from it', same ambiguity."]],"krv-a-0506":["But if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in themselves according to their origin, they are anything but cognitions from concepts. For they would not even be possible a priori if we did not draw in pure intuition (in mathematics) or conditions of a possible experience in general. That everything that happens has a cause cannot at all be concluded from the concept of that which happens in general; rather the principle shows how one could first of all get a determinate concept of experience of that which happens.",["Grundsätze = principles (Grundsatz); here Grundsatz is also 'principle' where Principien elsewhere; one English word for two German words (Grundsatz, Principium/Princip).","Erkentnisse aus Begriffen = 'cognitions from concepts'; 'herbey zögen' = 'draw in'; geschlossen = 'concluded' (schließen; folgern is also 'conclude').","'Erfahrungsbegriff' = 'concept of experience'; bestimt = determinate; the doubtful reading 'bestimten' is OCR-clean."]],"krv-a-0507":["Synthetic cognitions from concepts, therefore, the understanding cannot provide at all, and it is these that I properly call principles absolutely: while all universal propositions in general can be called comparative principles.",["Synthetische = synthetic; 'verschaffen' = 'provide'; 'schlechthin' = 'absolutely'; 'comparative' = comparative (comparativ); 'indessen, daß' = 'while'."]],"krv-a-0508":["It is an old wish, which, who knows how late, will perhaps someday be fulfilled: that one might for once, instead of the endless manifoldness of civil laws, seek out their principles; for in that alone can the secret consist of simplifying legislation, as one says. But the laws here are also only limitations of our freedom to conditions under which it thoroughly agrees with itself, hence they go to something that is entirely our own work, and of which we ourselves can be the cause through those concepts. But how objects in themselves, how the nature of things should stand under principles and be determined according to mere concepts, is, if not something impossible, at least very absurd in its demand. But however this may be (for we still have the investigation of it ahead of us), it at least becomes evident from this: that cognition from principles (in itself) is something quite different from mere cognition of the understanding, which, though it can also precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, in itself (so far as it is synthetic) does not rest on mere thinking, nor contain a universal according to concepts.",["Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (cf. Mannigfaltige = manifold); Gesetze = laws; Einschränkungen = limitations (Schranke = limitation).","Gegenstände an sich selbst = 'objects in themselves'; an sich = in itself; Dinge = things; bestimt = determined.","'sehr widersinnisches' = 'very absurd' (widersinnig); 'wo nicht ... wenigstens doch' = 'if not ... at least'.","'in seiner Forderung': 'seiner' (neuter) refers to the whole clause; 'its' kept.","'sie' in 'unter denen sie durchgängig mit sich selbst zusammenstimt' = 'it' (Freyheit, fem. sing.); 'iene Begriffe' = 'those concepts'.","'vorgehen' = 'precede' (could also be 'proceed'); 'Allgemeines' = 'a universal'."]],"krv-a-0509":["The understanding may be a faculty of the unity of appearances by means of rules, so reason is the faculty of the unity of the rules of the understanding under principles. It therefore never goes first to experience, or to any object, but to the understanding, in order to give a priori through concepts unity to its manifold cognitions, which may be called unity of reason, and is of a quite different kind from that which can be accomplished by the understanding.",["'Der Verstand mag ... seyn, so ist die Vernunft' kept as concessive 'may be, so is'; Erscheinungen = appearances; vermittelst = by means of.","Art = kind; Einheit = unity; Vernunfteinheit = 'unity of reason'.","'als sie von dem Verstande geleistet werden kan': 'sie' refers back to Einheit; 'from that which' supplies a relative; the English necessarily alters the construction.","'desselben' = 'its', referring to the understanding (Verstand)."]],"krv-a-0510":["That is the general concept of the faculty of reason, so far as it, with a total lack of examples (which are first to be given in the sequel), could be made comprehensible.",["Vernunftvermögen = 'faculty of reason'; Beyspielen = examples; Folge = sequel (rendered 'in the sequel').","'begreiflich' = 'comprehensible' (begreifen = comprehend); 'er' refers to Begriff (concept), masc."]],"krv-a-0511":["B. On the logical use of reason.",["Gebrauch = use."]],"krv-a-0512":["One makes a distinction between what is immediately cognized and what is only inferred. That in a figure bounded by three straight lines there are three angles is immediately cognized, but that these angles together are equal to two right angles is only inferred. Because we constantly need inferring and thereby finally become quite accustomed to it, we in the end no longer notice this distinction, and often, as with the so-called deception of the senses, take for immediately perceived something that we have nevertheless only inferred. In every inference there is a proposition that lies at the ground, another, namely the consequence that is drawn from the former, and finally the inferential sequence (consequence), according to which the truth of the latter is unfailingly connected with the truth of the former. If the inferred judgment already lies in the first in such a way that it can be derived from it without the mediation of a third representation, then the inference is called immediate (*consequentia immediata*); I would rather call it an inference of the understanding. But if, besides the cognition laid at the ground, yet another judgment is necessary in order to bring about the sequel, then the inference is called a syllogism. In the proposition: all human beings are mortal, there already lie the propositions: some human beings are mortal, or: some mortals are human beings, or: nothing that is immortal is a human being, and these are therefore immediate consequences from the former. By contrast, the proposition: all scholars are mortal, does not lie in the judgment laid beneath (for the concept of scholars does not occur in it at all) and it can be concluded from this only by means of an intermediate judgment.",["'erkant' = erkannt: *erkennen* = cognize; 'Erkentniß' = cognition. 'wahrgenommen' = perceived (*Wahrnehmung* = perception).","'Schluß' = inference; 'Vernunftschluß' = syllogism (glossary); 'Verstandesschluß' = inference of the understanding.","'Folgerung' = consequence; 'Schlußfolge (Consequenz)' = inferential sequence (consequence): 'Folge' = sequel/sequence, so 'consequence' renders both Folgerung and Consequenz; 'gefolgert' = concluded (*folgern*), while 'geschlossen' = inferred.","'Betruge der Sinne' rendered 'deception of the senses'; 'Satz' = proposition; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Vorstellung' = representation.","OCR/typographical: 'die: se' read as 'diese' (line-break hyphenation); 'untergelegten' rendered 'laid beneath'; 'Ersteren/ienem' = the former."]],"krv-a-0513":["In every syllogism I first think a rule (*maior*) through the understanding. Second, I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (*minor*) by means of the power of judgment. Finally I determine my cognition through the predicate of the rule (*conclusio*), hence *a priori* through reason. The relation, therefore, that the major premise, as the rule, represents between a cognition and its condition, constitutes the different ways of syllogisms. They are therefore precisely threefold, just like all judgments in general, insofar as they differ in the way they express the relation of the cognition in the understanding, namely: categorical or hypothetical or disjunctive syllogisms.",["'Art' = way (glossary); 'die verschiedene Arten' kept as 'the different ways'.","'Obersatz' = major premise; 'Urtheilskraft' = power of judgment; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'Vernunftschlüsse' = syllogisms.","'mithin a priori durch die Vernunft': 'a priori' may attach to 'determine' or to 'through reason'; left as in the German.","'im Verstande' follows 'des Erkentnisses': may attach to 'express' or to 'the relation of the cognition'; kept ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0514":["If, as mostly happens, the conclusion has been set as a judgment, in order to see whether it does not flow from judgments already given, through which, namely, an entirely different object is thought: then I seek out in the understanding the assertion of this conclusion, to see whether it is not found in it under certain conditions according to a general rule. If I now find such a condition and the object of the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then this is concluded from the rule, which is valid also for other objects of cognition. One sees from this: that reason, in inferring, seeks to bring the great manifold of the understanding's cognition to the smallest number of principles (general conditions) and thereby to effect the highest unity of it.",["'Gegendstand' = Gegenstand (obvious slip); 'Obiect' = Object: both rendered 'object' (Gegenstand / Object).","'Schlußsatz' rendered 'conclusion', as is 'Conclusion'; 'Assertion' = assertion (*behaupten* family); 'gefolgert' = concluded; 'schließen' = infer.","'Mannigfaltigkeit' rendered 'manifold' as a noun; 'Zahl' = number; 'Principien' = principles; 'gilt' = is valid (*gültig*).","'vor andere' = für andere ('for'); 'aufgegeben' rendered 'set' (cf. *Aufgabe* = problem)."]],"krv-a-0515":["C. On the pure use of reason.",["'Gebrauch' = use; 'rein' = pure."]],"krv-a-0516":["Can reason be isolated, and is it then still a proper source of concepts and judgments that arise solely from it, and through which it relates to objects, or is it a merely subaltern faculty, for giving to given cognitions a certain form, which is called logical, and whereby the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated only to one another and lower rules to others higher (whose condition comprises the condition of the former in its sphere), so far as this can be brought about by the comparison of them? This is the question with which we are now occupied only provisionally. In fact, manifoldness of rules and unity of principles is a demand of reason, in order to bring the understanding into thoroughgoing interconnection with itself, just as the understanding brings the manifold of intuition under concepts and thereby brings the former into connection. But such a principle prescribes no law to the objects, and does not contain the ground of the possibility of cognizing and determining them as such in general, but is merely a subjective law of the economy of the stock of our understanding, by the comparison of its concepts to bring their general use to the smallest possible number of them, without one being thereby entitled to demand from the objects themselves such a uniformity as would give furtherance to the convenience and extension of our understanding, and at the same time to give objective validity to that maxim. In a word, the question is: whether reason in itself, i.e., pure reason, contains synthetic principles and rules *a priori*, and what these principles may consist in?",["'Obiecten/Gegenstände' both = 'objects' (Object / Gegenstand); 'Grundsatz' and 'Principien' both = 'principle' (Grundsatz, Principium).","'Mannigfaltigkeit' = 'manifoldness'; 'das Mannigfaltige' = 'the manifold'; 'Zusammenhang' = interconnection; 'Verknüpfung' = connection.","'Ausbreitung' rendered 'extension' (cf. *Ausdehnung*, also 'extension'); 'Gemächlichkeit' = convenience; 'Haushaltung' = economy; 'Vorrathe' = stock.","'Sphäre' = sphere; 'Gültigkeit' = validity (*gültig*); 'an sich' = in itself.","The syntax of 'wodurch ... untergeordnet werden' and of 'dadurch sie sich auf Gegenstände bezieht' is kept as the German has it (the latter is irregular: 'and through which it relates'); 'iene' (the former) and 'derselben' have ambiguous antecedents, left unresolved.","'Vorschub thue' rendered 'give furtherance'."]],"krv-a-0517":["The formal and logical procedure of the latter in syllogisms already gives us sufficient guidance as to the ground on which the transcendental principle of it in synthetic cognition through pure reason will rest.",["'derselben' (twice) rendered 'the latter' / 'it'; the antecedent is reason (Vernunft).","'Principium' rendered 'principle'; 'Grund' = ground; 'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'transscendental' = transcendental."]],"krv-a-0518":["First, the syllogism does not go to intuitions, in order to bring them under rules (as the understanding does with its categories), but to concepts and judgments. If pure reason therefore also goes to objects, it still has no immediate relation to them and to their intuition, but only to the understanding and its judgments, which turn first to the senses and their intuition in order to determine their object. Unity of reason is therefore not unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this as unity of the understanding. That everything that happens has a cause is not at all a principle cognized and prescribed through reason. It makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without this relation to possible experience, from mere concepts, could not have commanded any such synthetic unity.",["'Vernunftschluß' = syllogism; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Beziehung' = relation; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Einheit' = unity.","'von dieser als der Verstandeseinheit': 'as the unity of the understanding' may attach to 'this' (possible experience) or to 'unity of reason' contrasted with it; kept as the German.","'erkanter' = erkannter (*erkennen* = cognize); 'Grundsatz' = principle; 'geschieht' = happens (cf. *Begebenheit*, *Ereignis* not used).","'sich an die Sinne ... wenden': 'Sinne' = senses; 'ihren Gegenstand' - 'their' may refer to the senses or to the judgments; left ambiguous."]],"krv-a-0519":["Second, reason in its logical use seeks the general condition of its judgment (the conclusion), and the syllogism is itself nothing other than a judgment by means of the subsumption of its condition under a general rule (major premise). Since this rule is now in turn exposed to the same attempt of reason, and the condition of the condition (by means of a prosyllogism) must thereby be sought as long as it is feasible, one sees well that the peculiar principle of reason in general (in the logical use) is: to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned, with which the unity of it is completed.",["'Schlußsatz' = conclusion; 'Obersatz' = major premise; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'bedingt' = conditioned; 'Unbedingte' = the unconditioned.","'eigenthümlich' rendered 'peculiar'; 'Prosyllogismus' kept as 'prosyllogism'.","'womit die Einheit desselben vollendet wird': 'desselben' may refer to the cognition or to the understanding; left ambiguous.","'so lange es angeht' rendered 'as long as it is feasible'."]],"krv-a-0520":["This logical maxim, however, can become a principle of pure reason in no other way than by assuming: if the conditioned is given, then the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another, which is consequently itself unconditioned, is also given (i.e., contained in the object and its connection).",["'Principium' = principle; 'Reihe' = series; 'Verknüpfung' = connection.","'die mithin selbst unbedingt ist': the relative 'die' may refer to the series or to the conditions; kept ambiguous.","'untergeordneten' rendered 'subordinated'."]],"krv-a-0521":["Such a principle of pure reason, however, is obviously synthetic; for the conditioned is related analytically to some condition, to be sure, but not to the unconditioned. There must also arise from it various synthetic propositions, of which the pure understanding knows nothing, as it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, whose cognition and synthesis is always conditioned. But the unconditioned, if it actually takes place, can be considered in particular, according to all the determinations that distinguish it from everything conditioned, and must thereby give material for many synthetic propositions *a priori*.",["'Bezieht' capitalized in the German; read as ordinary verb 'bezieht sich'. 'Sätze' = propositions; 'weiß' = knows (*Wissen*).","'wirklich' rendered 'actually' (Wirklichkeit = actuality); 'statt hat' = 'takes place'; 'besonders' rendered 'in particular'.","'Bestimmungen' = determinations; 'Stoff' = material; 'erwogen' rendered 'considered'.","'Erkentniß und Synthesis ... bedingt': singular verb kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0522":["The principles arising from this supreme principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in regard to all appearances, i.e., no empirical use adequate to it can ever be made of it. It will thus be entirely distinguished from all principles of the understanding (whose use is wholly immanent, since they have only the possibility of experience as their theme). Now whether that principle: that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of appearances, or also of the thinking of things in general) extends to the unconditioned, has its objective correctness or not, what consequences flow from it to the empirical use of the understanding, or whether instead there is no such objectively valid proposition of reason at all, but only a merely logical prescription, in ascending to ever higher conditions, to approach their completeness and thereby to bring the highest unity of reason possible for us into our cognition, whether, I say, this need of reason has been taken, through a misunderstanding, for a transcendental principle of pure reason, which hastily postulates such an unlimited completeness from the series of conditions in the objects themselves, but what misinterpretations and delusions may, even in this case, creep into the syllogisms whose major premise has been taken from pure reason (and which is perhaps more petition than postulate) and which ascend from experience upward to its conditions: that will be our business in the transcendental dialectic, which we now want to develop from its sources, which are deeply hidden in human reason. We will divide it into two chapters, of which the first is to deal with the transcendent concepts of pure reason, the second with transcendent and dialectical syllogisms of the same.",["Grundsatz = principle (Grundsätze, Princip); Satz in Vernunftsatz = proposition; Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms; Obersatz = major premise; Hauptstück = chapter.","'Er' (Princip, masc.) is rendered 'it'; 'von demselben' and 'derselben' are left with the same unspecified reference as in the German.","The long sentence 'Ob nun ... seyn' is left unbroken and its anacoluthic construction ('ob, sage ich ... was aber auch ...') is kept; 'adäquater', 'obiective', 'Folgerungen' = adequate, objective, consequences.","'Gegenständen' = objects; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Verblendungen' = delusions; 'Mißdeutungen' = misinterpretations."]],"krv-a-0523":["Transcendental Dialectic, First Book. On the Concepts of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-0524":["Whatever the situation may be with the possibility of concepts from pure reason: they are nevertheless not merely reflected but inferred concepts. Concepts of the understanding are also thought a priori before experience and for the sake of it, but they contain nothing further than the unity of reflection on appearances, insofar as they are necessarily to belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone cognition and determination of an object becomes possible. They thus first give material for inferring, and no concepts a priori of objects precede them from which they could be inferred. On the other hand their objective reality is grounded solely on this: that, because they constitute the intellectual form of all experience, their application must be able at all times to be shown in experience.",["'Bewandniß' rendered 'situation'; 'geschlossene Begriffe' = inferred concepts (schließen = infer); 'Stoff' = material; 'Reflexion' = reflection.","'Erkentniß und Bestimmung ... wird' has a singular verb in the German; kept singular."]],"krv-a-0525":["The term for a concept of reason, however, already shows in advance that it does not want to let itself be restricted within experience, because it concerns a cognition of which every empirical one is only a part (perhaps the whole of possible experience or of its empirical synthesis), up to which, to be sure, no actual experience ever fully suffices, but is nevertheless always belonging to it. Concepts of reason serve for comprehending, as concepts of the understanding for understanding (perceptions). If they contain the unconditioned, they concern something under which all experience belongs, which itself, however, is never an object of experience: something to which reason leads in its inferences from experience and according to which it estimates and measures the degree of its empirical use, but which never constitutes a member of the empirical synthesis. If such concepts nevertheless have objective validity, they can be called *conceptus ratiocinati* (correctly inferred concepts); if not, they are at least surreptitiously obtained through an illusion of inferring and may be called *conceptus ratiocinantes* (rationalizing concepts). But since this can first be settled only in the chapter on the dialectical syllogisms of pure reason, we cannot yet take account of it, but will provisionally, just as we named the pure concepts of the understanding categories, give the concepts of pure reason a new name and call them transcendental ideas, but now clarify and justify this term.",["'nichtig geschlossene Begriffe' in the source is evidently an OCR slip for 'richtig geschlossene'; silently corrected.","Benennung = term (first and last occurrences); Namen = name. Begreiffen = comprehending; verstehen = understanding; Wahrnehmung = perception; Schein = illusion; vernünftelnde = rationalizing; erläutern = clarify.","Ambiguity kept in 'bis dahin zwar keine wirkliche Erfahrung ... zureicht, aber doch iederzeit dazu gehörig ist': the subject of 'gehörig ist' is not stated (the cognition or experience); 'dahin' also unspecified.","'Schlüssen' (inferences) and 'Schliessen' (inferring) vs. 'Vernunftschlüsse' (syllogisms)."]],"krv-a-0526":["First Section of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. On Ideas in General.",["Abschnitt = section."]],"krv-a-0527":["With the great wealth of our languages the thinking head often nevertheless finds itself at a loss for the expression that fits its concept exactly, and in the want of which it can become properly understandable neither to others nor even to itself. To coin new words is a presumption to legislation in languages that seldom succeeds, and, before one proceeds to this desperate means, it is advisable to look around in a dead and learned language to see whether this concept together with its appropriate expression is not to be found there, and even if the old use of it has become somewhat wavering through the carelessness of its authors, it is nevertheless better to fix the meaning that was preeminently proper to it (even if it should remain doubtful whether one had at that time in mind exactly the very same one) than to spoil one's business merely by making oneself unintelligible.",["Ausdruck = expression; Begriff = concept; Mittel = means; Gebrauch = use; Bedeutung = meaning.","'im Sinne gehabt' rendered 'had in mind' (idiom; Sinn otherwise = sense); word order adjusted slightly for English.","'ihrer Urheber' has no clear antecedent for 'ihrer' (feminine singular/plural); rendered 'its authors' as the closest English, the ambiguity of reference remaining."]],"krv-a-0528":["For that reason, if for a certain concept only a single word should be found, which in an already introduced meaning fits this concept exactly, and whose distinction from other related concepts is of great importance, it is advisable not to deal with it wastefully, or to use it merely for variety, synonymously in place of others, but to reserve carefully for it its proper meaning; because otherwise it easily happens that, once the expression does not particularly occupy attention but is lost among the heap of others of very divergent meaning, the thought too is lost, which it alone could have preserved.",["'aufbehalten' / 'aufzubehalten' rendered 'preserved' / 'reserve' respectively.","Ausdruck = expression; Bedeutung = meaning; 'synonimisch' = synonymously."]],"krv-a-0529":["Plato availed himself of the expression idea in such a way that one readily sees he understood by it something that is not only never borrowed from the senses, but that even far surpasses the concepts of the understanding, with which Aristotle occupied himself, since in experience nothing is ever encountered congruent with it. Ideas are with him archetypes of the things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his opinion they flowed forth from the highest reason, from where they became the share of human reason, which, however, no longer finds itself in its original state, but must with effort recall the old, now very obscured ideas through recollection (which is called philosophy). I do not want to engage here in any literary investigation in order to settle the sense that the sublime philosopher connected with his expression. I only remark: that it is nothing at all unusual, in ordinary conversation as well as in writings, through the comparison of the thoughts that an author expresses about his object, to understand him even better than he understood himself, in that he did not determine his concept sufficiently, and thereby sometimes spoke, or even thought, contrary to his own intention.",["Sinne (Sinnen) = senses; Sinn = sense; Meinung = opinion; Absicht = intention; Erinnerung = recollection; Gegenstand = object; Urbilder = archetypes.","'Philosophie' is not Weltweisheit here; rendered 'philosophy'.","'Plato ... darunter etwas verstanden': 'verstehen' = understand; 'Verstandes' = of the understanding.","'mit Congruirendes' kept as 'congruent with it'; 'ihn' in 'ihn ... besser zu verstehen' refers back to 'Verfasser' (author)."]],"krv-a-0530":["Plato noticed very well that our power of cognition feels a far higher need than merely to spell out appearances according to synthetic unity, in order to be able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally soars to cognitions that go much further than that any object that experience can give could ever be congruent with them, but which nevertheless have their reality and are by no means mere figments of the brain.",["Erkentnißkraft = power of cognition (Kraft = power); Erkentnissen = cognitions; Realität = reality; 'Hirngespinste' = figments of the brain.","'synthetischer Einheit' = synthetic unity; 'buchstabiren' = spell out."]],"krv-a-0531":["Plato found his ideas preeminently in everything that is practical,*) i.e., that rests on freedom, which for its part stands under cognitions that are a peculiar product of reason. Whoever wanted to draw the concepts of virtue from experience, whoever wanted to make into a model, as a source of cognition, that which can at most serve as an example for imperfect clarification (as many have actually done), would make of virtue an ambiguous non-thing, changeable according to time and circumstances, usable for no rule. On the contrary, everyone becomes aware that when someone is presented to him as a model of virtue, he nevertheless always has the true original merely in his own head, with which he compares this alleged model, and merely according to which he estimates it. This, however, is the idea of virtue, with regard to which all possible objects of experience do service, to be sure, as examples (proofs of the practicability, in a certain degree, of that which the concept of reason demands), but not as archetypes. That a human being will never act adequately to that which the pure idea of virtue contains proves not at all something chimerical in this thought. For all judgment about moral worth or lack of worth is nevertheless possible only by means of this idea; consequently it necessarily lies at the ground of every approach to moral perfection, however far the obstacles in human nature, not to be determined as to their degree, may keep us distant from it.",["The asterisk *) marks the footnote translated under krv-a-0532.","Erkentnisse/Erkentnißquell: cognitions / source of cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition). Erläuterung: 'clarification' (glossary also allows 'elucidation').","Unding: rendered 'non-thing' (Ding = thing). Urbilder: 'archetypes'; Muster: 'model'; Beyspiel: 'example'.","'Thunlichkeit': 'practicability'. 'Chimerisches': 'chimerical'. 'Unwerth': rendered 'lack of worth' (literally 'unworth').","'inne werden': 'become aware'; 'schätzen': 'estimate'. 'Grade nach nicht zu bestimmende': the participial phrase is kept attached to 'Hindernisse' as in the German."]],"krv-a-0532":["*) He extended his concept, to be sure, also to speculative cognitions, if they were only given purely and entirely a priori, even beyond mathematics, although this has its object nowhere else than in possible experience. In this I cannot follow him, any more than in the mystical deduction of these ideas, or the exaggerations by which he as it were hypostatized them; although the lofty language of which he availed himself in this field is quite capable of a milder interpretation, adapted to the nature of things.",["'so gar über die Mathematik': 'even beyond mathematics' (über could also be 'over/about'; the English 'beyond' fixes one reading).","'Auslegung': 'interpretation'. 'hypostasirte': 'hypostatized'."]],"krv-a-0533":["The Platonic republic has become a proverb as a supposedly striking example of dreamed-up perfection, which can have its seat only in the brain of the idle thinker, and Brucker finds it ridiculous that the philosopher asserted that a prince would never govern well if he were not partaker of the ideas. But one would do better to pursue this thought further and (where the excellent man leaves us without help) to put it in light through new efforts, than to set it aside as useless, under the very wretched and harmful pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest human freedom according to laws that make it so that each one's freedom can coexist with that of the others (not of the greatest happiness, for this will already follow of itself) is at least a necessary idea, which one must lay at the ground not merely in the first draft of a constitution of a state, but also in all laws, and in which one must at the outset abstract from the present obstacles, which perhaps may arise not so much unavoidably from human nature as rather from the neglect of the genuine ideas in legislation. For nothing more harmful and more unworthy of a philosopher can be found than the vulgar appeal to supposedly conflicting experience, which would not exist at all if those arrangements had been made at the right time according to the ideas, and if in their place crude concepts, just because they were drawn from experience, had not frustrated every good aim. The more legislation and government were established in agreement with this idea, the rarer punishments would indeed become, and there it is quite reasonable (as Plato asserts) that with a perfect arrangement of these none of the kind would be necessary. Although the latter may never come to pass, the idea is nevertheless quite correct that sets up this maximum as archetype, in order, according to it, to bring the lawful constitution of human beings ever nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For what the highest degree may be at which humanity must stop, and how great therefore the chasm may be that necessarily remains between the idea and its execution, that no one can and should determine, just because it is freedom that can overstep every boundary that is specified.",["'Brucker' is the name of Johann Jakob Brucker, left as in the text.","'der Ideen theilhaftig': 'partaker of the ideas'. 'Anstalten': 'arrangements'; 'Anordnung': 'arrangement'.","'Verfassung' / 'Staatsverfassung': 'constitution' / 'constitution of a state'; 'gesetzliche Verfassung': 'lawful constitution'.","'dieser Maximum': the German gender (feminine/neuter demonstrative) is not that of the Latin noun; rendered 'this maximum'. 'Absicht': 'aim'.","'Gränze': 'boundary'. 'Gesetzgebung': 'legislation'. 'zum Grunde legen': 'lay at the ground'."]],"krv-a-0534":["But not merely in that in which human reason shows genuine causality and where ideas become efficient causes (of actions and their objects), namely in the moral, but also with regard to nature itself, Plato rightly sees distinct proofs of its origin from ideas. A plant, an animal, the regular arrangement of the structure of the world (presumably therefore also the whole natural order) show distinctly that they are possible only according to ideas, that no single creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, indeed congrues with the idea of the most perfect of its species (as little as the human being with the idea of humanity, which he even bears in his soul as the archetype of his actions), that nevertheless those ideas in the highest understanding are individual, unchangeable, thoroughly determined, and the original causes of things, and only the whole of their combination in the universe is solely and alone fully adequate to that idea. If one separates off what is exaggerated in the expression, then the flight of spirit of the philosopher, to ascend from the copying consideration of the physical aspect of the world-order to the architectonic connection of it according to ends, i.e., according to ideas, is an effort that deserves respect and emulation, but with regard to that which concerns the principles of morality, of legislation and of religion, where the ideas first make experience itself (of the good) possible, although they can never be fully expressed in it, it is a quite peculiar merit, which is failed to be recognized only because it is assessed through the very empirical rules whose validity, as principles, was supposed to be abolished precisely by them. For in consideration of nature experience hands us the rule and is the source of truth; with regard to moral laws, however, experience is (alas!) the mother of illusion, and it is highly reprehensible to take the laws about what I ought to do from that which is done, or to want to limit them by it.",["'congruire': Latinate verb of the original, rendered 'congrues'. 'copeylichen': 'copying' (a derivative of Copey, 'copy'); the English adjective is a literal choice, the form is unusual.","'im höchsten Verstande': 'in the highest understanding' (Verstand = understanding); could also be read as 'in the highest sense' — the ambiguity is preserved by the literal rendering.","'Sittlichen' / 'Sittlichkeit': 'the moral' / 'morality'. 'Caussalität': 'causality'. 'wirkende Ursachen': 'efficient causes'.","'Geistesschwung': 'flight of spirit' (Geist = spirit/mind). 'architectonischen': 'architectonic'.","'welches man nur darum nicht erkent': 'which is failed to be recognized' keeps the impersonal 'man' construction awkwardly; 'welches' has 'Verdienst' as antecedent. 'Beurtheilen': 'assess'.","'Verbindung' (combination) and 'Verknüpfung' (connection) are kept distinct. 'durch sie': 'by them' refers to the ideas; the German pronoun is ambiguous in the same way.","'Schein': 'illusion'."]],"krv-a-0535":["In place of all these considerations, whose proper execution in fact constitutes the distinctive dignity of philosophy, we now occupy ourselves with a labor not so brilliant, but yet also not without merit, namely: to make level and firm for building the ground for those majestic moral edifices, in which all sorts of mole-tunnels of a reason digging in vain, but with good confidence, for treasures are found, and which make that structure unsafe. The transcendental use of pure reason, its principles and ideas, are thus what it now behooves us to know precisely, in order to be able to determine and estimate properly the influence of pure reason and the worth of it. Yet before I lay aside this preliminary introduction, I request those to whom philosophy lies at heart, (which is saying more than one commonly encounters) if they should find themselves convinced by this and what follows, to take under protection the expression “idea” according to its original meaning, so that it not henceforth get among the remaining expressions with which all sorts of kinds of representation are usually designated in careless disorder, and science suffer loss thereby. We do not, after all, lack terms that are properly suited to each kind of representation, without our needing to encroach upon the property of another. Here is a graduated ladder of them. The genus is representation in general, (*repraesentatio*). Under it stands representation with consciousness (*perceptio*). A perception that relates merely to the subject as the modification of its state is sensation, (*sensatio*) an objective perception is cognition (*cognitio*). This is either intuition or concept (*intuitus vel conceptus*). The former relates immediately to the object and is singular, the latter mediately, by means of a mark that can be common to several things. The concept is either an empirical or a pure concept, and the pure concept, insofar as it has its origin merely in the understanding (not in the pure image of sensibility) is called *Notio*. A concept from notions, which goes beyond the possibility of experience, is the idea, or the concept of reason. To someone who has once become accustomed to this distinction, it must be unbearable to hear the representation of the red color called idea. It cannot even be called notion (concept of the understanding).",["Vorstellung = representation throughout; Vorstellungsart = “kind of representation” (Art = kind); Perception (German, from Latin) = “perception,” with Kant's *perceptio* beside it.","Gebäuden = “edifices”; Bauwerk = “structure”; sittlich = “moral”; Maulwurfsgänge = “mole-tunnels.”","Quotation marks around “idea” added because the word is mentioned, not used (method principle 6); the original has none.","Latin terms in the original are in roman type and are rendered in italics; “Notio” keeps the capital of the source, “notions” renders Notionen.","Stufenleiter = “graduated ladder”; Ausdruck = “expression”; Benennung = “term”; Bedeutung = “meaning.”","“Gegenstand” = “object” and “Subiect” = “subject” (Kant's spelling modernized)."]],"krv-a-0536":["Second Section of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. On the transcendental ideas.",[]],"krv-a-0537":["The transcendental analytic gave us an example of how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure concepts a priori, which represent objects prior to all experience, or rather indicate the synthetic unity which alone makes possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of judgments (transformed into a concept of the synthesis of intuitions) brought forth categories, which guide all use of the understanding in experience. Just so we can expect: that the form of inferences of reason, if one applies it to the synthetic unity of intuitions, following the guidance of the categories, will contain the origin of particular concepts a priori, which we can call pure concepts of reason, or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the whole of the entire experience according to principles.",["Vernunftschluß = “inference of reason” (glossary alternative “syllogism” not used), kept uniform across the passages; Schluß = “inference.”","Ambiguity kept: “a priori” may attach to “Begriffen” or to “enthalten”; “nach Principien” may attach to “bestimmen” or to “Verstandesgebrauch ... im Ganzen der gesamten Erfahrung.”","Besonder = “particular”; Maaßgebung = “guidance”; Principien = “principles” (Grundsatz would also be “principle”)."]],"krv-a-0538":["The function of reason in its inferences consisted in the universality of cognition according to concepts, and the inference of reason itself is a judgment that is determined a priori in the whole domain of its condition. The proposition: Caius is mortal, I could also draw merely through the understanding, from experience. But I seek a concept that contains the condition under which the predicate (assertion in general) of this judgment is given, (i.e. here the concept of man) and after I have subsumed under this condition, taken in its whole domain, (all men are mortal): so I determine accordingly the cognition of my object (Caius is mortal).",["OCR “darnach. die” silently corrected to “darnach die”; “bestimt” = bestimmt.","Umfang = “domain”; Allgemeinheit = “universality” (cf. Kant's *Universalitas* in the next passage); Mensch = “man.”","“Erkentniß” = “cognition”; “Gegenstand” = “object.”"]],"krv-a-0539":["Accordingly in the conclusion of an inference of reason we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after we have previously thought it in the major premise in its whole domain under a certain condition, this completed magnitude of the domain, in reference to such a condition, is called universality (*Universalitas*). To this corresponds in the synthesis of intuitions the totality (*Universitas*) or totality of the conditions. Thus the transcendental concept of reason is none other than that of the totality of the conditions to a given conditioned. Since now the unconditioned alone makes possible the totality of the conditions and, conversely, the totality of the conditions is always itself unconditioned: so a pure concept of reason in general can be explained through the concept of the unconditioned, insofar as it contains a ground of the synthesis of the conditioned.",["One English word for two German words: Allheit and Totalität are both “totality” (the Latin *Universitas* stands beside Allheit).","Beziehung = “reference” (so as not to collide with Verhältnis = “relation” in the next passage).","Original spelling “Vniversalitas/Vniversitas” given with modern U.","Obersatz = “major premise”; erklärt = “explained” (Erklärung could also be “definition”); Größe = “magnitude.”","“zu einem gegebenen bedingten”: the substantivized “bedingten” is rendered “conditioned,” as in the later “des Bedingten.”"]],"krv-a-0540":["However many kinds of relation there are that the understanding represents to itself by means of the categories, so many sorts of pure concepts of reason there will also be, and there will thus have to be sought first an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject, second of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series, third of the disjunctive synthesis of the parts in a system.",["Verhältnis = “relation”; Arten = “kinds,” vielerley = “sorts.”","The construction “ein Unbedingtes ... zu suchen seyn” is kept in the German order."]],"krv-a-0541":["For there are just as many kinds of inferences of reason, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned, the one to the subject, which is itself no longer predicate, the other to the presupposition, which presupposes nothing further, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the division, to which nothing further is required in order to complete the division of a concept. Hence the pure concepts of reason of the totality in the synthesis of conditions are, at least as problems, in order to continue the unity of the understanding, where possible, up to the unconditioned, necessary and grounded in the nature of human reason, even if these transcendental concepts otherwise lack a use in *concreto* appropriate to them and consequently have no other utility than to bring the understanding into the direction in which its use, while it is extended to the utmost, is at the same time made thoroughly consistent with itself.",["Aufgaben = “problems”; Gebrauch = “use”; Nutzen = “utility”; Eintheilung = “division”; Glieder = “members.”","“es mag auch übrigens” kept as a concessive clause (“even if ... otherwise”); “wo möglich” = “where possible.”","*in concreto* printed in roman in the source, hence italicized.","“Vernunftschlüsse” = “inferences of reason,” as above; “Prosyllogismen” = “prosyllogisms.”"]],"krv-a-0542":["But while we here speak of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned, as the common title of all concepts of reason, we again come upon an expression that we cannot do without and nevertheless, given an ambiguity attaching to it through long misuse, cannot use safely. The word absolute is one of the few words that in their original meaning were fitted to a concept to which afterwards absolutely no other word of the very same language fits precisely, and whose loss, or what is the same thing, its wavering use, must therefore also draw after it the loss of the concept itself, and indeed of a concept that, because it occupies reason very much, cannot be done without except to the great detriment of all transcendental judgments. The word absolute is now often used merely to indicate: that something holds of a thing considered in itself and thus internally. In this meaning absolutely possible would signify what is possible in itself (*interne*), which is in fact the least that one can say of an object. Conversely, it is also sometimes used to indicate that something is valid in every relation (without limitation), (e.g. absolute dominion) and absolutely possible would in this meaning signify that which is possible in every respect, in every relation, which in turn is the most that I can say about the possibility of a thing. Now these meanings do indeed coincide many times. Thus e.g. what is internally impossible is also impossible in every relation, hence absolutely impossible. But in most cases they are infinitely far apart, and I can in no way conclude: that because something is possible in itself, it is therefore also possible in every relation, hence absolutely possible. Indeed of absolute necessity I will show in what follows that it by no means in all cases depends on the internal, and hence must not be regarded as synonymous with it. That whose opposite is internally impossible, its opposite is of course also impossible in every respect, hence it is itself absolutely necessary, but I cannot conclude conversely, what is absolutely necessary, its opposite is internally impossible, i.e. the absolute necessity of things is an internal necessity; for this internal necessity is in certain cases a wholly empty expression, with which we cannot connect the least concept; whereas the one, of the necessity of a thing in every relation, (to everything possible) carries with it quite particular determinations. Since now the loss of a concept of great application in speculative philosophy can never be indifferent to the philosopher, I hope that the determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the concept hangs will also not be indifferent to him.",["Totalität = totality (not Allheit); Beurtheilungen = judgments (Urtheil is also rendered judgment).","Absicht in 'in aller Absicht' rendered 'respect' (glossary: aim; intention); Beziehung = relation; Weltweisheit = philosophy; Bestimmung = determination.","Bedeutung/bedeuten = meaning/signify; gültig = valid; 'besondere' = particular.","OCR 'Rothwendigkeit' silently corrected to 'Nothwendigkeit'.","Ambiguity kept: 'dessen Verlust, oder welches eben so viel ist, sein schwankender Gebrauch' — 'its' could refer to the word or the concept; German likewise open.","'der' in 'dagegen der, von der Nothwendigkeit eines Dinges…' is kept as 'the one' (referring back to the necessity/expression); the antecedent is not resolved."]],"krv-a-0543":["In this extended meaning I shall then make use of the word: absolute, and oppose it to what is valid merely comparatively or in a particular regard; for the latter is restricted to conditions, but the former holds without restriction.",["comparativ = 'comparatively' (Latinate form kept close to German); Rücksicht = regard; Restriction = restriction.","'dieses leztere … ienes' rendered 'the latter … the former'."]],"krv-a-0544":["Now the transcendental concept of reason always goes only to the absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions and never ends except with the simply — i.e. in every relation — unconditioned. For pure reason leaves everything to the understanding, which first relates itself to the objects of intuition or rather to their synthesis in the imagination. The former reserves for itself alone the absolute totality in the use of the concepts of the understanding, and seeks to carry the synthetic unity, which is thought in the category, out to the simply unconditioned. One can therefore call this the unity of reason of appearances, just as that which the category expresses, unity of understanding. Thus reason relates itself only to the use of the understanding and indeed not insofar as the latter contains the ground of possible experience, (for the absolute totality of conditions is not a concept usable in an experience, because no experience is unconditioned) but in order to prescribe to it the direction toward a certain unity, of which the understanding has no concept and which goes out to this: to grasp together all actions of the understanding, with regard to each object, into an absolute whole. Hence the objective use of the pure concepts of reason is always transcendent, while that of the pure concepts of the understanding must by its nature always be immanent, in that it restricts itself merely to possible experience.",["Gegenstand and Object both 'object' (here only Gegenstand, and 'obiective').","schlechthin = 'simply'; Schlechthinunbedingten = 'simply unconditioned'.","'Jene' (reason) and 'iene' (the unity expressed by the category): referents left as in German ('the former', 'that which').","Vernunfteinheit / Verstandeseinheit rendered 'unity of reason' / 'unity of understanding'; Handlungen = actions; Ansehung = regard.","Ambiguity kept: 'dessen/dieser' and 'ihm' referents ('the latter', 'it') left unresolved."]],"krv-a-0545":["By an idea I understand a necessary concept of reason to which no congruent object can be given in the senses. Thus the pure concepts of reason now under consideration are transcendental ideas. They are concepts of pure reason; for they consider all cognition of experience as determined through an absolute totality of conditions. They are not arbitrarily invented, but set as a task by the nature of reason itself, and therefore relate themselves necessarily to the whole use of the understanding. They are, finally, transcendent and overstep the boundary of all experience, in which, therefore, no object can ever occur that would be adequate to the transcendental idea. When one names an idea, one says, as regards the object, (as of an object of the pure understanding) very much, but as regards the subject (i.e. with regard to its actuality under empirical condition) for that very reason very little, because, as the concept of a maximum, it can never be given congruently *in concreto*. Since now the latter is in the merely speculative use of reason properly the whole aim, and the approximation to a concept which in exercise is nevertheless never reached is just as much as if the concept were entirely missed, one says of such a concept: it is only an idea. Thus one could say: the absolute whole of all appearances is only an idea, for, since we can never sketch anything of the kind in an image, it remains a problem without any solution. On the other hand, because in the practical use of the understanding it is wholly and solely a matter of exercise according to rules, the idea of practical reason can always be given actually, although only in part, *in concreto*, indeed it is the indispensable condition of every practical use of reason. Its exercise is always bounded and deficient, but under boundaries not determinable, thus always under the influence of the concept of an absolute completeness. Accordingly the practical idea is always most fruitful and, with regard to actual actions, unavoidably necessary. In it pure reason even has causality to bring forth actually what its concept contains, hence one cannot say of wisdom, as if disparagingly: it is only an idea, but precisely because it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible ends, it must serve as a rule to everything practical as an original, at least restricting, condition.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'verfehler' to 'verfehlt', 'bestimbaren' to 'bestimmbaren', 'ursprungliche' to 'ursprüngliche'.","Gegenstand and Object both 'object' ('dem Obiect nach' / 'einem Gegenstande'); Subiect = 'subject'.","aufgegeben = 'set as a task' (cf. Aufgabe = problem); Gränze/Gränzen = boundary/boundaries, begränzt = bounded.","Wirklichkeit = actuality, wirklich = actually; Absicht = aim; Ausübung = exercise; Ansehung = regard.","Erfahrungserkentniß = 'cognition of experience'; Caussalität = 'causality'; Auflösung = 'solution'.","Ambiguity kept: 'Ihre Ausübung' (the idea's exercise) and the antecedent of 'ihr Begriff' ('its concept': reason's or the idea's)."]],"krv-a-0546":["Although we must now say of the transcendental concepts of reason: they are only ideas, we shall nevertheless by no means have to regard them as superfluous and void. For even if no object can be determined through them, they can still, at bottom and unnoticed, serve the understanding as a canon of its extended and unanimous use, through which it indeed cognizes no more of an object than it would cognize according to its concepts, but is nevertheless guided better and further in this cognition. Not to mention: that they perhaps make possible a transition from concepts of nature to the practical ones, and in such a way can procure for the moral ideas themselves stability and interconnection with the speculative cognitions of reason. About all this one must await the disclosure in the continuation.",["Obiect and Gegenstand both 'object'; erkennen/Erkentniß = cognize/cognition.","einhellig = 'unanimous' (alternatives: harmonious); Haltung = 'stability'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'.","Aufschluß = 'disclosure'; Verfolg = 'continuation'; Naturbegriffen = 'concepts of nature'.","'dadurch er … keinen Gegenstand mehr erkent, als…' kept as literal 'no more of an object than'."]],"krv-a-0547":["In accordance with our aim, however, we here set the practical ideas aside and therefore consider reason only in the speculative use, and in this one still more narrowly, namely only in the transcendental use. Here we must now take the same path that we took above in the deduction of the categories, namely to weigh the logical form of the cognition of reason, and to see whether reason might not thereby also become a source of concepts, to regard objects in themselves as synthetically determined a priori, with respect to one or another function of reason.",["Absicht = aim; Gebrauch = use; Erkentniß = cognition; Begriffe = concepts; Obiecte = objects (Latinate Object, rendered 'object' as for Gegenstand).","'Deduction' kept as a cognate of Deduction; 'Categorien' = categories.","Ambiguity preserved: 'synthetisch a priori bestimt' could attach to 'Obiecte an sich selbst' or to the act of regarding (anzusehen); 'in Ansehung' = 'with respect to'.","The infinitive clause 'Obiecte ... anzusehen' is left dependent on 'Quell von Begriffen' as in the German; the construction is awkward and has been kept so.","'Quell' = source; 'erwägen' = weigh."]],"krv-a-0548":["Reason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of cognition, is the faculty of inferring, i.e., of judging mediately (through the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgment under the condition of a given one). The given judgment is the general rule (major premise, *Maior*). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgment under the condition of the rule is the minor premise (*Minor*); the actual judgment, which states the assertion of the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (*Conclusio*). The rule, namely, says something generally under a certain condition. Now in a case that occurs the condition of the rule takes place. Thus that which held generally under that condition will also be regarded as valid in the case that occurs (which carries this condition with it). One easily sees that reason arrives at a cognition through acts of the understanding, which constitute a series of conditions. If I arrive at the proposition: all bodies are alterable, only by beginning from the more remote cognition (in which the concept of body does not yet occur, but which nevertheless contains the condition of it): everything composite is alterable, proceeding from this to a closer one, which stands under the condition of the former: bodies are composite, and from this for the first time to a third, which now connects the remote cognition (alterable) with the one at hand: consequently bodies are alterable, then I have arrived through a series of conditions (premises) at a cognition (conclusion). Now every series, whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgment) is given, can be continued; hence precisely the same act of reason leads to the *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*, which is a series of inferences that can be continued to indeterminate distances either on the side of the conditions (*per prosyllogismos*) or of the conditioned (*per episyllogismos*).",["schliessen = infer; Schluß = inference; Schlußsatz = conclusion (*Conclusio*); Obersatz/Untersatz = major/minor premise.","Vermögen = faculty; Urtheil = judgment; Erkentniß = cognition; Bedingung = condition; bedingt = conditioned; Satz = proposition.","'Assertion' kept as Kant's Latinate word (behaupten = assert); 'Verstandeshandlungen' = acts of the understanding; 'Vernunfthandlung' = act of reason.","'Weiten' = distances (literally 'widths'); 'in unbestimmte Weiten' kept literal.","'Gründe'/'Prämissen' both appear later; 'Prämissen' = premises. 'veränderlich' = alterable; 'zusammengesezt' = composite; 'Cörper' = body.","'Exponent' kept as in German; 'gelanget/gelange' = arrive; 'verknüpfen' = connect."]],"krv-a-0549":["But one soon becomes aware: that the chain, or series, of prosyllogisms, i.e., of cognitions concluded on the side of the grounds, or of the conditions, for a given cognition, in other words: the ascending series of syllogisms, must nevertheless relate differently to the faculty of reason than the descending series, i.e., the progress of reason on the side of the conditioned through episyllogisms. For, since in the former case the cognition (*conclusio*) is given only as conditioned: one cannot arrive at it by means of reason in any other way than at least under the presupposition: that all members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premises), because only under their presupposition is the judgment at hand possible a priori; on the other hand, on the side of the conditioned, or of the consequences, only a becoming series, and not one already entirely presupposed or given, hence only a potential progress, is thought. Hence if a cognition is regarded as conditioned, reason is compelled to regard the series of conditions in the ascending line as completed and as given in its totality. But if precisely the same cognition is regarded at the same time as condition of other cognitions, which constitute among one another a series of consequences in a descending line, then reason can be entirely indifferent how far this progress extends itself *a parte posteriori*, and whether a totality of this series is possible at all; because it does not need such a series for the conclusion lying before it, since this is already sufficiently determined and secured by its grounds *a parte priori*. Now it may be: that on the side of the conditions the series of premises has a first, as supreme condition, or not, and thus is without bounds *a parte priori*, yet it must nevertheless contain totality of the condition, supposing that we could never succeed in grasping it, and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is regarded as a consequence springing from it, is to hold as true. This is a demand of reason, which announces its cognition as determined a priori and as necessary, either in itself, and then it needs no grounds, or, if it is derived, as a member of a series of grounds, which is itself true in an unconditioned way.",["gefolgerten = 'concluded' (folgern = conclude); Folgerung = consequence; Fortgang = progress; Totalität = totality (Allheit also glossed 'totality').","Gründe = grounds; Bedingungen = conditions; Gränzen = bounds ('without bounds').","Ambiguity preserved: 'a priori' in 'das vorliegende Urtheil a priori möglich' may attach to 'judgment' or to 'possible'; likewise 'als a priori bestimt'.","'dahin gelangen könten, sie zu fassen' rendered literally; 'fassen' = grasp. 'Schluß' = inference rendered 'syllogisms' for Vernunftschlüsse.","'Foderung' = demand; 'ankündigt' = announces; the final clause ('die selbst ... wahr ist') may refer to 'Glied' or 'Reihe'; German allows either (relative 'die' is feminine: 'Reihe').","'hinreichend' = sufficiently; 'gleichgültig' = indifferent."]],"krv-a-0550":["Of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic, Third Section. System of the Transcendental Ideas.",["Dialectic kept as 'Dialectic'; Abschnitt = section; Idee = idea."]],"krv-a-0551":["We have to do here not with a logical dialectic, which abstracts from all content of cognition and merely uncovers the false illusion in the form of syllogisms, but with a transcendental one, which, entirely a priori, is to contain the origin of certain cognitions from pure reason and of inferred concepts, whose object cannot be given empirically at all, which therefore lie wholly outside the faculty of the pure understanding. We have inferred from the natural relation that the transcendental use of our cognition must have, in inferences as well as in judgments, to the logical use: that there will be only three kinds of dialectical inferences, which relate to the three kinds of inference by which reason can arrive at cognitions from principles, and that in all of them its business is to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, to which the understanding always remains bound, to the unconditioned one, which it can never reach.",["'falschen Schein' = 'false illusion' (Schein = illusion).","'geschlossener Begriffe' = 'inferred concepts' (schließen = infer); 'Schlüssen' = inferences; 'Schlußarten' = kinds of inference; 'Arten' = kinds.","'abgenommen' = 'inferred' is an interpretive choice; literally 'taken off/derived'; the glossary has no entry.","Ambiguity preserved: 'sowol in Schlüssen, als Urtheilen' may modify 'transscendentale Gebrauch' or 'Beziehung'.","'die er niemals erreichen kan': 'er' = der Verstand; 'die' refers to 'Synthesis'.","Principien = principles (Grundsatz/Prinzip); Geschäfte = business."]],"krv-a-0552":["Now the general of all relation that our representations can have is 1) the relation to the subject, 2) the relation to objects and indeed, either first as appearances, or as objects of thinking in general. If one combines this subdivision with the upper one, then all relation of representations, of which we can make for ourselves either a concept or idea, is threefold: 1. the relation to the subject, 2. to the manifold of the object in appearance, 3. to all things in general.",["Obiecte (Latinate) and Gegenstände both = 'objects'; 'Obiecte' in 1) and 2), 'Gegenstände' of thinking.","'das allgemeine aller Beziehung' kept literal ('the general of all relation').","Subiect = subject; Verhältniß = relation (also Beziehung = relation; both rendered 'relation').","'Untereintheilung' = subdivision; 'der oberen' = 'the upper one' (feminine noun implied, left unsupplied).","Mannigfaltigen = manifold; Erscheinung = appearance."]],"krv-a-0553":["Now all pure concepts in general have to do with the synthetic unity of representations, but concepts of pure reason (transcendental ideas) with the unconditioned synthetic unity of all conditions in general. Consequently all transcendental ideas will be able to be brought under three classes, of which the first contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of conditions of appearance, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thinking in general.",["Einheit = unity; unbedingt = unconditioned; überhaupt = 'in general'.","'Classen' = classes; Subiect = subject."]],"krv-a-0554":["The thinking subject is the object of psychology, the sum total of all appearances (the world) the object of cosmology, and the thing which contains the supreme condition of the possibility of everything that can be thought (the being of all beings) the object of theology. Thus pure reason offers the idea for a transcendental doctrine of the soul (*psychologia rationalis*), for a transcendental science of the world (*cosmologia rationalis*), and finally also for a transcendental cognition of God (*Theologia transscendentalis*). The mere sketch even for the one as well as for the other of these sciences does not stem from the understanding at all, even if it were combined with the highest logical use of reason, i.e., with all conceivable inferences, in order to progress from one object of the same (appearance) to all others, to the most remote members of the empirical synthesis, but is solely a pure and genuine product, or problem, of pure reason.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Wesen aller Wesen = 'the being of all beings' (Wesen = being).","Seelenlehre = doctrine of the soul; Weltwissenschaft = science of the world (Wissenschaft = science); Gotteserkentniß = cognition of God.","'an die Hand geben' = 'offers' (literally 'gives to hand').","'zu einer sowol als der andern' rendered literally ('for the one as well as for the other'); 'desselben' refers ambiguously to 'Gebrauch' or 'Vernunft'; left ambiguous.","'Problem' printed in German as 'Problem' (cf. Aufgabe = problem); the Latinate word kept as 'problem'."]],"krv-a-0555":["What stand under these three titles of all transcendental ideas as *modi* of pure concepts of reason will be set out completely in the following chapter. They run along the thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates straight to objects, but rather to the concepts of the understanding of them. Likewise, it will also be possible to make distinct only in the complete execution how reason, merely through the synthetic use of the very same function of which it makes use for the categorical syllogism, must necessarily arrive at the concept of the absolute unity of the thinking subject, how the logical procedure in hypothetical ones must necessarily bring with it the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism must necessarily bring with it the highest concept of reason, of a being of all beings, a thought that at first glance seems extremely paradoxical.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism (glossary: 'inference of reason'); Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding.","'in hypothetischen' has no noun in the German; 'ones' is the English placeholder, referring back to Vernunftschlüsse (supplied from 'categorischen Vernunftschlusse').","'modi' is Latin in the original, kept in italics.","'Gegenstände' = objects (Object also = 'object' elsewhere).","'Wesen aller Wesen': Wesen = being.","'nach sich ziehen müsse' governs both the 'Idee' and the 'Vernunftbegriff' clauses; the sentence's 'wie ... müsse' construction is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0556":["Of these transcendental ideas no objective deduction is properly possible, such as we could provide of the categories. For in fact they have no relation to any object that could be given congruent to them, precisely because they are only ideas. But a subjective derivation of them from the nature of our reason we could undertake, and this has also been accomplished in the present chapter.",["'Obiect' = object (Latinate member; Gegenstand also 'object').","'Categorien' rendered 'categories'; 'konten' = 'could' (older spelling)."]],"krv-a-0557":["One readily sees: that pure reason has no other aim than the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions (be it of inherence, or of dependence, or of concurrence) and that it has nothing to do with absolute completeness on the part of the conditioned. For it needs only the former, in order to presuppose the whole series of the conditions, and thereby to give it to the understanding a priori. But if a completely (and unconditionally) given condition is once there, then there is no longer need of a concept of reason with regard to the continuation of the series; for the understanding takes every step downward, from the condition to the conditioned, by itself. In such a way the transcendental ideas serve only for ascending in the series of conditions, up to the unconditioned, i.e., to the principles. With regard to descending to the conditioned, however, there is indeed a widely extended logical use that our reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but no transcendental one at all, and, if we make for ourselves an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis (of the *progressus*), e.g., of the whole series of all future alterations of the world, then this is a thought-entity (*ens rationis*), which is only arbitrarily thought, and not necessarily presupposed by reason. For to the possibility of the conditioned the totality of its conditions is indeed presupposed, but not of its sequels. Consequently such a concept is no transcendental idea, with which we after all have to do here solely.",["Absicht = aim; Totalität rendered 'totality' (Allheit is also 'totality'); Bedingung = condition; Bedingtes = conditioned.","'Folgen' = sequels (glossary: Folge = sequel; sequence).","'Principien' = principles (Grundsatz/Principium; Latinate member).","'iener' (jener) refers to the 'side of the conditions' ('Seiten der Bedingungen'); rendered 'the former'. 'abwerts' = 'abwärts'.","'Weltveränderungen' rendered 'alterations of the world'; 'Veränderung' is not in the glossary.","'a priori' left in Latin as in the original; it could attach to 'presuppose' or 'give' — the ambiguity is kept.","'nothwendig' = necessarily; 'zu thun haben' = 'have to do'."]],"krv-a-0558":["Finally one also becomes aware: that among the transcendental ideas themselves a certain interconnection and unity shines forth, and that pure reason, by means of them, brings all its cognitions into a system. To proceed from the cognition of oneself (the soul) to the cognition of the world, and, by means of this, to the original being is such a natural progression that it seems similar to the logical progress of reason from the premises to the conclusion. Whether now here a kinship of the kind that there is between the logical and the transcendental procedure really lies secretly at the basis, is also one of the questions the answer to which one must expect only in the course of these investigations. We have already reached our end provisionally: in that we have been able to pull the transcendental concepts of reason, which otherwise usually mix with others in the theory of the philosophers, without these even properly distinguishing them from concepts of the understanding, out of this ambiguous position, to indicate their origin, and thereby at the same time their determinate number, beyond which there can be none at all, and to represent them in a systematic interconnection, whereby a special field for pure reason is staked out and limited.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Fortschritt = progression; Fortgang = progress (the two are distinguished by 'progression' / 'progress').","Urwesen = original being; Wesen = being.","'Prämissen' = premises; 'Schlußsatze' = conclusion (Schluß = conclusion in glossary).","Zweck = end; Zahl = number; bestimmt = determinate; besonder = special; vorstellen = represent.","'Ob nun hier ... zum Grunde liege': the 'of the kind' clause is kept in German order; 'Verwandschaft' = kinship.","The 'da' clause ('in that') is long and syntactically loose in the German (the infinitives depend on 'haben ... können'); the structure is preserved.","'Verstandesbegriffen' = concepts of the understanding; 'eingeschränkt' = limited."]],"krv-a-0559":["Second Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. On the dialectical inferences of pure reason.",["'Schlüsse' = inferences (glossary: Schluß = inference; conclusion)."]],"krv-a-0560":["One can say: the object of a mere transcendental idea is something of which one has no concept, although this idea has been generated quite necessarily in reason according to its original laws. For in fact of an object that is supposed to be adequate to the demand of reason no concept of the understanding is possible, i.e., one that can be shown in a possible experience and made intuitive. One would still express oneself better, and with less danger of misunderstanding, if one said: that we can have no cognition of the object that corresponds to an idea, although a problematic concept.",["Gegenstand and Object both 'object'; here 'Gegenstande' (first two) and 'Obiect' (last).","Kentniß = cognition here (Kenntnis in glossary is 'knowledge'/'acquaintance'; the German spelling 'Kentniß' is kept in correspondence with 'Kenntnis'); 'cognition' is the choice made for consistency in context, but the term is Kenntnis, not Erkenntnis.","anschaulich = intuitive; 'Foderung' = demand (older spelling of Forderung).","'obzwar' = although; 'adäquat' retained as 'adequate'."]],"krv-a-0561":["Now at least the transcendental (subjective) reality of the pure concepts of reason rests on this: that we are brought to such ideas through a necessary syllogism. Thus there will be syllogisms that contain no empirical premises and by means of which we infer from something that we know to something else of which we nevertheless have no concept and to which we nonetheless, through an unavoidable illusion, give objective reality. Such inferences are therefore, with regard to their result, to be called rather rationalizing ones than syllogisms; although, on account of their occasion, they can well bear the latter name, because they are after all not invented, or arisen contingently, but have sprung from the nature of reason. They are sophistications, not of human beings, but of pure reason itself, from which even the wisest of all human beings cannot free himself, and perhaps after much effort can guard against the error, but can never be fully rid of the illusion, which unceasingly torments and mocks him.",["Realität = reality; Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß/schliessen = inference/infer; vernünftelnd = rationalizing (vernünfteln = rationalize); Schein = illusion.","kennen = know (glossary: kennen = know; be acquainted with); Begriff = concept; zufällig = contingently.","'Sophisticationen' kept as 'sophistications'; 'zwakt' = 'zwackt' (torments, nags), 'äfft' = mocks."]],"krv-a-0562":["Of these dialectical syllogisms there are therefore only three kinds, as many as there are ideas in which their conclusions end up. In the syllogism of the first class I infer from the transcendental concept of the subject, which contains nothing manifold, to the absolute unity of this subject itself, of which I in this way have no concept at all. This dialectical inference I shall call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of rationalizing inferences is laid out on the transcendental concept of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given appearance in general, and I infer from the fact that I always have a self-contradictory concept of the unconditioned synthetic unity of the series on one side, to the correctness of the opposed unity, of which I nevertheless also have no concept. The state of reason in these dialectical inferences I shall call the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of rationalizing inferences, I infer from the totality of the conditions to think objects in general, so far as they can be given to me, to the absolute synthetic unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general, i.e., from things that I do not know according to their mere transcendental concept, to a being of all beings, which I know through a transcendent concept still less and of whose unconditioned necessity I can make for myself no concept. This dialectical syllogism I shall call the ideal of pure reason.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Schluß = inference; vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Mannigfaltiges = manifold; Erscheinung = appearance; Zustand = state.","Gegenstände (objects) is in apposition/construction with 'zu denken'; the clause 'von der Totalität der Bedingungen, Gegenstände überhaupt ... zu denken' is kept as awkward as in the German.","'kennen' = know; 'hade' in the OCR corrected to 'habe'.","'Paralogismus', 'Antinomie', 'Ideal' kept as cognates; 'transscendent' = transcendent (distinct from transscendental)."]],"krv-a-0563":["First Chapter of the Second Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. On the paralogisms of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-0564":["The logical paralogism consists in the falsity of a syllogism as to form, its content may otherwise be whatever it will. A transcendental paralogism, however, has a transcendental ground: to infer falsely as to form. In such a way a fallacious inference of this kind will have its ground in the nature of human reason and will carry with it an unavoidable, though not unresolvable, illusion.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; schliessen = infer; Fehlschluß = fallacious inference; Inhalt = content; Grund = ground; Form = form; Illusion rendered 'illusion' (as for Schein, but here the German word is Illusion).","'der Form nach' = 'as to form' (literal); 'Menschenvernunft' = human reason."]],"krv-a-0565":["We now come to a concept that was not listed above in the general list of transcendental concepts, and yet must be counted along with them, without thereby altering that table in the least and declaring it deficient. This is the concept, or, if one prefers, the judgment: I think. But one easily sees: that it is the vehicle of all concepts in general and consequently also of the transcendental ones, and is therefore always included among these and hence is equally transcendental, but can have no particular title, because it serves only to introduce all thinking, as belonging to consciousness. Meanwhile, as pure as it is of the empirical (the impression of the senses), it nevertheless serves to distinguish two kinds of objects from the nature of our power of representation. I, as thinking, am an object of inner sense and am called soul. That which is an object of outer senses is called body. Accordingly the expression: I, as a thinking being, already signifies the object of psychology, which may be called the rational doctrine of the soul, if I demand to know nothing more of the soul than what, independently of all experience (which determines me more closely and *in concreto*), can be inferred from this concept I, insofar as it occurs in all thinking.",["\"mit begriffen werde\" is rendered \"is included\"; this is the verb begreifen, which the glossary renders \"comprehend\" elsewhere.","Vorstellungskraft = \"power of representation\"; Cörper = \"body\"; geschlossen = \"inferred\" (Schluß = inference); \"vor mangelhaft zu erklären\" = für mangelhaft zu erklären.","Gegenstand = \"object\" throughout; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; bedeutet = \"signifies\"; Wissen/wissen: \"to know\" here translates the verb wissen."]],"krv-a-0566":["The rational doctrine of the soul is now actually an undertaking of this kind, for if the least empirical thing of my thinking, any particular perception of my inner state, were still mixed in among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would no longer be rational, but empirical doctrine of the soul. We thus already have before us an alleged science, which has been built on the single proposition: I think, and whose ground or groundlessness we can here investigate quite fittingly, and in keeping with the nature of a transcendental philosophy. One must not take offense at this, that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of oneself, I nevertheless have an inner experience, and consequently the rational doctrine of the soul, which is built on it, is never pure, but is grounded in part on an empirical principle. For this inner perception is nothing further than the mere apperception: I think, which even makes all transcendental concepts possible, in which it is said: I think the substance, the cause, etc. For inner experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general and its relation to other perception, without any particular distinction of it and determination being given empirically, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but must be regarded as cognition of the empirical in general, and belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is indeed transcendental. The least object of perception (e.g., merely pleasure or displeasure), which would be added to the general representation of self-consciousness, would at once transform rational psychology into an empirical one.",["Obiect (\"Das mindeste Obiect der Wahrnehmung\") and Gegenstand both = \"object\".","Erkentnißgründe = \"grounds of cognition\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Erfahrung = \"experience\"; Selbstbewustseyn = \"self-consciousness\".","Unterschied = \"distinction\" (could also be \"difference\"); \"derselben\" in \"ohne das irgend ein besonderer Unterschied derselben\" is left as \"of it\" (ambiguous between Wahrnehmung and Erfahrung); \"ohne das\" read as ohne daß.","\"Man darf sich daran nicht stossen\" is rendered literally with \"at this\"; \"seiner Selbst\" = \"of oneself\" (could be \"of itself\")."]],"krv-a-0567":["I think, is thus the sole text of rational psychology, out of which it is to unfold all its wisdom. One easily sees: that this thought, if it is to be related to an object (myself), can contain nothing other than transcendental predicates of it; because the least empirical predicate would spoil the rational purity and independence of the science from all experience.",["\"desselben\" = \"of it\" (the object); \"Reinigkeit\" = \"purity\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; auswickeln = \"unfold\"."]],"krv-a-0568":["But here we shall have to follow merely the guiding thread of the categories, only, since here first a thing, I, as thinking being, has been given, we shall indeed not alter the above order of the categories among one another, as it is represented in their table, but nevertheless begin here from the category of substance, through which a thing in itself is represented, and so go through their series backwards. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else that it may contain must be derived, is accordingly the following:",["\"Ding an sich selbst\" = \"thing in itself\"; Ding = \"thing\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Leitfaden = \"guiding thread\"; \"rückwerts\" = \"backwards\".","\"Ihrer Reihe\" can refer to the categories' series or to the category of substance's series; left as \"their series\" following the plural sense."]],"krv-a-0569":["1. The soul is substance",[]],"krv-a-0570":["2. According to its quality, simple",["Einfach = \"simple\"."]],"krv-a-0571":["3. According to the different times in which it is there, numerically identical, i.e., unity (not plurality.)",["\"da ist\" rendered literally as \"is there\" (i.e., exists); Zeit = \"time\"; Einheit = \"unity\"; Vielheit = \"plurality\"."]],"krv-a-0572":["4. In relation to possible objects in space *).",["The footnote marker \"*)\" is kept as in the original; Verhältniß = \"relation\"."]],"krv-a-0573":["*) The reader, who will not so easily guess from these expressions, in their transcendental abstractness, their psychological sense and why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find them sufficiently explained and justified in what follows. Moreover, on account of the Latin expressions that, instead of the equivalent German ones, contrary to the taste of good writing, have crept in, both in this section and with regard to the whole work, I have to adduce in excuse: that I would rather have taken something away from the elegance of language than make school use more difficult through the least unintelligibility.",["Existenz = \"existence\" (Daseyn is also rendered \"existence\" elsewhere in this selection).","Abgezogenheit = \"abstractness\"; Schulgebrauch = \"school use\"; \"Ausdrücke\" = \"expressions\"; \"erklärt\" = \"explained\" (Erklärung = explanation; definition)."]],"krv-a-0574":["From these elements arise all concepts of the pure doctrine of the soul, merely through composition, without cognizing in the least any other principle. This substance, merely as object of inner sense, gives the concept of immateriality, as simple substance, of incorruptibility; the identity of the same, as intellectual substance, gives personality; all these three pieces together, spirituality; the relation to the objects in space gives the *commercium* with bodies; consequently it represents the thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, i.e., it as soul (*anima*) and as the ground of animality, this limited by spirituality, immortality.",["\"Principium\" (roman type in the original) is rendered \"principle\"; *commercium* and *anima* are Latin in the original and kept in italics.","\"sie\" in \"stellet sie ... vor\" and \"derselben\" are left as pronouns (\"it\", \"the same\"); the syntax of the last clause (\"diese ... eingeschränkt, Immortalität\") is elliptical in the German and kept so.","erkennen = \"cognize\"; einschränken = \"limit\"; Stücke = \"pieces\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Grund = \"ground\"."]],"krv-a-0575":["To this now four paralogisms of a transcendental doctrine of the soul are related, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason, concerning the nature of our thinking being. But as a ground of the same we can lay down nothing other than the simple and for itself entirely empty-in-content representation: I, of which one cannot even say: that it is a concept, but a mere consciousness that accompanies all concepts. Through this I, or He, or It (the thing) which thinks, nothing further is now represented than a transcendental subject of thoughts = X, which is cognized only through the thoughts that are its predicates and of which, separately, we can never have the least concept, around which we therefore turn in a constant circle, in that we must always already make use of its representation in order to judge anything about it; an inconvenience that is not to be separated from it, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation that distinguishes a particular object, but a form of the same in general, insofar as it is to be called cognition; for of that alone can I say that through it I think anything.",["OCR/print slip \"können wird\" silently read as \"können wir\".","\"vor sich selbst\" = für sich selbst, rendered \"for itself\"; \"an Inhalt gänzlich leer\" kept as a compound \"entirely empty-in-content\" to preserve the German order; \"an sich\" = \"in itself\".","\"derselben\" in \"eine Form derselben überhaupt\" is feminine and left as \"of the same\"; it could refer to Vorstellung or Bewustseyn.","\"welches nur durch die Gedanken ... erkant wird und wovon ... um welches\" — the relative clauses are kept as in the German; \"Obiect\" and Gegenstand both = \"object\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"."]],"krv-a-0576":["But it must seem strange right at the outset: that the condition under which I think at all and which is consequently merely a constitution of my subject, is at the same time to be valid for everything that thinks, and that we can presume to ground an apodictic and universal judgment on a seemingly empirical proposition, namely: that everything that thinks is so constituted as the pronouncement of self-consciousness states it of me. The cause of this, however, lies in this: that we must necessarily attribute to things *a priori* all the properties that make up the conditions under which alone we think them. Now of a thinking being I can have the least representation through no outer experience, but merely through self-consciousness. Thus objects of the sort are nothing further than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things, which are only thereby represented as thinking beings. The proposition: I think, is however taken here only problematically; not insofar as it may contain a perception of an existence (the Cartesian *cogito; ergo sum*), but according to its mere possibility, in order to see what properties may flow from this so simple proposition to the subject of it (such a one may now exist or not).",["Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\" (\"beschaffen\" = \"constituted\"); Eigenschaft = \"properties\"; Bedingung = \"condition\"; Daseyn and existiren = \"existence\"/\"exist\".","\"Subiect\" = \"subject\"; \"Satz\" = \"proposition\"; \"Ausspruch\" = \"pronouncement\"; \"aussagt\" = \"states\"; \"vor alles\" = für alles, rendered \"for everything\".","\"es mag dergleichen nun existiren oder nicht\": the subject of \"mag\" is left as \"such a one\" (the subject of the proposition); the German is elliptical.","\"auf das Subiect desselben\": \"desselben\" = \"of it\" (the proposition)."]],"krv-a-0577":["If more than the *cogito* lay at the basis of our pure cognition of reason of thinking beings in general, if we also took to help the observations on the play of our thoughts and the laws of nature of the thinking self to be drawn from them: then an empirical psychology would arise, which would be a kind of physiology of inner sense and perhaps could explain the appearances of it, but could never serve to disclose such properties as do not belong at all to possible experience (like that of the simple), nor to teach anything apodictically about thinking beings in general that concerns their nature; it would thus be no rational psychology.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Beobachtungen = observations; Erscheinungen = appearances; Erfahrung = experience; Wesen = being; Naturgesetze = laws of nature; Eigenschaften = properties (Eigenschaft is not in the glossary; Beschaffenheit is also 'property').","'desselben' (of it) is left with the ambiguity of the German: it can refer to 'inneren Sinnes' or to the preceding noun phrase.","'Läge ... würden wir ... zu Hülfe nehmen' is rendered with the German subjunctive structure; 'zu Hülfe nehmen' kept literally as 'take to help'.","*cogito* is Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-0578":["Since now the proposition: I think (taken problematically), contains the form of every judgment of the understanding in general and accompanies all categories as their vehicle, it is clear: that the inferences from it can contain a merely transcendental use of the understanding, which rejects every admixture of experience, and of whose progression we, according to what we have shown above, can already in advance form no advantageous concept for ourselves. We want, then, to pursue it with a critical eye through all predicaments of the pure doctrine of the soul.",["Verstandesurtheil = judgment of the understanding; Fortgang = progression; Schlüsse = inferences; Seelenlehre = doctrine of the soul; Prädicamente = predicaments.","'ausschlägt' (schlägt aus) rendered 'rejects'; 'dessen' and 'ihn' have 'Gebrauch' (use) as grammatical antecedent, though 'Verstand' is also grammatically possible for 'dessen'.","'ihr' in 'ihr Vehikel' is feminine/plural-referring to 'Categorien'."]],"krv-a-0579":["First Paralogism of Substantiality.",[]],"krv-a-0580":["That, the representation of which is the absolute subject of our judgments and therefore cannot be used as determination of another thing, is substance.",["Vorstellung = representation; Bestimmung = determination; Urtheile = judgments."]],"krv-a-0581":["I, as a thinking being, am the absolute subject of all my possible judgments, and this representation of myself cannot be used as predicate of any other thing.",["Wesen = being; Subiect = subject; Prädicat = predicate."]],"krv-a-0582":["Thus I, as thinking being (soul), am substance.",[]],"krv-a-0583":["Critique of the first paralogism of pure psychology.",[]],"krv-a-0584":["We have shown in the analytic part of transcendental logic: that pure categories (and among these also that of substance) in themselves have no objective meaning at all, where an intuition is not laid beneath them, to whose manifold they can be applied, as functions of synthetic unity. Without that they are merely functions of a judgment without content. Of every thing in general I can say that it is substance, insofar as I distinguish it from mere predicates and determinations of things. Now in all our thinking the I is the subject, in which thoughts inhere only as determinations, and this I cannot be used as the determination of another thing. Thus everyone must necessarily regard himself as the substance, but thinking only as accidents of his existence and determinations of his state.",["Bedeutung = meaning; Anschauung = intuition; Mannigfaltiges = manifold; Einheit = unity; Inhalt = content; Dasein = existence; Zustand = state; Accidenzen = accidents.","'an sich selbst' = 'in themselves' (glossary 'an sich').","'dem Gedanken nur als Bestimmungen inhäriren': 'dem' is the relative pronoun (dative, the subject), 'Gedanken' the plural subject."]],"krv-a-0585":["But what use am I now to make of this concept of a substance. That I, as a thinking being, endure for myself, naturally neither arise nor perish, that I can in no way infer from it, and yet to this alone can the concept of the substantiality of my thinking subject be of use to me, without which I could very well do without it.",["schließen = infer (cf. Schluß = inference); fortdaure = endure (not beharren, which is 'persist'); Gebrauch = use; Begriff = concept.","'vor mich selbst' (= für mich selbst) rendered 'for myself'.","The relative 'ohne welches' is left without a stated antecedent, as in German (neuter: the foregoing 'das'/'dazu', or the concept's utility).","The sentence is a question in sense but ends with a period in the source; kept."]],"krv-a-0586":["So much is lacking for one to be able to infer these properties from the mere pure category of a substance, that we must rather lay at the basis the persistence of a given object from experience, if we want to apply to it the empirically usable concept of a substance. Now, however, in our proposition we have laid no experience at the basis, but have inferred merely from the concept of the relation that all thinking has to the I, as the common subject, in which it inheres. We also could, even if we were to aim at it, demonstrate no such persistence through any secure observation. For the I is indeed in all thoughts; but with this representation there is not the least intuition combined, that would distinguish it from other objects of intuition. One can thus indeed perceive that this representation recurs again and again in all thinking, but not that it is a standing and abiding intuition, in which the thoughts (as changeable) alternate.",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Gegenstand = object; Beobachtung = observation; wahrnehmen = perceive (Wahrnehmung = perception); verbunden = combined; Anschauung = intuition.","'aus der Erfahrung' may attach to 'zum Grunde legen' or to 'Beharrlichkeit'; left ambiguous.","'Eigenschaften': see 0577. 'schließen/geschlossen' = infer/inferred.","'empirischbrauchbaren' printed as one word; rendered 'empirically usable'.","'wechselten' (subjunctive) rendered with the plain present 'alternate'."]],"krv-a-0587":["From this it follows: that the first syllogism of transcendental psychology foists on us only a supposed new insight, in that it passes off the constant logical subject of thinking for the cognition of the real subject of inherence, of which we have not the least acquaintance, nor can have, because consciousness is the only thing that makes all representations into thoughts, and in which therefore all our perceptions, as in the transcendental subject, must be encountered, and we, apart from this logical meaning of the I, have no acquaintance with the subject in itself, which lies at the basis of this, as of all thoughts, as substratum. Meanwhile one can very well let the proposition: the soul is substance, count as valid, if one only resigns oneself: that this concept of ours does not lead the least bit further, or can teach any of the usual consequences of the rationalizing doctrine of the soul, such as e.g. the everlasting duration of it through all changes and even the death of the human being, that it thus designates only a substance in the idea, but not in reality.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Erkentniß = cognition; Kentniß = acquaintance; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Einsicht = insight; Folgerungen = consequences; vernünftelnden = rationalizing; Idee = idea; Realität = reality; Bedeutung = meaning.","'unser dieser Begriff' is the reading as it stands (cf. 'uns'?); rendered 'this concept of ours' and the doubt flagged; 'führe' has no explicit object.","'was diesem ... zum Grunde liegt': 'diesem' ('this') is left ambiguous between 'Bewustseyn' and 'Subiecte'; 'er' = 'Begriff'.","'Substratum' is Latin in the source (roman type); italicized here as foreign: *substratum*. 'ausgiebt' rendered 'passes off'; 'aufhefte' rendered 'foists on'."]],"krv-a-0588":["Second Paralogism of Simplicity.",["'Simplicität' (Latinate) and 'einfach' (0589) are both rendered with 'simple/simplicity'; the heading's word is Simplicität."]],"krv-a-0589":["That thing, whose action can never be regarded as the concurrence of many acting things, is simple.",["Handlung = action; Concurrenz = concurrence; einfach = simple."]],"krv-a-0590":["Now the soul, or the thinking I, is such a one: Thus etc.",[]],"krv-a-0591":["Critique of the second paralogism of transcendental psychology.",[]],"krv-a-0592":["This is the Achilles of all dialectical inferences of the pure doctrine of the soul, not perhaps merely a sophistical play, which a dogmatist contrives in order to give his assertions a fleeting illusion, but an inference that seems to withstand even the sharpest examination and the greatest scrupulousness of inquiry. Here it is.",["Schlüsse = inferences; Schein = illusion; Behauptungen = assertions; Bedenklichkeit = scrupulousness; Nachforschens = inquiry (genitive of the noun-infinitive)."]],"krv-a-0593":["Every composite substance is an aggregate of many, and the action of a composite, or that which inheres in it, as such a one, is an aggregate of many actions or accidents, which are distributed among the multitude of substances. Now an effect that arises from the concurrence of many acting substances is indeed possible, if this effect is merely external (as e.g. the motion of a body is the united motion of all its parts). But with thoughts, as accidents belonging internally to a thinking being, it is constituted otherwise. For, posit that the composite thought: then every part of it would contain a part of the thought, but all taken together would first contain the whole thought. Now this, however, is contradictory. For, because the representations that are distributed among different beings (e.g. the individual words of a verse) never make up a whole thought (a verse): the thought cannot inhere in a composite, as such a one. It is thus possible only in a substance that is not an aggregate of many, hence absolutely simple.*)",["Wirkung = effect; Menge = multitude; Theil = part; Wesen = being; Vorstellungen = representations; widersprechend = contradictory (cf. Widerspruch = contradiction); setzet = posit (imperative plural); allererst = first.","'dächte' (subjunctive) rendered 'thought' is not possible in English as a verb; read 'posit that the composite think'—the translation as given, 'posit that the composite thought', should be read as 'posit that the composite thinks'.","'Zusammengesezte' = composite (noun); 'zusammengesezte Substanz' = composite substance.","The footnote marker *) is carried over as in the source."]],"krv-a-0594":["*) It is very easy to give this proof the usual school-correct measuredness of dress. But for my end it is already sufficient to lay before the eyes the mere ground of proof, at most in a popular way.",["Beweis = proof; Beweißgrund = ground of proof; Zweck = end; 'Einkleidung' (literally 'clothing') rendered 'dress'; 'Abgemessenheit' rendered 'measuredness'; 'schulgerecht' = 'school-correct'."]],"krv-a-0595":["The so-called *nervus probandi* of this argument lies in the proposition: that many representations must be contained in the absolute unity of the thinking subject in order to make up a thought. But no one can prove this proposition from concepts. For how would he indeed go about accomplishing this? The proposition: A thought can only be the effect of the absolute unity of the thinking being, cannot be treated as analytic. For the unity of the thought, which consists of many representations, is collective and, according to mere concepts, can just as well be related to the collective unity of the substances cooperating in it, (as the motion of a body is the composite motion of all parts of it) as to the absolute unity of the subject. According to the rule of identity, therefore, the necessity of the presupposition of a simple substance, in the case of a composite thought, cannot be seen. But that the very same proposition is supposed to be cognized as synthetic and completely *a priori* from mere concepts, no one will dare to answer for who has insight into the ground of the possibility of synthetic propositions *a priori*, as we have set it out above.",["Wesen = being; Satz = proposition; Wirkung = effect; Begriff = concept; Erkenntnis/erkennen = cognition/cognize.","eingesehen / einsieht (einsehen) rendered 'seen' / 'has insight into'.","'nervus probandi' is printed 'neruus' in the source (u/v); given in modern Latin spelling."]],"krv-a-0596":["Now it is also impossible to derive this necessary unity of the subject, as the condition of the possibility of every thought, from experience. For this gives us no necessity to cognize, to say nothing of the fact that the concept of absolute unity is far beyond its sphere. Whence, then, do we take this proposition, on which the whole psychological syllogism rests?",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism (inference of reason), per the glossary.","'diese' (this) in 'Denn diese giebt' refers back to Erfahrung (experience); 'ihre Sphäre' likewise; left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0597":["It is obvious: that if one wants to represent a thinking being to oneself, one must put oneself in its place, and thus substitute one's own subject for the object that one wanted to consider, (which is the case in no other kind of investigation) and that we demand absolute unity of the subject for a thought only because otherwise it could not be said: I think (the manifold in one representation). For although the whole of the thought could be divided and distributed among many subjects, the subjective I still cannot be divided and distributed, and this we do presuppose in all thinking.",["Obiect = object (Latinate Object, as in the glossary's Gegenstand/Object); 'Art' = kind.","Mannigfaltige = manifold."]],"krv-a-0598":["Thus here, just as in the previous paralogism, the formal proposition of apperception: I think, remains the whole ground on which rational psychology ventures the expansion of its cognitions, which proposition is admittedly no experience, but the form of apperception, which attaches to every experience and precedes it, yet nevertheless is always to be regarded only with regard to a possible cognition in general, as a merely subjective condition of the same, which we wrongly make into the condition of the possibility of a cognition of objects, namely into a concept of the thinking being in general, because we cannot represent this to ourselves without putting ourselves, with the formula of our consciousness, in the place of every other intelligent being.",["Erkentnisse/Erkentniß = cognition(s); Gegenstände = objects; Bewustseyn = consciousness.","'derselben' (of the same) may refer to the proposition or to the form of apperception; the ambiguity is kept.","'welches' / 'dieses' (this) in the last clause is left without a resolved referent."]],"krv-a-0599":["But the simplicity of myself (as soul) is also actually not inferred from the proposition: I think, but rather the former already lies in every thought itself. The proposition: I am simple, must be regarded as an immediate expression of apperception, just as the supposed Cartesian inference, *cogito, ergo sum*, is in fact tautological, in that the *cogito* (*sum cogitans*) states actuality immediately. I am simple, however, signifies nothing more than that this representation: I, comprises not the least manifold in itself, and that it is absolute (though merely logical) unity.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality (glossary gives 'actuality; reality'); Schluß/geschlossen = inference/inferred; Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = signify.","'der erstere' (the former) is ambiguous in the German between the simplicity and the first-named item; rendered 'the former' without resolution.","'tavtologisch' is the source's spelling (v for u)."]],"krv-a-0600":["Thus the so famous psychological proof is grounded merely on the indivisible unity of a representation, which directs the verb only with regard to a person. But it is obvious: that the subject of inherence is designated only transcendentally by the I attached to the thought, without noting the least property of it, or in general being acquainted with or knowing anything about it. It signifies a something in general (transcendental subject), the representation of which must indeed be simple, precisely because nothing at all is determined in it, as certainly nothing can be represented more simply than through the concept of a mere something. But the simplicity of the representation of a subject is on that account not a cognition of the simplicity of the subject itself, for its properties are abstracted from entirely when it is designated merely through the expression I, entirely empty as to content, (which I can apply to every thinking subject).",["kennen = be acquainted with; wissen (verb) = know; Eigenschaft rendered 'property' (not in the glossary; Beschaffenheit also 'property').","bestimmen = determine; Inhalt = content; Beweis = proof; Einfachheit = simplicity.","'desselben' (of it) after 'Eigenschaft' refers to the subject of inherence; 'ihm' likewise."]],"krv-a-0601":["So much is certain: that through the I I always think of an absolute but logical unity of the subject (simplicity), but not that I thereby cognize the actual simplicity of my subject. Just as the proposition: I am substance, signified nothing but the pure category, of which I can make no use (empirical) *in concreto*: so it is also permitted to me to say: I am a simple substance, i.e., one the representation of which never contains a synthesis of the manifold; but this concept, or also this proposition, teaches us not the least with regard to myself as an object of experience, because the concept of substance itself is used only as function of synthesis, without an underlying intuition, hence without object, and holds only of the condition of our cognition, but not of any object that could be given. We want to undertake an attempt on the supposed usefulness of this proposition.",["'wirkliche' = actual; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstand and Obiect both rendered 'object' (Gegenstand in 'Gegenstande' of experience and 'anzugebenden Gegenstande'; Obiect in 'ohne Obiect').","'Versuch anstellen' rendered 'undertake an attempt'; 'Brauchbarkeit' = usefulness."]],"krv-a-0602":["Everyone must admit: that the assertion of the simple nature of the soul is of some value only insofar as I can thereby distinguish this subject from all matter and consequently exempt it from the perishability to which this is always subject. The above proposition is also quite properly designed for this use, hence it is also for the most part expressed thus: the soul is not corporeal. If I can now show: that although one grants all objective validity to this cardinal proposition of the rational doctrine of the soul, in the pure meaning of a mere judgment of reason, (from pure categories), (everything that thinks is simple substance), nevertheless not the least use can be made of this proposition with regard to the dissimilarity, or kinship, of the same with matter: then this will be just as much as if I had relegated this supposed psychological insight to the field of mere ideas, which lack reality of objective use.",["Behauptung = assertion; Gültigkeit = validity; Gebrauch = use; Bedeutung = meaning; Realität = reality; Idee = idea; Materie = matter.","'derselben' (of the same) is ambiguous (the soul, or the proposition); kept as 'of the same'.","Hinfälligkeit rendered 'perishability'; Vernunfturteil = judgment of reason."]],"krv-a-0603":["We have incontestably proved in the transcendental aesthetic: that bodies are mere appearances of our outer sense, and not things in themselves. In accordance with this we can rightly say: that our thinking subject is not corporeal, that is: that, since it is represented by us as object of inner sense, it, insofar as it thinks, cannot be an object of outer senses, i.e., no appearance in space. This now wants to say as much as: thinking beings, as such, can never occur to us among outer appearances, or, we cannot intuit their thoughts, their consciousness, their desires etc. outwardly; for all this belongs before inner sense. In fact this argument also seems to be the natural and popular one, on which even the most common understanding seems to have hit from of old, and thereby very early on began to consider souls as beings entirely different from bodies.",["Erscheinung = appearance; Ding an sich selbst = thing in itself; Sinn = sense; Raum = space; Verstand = understanding; Beweis/bewiesen = proof/proved.","'gehört vor den innern Sinn': 'vor' rendered literally as 'before' (possibly 'to'); 'unterschiedene' rendered 'different' to keep 'distinct' (deutlich) for that term.","'Gegenstand' / 'Gegenstand des inneren Sinnes' and 'Cörper' (bodies) as in the source; 'anschauen' = intuit."]],"krv-a-0604":["Although now extension, impenetrability, interconnection and motion, in short everything that outer senses can only deliver to us, are not, or will not contain, thoughts, feeling, inclination or resolution, as these are in general no objects of outer intuition, yet that something which lies at the ground of outer appearances, which so affects our sense that it gets the representations of space, matter, shape etc., this something, considered as noumenon (or better, as transcendental object), could yet also at the same time be the subject of thoughts, although through the way in which our outer sense is thereby affected we get no intuition of representations, will etc., but merely of space and its determinations. But this something is not extended, not impenetrable, not composite, because all these predicates concern only sensibility and its intuition, so far as we are affected by such (otherwise unknown to us) objects. These expressions, however, do not at all give to cognize what sort of object it is, but only that, as one that is considered in itself without relation to outer senses, these predicates of outer appearances cannot be attributed to it. But the predicates of inner sense, representations and thinking, do not contradict it. Accordingly, even through the conceded simplicity of nature the human soul is not at all sufficiently distinguished from matter, if one considers it (as one should) merely as appearance, with regard to the substratum of the same.",["Gegenstand (and Object, Obiecten) = 'object' throughout; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Ausdehnung = 'extension'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Sinn = 'sense'.","'zum Grunde liegt' rendered literally 'lies at the ground of' (Grund = ground).","'zusammengesezt' = 'composite'; 'Art' = 'way' in 'durch die Art, wie'; 'zu erkennen geben' = 'give to cognize'.","'nicht Gedanken ... seyn, oder solche enthalten werden': the German is irregular in tense and mood ('be, or will contain'); kept as it stands.","Latin 'Noumenon' and 'Substrati' (genitive, here 'substratum') as in the German; 'als die überall keine ...' kept as a loosely attached clause.","'dessen Bestimmungen' / 'derselben': the antecedent of 'derselben' (Seele or Erscheinung) is ambiguous in the German and left so."]],"krv-a-0605":["Were matter a thing in itself, then as a composite being it would be wholly and entirely distinguished from the soul, as a simple one. Now, however, it is merely outer appearance, whose substratum is cognized through no predicates that can be indicated; consequently I can indeed assume of this that it is simple in itself, although in the way in which it affects our senses it produces in us the intuition of the extended and hence of the composite, and that therefore thoughts may inhere in itself in the substance to which extension pertains with regard to our outer sense, which can be represented with consciousness through its own inner sense. In such a way precisely the same thing that in one relation is called corporeal would in another at the same time be a thinking being, whose thoughts we cannot indeed intuit, but yet the signs of them in the appearance. Thereby the expression would fall away that only souls (as particular species of substances) think; it would rather be said, as usual, that human beings think, i.e., precisely the same thing which, as outer appearance, is extended, is inwardly (in itself) a subject that is not composite but simple and thinks.",["'Ding an sich selbst' = 'thing in itself'; 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'Art' = 'species' in 'besondere Arten von Substanzen'; 'Ausdehnung' = 'extension'.","'der Substanz, der ... zukomt': the clause 'daß ... der Substanz ... an sich selbst Gedanken beywohnen' is preserved with German word order; 'die ... vorgestellt werden können' could refer to Gedanken or to the substance's thoughts only; English 'which' is similarly loose.","'Ausdruck' = 'expression'; 'Bewustseyn' = 'consciousness'; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition' (verb anschauen = 'intuit')."]],"krv-a-0606":["But, without permitting such hypotheses, one can remark in general: that if by soul I understand a thinking being in itself, the question is in itself already unsuitable: whether namely it is of the same kind with matter (which is no thing in itself at all, but only a kind of representations in us) or not; for that already goes without saying, that a thing in itself is of another nature than the determinations that merely constitute its state.",["'Art' = 'kind' (both occurrences); 'verstehe' = 'understand'; 'Zustand' = 'state'; 'Bestimmungen' = 'determinations'.","'an sich schon unschicklich': 'an sich' could attach to 'die Frage' or be adverbial to 'unschicklich'; rendered 'in itself already unsuitable' to keep both."]],"krv-a-0607":["But if we compare the thinking I not with matter, but with the intelligible that lies at the ground of the outer appearance that we call matter: then, because we know nothing at all of the latter, we also cannot say: that the soul distinguishes itself inwardly from this in anything.",["'Intelligibelen' = 'the intelligible'; 'wissen' = 'know'; 'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the ground of'.","'irgend worin' = 'in anything' ('in any respect' is also possible); 'innerlich' = 'inwardly'."]],"krv-a-0608":["Thus the simple consciousness is accordingly no acquaintance with the simple nature of our subject, insofar as this is thereby to be distinguished from matter, as a composite being.",["'Kentniß' = 'acquaintance' (glossary Kenntnis), distinct from 'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'.","'dadurch' could refer to the simple consciousness or to the simple nature; left open."]],"krv-a-0609":["But if this concept is not fit for determining the peculiar and distinguishing feature of its nature in the sole case in which it is usable, namely in the comparison of myself with objects of outer experience, one may always pretend to know: the thinking I, the soul (a name for the transcendental object of inner sense) is simple; this expression nevertheless has no use at all extending to actual objects and can therefore not in the least expand our cognition.",["The German word order 'ihn ... das Eigenthümliche und Unterscheidende seiner Natur zu bestimmen' (with 'ihn' the concept) is slightly irregular; 'das Eigenthümliche und Unterscheidende' rendered 'the peculiar and distinguishing feature' (nominalized adjectives).","'Gebrauch' = 'use'; 'wirklich' = 'actual'; 'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; 'Ausdruck' = 'expression'; 'Gegenstand' = 'object'."]],"krv-a-0610":["Thus the whole of rational psychology accordingly falls with its chief support, and we can as little hope here as ever elsewhere to expand insights through mere concepts (still less, however, through the mere subjective form of all our concepts, consciousness), without relation to possible experience, especially since even the fundamental concept of a simple nature is of such a kind that it can be encountered in no experience at all, and consequently there is no path at all to arrive at it as an objectively valid concept.",["'Begriff' = 'concept'; 'obiectivgültig' = 'objectively valid'; 'Einsichten' = 'insights'; 'Bewustseyn' = 'consciousness'.","'überall keine' = 'in no ... at all'."]],"krv-a-0611":["Third Paralogism of Personality.",["'Paralogism' as in the German; 'Personalität' = 'personality'."]],"krv-a-0612":["What is conscious of the numerical identity of itself in different times is to that extent a person:",["'so fern' = 'to that extent' (also 'insofar'); 'sich ... bewust ist' = 'is conscious of'."]],"krv-a-0613":["Now the soul is etc.",["Abbreviated in the original ('etc.'); not completed."]],"krv-a-0614":["Therefore it is a person.",[]],"krv-a-0615":["Critique of the third paralogism of transcendental psychology.",["'Critik' = 'critique'."]],"krv-a-0616":["If I want to cognize the numerical identity of an outer object through experience, I will pay attention to the persistent of that appearance to which, as subject, everything else is related as determination, and note the identity of the former in the time during which the latter changes. Now, however, I am an object of inner sense and all time is merely the form of inner sense. Consequently I relate each and every one of my successive determinations to the numerically identical self, in all time, i.e., in the form of the inner intuition of myself. On this footing the personality of the soul would have to be regarded not even as inferred, but as a fully identical proposition of self-consciousness in time, and that is also the cause why it holds a priori. For it really says nothing more than: in the whole time in which I am conscious of myself, I am conscious of this time as belonging to the unity of my self, and it is all one whether I say: this whole time is in me, as individual unity, or: I am to be found, with numerical identity, in all this time.",["'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Beharrliche' = 'the persistent'; 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'; 'Selbstbewustseyn' = 'self-consciousness'; 'Satz' = 'proposition'; 'Einheit' = 'unity'; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'.","'succeßiven' = 'successive' (Latinate); 'a priori' left in Latin as in the German.","'ienem' / 'dieses' ('the former' / 'the latter'): the referents (the persistent vs. the appearance or its determinations) are ambiguous; kept so.","'Auf diesen Fuß' = 'On this footing'; 'geschlossen' = 'inferred' (cf. Schluß = inference)."]],"krv-a-0617":["The identity of the person is therefore unfailingly to be encountered in my own consciousness. But if I consider myself from the standpoint of another (as object of his outer intuition), then this outer observer first of all weighs me in time, for in apperception time is properly represented only in me. He will therefore from the I, which accompanies all representations at all times in my consciousness, and indeed with complete identity, even though he concedes it, nevertheless not yet infer the objective persistence of myself. For since then the time in which the observer posits me is not that which is encountered in my own, but that in his sensibility, the identity that is necessarily combined with my consciousness is not on that account combined with his, i.e., with the outer intuition of my subject.",["'Beharrlichkeit' = 'persistence'; 'Sinnlichkeit' = 'sensibility'; 'Vorstellung' = 'representation'; 'setzet' = 'posits'; 'verbunden' = 'combined'; 'Gegenstand' = 'object'.","'erwägt' rendered 'weighs'; 'Apperception' in the German spelling, English 'apperception'.","'in meiner eigenen' / 'in seiner Sinnlichkeit': 'meiner eigenen' lacks an explicit noun (Sinnlichkeit understood); kept elliptical.","'ob er es gleich einräumt': 'es' (the I or its identity) left as 'it'."]],"krv-a-0618":["Thus the identity of the consciousness of myself in different times is only a formal condition of my thoughts and their interconnection, but does not at all prove the numerical identity of my subject, in which, notwithstanding the logical identity of the I, there can nevertheless have occurred such a change as does not permit retaining its identity; although the I of the same sound can still always be assigned to it, which in every other state, even in the transformation of the subject, could nevertheless always preserve the thought of the preceding subject and so also hand it over to the following.*)",["The '*)' is the footnote marker of the original; the footnote itself is not in this passage.","'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'; 'Zustand' = 'state'; 'Bedingung' = 'condition'; 'Beweis' (beweiset) = 'prove'.","'gleichlautende Ich' = 'the I of the same sound' (literally 'equally sounding'); 'welches' could refer to the I or to the sound; left ambiguous.","'ohnerachtet' = 'notwithstanding'; 'Subiect' = 'subject'; the sentence's final clause is syntactically loose in the German and is kept so."]],"krv-a-0619":["*) An elastic ball that strikes an equal one in a straight direction communicates to the latter its whole motion, hence its whole state (if one looks merely to the places in space). Assume now, according to the analogy with such bodies, substances of which the one would instill into the other representations, together with their consciousness, then a whole series of them will let itself be thought, of which the first would communicate its state, together with its consciousness, to the second, this its own state, together with that of the previous substance, to the third, and this likewise the states of all the previous ones, together with its own and their consciousness. The last substance would thus be conscious of all the states of the substances changed before it as of its own, because those had been transferred into it together with the consciousness, and notwithstanding this, it would still not have been precisely the same person in all these states.",["Zustand = state; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Vorstellungen = representations; Substanzen = substances.","\"Nehmet ... an\" is the imperative (plural) and is kept as such; \"so wird sich ... denken lassen\" is rendered literally as \"will let itself be thought\".","The footnote sign *) is carried over as it stands; the passage is a footnote."]],"krv-a-0620":["Even if the proposition of some ancient schools, that everything is in flux and nothing in the world is persistent and abiding, cannot take place as soon as one assumes substances, it is still not refuted by the unity of self-consciousness. For we ourselves cannot judge from our consciousness about whether we as soul are persistent or not, because we count as belonging to our identical self only that of which we are conscious, and so must certainly necessarily judge that we are precisely the same in the whole time of which we are conscious. From the standpoint of a stranger, however, we cannot yet declare this valid, because, since we encounter in the soul no persistent appearance except merely the representation I, which accompanies and connects them all, so we can never make out whether this I (a mere thought) does not flow just as well as the remaining thoughts that are chained to one another by it.",["beharrlich = persistent; Selbstbewustseyn = self-consciousness; Erscheinung = appearance; urtheilen = judge; gültig = valid.","\"bleibend\" is rendered \"abiding\" to keep it distinct from \"beharrlich\" = persistent.","The construction \"weil, da ... so können wir\" is kept with its redundant \"so\"; \"dadurch\" (by it) is left without a stated antecedent, as in the German.","\"Standpuncte eines Fremden\" = \"standpoint of a stranger\"; \"vor gültig\" (= für gültig) read as \"valid\"."]],"krv-a-0621":["It is remarkable, however, that personality and its presupposition, persistence, hence the substantiality of the soul, must now first be proved. For if we could presuppose these, there would indeed not yet follow from this the continuance of consciousness, but still the possibility of a continuing consciousness in an abiding subject, which is already sufficient for personality, which, by its effect being perhaps interrupted for a time, does not itself cease right away. But this persistence is given to us by nothing before the numerical identity of our self, which we conclude from the identical apperception, but is rather first concluded from it (and upon this, if it went right, the concept of substance, which alone is empirically usable, would have to first follow). Since now this identity of the person does not at all follow from the identity of the I in the consciousness of all time in which I cognize myself: so the substantiality of the soul could not have been grounded on it above either.",["Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Fortdauer = continuance; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirkung = effect; Begriff = concept; erkenne = cognize.","\"folgeren\" (= folgern) is rendered \"conclude\"; \"folgen\" is rendered \"follow\", so the German play between the two is kept.","\"vor der numerischen Identität\" is read as \"before\" (= prior to); \"vor\" could also be read as \"for\"; the relative \"die\" after \"Persönlichkeit\" is left as ambiguous as the German.","The final sentence's colon and \"so\" are kept as in the German; \"hat ... gegründet werden können\" is rendered literally."]],"krv-a-0622":["Meanwhile, just like the concept of substance and of the simple, so too the concept of personality (insofar as it is merely transcendental, i.e., unity of the subject, which is otherwise unknown to us, but in whose determinations there is a thoroughgoing connection through apperception) can remain, and insofar this concept is also necessary and sufficient for practical use, but we can never count on it, as an extension of our self-cognition through pure reason, which holds out to us an uninterrupted continuance of the subject from the mere concept of the identical self, since this concept always turns around itself, and brings us further in regard to no single question that is laid out for synthetic cognition. What matter is as a thing in itself (transcendental object) is indeed entirely unknown to us; nevertheless its persistence as appearance, since it is represented as something external, can be observed. But since, when I want to observe the mere I during the change of all representations, I have no other *correlatum* of my comparisons than again myself, with the general conditions of my consciousness, I can give no other than tautological answers to all questions, in that I namely substitute my concept and its unity for the properties that pertain to me myself as object, and presuppose that which one demanded to know.",["Obiect = object; Erkentniß = cognition (Selbsterkentniß = self-cognition); Bestimmungen = determinations; Verknüpfung = connection; Gebrauch = use; Ding an sich selbst = thing in itself.","\"Staat machen\" (idiom) rendered \"count on\"; \"vorspiegelt\" rendered \"holds out to us\" (literally something like \"mirrors before us\").","\"Eigenschaften\" (not in the glossary) = \"properties\"; \"Beschaffenheit\" would also be \"property\".","\"Correlatum\" is Latin in the original; \"tavtologische\" = tautological; \"Wechsel\" = change; \"Wirklichkeit\" not present here.","\"auf ihn\" is ambiguous in reference only as the German is (the concept of personality); \"Mich selbst\" with capital M is not otherwise marked."]],"krv-a-0623":["The fourth paralogism of ideality. (of outer relation).",["Verhältnis = relation (the same English word is used for Beziehung in later passages)."]],"krv-a-0624":["That upon whose existence, only as a cause of given perceptions, it can be inferred, has only a doubtful existence:",["Daseyn and Existenz are both rendered \"existence\" (Daseyn in the first clause, Existenz in the second).","geschlossen werden = be inferred (Schluß = inference); Wahrnehmungen = perceptions; the opening construction (\"Dasjenige, auf dessen Daseyn ... geschlossen werden kan\") is kept as in the German.","The colon at the end marks the break after the major premise."]],"krv-a-0625":["Now all outer appearances are of the kind that their existence is not immediately perceived, but can be inferred solely to them, as the cause of given perceptions:",["Art = kind; Daseyn = existence; unmittelbar = immediately; Erscheinungen = appearances.","\"auf sie ... geschlossen werden\" is rendered literally as \"inferred to them\"."]],"krv-a-0626":["Thus the existence of all objects of outer senses is doubtful. This uncertainty I call the ideality of outer appearances, and the doctrine of this ideality is called idealism, in comparison with which the assertion of a possible certainty of objects of outer senses is called dualism.",["Gegenstände = objects; Lehre = doctrine; Behauptung = assertion; Daseyn = existence.","\"genent\" = \"called\" (nennen/heißen both \"call\")."]],"krv-a-0627":["Critique of the fourth paralogism of transcendental psychology.",[]],"krv-a-0628":["First we want to subject the premises to examination. We can rightly assert that only that which is in ourselves can be immediately perceived, and that my own existence alone can be the object of a mere perception. Thus the existence of an actual object outside me (if this word is taken in an intellectual meaning) is never given directly in the perception, but can only be added in thought to this, which is a modification of inner sense, as its outer cause, and hence inferred. Hence Cartesius too rightly restricted all perception in the narrowest meaning to the proposition: I (as a thinking being) am. It is namely clear: that, since the outer is not in me, I cannot encounter it in my apperception, hence also in no perception, which is properly only the determination of apperception.",["Gegenstand = object; Wirklichen = actual; Bedeutung = meaning; Satz = proposition; Wesen = being; Bestimmung = determination; Wahrnehmung = perception; Existenz and Daseyn both \"existence\".","\"gerade zu\" rendered \"directly\"; \"hinzu gedacht\" rendered \"added in thought\".","\"welche eine Modification des inneren Sinnes ist\": \"welche\" could refer to \"Wahrnehmung\" or (less naturally) another noun; read as the perception, as the German allows only a loose reading; the ambiguity is not resolved in the English.","\"Cartesius\" kept as in the original; \"Apperception\" as apperception."]],"krv-a-0629":["I can thus properly not perceive outer things, but only infer to their existence from my inner perception, in that I regard this as the effect for which something outer is the nearest cause. Now, however, the inference from a given effect to a determinate cause is always uncertain; because the effect can have arisen from more than one cause. Accordingly in the relation of perception to its cause it always remains doubtful whether this is inner or outer, whether thus all so-called outer perceptions are not a mere play of our inner sense, or whether they relate to outer actual objects as their cause. At least the existence of the latter is only inferred, and runs the danger of all inferences, whereas the object of inner sense (I myself with all my representations) is immediately perceived, and the existence of it suffers no doubt at all.",["Schluß/schließen = inference/infer; Wirkung = effect; Ursache = cause; Beziehung = relation (Verhältnis in 0623 is also \"relation\"); bestimt = determinate; Vorstellungen = representations.","\"Daseyn\" and \"Existenz\" are both \"existence\".","\"ob diese innerlich, oder äusserlich sey\": \"diese\" (this) is left without a noun, as in the German; it may refer to the cause (Ursache) or the perception."]],"krv-a-0630":["By an idealist one must thus not understand him who denies the existence of outer objects of the senses, but who only does not concede that it is cognized through immediate perception, but infers from this that we can never become fully certain of their actuality through all possible experience.",["verstehen = understand; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Erfahrung = experience; erkant = cognized; Daseyn = existence; Gegenstände = objects.","\"daß es ... erkant werde\": \"es\" is left as \"it\", as in the German (the existence); \"ihrer\" refers to the objects."]],"krv-a-0631":["Before I now present our paralogism in its deceptive illusion, I must first remark that one must necessarily distinguish a twofold idealism, the transcendental and the empirical. By transcendental idealism of all appearances, however, I understand the doctrinal concept according to which we regard them all together as mere representations, and not as things in themselves, and in accordance with which time and space are only sensible forms of our intuition, but not determinations given for themselves, or conditions of objects as things in themselves. To this idealism a transcendental realism is opposed, which regards time and space as something given in itself (independently of our sensibility). The transcendental realist therefore represents outer appearances (if one concedes their actuality) as things in themselves, which exist independently of us and our sensibility, and thus would also be outside us according to pure concepts of the understanding. It is this transcendental realist who actually plays the empirical idealist afterwards, and, after he has falsely presupposed about objects of the senses that, if they are to be outer ones, they would have to have their existence in themselves even without senses, finds in this point of view all our representations of the senses insufficient to make the actuality of the same certain.",["Obiecte (Objecte) and Gegenstände are both rendered 'object'; here 'Obiecte' (conditions of the objects) is the Latinate member, 'Gegenständen' occurs in the last sentence.","Lehrbegriff rendered 'doctrinal concept' (literal compound of Lehre and Begriff).","Wirklichkeit = 'actuality' throughout; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Vorstellung = 'representation'; Anschauung = 'intuition'.","'vor sich gegebene' (= für sich gegeben) rendered 'given for themselves'; 'vor' is the 1781 spelling of 'für'.","'in diesem Gesichtspuncte' ('in this point of view') could attach to 'findet ... unzureichend' as rendered; German word order is kept.","'die Wirklichkeit derselben': 'derselben' refers to the representations (Vorstellungen); kept as 'the same'."]],"krv-a-0632":["The transcendental idealist, by contrast, can be an empirical realist, hence, as one calls him, a dualist, i.e., concede the existence of matter without going beyond mere self-consciousness, and assume anything more than the certainty of the representations in me, hence the *cogito, ergo sum*. For because he lets this matter and even its inner possibility count merely as appearance, which, separated from our sensibility, is nothing: so for him it is only a species of representations (intuition), which are called outer, not as if they related to objects that are outer in themselves, but because they relate perceptions to space, in which everything is outside one another, but space itself is in us.",["'vor Erscheinung' = 'für Erscheinung' (1781 spelling), rendered 'as appearance'.","Art = 'species' here ('a species of representations'); elsewhere Art could be 'kind' or 'way'.","'sie' in 'so ist sie bey ihm' refers to 'Materie' (feminine singular); 'welche äusserlich heissen' is plural, agreeing with 'Vorstellungen'. Kept.","'Selbstbewustseyn' = 'self-consciousness'; 'Wahrnehmungen' = 'perceptions'.","'die, von unserer Sinnlichkeit abgetrent, nichts ist': the relative clause's antecedent could be Erscheinung (as rendered) or, less naturally, matter; kept ambiguous as far as English allows."]],"krv-a-0633":["For this transcendental idealism we have already declared ourselves at the beginning. Thus with our doctrinal concept all scruple falls away about assuming the existence of matter just so on the testimony of our mere self-consciousness and declaring it thereby proved, like the existence of myself as a thinking being. For I am indeed conscious of my representations; thus these exist and I myself, who have these representations. Now, however, outer objects (the bodies) are mere appearances, hence also nothing other than a species of my representations, whose objects are something only through these representations, but, separated from them, are nothing. Thus outer things exist just as well as I myself exist, and indeed both on the immediate testimony of my self-consciousness, only with the difference that the representation of my self, as of the thinking subject, is related merely to inner sense, but the representations, which designate extended beings, are also related to outer sense. In regard to the actuality of outer objects I have just as little need to infer, as in respect of the actuality of the object of my inner sense (my thoughts), for both are nothing but representations, whose immediate perception (consciousness) is at the same time a sufficient proof of their actuality.",["'Vor diesen' = 'Für diesen' (1781 spelling), rendered 'For this'.","'Gegenstand' and 'Object'/'Subiect' : Gegenstände = 'objects' throughout; 'Subiect' (Latinate) = 'subject'.","'Wesen' = 'being(s)'; 'ausgedehnte' relates to Ausdehnung = extension.","'in Absicht auf' rendered 'in regard to' and 'in Ansehung' 'in respect of'; 'Absicht' is otherwise 'aim; intention'.","'auf das Zeugniß ... anzunehmen und dadurch vor bewiesen zu erklären': 'vor' = 'für', 'declared proved'.","'schliessen' = 'infer' (Schluß = inference); 'Beweis' = 'proof'; 'genugsamer' = 'sufficient' (not zureichend, but rendered alike)."]],"krv-a-0634":["Thus the transcendental idealist is an empirical realist and grants to matter, as appearance, an actuality that need not be inferred, but is immediately perceived. Transcendental realism, on the other hand, necessarily comes into embarrassment, and sees itself compelled to make room for empirical idealism, because it regards the objects of outer senses as something different from the senses themselves, and mere appearances as self-subsisting beings that are found outside us; where then, to be sure, with our best consciousness of our representation of these things, it is still far from certain that, if the representation exists, the object corresponding to it also exists; whereas in our system these outer things, namely matter, in all its shapes and alterations, are nothing but mere appearances, i.e., representations in us, of whose actuality we become immediately conscious.",["'vor Etwas' and 'vor selbstständige Wesen' = 'für ...' (1781 spelling): 'as something', 'as self-subsisting beings'.","'gestehet ... zu' rendered 'grants'; 'geschlossen werden darf' = 'need not be inferred' (literally 'may not be inferred'; 'darf' with negation is kept ambiguous only in the German; the literal rendering would be 'may not be inferred').","'Veränderungen' = 'alterations'; 'Gestalten' = 'shapes'.","'correspondirende' = 'corresponding'."]],"krv-a-0635":["Since now, as far as I know, all psychologists who adhere to empirical idealism are transcendental realists, they have, to be sure, proceeded quite consistently in conceding great importance to empirical idealism, as to one of the problems from which human reason would hardly know how to help itself. For in fact, if one regards outer appearances as representations that are effected in us by their objects, as things that are in themselves outside us, then it is not to be seen how one could cognize the existence of these otherwise than through the inference from the effect to the cause, in which it must always remain doubtful whether the latter is in us or outside us. Now one can indeed concede that something that may be outside us in the transcendental sense is the cause of our outer intuitions, but this is not the object that we understand by the representations of matter and corporeal things; for these are merely appearances, i.e., mere ways of representing, which are always found only in us, and whose actuality rests on immediate consciousness just like the consciousness of my own thoughts. The transcendental object is, with regard to inner as well as outer intuition, equally unknown. Of it, however, there is also no talk, but of the empirical one, which is then called an outer one if it is represented in space, and an inner object if it is represented merely in temporal relation; but space and time are both to be encountered only in us.",["'Problemen' = 'problems' (cf. Aufgabe = problem, a different German word, not used here).","'Gegenstand' = 'object' throughout, including 'transcendentale Gegenstand'; 'Vorstellungsarten' = 'ways of representing' (Art = 'way').","'im transscendentalen Verstande' rendered 'in the transcendental sense'; 'Verstand' here is the older 'Verstande' = sense/meaning, not 'understanding' as faculty; the ambiguity with 'understanding' is noted. In the next passage 'Sinne' occurs for 'transscendentalen Sinne'.","'Schluß' = 'inference'; 'Wirkung' = 'effect'; 'Ursache' = 'cause'.","'dieser ihr Daseyn': 'dieser' refers to the appearances/representations; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'.","'nicht abzusehen' rendered 'not to be seen'; 'Zeitverhältnisse' = 'temporal relation'.","'die Rede' ('talk'): 'Von ihm aber ist auch nicht die Rede' kept literal."]],"krv-a-0636":["Since in the meantime the expression: outside us, carries with it an unavoidable ambiguity, in that it signifies now something that exists as a thing in itself distinguished from us, now what belongs merely to outer appearance, we want, in order to put this concept in the latter signification, as that in which the psychological question about the reality of our outer intuition is properly taken, out of uncertainty, to distinguish empirically outer objects from those that might be so called in the transcendental sense by calling them straight out things that are to be encountered in space.",["'Ausdruck' = 'expression'; 'bedeuten / Bedeutung' = 'signify / signification' (glossary: 'significance; meaning'; 'signification' used for Bedeutung).","'Realität' = 'reality' (here), distinct from 'Wirklichkeit' = 'actuality' elsewhere.","'in der lezteren Bedeutung, als in welcher ...' and 'ausser Unsicherheit zu setzen': the infinitive's attachment ('this concept') kept as in German; 'taken' (genommen) could attach to the question or the concept.","'empirisch äusserliche Gegenstände': 'empirisch' could modify 'äusserliche' (as rendered) or the whole phrase; kept ambiguous.","'gerade zu' rendered 'straight out' (= geradezu, 'directly').","'dadurch ... daß' construction kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0637":["Space and time are, to be sure, representations *a priori*, which dwell with us as forms of our sensible intuition, before an actual object has yet determined our sense through sensation, in order to represent it under those sensible relations. But this material or real, this something that is to be intuited in space, necessarily presupposes perception, and independently of this, which indicates the actuality of something in space, can be fabricated and produced by no imagination. Sensation is therefore that which designates an actuality in space and time, according as it is related to the one or the other way of sensible intuition. Once sensation is given, (which, if it is applied to an object in general without determining it, is called perception,) then through the manifoldness of the same many an object can be fabricated in imagination, which outside imagination has no empirical place in space or time. This is undoubtedly certain, one may take the sensations, pleasure and pain, or also those of the outer, as colors, warmth etc., so perception is that through which the material, in order to think objects of sensible intuition, must first be given. This perception therefore represents, (so that we remain this time only with outer intuitions) something actual in space. For first, perception is the representation of an actuality, just as space is the representation of a mere possibility of being together. Second, this actuality is represented before outer sense, i.e., in space. Third, space itself is nothing other than mere representation, consequently only that can count as actual in it which is represented*) in it, and conversely, what is given in it, i.e., represented through perception, is also actual in it; for if it were not actual in it, i.e., given immediately through empirical intuition, it also could not be invented, because one cannot think up the real of intuitions at all *a priori*.",["Wirklichkeit/wirklich = actuality/actual; Realität = reality; das Reale = the real; das Materielle = the material (Stoff = material, Materie = matter).","beywohnen: rendered literally 'dwell with'; 'um ihn ... vorzustellen' is ambiguous as to whether 'ihn' is the sense (Sinn) or the object (Gegenstand); kept ambiguous.","dichten/erdichten = fabricate/invent; erdenken = think up; Einbildungskraft = imagination, Einbildung also 'imagination'; Stelle (place) and Ort share 'place'.","Mannigfaltigkeit rendered 'manifoldness' (glossary gives Mannigfaltige = manifold); 'derselben' (feminine sing.) refers to Empfindung.","'der äusseren' is a bare genitive in the German, left as 'of the outer'; the 'so' after the 'man mag nun' clause is kept.","mithin = consequently; Beysammenseyn = being together; gelten = count as. The '*)' marks the footnote (krv-a-0638)."]],"krv-a-0638":["*) One must well note this paradoxical but correct proposition: that nothing is in space except what is represented in it. For space is itself nothing other than representation, consequently what is in it must be contained in the representation, and in space there is nothing at all, except insofar as it is actually represented in it. A proposition that must indeed sound strange: that a thing can exist only in the representation of it, but which here loses what is offensive, because the things with which we have to do are not things in themselves, but only appearances, i.e., representations.",["Sache (Sachen) and Ding (Dinge) both appear; both rendered 'thing'.","Satz = proposition; folglich = consequently; Erscheinungen = appearances; Dinge an sich = things in themselves.","'wol merken' rendered 'well note'; 'das anstößige' = 'what is offensive'."]],"krv-a-0639":["All outer perception therefore proves immediately something actual in space, or is rather the actual itself and insofar empirical realism is therefore beyond doubt, i.e., something actual in space corresponds to our outer intuitions. To be sure space itself, with all its appearances, as representations, is only in me, but in this space nevertheless the real, or the material of all objects of outer intuition, is given actually and independently of all fabrication, and it is also impossible: that anything outside us (in the transcendental sense) should be given in this space, because space itself is nothing outside our sensibility. Hence the strictest idealist cannot demand that one should prove: that the object outside us (in strict meaning) corresponds to our perception. For if there were anything of the kind, it still could not be represented and intuited as outside us, because this presupposes space, and the actuality in space, as a mere representation, is nothing other than perception itself. The real of outer appearances is therefore actual only in perception and can be actual in no other way.",["'in stricter Bedeutung' appears in the text as printed (possibly a Latinizing form or an OCR slip for 'strictem'); rendered 'in strict meaning'.","Wirklichkeit/wirklich = actuality/actual; Realismus kept as 'realism'; correspondirt = corresponds; ausser uns = outside us.","'Also' rendered 'Hence' in the sentence on the idealist; 'es' in 'es correspondirt' is rendered 'something ... corresponds' following the German construction."]],"krv-a-0640":["From perceptions cognition of objects can now be generated, either through a mere play of imagination, or also by means of experience. And there deceptive representations can indeed arise, to which the objects do not correspond and in which the deception is to be attributed now to a dazzlement of imagination, (in a dream) now to a misstep of the power of judgment (in the so-called fraud of the senses). In order now to escape the false illusion in this, one proceeds according to the rule: What is interconnected with a perception according to empirical laws is actual. But this deception as well as the safeguard against it concerns idealism just as much as dualism, since it is here only a matter of the form of experience. To refute empirical idealism, as a false scruple regarding the objective reality of our outer perceptions, it is already sufficient: that outer perception proves immediately an actuality in space, which space, although in itself it is only mere form of representations, nevertheless has objective reality with regard to all outer appearances (which are also nothing other than mere representations); likewise: that without perception even fabrication and dream are not possible, our outer senses therefore, according to the *datis* from which experience can arise, have their actual corresponding objects in space.",["Täuschung = deception; Betrug = fraud; Schein = illusion; Blendwerk = 'dazzlement' (literal sense of the compound).","Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Erkentniß = cognition; Erfahrung = experience; zusammenhängen = be interconnected (Zusammenhang = interconnection).","hinreichend rendered 'sufficient' (glossary gives zureichend = sufficient; the same English word thus stands for both).","'Idealism' and 'Dualism' are in the German in Latinate spelling; *datis* is Latin in the German text.","'beweise' is subjunctive in the German (indirect statement); rendered 'proves'. Bedenklichkeit = scruple."]],"krv-a-0641":["The dogmatic idealist would be the one who denies the existence of matter, the skeptical one, who doubts it, because he holds it to be unprovable. The former can be so only because he believes he finds contradictions in the possibility of a matter in general and with this we do not yet have to do now. The following section on dialectical inferences, which presents reason in its inner conflict with regard to the concepts that it represents to itself of the possibility of that which belongs in the interconnection of experience, will also remedy this difficulty. But the skeptical idealist, who only looks at the ground of our assertion and declares our persuasion of the existence of matter, which we believe to ground on immediate perception, to be insufficient, is insofar a benefactor of human reason, as he constrains us, even in the smallest step of common experience, to open our eyes well, and not to take up immediately into our possession as well-acquired what we perhaps only obtain by stealth. The benefit that these idealistic objections create here falls clearly into the eyes now. They drive us with force to this, if we do not want to entangle ourselves in our most common assertions, to regard all perceptions, whether they may be called inner or outer, merely as a consciousness of that which adheres to our sensibility and its outer objects not as things in themselves, but only as representations, of which we can become immediately conscious, like every other representation, but which are called outer because they adhere to that sense which we call outer sense, whose intuition is space, which however is itself nothing other than an inner way of representing, in which certain perceptions connect with one another.",["Daseyn = existence (Existenz would also be 'existence'); Materie = matter; unerweislich rendered 'unprovable' (Beweis = proof).","'die sich ... vorstellt': the relative 'die' and singular verb leave the subject ambiguous (Vernunft, or the Abschnitt); 'it' in 'that it represents to itself' preserves this; the 'von' phrase is kept literal.","Behauptung = assertion; Ueberredung = persuasion; erklären = declare; unzureichend = insufficient; 'die wir ... zu gründen glauben': 'which' could refer to Materie or Ueberredung; kept ambiguous.","erschleichen = obtain by stealth; wolerworben = well-acquired; anhängen = adhere; 'vor' in the old spelling = 'für' ('for'), rendered 'to be' / 'as' per construction ('vor Dinge an sich selbst' = 'as things in themselves').","Vorstellungsart = 'way of representing' (Art = way); verknüpfen = connect; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Schlüsse = inferences."]],"krv-a-0642":["If we let outer objects count as things in themselves, then it is absolutely impossible to comprehend how we should arrive at the cognition of their actuality outside us, since we rely merely on the representation that is in us. For one cannot sense outside oneself, but only in oneself, and the whole self-consciousness therefore yields nothing except merely our own determinations. Thus skeptical idealism compels us to take the only refuge that remains to us, namely to the ideality of all appearances, which we have set forth in the transcendental aesthetic independently of these consequences, which we could not foresee at that time. If one now asks whether, in accordance with this, dualism alone takes place in the doctrine of the soul, the answer is: By all means! but only in the empirical sense, i.e. in the interconnection of experience matter is actually given, as substance in appearance, to the outer sense, just as the thinking I, likewise as substance in appearance, is given before the inner sense, and according to the rules which this category brings into the interconnection of our outer as well as inner perceptions into one experience, appearances on both sides must also be connected among themselves. But if one wanted to extend the concept of dualism, as usually happens, and take it in the transcendental sense, then neither it, nor pneumatism, opposed to it on one side, or materialism on the other side, would have not the least ground, since one would then miss the determination of one's concepts, and hold the difference in the way of representing objects, which remain unknown to us as to what they are in themselves, for a difference of these things themselves. I, represented through the inner sense in time, and objects in space, outside me, are indeed skeptically wholly different appearances, but they are not thereby thought as different things. The transcendental object, which to the outer appearances, likewise that which to the inner intuition lies at the ground, is neither matter, nor a thinking being in itself, but a ground of the appearances unknown to us, which furnish the empirical concept of the first as well as of the second kind.",["Gegenstand and Object (Obiect) are both rendered 'object'; 'Gegenstände' occurs in the first passage and 'Obiect' in the last sentence.","Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Wahrnehmung = 'perception'.","'im empirischen Verstande' / 'im transscendentalen Verstande': Verstand here in the idiom 'in the ... sense'; rendered 'sense', which is not the glossary 'sense (n.)' = Sinn; the glossary 'understanding' is the other possible rendering.","'sceptisch' rendered 'skeptically' (adverbial/predicate use as in the original); its attachment ('skeptically different' vs. different for the skeptic) is left open.","'Folgen' (in 'diesen Folgen') rendered 'consequences' rather than the glossary 'sequel; sequence', since it clearly means results here.","'nicht den mindesten Grund': the double negative ('neither ... nor ... would have not the least ground') is kept as in the German.","The last sentence is grammatically irregular in the German ('welches den äusseren Erscheinungen, imgleichen das, was der innern Anschauung zum Grunde liegt'); kept without emendation.","'Pnevmatismus' = 'pneumatism'; 'Art' in 'Vorstellungsart' = 'way', and in 'ersten sowol als zweiten Art' = 'kind'."]],"krv-a-0643":["If we therefore, as the present critique evidently compels us to do, remain true to the rule established above, not to push our questions further than as far as possible experience can furnish us the object of the same: then we will not even let it occur to us to institute an inquiry about the objects of our senses concerning that which they may be in themselves, i.e. without any relation to the senses. But if the psychologist takes appearances for things in themselves, then whether as materialist he admits into his doctrinal concept solely and only matter, or as spiritualist merely thinking beings (namely according to the form of our inner sense), or as dualist both, as things existing for themselves, he is nevertheless always held up by misunderstanding about the way to rationalize how that might exist in itself which is nevertheless no thing in itself, but only the appearance of a thing in general.",["Obiect and Gegenstände both 'object'.","Lehrbegriff rendered 'doctrinal concept' (Lehre = doctrine; Begriff = concept).","'vernünfteln' = 'rationalize'; 'Art' = 'way'; 'Mißverstand hingehalten' (held up/put off) is kept close to the German and is somewhat obscure.","'so ist er doch immer' follows the long 'wenn/ob'-clause as in the German; the syntax (a concessive 'mag ... aufnehmen, so ist er doch') is preserved."]],"krv-a-0644":["Consideration on the sum total of pure doctrine of the soul, in accordance with these paralogisms.",["Betrachtung = 'consideration'; Summe rendered 'sum total' (cf. Inbegriff in the glossary, which is a different word).","'Zu Folge' = 'in accordance with'; Seelenlehre = 'doctrine of the soul'."]],"krv-a-0645":["If we compare the doctrine of the soul, as the physiology of inner sense, with the doctrine of bodies, as a physiology of the objects of outer senses: then we find, besides that in both much can be cognized empirically, nevertheless this remarkable difference, that in the latter science much can nevertheless be cognized a priori, from the mere concept of an extended impenetrable being, but in the former, from the concept of a thinking being, nothing at all can be cognized a priori synthetically. The cause is this. Although both are appearances, the appearance before the outer sense nevertheless has something standing or abiding, which furnishes a substratum lying at the ground of the changeable determinations and hence a synthetic concept, namely that of space and of an appearance in it, whereas time, which is the sole form of our inner intuition, has nothing abiding, hence gives to be cognized only the alternation of determinations, but not the determinable object. For in that which we call soul everything is in continual flux and nothing abiding, except perhaps (if one absolutely wants it) the I, so simple only because this representation has no content, hence no manifold, on account of which it also seems to represent, or better said, to designate, a simple object. This I would have to be an intuition, which, since it would be presupposed in thinking in general (before all experience), as intuition a priori would yield synthetic propositions, if it were to be possible to bring about a pure rational cognition of the nature of a thinking being in general. But this I is as little intuition as concept of any object, but rather the mere form of consciousness, which can accompany both kinds of representations, and thereby elevate them to cognitions, insofar, namely, as something else besides is given in intuition, which offers material for a representation of an object. Thus the whole of rational psychology falls, as a science surpassing all powers of human reason, and nothing remains to us except to study our soul on the guideline of experience and to keep ourselves within the limitations of the questions, which go no further than possible inner experience can lay out their content.",["Gegenstand/Obiect = 'object'; Wesen = 'being'; Erkentniß/erkennen = 'cognition/cognize'; Satz = 'proposition' (Sätze).","'Sinn' (äusseren Sinne, inneren Sinnes) = 'sense'; 'Cörperlehre' = 'doctrine of bodies'.","'Kräfte' = 'powers' (glossary: Kraft = force; power); 'Schranken' = 'limitations'; 'Stoff' = 'material'; 'Inhalt' = 'content'; 'Mannigfaltiges' = 'manifold'.","'a priori synthetisch' is kept as in the German, with the adverb left to attach to 'erkant' ('cognized a priori synthetically'); 'a priori' printed in roman type in the original is not italicized here.","'Leitfaden' = 'guideline'; 'Wechsel' = 'alternation'; 'bezeichnen' = 'designate'.","'als eine, alle Kräfte ... übersteigende Wissenschaft': Wissenschaft = 'science'; the clause 'Also fällt ... und es bleibt' is kept as one sentence.","The last clause ('die nicht weiter gehen, als mögliche innere Erfahrung ihren Inhalt darlegen kan') is kept as in the German; 'ihren' could refer back to 'Fragen' or be read with 'Erfahrung'."]],"krv-a-0646":["But although it has no use as an expanding cognition, but as such is composed of sheer paralogisms, one cannot nevertheless deny it an important negative use if it is to count for nothing more than a critical treatment of our dialectical inferences, and indeed of the common and natural reason.",["Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Schlüsse = 'inferences'; Vernunft = 'reason'; gemein = 'common'.","'sie' refers back to the doctrine of the soul (Seelenlehre) in the preceding passages; the German 'zugleich' constructions are not altered.","The word order of 'ob sie nun aber gleich' is rendered literally."]],"krv-a-0647":["For what do we indeed need a doctrine of the soul grounded merely on pure principles of reason? Without doubt preeminently with the aim of securing our thinking self against the danger of materialism. But the rational concept of our thinking self that we have given accomplishes this. For far from any fear remaining in accordance with it, that if one took matter away, all thinking and even the existence of thinking beings would thereby be canceled, it is rather clearly shown: that if I take away the thinking subject, the whole corporeal world must fall away, as that which is nothing but the appearance in the sensibility of our subject and a kind of representations of it.",["Absicht = 'aim'; Grundsatz/principles: 'Vernunftprincipien' rendered 'principles of reason'; Dasein/Existenz: 'Existenz' = 'existence'.","'Vernunftbegriff' = 'rational concept' (literally 'concept of reason'); Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'.","'Cörperwelt' = 'corporeal world'; 'eine Art Vorstellungen' = 'a kind of representations' (Art = way; kind; manner).","'weit gefehlt, daß ... so wird vielmehr' is kept as one sentence with its irregular construction."]],"krv-a-0648":["Thereby I do indeed cognize this thinking self no better as to its properties, nor can I have insight into its persistence, indeed not even the independence of its existence from the possible transcendental substratum of outer appearances, for this is unknown to me, just as much as that. But since it is nevertheless possible that I might take cause from elsewhere than from merely speculative grounds to hope for a self-subsisting existence of my thinking nature, persisting through all possible alternation of my state, much is thereby already gained, with the free admission of my own ignorance, to be able nevertheless to repel the dogmatic attacks of a speculative opponent, and to show him: that he could never know more of the nature of my subject, in order to deny possibility to my expectations, than I, in order to hold to them.",["Eigenschaften = 'properties' (glossary gives 'property' for Beschaffenheit; Eigenschaft is not listed); Beharrlichkeit = 'persistence'; beharrlich = 'persisting'.","'Existenz' = 'existence'; Zustand = 'state'; Grund/Gründe = 'ground(s)'; Unwissenheit = 'ignorance'; wissen = 'know'.","'Ursache' rendered 'cause'; 'einsehen' = 'have insight into'; 'selbstständige' = 'self-subsisting'.","'dieses ... eben sowol als ienes' : 'this' refers to the transcendental substratum and 'that' to the thinking self (or vice versa by proximity); left as in the German.","The passage ends at the end of a sentence; the clause 'um meinen Erwartungen die Möglichkeit abzusprechen, als ich, um mich an ihnen zu halten' is elliptical and kept so."]],"krv-a-0649":["On this transcendental illusion of our psychological concepts there are then further grounded three dialectical questions, which make up the proper goal of rational psychology, and can be decided nowhere else than through the above investigations: namely 1) concerning the possibility of the community of the soul with an organic body, i.e., the animality and the state of the soul in the life of the human being, 2) concerning the beginning of this community, i.e., of the soul in and before the birth of the human being, 3) the end of this community, i.e., of the soul in and after the death of the human being (the question concerning immortality).",["Schein = illusion; Gemeinschaft = community; Zustand = state; Möglichkeit = possibility; Begriffe = concepts.","Seele rendered 'soul'; Cörper 'body'; Anfange 'beginning'; Ende 'end' (not Zweck).","'Ziel' rendered 'goal' (not in glossary).","The construction in items 2) and 3) is elliptical in the German ('dem Ende', in the dative where 'vom' might be expected); kept as it stands."]],"krv-a-0650":["I now assert: that all the difficulties that one believes one finds in these questions, and with which, as dogmatic objections, one seeks to give oneself the appearance of a deeper insight into the nature of things than the common understanding may well have, rest on a mere delusion, according to which one hypostatizes what exists merely in thoughts, and assumes it, in precisely the same quality, as an actual object outside the thinking subject, namely taking extension, which is nothing but appearance, for a property of outer things subsisting even without our sensibility, and motion for their effect, which also actually goes on in itself outside our senses. For matter, whose community with the soul arouses such great concern, is nothing other than a mere form, or a certain way of representing an unknown object, through that intuition which one calls the outer sense. There may well, then, be something outside us to which this appearance, which we call matter, corresponds; but, in the same quality as appearance it is not outside us, but merely as a thought in us, although this thought, through the named sense, represents it as located outside us. Matter therefore does not signify a kind of substances so entirely different from and heterogeneous to the object of inner sense (soul), but only the dissimilarity of the appearances of objects (which are unknown to us in themselves), whose representations we call outer, in comparison with those that we count to inner sense, although they belong just as much merely to the thinking subject as all the remaining thoughts, only that they have this deceptive feature about them: that, since they represent objects in space, they detach themselves, as it were, from the soul and seem to hover outside it, although space itself, in which they are intuited, is nothing but a representation, whose counterpart in the same quality can not be encountered at all outside the soul. Now the question is no longer about the community of the soul with other known and foreign-natured substances outside us, but merely about the connection of the representations of inner sense with the modifications of our outer sensibility, and how these may be connected with one another according to constant laws, so that they cohere in one experience.",["Gegenstand = object throughout; no Object occurs here.","Erscheinung = appearance; Vorstellung = representation; Anschauung = intuition; Ausdehnung = extension; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Verknüpfung = connection; Wirkung = effect; Gesetze = laws; Erfahrung = experience.","hypostasirt kept as 'hypostatizes'; Qualität rendered 'quality'; Subiecte 'subject'; Modificationen 'modifications'.","'bedeutet' = 'signify' (glossary).","'Vorstellungsart' rendered 'way of representing'; 'Art' in 'Art von Substanzen' rendered 'kind'.","'sich ... ablösen ... zu schweben scheinen' and 'es' in 'vorstellt' (referring to the thought/appearance) kept as in German.","'kan' (for 'kann') in 'angetroffen werden kan' is rendered 'can not' as in the original spelling's sense; 'unbekant' = 'unknown'.","'wol' rendered 'well'/'just as much' according to context ('eben sowol' = 'just as much')."]],"krv-a-0651":["So long as we hold together inner and outer appearances, as mere representations in experience, we find nothing absurd and nothing that would make the community of both kinds of sense strange. But as soon as we hypostatize the outer appearances, no longer as representations, but, in the same quality as they are in us, also as things subsisting for themselves outside us, but relate their actions, which they show as appearances in relation to one another, to our thinking subject, we then have a character of efficient causes outside us that will not rhyme together with their effects in us, because the former relates merely to outer senses, but the latter to inner sense, which, although they are united in one subject, are nevertheless highly dissimilar. There we then have no other outer effects than changes of place, and no forces than mere strivings, which run out into relations in space as their effects. In us, however, the effects are thoughts, among which no relation of place, motion, shape, or determination of space in general takes place, and we entirely lose the thread of causes in the effects that were supposed to show themselves from them in inner sense. But we should consider: that bodies are not objects in themselves that are present to us, but a mere appearance, who knows of what unknown object, that motion is not the effect of this unknown cause, but merely the appearance of its influence on our senses, that consequently both are not something outside us, but merely representations in us, hence that it is not the motion of matter that effects representations in us, but that it itself (hence also the matter that makes itself cognizable through it) is a mere representation, and finally the whole self-made difficulty runs out into this: how and through what cause the representations of our sensibility stand in combination with one another in such a way that those which we call outer intuitions can be represented, according to empirical laws, as objects outside us, which question now contains not at all the supposed difficulty of explaining the origin of the representations from entirely foreign-natured efficient causes located outside us, in that we take the appearances of an unknown cause for the cause outside us, which can occasion nothing but confusion. In judgments in which a misinterpretation rooted by long habit occurs, it is impossible to bring the correction at once to that comprehensibility which can be demanded in other cases, where no such unavoidable illusion confuses the concept. Hence this our liberation of reason from sophistical theories will hardly yet have the distinctness that is necessary for its complete satisfaction.",["'Verbindung' (combination) and 'Verknüpfung' (connection, in preceding passage) are distinguished; 'Zusammenhang' not used, 'zusammenhängen' rendered 'cohere' (A 385).","'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Anschauungen' = intuitions; 'Urtheilen' = judgments; 'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness; 'Illusion' = illusion (Latinate; Schein elsewhere also 'illusion').","'wirkende Ursachen' = 'efficient causes'; 'Wirkung' = effect; 'wirke' = 'effects' (verb).","'sich mit ... nicht zusammen reimen will': literal 'will not rhyme together with'.","'Character' kept as 'character'; 'Kräfte' = forces; 'Bestrebungen' = strivings.","'kennbar' = 'cognizable' is a choice for kennbar; 'kennen' is in the glossary as 'know; be acquainted with', so 'cognizable' is a departure and doubtful; 'knowable' is an alternative.","'Faßlichkeit' rendered 'comprehensibility'; 'gefördert' rendered 'demanded' (original reads 'gefördert', probably 'gefordert'; possible misreading or old spelling, so doubtful).","'widersinnisches' rendered 'absurd'; 'Handlungen' rendered 'actions'.","'ihr' in 'ihr zur völligen Befriedigung' refers to 'Vernunft' or 'Befreiung' (ambiguous in German); left ambiguous as 'its'. 'Befreiung' = 'liberation'."]],"krv-a-0652":["I believe I can promote this in the following way.",["'diese' (feminine) has no clear antecedent in this passage (cf. 'Deutlichkeit' at the end of the preceding one); rendered 'this' and left unresolved.","'befördern' rendered 'promote'."]],"krv-a-0653":["All objections can be divided into dogmatic, critical, and skeptical. The dogmatic objection is that which is directed against a proposition, the critical that which is directed against the proof of a proposition. The former requires an insight into the constitution of the nature of the object, in order to be able to assert the opposite of that which the proposition pretends about this object; it is therefore itself dogmatic and pretends to know the constitution of which there is talk better than does the opponent. The critical objection, because it leaves the proposition untouched in its worth or worthlessness, and only contests the proof, does not require at all to know the object better, or to arrogate to itself a better acquaintance with it; it shows only that the assertion is groundless, not that it is incorrect. The skeptical sets proposition and counterproposition alternately against one another, as objections of equal weight, each of them alternately as dogma and the other as its objection, and is thus on two opposed sides in appearance dogmatic, in order to annihilate entirely all judgment about the object. The dogmatic as well as the skeptical objection must therefore both pretend as much insight into their object as is necessary to assert something of it affirmatively or negatively. The critical is alone of such a kind that, in that it merely shows that one assumes, for the sake of one's assertion, something that is null and merely imagined, it overthrows the theory by withdrawing from it the arrogated foundation, without otherwise wanting to settle anything about the constitution of the object.",["Einwurf = objection; Satz = proposition; Beweis = proof; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Gegenstand = object; Urtheil = judgment; behaupten = assert; Behauptung = assertion; bejahen = affirm (beiahend); Kentniß = acquaintance (cf. kennen).","'der Gegentheil' (masculine article in the German) rendered 'the opponent'; the gender is irregular, so the reading is doubtful (cf. 'das Gegentheil' earlier in the same sentence, 'the opposite').","'dem Scheine nach' rendered 'in appearance'; Schein elsewhere 'illusion', here the phrase is adverbial.","'Grundlage' = 'foundation'; 'grundlos' = 'groundless' (Grund = ground).","'Der sceptische' and 'Der critische' are elliptical for 'Einwurf'; kept elliptical.","'Dogma' kept as 'dogma'; 'Theorie' = 'theory'."]],"krv-a-0654":["Now according to the common concepts of our reason we are dogmatic with regard to the community in which our thinking subject stands with the things outside us, and regard these as true objects subsisting independently of us, in accordance with a certain transcendental dualism, which does not count those outer appearances as representations belonging to the subject, but rather, just as sensible intuition delivers them to us, transposes them outside us as objects and wholly separates them from the thinking subject. This subreption is now the foundation of all theories about the community between soul and body, and it is never asked: whether then this objective reality of the appearances is so entirely correct, but rather this is presupposed as conceded and only rationalized about the way in which it must be explained and comprehended. The usual three systems devised about this, and really the only possible ones, are those of physical influence, of preestablished harmony, and of supernatural assistance.",["Gemeinschaft = community; Vernunft = reason; Erscheinung = appearance; Vorstellung = representation; Anschauung = intuition; vernünfteln = rationalize; begreifen = comprehend.","Gegenstände (\"objects\") and Obiecte (\"objects\") are both rendered \"object\"; the Latinate Object occurs here in \"als Obiecte versezt\".","Subiect = subject; Subreption (Latin-derived) = subreption; Cörper = body (spelling as in A)."]],"krv-a-0655":["The latter two ways of explaining the community of the soul with matter are grounded on objections against the first, which is the representation of the common understanding, namely that that which appears as matter cannot, through its immediate influence, be the cause of representations, as an entirely heterogeneous kind of effects. But they cannot then connect with what they understand by the object of outer senses the concept of a matter which nothing but appearance, hence already in itself mere representation, that has been effected by some outer objects, for otherwise they would say: that the representations of outer objects (the appearances) cannot be outer causes of the representations in our mind, which would be an entirely senseless objection, because it will occur to no one to hold for an outer cause that which he has once recognized as mere representation. According to our principles they must therefore direct their theory to this: that that which is the true (transcendental) object of our outer senses cannot be the cause of those representations (appearances) that we understand under the name matter. Since now no one can with ground pretend to know anything of the transcendental cause of our representations of outer senses, their assertion is entirely groundless. But if the supposed improvers of the doctrine of physical influence, according to the common way of representing a transcendental dualism, wanted to regard matter, as such, as a thing in itself (and not as mere appearance of an unknown thing) and to direct their objection to showing: that such an outer object, which shows no other causality than that of movements in itself, can never be the efficient cause of representations, but that a third being must therefore interpose itself, in order, if not interaction, then at least to establish correspondence and harmony between the two: then they would begin their refutation by assuming the πρῶτον ψεῦδος of physical influence in their dualism, and thus through their objection refute not so much natural influence as their own dualistic presupposition. For all difficulties that befall the combination of thinking nature with matter arise without exception solely from that surreptitious dualistic representation: that matter, as such, is not appearance, i.e., mere representation of the mind to which an unknown object corresponds, but the object in itself, just as it exists outside us and independently of all sensibility.",["The German lacks a finite verb in \"welche nichts als Erscheinung, mithin schon an sich selbst blosse Vorstellung, die ... gewirkt worden\"; the English keeps the construction as it stands, without supplying one.","Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; Gegenstand is the term here throughout. Gemüth = mind; Wirkung/gewirkt = effect/effected; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Verbindung = combination; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","\"sinnleer\" rendered \"senseless\"; literally \"empty of sense\" (Sinn). \"vor ein Ding\" and \"vor eine äussere Ursache\" (vor = für) rendered \"for\"/\"as\" per the construction; \"ins Mittel schlagen\" rendered \"interpose itself\".","erschlichen = surreptitious; Grund = ground (\"mit Grunde\" = \"with ground\"); vorgeben = pretend; Behauptung = assertion; kennen = know. Greek πρῶτον ψεῦδος left as in the text."]],"krv-a-0656":["Against the commonly assumed physical influence, therefore, no dogmatic objection can be made. For if the opponent assumes: that matter and its motion are mere appearances and thus themselves only representations, then he can place the difficulty only in this: that the unknown object of our sensibility cannot be the cause of the representations in us, to claim which, however, entitles him not in the least, since no one can settle about an unknown object what it can do or not do. But he must, according to our proofs above, necessarily admit this transcendental idealism, unless he manifestly wants to hypostasize representations and transpose them, as true things, outside himself.",["Einwurf = objection; Beweis = proof; einräumen = admit; hypostasiren = hypostasize (Greek-derived verb retained in sense).","\"welches aber vorzugeben ihn nicht das mindeste berechtigt\": the antecedent of \"welches\" is kept as ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-a-0657":["Nevertheless a well-grounded critical objection can be made against the common doctrinal opinion of physical influence. Such a pretended community between two kinds of substances, the thinking and the extended, lays a crude dualism at the ground and makes the latter, which are yet nothing but mere representations of the thinking subject, into things that subsist for themselves. Thus the misunderstood physical influence can be wholly frustrated by uncovering its ground of proof as null and surreptitious.",["The German has the singular \"die leztere\" with the plural \"sind\"; this is preserved as it stands.","Lehrmeinung = doctrinal opinion; Arten = kinds; Beweisgrund = ground of proof; ausgedehnt = extended."]],"krv-a-0658":["The notorious question concerning the community of the thinking and the extended would thus, if one sets aside everything imagined, run out solely to this: how in a thinking subject in general outer intuition, namely that of space (a filling of the same, shape and motion), is possible. But to this question it is not possible for any human being to find an answer, and one can never fill this gap in our knowledge, but only designate it by ascribing the outer appearances to a transcendental object, which is the cause of this kind of representations, but which we do not know at all, nor will ever get any concept of it. In all problems that may occur in the field of experience we treat those appearances as objects in themselves, without troubling ourselves about the first ground of their possibility (as appearances). But if we go beyond their boundary, then the concept of a transcendental object becomes necessary.",["\"Erfüllung desselben\": \"desselben\" most naturally refers to space (Raumes); the English \"of the same\" keeps the reference as open as the German.","Wissen = knowledge; kennen = know; Aufgaben = problems; Gränze = boundary; Erfahrung = experience; \"an sich selbst\" = in themselves.","Gegenstand/Gegenstände = object(s) throughout."]],"krv-a-0659":["From these remarks on the community between the thinking and the extended beings, the decision of all disputes or objections that concern the state of thinking nature before this community (life), or after such community has been canceled (in death), is an immediate sequel. The opinion that the thinking subject could have thought before all community with bodies would express itself thus: that before the beginning of this kind of sensibility, through which something appears to us in space, the same transcendental objects, which in the present state appear as bodies, could have been intuited in an entirely different way. But the opinion that the soul, after the cancellation of all community with the corporeal world, could still continue to think, would announce itself in this form: that, if the kind of sensibility, through which transcendental and for now entirely unknown objects appear to us as a material world, should cease: then all intuition of them would not thereby yet be canceled and it would be entirely possible that just the same unknown objects would continue to be cognized by the thinking subject, although admittedly no longer in the quality of bodies.",["Folge = sequel (glossary), not Folgerung (consequence). Aufhebung/aufgehoben = cancellation/canceled.","\"dieselbe transscendentale Gegenstände\" and \"eben dieselbe unbekante Gegenstände\": the German has singular \"dieselbe\" with plural nouns; rendered \"the same\"/\"just the same\".","erkant = cognized (erkennen); anschauen = intuit; Qualität = quality; Meinung = opinion; Art = kind/way as the context requires."]],"krv-a-0660":["Now to be sure no one can adduce the least ground for such an assertion from speculative principles, indeed cannot even demonstrate the possibility of it, but only presuppose it; but just as little can anyone make any valid dogmatic objection against it. For, whoever he may be, he knows just as little of the absolute and inner cause of outer and bodily appearances as I, or anyone else. He can therefore also not with ground pretend to know on what the actuality of the outer appearances in the present state (in life) rests, hence also not: that the condition of all outer intuition, or also the thinking subject itself, will cease after the same (in death).",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; wissen = know; Zustand = state; Bedingung = condition; gültig = valid; Behauptung = assertion.","\"nach demselben\" is kept as \"after the same\"; the German leaves open whether it refers to the present state or to life."]],"krv-a-0661":["Thus all dispute about the nature of our thinking being and the connection of the same with the world of bodies is merely a sequel of the fact that, with regard to that of which one knows nothing, one fills the gap with paralogisms of reason, since one makes one's thoughts into things and hypostatizes them, from which imaginary science arises, both with regard to him who asserts affirmatively and to him who asserts negatively, in that each either supposes himself to know something about objects of which no human being has any concept, or makes his own representations into objects, and so turns around in an eternal circle of ambiguities and contradictions. Nothing but the sobriety of a strict but just critique can free from this dogmatic delusion, which holds so many up through imagined happiness among theories and systems, and restrict all our speculative claims merely to the field of possible experience, not perhaps through insipid mockery over attempts so often failed, or pious sighs over the limitations of our reason, but by means of a determination of boundaries of the same carried out according to secure principles, which with the greatest reliability fastens its *nihil ulterius* to the Herculean pillars that nature herself has set up, in order to continue the voyage of our reason only as far as the steadily continuing coasts of experience reach, which we cannot leave without venturing onto a shoreless ocean, which under ever deceptive prospects finally constrains us to give up all arduous and tedious effort as hopeless.",["Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); Wissenschaft = \"science\"; wissen = \"know\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Schranken = \"limitations\"; Gränzbestimmung = \"determination of boundaries\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Cörperwelt = \"world of bodies\".","Blendwerk (neuter) is followed by the masculine relative \"der\"; I render \"which\" as the German leaves the antecedent unmarked. \"Delusion\" is my choice for Blendwerk (not in the glossary).","\"befreien\" has no expressed object in the German (\"can free from this ... delusion\"); kept as is.","\"welche\" (\"which fastens\") most naturally refers back to Gränzbestimmung; \"die wir nicht verlassen können\" has Küsten as antecedent.","\"ein ieder ... vermeint\": \"supposes himself to know\" is the closest English for the reflexive sense of vermeinen with an infinitive."]],"krv-a-0662":["We have still, up to now, remained indebted for a distinct and general exposition of the transcendental and yet natural illusion in the paralogisms of pure reason, likewise the justification of the systematic arrangements of the same, running parallel to the table of the categories. We could not have undertaken it at the beginning of this section without getting into danger of obscurity, or inappropriately anticipating ourselves. Now we will seek to fulfill this obligation.",["Erörterung = \"exposition\" (expositio); Schein = \"illusion\"; deutlich = \"distinct\".","\"der systematischen und der Tafel der Categorien parallel laufenden Anordnungen\": the two adjectives are kept in the German order, so that \"systematic\" and \"running parallel\" are both left attached to \"arrangements\"."]],"krv-a-0663":["One can posit all illusion in this: that the subjective condition of thinking is taken for the cognition of the object. Further, we have shown in the introduction to the transcendental dialectic: that pure reason occupies itself solely with the totality of the synthesis of the conditions, for a given conditioned. Since now the dialectical illusion of pure reason cannot be an empirical illusion, which is found in determinate empirical cognition: so it will concern the general of the conditions of thinking, and there will be only three cases of the dialectical use of pure reason,",["Schein = \"illusion\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; Obiect = \"object\" (Gegenstand also \"object\"); Totalität = \"totality\" (glossary lists Allheit); Bedingtem = \"conditioned\".","\"das Allgemeine\" rendered \"the general\"; \"vor\" in \"vor die Erkentniß\" rendered \"for\".","The passage breaks off with a comma; the list follows in the next passages."]],"krv-a-0664":["1. The synthesis of the conditions of a thought in general,",[]],"krv-a-0665":["2. The synthesis of the conditions of empirical thinking.",[]],"krv-a-0666":["3. The synthesis of the conditions of pure thinking.",[]],"krv-a-0667":["In all these three cases pure reason occupies itself merely with the absolute totality of this synthesis, i.e., with that condition which is itself unconditioned. On this division is also grounded the threefold transcendental illusion, which gives occasion for three sections of the dialectic, and furnishes the idea for just as many seeming sciences from pure reason, transcendental psychology, cosmology and theology. We have to do here only with the first.",["Eintheilung = \"division\"; Wissenschaften = \"sciences\"; scheinbar = \"seeming\"; Idee = \"idea\" (singular in the German).","\"an die Hand giebt\" rendered \"furnishes\"."]],"krv-a-0668":["Because in thinking in general we abstract from all relation of the thought to any object (be it of the senses or of the pure understanding): so the synthesis of the conditions of a thought in general (no. 1) is not at all objective, but merely a synthesis of the thought with the subject, which however is falsely taken for a synthetic representation of an object.",["Obiect = \"object\" (Gegenstand elsewhere also \"object\"); Gedanke / Gedanken = \"thought\".","\"vor\" in \"vor eine ... Vorstellung\" rendered \"for\"."]],"krv-a-0669":["But it also follows from this: that the dialectical inference to the condition of all thinking in general, which is itself unconditioned, does not commit an error in content, (for it abstracts from all content or object) but that it errs solely in form and must be called a paralogism.",["Schluß = \"inference\"; Inhalt = \"content\"; Obiect = \"object\"; Fehler = \"error\"; \"fehle\" = \"errs\" (fehlen: to be wanting or to err).","The parenthesis is placed as in the German, in the middle of the clause."]],"krv-a-0670":["Because further the sole condition that accompanies all thinking is the I, in the general proposition I think, reason has to do with this condition, insofar as it is itself unconditioned. It is however only the formal condition, namely the logical unity of every thought, in which I abstract from every object, and is nevertheless represented as an object that I think, namely: I myself and the unconditioned unity of the same.",["Satz = \"proposition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\" (Obiect elsewhere also \"object\"); Einheit = \"unity\"; Bedingung = \"condition\".","\"desselben\" is rendered \"of the same\" to leave its antecedent as open as in the German; the subject of \"wird ... vorgestellet\" is likewise left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0671":["If someone were to raise the question in general: of what constitution is a thing that thinks? then I know nothing in the least to answer to it a priori, because the answer is supposed to be synthetic (for an analytic one perhaps indeed explains thinking, but gives no extended cognition of that on which this thinking, as regards its possibility, rests. To every synthetic solution, however, intuition is required, which in the so general problem has been entirely left out. Just so no one can answer the question in its generality: what sort of thing must that be which is movable? For impenetrable extension (matter) is then not given. Now although I know no answer to that question in general: still it seems to me that I can give it in the single case, in the proposition that expresses self-consciousness: I think. For this I is the first subject, i.e., substance, it is simple, etc. These would, however, then have to be nothing but propositions of experience, which nevertheless without a general rule that stated the conditions of the possibility of thinking in general and a priori, could contain no such predicates (which are not empirical). In such a way my at first so seeming insight, in judging about the nature of a thinking being, and indeed from nothing but concepts, becomes suspect to me, although I have not yet discovered the error of the same.",["The opening parenthesis \"(denn eine analytische ...\" is never closed in the German; kept unclosed.","Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Aufgabe = \"problem\"; Ausdehnung = \"extension\"; Selbstbewustseyn = \"self-consciousness\"; Satz = \"proposition\"; Erfahrungssätze = \"propositions of experience\"; Auflösung = \"solution\".","\"ob ich gleich den Fehler derselben\": \"derselben\" could refer to the insight (Einsicht) or to the nature/judging; kept as \"of the same\".","\"aus lauter Begriffen zu urtheilen\" is left as an appended phrase after \"Einsicht, über der Natur ...\"; whether it attaches to \"judging\" or to \"insight\" is as open as in the German.","\"scheinbar\" = \"seeming\" (Schein = \"illusion\")."]],"krv-a-0672":["But further research behind the origin of these attributes, which I ascribe to Myself as a thinking being in general, can uncover this error. They are nothing more than pure categories, through which I never think a determinate object, but only the unity of representations, in order to determine an object for them. Without an intuition lying at the basis, the category alone can furnish me no concept of an object; for only through intuition is the object given, which afterward is thought in accordance with the category. If I declare a thing to be a substance in the appearance, then predicates of its intuition must previously have been given to me, by which I distinguish the persistent from the changeable and the substratum (thing itself) from that which merely adheres to it. If I call a thing simple in the appearance, then I understand by that that the intuition of it is indeed a part of the appearance, but itself cannot be divided, etc. But if something is cognized as simple only in the concept and not in the appearance, then I thereby really have no cognition at all of the object, but only of my concept, which I make for myself of something in general that is capable of no proper intuition. I say only that I think something entirely simple, because I really know nothing further to say than merely that it is something.",["\"Mir\" is capitalized in the German and kept so (\"Myself\").","Gegenstand = \"object\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; erkant = \"cognized\"; Beharrliche = \"persistent\"; einfach = \"simple\"; Theil = \"part\"; verstehe = \"understand\"; erklären = \"declare\" (glossary: explanation/definition/declaration); Vorstellungen = \"representations\".","\"Ding selbst\" = \"thing itself\" (in the parenthesis; not the technical Ding an sich).","\"ihm\" in \"das ihm blos anhängt\" has the substratum/thing as its antecedent; \"vor eine Substanz\" = \"to be a substance\" (vor = for).","\"nur vor einfach ... erkant\": \"vor\" = \"as\"/\"for\"; rendered \"as simple\"."]],"krv-a-0673":["Now the mere apperception (I) is substance in concept, simple in concept, etc., and so all those psychological theorems have their indisputable correctness. Nevertheless that which one actually wants to know is by no means thereby cognized of the soul, for all these predicates do not hold of intuition at all, and can therefore also have no consequences that would be applied to objects of experience, hence they are entirely empty. For that concept of substance teaches me not: that the soul endures for itself, not that it is a part of the outer intuitions that itself can no longer be divided, and that therefore can arise or perish through no alterations of nature; sheer properties that could make the soul knowable to me in the interconnection of experience, and, with regard to its origin and future state, give disclosure. But if I now say through the mere category: the soul is a simple substance, then it is clear that, since the naked concept of the understanding of substance contains nothing further than that a thing is to be represented as subject in itself, without in turn being predicate of another, nothing of persistence follows from it, and the attribute of the simple certainly cannot add this persistence, hence one is thereby not in the least instructed about what could befall the soul in the alterations of the world. If one could say to us, it is a simple part of matter, then we could derive from this, from what experience teaches of it, the persistence and, together with the simple nature, the indestructibility of it. But of that the concept of I, in the psychological principle (I think), tells us not a word.",["Lehrsätze = theorems; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstände = objects; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Grundsatz = principle.","Eigenschaften rendered 'properties' (the glossary's Beschaffenheit is also 'property'); kenbar = 'knowable' (from kennen, not erkennen). 'vor sich selbst' = 'for itself'.","Veränderungen = 'alterations' (also in 'Weltveränderungen'). 'Wissen' in the verb form wissen = 'know'.","'der nakte Verstandesbegriff von Substanz' kept as 'the naked concept of the understanding of substance'; 'Verstandesbegriff' is one word in German."]],"krv-a-0674":["But that the being that thinks in us believes itself to cognize itself through pure categories, and indeed those that express the absolute unity under each title of them, stems from this. The apperception is itself the ground of the possibility of the categories, which for their part represent nothing other than the synthesis of the manifold of intuition insofar as the latter has unity in apperception. Hence self-consciousness in general is the representation of that which is the condition of all unity, and yet itself unconditioned. One can therefore say of the thinking I (soul), which presents itself as substance, simple, numerically identical in all time, and the correlate of all existence, from which all other existence must be inferred: that it cognizes not so much itself through the categories, but the categories, and through them all objects, in the absolute unity of apperception, hence through itself. Now it is indeed very evident: that I cannot cognize as object itself that which I must presuppose in order to cognize an object at all, and that the determining self (the thinking) is distinguished from the determinable self (the thinking subject), as cognition from the object. Nevertheless nothing is more natural and seductive than the illusion of taking the unity in the synthesis of thoughts for a perceived unity in the subject of these thoughts. One could call it the subreption of the hypostatized consciousness (*apperceptiones substantiatae*).",["Wesen = being; Erkenntnis = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Selbstbewußtsein = self-consciousness; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Schein = illusion; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Dasein = existence.","Gegenstand and Obiect both = 'object' (here Gegenstände, Gegenstande, and Obiect).","The German has no verb after 'das sich als Substanz, einfach, ... und das Correlatum alles Daseyns'; 'presents' is supplied in the English.","geschlossen = 'inferred' (Schluß = inference/conclusion). 'bestimbaren' (sic) = determinable."]],"krv-a-0675":["If one wants to title the paralogism in the dialectical syllogisms of the rational doctrine of the soul logically, insofar as they nevertheless have correct premises: then it can count as a *sophisma figurae dictionis*, in which the major premise makes, of the category, with regard to its condition, a merely transcendental use, but the minor premise and the conclusion, with regard to the soul that has been subsumed under this condition, make an empirical use of the very same category. Thus e.g. the concept of substance in the paralogism of simplicity is a pure intellectual concept, which without conditions of sensible intuition is merely of transcendental, i.e. of no use at all. But in the minor premise the very same concept is applied to the object of all inner experience, without however positing in advance and laying at the basis the condition of its application *in concreto*, namely the persistence of it, and therefore an empirical, though here inadmissible use has been made of it.",["Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms; Obersatz / Untersatz = major / minor premise; Schlußsatz = conclusion (Schluß, glossed 'inference; conclusion').","'blos von transscendentalen, d. i. von gar keinem Gebrauch' kept elliptical: 'of transcendental' with 'use' only implied.","'macht' is singular after the compound subject 'der Untersatz aber und der Schlußsatz'; English plural 'make' is required.","Gebrauch = use; Bedingung = condition; sinnlich = sensible; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0676":["In order finally to show the systematic interconnection of all these dialectical assertions, in a rationalizing doctrine of the soul, in an interconnection of pure reason, hence the completeness of them, note: that the apperception is carried through all classes of the categories, but only to those concepts of the understanding that in each of them lie at the basis of the unity of the others in a possible perception, consequently: subsistence, reality, unity (not multiplicity) and existence, only that reason here represents them all as conditions of the possibility of a thinking being that are themselves unconditioned. Thus the soul cognizes in itself",["Passage breaks off at 'an sich selbst'; the list continues in the following passages.","'an sich selbst' = 'in itself' (an sich); the German could also be read as 'on/at itself'; left as 'in itself'.","Zusammenhang = interconnection; vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Vielheit = 'multiplicity' (Menge is also 'multiplicity' in the glossary); Existenz and Dasein both = 'existence' (here Existenz).","Hauptstück not occurring here; Wesen = being; Wahrnehmung = perception."]],"krv-a-0677":["1. The unconditioned unity of relation, i.e. of itself, not as inhering, but subsisting",["Verhältnis = 'relation'; inhärirend / subsistirend = 'inhering' / 'subsisting'. 'sich selbst' is in the accusative/dative-ambiguous form in the German and is rendered 'itself'."]],"krv-a-0678":["2. The unconditioned unity of quality, i.e. not as real whole, but simple*)",["The footnote marker *) is kept as in the source; 'real' = reales (from Realität)."]],"krv-a-0679":["*) How the simple here in turn corresponds to the category of reality, I cannot yet show now, but it will be shown in the following chapter, on the occasion of another use of reason of the very same concept.",["Footnote to item 2. Hauptstück = 'chapter'; Vernunftgebrauch = 'use of reason'; Realität = 'reality'."]],"krv-a-0680":["3. The unconditioned unity in the multiplicity in time, i.e. not numerically different in different times, but as one and the very same subject",["Vielheit = 'multiplicity'; 'Eines' (capitalized in the German) = 'one'."]],"krv-a-0681":["4. The unconditioned unity of existence in space, i.e. not as the consciousness of several things outside it, but only of the existence of itself, but of other things merely as its representations.",["Daseyn = 'existence'; 'ihr/ihrer' refers to the (feminine) soul and is rendered 'it/its'. Bewustseyn = consciousness."]],"krv-a-0682":["Reason is the faculty of principles. The assertions of pure psychology contain not empirical predicates of the soul, but those that, if they take place, are supposed to determine the object in itself independently of experience, hence through mere reason. They would therefore properly have to be grounded on principles and universal concepts of thinking natures in general. Instead of this it is found: that the single representation, I am, governs them all, which, precisely because it expresses the pure formula of all my experience (indeterminately), announces itself as a universal proposition that would hold for all thinking beings, and, since it is nevertheless in every aim single, carries with it the illusion of an absolute unity of the conditions of thinking in general, and thereby spreads itself further than possible experience could reach.",["'in aller Absicht' is an idiom ('in every respect'); rendered literally with the glossary's Absicht = 'aim'.","'Principien' (Latinate) and Grundsatz both = 'principle'; Satz = 'proposition'; Wesen = 'being'; Vermögen = 'faculty'.","'welche' (relative pronoun) could refer to 'Vorstellung' or loosely to the assertions; the German syntax kept. 'statt finden' = 'take place'."]],"krv-a-0683":["The Transcendental Dialectic's Second Book. Second Chapter. The Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["Hauptstück = 'chapter'; Buch = 'book'."]],"krv-a-0684":["We have shown in the introduction to this part of our work: that all transcendental illusion of pure reason rests on dialectical inferences, whose schema logic provides in the three formal species of syllogisms in general, just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgments. The first species of these rationalizing inferences went to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or of the soul), in correspondence with categorical syllogisms, whose major premise, as principle, states the relation of a predicate to a subject. The second species of the dialectical argument will therefore, according to the analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, make the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in appearance its content, just as the third species, which will occur in the following chapter, has as its theme the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.",["Art = 'species' (glossary lists also way, kind, manner); Schlüsse = 'inferences', Vernunftschlüsse = 'syllogisms'; vernünftelnd = 'rationalizing'.","Beziehung = 'relation' (Verhältnis is also rendered 'relation' elsewhere); 'an die Hand giebt' = 'provides'; 'gieng auf' = 'went to'.","Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Inhalt = 'content'; Gegenstände = 'objects' (obiectiven = 'objective')."]],"krv-a-0685":["But it is remarkable: that the transcendental paralogism effected a merely one-sided illusion, with regard to the idea of the subject of our thinking, and for the assertion of the opposite not the least illusion from concepts of reason wants to be found. The advantage is entirely on the side of pneumatism, although the latter cannot deny the hereditary defect, with all the illusion favorable to it, of dissolving into sheer vapor in the fire test of critique.",["'sich ... vorfinden will' kept literally as 'wants to be found'. 'bey allem ihm günstigen Schein' could attach to 'verläugnen' or to 'aufzulösen'; the English keeps the placement ambiguous.","Schein = 'illusion'; Behauptung = 'assertion'; Erbfehler = 'hereditary defect'; Feuerprobe = 'fire test'; Pnevmatismus = 'pneumatism'."]],"krv-a-0686":["It turns out quite differently when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of appearances, where it indeed thinks to make its *principium* of unconditioned unity valid with much illusion, but soon entangles itself in such contradictions that it is compelled, in a cosmological aim, to desist from its demand.",["Object-ive: 'obiective' = objective (from Object, not Gegenstand; no occurrence of either noun here).","Principium kept in Latin, as in the German (roman type).","Absicht = aim; 'in cosmologischer Absicht' kept literal ('in a cosmological aim'); Schein = illusion; Widersprüche = contradictions; Foderung = demand (modern Forderung).","'geltend zu machen denkt' = 'thinks to make valid'; 'geltend' is not in the glossary (cf. gültig = valid); the rendering is literal."]],"krv-a-0687":["For here there shows itself a new phenomenon of human reason, namely: a quite natural antithetic, over which no one needs to brood or to lay artificial snares, but into which reason falls of itself and indeed unavoidably, and is thereby indeed guarded against the slumber of an imagined conviction, which a merely one-sided illusion produces, but at the same time is brought into temptation either to abandon itself to a skeptical hopelessness, or to adopt a dogmatic defiance and stiffly set its head on certain assertions, without letting the grounds of the opposite meet with a hearing and justice. Both are the death of a healthy philosophy, although the former could at most still be called the euthanasia of pure reason.",["Phänomen = phenomenon (distinct from Erscheinung = appearance); Antithetik rendered 'antithetic' (noun).","Ueberzeugung = conviction; Schein = illusion; Gründe = grounds; Behauptungen = assertions; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","'eingebildet' = imagined (cf. Einbildungskraft = imagination).","'Gegentheil' rendered 'the opposite'; 'iener' (jener) = 'the former'; 'Beides' = 'Both'.","'Gehör und Gerechtigkeit wiederfahren zu lassen' (wiederfahren = widerfahren) rendered literally, 'meet with'; the construction is somewhat awkward in German and left so.","'sceptischen' rendered with American spelling 'skeptical'; 'genant' = genannt."]],"krv-a-0688":["Before we let the scenes of dissension and disruption be seen, which this conflict of the laws (antinomy) of pure reason occasions, we want to give certain expositions, which can clarify and justify the method of which we avail ourselves in the treatment of our object. I call all transcendental ideas, insofar as they concern the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances, concepts of the world, partly on account of just this unconditioned totality, on which the concept of the world-whole also rests, which itself is only an idea, partly because they go merely to the synthesis of appearances, hence the empirical one, whereas the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things in general will occasion an ideal of pure reason, which is entirely different from the concept of the world, although it stands in relation to it. Hence, just as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the ground for a dialectical psychology, so the antinomy of pure reason will set before the eyes the transcendental principles of a putative pure (rational) cosmology, not in order to find them valid and to appropriate them, but, as the very term of a conflict of reason already indicates, in order to present them, as an idea that cannot be reconciled with appearances, in their dazzling but false illusion.",["Erörterungen = expositions (Latin expositio); erläutern = clarify; Gegenstand = object; Benennung = term; Grundsätze = principles; Schein = illusion (here 'falschen Scheine' = 'false illusion').","Widerstreit der Gesetze rendered 'conflict of the laws'; Widerstreit der Vernunft = 'conflict of reason'; Antinomie kept as 'antinomy'.","Weltbegriffe = 'concepts of the world'; Weltganzen = 'world-whole'; Ideal = 'ideal'.","'die empirische' (sc. Synthesis) rendered 'the empirical one'; 'darauf in Beziehung steht' = 'stands in relation to it' ('darauf' could refer to Weltbegriff or the totality; left as 'it').","'sie' in 'um sie gültig zu finden' and 'sie ... darzustellen' = the principles; 'als eine Idee' left in the singular as in German, so the grammatical referent of 'idea' is ambiguous.","'vermeinten' = putative; 'vor Augen stellen' = 'set before the eyes'; 'zuzueignen' = 'appropriate'; Totalität = totality."]],"krv-a-0689":["First Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. System of the Cosmological Ideas.",["Heading; 'Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft Erster Abschnitt' rendered with the genitive literally ('of the Antinomy of Pure Reason')."]],"krv-a-0690":["In order now to be able to enumerate these ideas according to a principle with systematic precision, we must first remark: that only the understanding can be that from which pure and transcendental concepts can arise, that reason properly generates no concept at all, but at most only frees the concept of the understanding from the unavoidable limitations of a possible experience, and so seeks to extend it beyond the boundaries of the empirical, yet still in connection with it. This happens thereby: that for a given conditioned, on the side of the conditions (under which the understanding subjects all appearances to synthetic unity), it demands absolute totality, and thereby makes the category into a transcendental idea, in order to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis by continuing it up to the unconditioned (which is never encountered in experience, but only in the idea). Reason demands this in accordance with the principle: if the conditioned is given, then the whole sum of the conditions, hence the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, through which alone the former was possible. Thus, first, the transcendental ideas will properly be nothing but categories extended up to the unconditioned, and those will let themselves be brought into a table that is arranged according to the titles of the latter. Second, however, not all categories will be suited to this either, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series, and indeed of the conditions subordinated to one another (not coordinated) to a conditioned. The absolute totality is demanded by reason only insofar as it concerns the ascending series of the conditions to a given conditioned, hence not when there is talk of the descending line of the consequences, nor of the aggregate of coordinated conditions for these consequences. For conditions are already presupposed with regard to the given conditioned and are to be regarded as given along with it, whereas, since the consequences do not make their conditions possible but rather presuppose them, in the progression to the consequences (or in the descending from the given condition to the conditioned) one can be unconcerned whether the series ceases or not, and the question about its totality is in general no presupposition of reason at all.",["Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Begriff = concept; Erfahrung = experience; Erscheinungen = appearances; Bedingung/Bedingtes = condition/conditioned; Totalität = totality; Summe = sum.","Gränzen = boundaries (Grenze = bound(ary)); Einschränkungen = limitations (Schranke is not used here); Folgen = consequences (glossary Folge = sequel/sequence; 'consequences' chosen to match the sense of 'Folgerung' elsewhere—flagged as a departure); Fortgang = progression.","'ienes' / 'iene' / 'der lezteren' = 'the former' / 'those' / 'the latter'; antecedents left as in German ('dasselbe', 'es' not resolved).","'wodurch ienes allein möglich war': 'ienes' rendered 'the former' (referent ambiguous, as in German).","'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Fortsetzung' = continuing; 'Vollständigkeit' = completeness; 'Aggregat' = aggregate; 'Voraussetzung' = presupposition.","'die Frage, wegen ihrer Totalität' kept with the German's comma placement; 'ihrer' = the series' (or the question's), ambiguity of reference left as in German."]],"krv-a-0691":["Thus one necessarily thinks to oneself a time entirely elapsed up to the given moment as also given, (even if not determinable by us). But as concerns the future, since it is not the condition of attaining the present, it is, in order to comprehend the latter, entirely indifferent how we want to hold with future time, whether one wants to let it cease somewhere or run into the infinite. Let the series be m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned with regard to m, but at the same time as condition of o; let the series go upward from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i etc.), likewise downward from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r etc.), then I must presuppose the former series in order to regard n as given, and n is possible according to reason (the totality of the conditions) only by means of that series; its possibility, however, does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which therefore also can be regarded not as given, but only as *dabilis*.",["'Zeit' = time; 'Augenblick' = moment; 'bestimbar' = bestimmbar, rendered 'determinable' (bestimmen = determine).","'diese zu begreiffen': 'diese' = the present (Gegenwart); begreifen = comprehend; the referent could also be read as 'the future'; I have rendered 'the latter', which preserves some ambiguity but leans toward the present—reconsider if a strictly neutral 'this' is desired.","'die künftige' = 'the future' (sc. Zeit); 'Möglichkeit' = possibility; 'Reihe' = series.","'dabilis' is Latin in the German and kept in italics.","The letters in the series (m, n, o; l, k, i; p, q, r) are reproduced as printed; 'i' for 'h' in A is possibly an error in the source but is left as it stands."]],"krv-a-0692":["I will call the synthesis of a series on the side of the conditions, thus from that one which is nearest to the given appearance and so on to the more remote conditions, the regressive, but that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the nearest sequel to the more remote, the progressive synthesis. The former goes *in antecedentia*, the second *in consequentia*. The cosmological ideas thus occupy themselves with the totality of the regressive synthesis and go *in antecedentia*, not *in consequentia*. If this latter happens, it is an arbitrary and not necessary problem of pure reason, because for the complete comprehensibility of that which is given in the appearance we indeed need grounds, but not consequences.",["Folge in 'nächsten Folge' = sequel (glossary); in the final clause 'Folgen' = consequences, matching the Latin *consequentia*; the German word is the same, so one English word is not used throughout—flagged.","Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility (cf. begreifen = comprehend); Problem = problem (Aufgabe in glossary = problem, but the German here is 'Problem').","'Gründe' = grounds; 'willkührliches' = arbitrary; 'notwendiges' = necessary.","'dieses leztere' = 'this latter', referent (the going in consequentia) left as in German."]],"krv-a-0693":["In order now to set up the table of ideas according to the table of categories, we take first the two original *quanta* of all our intuition, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series) and therefore in it, with regard to a given present, the *antecedentia* as conditions (the past) are to be distinguished a priori from the *consequentibus* (the future). Consequently the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given conditioned goes only to all past time. According to the idea of reason the whole elapsed time is necessarily thought as given, as condition of the given moment. But as far as space is concerned, in it in itself there is no difference of the *progressus* from the *regressus*, because it makes up an aggregate but no series, in that its parts are all together at the same time. The present point of time I could regard, with regard to past time, only as conditioned, but never as condition of the same, because this moment first arises only through the elapsed time (or rather through the elapsing of the preceding time). But since the parts of space are not subordinated to one another but coordinated with one another, one part is not the condition of the possibility of the other, and it does not, like time, make up a series in itself. Yet the synthesis of the manifold parts of space, through which we apprehend it, is nevertheless successive, thus occurs in time and contains a series. And since in this series of aggregated spaces (e.g., of the feet in a rod), starting from a given one, the ones thought further on are always the condition of the boundary of the previous one, the measuring of a space is also to be regarded as a synthesis of a series of conditions for a given conditioned, only that the side of the conditions is in itself not distinguished from the side toward which the conditioned lies, consequently *regressus* and *progressus* in space seem to be one and the same. Since in the meantime a part of space is not given through the other but only bounded, we must to this extent regard every bounded space also as conditioned, which presupposes another space as the condition of its boundary, and so on. With regard to the bounding, therefore, the progression in space is also a regress, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in the series of conditions also concerns space, and I can just as well ask after the absolute totality of the appearance in space as of that in the elapsed time. Whether, however, an answer to this is possible at all will be able to be determined in the future.",["Latin kept in italics as printed: *quanta*, *antecedentia*, *consequentibus*, *progressus*, *regressus*; *consequentibus* is the text's own form (dative/ablative) after 'von'.","Anschauung = intuition; Bedingung = condition; Bedingtes = conditioned; Erscheinung = appearance; Theil = part; Gränze = boundary; Fortgang = progression.","'Reihe' = series; 'Gegenwart' and 'Zeitpunct' and 'Augenblick' rendered 'present', 'point of time', 'moment'.","'a priori' stands after 'zu unterscheiden' and could attach to the verb or to the preceding nouns; left as in German.","'konte' (= konnte) and 'seyn' in 'die weiter hinzugedachte immer die Bedingung ... seyn' (plural subjunctive-like form) are as printed; English 'the ones thought further on' follows the German plural sense of the construction.","'Ruthe' = rod (unit of length); 'apprehendiren' = apprehend; 'Synthesis' = synthesis.","'Allein' at 'Allein die Synthesis' and 'Allein, da' (0695) both rendered 'Yet'/'However' as context-fitting; 'Allein' = 'but/yet'."]],"krv-a-0694":["Second, reality in space, i.e., matter, is a conditioned, whose inner conditions are its parts, and the parts of the parts the remote conditions, so that here a regressive synthesis takes place, whose absolute totality reason demands, which can take place in no other way than through a completed division, through which the reality of matter vanishes either into nothing or at least into that which is no longer matter, namely the simple. Consequently here too there is a series of conditions and a progress toward the unconditioned.",["Realität = reality; Materie = matter; Einfache = simple (einfach); Theilung = division; Fortschritt = progress (distinct from Fortgang, 'progression', in 0693).","'Bedingtes' = conditioned (noun); 'Unbedingten' = unconditioned."]],"krv-a-0695":["Third, as far as the categories of real relation among appearances are concerned, the category of substance with its accidents does not suit a transcendental idea, i.e., reason has no ground, with regard to it, to go regressively to conditions. For accidents are (insofar as they inhere in a single substance) coordinated with one another, and make up no series. With regard to the substance, however, they are not actually subordinated to it, but are the way of existing of the substance itself. What might still seem here to be an idea of transcendental reason would be the concept of the substantial. However, since this signifies nothing other than the concept of the object in general, which subsists insofar as one thinks in it merely the transcendental subject without any predicates, but here there is talk only of the unconditioned in the series of appearances, it is clear: that the substantial cannot make up a member in it. Just the same holds also of substances in community, which are mere aggregates, and have no exponent of a series, in that they are not subordinated to one another as conditions of their possibility, which one could well say of spaces, whose boundary was never in itself but always determined through another space. There remains, therefore, only the category of causality, which offers a series of causes to a given effect, in which one can ascend from the latter as the conditioned to the former as conditions, and answer the question of reason.",["Gegenstand = object; Gemeinschaft = community; Art zu existiren = way of existing; Grund = ground; Wirkung = effect; Bedeutet = signifies.","Accidenzen, Substanz, Caussalität kept as English cognates; 'subsistirt' = subsists; 'inhäriren' = inhere; 'coordinirt/subordinirt' = coordinated/subordinated.","'ienen' (= jenen) refers to the causes, 'der lezteren' to the effect; the pronouns are kept as in German ('the former', 'the latter').","'Vernunftfrage' = 'question of reason' (genitive compound left unresolved).","'bestimt' (= bestimmt) = determined; 'Exponenten' = exponent."]],"krv-a-0696":["Fourth, the concepts of the possible, actual and necessary lead to no series, except only insofar as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and according to the rule of the understanding points to a condition, under which it is necessary for this to point to a higher condition, until reason encounters unconditioned necessity only in the totality of this series.",["Wirklichen = actual; Zufällige = contingent (zufällig); Daseyn = existence (Dasein, not Existenz); Verstand = understanding; Totalität = totality; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.","'darunter es nothwendig ist, diese auf eine höhere Bedingung zu weisen': 'diese' is left ambiguous in reference, as in the German."]],"krv-a-0697":["There are accordingly no more than four cosmological ideas, according to the four titles of the categories, if one picks out those which necessarily bring with them a series in the synthesis of the manifold.",["Mannigfaltigen = manifold (n.); Categorien = categories; 'aushebt' = picks out; 'dieienige' = diejenige, 'those'."]],"krv-a-0698":["1. The absolute completeness of the composition of the given whole of all appearances",["Vollständigkeit = completeness (not in glossary); Zusammensetzung = composition."]],"krv-a-0699":["2. The absolute completeness of the division of a given whole in the appearance",["Theilung = division (Teilung)."]],"krv-a-0700":["3. The absolute completeness of the arising of an appearance in general",["Entstehung = arising; 'überhaupt' = in general."]],"krv-a-0701":["4. The absolute completeness of the dependence of the existence of the alterable in the appearance.",["Daseyn = existence; Veränderlichen = alterable (the changeable); Abhängigkeit = dependence."]],"krv-a-0702":["First it is to be remarked here: that the idea of absolute totality concerns nothing other than the exposition of appearances, hence not the pure concept of the understanding of a whole of things in general. Here, therefore, appearances are considered as given, and reason demands the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, insofar as these make up a series, hence a simply (i.e., in every aim) complete synthesis, through which the appearance could be exposited according to laws of the understanding.",["Exposition = exposition (Latin *expositio* in glossary); 'exponirt' = exposited; Absicht = aim; Verstandesgesetzen = laws of the understanding; rein = pure.","'Verstandesbegriff' = concept of the understanding; 'in aller Absicht' = 'in every aim' (literal; 'respect' not used, per glossary).","'Ganzen der Dinge überhaupt': 'überhaupt' could attach to 'Dinge' or to the whole phrase; left as in German."]],"krv-a-0703":["Second, it is really only the unconditioned that reason seeks, in this synthesis of conditions, continued serially and indeed regressively, as it were the completeness in the series of premises, which together presuppose no further one. This unconditioned is now always contained in the absolute totality of the series, if one represents it to oneself in imagination. But this absolutely completed synthesis is in turn only an idea; for one cannot know, at least in advance, whether such a synthesis is also possible with appearances. If one represents everything through mere pure concepts of the understanding, without conditions of sensible intuition, one can say straight out: that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another is also given; for the former is given only through the latter. But with appearances a particular limitation of the way in which conditions are given is to be encountered, namely through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which is supposed to be complete in the regress. Whether this completeness is now sensibly possible is still a problem. But the idea of this completeness nevertheless lies in reason, regardless of the possibility or impossibility of connecting empirical concepts adequate to it. Thus, since the unconditioned is necessarily contained in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in the appearance (under the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions for a given conditioned), one may also leave undecided whether and how this totality is to be brought about: so reason here takes the way of setting out from the idea of totality, even though it really has as its final aim the unconditioned, be it of the whole series, or of a part of it.",["Unbedingte = 'the unconditioned' (substantivized); bedingt = 'conditioned'; Bedingung = 'condition'.","Synthesis, Regressus kept as in the German; 'Idee' = idea; 'Totalität' rendered 'totality' (glossary Allheit is a different word).","'Vorstellung/vorstellen' = representation/represent; 'Einbildung' (not Einbildungskraft) = 'imagination'.","'Verstandesbegriffe' = concepts of the understanding; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Mannigfaltige' = manifold.","'Endabsicht' = 'final aim' (Absicht = aim). 'Problem' is in the German.","The syntax of 'Also, da ... : so nimt ...' is kept; 'man mag auch unausgemacht lassen' is a concessive clause within the 'da'-clause, rendered as it stands (ambiguous in the German as in the English).","Original 'kan', 'nimt', 'gesezt' etc. are older spellings; silently normalized."]],"krv-a-0704":["This unconditioned one can now think either as consisting merely in the whole series, in which therefore all members without exception would be conditioned and only the whole of them absolutely unconditioned, and then the regress is called infinite: or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the remaining members of it are subordinated, but which itself stands under no other condition.*) In the former case the series is *a parte priori* without bounds (without beginning), i.e., infinite, and nevertheless entirely given, but the regress in it is never completed, and can only be called infinite *potentialiter*. In the second case there is a first of the series, which with regard to elapsed time is called the beginning of the world, with regard to space the boundary of the world, with regard to the parts of a whole given in its bounds the simple, with regard to causes absolute self-activity (freedom), with regard to the existence of alterable things absolute natural necessity.",["Gränze = 'bound(ary)': 'ohne Gränzen' = 'without bounds'; 'Weltgränze' = 'boundary of the world'; 'in seinen Gränzen' = 'in its bounds'.","Glied = 'member'; Theil = 'part'; Einfache = 'the simple'; Daseyn = 'existence'; veränderlich = 'alterable'.","Footnote marker *) kept where it stands in the German. 'Weltanfang' = 'beginning of the world'.","'Selbstthätigkeit' = 'self-activity'; 'Naturnothwendigkeit' = 'natural necessity'.","'dem ... untergeordnet' vs 'subordinirter' (Bedingungen) both 'subordinated'."]],"krv-a-0705":["*) The absolute whole of the series of conditions for a given conditioned is always unconditioned; because outside it there are no more conditions with regard to which it could be conditioned. But this absolute whole of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematic concept, whose possibility must be investigated, and indeed in relation to the way in which the unconditioned, as the proper transcendental idea, on which it depends, may be contained therein.",["'untersucht werde muß' in the source is an evident misprint for 'werden muß'; rendered 'must be investigated'.","'Art' = 'way'; 'Begriff' = 'concept'; 'Möglichkeit' = 'possibility'.","'worauf es ankomt' = 'on which it depends' (literally idiomatic; 'it' unspecified in the German)."]],"krv-a-0706":["We have two expressions: world and nature, which sometimes run into one another. The first signifies the mathematical whole of all appearances and the totality of their synthesis, in the large as well as in the small, i.e., as much in the progress of it through composition as through division. This same world, however, is called nature*) insofar as it is considered as a dynamical whole, and one looks not to the aggregation in space or time, in order to bring it about as a magnitude, but to the unity in the existence of appearances. There the condition of that which happens is called the cause, and the unconditioned causality of the cause in the appearance, freedom, the conditioned, on the other hand, is called in the narrower understanding, natural cause. The conditioned in existence in general is called contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of appearances can be called natural necessity.",["Ausdruck = 'expression'; bedeuten = 'signify'; Fortschritt = 'progress'; Zusammensetzung = 'composition'; Theilung = 'division'.","'Grösse' = 'magnitude'; 'Einheit' = 'unity'; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'; 'zufällig' = 'contingent'.","'im engeren Verstande' = 'in the narrower understanding' (Verstand in a non-technical sense, 'sense/meaning').","'die unbedingte Caussalität ... die Freiheit, die bedingte dagegen heißt ...': the punctuation and apposition are kept as in the German, with the ambiguity of the construction.","'Da heißt nun' kept as 'There ... is called'; 'Naturursache' = 'natural cause'.","'unbedingte Nothwendigkeit' in the last sentence is translated 'unconditioned necessity' for 'unbedingte Nothwendigkeit'; 'Naturnothwendigkeit' = 'natural necessity'."]],"krv-a-0707":["*) Nature, taken adjectively (*formaliter*), signifies the interconnection of the determinations of a thing, according to an inner principle of causality. By contrast one understands by nature, substantively (*materialiter*), the sum total of appearances, insofar as these, by virtue of an inner principle of causality, are thoroughly interconnected. In the former sense one speaks of the nature of fluid matter, of fire, etc. and makes use of this word only adjectively; by contrast, when one speaks of the things of nature, one has in thought a subsisting whole.",["Latin *adiectiue* (printed 'adiectiue'), *substantiue*, *formaliter*, *materialiter* kept as in the source, italicized per method; the source prints the first two in roman type.","Zusammenhang = 'interconnection' (zusammenhängen = 'be interconnected'); Bestimmung = 'determination'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'.","'Im ersteren Verstande' = 'In the former sense' (Verstand = sense/meaning here, not 'understanding' as faculty).","'Principium' = 'principle'; 'Materie' = 'matter'; 'durchgängig' = 'thoroughly'."]],"krv-a-0708":["The ideas with which we are now occupied I called above cosmological ideas, partly because by world is understood the sum total of all appearances, and our ideas are also directed only to the unconditioned among appearances, partly also because the word world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the sum total of existing things, and we direct our attention solely to the completeness of the synthesis (although really only in the regress to the conditions). In consideration of the fact that, moreover, these ideas are all transcendent, and, although they do not exceed the object, namely appearances, as to kind, but have to do solely with the world of sense (not with noumena), nevertheless push the synthesis to a degree that surpasses all possible experience, one can in my opinion call them all quite fittingly concepts of the world. With regard to the difference of the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned, at which the regress aims, I would nevertheless call the first two, in the narrower signification, concepts of the world (of the world in the large and the small), but the two remaining ones transcendent concepts of nature. This distinction is for now not yet of any particular importance, but it can become more important in the progress.",["'Obiect' = 'object' (Gegenstand is also 'object'; here only Obiect occurs). 'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of sense'; *Noümenis* printed in the source as Latin-form, rendered 'noumena'.","'der Art nach' = 'as to kind' (Art = kind).","'Weltbegriffe' = 'concepts of the world'; 'Naturbegriffe' = 'concepts of nature'; 'Bedeutung' = 'signification' here, bedeuten = 'signify'.","'Fortgange' = 'progress'; 'Grad' = 'degree'; 'Erfahrung' = 'experience'; 'Meinung' = 'opinion' (in 'meiner Meinung nach' rendered 'in my opinion').","'Mathematisch- und des Dynamischunbedingten' kept as a compressed construction: 'of the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned'.","'Augenmerk' = 'attention'."]],"krv-a-0709":["Second Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. Antithetic of Pure Reason.",["Heading; 'Antithetik' kept as 'Antithetic'. 'rein' = 'pure'."]],"krv-a-0710":["If thetic is any sum total of dogmatic doctrines, then by antithetic I understand not dogmatic assertions of the opposite, but the conflict of cognitions dogmatic in illusion, (*thesin cum antithesi*) without one's attributing to one a preferable claim to approval over the other. The antithetic therefore does not occupy itself at all with one-sided assertions, but considers general cognitions of reason only according to their conflict with one another and the causes of it. The transcendental antithetic is an investigation of the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we employ our reason not merely, for the use of the principles of the understanding, on objects of experience, but dare to extend the former beyond the boundary of the latter, there arise rationalizing theorems, which may neither hope for confirmation in experience nor fear refutation, and each of which is not only in itself without contradiction, but even finds in the nature of reason conditions of its necessity, except that unfortunately the opposite has on its side equally valid and necessary grounds of assertion.",["'dem Scheine nach' = 'in illusion' is a literal rendering of Schein = illusion; it could also be read 'in appearance/seemingly'; the glossary fixes 'illusion'. 'dogmatischen Erkentnisse' = 'dogmatic cognitions'.","*thesin cum antithesi* kept in Latin, italicized.","'Gränze' = 'boundary'; 'vernünftelnde' = 'rationalizing' (vernünfteln = rationalize); 'Lehrsätze' = 'theorems'; 'Lehren' = 'doctrines'.","'einen vorzüglichen Anspruch ... beilegt': 'attributing ... a preferable claim' kept literal; 'Behauptung' = 'assertion'; 'Grund' = 'ground'; 'Verstandesgrundsätze' = 'principles of the understanding'; 'Widerspruch' = 'contradiction'; 'Widerstreit' = 'conflict'.","'iene ... auszudehnen': 'iene' (the former) refers to reason; 'der lezteren' = 'the latter' (Erfahrung); in 'ieder' the antecedent is Lehrsätze."]],"krv-a-0711":["The questions which present themselves naturally in such a dialectic of pure reason are therefore 1. In which propositions then really pure reason is unavoidably subject to an antinomy. 2. On which causes this antinomy rests. 3. Whether and in what way a path to certainty nevertheless remains open to reason under this contradiction.",["'Sätzen' = 'propositions'; 'Ursachen' = 'causes'; 'Widerspruch' = 'contradiction'; 'Art' = 'way'; 'Gewißheit' = 'certainty'.","The numerals 1., 2., 3. are kept inline as in the German; the questions are framed as dependent clauses, not rephrased into direct questions."]],"krv-a-0712":["A dialectical theorem of pure reason must accordingly have this in itself, distinguishing it from all sophistical propositions, that it does not concern an arbitrary question, which one raises only with a certain optional aim, but one on which every human reason in its progress must necessarily stumble, and second: that it, with its opposite, carries with it not a merely artificial illusion, which, if one has insight into it, immediately vanishes, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when one is no longer deceived by it, still always deludes, though it does not defraud, and thus can indeed be made harmless, but never extirpated.",["Lehrsatz = theorem; Satz = proposition; Absicht = aim; Fortgang = progress; Schein = illusion.","hintergangen = 'deceived'; täuscht = 'deludes'; betrügt = 'defrauds'. Three different German words; the English distinction is a choice of rendering.","'ihn' (first clause) and 'es' are left to refer as in the German; 'it' in 'that it, with its opposite' refers back to the theorem."]],"krv-a-0713":["Such a dialectical doctrine will relate itself not to the unity of the understanding in concepts of experience, but to the unity of reason in mere ideas, the conditions of which, since it is supposed first, as synthesis according to rules, to be congruent with the understanding and yet at the same time, as absolute unity thereof, with reason, will, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, be too great for the understanding, and, if appropriate to the understanding, too small for reason; from which a conflict must then arise that cannot be avoided, however one may go about it.",["Preserved: 'deren Bedingungen ... sie ... soll' shifts from plural to singular and the antecedent of 'sie' and 'derselben' is left as open as in the German.","Verstandeseinheit = unity of the understanding; Vernunfteinheit = unity of reason; Widerstreit = conflict; congruiren = 'be congruent'."]],"krv-a-0714":["These rationalizing assertions therefore open a dialectical battlefield, where each party retains the upper hand that has permission to make the attack, and that one certainly succumbs who is compelled to conduct himself merely by way of defense. Hence also sturdy knights, whether they vouch for the good or the bad cause, are certain to carry off the wreath of victory, if they only take care that they have the prerogative of making the last attack, and are not obliged to withstand a new onslaught of the adversary. One can easily imagine that this tilting-ground has been entered often enough from time immemorial, that many victories have been won on both sides, but that for the last one, which decided the matter, care was always taken that the champion of the good cause alone kept the field, by his adversary being forbidden to take weapons in hand any further. As impartial judges of the contest we must set entirely aside whether it is the good or the bad cause for which the combatants are fighting, and let them first settle their matter among themselves. Perhaps, after they have wearied one another more than harmed one another, they will see the nullity of their quarrel of themselves and part as good friends.",["vernünftelnd = 'rationalizing' (vernünfteln); Kampfplatz = battlefield; Tummelplatz = tilting-ground; Kampfrichter = judges of the contest.","'sich blos vertheidigungsweise zu führen' rendered literally as 'to conduct himself merely by way of defense'."]],"krv-a-0715":["This method of watching a dispute of assertions, or rather of occasioning it oneself, not in order finally to decide in favor of the one or the other party, but in order to investigate whether its object is not perhaps a mere deception after which each grasps in vain and in which he can gain nothing even if he were not resisted at all, this procedure, I say, can be called the skeptical method. It is entirely different from skepticism, a principle of an artful and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all cognition in order, where possible, to leave everywhere no reliability and certainty of it. For the skeptical method aims at certainty, in that in such a dispute, honestly meant on both sides and conducted with understanding, it seeks to discover the point of misunderstanding, in order, as wise legislators do, to draw for themselves, from the embarrassment of judges in legal disputes, teaching about what is deficient and not exactly determined in their laws. The antinomy that reveals itself in the application of laws is, given our limited wisdom, the best test of nomothetics, in order thereby to make reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become aware of its missteps, attentive to the moments in the determination of its principles.",["Gegenstand = object; Erkentniß = cognition; Gesetz = law; Belehrung = teaching; Grundsatz = principle; Bestimmung = determination.","Blendwerk = 'deception' (doubtful: could also be 'phantom'); 'sceptische' rendered 'skeptical' (American spelling).","Preserved: 'um ... aus der Verlegenheit ... vor sich selbst Belehrung ... zu ziehen' — 'vor sich selbst' is 'for themselves' (legislators); 'Momente' = 'moments'.","Nomothetik rendered 'nomothetics'."]],"krv-a-0716":["This skeptical method, however, is essentially proper only to transcendental philosophy alone, and can at a pinch be dispensed with in every other field of investigations, only not in this one. In mathematics its use would be absurd; because no false assertions can hide and make themselves invisible in it, since the proofs must always proceed along the thread of pure intuition, and indeed through an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy a doubt of postponement may well be useful, but at least no misunderstanding is possible that could not easily be removed, and in experience the final means of deciding the dispute must after all lie, whether they be found early or late. Morals can give its principles all together also *in concreto*, along with the practical consequences, at least in possible experiences, and thereby avoid the misunderstanding of abstraction. By contrast the transcendental assertions, which lay claim to insights extending even beyond the field of all possible experiences, are neither in the case that their abstract synthesis could be given in some intuition *a priori*, nor so constituted that the misunderstanding could be discovered by means of some experience. Transcendental reason therefore permits no other touchstone than the attempt at the unification of its assertions among themselves, and hence beforehand of the free and unhindered contest of them with one another, and this we now want to institute.*)",["Transscendentalphilosophie = transcendental philosophy; Anschauung = intuition; Beweis = proof; Mittel = means; Grundsatz = principle; Beschaffenheit (beschaffen) = 'constituted'.","Latin *in concreto*, *a priori* kept in italics. 'a priori' could attach to 'gegeben' or to 'Anschauung'; left as ambiguous as in the German.","'ein Zweifel des Aufschubs' rendered literally as 'a doubt of postponement'.","'Erfahrungen' plural kept; the footnote marker *) follows 'anstellen' in the original; the note is the next passage."]],"krv-a-0717":["*) The antinomies follow one another in the order of the transcendental ideas cited above.",["Footnote to 'anstellen' in the preceding passage."]],"krv-a-0718":["The Antinomy of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-a-0719":["First Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",["Widerstreit = conflict."]],"krv-a-0720":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0721":["The world has a beginning in time and is also enclosed in boundaries as to space.",["Gränzen = 'boundaries' (Grenze)."]],"krv-a-0722":["Proof.",["Beweis."]],"krv-a-0723":["For one assumes the world has no beginning as to time: then up to every given point of time an eternity has elapsed, and consequently an infinite series of states of things in the world following upon one another has passed away. Now, however, the infinity of a series consists precisely in this: that it can never be completed through successive synthesis. Thus an infinite elapsed series of the world is impossible, consequently a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence, which was first to be proved.",["Zustände = states; Dasein = existence; Ding = thing; Welt = world; Reihe = series; 'Weltreihe' = 'series of the world'.","'Ewigkeit' = eternity; 'Zeitpuncte' = point of time."]],"krv-a-0724":["With regard to the second, one assumes the opposite in turn: then the world will be an infinite given whole of simultaneously existing things. Now we can think the magnitude of a *quantum* that is not given within certain boundaries of every intuition,*) in no other way than only through the synthesis of the parts, and the totality of such a *quantum* only through the completed synthesis, or through repeated addition of unity to itself**). Accordingly, in order to think the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world would have to be regarded as completed, i.e., an infinite time would have to be regarded as elapsed, in the counting-through of all coexisting things, which is impossible. Accordingly an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be regarded as a given whole, consequently also not as given simultaneously. A world is consequently, as to extension in space, not infinite, but enclosed in its boundaries; which was the second.",["Grösse = magnitude; Totalität = totality (Allheit in the glossary is a different word); Ausdehnung = extension; wirklich = actual; Theil = part.","Latin *Quanti* (genitive) rendered as *quantum*; the Latin is kept in italics.","Ambiguity preserved: 'nicht innerhalb gewissen Gränzen ieder Anschauung gegeben wird' — 'ieder' could modify 'Anschauung' (of every intuition) or be read otherwise; rendered literally.","Footnote markers *) and **) kept at their places in the text."]],"krv-a-0725":["*) We can intuit an indeterminate *quantum* as a whole if it is enclosed in boundaries, without needing to construct its totality through measurement, i.e., the successive synthesis of its parts. For the boundaries already determine the completeness, in that they cut off everything more.",["'alles Mehrere' rendered literally as 'everything more'.","anschauen = intuit (verb of Anschauung); Messung = measurement."]],"krv-a-0726":["**) The concept of totality is in this case nothing other than the representation of the completed synthesis of its parts, because, since we cannot abstract the concept from the intuition of the whole (as that is impossible in this case), we can grasp it only through the synthesis of the parts, up to the completion of the infinite, at least in the idea.",["Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation; Idee = idea; fassen = 'grasp'; 'abziehen' = 'abstract'.","'diesen' (the concept) and 'es' referents left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0727":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0728":["The world has no beginning and no boundaries in space, but is, as much with regard to time as to space, infinite.",["Gränzen = boundaries."]],"krv-a-0729":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0730":["For let one posit: it has a beginning. Since the beginning is an existence preceded by a time in which the thing is not, a time must have preceded in which the world was not, i.e., an empty time. Now in an empty time no arising of any thing is possible; because no part of such a time has, over another, any distinguishing condition of existence rather than of non-being in itself (one may assume that it arises of itself or through another cause). Thus in the world many a series of things can indeed begin, but the world itself can have no beginning, and is therefore, with regard to past time, infinite.",["Daseyn = existence; Nichtseyn = non-being; Ursache = cause.","'vor einem anderen' and 'vor die des Nichtseyns' are rendered 'over another' and 'rather than of non-being' for 'vor'; the German is compressed and the construction is left as it stands.","'man setze' rendered 'let one posit' (setzen = posit); 'Ansehung' = 'regard'."]],"krv-a-0731":["As for the second, let one first assume the opposite: that the world is, according to space, finite and bounded, so it finds itself in an empty space that is not bounded. Thus there would be encountered not only a relation of things in space, but also of things to space. Since the world is now an absolute whole, outside which no object of intuition, and consequently no *correlatum* of the world, is encountered, to which it might stand in relation, the relation of the world to empty space would be a relation of it to no object. But such a relation, and consequently also the bounding of the world by empty space, is nothing; thus the world is, according to space, not bounded at all, i.e., it is with regard to extension infinite*).",["Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Ausdehnung = extension; Verhältniß = relation; Ganze = whole.","begränzt / Begränzung = bounded / bounding. The sentence 'so befindet sie sich...' follows the German word order, with its 'so' after the 'daß'-clause.","*Correlatum* is the Latin word as printed; '*)' is Kant's footnote sign, which refers to the next passage."]],"krv-a-0732":["*) Space is merely the form of outer intuition (formal intuition), but no actual object that can be intuited outwardly. Space, prior to all things that determine it (fill or bound it), or that rather give an empirical intuition conforming to its form, is, under the name of absolute space, nothing other than the mere possibility of outer appearances, insofar as they either exist in themselves, or can still be added to given appearances. Empirical intuition is thus not composed of appearances and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the other's *correlatum* of synthesis, but only combined in one and the same empirical intuition, as matter and form of it. If one wants to posit one of these two pieces outside the other (space outside all appearances) there arise from this all sorts of empty determinations of outer intuition, which are nonetheless not possible perceptions. E.g., motion, or rest of the world in infinite empty space, a determination of the relation of the two to one another, which can never be perceived, and is therefore also the predicate of a mere thought-entity.",["OCR 'Naumes' silently corrected to 'Raumes'.","'vor allen Dingen' is kept as 'prior to all things'; 'vor' could also be read 'before' in a non-temporal sense.","'eine dieser zween Stücke' is kept with its grammatical mismatch ('one of these two pieces').","Gedankending = thought-entity (*ens rationis*); Wahrnehmung = perception; Verbindung/verbunden = combination/combined; Materie = matter; Prädicat = predicate."]],"krv-a-0733":["Remark on the First Antinomy. I. On the Thesis",["Thesis, Antinomie kept as cognates."]],"krv-a-0734":["In these conflicting arguments I have not sought dazzling tricks, perhaps (as one says) to conduct a lawyer's proof, which makes use of the incautiousness of the opponent to its advantage, and gladly lets his appeal to a misunderstood law count, in order to build its own unjust claims on the refutation of it. Each of these proofs has been drawn from the nature of the matter and the advantage set aside that the fallacious inferences of the dogmatists on both sides could give us.",["Beweis = proof; Gesetz = law; 'Sache Natur' = 'the nature of the matter' (genitive 'der Sache' omitted in the German as printed).","'von beiden Theilen' = 'on both sides' (Theil = part, elsewhere 'part'); Fehlschlüsse = fallacious inferences.","'ein mißverstanden Gesetz' preserves the uninflected adjective of the original."]],"krv-a-0735":["I could also have proved the thesis in appearance by having sent ahead, according to the habit of the dogmatists, a faulty concept of the infinity of a given magnitude. A magnitude is infinite beyond which none greater (i.e., beyond the multitude contained in it of a given unity) is possible. Now no multitude is the greatest, because one or more unities can still always be added. Thus an infinite given magnitude, and consequently also an infinite world (in respect of the elapsed series as well as of extension) is impossible: it is therefore bounded on both sides. Thus I could have conducted my proof: but this concept does not agree with what one understands by an infinite whole. It does not thereby represent how great it is, consequently its concept is also not the concept of a *maximum*, but only its relation to an arbitrarily assumable unity, with regard to which it is greater than all number, is thereby thought. According as the unity is now assumed greater or smaller, the infinite would be greater or smaller, but the infinity, since it consists merely in the relation to this given unity, would always remain the same, although to be sure the absolute magnitude of the whole would not be cognized at all thereby, which is also not at issue here.",["Einheit = unity throughout (so 'a given unity', 'unities'); Menge = multitude; Grösse = magnitude; Zahl = number; Ganze = whole; erkant = cognized.","'dem Scheine nach' = 'in appearance' (Schein = illusion elsewhere in the glossary; the phrase may also be read 'to all appearances').","'in Ansehung deren dasselbe...' : 'deren' refers to the unity, 'dasselbe' to the infinite whole or the relation; left ambiguous as in the German.","*maximum* is Latin as printed; 'davon auch hier nicht die Rede ist' kept literal ('of which also here there is no talk' = 'which is also not at issue here')."]],"krv-a-0736":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0737":["The proof for the infinity of the given world-series and of the sum total of the world rests on this: that in the opposite case, an empty time, likewise an empty space, would have to make up the boundary of the world. Now it is not unknown to me that evasions are sought against this consequence, in that one pretends: a boundary of the world, according to time and space, is quite possible, without one's having to assume precisely an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extended outside the actual world, which is impossible. I am quite well satisfied with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnizian school. Space is merely the form of outer intuition, but no actual object that can be intuited outwardly, and no *correlatum* of appearances, but the form of appearances itself. Space can therefore not occur absolutely (by itself alone) as something determining in the existence of things, because it is no object at all, but only the form of possible objects. Things, therefore, as appearances, do indeed determine space, i.e., among all its possible predicates (magnitude and relation) they make it be that these or those belong to actuality; but conversely space, as something that subsists by itself, cannot determine the actuality of things with regard to magnitude or shape, because in itself it is nothing actual. A space (be it full or empty*) can therefore indeed be bounded by appearances, but appearances cannot be bounded by an empty space outside them. The very same holds also of time. All this now granted, it is nonetheless indisputable: that one must assume these two non-things, the empty space outside and the empty time before the world, if one assumes a boundary of the world, be it according to space or according to time.",["Weltinbegriff rendered 'sum total of the world' (Inbegriff = sum total); Weltreihe = 'world-series'; Weltgränze = 'boundary of the world'.","Consequenz = 'consequence' (Latinate; Folgerung is also 'consequence'); Gegenstand = object; Wirklichkeit/wirklich = actuality/actual; Undinge = non-things.","'vor sich allein' / 'vor sich besteht' = 'by itself alone' / 'subsists by itself'; 'vor der Welt Anfang' = 'before the beginning of the world'.","'es sey dem Raume oder der Zeit nach' and 'es sey eine Gränze...' keep the subjunctive of the German; *correlatum* is Latin as printed."]],"krv-a-0738":["*) One easily notices that what is meant to be said hereby is: empty space, insofar as it is bounded by appearances, consequently that within the world, does not contradict, at least not the transcendental principles, and can therefore with regard to these be granted (although its possibility is not on that account straightaway asserted).",["'widerspreche, wenigstens nicht den transscendentalen Principien' is kept literal, with the ambiguity of 'at least not' preserved.","eingeräumt = granted; behaupten = assert."]],"krv-a-0739":["The true (transcendental) concept of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the traversal of a *quantum* can never be completed*). From this it follows quite certainly: that an eternity of actual states following one another up to a given (the present) point of time cannot have elapsed, the world must therefore have a beginning.",["Durchmessung = 'traversal' (literally 'measuring through'); Einheit = unity; Zustände = states; Zeitpuncte = point of time.","*quantum* is Latin as printed; the comma before 'die Welt also...' is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-0740":["*) This thereby contains a multitude (of given unity) that is greater than all number, which is the mathematical concept of the infinite.",["'Dieses' has no stated antecedent in the passage; left as 'This'.","Menge = multitude; Einheit = unity; Zahl = number."]],"krv-a-0741":["With regard to the second part of the thesis, the difficulty of an infinite and yet elapsed series indeed falls away; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is given simultaneously. But in order to think the totality of such a multitude, since we cannot appeal to boundaries that constitute this totality of themselves in intuition, we must give an account of our concept, which in such a case cannot go from the whole to the determinate multitude of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole through the successive synthesis of the parts. Since this synthesis would now have to constitute a series never to be completed: one cannot think a totality before it, and consequently also not through it. For the concept of totality itself is in this case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts, and this completion, consequently also the concept of it, is impossible.",["Ausdehnung = extension; Totalität = totality; Menge = multitude; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Vorstellung = representation.","Gränzen is rendered 'boundaries' (Grenze).","'bestimten' (for bestimmten) and 'kan' are old spellings; rendered 'determinate' and 'can'.","'nicht vor ihr, und mithin auch nicht durch sie': the German 'vor' could be 'before' or 'prior to'; 'before' kept to preserve this.","'nie zu vollendende' kept as 'never to be completed'; 'Vollendung' = 'completion'."]],"krv-a-0742":["For as regards the way out by which one seeks to evade the consequence according to which we say: that if the world has boundaries (in time and space), the infinite void must determine the existence of actual things as to their magnitude, it consists secretly only in this: that instead of a world of sense one thinks to oneself, who knows what, intelligible world, and, instead of the first beginning (an existence before which a time of non-being precedes), thinks to oneself an existence in general, which presupposes no other condition in the world, instead of the boundary of extension thinks limitations of the world-whole, and thereby gets out of the way of time and space. But here there is talk only of the *mundus phaenomenon* and of its magnitude, in which one can by no means abstract from the thought conditions of sensibility without sublating the essence of it. The world of sense, if it is bounded, necessarily lies in the infinite void. If one wants to leave this out, and consequently space in general, as a condition of the possibility of appearances *a priori*, then the whole world of sense falls away. In our problem only this is given to us. The *mundus intelligibilis* is nothing but the general concept of a world in general, in which one abstracts from all conditions of the intuition of it, and with regard to which consequently no synthetic proposition at all is possible, neither affirmative nor negative.",["Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Wesen = essence (in 'das Wesen desselben'); Schranken = limitations; Gränze = boundary.","Daseyn = existence; Grösse = magnitude; Aufgabe = problem; Erscheinungen = appearances; Satz = proposition; Bedingung = condition.","'wer weiß welche, intelligibele Welt' is awkward in the German and kept so ('who knows what, intelligible world').","'gedachten Bedingungen der Sinnlichkeit' is literally 'thought conditions'; the sense of 'gedacht' here is left as in the German.","'aufheben' rendered 'sublate' in the one place it appears here; elsewhere 'abstrahiren' = 'abstract'. Latin *mundus phaenomenon* kept as printed (original spelling 'phænomenon').","'beiahend' = 'bejahend' (affirmative); 'verneinend' = negative."]],"krv-a-0743":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["rein = pure; Vernunft = reason."]],"krv-a-0744":["second conflict of the transcendental ideas.",["Widerstreit = conflict; Idee = idea; 'transscendentalen' = transcendental."]],"krv-a-0745":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0746":["Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists everywhere nothing except the simple, or what is composed of it.",["zusammengesetzt = composite; einfach = simple; Theil = part; 'existiret' = exists (Existenz).","'aus diesem' = 'of it' (referring to 'das Einfache'); 'überall nichts' kept as 'everywhere nothing'."]],"krv-a-0747":["Proof.",["Beweis = proof."]],"krv-a-0748":["For assume: the composite substances did not consist of simple parts, then, if all composition were removed in thought, no composite part, and (since there are no simple parts) also no simple one, consequently nothing at all would remain, consequently no substance would have been given. Either, therefore, it is impossible to remove all composition in thought, or after its removal something subsisting without all composition, i.e., the simple, must remain. But in the first case the composite would in turn not consist of substances (because with these composition is only a contingent relation of substances, without which these, as beings persisting for themselves, must subsist). Since now this case contradicts the presupposition, only the second remains: that namely the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.",["aufheben = remove (in 'in Gedanken aufheben'); Wesen = beings; zufällig = contingent; beharrlich = persisting; Voraussetzung = presupposition; widerspricht = contradicts (Widerspruch).","'vor sich beharrliche Wesen' rendered 'beings persisting for themselves'; 'vor' = 'für'.","'bestehen' rendered 'consist' with 'aus' and 'subsist' where used absolutely."]],"krv-a-0749":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0750":["No composite thing in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nowhere anything simple in it.",["'Kein zusammengesezte Ding' is ungrammatical in the German (for 'Kein zusammengesetztes'); Ding = thing.","'überall nichts' here rendered 'nowhere anything' to fit the English; the same German phrase appears in the Thesis, rendered 'everywhere nothing'."]],"krv-a-0751":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0752":["Suppose: a composite thing (as substance) consisted of simple parts. Because all external relation, consequently also all composition out of substances, is possible only in space: the space it occupies must consist of just as many parts as the composite consists of. Now space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely first parts of everything composite are simple. Therefore the simple occupies a space. Since now everything real that occupies a space comprehends in itself a manifold lying outside one another, consequently is composite, and indeed as a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot be outside one another without substance), consequently of substances, the simple would be a substantial composite, which contradicts itself.",["Verhältniß = relation; Zusammensetzung = composition; Raum = space; Reale = 'real' (n.); Accidenzen = accidents; Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); 'schlechthin' = 'absolutely'.","'aus so viel Theilen ... aus eben so viel Theilen' kept as in the German.","'einnehmen' = 'occupy'; 'fassen ... in sich' = 'comprehend in itself' (not begreifen).","'ausserhalb einander befindliches' = 'lying outside one another'; 'ausser einander seyn' = 'be outside one another'."]],"krv-a-0753":["The second proposition of the antithesis, that nothing simple at all exists in the world, is here to signify only as much as: The existence of the absolutely simple could be demonstrated from no experience or perception, neither outer nor inner, and the absolutely simple is thus a mere idea, whose objective reality can never be demonstrated in any possible experience, consequently in the exposition of appearances is without all application and object. For we want to assume that an object of experience could be found for this transcendental idea: then the empirical intuition of some object would have to be recognized as one that absolutely contains no manifold outside one another and combined into unity. Since now from the non-consciousness of a manifold to the total impossibility of such in any intuition of the same object no inference holds, but the latter is thoroughly necessary for absolute simplicity, it follows: that this cannot be concluded from any perception, whichever it may be. Since therefore something as an absolutely simple object can never be given in any possible experience, but the world of sense must be regarded as the sum total of all possible experiences: nothing simple at all is given in it.",["bedeuten = signify; Erfahrung = experience; Wahrnehmung = perception; Realität = reality; Exposition (Latinate) = exposition; Anwendung = application; Gegenstand and Obiect/Obiects both = 'object'; Gegenstand (Latinate Obiect marked).","'erkant' = erkannt, rendered 'recognized' (erkennen); 'Schluß' = inference; 'geschlossen' = 'concluded'.","'Nichtbewustseyn' = 'non-consciousness' (Bewußtsein); Inbegriff = sum total; Einheit = unity.","'vor diese transscendentale Idee' = 'for this transcendental idea' (vor = für).","'ein solches' refers to 'Mannigfaltigen'; 'dieses leztere' = 'the latter'; 'schlechthin' = 'absolutely'.","'als eine solche erkant werden, welche' kept literal; 'obiective' = objective."]],"krv-a-0754":["From this it follows immediately: that the things of the world are all simple beings, that composition is only an external state of them, and that, even if we can never completely set the elementary substances out of this state of combination and isolate them, reason must nevertheless think them as the first subjects of all composition and consequently, prior to it, as simple beings.",["Zustand = state; Verbindung = combination; Vernunft = reason; Wesen = beings; Composition kept as 'composition'; Subiect = subject.","'setzen' = 'set' (setzen), not 'posit' since used with 'aus ... Zustande'; 'vor derselben' = 'prior to it'.","'gleich' in 'wenn ... gleich ... doch' rendered 'even if ... nevertheless'."]],"krv-a-0755":["This second proposition of the antithesis goes much further than the first, which banishes the simple only from the intuition of the composite, whereas this one removes it from the whole of nature, whence it also could not be proved from the concept of a given object of outer intuition (of the composite), but only from the relation of the same to a possible experience in general.",["Gegenstand = object; Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Natur = nature; Verhältniß = relation.","'verbant' = verbannt; 'wegschaft' = wegschafft.","'er' (in 'daher er auch nicht') and 'desselben' are left as in the German; 'er' refers to the second proposition ('dieser Satz'), 'desselben' to the given object."]],"krv-a-0756":["Remark on the second antinomy. I. On the thesis.",["Anmerkung = remark; Antinomie = antinomy."]],"krv-a-0757":["When I speak of a whole that necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand by this only a substantial whole, as the proper *compositum*, i.e., that contingent unity of the manifold which, given separately (at least in thought), is posited in a reciprocal combination, and thereby constitutes one. Space ought properly not to be called *compositum* but *totum*, because the parts of it are possible only in the whole and not the whole through the parts. It could at most be called a *compositum ideale*, but not *reale*. Yet this is only subtlety. Since space is no composite of substances (not even of real accidents), then, if I cancel all composition in it, nothing, not even the point, can remain; for this is possible only as the boundary of a space, (hence of a composite). Space and time therefore do not consist of simple parts. What belongs only to the state of a substance, although it has a magnitude, (e.g., alteration), also does not consist of the simple, i.e., a certain degree of alteration does not arise through an accretion of many simple alterations. Our inference from the composite to the simple holds only of things subsisting for themselves. But accidents of the state do not subsist for themselves. One can therefore easily spoil the proof for the necessity of the simple, as the constituent of everything substantial-composite, and thereby its cause in general, if one extends it too far and wants to make it valid for everything composite without distinction, as has actually already happened several times.",["einfach = simple; Zusammensetzung = composition; Zusammengesetztes = composite; Bestandtheil = constituent; Veränderung = alteration (not in glossary); Accidenzen = accidents.","Gränze = boundary; Anwachs = accretion; Sache in 'seine Sache' rendered 'its cause' (the German could also be read as 'its case'; doubtful).","'muß ... nichts ... übrig bleiben' is rendered 'can remain' for the sense of the negated 'muß'; literally 'must ... remain'. Latin terms compositum, totum, ideale, reale kept in italics."]],"krv-a-0758":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",["Anmerkung = remark."]],"krv-a-0759":["Against this proposition of an infinite division of matter, whose ground of proof is merely mathematical, objections are brought forward by the monadists, which already make themselves suspect by this: that they do not want to let the clearest mathematical proofs count as insights into the constitution of space, insofar as it is in fact the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but only regard them as inferences from abstract but arbitrary concepts that could not be related to actual things. As if it were even possible to think up another kind of intuition than that which is given in the original intuition of space, and the determinations of it a priori did not at the same time concern everything that is possible only through its filling this space. If one gives them a hearing: then one would have to think, besides the mathematical point, which is simple, but no part, rather merely the boundary of a space, also physical points, which are also simple, but have the prerogative, as parts of space, of filling the same through their mere aggregation. Without now repeating here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which one finds in multitude, as it is indeed entirely in vain to want to rationalize away the evidence of mathematics through merely discursive concepts, I remark only: that if philosophy here chicanes with mathematics, it happens because it forgets: that in this question it is only a matter of appearances and their condition. Here, however, it is not enough to find, for the pure concept of the understanding of the composite, the concept of the simple, but, for the intuition of the composite (of matter), the intuition of the simple, and this is according to laws of sensibility, hence also with objects of the senses, entirely impossible. Thus, of a whole of substances, which is thought merely through pure understanding, it may always hold: that before all composition of it we must have the simple, yet this does not hold of the *totum substantiale phaenomenon*, which, as empirical intuition in space, carries with it the necessary property: that no part of it is simple, because no part of space is simple. Meanwhile the monadists have been subtle enough to want to evade this difficulty by presupposing not space as a condition of the possibility of the objects of outer intuition (bodies), but these, and the dynamical relation of substances in general, as the condition of the possibility of space. Now we have a concept of bodies only as appearances, but as such they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of the possibility of all outer appearance, and the evasion is therefore futile, as it has also been sufficiently cut off above in the transcendental aesthetic. Were they things in themselves, the proof of the monadists would certainly hold.",["Beweisgrund = ground of proof; vernünfteln = rationalize (glossary); 'vor Einsichten' read as 'für Einsichten'; 'kläreste' = clearest.","Gegenstand(e) = object(s) throughout; Eigentschaft (sic, for Eigenschaft) = property (Beschaffenheit would also be 'property' in the glossary).","Latin *totum substantiale phaenomenon* kept in italics; 'a priori' left unitalicized as in the German's usage.","'Es mag also ... immer gelten, so gilt dieses doch nicht' kept as a concessive 'may ... hold, yet ... does not hold'; 'chicanirt' = 'chicanes'."]],"krv-a-0760":["Incidentally, I speak here only of the simple insofar as it is necessarily given in the composite, in that the latter can be resolved into it as into its constituents. The proper meaning of the word *Monas* (according to Leibniz's use) should presumably go only to the simple that is given immediately as simple substance (e.g., in self-consciousness) and not as element of the composite, which one could better call the *Atomus*. And since I want to prove the simple substances, as the elements of it, only with regard to the composite, I could call the antithesis of the second antinomy transcendental atomistics. But since this word has long since been used for the designation of a particular way of explaining corporeal appearances (*molecularum*), and thus presupposes empirical concepts, it may be called the dialectical principle of monadology.",["Bedeutung = meaning; Gebrauch = use; Selbstbewußtsein = self-consciousness; Grundsatz = principle; Erklärungsart = way of explaining (Art = way).","'er' in 'so mag er ... heissen' is masculine in the German and its referent is not made explicit; rendered 'it'. 'solte wol' = 'should presumably'.","Latin *Monas*, *Atomus*, *molecularum* kept in italics as printed."]],"krv-a-0761":["The second dialectical assertion has this peculiarity about it: that it has against it a dogmatic assertion, which among all rationalizing ones is the only one that undertakes to prove evidently, on an object of experience, the actuality of that which we above counted merely among transcendental ideas, namely the absolute simplicity of substance: namely that the object of inner sense, the I that thinks there, is a downright simple substance. Without going into this now, (since it has been weighed more extensively above) I remark only: that if something is thought merely as object, without adding any synthetic determination of its intuition, (as indeed this happens through the quite bare representation: I) then to be sure nothing manifold and no composition could be perceived in such a representation. Since moreover the predicates through which I think this object are merely intuitions of inner sense, nothing can occur in it either that would prove a manifold outside one another, hence real composition. Thus self-consciousness only brings it with it that, because the subject that thinks is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself (although the determinations inhering in it); for with regard to itself every object is absolute unity. Nonetheless, if this subject is considered externally, as an object of intuition, it would presumably still show composition in the appearance in itself. But it must be considered so at all times if one wants to know whether there is in it a manifold outside one another or not.",["Behauptung = assertion; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Simplicität = simplicity (Latinate, einfach = simple); Gegenstand = object; Obiect/Object also 'object'.","'so könne ... wahrgenommen werden' is subjunctive (indirect speech after 'bemerke ich nur'); rendered 'could be perceived'. 'Erscheinung an sich' kept literally as 'appearance in itself'.","'(obgleich die ihm inhärirende Bestimmungen)' is elliptical and kept elliptical; the German does not say what the clause lacks. Prädicate = predicates; schlechthin = downright.","'zeigen' and 'wol' rendered 'show' and 'presumably'; 'inhärirende' = inhering."]],"krv-a-0762":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["Der Antinomie ... is a fragment of the heading (genitive/dative case in German); rendered with 'Of' and left uncompleted."]],"krv-a-0763":["Third Conflict of the Transcendental Ideas.",["Widerstreit = conflict; Idee = idea."]],"krv-a-0764":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0765":["Causality in accordance with laws of nature is not the only one from which the appearances of the world all together can be derived. It is necessary to assume yet a causality through freedom for the explanation of them.",["Caussalität = causality; Erscheinungen = appearances; Welt = world; Erklärung = explanation; insgesamt = all together.","'derselben' is rendered 'of them' (the plural 'appearances' is the nearest plural referent; the German form is also compatible with a feminine singular, e.g. 'Caussalität')."]],"krv-a-0766":["Proof.",["Beweis = proof."]],"krv-a-0767":["Assume: there is no other causality than in accordance with laws of nature; then everything that happens presupposes a previous state, upon which it unfailingly follows according to a rule. Now, however, the previous state itself must be something that has happened (has come to be in time, since it was not there before), because, if it had always been there, its sequel too would not have first arisen, but would always have been. Thus the causality of the cause, through which something happens, is itself something that has happened, which according to the law of nature in turn presupposes a previous state and its causality, but this one likewise an even older one, and so on. If, therefore, everything happens according to mere laws of nature, then there is always only a subaltern, but never a first beginning, and thus in general no completeness of the series on the side of the causes descending from one another. Now, however, the law of nature consists precisely in this: that nothing happens without a cause sufficiently determined a priori. Thus the proposition, as if all causality were possible only according to laws of nature, contradicts itself in its unrestricted generality, and this generality therefore cannot be assumed as the only one.",["Caussalität rendered 'causality' (original spelling 'Caussalität'); Ursache = 'cause'; Folge = 'sequel' per glossary; Zustand = 'state'; Gesetz = 'law'.","'geschehen' = 'happen'; 'Geschehenes' = 'something that has happened'; 'werden' in 'in der Zeit geworden' = 'come to be'.","'hinreichend' rendered 'sufficiently' (glossary lists 'zureichend' = sufficient; 'hinreichend' is a different German word, same English).","'subaltern' kept as in the German.","'Satz' rendered 'proposition'; 'diese' in the last clause is feminine and refers back to 'Allgemeinheit' ('generality'); 'die einzige' is likewise feminine, so 'as the only one' is ambiguous in English as it is not in German only insofar as the antecedent is fixed grammatically."]],"krv-a-0768":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0769":["There is no freedom, but everything in the world happens solely in accordance with laws of nature:",["The passage ends with a colon, as in the German; the proof follows."]],"krv-a-0770":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0771":["Suppose: there is a freedom in the transcendental sense, as a special kind of causality according to which the occurrences of the world could ensue, namely a faculty of absolutely beginning a state, hence also a series of consequences of it, then not only will a series begin absolutely through this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, i.e., the causality will begin absolutely, so that nothing precedes through which this action that takes place is determined according to constant laws. But every beginning to act presupposes a state of the cause that is not yet acting, and a dynamically first beginning of the action, a state that has no interconnection of causality at all with the preceding one of the very same cause, i.e., in no way follows from it. Thus transcendental freedom is contrary to the causal law, and is such a combination of the successive states of efficient causes according to which no unity of experience is possible, which therefore is also not encountered in any experience, hence is an empty thought-entity.",["'im transscendentalen Verstande' rendered 'in the transcendental sense'; Verstand here is the word otherwise rendered 'understanding' (the phrase is idiomatic, 'in the transcendental meaning').","'Begebenheiten' = 'occurrences'; 'Art' = 'kind' (glossary also allows 'way', 'species', 'manner'); 'Folgen' = 'consequences' (not Folgerung); 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'.","'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'; 'Verbindung' = 'combination'; 'Gedankending' = 'thought-entity' (ens rationis).","'Hervorbrinnung' is Kant's spelling of 'Hervorbringung' ('production'). 'wirkender Ursachen' = 'efficient causes' (Latin: causae efficientes).","'einen Zustand' after 'und ein ... Anfang der Handlung' depends on 'sezt ... voraus' ('presupposes'), carried over elliptically; the German is kept elliptical. 'der noch nicht handelnden Ursache' = 'of the cause not yet acting'.","'daraus erfolgt': 'erfolgen' rendered 'ensue' in the first sentence and 'follow' here; 'folgen' elsewhere = 'follow'."]],"krv-a-0772":["Accordingly a causality must be assumed through which something happens without the cause of it being further determined, through another preceding cause, according to necessary laws, i.e., an absolute spontaneity of the causes to begin of themselves a series of appearances that runs according to laws of nature, hence transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of appearances on the side of the causes is never complete.",["'Reihenfolge' rendered 'succession'; the glossary's Abfolge is also 'succession' (here the word is Reihenfolge).","'von selbst' = 'of themselves' (the antecedent 'Ursachen' is plural; 'Spontaneität' is singular, so 'of itself' is also grammatically possible).","This passage belongs to the Thesis proof; its place after the Antithesis proof in this list follows the source sequence given."]],"krv-a-0773":["We therefore have nothing but nature in which we must seek the interconnection and order of the occurrences of the world. Freedom (independence) from the laws of nature is indeed a liberation from coercion, but also from the guideline of all rules. For one cannot say that, in place of the laws of nature, laws of freedom enter into the causality of the course of the world, because, if this were determined according to laws, it would not be freedom, but itself nothing other than nature. Nature, therefore, and transcendental freedom differ as lawfulness and lawlessness, of which the former indeed burdens the understanding with the difficulty of seeking the descent of the occurrences ever higher up in the series of causes, because the causality in them is always conditioned, but as compensation promises thoroughgoing and law-governed unity of experience, whereas the semblance of freedom, to be sure, promises rest to the inquiring understanding in the chain of causes, in that it leads it to an unconditioned causality that begins to act of itself, but which, since it is itself blind, tears off the guideline of rules, by which alone a thoroughly coherent experience is possible.",["'Blendwerk' rendered 'semblance'; it is not the glossary's 'Schein' (illusion).","'Caussalitat' in the source is an OCR/typographic slip for 'Caussalität'.","'diese' (feminine) in 'wenn diese nach Gesetzen bestimt wäre' refers grammatically to 'Caussalität'; 'sie' likewise; 'it' preserves this antecedent ambiguity only imperfectly in English.","'iene' = 'the former' (Natur/Gesetzmässigkeit); 'an ihnen' = 'in them' (the occurrences, Begebenheiten).","'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'; 'zusammenhängende' = 'coherent'; 'Leitfaden' = 'guideline'; 'bedingt' = 'conditioned'; 'unbedingt' = 'unconditioned'; 'Zwang' = 'coercion'.","'die aber, da sie selbst blind ist': the relative 'die' (feminine) is ambiguous between 'Caussalität' and 'Freiheit'; kept as 'which'. 'Abstammung' = 'descent'."]],"krv-a-0774":["Remark on the third Antinomy. I. On the Thesis.",["'Anmerkung' = 'Remark'."]],"krv-a-0775":["The transcendental idea of freedom, to be sure, by far does not make up the whole content of the psychological concept of this name, which is for the most part empirical, but only that of the absolute spontaneity of the action, as the proper ground of the imputability of it, but is nevertheless the proper stumbling block for philosophy, which finds insurmountable difficulties in admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That, therefore, in the question about the freedom of the will which has always put speculative reason into such great embarrassment, is properly only transcendental and goes solely to this: whether a faculty must be assumed of beginning of itself a series of successive things or states. How such a one is possible, is not just as necessary to be able to answer, since in the case of causality according to laws of nature too we must be content with cognizing a priori that such a one must be presupposed, even though we in no way comprehend the possibility of how through a certain existence the existence of another is posited, and in this regard must hold solely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a first beginning of a series of appearances from freedom, indeed only properly insofar as is requisite for the comprehensibility of an origin of the world, while one can take all subsequent states for a succession according to mere laws of nature. But because thereby the faculty of beginning a series in time entirely of itself is nevertheless once proved (though not insight into), it is now also permitted to us to let various series begin of themselves in the middle of the course of the world, as regards causality, and to attribute to the substances of them a faculty of acting from freedom. But one should not let oneself be held up here by a misunderstanding: that, namely, since a successive series in the world can have only a comparatively first beginning, in that a state of things in the world always precedes, perhaps no absolutely first beginning of the series is possible during the course of the world. For we are not speaking here of the most absolute beginning according to time, but according to causality. If I now (for example) get up from my chair entirely freely, and without the necessarily determining influence of natural causes, a new series begins absolutely in this occurrence, together with its natural consequences into the infinite, although according to time this occurrence is only the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and deed lies not at all in the succession of mere natural effects, and is not a mere continuation of them, but the determining natural causes cease entirely above them, with regard to this event, which indeed follows upon them, but does not ensue from them, and therefore, though not according to time, yet with regard to causality, must be called an absolutely first beginning of a series of appearances.",["'Imputabilität' kept as 'imputability'; 'Stein des Anstosses' = 'stumbling block'; 'Verlegenheit' = 'embarrassment'.","'bewiesen (obzwar nicht eingesehen)': 'eingesehen' = 'insight into'; the German participial construction is kept elliptical ('proved (though not insight into)'); the sense 'seen into' is the literal reading.","'Begreiflichkeit' = 'comprehensibility'; 'begreifen' = 'comprehend'; 'erkennen' = 'cognize'; 'erfoderlich' = 'requisite'.","'Begebenheit' = 'occurrence'; 'Eräugniß' (Ereigniß) = 'event' (glossary Ereignis); both are 'event/occurrence' distinct in English only by choice. 'Abfolge' = 'succession'; 'Reihe' = 'series'.","'Dasjenige ... was' and the verb 'ist ... und gehet': 'gehet lediglich darauf' = 'goes solely to this'. 'ob' clause kept dependent on 'darauf'.","'Ding' = 'thing'; 'Zustand' = 'state'; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'; 'Ursprung' = 'origin'.","'die bestimmende Naturursachen hören oberhalb derselben ... auf, die zwar auf iene folgt': 'die' (feminine singular) after 'Eräugniß' has as antecedent 'Entschliessung und That' or 'Eräugniß'; the ambiguity is preserved only as 'which', and 'iene' refers to the natural causes/effects. Source text is probably corrupt or irregular here ('bestimmende ... hören' disagreement)."]],"krv-a-0776":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",["'Anmerkung' = 'Remark'."]],"krv-a-0777":["The defender of the omnipotence of nature (transcendental physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would assert his proposition against the rationalizing inferences of the latter in the following way. If you do not assume in the world anything mathematically first in accordance with time, then you also have no need to seek something dynamically first in accordance with causality. Who commanded you to devise an absolutely first state of the world, and hence an absolute beginning of the successively elapsing series of appearances, and, so that you may procure a resting point for your imagination, to set boundaries to unlimited nature. Since the substances in the world have always been, at least the unity of experience makes such a presupposition necessary, there is no difficulty in assuming also: that the alternation of their states, i.e., a series of their alterations, has always been, and hence that no first beginning, neither mathematical nor dynamical, may be sought. The possibility of such an infinite descent, without a first member, in regard to which everything else is merely subsequent, cannot, as to its possibility, be made comprehensible. But if you want to throw away these riddles of nature on that account, then you will see yourselves compelled to reject many synthetic fundamental properties (fundamental forces) that you can comprehend just as little, and even the possibility of an alteration in general must become offensive to you. For if you did not find through experience that it is actual, you would never be able to devise *a priori* how such an incessant sequence of being and non-being is possible.",["Allvermögenheit rendered 'omnipotence'; Einbildung (not Einbildungskraft) rendered 'imagination'.","Veränderung = 'alteration' throughout; Wechsel = 'alternation'; Zustand = 'state'; Folge = 'sequence'.","Grundbeschaffenheiten = 'fundamental properties' (Beschaffenheit = property); Grundkräfte = 'fundamental forces'.","'Wer hat euch geheissen ...' is a sentence ending in a period in the German, not a question mark; kept as is.","Begreifen/begreiflich = 'comprehend'/'comprehensible'; 'Satz' = 'proposition'; 'Schlüsse' = 'inferences'; 'Gränzen' = 'boundaries'."]],"krv-a-0778":["The confirmation of the need of reason to appeal, in the series of natural causes, to a first beginning from freedom, is quite clearly evident from this: that (the Epicurean school excepted) all the philosophers of antiquity saw themselves compelled, for the explanation of the motions of the world, to assume a first mover, i.e., a freely acting cause, which first and of itself began this series of states. For from mere nature they did not venture to make a first beginning comprehensible.",["Bedürfniß (neuter/feminine in the original) = 'need'; Erklärung = 'explanation'.","Philosophen = 'philosophers' (not Weltweisen)."]],"krv-a-0779":["Even if, in the meantime, a transcendental faculty of freedom is at all events conceded in order to begin the alterations of the world, this faculty would nevertheless at least have to be only outside the world (although it always remains a bold presumption to assume, outside the sum total of all possible intuitions, still an object that cannot be given in any possible perception). But to ascribe such a faculty to the substances in the world itself can never be permitted, because then the interconnection of appearances necessarily determining one another in accordance with universal laws, which one calls nature, and with it the mark of empirical truth, which distinguishes experience from dream, would for the most part vanish. For alongside such a lawless faculty of freedom, nature can hardly still be thought; because the laws of the latter are incessantly altered by the influences of the former, and the play of appearances, which in accordance with mere nature would be regular and uniform, is thereby made confused and incoherent.",["Vermögen = 'faculty'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Merkmal = 'mark'.","'ausserhalb dem Inbegriffe' follows the German dative after ausserhalb.","Veränderungen = 'alterations'; abgeändert = 'altered'."]],"krv-a-0780":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["Running heading from the original; given as a fragment of the title (genitive)."]],"krv-a-0781":["fourth conflict of the transcendental ideas.",["Widerstreit = 'conflict'; Idee = 'idea'."]],"krv-a-0782":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-a-0783":["To the world belongs something that, either as its part or its cause, is an absolutely necessary being.",["Wesen = 'being'; schlechthin nothwendig = 'absolutely necessary' (cf. absolutnothwendig, also 'absolutely necessary'); Theil = 'part'."]],"krv-a-0784":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0785":["The world of sense, as the whole of all appearances, contains at the same time a series of alterations. For without these even the representation of the time series, as a condition of the possibility of the world of sense, would not be given to us*). Every alteration, however, stands under its condition, which precedes it in accordance with time and under which it is necessary. Now every conditioned that is given presupposes, in regard to its existence, a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. Thus something absolutely necessary must exist, if an alteration exists as its sequel. But this necessary thing itself belongs to the world of sense. For suppose it were outside of it: then the series of alterations of the world would derive its beginning from it, without this necessary cause itself belonging to the world of sense. Now this is impossible. For since the beginning of a time series can be determined only through that which precedes in accordance with time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of alterations must exist in the time when this was not yet, (for the beginning is an existence before which a time precedes in which the thing that begins was not yet). Thus the causality of the necessary cause of the alterations, hence also the cause itself, belongs to time, hence to appearance (in which time alone is possible as its form), consequently it cannot be thought as separated from the world of sense, as the sum total of all appearances. Thus something absolutely necessary is contained in the world itself (whether this be the whole world-series itself, or a part of it).",["Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense' (Sinn = 'sense'); Daseyn and Existenz both = 'existence'; existiren = 'exist'.","Schlechthinunbedingte = 'absolutely unconditioned'; schlechthin nothwendig / absolutnothwendig / Schlechthinnothwendiges all = 'absolutely necessary'.","Folge = 'sequel'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Bedingte = 'conditioned'; bestimt = 'determined'.","The asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0786); parentheses and 'da diese noch nicht war' kept as in German ('diese' could refer to the time or the series; 'when this was not yet' preserves the ambiguity)."]],"krv-a-0786":["*) Time, to be sure, as the formal condition of the possibility of alterations, precedes these objectively, but subjectively, and in the actuality of consciousness, this representation is, like every other, given only on the occasion of perceptions.",["Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Bewustseyn = 'consciousness'; obiectiv/subiectiv = 'objectively'/'subjectively'; Wahrnehmungen = 'perceptions'."]],"krv-a-0787":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-a-0788":["There exists nowhere an absolutely necessary being, neither in the world, nor outside the world as its cause.",["'überall' with negation rendered 'nowhere' (literally 'at all / anywhere'); schlechthinnothwendig = 'absolutely necessary'."]],"krv-a-0789":["Proof.",[]],"krv-a-0790":["Suppose: the world itself, or in it, is a necessary being, then in the series of its alterations there would be either a beginning that would be unconditionally necessary, hence without cause, which conflicts with the dynamical law of the determination of all appearances in time, or the series itself would be without any beginning, and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, in the whole nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned, which contradicts itself; because the existence of a multitude cannot be necessary if no single part of it possesses an existence necessary in itself.",["'die Welt selber, oder in ihr' kept elliptical ('or in it' could mean 'or [a necessary being] in it').","unbedingtnothwendig = 'unconditionally necessary'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Menge = 'multitude'; an sich = 'in itself'; widerstreitet = 'conflicts with'; widerspricht = 'contradicts' (Widerspruch = contradiction).","Bestimmung = 'determination'; Theile = 'parts'."]],"krv-a-0791":["Suppose, on the other hand: there is an absolutely necessary cause of the world outside the world, then it, as the supreme member in the series of the causes of the alterations of the world, would first begin the existence of the latter and their series*). Now it would then, however, also have to begin to act, and its causality would belong to time, but for just that reason to the sum total of appearances, i.e., to the world, consequently it itself, the cause, would not be outside the world, which contradicts the presupposition. Thus neither in the world, nor outside it (but in causal connection with it) is there any absolutely necessary being.",["'dieselbe ... das Daseyn der lezteren und ihre Reihe': 'the latter' refers to the alterations; 'ihre' is ambiguous between the world's alterations and the cause, left as in German ('their series').","Caussalverbindung = 'causal connection' (Verbindung); Voraussetzung = 'presupposition'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'.","The asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-0792)."]],"krv-a-0792":["*) The word: to begin, is taken in a twofold meaning. The first is active, where the cause begins a series of states as its effect (*infit*). The second passive, where the causality in the cause itself commences (*fit*). I infer here from the former to the latter.",["Bedeutung = 'meaning'; Zustände = 'states'; Wirkung = 'effect'; anfangen / anheben both = 'begin' / 'commence' (anheben rendered 'commence'); schliessen = 'infer'.","'lezte' = 'latter', here the second (passive) meaning."]],"krv-a-0793":["Remark on the fourth antinomy I. on the thesis.",["Anmerkung = 'remark'; Thesis kept as 'thesis'."]],"krv-a-0794":["In order to prove the existence of a necessary being, it is incumbent on me here to use no other than a cosmological argument, namely one that ascends from the conditioned in appearance to the unconditioned in concept, in that one regards the latter as the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. To attempt the proof from the mere idea of a supreme being of all beings in general belongs to another principle of reason, and such a proof will therefore have to be presented separately.",["Daseyn = 'existence'; Begriff = 'concept'; Allheit not used here but Totalität = 'totality'; Bedingte/Unbedingte = 'conditioned'/'unconditioned'.","'Princip' = 'principle' (Grundsatz would also be 'principle'); 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'eines obersten aller Wesen' = 'of a supreme of all beings' rendered 'a supreme being of all beings'; the German has no noun after 'obersten'.","'besonders' = 'separately' (could also be 'specially'); 'ein solcher' refers to 'der Beweis' (masculine)."]],"krv-a-0795":["The pure cosmological proof can now demonstrate the existence of a necessary being in no other way than by leaving it at the same time undecided whether this being is the world itself or a thing distinct from it. For in order to make out the latter, principles are required that are no longer cosmological and do not proceed onward in the series of appearances, but rather concepts of contingent beings in general (insofar as they are considered merely as objects of the understanding) and a principle for connecting such beings with a necessary being through mere concepts, all of which belongs to a transcendent philosophy, for which this is not yet the place.",["Beweis = proof; Daseyn = existence; Wesen = being; Erscheinungen = appearances; Begriffe = concepts; Gegenstände = objects; Verstandes = understanding's/of the understanding.","'Grundsätze' rendered 'principles' and 'Princip' also 'principle': one English word for two German/Latinate terms (Grundsatz, Princip).","'vor eine transscendente Philosophie' = 'for a transcendent philosophy' (vor = für, old spelling); Weltweisheit not used here, Philosophie = philosophy.","'das leztere' = 'the latter' (that the necessary being is distinct from the world)."]],"krv-a-0796":["But if one once begins the proof cosmologically, by laying at the ground the series of appearances and the regressus in it according to empirical laws of causality, then one can afterwards not leap away from this and pass over to something that does not belong in the series at all as a member. For something must be regarded as condition in precisely the same meaning in which the relation of the conditioned to its condition was taken in the series, which was supposed to lead to this highest condition in continuous progress. Now if this relation is sensible and belongs to the possible empirical use of the understanding, then the supreme condition or cause can conclude the regressus only according to laws of sensibility, hence only as belonging to the time-series, and the necessary being must be regarded as the supreme member of the world-series.",["Fortschritt = progress; Bedeutung = meaning; Bedingung = condition; Verhältniß and Relation both rendered 'relation' (Relation is the Latinate member; Verhältniß occurs in 'dieses Verhältniß').","Caussalität = causality; Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; sinnlich = sensible.","'Regressus' kept in Latin, italics not added in the source (printed in roman); here set as 'regressus' per method: should be italic.","'beschliessen' = conclude (bring to a close), not 'schliessen' in the sense of infer."]],"krv-a-0797":["II. Remark on the Antithesis.",["Anmerkung = remark; Antithesis kept as the Greek-Latin cognate."]],"krv-a-0798":["If, in ascending in the series of appearances, one believes oneself to encounter difficulties against the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, then these must also not be grounded on mere concepts of the necessary existence of a thing in general and hence not be ontological, but must be found to arise out of the causal connection with a series of appearances, in order to assume for it a condition that is itself unconditioned, and consequently must be concluded cosmologically and according to empirical laws. For it must namely be shown that the ascent in the series of causes (in the world of sense) can never end with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the states of the world, in accordance with their alterations, turns out against the assumption of a first cause, one absolutely beginning the series first. But in this antinomy there shows itself a strange contrast: namely that from precisely the same ground of proof from which in the thesis the existence of an original being was inferred, in the antithesis the non-being of it is inferred, and indeed with the same rigor. First it was said: there is a necessary being, because the whole past time comprises in itself the series of all conditions and with this therefore also the unconditioned (the necessary); now it is said: there is no necessary being, precisely because the whole elapsed time comprises in itself the series of all conditions (which are therefore all in turn conditioned). The cause of this is the following. The first argument looks only to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, of which one determines the other in time, and thereby gets an unconditioned and necessary. The second, on the contrary, takes into consideration the contingency of everything that is determined in the time-series (because before each there precedes a time in which the condition itself must in turn be determined as conditioned), whereby all that is unconditioned, and all absolute necessity, entirely drops away. Meanwhile the mode of inference in both is entirely suited even to common human reason, which more than once falls into the case of falling into disunity with itself, according as it weighs its object from two different standpoints. Herr von Mairan held the dispute of two famous astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty over the choice of standpoint, to be a sufficiently remarkable phenomenon to compose a special treatise about it. The one namely inferred thus: the moon turns about its axis, because it constantly turns the same side toward the earth; the other: the moon does not turn about its axis, precisely because it constantly turns the same side toward the earth. Both inferences were correct, according to the standpoint one took from which one wanted to observe the motion of the moon.",["Schwierigkeiten anzutreffen vermeint, 'so müssen sich diese auch nicht...gründen': the 'auch nicht' is kept literally.","Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Urwesen = original being; Nichtseyn = non-being; Totalität = totality; Zufälligkeit = contingency; zufällig/Zufälligkeit from the same root.","Schluß-/schließen/folgern: 'gefolgert' = concluded, 'geschlossen' = inferred; 'Schlußart' = mode of inference; 'Schlüsse' = inferences.","'Argument' and 'Beweisgrund' (ground of proof) are distinct; Beweis = proof.","'sich mit sich selbst zu entzweien' is rendered 'falling into disunity with itself', awkwardly paralleling the German 'in den Fall geräth'; the syntax is kept.","'Betrachtung' = consideration; 'beobachten' = observe; 'Standpunct' = standpoint; 'Phänomen' = phenomenon.","'Schärfe' = rigor (also 'sharpness'); 'Veränderungen' rendered 'alterations' here but 'Veränderung' = alteration/change elsewhere in these passages is rendered 'alteration' for consistency; 'verändert' = altered."]],"krv-a-0799":["Nevertheless one has taken the freedom to make such a leap (μεταβασις ἐις ἀλλο γενος). For one inferred from the alterations in the world to empirical contingency, i.e., the dependence of these on empirically determining causes, and obtained an ascending series of empirical conditions, which was also quite right. But since one could find in this no first beginning and no supreme member, one suddenly departed from the empirical concept of contingency and took the pure category, which then occasioned a merely intelligible series, whose completeness rested on the existence of an absolutely necessary cause, which now, since it was bound to no sensible conditions, was also freed from the condition of time, of beginning its causality itself. But this procedure is entirely unlawful, as can be inferred from the following.",["Greek phrase kept as printed; accents as in the source (ἐις; standard ἀλλο γένος / εἰς).","Veränderungen = alterations; Categorie = category; intelligibele = intelligible; Vollständigkeit = completeness; widerrechtlich = unlawful.","'Zeitbedingung, ihre Caussalität selbst anzufangen': genitive construction kept; the German is awkward ('from the condition of time, of beginning its causality itself').","'schlechthinnothwendig' = absolutely necessary (schlechthin = absolutely)."]],"krv-a-0800":["Contingent, in the pure sense of the category, is that whose contradictory opposite is possible. Now one cannot infer from empirical contingency to that intelligible one at all. What is altered, the opposite of whose (state) is actual at another time, hence also possible; hence this is not the contradictory opposite of the previous state, for which it is required that in the same time in which the previous state was, its opposite could have been in its place, which cannot be inferred from the alteration at all. A body that was in motion = A comes to rest = non A. From the fact that a state opposed to the state A follows upon it, it cannot at all be inferred that the contradictory opposite of A is possible, hence that A is contingent; for to that it would be required that in the same time in which the motion was, rest could have been in place of it. Now we know nothing further than that rest in the following time was actual, hence also possible. But motion at one time and rest at another time are not contradictorily opposed to one another. Therefore the succession of opposed determinations, i.e., the alteration, by no means proves contingency according to concepts of the pure understanding, and can therefore also not lead to the existence of a necessary being according to pure concepts of the understanding. The alteration proves only empirical contingency, i.e., that the new state could not at all have taken place by itself, without a cause that belongs to the previous time, in accordance with the law of causality. This cause, even if it is assumed as absolutely necessary, must in this way nevertheless be encountered in time, and belong to the series of appearances.",["Gegentheil = opposite; contradictorisch = contradictory; wirklich = actual; Succession = succession (Latinate; Abfolge in glossary is the German counterpart, not used here).","Bestimmungen = determinations; Zustand = state; Veränderung = alteration; Stelle = place (Ort in glossary is 'place', Stelle also 'place': one English word for two German words).","'wissen' = know (Wissen); 'beweiset' = proves; 'Beweis' = proof.","'dessen Gegentheil (seines Zustandes)': parenthesis kept; 'whose (state)' preserves the elliptical construction.","'vor sich selbst' = 'by itself' (vor = für); could also be read 'for itself'.","'zu Folge dem Gesetze' = 'in accordance with the law'; Folge here not the glossary's 'sequel'."]],"krv-a-0801":["Third Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. On the Interest of Reason in this its Conflict.",["Abschnitt = section; Widerstreit = conflict; Interesse = interest; rein = pure."]],"krv-a-0802":["There we now have the whole dialectical play of the cosmological ideas, which do not permit at all that a congruent object be given to them in any possible experience, indeed not even that reason think them in unison with universal laws of experience, which are nevertheless not arbitrarily thought up, but to which reason is necessarily led in the continuous progress of the empirical synthesis, if it wants to free from all condition, and comprehend in its unconditioned totality, what according to rules of experience can always only be determined as conditioned. These rationalizing assertions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason, of which there can therefore be just so many, no more and no fewer, because there are no more series of synthetic presuppositions that bound the empirical synthesis a priori.",["Ideen = ideas; Gegenstand = object; Erfahrung = experience; Fortgang = progress; vernünftelnd = rationalizing (vernünfteln); Behauptung = assertion.","'Problemen' rendered 'problems' (Aufgabe = problem in glossary; here the Latinate Problem is used: one English word for two German words).","'begränzen' = bound (Grenze = bound); 'a priori' may attach to 'begränzen' or to 'Synthesis'/'Voraussetzungen'; the ambiguity is kept.","'fassen' = comprehend, distinct from 'begreifen' (comprehend, comprehendere), which glossary also renders 'comprehend'.","'ia' = 'indeed' (ja); 'willkührlich' = arbitrarily."]],"krv-a-0803":["We have presented the brilliant pretensions of the reason that extends its territory beyond all boundaries of experience only in dry formulas, which contain merely the ground of its rightful claims, and, as befits a transcendental philosophy, have stripped these of everything empirical, although the whole splendor of the assertions of reason can shine forth only in combination with it. In this application, however, and in the progressive extension of the use of reason, in that it begins from the field of experiences and gradually soars up to these sublime ideas, philosophy shows a dignity which, if it could only assert its pretensions, would leave the worth of all other human science far beneath it, in that it promises the foundation for our greatest expectations and prospects regarding the final ends in which all efforts of reason must at last unite. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and some boundary of its extension in space, whether there is somewhere, and perhaps in my thinking self, an indivisible and indestructible unity, or nothing but the divisible and perishable, whether in my actions I am free, or, like other beings, am led on the thread of nature and of fate, whether finally there is a highest cause of the world, or the things of nature and their order constitute the final object at which we must stop in all our considerations: these are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would gladly give up his whole science; for this can after all procure him no satisfaction with regard to the highest and most pressing ends of humanity. Even the proper dignity of mathematics (this pride of human reason) rests on this: that, since it gives reason the guidance to have insight into nature, in the great as well as in the small, in its order and regularity, likewise in the admirable unity of the forces that move it, far beyond all expectation of the philosophy that builds on common experience, it thereby itself gives occasion and encouragement to the use of reason extended beyond all experience, likewise provides the philosophy occupied with it with the most excellent materials, to support its investigation, so far as its constitution permits, through appropriate intuitions.",["Anmassungen = 'pretensions'; Gebiete = 'territory'; Gränzen = 'boundaries'; Ausdehnung = 'extension'.","Philosophie and Weltweisheit are both rendered 'philosophy'; the German words differ.","Materialien (Latinate) = 'materials'; the glossary's Stoff = 'material' is a different word.","The syntax of 'daß, da sie ... giebt, ... einzusehen, sie dadurch selbst ... giebt' is kept as it stands; the 'sie' that 'gives' (the second 'sie') and the 'ihre' of 'ihre Nachforschung' are left as ambiguous as the German.","Wissenschaft = 'science'; 'Wesen' = 'beings'; Zwecke = 'ends'; Betrachtungen = 'considerations'; Anschauungen = 'intuitions'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'. 'Untheilbare/Theilbare' = 'indivisible/divisible'."]],"krv-a-0804":["Unfortunately for speculation (but perhaps fortunately for the practical determination of the human being) reason sees itself, in the midst of its greatest expectations, so entangled in a throng of grounds and counter-grounds that, since it is not feasible, for the sake of its honor as well as even its security, to withdraw and look on at this quarrel with indifference as at a mere sham-fight, still less simply to command peace, because the object of the strife is very interesting, nothing further remains for it but to reflect on the origin of this disunity of reason with itself, whether perhaps a mere misunderstanding might not be to blame for it, after whose exposition proud claims on both sides may indeed fall away, but in their place a lasting, tranquil regime of reason over understanding and senses would take its beginning.",["Gründe/Gegengründe = 'grounds/counter-grounds'; Erörterung = 'exposition'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Gegenstand = 'object'.","'vor' in 'vor die Speculation' and 'vor die practische Bestimmung' is rendered 'for' (modern 'für'); 'davor' = 'in their place' (or 'in return for it').","'dessen' / 'nach dessen Erörterung' refers back to Mißverstand; 'Sinne' = 'senses' (plural of Sinn)."]],"krv-a-0805":["For now we want to postpone this thorough exposition somewhat, and first to take into consideration: to which side we would most like to throw in our lot, if we should perhaps be compelled to take sides. Since in this case we consult not the logical touchstone of truth but merely our interest, such an investigation, although it settles nothing with regard to the disputed right of both parties, will nevertheless have the use of making it comprehensible why the participants in this dispute have preferred to throw themselves on the one side rather than on the other, without a superior insight into the object having been the cause of it, likewise of explaining certain other side-matters, e.g. the zealous heat of the one and the cold assertion of the other party, why they gladly shout joyful applause to the one party and are, in advance, irreconcilably prejudiced against the other.",["Theil (Theile) and Parthey are both rendered 'party' ('both parties' / 'the one party'); in 'der andern Theils' the German Theil is literally 'part/side'.","Erörterung = 'exposition'; Erwegung = 'consideration' (Betrachtung in the glossary is also 'consideration'); Behauptung = 'assertion'; Gegenstand = 'object'.","'voriezt' = 'for now'; 'begreiflich' = 'comprehensible'; the second clause 'imgleichen ... zu erklären' is left parallel to 'es begreiflich zu machen' as in the German."]],"krv-a-0806":["But there is something that determines, in this preliminary assessment, the point of view from which alone it can be carried out with proper thoroughness, and this is the comparison of the principles from which both parties proceed. One notices among the assertions of the antithesis a perfect uniformity of the way of thinking and complete unity of the maxim, namely a *principium* of pure empiricism, not only in the explanation of the appearances in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas of the universe itself. By contrast, the assertions of the thesis lay at the ground, besides the empirical way of explaining within the series of appearances, still intellectual beginnings, and the maxim is to this extent not simple. But I will call it, from its essential distinguishing mark, the dogmatism of pure reason.",["Beurtheilung = 'assessment'; Erscheinungen = 'appearances'; Erklärung = 'explanation' (Erklärungsart = 'way of explaining'); Merkmal = 'mark'; einfach = 'simple'; Grunde = 'ground'.","Principium is printed in Latin in the original (Principien = 'principles').","'Auflösung' = 'solution'; 'Weltall' = 'universe'; 'Denkungsart' = 'way of thinking'; Art in Erklärungsart is also 'way'.","'zum Grunde legen' (rendered 'lay at the ground') is kept literal; 'den Dogmatism' is accusative, object of 'nennen'. 'Sie' ('Ich will sie') could refer to the maxim or the thesis's assertions; left as 'it'."]],"krv-a-0807":["Thus on the side of dogmatism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas of reason, or of the thesis, there shows itself",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage ('zuerst ein gewisses practisches Interesse ...').","Bestimmung = 'determination'; 'Vernunftideen' = 'ideas of reason'."]],"krv-a-0808":["first a certain practical interest, in which every well-disposed person, if he understands his true advantage, heartily takes part. That the world has a beginning, that my thinking self is of simple and therefore imperishable nature, that this is at the same time free in its voluntary actions and elevated above natural constraint, and that finally the whole order of the things that constitute the world descends from an original being, from which everything borrows its unity and purposive connection, these are so many cornerstones of morals and religion. The antithesis robs us of all these supports, or at least seems to rob us of them.",["Begins in mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage.","Urwesen = 'original being'; einfach = 'simple'; Einheit = 'unity'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Handlungen = 'actions'.","'unverweslich' = 'imperishable'; 'Naturzwang' = 'natural constraint'; 'zweckmässig' = 'purposive' (Zweck = 'end'); 'Stützen' = 'supports'."]],"krv-a-0809":["Second, a speculative interest of reason also manifests itself on this side. For if one accepts and uses the transcendental ideas in such a way, one can grasp completely *a priori* the whole chain of conditions, and comprehend the derivation of the conditioned, in that one begins from the unconditioned, which the antithesis does not accomplish, which recommends itself very badly thereby, that to the question regarding the conditions of its synthesis it can give no answer that would not leave over still further asking without end. According to it one must ascend from a given beginning to a still higher one, every part leads to a still smaller part, every occurrence always has yet another occurrence above it as cause, and the conditions of existence in general always support themselves in turn on others, without ever getting unconditioned hold and support in a self-subsisting thing as original being.",["fassen = 'grasp'; begreifen = 'comprehend'; Bedingung = 'condition'; das Bedingte/Unbedingte = 'the conditioned/unconditioned'; Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Dasein = 'existence'; Theil = 'part'.","'welches die Antithesis nicht leistet, die dadurch sich sehr übel empfielt, daß ...': the relative clauses are kept in the German order; 'welches' has no explicit antecedent beyond the preceding clause.","'nicht ohne Ende immer weiter zu fragen übrig liesse' is kept close to the German; 'ohne Ende' could attach to 'fragen' or to 'übrig liesse' (read here with 'asking')."]],"krv-a-0810":["Third, this side also has the advantage of popularity, which certainly makes up not the smallest part of its recommendation. Common understanding finds not the slightest difficulty in the ideas of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis, since it is in any case more accustomed to go downward to the sequels than to ascend to the grounds, and has in the concepts of the absolutely first (about whose possibility it does not brood) a convenience and at the same time a fixed point to tie the guideline of its steps to, whereas it, on the other hand, in the restless ascending from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, can find no pleasure at all.",["gemeine Verstand = 'common understanding'; Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge); Gründe = 'grounds'; Begriffe = 'concepts'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility'.","'seiner Empfehlung': the German 'seiner' (masculine) is rendered 'its'; the antecedent is 'Vorzug' or 'diese Seite' and the German is not explicit.","'da er hingegen ...' : the German 'er' = Verstand, rendered 'it'; the clause is left unbroken."]],"krv-a-0811":["On the side of empiricism in the determination of the cosmological ideas, or of the antithesis",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage ('findet sich erstlich ...').","Bestimmung = 'determination'."]],"krv-a-0812":["there is found, first, no such practical interest from pure principles of reason as morals and religion bring with them. Rather mere empiricism seems to take from both all force and influence. If there is no original being different from the world, if the world is without beginning and thus also without author, our will not free and the soul of equal divisibility and perishability with matter, then the moral ideas and principles also lose all validity, and fall with the transcendental ideas, which constituted their theoretical support.",["Begins in mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage.","rein = 'pure'; Principien and Grundsätze both 'principles' (Grundsätze is the Latinate/German contrast: Principien = Latinate); Kraft = 'force'; Gültigkeit = 'validity'; Urwesen = 'original being'; Materie = 'matter'.","'Verweslichkeit' = 'perishability' (cf. 'unverweslich' = 'imperishable' above); 'Urheber' = 'author'."]],"krv-a-0813":["On the other hand, however, empiricism offers to the speculative interest of reason advantages that are very enticing and far surpass those that the dogmatic teacher of the ideas of reason may promise. According to the former the understanding is at all times on its own proper ground, namely the field of mere possible experiences, whose laws it can trace and by means of which it can extend its secure and comprehensible cognition without end. Here it can and should present the object, both in itself and in its relations, to intuition, or else in concepts, whose image can be set out clearly and distinctly in given similar intuitions. Not only that it has no need to leave this chain of the order of nature in order to attach itself to ideas, whose objects it does not know, because as thought-entities they can never be given, but it is not even permitted to leave its business and, under the pretext that it has now been brought to an end, to pass over into the territory of idealizing reason and to transcendent concepts, where it no longer has need to observe and to investigate in accordance with the laws of nature, but only to think and to invent, secure that it cannot be refuted by facts of nature, because it is just not bound to their testimony, but may pass it by, or even subordinate it to a higher authority, namely that of pure reason.",["Erkentniß = cognition; kent (kennen) = know; Anschauung = intuition; Gedankendinge = thought-entities; Gegenstand = object; Gebiete rendered 'territory'.","'ihr Zeugniß' (feminine singular, referring to Natur) is rendered 'their testimony' because 'facts' (Thatsachen) is the nearest plural antecedent in English; the German pronoun could be read either as referring to Natur or to the facts. Doubtful in English; flagged.","'dichten' rendered 'to invent'; 'faßliche' rendered 'comprehensible'. 'Empirism' rendered 'empiricism'."]],"krv-a-0814":["The empiricist will therefore never permit assuming any epoch of nature as the absolutely first, or regarding any boundary of his prospect into the domain of the same as the outermost, nor passing over from the objects of nature, which he can resolve through observation and mathematics and determine synthetically in intuition, (the extended) to those that neither sense nor imagination can ever present *in concreto* (the simple), nor granting that one lay at the ground, even in nature, a faculty to act independently of laws of nature, (freedom), and thereby curtail the understanding in its business of tracing, on the guiding thread of necessary rules, the arising of appearances, nor finally conceding that one seek the cause of anything outside nature, (original being) because we know nothing further than this, since it alone is what offers us objects and can instruct us about its laws.",["Umfang = domain; Gränze = boundary; Vermögen = faculty; Urwesen = original being; Wesen elsewhere 'being'.","'derselben' (after 'Umfang') refers to Natur; rendered 'the same' to keep the German pronoun. 'einräumen' = permit/grant and 'zugeben' = concede are both dependent on 'erlauben' in the German construction.","'schlechthinerste' = absolutely first; 'Leitfaden' = guiding thread; 'Entstehen' = arising."]],"krv-a-0815":["To be sure, if the empirical philosopher has with his antithesis no other aim than to strike down the impertinence and audacity of reason, which misjudges its true vocation, which boasts of insight and knowledge there where insight and knowledge properly cease, and wants to pass off as a furtherance of the speculative interest what one lets count with regard to the practical interest, in order, where it is conducive to its comfort, to tear off the thread of physical investigations, and with a pretense of extension of cognition to tie it to transcendental ideas, through which one properly cognizes only that one knows nothing, if, I say, the empiricist were content with this, then his principle would be a maxim of moderation in claims, of modesty in assertions and at the same time of the greatest possible extension of our understanding, through the teacher properly set over us, namely experience. For, in such a case, intellectual presuppositions and belief would not be taken from us for the sake of our practical concern, only one could not let them step forth under the title and pomp of science and insight of reason; because actual speculative knowledge can nowhere strike upon any other object than that of experience and, if one oversteps its boundary, the synthesis, which attempts new cognitions independent of that one, has no *substratum* of intuition on which it could be exercised.",["Bestimmung = vocation; Absicht = aim; Wissen = knowledge; Einsicht = insight; erkennen = cognize; wissen = know; Wissenschaft = science; Glaube = belief; Grundsatz = principle.","'Vorwitz' rendered 'impertinence' (doubtful single-word equivalent). 'groß thut' rendered 'boasts'.","'ihr(e) Gränze' refers to experience (Erfahrung); 'iener' = 'that one', i.e. the feminine antecedent; both left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0816":["But thus, if empiricism with regard to the ideas (as mostly happens) itself becomes dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its intuiting cognitions, then it itself falls into the error of immodesty, which is here all the more blameworthy because thereby an irreparable disadvantage is caused to the practical interest of reason.",["'anschauenden Erkentnisse' rendered 'intuiting cognitions'; 'dreust' = boldly; 'Ansehung' = regard."]],"krv-a-0817":["This is the opposition of Epicureanism*) to Platonism.",["The marker *) is the footnote sign of the original, kept as printed; the footnote is krv-a-0818. Gegensatz = opposition."]],"krv-a-0818":["*) It is still the question, however, whether Epicurus ever presented these principles as objective assertions? If they were perhaps nothing further than maxims of the speculative use of reason, then he showed in this a more genuine philosophical spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity: that in the explanation of appearances one must proceed in such a way as if the field of investigation were cut off by no boundary or beginning of the world, to assume the material of the world as it must be if we want to be taught by experience about it, that no other generation of occurrences than as they are determined through unchangeable laws of nature, and finally no cause distinct from the world must be used, are still now very correct but little observed principles, to extend speculative philosophy, as also to find out the principles of morals, independently of foreign sources of help, without the one who demands to ignore those dogmatic propositions, so long as we are occupied with mere speculation, being on that account accused of wanting to deny them.",["OCR 'Weit' in 'von der Weit unterschiedene Ursache' corrected silently to 'Welt' (world).","The syntax of the German is irregular (the daß-clauses are followed by 'sind … Grundsätze'); kept as it stands, without supplying a subject or verb.","Grundsätze = principles; Principien = principles (same English word); Sätze = propositions; Weltweisen = philosophers (Weltweisheit in the glossary = philosophy); Begebenheiten = occurrences; Erklärung = explanation; Stoff = material; Gränze = boundary.","'darum' in 'darum beschuldigt werden darf' rendered 'on that account'."]],"krv-a-0819":["Each of the two says more than he knows, yet in such a way that the former encourages and promotes knowledge, although to the disadvantage of the practical, the second, to be sure, offers excellent principles for the practical, but just thereby, with regard to everything in which alone a speculative knowledge is granted to us, permits reason to pursue ideal explanations of natural appearances and to neglect physical investigation over it.",["'idealischen' rendered 'ideal'. 'darüber' rendered 'over it'; its reference (explanations / pursuing them) is ambiguous in the German and left so.","weiß = knows; Wissen = knowledge; Erklärungen = explanations; Naturerscheinungen = natural appearances."]],"krv-a-0820":["As for what, finally, concerns the third moment, which can be looked to in the provisional choice between the two disputing parties: it is exceedingly strange that empiricism is entirely contrary to all popularity, although one should believe that common understanding would eagerly take up a plan that promises to satisfy it through nothing but cognitions of experience and their interconnection in accordance with reason, instead of which transcendental dogmatism necessitates it to ascend to concepts that far exceed the insight and the faculty of reason of the heads most practiced in thinking. But precisely this is its motive. For it then finds itself in a state in which even the most learned can claim nothing over it. If it understands little or nothing of this, then nobody can boast of understanding much more of it either, and, although it cannot speak about it as scholastically correctly as others, it can nevertheless rationalize about it infinitely more, because it wanders about among mere ideas, about which one is most eloquent precisely because one knows nothing of them; instead of which, in the investigation of nature, it would have to fall entirely silent and confess its ignorance. Comfort and vanity are thus already a strong recommendation of these principles. Besides, although it becomes very hard for a philosopher to assume something as a principle without being able to give an account of it to himself, still less to introduce concepts whose objective reality cannot be having insight into, nothing is more usual for common understanding. It wants to have something with which it can begin confidently. The difficulty of comprehending such a presupposition itself does not disquiet it, because it (which does not know what comprehending means) never comes into its mind, and it holds as known what has become familiar to it through frequent use. In the end, however, all speculative interest vanishes in it before the practical, and it imagines that it has insight into and knows what its anxieties or hopes drive it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of transcendentally idealizing reason is entirely robbed of all popularity, and, however much that is disadvantageous to the supreme practical principles it may contain, there is nevertheless no cause at all to fear: that it will ever overstep the boundaries of the school and acquire in the common being any considerable, even if only some, standing and some favor with the great multitude.",["Rendered literally: 'dessen obiective Realität nicht eingesehen werden kan' is 'cannot be having insight into' — awkward in English; the German is 'cannot be gained insight into' (einsehen), kept as stated rather than smoothed. 'einsehen' = have insight into; Einsicht = insight.","Vernunftvermögen = 'faculty of reason'; vernünfteln = 'rationalize' (glossary); vernunftmäßig = 'in accordance with reason'.","Begriff / begreifen: 'concepts' / 'comprehending'; Begriff in 'so viel Begriff' elsewhere not here.","Bewegungsgrund = 'motive'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Zustand = 'state'; Grundsätze = 'principles'; Gränzen = 'boundaries'.","'im gemeinen Wesen' = 'in the common being' (Wesen = being; the sense 'commonwealth' is not imposed). 'Ansehen' = 'standing'.","'sie' in the last sentence: grammatically refers to 'Vernunft' (feminine); the German 'sie ... enthalten mag' and 'werde' kept as is.","Possible OCR/edition note: 'Mehr' capitalized in the original text, kept as 'more'."]],"krv-a-0821":["Human reason is by its nature architectonic, i.e., it considers all cognitions as belonging to a possible system, and therefore also permits only such principles as at least do not make a cognition in hand incapable of standing together with others in some system or other. The propositions of the antithesis, however, are of such a kind: that they make the completion of an edifice of cognitions entirely impossible. According to them there is, beyond a state of the world, always a still older one, in every part always still others that are in turn divisible, before every occurrence another, which was likewise in turn generated elsewhere, and in existence in general everything is always only conditioned, without recognizing any unconditioned and first existence. Since the antithesis therefore nowhere grants a first and no beginning that could serve absolutely as the basis of the structure, a complete edifice of cognition is, given such presuppositions, entirely impossible. Hence the architectonic interest of reason (which demands not empirical but pure unity of reason *a priori*) carries with it a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Zustand = state; Begebenheit = occurrence; Daseyn = existence; Grund = ground/basis (here 'zum Grunde dienen' = 'serve as the basis').","'anzuerkennen' = 'recognizing' (anerkennen, not erkennen/cognize).","'Vernunfteinheit a priori' : the phrase 'a priori' could attach to 'fodert' or to 'Vernunfteinheit'; left in place so as to preserve the ambiguity.","'Behauptungen' = 'assertions' (behaupten = assert)."]],"krv-a-0822":["But if a human being could renounce all interest, and take the assertions of reason into consideration, indifferent to all consequences, merely according to the content of their grounds: then such a one, supposing that he knew of no way out of the press other than by professing one or the other of the disputed doctrines, would be in a state of incessant wavering. Today it would seem convincing to him: that the human will is free; tomorrow, if he considered the indissoluble chain of nature, he would hold: that freedom is nothing but self-deception and everything is merely nature. But if it then came to doing and acting, this play of merely speculative reason would vanish like shadow-images of a dream, and he would choose his principles merely according to practical interest. Since, however, it is still fitting for a being that reflects and investigates to devote certain times solely to the examination of its own reason, but in this to strip away all partisanship completely, and thus to communicate its remarks publicly to others for their judgment: it can be blamed on no one, still less forbidden, to let propositions and counterpropositions appear, as they, frightened by no threat, can defend themselves before jurors of their own station (namely the station of weak human beings).",["überzeugend = 'convincing' (Überzeugung = conviction); Beurteilung = 'judgment'; Betrachtung = 'consideration'; Gründe = 'grounds'.","Gedränge = 'press' (literally a crush/predicament), kept literal.","'Sätze und Gegensätze' = 'propositions and counterpropositions' (Satz = proposition).","'so wie sie ... vertheidigen können': 'they' may refer to the propositions or to the assertions; the clause is left as ambiguous as the German.","'Stande' (twice) = 'station' (Stand); 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'Partheylichkeit' = 'partisanship'."]],"krv-a-0823":["Fourth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. On the Transcendental Problems of Pure Reason, insofar as They Absolutely Must Be Capable of Being Resolved.",["Aufgaben = 'problems' (glossary); 'aufgelöset' = 'resolved'.","Heading; 'Transscendentalen' rendered 'Transcendental'."]],"krv-a-0824":["To want to resolve all problems and answer all questions would be an impudent boasting and such an extravagant self-conceit that one would thereby immediately have to forfeit all trust. Nevertheless there are sciences whose nature brings it with it that every question occurring in them must be absolutely answerable from what one knows, because the answer must arise from the same sources from which the question arises, and where it is by no means permitted to plead unavoidable ignorance, but the resolution can be demanded. What is right or wrong in all possible cases one must, as a rule, be able to know, because it concerns our obligation, and for what we cannot know we also have no obligation. In the explanation of the appearances of nature, however, much must remain uncertain to us and many a question unresolvable, because what we know of nature is by far not in all cases sufficient for what we are to explain. It is now asked: whether in transcendental philosophy any question that concerns an object laid before reason is unanswerable through precisely this pure reason, and whether one can rightfully withdraw from its decisive answering by counting it, as absolutely uncertain (from all that we can cognize), among that of which we do indeed have so much concept as to raise a question, but entirely lack the means or the faculty ever to answer it.",["Wissenschaften = 'sciences' (Wissenschaft, scientia); wissen = 'know'; erkennen = 'cognize'.","Verbindlichkeit = 'obligation'; Erklärung = 'explanation'; Erscheinungen = 'appearances'; Obiect = 'object'; Begriff = 'concept'; Mittel = 'means'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; zureichend = 'sufficient'.","'Transscendentalphilosophie' = 'transcendental philosophy'; 'Rechte oder Unrecht' = 'right or wrong'.","'man’es' in the source (apostrophe) read as 'man es'; 'es' left implicit as in 'counting it'.","'demienigen' = 'that', antecedent of 'wovon'; 'sie' in 'sie iemals zu beantworten' = the question."]],"krv-a-0825":["I now assert that transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity among all speculative cognition: that no question at all that concerns an object given to pure reason is unresolvable for this very same human reason, and that no pleading of an unavoidable ignorance and of unfathomable depth of the problem can release one from the obligation to answer it thoroughly and completely; because precisely the same concept that puts us in the position to ask must also thoroughly make us competent to answer this question, since the object is not encountered at all outside the concept (as in right and wrong).",["Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Gegenstand = 'object' (Obiect in the preceding passage is also 'object'; Latinate member marked there).","'Vor eben dieselbe menschliche Vernunft' : 'vor' = 'for' (modern 'für').","'Recht und Unrecht' = 'right and wrong'; 'Aufgabe' = 'problem'; 'Verbindlichkeit' = 'obligation'.","'sie' in 'sie ... zu beantworten' could refer to the question or the problem (Aufgabe); left ambiguous as 'it'."]],"krv-a-0826":["But in transcendental philosophy there are no others than only the cosmological questions with regard to which one can rightly demand a satisfactory answer that concerns the constitution of the object, without the philosopher being permitted to evade them by pretending impenetrable obscurity, and these questions can concern only cosmological ideas. For the object must be given empirically and the question goes only to the adequacy of the same with an idea. If the object is transcendental and thus itself unknown, e.g., whether the something whose appearance (in ourselves) is thinking (soul) is a being simple in itself, whether there is a cause of all things together, that is absolutely necessary, etc., then we are to seek an object for our idea, of which we can concede that it is unknown to us, but nevertheless not for that reason impossible*). The cosmological ideas alone have this peculiarity about them, that they can presuppose as given their object and the empirical synthesis required for its concept, and the question that arises from them concerns only the progression of this synthesis, insofar as it is to contain absolute totality, the latter of which is nothing empirical any longer, since it cannot be given in any experience. Since now here it is a matter solely of a thing as object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendent cosmological question can lie nowhere else than in the idea, for it concerns no object in itself, and with regard to possible experience, what is asked after is not that which can be given *in concreto* in some experience, but what lies in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis is merely to approximate: thus it must be possible to resolve it from the idea alone; for this is a mere creature of reason, which therefore cannot disclaim the responsibility and shove it onto the unknown object.",["Ansehung = \"regard\" (\"in Ansehung deren\" = \"with regard to which\"); Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Fortgang = \"progression\"; Totalität = \"totality\" (the glossary's Allheit is also \"totality\").","\"Ding\" (\"von einem Dinge\") and \"Sache\" (\"einer Sache an sich selbst\") are both rendered \"thing\".","\"Auflösung/auflösen\" (not in the glossary) is rendered \"resolution/resolve\" throughout; \"Frage\" = \"question\"; \"Geschöpf\" = \"creature\".","The text has a period after \"gebe\" (\"gebe. die schlechthin nothwendig ist\"); I have printed a comma, treating the period as a printing slip. The asterisk after \"sey\" marks the footnote (krv-a-0827).","Ambiguity kept: \"sie\" in \"denn sie betrift keinen Gegenstand\" and in \"also muß sie ... aufgelöset werden können\" may refer to \"die Beantwortung\" or to \"die ... Frage\"; the English \"it\" is left as ambiguous. The \"so\" after \"in Ansehung der möglichen Erfahrung\" is not rendered separately.","\"Idee\" = \"idea\"; \"transscendent\" = \"transcendent\" and \"transscendental\" = \"transcendental\"; \"in concreto\" is Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-0827":["*) One can indeed give no answer to the question what constitution a transcendental object has, namely what it is, but indeed that the question itself is nothing, because no object of it has been given. Hence all questions of the transcendental doctrine of the soul are also answerable and actually answered; for they concern the transc. subject of all inner appearances, which is itself not appearance and thus is not given as object, and on which none of the categories (to which, after all, the question is properly put) encounter conditions of their application. Thus here is the case in which the common expression holds: that no answer is also an answer, namely that a question about the constitution of that something which cannot be thought through any determinate predicate, because it is posited entirely outside the sphere of objects that can be given to us, is entirely null and empty.",["Elliptical \"aber wol daß die Frage selbst nichts sey\": no verb of saying is expressed in the German, and none is supplied; \"wol\" is rendered \"indeed\".","\"vor eine Beschaffenheit\" = \"für eine Beschaffenheit\"; rendered \"what constitution\". \"keine der Categorien ... antreffen\": plural verb after \"keine\", kept as \"none ... encounter\".","\"Seelenlehre\" = \"doctrine of the soul\" (Lehre = doctrine); \"wirklich\" = \"actually\"; \"nichtig\" = \"null\"; \"bestimmt\" = \"determinate\"; \"gesezt\" = \"posited\"; \"Subiect\" = \"subject\"; \"transsc.\" is the abbreviation as printed.","\"Gegenstand\" = \"object\" throughout; \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\"; \"Categorien\" = \"categories\"."]],"krv-a-0828":["It is not so extraordinary as it initially seems that a science with regard to all questions belonging in its sum total (*quæstiones domesticæ*) can demand and expect nothing but certain resolutions, even though perhaps they have not yet been found at the present time. Besides transcendental philosophy there are still two pure sciences of reason, one of merely speculative, the other of practical content: pure mathematics and pure morals. Has one ever heard that, as it were on account of a necessary ignorance of the conditions, it was declared uncertain what relation the diameter has to the circle quite exactly in rational or irrational numbers. Since it cannot be given congruently at all through the former, but through the second has not yet been found, one judged that at least the impossibility of such a resolution could be cognized with certainty, and Lambert gave a proof of this. In the general principles of morals nothing uncertain can be, because the propositions are either entirely null and void of sense, or must flow merely from our concepts of reason. By contrast there is in the study of nature an infinity of conjectures, with regard to which certainty can never be expected, because natural appearances are objects that are given to us independently of our concepts, to which therefore the key lies not in us and our pure thinking but outside us, and just for that reason in many cases cannot be found, hence no secure disclosure can be expected. I do not count the questions of the transcendental analytic, which concern the deduction of our pure cognition, as belonging here, because we are now dealing only with the certainty of judgments with regard to objects and not with regard to the origin of our concepts themselves.",["\"Inbegriff\" = \"sum total\"; \"Wissenschaft\" = \"science\"; \"Auflösung\" = \"resolution\"; \"erkennen\" = \"cognize\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"cognition\"; \"Urtheil\" = \"judgment\"; \"Beweis\" = \"proof\".","\"Hat man wol iemals gehört: daß ...\" is punctuated in the German as a question without a question mark; I have kept the period.","\"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\"; \"Principien\" = \"principles\" (Grundsatz is \"principle\" and Satz \"proposition\" in the glossary); \"Sitten\" = \"morals\"; \"Moral\" = \"morals\"; \"Naturkunde\" = \"study of nature\" (not in the glossary).","\"sinnleer\" = \"void of sense\"; \"Aufschluß\" = \"disclosure\"; \"Vermuthungen\" = \"conjectures\".","In \"nicht aufgefunden, mithin kein sicherer Aufschluß erwartet werden kan\" the German leaves \"kan\" to be understood after \"aufgefunden\"; the English supplies \"cannot be found\" for grammar.","\"allgemein\" is rendered \"general\"; \"quæstiones domesticæ\" is Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-0829":["We will therefore not be able to evade the obligation of an at least critical resolution of the questions of reason put before us by raising complaints about the narrow limitations of our reason and, with the illusion of a humble self-cognition, confessing: that it is beyond our reason to decide whether the world has been from eternity or has a beginning; whether world-space is filled to infinity with beings or is enclosed within certain boundaries; whether anything in the world is simple or whether everything must be divided to infinity; whether there is a generation and production from freedom or whether everything hangs on the chain of the natural order; finally whether there is any being entirely unconditioned and necessary in itself, or whether everything according to its existence is conditioned and hence externally dependent and contingent in itself. For all these questions concern an object that can be given nowhere else than in our thoughts, namely the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of appearances. If we can say and decide nothing certain about it from our own concepts, then we may not shove the blame onto the thing that conceals itself from us; for such a thing (because it is encountered nowhere outside our idea) cannot be given to us at all, rather we must seek the cause in our idea itself, which is a problem that admits of no resolution and about which we nevertheless obstinately assume, as if an actual object corresponded to it. A distinct presentation of the dialectic that lies in our concept itself would soon bring us to complete certainty about what we have to judge with regard to such a question.",["The text reads \"Eine deutlich Darlegung\"; silently corrected to \"deutliche\" (\"A distinct presentation\"). \"Darlegung\" is not in the glossary and is rendered \"presentation\".","\"Schranken\" = \"limitations\"; \"Gränzen\" = \"boundaries\" (Grenze = bound(ary)); \"Scheine\" = \"illusion\"; \"Selbsterkentniß\" = \"self-cognition\"; \"Weltraum\" = \"world-space\"; \"Wesen\" = \"being\"; \"Daseyn\" = \"existence\".","\"Auflösung\" = \"resolution\"; \"Problem\" = \"problem\" (the glossary's Aufgabe is also \"problem\"); \"Totalität\" = \"totality\"; \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\"; \"Sache\" = \"thing\" (distinct from Ding).","Ambiguity kept: \"welche ein Problem ist\" is left with the same loose antecedent as in the German (taken to be \"Idee\"); \"ihr\" in \"als entspreche ihr\" is rendered \"it\".","\"über unsere Vernunft\" is rendered literally \"beyond our reason\"; \"hartnäckigt\" = \"obstinately\"."]],"krv-a-0830":["One can first oppose to your pretext of uncertainty with regard to these problems this question, which you must at least answer distinctly: Whence do the ideas come to you, the resolution of which entangles you here in such difficulty? Are they perhaps appearances, the explanation of which you require and of which, in accordance with these ideas, you have only to seek the principles, or the rule of their exposition? Assume that nature were entirely uncovered before you; that nothing were hidden from your senses, and from the consciousness of everything that is laid before your intuition: still you will not be able through any single experience to cognize the object of your ideas *in concreto* (for beyond this complete intuition there is also required a completed synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality, which is possible through no empirical cognition at all), consequently your question can in no way be necessary for the explanation of any appearance that occurs, and thus be as it were set as a problem by the object itself. For the object can never occur to you, because it cannot be given through any possible experience. With all possible perceptions you remain always ensnared under conditions, whether in space or in time, and come upon nothing unconditioned, in order to make out whether this unconditioned is to be posited in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without any beginning. But the all in empirical meaning is at all times only comparative. The absolute all of magnitude (the universe), of division, of descent, of the condition of existence in general, with all the questions whether it is to be brought about through finite synthesis or through synthesis to be continued to infinity, does not concern any possible experience at all. You would, e.g., not be able to explain the appearances of a body in the least better, or even just differently, whether you assume that it consists of simple parts, or throughout always of composite parts; for no simple appearance, and just as little an infinite composition, can ever occur to you. Appearances demand only to be explained so far as their conditions of explanation are given in perception, but everything that may ever be given in them, taken together in an absolute whole, is itself a perception. But it is this all, properly, whose explanation is demanded in the transcendental problems of reason.",["Auflösung = resolution; aufgegeben (from Aufgabe, glossary 'problem') = 'set as a problem'; Principien = principles; Exposition (German, Latinate) = exposition, not Erörterung.","Gegenstand/Object: both 'object'; the Latinate Object occurs in 0831 and 0834 in the roman-type form 'Obiect'.","'in concreto' is Latin in the original and kept in italics; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize.","'ist selbst eine Wahrnehmung' is translated as printed in the text (A); no emendation made.","'Das All' = 'the all'; 'Weltall' = 'universe' (in parentheses); Bedeutung = meaning; Erklärung = explanation; vorkommen = 'occur' (to you).","'ihr bleibt ... befangen' = 'you remain ensnared'; the German 'ob ihr annehmet' is kept as a plain 'whether you assume'."]],"krv-a-0831":["Since, then, even the resolution of these problems can never occur in experience, you cannot say that it is uncertain what is to be attributed to the object in this regard. For your object is merely in your brain and cannot be given at all outside it, hence you have only to take care to become at one with yourselves and to prevent the amphiboly, which makes your idea into a supposed representation of something empirically given and thus also of an object to be cognized according to laws of experience. The dogmatic resolution is therefore not perhaps uncertain, but impossible. But the critical one, which can be fully certain, considers the question not at all objectively, but according to the foundation of the cognition on which it is grounded.",["Aufgaben = problems; Auflösung = resolution; Obiect (roman-type Latinate) = object, as is Gegenstand; Amphibolie = amphiboly.","'Die critische' (feminine) is rendered 'the critical one', its referent (Auflösung) left to the reader as in the German.","Fundament = foundation; Erkentniß = cognition; Vorstellung = representation."]],"krv-a-0832":["Antinomy of Pure Reason, Fifth Section. Skeptical Representation of the Cosmological Questions through All Four Transcendental Ideas.",["Vorstellung = representation; sceptisch rendered 'skeptical' (American spelling); cosmologisch = cosmological."]],"krv-a-0833":["We would gladly desist from the demand to see our questions answered dogmatically if we comprehended in advance: the answer might turn out however it would, it would only increase our ignorance still further and plunge us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into a still greater one and perhaps even into contradictions. If our question is posed merely to affirmation or negation, then it is prudently done to leave the presumptive grounds of the answer aside for the time being and first to take into consideration what one would then gain if the answer fell on the one side and what if on the opposite side. Now if it happens that in both cases mere nonsense (*Nonsens*) comes out, then we have a well-founded summons to investigate our question itself critically, and to see whether it does not itself rest on a groundless presupposition and play with an idea that betrays its falsity better in the application and through its consequences than in the separated representation. That is the great utility that the skeptical way has of treating the questions which pure reason puts to pure reason, and through which one can be relieved of a great dogmatic rubbish at little expense, in order to put in its place a sober critique, which, as a true *Catarcticon*, will happily purge the delusion together with its retinue, polymathy.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Berneinung' → Verneinung (negation); 'mir einer Idee' → 'mit einer Idee'.","begreifen = comprehend; Unbegreiflichkeit = incomprehensibility; Widersprüche = contradictions; Folgen = consequences (the glossary's Folge is 'sequel'; here plural in a non-technical sense).","'Nonsens' and 'Catarcticon' are foreign words, kept in italics; Wahn = delusion; Vielwisserey = polymathy (literally 'much-knowing').","'die Antwort möchte ausfallen, wie sie wolte' is kept in its colon-and-clause order; the sense is concessive ('however it might turn out')."]],"krv-a-0834":["If, accordingly, I could see in advance of a cosmological idea that, to whichever side of the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of appearances it might incline, it would still be either too large or too small for any concept of the understanding, then I would comprehend that, since that one has to do only with an object of experience, which is supposed to be commensurate with a possible concept of the understanding, it must be entirely empty and without significance, because the object does not fit it, however I may accommodate it to the latter. And this is really the case with all world-concepts, which also, precisely for that reason, entangle reason, as long as it adheres to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For assume",["Ambiguity preserved: 'welche' (feminine) in 'einem Gegenstande der Erfahrung ..., welche ... angemessen seyn soll' can be read as referring to Erfahrung (experience); 'iene' ('that one') and 'derselben' ('the latter') are left unresolved.","Bedeutung = significance (glossary: 'signify / significance; meaning'); Weltbegriffe = world-concepts; Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding.","The passage breaks off at 'Denn nehmt' and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-a-0835":["First: the world has no beginning, then it is too large for your concept; for this, which consists in a successive regress, can never reach the whole elapsed eternity. Suppose: it has a beginning, then it is in turn too large [sic: too small] for your concept of the understanding, in the necessary empirical regress. For, because the beginning still always presupposes a time that precedes, it is not yet unconditioned, and the law of the empirical use of the understanding imposes on you to ask still after a higher condition of time, and the world is thus obviously too small for this law.",["Continues the previous passage: 'Denn nehmt | Erstlich an:' → 'For assume | First: ...'; rendered here as it stands, beginning at 'Erstlich an'.","ERRATUM: in the second sentence the text reads 'zu klein'; the English should read 'too small', not 'too large [sic: too small]'.","Regressus = regress (Latinate form, one English word); Gebrauch = use; Gesetz = law; Bedingung = condition; unbedingt = unconditioned."]],"krv-a-0836":["It is just so with the double answering of the question about the magnitude of the world, according to space. For if it is infinite and unbounded, then it is too large for every possible empirical concept. If it is finite and bounded, then you rightly still ask, what determines this boundary? Empty space is not a *correlatum* of things subsisting for itself and can be no condition at which you could stop, still much less an empirical condition that would make up a part of a possible experience (for who can have an experience of the absolutely empty). But for the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis it is at all times required that the unconditioned be a concept of experience. Thus a bounded world is too small for your concept.",["'vor' is the older spelling of 'für' ('for'); 'vor sich bestehendes' = 'subsisting for itself'.","Gränze = boundary; begränzt/unbegränzt = bounded/unbounded; Correlatum is Latin, italicized; Totalität = totality; Theil = part; bestimmen = determine.","'Schlechthinleeren' = 'the absolutely empty' (substantivized adjective)."]],"krv-a-0837":["Second, if every appearance in space (matter) consists of infinitely many parts, then the regress of division is at all times too large for your concept, and if the division of space is to cease at some member of it (the simple), then it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For this member still always leaves over a regress to more parts contained in it.",["Theilung = division; einfach = simple; Glied = member; Materie = matter; 'er' (the regress) rendered 'it'.","'iede Erscheinung im Raume (Materie)' keeps the parenthesis as in the German."]],"krv-a-0838":["Third, you assume: in everything that happens in the world there is nothing but outcome according to laws of nature, then the causality of the cause is always again something that happens, and makes necessary your regress to a still higher cause, hence the lengthening of the series of conditions *a parte priori* without ceasing. Mere efficient nature is thus too large for all your concepts, in the synthesis of world-occurrences.",["Erfolg = 'outcome' (not the glossary's Folge, 'sequel'); Caussalität = causality; Ursache = cause; Begebenheiten = occurrences (Weltbegebenheiten = world-occurrences).","'wirkende Natur' = 'efficient nature' (literally 'effecting/acting nature'); *a parte priori* is Latin, italicized.","'vor allen euren Begriff' kept in the singular as in the German ('for all your concept'), here rendered 'for all your concepts' for English idiom; the German singular is noted."]],"krv-a-0839":["If you choose, here and there, occurrences effected of themselves, hence generation from freedom: then the Why pursues you according to an unavoidable law of nature, and compels you to go beyond this point according to the causal law of experience, and you find that such a totality of connection is too small for your necessary empirical concept.",["Begebenheit = occurrence; Verknüpfung = connection; Totalität = totality; \"vor\" is the older spelling of \"für\" (\"for\").","\"Wählt\" rendered literally as \"choose\"; \"Caussalgesetz\" = causal law."]],"krv-a-0840":["Fourthly. If you assume a absolutely necessary being (be it the world itself or something in the world or the cause of the world): then you posit it in a time infinitely distant from every given point of time; because otherwise it would be dependent on another and older existence. But then this existence is inaccessible to your empirical concept and too great for you ever to be able to arrive at it through any continued regress.",["Wesen = being; schlechthin = absolutely; Dasein and Existenz are both rendered \"existence\" (Dasein in \"älteren Daseyn\", Existenz in \"diese Existenz\").","\"Weltursache\" = \"cause of the world\"; \"Zeitpunct\" = \"point of time\"; \"vor\" = \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0841":["But if, in your opinion, everything that belongs to the world (be it as conditioned or as condition) is contingent: then every existence given to you is too small for your concept. For it compels you still always to look around for another existence on which it is dependent.",["Meinung = opinion; Bedingt/Bedingung = conditioned/condition; zufällig = contingent; Existenz = existence.","\"vor\" = \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0842":["We have said in all these cases: that the world-idea is either too large, or also too small for the empirical regress, hence for every possible concept of the understanding. Why have we not expressed ourselves conversely and said: that, in the first case, the empirical concept is always too small for the idea, in the second, however, too large, and hence as it were the blame attaches to the empirical regress, instead of our accusing the cosmological idea of having deviated, in the too much or too little, from its end, namely possible experience? The ground was this. Possible experience is that which alone can give reality to our concepts; without it every concept is only idea, without truth and relation to an object. Hence the possible empirical concept was the standard of measure according to which the idea had to be assessed, whether it is a mere idea and thought-entity, or finds its object in the world. For one says only of that, that it is relatively too large or too small for something else, which is assumed only for the sake of the latter, and must be arranged accordingly. To the plaything of the old dialectical schools there also belonged this question: if a ball does not go through a hole, what should one say: Is the ball too large, or the hole too small? In this case it is indifferent how you want to express yourselves; for you do not know which of the two is there for the sake of the other. On the other hand you will not say: the man is too long for his clothes, but rather the clothes are too short for the man.",["Gedankending = thought-entity (ens rationis); Zweck = end; Realität = reality; beurteilen = assess; Gegenstand = object.","\"Richtmaas\" = \"standard of measure\" (not in the glossary); \"Spielwerk\" = \"plaything\"; \"Kugel\" = \"ball\".","\"Zuviel oder Zuwenig\" kept as nominalized \"too much or too little\"; \"vor\" = \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0843":["We are thus brought at least to the well-grounded suspicion: that the cosmological ideas, and, with them, all the rationalizing assertions set into dispute with one another, perhaps have at their ground an empty and merely imagined concept of the way in which the object of these ideas is given to us, and this suspicion can already lead us onto the right track for discovering the delusion that has led us astray for so long.",["vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Behauptung = assertion; Grund = ground; Gegenstand = object.","\"von der Art, wie ...\" attaches to \"Begriff\" (\"concept of the way in which\"); \"Blendwerk\" = \"delusion\" (not in the glossary)."]],"krv-a-0844":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason Sixth Section. Transcendental idealism, as the key to the resolution of the cosmological dialectic.",["Heading; \"Der Antinomie\" is genitive (\"Of the Antinomy\"). \"Auflösung\" = \"resolution\" (not in the glossary)."]],"krv-a-0845":["We have sufficiently proved in the transcendental aesthetic: that everything that is intuited in space or time, hence all objects of an experience possible for us, are nothing but appearances, i.e., mere representations, which, as they are represented, as extended beings, or series of alterations, have no existence grounded in itself outside our thoughts. This doctrinal concept I call transcendental idealism. The realist in transcendental signification makes of these modifications of our sensibility things subsisting in themselves, and hence mere representations into things in themselves.",["Anschauung = intuition; Erscheinung = appearance; Vorstellung = representation; Wesen = being; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Bedeutung = signification.","\"an sich gegründete Existenz\" is ambiguous in the German (\"grounded in itself\" or \"grounded in itself\" as \"an sich\"); left as \"grounded in itself\".","\"Lehrbegriff\" = \"doctrinal concept\"; \"Ding\" and \"Sache\" are both rendered \"thing\" (Dinge / Sachen an sich selbst); \"Veränderung\" = \"alteration\"."]],"krv-a-0846":["One would do us an injustice if one wanted to impute to us the empirical idealism so long since decried, which, in that it assumes the proper actuality of space, denies the existence of the extended beings in it, at least finds it doubtful, and grants in this respect no sufficiently demonstrable difference between dream and truth. As regards the appearances of inner sense in time, it finds no difficulty with them, as actual things, indeed it even asserts: that this inner experience alone and solely suffices to prove the actual existence of its object (in itself), (with all this determination of time).",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; wirklich = actual; Dasein = existence; Erscheinung = appearance; behaupten = assert; Erfahrung = experience.","\"Obiect\" = \"object\" (Latinate Object; Gegenstand elsewhere also \"object\"); \"eigene\" = \"proper\"; \"Zeitbestimmung\" = \"determination of time\"; \"Stück\" = \"respect\"."]],"krv-a-0847":["Our transcendental idealism, on the contrary, allows: that the objects of outer intuition, just as they are intuited in space, also be actual, and in time all alterations, as inner sense represents them. For, since space is already a form of that intuition which we call the outer, and, without objects in it, there would be no empirical representation at all: so we can and must assume extended beings in it as actual, and just so it is also with time. But that space itself, together with this time and, at the same time with both, all appearances, are nevertheless in themselves no things, but nothing but representations and cannot exist at all outside our mind, and even the inner and sensible intuition of our mind, (as object of consciousness), whose determination is represented through the succession of different states in time, is also not the proper self, as it exists in itself, or the transcendental subject, but only an appearance that has been given to the sensibility of this being unknown to us. The existence of this inner appearance, as of a thing existing thus in itself, cannot be granted, because its condition is time, which cannot be a determination of any thing in itself. In space and time, however, the empirical truth of appearances is sufficiently secured, and sufficiently distinguished from kinship with dream, if both hang together correctly and thoroughly according to empirical laws in one experience.",["OCR \"finnliche\" corrected silently to \"sinnliche\" (sensible); \"Bewustseyn\" = consciousness; Gemüth = mind; Bestimmung = determination; Zustand = state; Succession = succession.","Dasein = existence; \"eigentliche Selbst\" = \"proper self\" (eigentlich and eigen both \"proper\"); Wesen = being; Gegenstand = object.","\"wenn beide ... zusammen hängen\": \"both\" could refer to space and time or to appearances/dream and truth; left as ambiguous as the German. \"nach empirischen Gesetzen\" could attach to \"hängen\" or \"in einer Erfahrung\"; order kept."]],"krv-a-0848":["Accordingly the objects of experience are never given in themselves, but only in experience, and do not exist at all outside it. That there could be inhabitants in the moon, although no human being has ever perceived them, must certainly be granted, but it signifies only this much: that we could encounter them in the possible progress of experience; for everything is actual that stands with a perception in one context according to laws of empirical progression. They are therefore actual then when they stand with my actual consciousness in an empirical interconnection, although they are not on that account actual in themselves, i.e., outside this progress of experience.",["Wahrnehmung = perception; bedeuten = signify; Fortschritt = progress, Fortgang = progression (distinguished); Zusammenhang = interconnection; Bewustseyn = consciousness.","\"Context\" is the German's own Latinate word (Context), rendered \"context\"; \"alsdenn\" = \"then\"."]],"krv-a-0849":["Nothing is really given to us except perception and the empirical progress from this to other possible perceptions. For in themselves the appearances, as mere representations, are real only in perception, which in fact is nothing other than the reality of an empirical representation, i.e., appearance. To call an appearance a real thing prior to perception signifies either that in the progress of experience we must encounter such a perception, or it has no meaning at all. For that it exists in itself, without relation to our senses and possible experience, could certainly be said if the talk were of a thing in itself. But the talk is merely of an appearance in space and time, both of which are not determinations of things in themselves, but only of our sensibility; hence that which is in them (appearances) is not something in itself, but mere representations, which, if they are not given in us (in perception), are encountered nowhere at all.",["wirklich/Wirklichkeit = real/reality (glossary: Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality); Realität would also be 'reality' (not occurring here).","Fortschritt and Fortgange both rendered 'progress' (Fortgang: progression; progress); Fortschritt is not in the glossary.","bedeutet/Bedeutung = signifies/meaning.","Bestimmungen = determinations; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Vorstellungen = representations; Erscheinung = appearance.","'überall nirgend' rendered 'nowhere at all'; the German pairing is idiomatic and awkward.","Original spelling 'nichts anders' and 'könte' kept in sense only; no emendation made."]],"krv-a-0850":["The sensible faculty of intuition is properly only a receptivity, to be affected in a certain way with representations, whose relation to one another is a pure intuition of space and of time, (nothing but forms of our sensibility) and which, insofar as they are connected and determinable in this relation (space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The nonsensible cause of these representations is entirely unknown to us, and we therefore cannot intuit it as object; for such an object would have to be represented neither in space nor in time (as mere conditions of sensible representation), without which conditions we cannot think any intuition at all. In the meantime we can call the merely intelligible cause of appearances in general the transcendental object, merely so that we may have something that corresponds to sensibility as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we can ascribe the whole domain and interconnection of our possible perceptions and say: that it is given in itself prior to all experience. The appearances, however, are, in conformity with it, given not in themselves but only in this experience, because they are mere representations, which as perceptions only signify a real object, if namely this perception is interconnected with all the others according to the rules of the unity of experience. Thus one can say: the real things of past time are given in the transcendental object of experience; but they are for me objects and real in past time only insofar as I represent to myself: that a regressive series of possible perceptions, (be it by the guiding thread of history, or by the footsteps of causes and effects), according to empirical laws, in a word, the course of the world, leads to an elapsed series of time as condition of the present time, which is then nevertheless represented as real only in the interconnection of a possible experience and not in itself, so that all occurrences that have elapsed from an unthinkable time before my existence still signify nothing other than the possibility of extending the chain of experience, from the present perception, upward to the conditions that determine this perception as to time.",["Gegenstand and Object (Obiect) are both rendered 'object' (glossary); 'Obiect' occurs in 'als Obiect anschauen' and 'transscendentale Obiect'; elsewhere Gegenstand(e), including 'transscendentalen Gegenstande'.","Umfang = domain; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Begebenheiten = occurrences; Anschauungsvermögen = faculty of intuition.","wirklich = real; 'Wirklichkeit' not occurring here; 'bedeuten' = signify.","'vor mich' (for 'für mich') rendered 'for me'; 'vor aller Erfahrung' = 'prior to all experience' (vor as temporal/prior in both).","'welche, so fern sie ... verknüpft und bestimbar sind' (first sentence): the relative 'welche' could refer to the representations or to the relation; left as in German ('which').","'welche diese der Zeit nach bestimmen': 'welche' = 'conditions', 'diese' = 'this' (perception) or possibly 'these' (conditions' object); left as 'this perception' following the singular 'Wahrnehmung'; ambiguous in grammar.","'bestimbar' = 'determinable' (OCR/old spelling)."]],"krv-a-0851":["If I accordingly represent to myself all existing objects of the senses in all time and all spaces together: then I do not posit them into both prior to experience, but this representation is nothing other than the thought of a possible experience, in its absolute completeness. In it alone are those objects (which are nothing but mere representations) given. But that one says: they exist prior to all my experience, signifies only: that they are to be encountered in the part of experience to which I, beginning from perception, must first of all progress. The cause of the empirical conditions of this progress, hence on which members, or also how far I could encounter such in the regressus, is transcendental and therefore necessarily unknown to me. But it is also not a matter of this, but only of the rule of the progress of experience, in which the objects, namely appearances, are given to me. It is also in the outcome entirely the same whether I say: I could in the empirical progression in space encounter stars that are a hundred times farther away than the outermost ones that I see: or whether I say, perhaps there are such to be encountered in cosmic space, even if no human has ever perceived them, or will perceive them; for even if they were given as things in themselves, without relation to possible experience, in general: they are nevertheless nothing for me, hence no objects, except insofar as they are contained in the series of the empirical regressus. Only in another relation, if precisely these appearances are to be used for the cosmological idea of an absolute whole and if it is thus a matter of a question that goes beyond the boundaries of possible experience, is the distinction of the way in which one takes the reality of thought objects of the senses of significance, in order to forestall a deceptive delusion, which must unavoidably arise from the misinterpretation of our own concepts of experience.",["Fortschritt/Fortgang = progress (Fortgang: progression; progress); 'fortschreiten' = progress (v.). Regressus left in Latin as in the German, italicized: *regressus*.","Gegenstände = objects throughout; 'gedachter Gegenstände' rendered 'thought objects' (literal, 'objects thought').","Wirklichkeit = reality (glossary: Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality); Idee = idea; Gränzen = boundaries; Art = way.","'in beide hinein' = 'into both' (space and time); 'Ausgange' = 'outcome'.","'seyn' in 'enthalten seyn' is an infinitive/plural form for 'sind'; rendered 'are contained'.","'welche' in the last clause refers to 'Wahne' (delusion); 'Erheblichkeit' rendered 'significance', not in the glossary; 'of significance' also lacks 'bedeuten'.","The full stop after 'Erfahrung.' before 'zu welchem' appears to be an OCR slip for a comma; corrected silently."]],"krv-a-0852":["Seventh Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. Critical Decision of the Cosmological Conflict of Reason with Itself.",["'Streit' = conflict (no glossary entry)."]],"krv-a-0853":["The whole antinomy of pure reason rests on the dialectical argument: If the conditioned is given, then the whole series of all its conditions is also given: Now objects of the senses are given to us as conditioned, consequently etc. Through this syllogism, whose major premise seems so natural and evident, there are now introduced, according to the diversity of the conditions (in the synthesis of appearances), insofar as they make up a series, just as many cosmological ideas, which postulate the absolute totality of these series and by precisely this place reason unavoidably in conflict with itself. But before we uncover the deceptive element of this rationalizing argument, we must put ourselves in a position to do so by correcting and determining certain concepts occurring in it.",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; Obersatz = major premise; Gegenstände = objects; Totalität = totality (Allheit in glossary; here the Latinate 'Totalität', also rendered 'totality').","Bedingte = conditioned; Bedingungen = conditions; Synthesis = synthesis; vernünftelnden = rationalizing.","Missing full stop after 'versetzen' in the source (OCR or original) kept as a sentence break; 'Widerstreit' = conflict (Streit in heading also 'conflict').","'Berichtigung und Bestimmung' = 'correcting and determining'."]],"krv-a-0854":["First the following proposition is clear and undoubtedly certain: that, if the conditioned is given, a regressus in the series of all conditions for it is thereby given to us as a problem; for the concept of the conditioned already brings this with it: that thereby something is related to a condition and, if this is in turn conditioned, to a more remote condition and so through all the members of the series. This proposition is therefore analytic and rises above all fear of a transcendental critique. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue through the understanding that connection of a concept with its conditions which already attaches to the concept itself, and to continue it as far as possible.",["aufgegeben = given as a problem (Aufgabe = problem); Satz = proposition; Begriff = concept; Verstand = understanding; Verknüpfung = connection.","Regressus left in Latin, italicized: *regressus*.","'dieienige Verknüpfung ... die schon dem Begriffe selbst anhängt': the relative clause could attach to 'Verknüpfung' or, less naturally, be read with 'Bedingungen'; left as in German.","'Critik' rendered 'critique'; 'Furcht vor eine' retained as 'fear of a'."]],"krv-a-0855":["Further: if the conditioned as well as its condition are things in themselves, then, when the former has been given, not merely is the regress to the second set as a task, but this is thereby already really given along with it, and, because this holds of all members of the series: so the complete series of conditions, hence also the unconditioned, is thereby given at the same time, or rather presupposed, by the fact that the conditioned, which was possible only through that series, is given. Here the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition is a synthesis of the mere understanding, which represents things as they are, without paying attention to whether, and how, we can arrive at cognition of them. If, on the other hand, I have to do with appearances, which, as mere representations, are not given at all, if I do not arrive at cognition of them (i.e., at them themselves, for they are nothing but empirical cognitions), then I cannot say in the very same meaning: if the conditioned is given, then all the conditions (as appearances) for it are also given, and can hence in no way infer the absolute totality of the series of them. For the appearances are, in apprehension, themselves nothing other than an empirical synthesis (in space and time) and are therefore given only in this. Now it does not follow at all that, if the conditioned (in the appearance) is given, the synthesis that constitutes its empirical condition is thereby also given along with it and presupposed, but this takes place for the first time in the regress, and never without it. But what one can well say in such a case is that a regress to the conditions, i.e., a continued empirical synthesis on this side, is commanded or set as a task, and that there cannot be a lack of conditions that are given through this regress.",["'Aufgabe/aufgegeben' rendered 'task'/'set as a task'; the glossary gives 'problem' for Aufgabe, but the verb 'aufgeben' is not listed. The wordplay with 'gegeben' (given) is kept visible by the verb 'set'.","'Regressus' is Latin in the German text and is rendered 'regress'; 'Synthesis', 'Apprehension' rendered by cognates.","'Kentniß' (here 'Kentnisse') = 'cognition' per the glossary's Erkenntnis? No: Kenntnis is glossed 'acquaintance; knowledge'; the context ('empirische Kentnisse') is rendered 'cognition(s)' in this passage, an acknowledged departure from the glossary entry; 'Kentniß' is an old spelling of 'Kenntnis'.","'Dinge an sich selbst' = 'things in themselves'; 'Bedeutung' = 'meaning'; 'Verstand' = 'understanding'; 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'Vorstellung' = 'representation'.","'so wol, als' = 'as well as' (modern 'sowohl ... als'); 'dadurch' in 'dadurch zugleich gegeben, oder vielmehr vorausgesezt, daß ...' is left with the same ambiguity as the German ('thereby ... by the fact that')."]],"krv-a-0856":["From this it is clear that the major premise of the cosmological syllogism takes the conditioned in the transcendental meaning of a pure category, but the minor premise in the empirical meaning of a concept of the understanding applied to mere appearances, consequently that dialectical deception is encountered in it which is called *sophisma figurae dictionis*. This deception is not contrived, however, but a quite natural illusion of common reason. For through the latter we presuppose (in the major premise) the conditions and their series, as it were without looking, when something is given as conditioned, because this is nothing other than the logical demand to assume complete premises for a given conclusion, and there no temporal order is to be encountered in the connection of the conditioned with its condition; they are presupposed in themselves as given at the same time. Further, it is just as natural (in the minor premise) to regard appearances as things in themselves and just as well as objects given to the mere understanding, as happened in the major premise, since I abstracted from all conditions of intuition under which alone objects can be given. Now, however, we had in this overlooked a noteworthy difference between the concepts. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition and the whole series of the latter (in the major premise) carried with it nothing at all of limitation through time and no concept of succession. On the other hand, the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions in the appearance (which is subsumed in the minor premise) is necessarily successive and given only in time one after another; consequently I could not presuppose the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series represented thereby here just as well as there, because there all members of the series are given in themselves (without temporal condition), but here are possible only through the successive regress, which is given only by one's actually carrying it out.",["'Obersatz/Untersatz' = 'major premise/minor premise'; 'Vernunftschluß' = 'syllogism'; 'Schlußsatz' = 'conclusion'; 'Prämissen' = 'premises'.","'Sophisma figurae dictionis' kept in Latin, italicized. 'Betrug' = 'deception'; 'Täuschung' = 'illusion'; 'Schein' not used here.","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'; 'Succeßion' = 'succession'; 'Totalität' = 'totality'. 'Einschränkung' = 'limitation' (Schranke is the glossary's 'limitation' but not the word here).","'hiebey' is rendered 'in this'; 'Begriffen' ('concepts') is left without specification as in the German.","'die im Untersatze subsumirt wird' has singular 'wird' after the plural-looking 'Reihe' (singular); the relative clause could attach to 'Erscheinung' or 'Reihe'; kept ambiguous ('which is subsumed').","'nent' = 'nennt'; 'Dieses Betrug' (sic) for 'Dieser Betrug'; 'dadurch ... daß' ambiguity in the last clause ('given only by one's actually carrying it out') preserves 'daß man ihn wirklich vollführt'."]],"krv-a-0857":["After the conviction of such a misstep, of the argument laid in common at the ground (of the cosmological assertions), both contending parties can rightly be dismissed as those who ground their demand on no thorough title. But their dispute is not thereby ended to the extent that they would have been convinced that they, or one of the two, would be in the wrong in the matter itself that he asserts (in the conclusion), even if he did not know how to build it on sound grounds of proof. Yet nothing seems clearer than that of two, of whom the one asserts: the world has a beginning, the other: the world has no beginning, but it is from eternity, one must nevertheless be right. But if this is so, then, because the clarity is equal on both sides, it is nevertheless impossible ever to find out on which side the right lies, and the dispute continues as before, even if the parties have been referred to peace by the court of reason. There remains, then, no means left to end the dispute thoroughly and to the satisfaction of both parties, except that, since they can after all so beautifully refute one another, they finally be convinced that they are disputing about nothing, and that a certain transcendental illusion has painted for them a reality there where none is to be encountered. This path of settling a dispute that cannot be adjudicated we now want to take.",["'Ueberweisung' = 'conviction' (in the legal sense, of a fault); 'überführt' = 'convinced'; Überzeugung (glossary 'conviction') is not the word used here.","'Schein' = 'illusion'; 'Wirklichkeit' = 'reality' (glossary: 'actuality; reality'); 'Mittel' = 'means'; 'Beweisgründe' = 'grounds of proof'; 'Gerichtshof' = 'court'.","'zum Grunde gelegten' = 'laid at the ground'; 'gemeinschaftlich' = 'in common'. The parenthesis '(der cosmologischen Behauptungen)' could attach to 'Grunde' or 'Arguments'; left as in German.","'Streit' = 'dispute'; 'Zwist' = 'dispute' as well (one English word for two German words).","'sie, oder einer von beiden, hätte ... Unrecht' is kept as a subjunctive of indirect speech; 'Parteyen' = 'parties'."]],"krv-a-0858":["The Eleatic Zeno, a subtle dialectician, was already very much censured by Plato as a wanton sophist for having, in order to show his art, sought to prove one and the same proposition through apparent arguments and soon afterward to overturn it again through others just as strong. He asserted: God (presumably for him it was nothing but the world) is neither finite nor infinite, is neither in motion nor at rest, is neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those who judged him about this that he wanted to deny entirely two propositions contradicting one another, which is absurd. But I do not find that this can rightly be laid to his charge. The first of these propositions I shall soon illuminate more closely. As for the rest, if he understood by the word: God, the universe, then he certainly had to say that this is neither persistently present in its place (at rest), nor changes the same (moves), since all places are only in the universe, which itself is therefore in no place. If the all of the world comprises in itself everything that exists, then it is also to this extent neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing, since outside it there is no other thing with which it could be compared. If two opposed judgments presuppose an inadmissible condition, then, regardless of their conflict (which is nevertheless no proper contradiction), both of them fall away, because the condition falls away under which alone each of these propositions was supposed to hold.",["'Satz' = 'proposition' throughout; 'Urteil' = 'judgment'; 'beurteilen' = 'judge' here ('assess; judge' in the glossary), rendered 'judged'.","'beharrlich' = 'persistently' (cf. beharren/Beharrlichkeit = persist/persistence); 'Ort/Oerter' = 'place(s)'; 'Universum/Univers' = 'universe'; 'Weltall' = 'all of the world'.","'Widerstreit' = 'conflict'; 'Widerspruch' = 'contradiction'; 'widersprechende' = 'contradicting'.","'denselben verändere' — 'denselben' (masc.) refers back to 'Orte'; rendered 'the same'. 'Was die übrige betrift' (sic, 'übrigen') = 'As for the rest'.","'Den ersteren dieser Sätze': 'the first of these propositions' (literal 'the former'); 'der Eleatische Zeno' retains 'Eleatic'.","'muthwillig' = 'wanton'; 'scheinbare Argumente' = 'apparent arguments' (ambiguous between 'seeming' and 'illusory' as in the German)."]],"krv-a-0859":["If someone said: every body either smells good or does not smell good, a third case takes place, namely that it does not smell at all (exhales), and so both conflicting propositions can be false. If I say, it is either good-smelling or it is not good-smelling: (*vel suaueolens vel non suaueolens*) then both judgments are opposed to one another contradictorily and only the first is false, but its contradictory opposite, namely some bodies are not good-smelling, also comprises in itself the bodies that do not smell at all. In the previous opposition (*per disparata*) the contingent condition of the concept of body (the smell) remained with the conflicting judgment, and was therefore not also sublated by it, hence the latter was not the contradictory opposite of the former.",["'widerstreitend' = 'conflicting'; 'contradictorisch' = 'contradictorily'; 'Gegentheil' = 'opposite'; 'entgegengesezt' = 'opposed'.","'zufällige' = 'contingent'; 'Begriff' = 'concept'; 'aufgehoben' = 'sublated' (rendered with the standard cognate; not in the glossary).","'ausdufte' (sic, 'ausdufte' from 'ausdunsten/ausduften') rendered 'exhales' in parentheses as in the German; 'Cörper' = 'body'.","Latin phrases 'vel suaueolens vel non suaueolens' and 'per disparata' are Kant's; 'suaueolens' is Kant's spelling (for 'suaveolens') and is kept.","'der erste ist falsch' and 'der vorigen Entgegenstellung' are rendered literally; 'das letztere'/'des ersteren' = 'the latter'/'the former'."]],"krv-a-0860":["If I say, accordingly: the world is, as to space, either infinite, or it is not infinite (*non est infinitus*), then, if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite: the world is not infinite, must be true. Thereby I would only cancel an infinite world without positing another, namely the finite. But if it were said: the world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite), both could be false. For I then regard the world as determined in itself, as to its magnitude, in that in the opposition I do not merely cancel the infinity and, with it, perhaps its whole separate existence, but add to the world a determination, as to a thing actual in itself, which can just as well be false, namely if the world should not be given at all as a thing in itself, hence also not as to its magnitude, neither as infinite nor as finite. May I be permitted to call such an opposition the dialectical, but that of contradiction the analytic opposition. Thus of two judgments dialectically opposed to one another both can be false, because the one does not merely contradict the other, but says something more than is required for a contradiction.",["Original reads 'fasch'; silently corrected to 'falsch' (false).","'Widerspruch' = contradiction; 'contradictorisch' kept as 'contradictory' (Latinate), 'Widerstreit' elsewhere = 'conflict'.","'Grösse' = magnitude; 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Ding an sich' = thing in itself; 'Urtheile' = judgments.","'zur Welt ... hinzusetze': 'hinzusetzen' rendered 'add' (not the glossary's 'setzen' = posit, which is used for 'zu setzen').","'als einem an sich selbst wirklichen Dinge': dative, rendered 'as to a thing actual in itself' to keep the construction ambiguous between 'to the world, as to a thing...' and 'determination... as to a thing'."]],"krv-a-0861":["If one regards the two propositions: the world is infinite as to magnitude, the world is finite as to its magnitude, as opposed to one another contradictorily, then one assumes that the world (the whole series of appearances) is a thing in itself. For it remains, whether I cancel the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its appearances. But if I take away this presupposition, or this transcendental illusion, and deny that it is a thing in itself, then the contradictory conflict of the two assertions is transformed into a merely dialectical one and the world, because it does not exist at all in itself (independently of the regressive series of my representations): thus it exists neither as an in itself infinite, nor as an in itself finite whole. It is to be encountered only in the empirical regress of the series of appearances and not at all for itself. Hence, if this is always conditioned, then it is never given as a whole, and the world is therefore no unconditioned whole, and so does not exist as such either, neither with infinite nor finite magnitude.",["'Schein' = illusion; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Regressus' kept as 'regress'.","'Sätze' = propositions; 'Behauptungen' = assertions.","'sie' in 'Denn sie bleibt' and 'wenn diese iederzeit bedingt ist' left as pronouns; antecedents ('world', 'series', 'regress') not resolved.","'vor sich selbst' rendered 'for itself' (also possible: 'by itself').","'Ganze' rendered 'whole'; 'unbedingt' = unconditioned; 'bedingt' = conditioned."]],"krv-a-0862":["What has been said here of the first cosmological idea, namely the absolute totality of magnitude in the appearance, holds also of all the rest. The series of conditions is to be encountered only in the regressive synthesis itself, but not in itself in the appearance, as a peculiar thing given prior to all regress. Hence I shall also have to say: the multitude of parts in a given appearance is in itself neither finite nor infinite, because appearance is nothing existing in itself, and the parts are first given through the regress of the decomposing synthesis, and in it, which regress is never given absolutely whole, neither as finite nor as infinite. The very same holds of the series of causes ordered over one another, or of the conditioned up to unconditionally necessary existence, which can never be regarded either in itself as to its totality as finite, or as infinite, because as a series of subordinated representations it subsists only in the dynamical regress, but prior to it and, as a series of things subsisting for themselves, cannot exist at all in itself.",["'Totalität' = totality; 'Menge' = multitude; 'Theile' = parts; 'Existenz' = existence.","'decomponirenden' rendered 'decomposing'.","'welcher Regressus ... gegeben ist': relative clause attaches to 'regress'; 'in demselben' = 'in it' (regress or synthesis ambiguous, preserved).","'weder an sich ... als endlich, noch als unendlich' keeps German ambiguity of 'an sich' attaching to 'angesehen' or to 'Totalität'.","'ihrer Totalität nach': 'its totality' retained; 'nothing existing in itself' for 'nichts an sich selbst existirendes'."]],"krv-a-0863":["Thus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas is removed, by showing that it is merely dialectical and a conflict of an illusion, which arises from one's having applied the idea of absolute totality, which holds only as a condition of things in themselves, to appearances, which exist only in the representation and, if they make up a series, in the successive regress, but otherwise not at all. But one can also conversely draw from this antinomy a true benefit, not indeed dogmatic, but still critical and doctrinal: namely to prove indirectly the transcendental ideality of appearances, in case someone should perhaps not be satisfied with the direct proof in the transcendental aesthetic. The proof would consist in this dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself: then it is either finite or infinite; Now the former as well as the second is false (according to the proofs of the antithesis adduced above, on the one side, and of the thesis on the other). Therefore it is also false that the world (the sum total of all appearances) is a whole existing in itself. From which it then follows: that appearances in general are nothing outside our representations, which is just what we wanted to say by their transcendental ideality.",["'Inbegriff' = sum total; 'Beweis' = proof; 'Aesthetik' kept as spelled; 'Idealität' = ideality.","'Widerstreit' = conflict; 'Schein' = illusion.","'einer und der Thesis anderer Seits' rendered literally as 'of the antithesis on the one side, and of the thesis on the other'; the text 'einer' is odd and may be corrupt/misprinted (cf. 'einer Seits').","'Nun ist das erstere sowol als das zweite falsch': 'das zweite' kept as 'the second'.","'sie sey blos dialectisch': 'sie' = the antinomy, not resolved in text; subjunctive 'sey' rendered 'is'."]],"krv-a-0864":["This remark is of importance. One sees from it: that the above proofs of the fourfold antinomy were not deceptions, but well-grounded, under the presupposition, namely: that appearances or a sensible world, which comprehends them all together in itself, were things in themselves. But the conflict of the propositions drawn from this discovers that a falsity lies in the presupposition, and thereby brings us to a discovery of the true constitution of things, as objects of the senses. The transcendental dialectic thus does not at all lend any support to skepticism, but indeed to the skeptical method, which can point in it to an example of its great utility, when one lets the arguments of reason step forth against one another in their greatest freedom, which, even though they will not in the end deliver what one was seeking, will nevertheless always deliver something useful and serviceable for the correction of our judgments.",["'begreift' = comprehends (*comprehendere*); 'Beschaffenheit' = constitution; 'Gegenstände' = objects.","'Sinnenwelt' rendered 'sensible world'; 'Sinne' = senses.","'Scepticism' / 'sceptischen' rendered with American spelling 'skepticism' / 'skeptical'.","'Blendwerke' rendered 'deceptions' (literally 'dazzling-works').","'welche an ihr' ('which ... in it'): 'ihr' = the dialectic; 'die, ob sie gleich...' relative pronoun left with ambiguous antecedent ('arguments')."]],"krv-a-0865":["Eighth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason with Regard to the Cosmological Ideas.",["'Princip' rendered 'principle' (*Grundsatz*/*Princip*: Latinate 'Princip' here, same English word).","'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'."]],"krv-a-0866":["Since through the cosmological principle of totality no maximum of the series of conditions in a world of sense, as a thing in itself, is given, but can only be set as a task in the *regressus* of the same, the aforementioned principle of pure reason, in its meaning thus rectified, still retains its good validity, to be sure not as an axiom, to think the totality in the object as actual, but as a problem for the understanding, thus for the subject, in order, in keeping with the completeness in the idea, to institute and continue the *regressus* in the series of conditions for a given conditioned. For in sensibility, i.e., in space and time, every condition to which we can attain in the exposition of given appearances is in turn conditioned; because these are no objects in themselves, in which the absolutely unconditioned might possibly take place, but merely empirical representations, which must always find their condition in intuition, which determines them according to space or time. The principle of reason is therefore properly only a rule, which commands a *regressus* in the series of conditions of given appearances, for which it is never permitted to stop at an absolutely unconditioned. It is therefore no *principium* of the possibility of experience and of the empirical cognition of the objects of the senses, consequently no principle of the understanding; for every experience is enclosed within its boundaries (in accordance with the given intuition), also no constitutive principle of reason, to extend the concept of the world of sense beyond all possible experience, but a principle of the greatest possible continuation and extension of experience, according to which no empirical boundary must count as an absolute boundary, thus a *principium* of reason, which, as a rule, postulates what is to happen on our part in the *regressus*, and does not anticipate what is given in the object in itself prior to all *regressus*. Hence I call it a regulative principle of reason, whereas the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as given in the object (the appearances) in itself, would be a constitutive cosmological principle, whose nullity I have wanted to indicate precisely through this distinction and thereby to prevent: that one not, as otherwise unavoidably happens, (through transcendental subreption) attribute objective reality to an idea which serves merely as a rule.",["Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Sinne = 'senses'.","Totalität = 'totality' (Allheit would also be 'totality'). Problem and Aufgabe: 'problem' for Problem; 'set as a task' for aufgegeben, to keep the Aufgabe connection visible.","Grundsatz and Princip/Principium are both 'principle'; Principium is kept in Latin, italicized. Regressus is kept in Latin, italicized, throughout.","Obiect (object) and Gegenstand (objects) are both 'object'. Gränze = 'boundary'.","'welche sie dem Raume oder der Zeit nach bestimt': the antecedent of 'welche' (intuition or condition) is left as ambiguous as in the German.","'von uns' rendered 'on our part'; 'vor' in 'vor absolute Gränze' and 'vor allem Regressus' rendered as 'as'/'prior to' respectively, per the German usage ('vor' = für in the first)."]],"krv-a-0867":["In order now to determine properly the sense of this rule of pure reason, it is first of all to be noted: that it cannot say what the object is, but how the empirical *regressus* is to be instituted in order to arrive at the complete concept of the object. For if the former took place, it would be a constitutive *principium*, of the kind that is never possible from pure reason. One can therefore by no means have the intention with it of saying: the series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite, or infinite; for thereby a mere idea of absolute totality, which is created solely in itself, would think an object that can be given in no experience, in that to a series of appearances an objective reality independent of empirical synthesis would be imparted. The idea of reason will therefore prescribe only to the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions a rule, according to which it proceeds from the conditioned, by means of all the conditions subordinated to one another, to the unconditioned, although this is never reached. For the absolutely unconditioned is not encountered at all in experience.",["Obiect / Gegenstand = 'object' in both cases ('Obiect' in the first sentences, 'Gegenstand' in 'einen Gegenstand denken').","Absicht = 'intention'. Schlechthinunbedingte = 'the absolutely unconditioned'.","'in ihr selbst geschaffen': 'ihr' refers to the idea or to totality; the English 'itself' leaves this open as the German does.","'dieses' in 'obgleich dieses niemals erreicht wird' is rendered 'this', its antecedent left open (the unconditioned, as the nearest noun)."]],"krv-a-0868":["To this end, first, the synthesis of a series, insofar as it is never complete, is to be determined precisely. With this intention one usually makes use of two expressions, which are supposed to differ somewhat in this, without one however knowing quite how to give the ground of this distinction. The mathematicians speak solely of a *progressus in infinitum*. The investigators of concepts (philosophers) want to let count in its place only the expression of a *progressus in indefinitum*. Without dwelling on the examination of the scruple that advised such a distinction to these, and on the good or fruitless use of it, I will seek to determine these concepts precisely in relation to my intention.",["Ausdruck = 'expression'; Absicht = 'intention' (both occurrences); Begriff = 'concept'; Grund = 'ground'.","'Progressus' is kept in Latin, italicized, as Kant prints it in Latin type.","'diesen' ('advised ... to these') refers back to the philosophers; 'dessen statt' refers to the mathematicians' expression. The German is kept as is.","'geltenlassen' = 'let count' (lit. let be valid); Bedenklichkeit = 'scruple'."]],"krv-a-0869":["Of a straight line one can say with right that it can be extended to infinity, and here the distinction of the infinite and of the indeterminably far progression (*progressus in indefinitum*) would be an empty subtlety. For, although, when it is said: draw a line on, it indeed sounds more correct if one adds, *in indefinitum*, than if it is said, *in infinitum*; because the former signifies no more than: extend it as far as you like, but the second: you shall never cease extending it (which is just not the intention here), still, if only the can is at issue, the former expression is entirely correct; for you can always make it greater to infinity. And so it stands also in all cases where one speaks only of the *progressus*, i.e., of the progression from the condition to the conditioned; this possible progression goes on to infinity in the series of appearances. From a pair of parents you can go on without end in the descending line of procreation and also quite well think that it actually goes on so in the world. For here reason never needs absolute totality of the series, because it does not presuppose such as condition and as given (*datum*), but only as something conditioned, which is only specifiable (*dabile*), and is added on without end.",["unbestimbar (sic, for unbestimmbar) = 'indeterminably'; Fortgang = 'progression'; Progressus kept as *progressus*.","'angeblich (dabile)': 'angeblich' ordinarily means 'alleged'; given the Latin gloss dabile it is rendered 'specifiable' (cf. angeben); the reading is doubtful.","'welches hiebey eben nicht die Absicht ist': 'which' has the preceding clause as antecedent; 'to infinity' appears for 'ins Unendliche' in both places.","'ihr/euch' = plural 'you'; 'sie' in 'daß sie wirklich ... fortgehe' refers to the line (or the series); left as 'it'.","'Welt' = 'world'; Zeugung = 'procreation'; bedeuten = 'signify'."]],"krv-a-0870":["Quite otherwise is it with the problem: how far the *regressus*, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions in a series, extends, whether I can say: it is a going back to infinity, or only a going back extending indeterminably far (*in indefinitum*), and whether I can thus ascend upward to infinity from the human beings now living, in the series of their forebears, or whether it can only be said: that, however far I have gone back, no empirical ground is ever encountered for holding the series to be bounded anywhere, so that I am entitled and at the same time bound, for each of the primal fathers, to seek out still further his forefather, although not exactly to presuppose it.",["Aufgabe = 'problem'. Rückgang = 'going back'; Voreltern = 'forebears'; Urväter = 'primal fathers'; Vorfahren = 'forefather' (singular 'seinen').","'vor begränzt' = 'as bounded' (vor for für); 'ieztlebenden' = 'now living'.","'ob gleich eben nicht vorauszusetzen': the passage ends here; the object of 'to presuppose' is not stated in the German (the 'it' in English is a minimal grammatical necessity and could refer to the forefather or to his seeking)."]],"krv-a-0871":["I say accordingly: if the whole has been given in empirical intuition, then the regress in the series of its inner conditions goes to infinity; but if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is first to proceed to absolute totality: then only a going back to indeterminate distance (*in indefinitum*) takes place. Thus of the division of a matter given between its boundaries (of a body) it must be said: it goes to infinity. For this matter is given whole, consequently with all its possible parts, in empirical intuition. Since now the condition of this whole is its part and the condition of this part is the part of the part, etc., and in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned (indivisible) member of this series of conditions is never encountered, there is not only nowhere an empirical ground for stopping in the division, but the further members of the division to be continued are themselves empirically given even before this further-going division, i.e., the division goes to infinity. On the other hand, the series of forebears of a given human being is given in no possible experience in its absolute totality, yet the regress goes from each member of this generation to a higher one, so that no empirical boundary is to be encountered that would exhibit a member as absolutely unconditioned. But since nevertheless the members that could furnish the condition for this also do not already lie in the empirical intuition of the whole before the regress: this goes not to infinity (of the division of what is given), but to indeterminate distance, of the searching out of more members for the given ones, which in turn are always given only as conditioned.",["Regressus is Kant's Latin word, kept in the text; 'in indefinitum' is italicized as Latin.","'Gränzen' and 'Gränze' rendered 'boundaries'/'boundary' (glossary Grenze).","'Totalität' = totality (Allheit is the glossary term; Totalität is the Latinate member, also rendered 'totality').","'unbestimte Weite' and later 'unbestimbare Weite' (original misprint for unbestimmte, kept as in the text) both rendered 'indeterminate distance'.","'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'Glied' = member; 'Theilung' = division (Teilung).","'der Theilung des gegebenen' and 'der Aufsuchung mehrerer Glieder' are appositive genitives; I have kept the construction as it stands, 'of the division...', 'of the searching out...'.","The space before the colon in 'liegen :' is OCR/typographical; punctuation normalized. 'Cörpers' = body."]],"krv-a-0872":["In neither of the two cases, the *regressus in infinitum* as well as that *in indefinitum*, is the series of conditions regarded as given as infinite in the object. They are not things that are given in themselves, but only appearances, which, as conditions of one another, are given only in the regress itself. Thus the question is no longer: how great this series of conditions in itself may be, whether finite or infinite, for it is nothing in itself, but: how we are to set up the empirical regress and how far we are to continue it. And here there is a notable difference with regard to the rule of this progress. If the whole has been given empirically, then it is possible to go back to infinity in the series of its inner conditions. But if that is not given, but is first to be given through empirical regress, then I can only say: it is possible to go on to infinity to ever higher conditions of the series. In the former case I could say: there are always more members there and empirically given than I reach through the regress (of decomposition); but in the second: I can always still go further in the regress, because no member is given empirically as absolutely unconditioned, and thus a higher member is still always admitted as possible and hence the inquiry after it as necessary. There it was necessary to encounter more members of the series, but here it is always necessary to ask after more, because no experience bounds absolutely. For you have either no perception that absolutely bounds your empirical regress, and then you must not hold your regress to be completed, or you have such a perception bounding your series, then this cannot be a part of your series traversed (because that which bounds must be distinguished from that which is bounded by it) and you must therefore continue your regress also to this condition, and so on.",["'Obiect' (Latinate) and 'Gegenstand' both 'object'; here only 'Obiect' occurs.","'Fortschritt' rendered 'progress'; 'fortgehen' 'go on'.","'Gränzen/begränzt' rendered 'bound(s)/bounded' (Grenze).","'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Wahrnehmung' = perception; 'Theil' = part.","'denn' in 'und denn müßt ihr' is the older 'dann' ('then'); 'vor vollendet halten' = 'hold to be completed' ('vor' for 'für').","The sentence 'oder habt eine solche ... so kan diese' is kept with its irregular syntax (no 'wenn')."]],"krv-a-0873":["The following section will put these remarks in their proper light through their application.",[]],"krv-a-0874":["Ninth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason. On the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason, with Regard to All Cosmological Ideas.",["'Gebrauch' = use; 'Princip' = principle (Latinate; Grundsatz also 'principle', not occurring here); 'Idee' = idea."]],"krv-a-0875":["Since, as we have shown several times, there is no transcendental use, as little of pure concepts of the understanding as of concepts of reason, since the absolute totality of the series of conditions in the world of sense rests solely on a transcendental use of reason, which demands this unconditioned completeness from that which it presupposes as a thing in itself, but the world of sense contains nothing of the kind: so there can never again be talk of the absolute magnitude of the series in it, whether they may be bounded or in themselves unbounded, but only how far we are to go back in the empirical regress, in tracing experience back to its conditions, in order, according to the rule of reason, to stop at no other answering of its questions than that appropriate to the object.",["'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of sense' (Sinn, Welt).","'Verstandes- als Vernunftbegriffen' = 'concepts of the understanding' and 'concepts of reason'.","'Grösse' = magnitude; 'Gegenstand' = object.","'Beantwortung der Ffragen derselben': 'derselben' could refer to reason or to the series; kept as 'its', ambiguous as in German.","'bey keiner andern, als dem Gegenstande angemessenen Beantwortung' is kept as literal as possible; the construction is awkward in the original too.","'Dasselbe/derselben' in 'in derselben' refers to the world of sense or the series; ambiguity preserved as 'in it'."]],"krv-a-0876":["It is thus only the validity of the principle of reason, as a rule of the continuation and magnitude of a possible experience, that alone remains left to us, after its invalidity, as a constitutive principle of appearances in themselves, has been sufficiently demonstrated. Also, if we can lay that indubitably before our eyes, the conflict of reason with itself will be completely ended, in that not only is the illusion that set it at variance with itself removed through critical resolution, but in its place the sense in which it agrees with itself, and whose misinterpretation alone occasioned the conflict, is disclosed and an otherwise dialectical principle is transformed into a doctrinal one. In fact, if this, according to its subjective significance, can be confirmed as determining the greatest possible use of the understanding in experience appropriately to the objects of it, then it is just as much as if it, like an axiom (which is impossible from pure reason), determined the objects in themselves *a priori*; for even this could have no greater influence with regard to the objects of experience on the extension and correction of our cognition than that it proved itself active in the most extended use of our understanding in experience.",["'Grundsatz' = principle (Grundsatz); 'Princip' also 'principle'.","'Gültigkeit/Ungültigkeit' = validity/invalidity; 'Schein' = illusion; 'Sinn' = sense (n.); 'Bedeutung' = significance.","'Gegenstände' and 'Obiecte' both 'object(s)'; 'Obiecte' is the Latinate member.","'iene' ('that') has no clear antecedent in the German (the validity); kept as 'that'.","'dieses' in 'auch dieses könte' (the axiom) vs. 'dieser' (the principle) kept as 'this'/'even this'; 'ob er' = 'it' referring to the principle.","'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'a priori' italicized as Latin.","'den Gegenständen derselben angemessen' could attach to 'bestimmen' or 'Verstandesgebrauch'; kept ambiguous as 'appropriately to the objects of it'."]],"krv-a-0877":["I. Resolution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of the Appearances of a World-Whole.",["'Weltganzes' = 'world-whole'; 'Zusammensetzung' = composition; 'Auflösung' = resolution."]],"krv-a-0878":["Both here and in the remaining cosmological questions the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition: that in the empirical regress no experience of an absolute boundary, hence of no condition as one that is empirically absolutely unconditioned, can be encountered. But the ground of this is: that such an experience would have to contain in itself a bounding of appearances by nothing, or the empty, on which the continued regress by means of a perception could come upon, which is impossible.",["'Satz' = proposition; 'Grund' = ground; 'Gränze' = boundary; 'Begränzung' = bounding.","'darauf der fortgeführte Regressus ... stoßen könte' is awkward in the German (relative 'darauf' with 'stoßen'); kept as 'on which ... could come upon'.","'Nichts' capitalized in the original; rendered 'nothing'."]],"krv-a-0879":["This proposition now, which says just as much as: that in the empirical regress I always arrive only at a condition that must itself in turn be regarded as empirically conditioned, contains the rule *in terminis*: that however far I may have come with it in the ascending series, I must always ask after a higher member of the series, whether this may become known to me through experience or not.",["The OCR 'Beding un' is silently corrected to 'Bedingung'.","'in terminis' kept in Latin and italicized.","'damit' = 'with it', antecedent unclear (kept literal); 'bekant' = 'known' (bekannt)."]],"krv-a-0880":["Now for the resolution of the first cosmological problem nothing further is necessary than still to settle: whether in the regress to the unconditioned magnitude of the world-whole (in respect of time and space) this never bounded ascending can be called a going back to infinity, or only an indeterminately continued regress (*in indefinitum*).",["'Aufgabe' = problem; 'Grösse' = magnitude; 'Weltganzes' = world-whole.","'unbestimbar' (misprint for unbestimmt) rendered 'indeterminately'.","'Rückgang ins Unendliche' = 'going back to infinity'; 'Aufsteigen' = 'ascending' (nominalized infinitive)."]],"krv-a-0881":["The mere general representation of the series of all past states of the world, likewise of the things that are simultaneously in the world-space, is itself nothing other than a possible empirical regress, which I think to myself, although still indeterminately, and through which alone the concept of such a series of conditions for the given perception can arise*). Now I have the world-whole at all times only in the concept, but by no means (as a whole) in intuition. Thus I cannot infer from its magnitude to the magnitude of the regress, and determine the latter in conformity with the former, but rather I must first make for myself a concept of the magnitude of the world through the magnitude of the empirical regress. Of this, however, I never know anything more than that from each given member of the series of conditions I must always still proceed empirically to a higher (more remote) member. Thus through this the magnitude of the whole of appearances is not determined at all absolutely, consequently one also cannot say: that this regress goes to infinity, because this would anticipate the members to which the regress has not yet arrived and would represent their multitude as so great that no empirical synthesis can arrive at it, consequently would determine the magnitude of the world prior to the regress (though only negatively), which is impossible. For this is given to me through no intuition (according to its totality), consequently its magnitude prior to the regress is also not given at all. Accordingly we can say nothing at all about the magnitude of the world in itself, not even that a *regressus in infinitum* takes place in it, but rather must seek the concept of its magnitude only in accordance with the rule that determines the empirical regress in it. This rule, however, says nothing more than that, however far we may have come in the series of empirical conditions, we should nowhere assume an absolute boundary, but rather subordinate every appearance, as conditioned, to another as its condition, and thus must progress further to this, which is the *regressus in indefinitum*, which, because it determines no magnitude in the object, is to be distinguished clearly enough from that *in infinitum*.",["\"feiner Grösse\" read as OCR slip for \"seiner Grösse\".","Weltzustände = states of the world; Weltraume = world-space; Weltganze = world-whole; Glied = member; Gränze = boundary; Object = object (Latinate form; Gegenstand is also \"object\").","\"und diese jener gemäß bestimmen\": \"diese\" and \"jener\" kept as \"the latter\" and \"the former\"; the German pronouns are not replaced by nouns.","\"vor dem Regressus\" = \"prior to the regress\" (vor can also be \"before\"); \"*) \" marks a footnote, translated as krv-a-0882."]],"krv-a-0882":["*) This series of the world can thus be neither greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, on which alone its concept rests. And since this can give no determinate infinite, but just as little a determinately finite one (absolutely bounded): so it is clear from this that we can assume the magnitude of the world neither as finite nor as infinite, because the regress (through which that is represented) permits neither of the two.",["\"bestimtendliches\" is an unusual compound (bestimt + endlich); rendered \"determinately finite\", with the gloss in parentheses \"schlechthinbegränztes\" = \"absolutely bounded\".","\"iene\" (that) refers back to the series/magnitude of the world; left as \"that\"."]],"krv-a-0883":["I can accordingly not say: the world is infinite according to past time, or according to space. For such a concept of magnitude, as of a given infiniteness, is empirical, consequently also with regard to the world, as an object of the senses, absolutely impossible. I will also not say: the regress from a given perception onward, to all that which bounds this in a series in space as well as in past time, goes to infinity; for this presupposes the infinite magnitude of the world; also not: it is finite; for the absolute boundary is likewise empirically impossible. Accordingly I will be able to say nothing about the whole object of experience (the world of sense), but only about the rule according to which experience, suitably to its object, is to be instituted and continued.",["Gegenstand = object (Object elsewhere also \"object\"); Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Gränze = boundary.","\"was diese ... begränzt\": \"diese\" (this) is left unresolved; it may refer to the perception (feminine) as in the German.","\"angemessen\" rendered \"suitably\"; \"angestellt\" rendered \"instituted\"."]],"krv-a-0884":["To the cosmological question, therefore, concerning the magnitude of the world, the first and negative answer is: the world has no first beginning in time and no outermost boundary according to space.",["\"dem Raume nach\" rendered \"according to space\", parallel to the preceding passage."]],"krv-a-0885":["For in the opposite case it would be bounded by empty time on the one side, and by empty space on the other. Since it now, as appearance, can be neither of the two in itself, for appearance is no thing in itself, a perception of the bounding through absolutely empty time, or empty space, would have to be possible, through which these ends of the world would be given in a possible experience. But such an experience, as completely empty of content, is impossible. Thus an absolute boundary of the world is empirically, consequently also absolutely impossible*).",["\"Da sie nun ... keines von beiden an sich selbst seyn kan\": the German is loose; \"neither of the two\" kept without specifying what the two are.","Erscheinung = appearance; Ding an sich selbst = thing in itself; Inhalt = content; Wahrnehmung = perception.","\"*)\" marks the footnote translated as krv-a-0886."]],"krv-a-0886":["*) One will remark: that the proof here has been conducted in an entirely different way from the dogmatic one, above in the antithesis of the first antinomy. There we had let the world of sense, according to the common and dogmatic way of representation, count as a thing that was given in itself, prior to all regress, according to its totality, and had denied it, if it did not occupy all time and all spaces, any determinate place at all in both. Hence the consequence was also different from here, namely it was inferred to its actual infinity.",["Art = way (\"auf ganz andere Art\"); Vorstellungsart = way of representation; Folgerung = consequence; wirklich = actual.","\"vor ein Ding\" (modern \"für\") rendered \"as a thing\"; \"was an sich selbst ... gegeben war\" kept in German order.","\"Stelle\" rendered \"place\"; Ort in the glossary is also \"place\"."]],"krv-a-0887":["From this there follows at once the affirmative answer: the regress in the series of appearances of the world, as a determination of the magnitude of the world, goes *in indefinitum*, which says just as much as: the world of sense has no absolute magnitude, but rather the empirical regress (through which alone it can be given on the side of its conditions) has its rule, namely from each member of the series, as a conditioned one, always to proceed to one still more remote (whether through one's own experience, or the guiding thread of history, or the chain of effects and their causes), and nowhere to exempt oneself from the extension of the possible empirical use of its understanding, which then is also the proper and sole business of reason in its principles.",["\"sich ... nirgend zu überheben\" rendered \"nowhere to exempt oneself\"; the reflexive subject is left as in the German (infinitive construction).","\"seines Verstandes\": \"its understanding\" is literal; the antecedent of \"seines\" is not specified in the German.","Bestimmung = determination; Gebrauch = use; Wirkung = effect; Principien = principles (Grundsatz would also be \"principle\")."]],"krv-a-0888":["A determinate empirical regress that would go on without ceasing in a certain kind of appearances is not prescribed by this, e.g., that from a living human being one must always ascend in a series of forebears, without expecting a first pair, or in the series of world-bodies without admitting an outermost sun, but rather only the progress from appearances to appearances is commanded, even if these should yield no actual perception (if it is in degree too weak for our consciousness to become experience), because they nonetheless belong to possible experience.",["\"sie ... ist\" in the parenthesis is singular in the German and probably refers to Wahrnehmung, while the \"diese ... abgeben\" is plural; both kept as written.","Art = kind; Weltcörper = world-bodies; Grad = degree; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Fortschritt = progress."]],"krv-a-0889":["All beginning is in time and all boundary of the extended in space. But space and time are only in the world of sense. Consequently only appearances in the world are in a conditioned way, but the world itself neither conditioned, nor bounded in an unconditioned way.",["\"bedingterweise\" rendered \"in a conditioned way\"; Art = way.","\"das Ausgedehnte\" rendered \"the extended\" (cf. Ausdehnung = extension)."]],"krv-a-0890":["Precisely for this reason, and since the world can never be given whole, and not even the series of conditions for a given conditioned, as a series of the world, whole, the concept of the magnitude of the world is given only through the regress and not prior to it in a collective intuition. That, however, always consists only in determining the magnitude, and thus gives no determinate concept, thus also no concept of a magnitude that would be infinite with respect to a certain measure, thus does not go to infinity (as it were given), but into indeterminate distance, in order to give a magnitude (of experience) that first becomes actual through this regress.",["\"Jener\" (that) = the regress, as opposed to \"demselben\" (it); left as pronouns.","\"Eben um deswillen\" rendered \"Precisely for this reason\"; \"ganz\" rendered \"whole\", \"wirklich\" \"actual\".","Anschauung = intuition; Bestimmen (noun use) = \"determining\"; Maasses = measure; Weite = distance."]],"krv-a-0891":["II. Resolution of the cosmological idea of the totality of the division of a given whole in intuition.",["Auflösung = resolution; Theilung = division; Totalität = totality; Anschauung = intuition.","\"in der Anschauung\" may attach to \"given whole\" or to \"division\"; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"krv-a-0892":["If I divide a whole that is given in intuition, then I proceed from a conditioned to the conditions of its possibility. The division of the parts (*subdivisio* or *decompositio*) is a *regressus* in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be given only if the *regressus* could arrive at simple parts. But if all parts in a continually progressing decomposition are always divisible again, then the division, i.e., the *regressus*, goes from the conditioned to its conditions *in infinitum*; because the conditions (the parts) are contained in the conditioned itself and, since this is given entirely in an intuition enclosed between its boundaries, are all also given along with it. The *regressus* may therefore not be called merely a going back *in indefinitum*, as the previous cosmological idea alone permitted, since I was to proceed from the conditioned to its conditions, which, being outside it, were thus not given along with it thereby, but which first came to be added in the empirical *regressus*. Notwithstanding this, it is nevertheless in no way permitted to say of such a whole, which is divisible to infinity: that it consists of infinitely many parts. For although all parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is nevertheless not contained in it, which consists only in the continually progressing decomposition, or the *regressus* itself, which first makes the series actual. Since this *regressus* is now infinite, all members (parts) to which it arrives are indeed contained in the given whole as aggregates, but not the whole series of the division, which is successively infinite and never whole, and consequently can present no infinite multitude, and no taking together of the same in a whole.",["Rückgang (\"going back\") is distinguished from the Latin *regressus* (German Regressus); *in infinitum* and *in indefinitum* are Kant's own Latin.","Theilung = division; Gränzen = boundaries; Glieder = members; Menge = multitude; Totalität rendered \"totality\" (the glossary's Allheit is a different word).","\"derselben\" in the last clause could refer back to \"Menge\" or to \"Reihe\"; the English \"the same\" leaves this open.","\"als Aggregate\" could attach to \"Glieder (Theile)\" or to \"Ganzen\"; left ambiguous in English."]],"krv-a-0893":["This general reminder lets itself be applied first, very easily, to space. Every space intuited within its boundaries is such a whole, whose parts in all decomposition are always spaces again, and it is therefore divisible to infinity.",["\"Erinnerung\" = \"reminder\" (literally a remark that recalls).","ins Unendliche = \"to infinity\"."]],"krv-a-0894":["From this there follows also quite naturally the second application, to an outer appearance enclosed within its boundaries (body). The divisibility of the same grounds itself on the divisibility of space, which constitutes the possibility of the body, as an extended whole. This is therefore divisible to infinity, without thereby consisting of infinitely many parts.",["\"desselben\" (the divisibility of the same) refers to the appearance/body; \"Dieser\" (This) could refer to the body or to space, and the English \"This\" preserves that.","Cörper = body; Erscheinung = appearance."]],"krv-a-0895":["It seems, to be sure: that, since a body must be represented as substance in space, it will be different from the latter in this, as far as the law of the divisibility of space is concerned: for one can well grant at all events: that the decomposition in the latter could never remove all composition, since then even all space, which otherwise has nothing self-subsisting, would cease (which is impossible); only that, if all composition of matter were canceled in thought, nothing at all should remain, seems not to let itself be united with the concept of a substance, which should properly be the subject of all composition, and would have to remain in its elements, even if their connection in space, through which they make up a body, were canceled. But with what is called substance in the appearance, it is not so constituted as one would think it of a thing in itself through a pure concept of the understanding. The former is not absolute subject, but persistent image of sensibility and nothing but intuition, in which nothing unconditioned is encountered at all.",["Subiect (Latinate spelling) = \"subject\"; Substanz = \"substance\"; Ding an sich selbst = \"thing in itself\".","\"Jenes\" = \"The former\" (the substance in the appearance); \"dem lezteren\" = \"the latter\" (space).","\"in der überall nichts Unbedingtes angetroffen wird\": \"überall\" could go with \"nichts\" (nothing at all) or mean \"anywhere\"; the English keeps \"at all\".","\"ist es nicht so bewandt\" rendered \"it is not so constituted\" (idiomatic: \"it is not the case\")."]],"krv-a-0896":["But although this rule of progress to infinity in the subdivision of an appearance, as a mere filling of space, finds place without any doubt: it nevertheless cannot hold if we want to extend it also to the multitude of the parts already separated in a certain way in the given whole, through which these make up a *quantum discretum*. To assume: that in every articulated (organized) whole every part is again articulated, and that in such a way, in the dissection of the parts to infinity, one always encounters new artificial parts, in a word, that the whole is articulated to infinity, simply does not let itself be thought, although indeed that the parts of matter, in their decomposition to infinity, could become articulated. For the infinity of the division of a given appearance in space grounds itself solely on this: that through this merely the divisibility, i.e., a multitude of parts in itself absolutely undetermined, is given, but the parts themselves are given and determined only through the subdivision, in short that the whole is not in itself already divided. Hence the division can determine a multitude in it, which goes as far as one wants to progress in the *regressus* of the division. On the other hand, in the case of an organic body articulated to infinity, the whole is, precisely through this concept, already represented as divided, and a multitude of the parts, in itself determined but infinite, is encountered in it before all *regressus* of the division, whereby one contradicts oneself; in that this infinite enfolding is regarded as a series never to be completed (infinite) and yet nevertheless as completed in a taking together. The infinite division designates only the appearance as *quantum continuum* and is inseparable from the filling of space; because precisely in the latter lies the ground of the infinite divisibility. But as soon as something is assumed as *quantum discretum*: the multitude of unities in it is determined; hence also at every time equal to a number. How far, then, the organization in an articulated body may go, only experience can decide, and even if it did not arrive with certainty at any unorganized part, such parts must nevertheless at least lie in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of an appearance in general extends is not at all a matter of experience, but a *principium* of reason, never to hold the empirical *regressus*, in the decomposition of the extended, in accordance with the nature of this appearance, as absolutely completed.",["*quantum discretum*, *quantum continuum*, *principium* (German Principium) are Kant's Latin; *regressus* (Regressus) kept in Latin.","Menge = multitude; Theilung = division; Zerlegung = \"dissection\"; Einwickelung = \"enfolding\"; Kunsttheile = \"artificial parts\"; gegliedert = \"articulated\".","\"in derselben\" (\"in the latter\") refers back grammatically to the filling (Erfüllung); \"in ihm\" and \"in demselben\" refer to the whole.","\"vor schlechthin vollendet zu halten\" literally \"to hold as absolutely completed\"; \"vor\" is the older spelling of \"für\"."]],"krv-a-0897":["Concluding remark on the resolution of the mathematical-transcendental, and preliminary remark on the resolution of the dynamical-transcendental ideas.",["\"der Mathematischtransscendentalen\" is substantivized; the noun (Ideen) is supplied in the German only after the second adjective pair, and the English follows that.","Auflösung = \"resolution\"."]],"krv-a-0898":["When we presented the antinomy of pure reason through all transcendental ideas in a table, since we indicated the ground of this conflict and the sole means for removing it, which consisted in this: that both opposed assertions were declared false: so we have everywhere represented the conditions as belonging to their conditioned according to relations of space and time, which is the usual presupposition of common human understanding, on which then also that conflict rested entirely. In this respect all dialectical representations of the totality, in the series of conditions for a given conditioned, were also through and through of the same kind. It was always a series, in which the condition was connected with the conditioned, as members of it, and thereby were homogeneous, whereby the *regressus* was never thought as completed, or, if this were to happen, a member conditioned in itself would have to be falsely assumed as a first, hence as unconditioned. Thus not everywhere the object, i.e., the conditioned, but yet the series of conditions for it, would be weighed merely according to its magnitude, and there the difficulty, which could be removed by no compromise, but only by a complete cutting off of the knot, consisted in this, that reason made it either too long or too short for the understanding, so that the latter could never equal its idea.",["Obiect = \"object\" (the only German word for object in this passage); Totalität = \"totality\"; Menschenverstand = \"human understanding\"; Behauptung = \"assertion\".","\"Es würde also zwar nicht allerwerts\" is subjunctive (\"would be weighed\"); the English keeps the conditional and the unexpressed contrast with \"zwar\".","\"seiner Grösse nach erwogen\": erwogen rendered \"weighed\"; \"ihrer Grösse\" and \"ihrer Idee\": the antecedent of the feminine possessive (Reihe or Vernunft) is left as ambiguous as in the German, \"its\" in both places.","\"Vergleich\" = \"compromise\"; \"Abschneidung des Knotens\" = \"cutting off of the knot\"."]],"krv-a-0899":["But we have here overlooked an essential difference that prevails among the objects, i.e., the concepts of the understanding, that reason strives to elevate to ideas, in that, namely, according to our table of the categories above, two of them signify a mathematical, but the two remaining a dynamical synthesis of appearances. Up to here this could also very well happen, in that, just as in the general representation of all transcendental ideas we always remained only under conditions in the appearance, so likewise in the two mathematically transcendental ones we had no other object than the one in the appearance. But now, since we are proceeding to dynamical concepts of the understanding, insofar as they are to fit the idea of reason, that distinction becomes important and opens up to us an entirely new prospect with regard to the dispute in which reason is entangled and which, since it was previously dismissed as built on false presuppositions on both sides, can now, since perhaps in the dynamical antinomy there takes place such a presupposition as can subsist together with the pretension of reason, be settled from this point of view and, since the judge supplies the lack of legal grounds that had been misjudged on both sides, to the satisfaction of both parties, which could not be done in the dispute in the mathematical antinomy.",["Obiecten / Gegenstand: 'Obiecten' (Objecten) and 'Gegenstand' both rendered 'object'; the Latinate member is 'Obiecten' here.","'Verstandesbegriffen' = concepts of the understanding; 'Prätension' kept as 'pretension'.","'Synthesis' kept as in the German; 'Categorien' = categories.","'bedeuten' = signify (glossary).","'Streithandel' = 'dispute'; 'Rechtsgründe' = 'legal grounds'; 'verkant' (verkannt) = 'misjudged' (literally: misrecognized).","The final long sentence's syntax (the 'welcher ... verglichen werden kan' construction) is kept; the 'da ... da' clauses are left in their German order and ambiguity."]],"krv-a-0900":["The series of conditions are, to be sure, all homogeneous insofar as one looks merely to their extension: whether they are adequate to the idea, or whether this is too large or too small for them. But the concept of the understanding that lies at the ground of these ideas contains either merely a synthesis of the homogeneous (which is presupposed in every magnitude, in its composition as well as its division), or also of the heterogeneous, which can at least be admitted in the dynamical synthesis, in the causal combination as well as in that of the necessary with the contingent.",["'seyn' in 'ob diese vor jene zu groß, oder zu klein seyn' is plural subjunctive in the German; 'diese' is left as 'this' in English following the singular 'der Idee'; the grammatical mismatch is preserved only partly.","Größe = magnitude; Zusammensetzung = composition; Theilung = division; Zufälligen = contingent; Nothwendigen = necessary.","'Caussalverbindung' = 'causal combination' (Verbindung = combination).","'gleichartig'/'ungleichartig' = homogeneous/heterogeneous."]],"krv-a-0901":["Hence it comes about that in the mathematical connection of the series of appearances no other than a sensible condition can enter, i.e., one that is itself a part of the series, whereas the dynamical series of sensible conditions nevertheless still admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a part of the series but, as merely intelligible, lies outside the series, whereby reason is then given satisfaction and the unconditioned is set before the appearances, without thereby confusing the series of the latter, as always conditioned, and breaking it off, contrary to the principles of the understanding.",["'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Verstandesgrundsätzen' = principles of the understanding.","'vorgesezt' rendered 'set before'; it could also be read as 'placed ahead of' or 'presupposed' (German 'vorsetzen').","'ein Gnüge gethan' = 'satisfaction given' (Genüge)."]],"krv-a-0902":["Now through the fact that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of the appearances outside their series, i.e., one that is itself not appearance, something happens that is entirely different from the outcome of the antinomy. This, namely, caused both dialectical counter-assertions to have to be declared false. On the other hand, that which is thoroughly conditioned in the dynamical series, which is inseparable from them as appearances, combined with the empirically unconditioned but also non-sensible condition, can give satisfaction to the understanding on one side and to reason on the other side,*) and, since the dialectical arguments that sought unconditioned totality in mere appearances in one way or another fall away, the propositions of reason, in the meaning corrected in this way, can both be true; which can never take place with the cosmological ideas that concern merely mathematically unconditioned unity, because in them no condition of the series of appearances is encountered that is not itself also appearance and as such constitutes a member of the series.",["The footnote marker *) stands where the German has it, after 'anderer Seits'.","'Gegenbehauptungen' = counter-assertions (behaupten = assert); 'Sätze' in 'Vernunftsätze' = propositions.","'Durchgängigbedingte' = 'thoroughly conditioned' (bedingt = conditioned).","'Bedeutung' = meaning (glossary); 'Erfolg' = outcome; 'Glied' = member.","The syntax of 'dem Verstande ... Gnüge leisten und ... können' is left as in the German, with 'können' governing the whole; the grammar is loose in the original."]],"krv-a-0903":["*) For the understanding permits among appearances no condition that would itself be empirically unconditioned. But if an intelligible condition, which accordingly would not belong along in the series of appearances as a member, could be thought for something conditioned (in the appearance), without thereby interrupting in the least the series of empirical conditions: then such a one could be admitted as empirically unconditioned, so that thereby no harm would happen anywhere to the empirical continuous regress.",["'Liesse sich ... gedenken' is a conditional without 'wenn'; kept as an inverted conditional.","'intelligibele' = intelligible; 'Regressus' kept as Latin-derived 'regress'."]],"krv-a-0904":["III. Resolution of the cosmological ideas, of the totality of the derivation of world-occurrences from their causes.",["'Auflösung' = resolution; 'Ableitung' = derivation; 'Weltbegebenheiten' = world-occurrences (Begebenheit = occurrence, Welt = world).","'Totalität' rendered 'totality' (Allheit also = totality in the glossary)."]],"krv-a-0905":["One can think only two kinds of causality with regard to that which happens, either according to nature or from freedom. The first is the connection of a state with a previous one in the world of sense, upon which the former follows according to a rule. Since now the causality of appearances rests on conditions of time, and the previous state, if it had always been, would also not have produced an effect that first arises in time: so the causality of the cause of that which happens, or arises, has also arisen, and in accordance with the principle of the understanding itself again requires a cause.",["'Sinnenwelt' = world of sense; 'Zustand' = state; 'Wirkung' = effect; 'Verstandesgrundsatze' = principle of the understanding.","'zweierley' rendered 'two kinds'; 'Art' is not used here.","The anacoluthic structure 'Da nun ... : so ist' is preserved, including the colon.","'geschieht' = happens; 'entsteht'/'entstanden' = arises/arisen; 'Caussalität' = causality."]],"krv-a-0906":["By freedom, on the contrary, in the cosmological sense, I understand the faculty of beginning a state of itself, whose causality therefore does not stand in turn under another cause that would determine it in time according to the law of nature. Freedom in this meaning is a pure transcendental idea, which, first, contains nothing borrowed from experience, second, whose object also cannot be given determinately in any experience, because it is a general law, even of the possibility of all experience, that everything that happens must have a cause, hence also the causality of the cause, which itself has happened or arisen, must in turn have a cause; whereby then the whole field of experience, however far it may extend, is transformed into a sum total of mere nature. But since in such a way no absolute totality of conditions in the causal relation is to be obtained, reason creates for itself the idea of a spontaneity that can begin of itself to act, without another cause having to be sent ahead to determine it in turn to action according to the law of causal connection.",["'im cosmologischen Verstande' rendered 'in the cosmological sense'; 'Verstande' here is not 'understanding' as faculty (cf. 'im practischen Verstande' in the next passage).","'deren Caussalität' has a feminine antecedent unclear in German ('Vermögen' is neuter, 'Zustand' masculine); the relative 'deren' is kept without resolution.","'sie' in 'sie wiederum ... zu bestimmen' could refer to the spontaneity or the causality; left ambiguous.","'bestimt' in 'bestimmen' = determine; 'bestimt gegeben' = 'given determinately'.","Inbegriff = sum total; Möglichkeit = possibility; Vermögen = faculty; 'Spontaneität' kept as cognate."]],"krv-a-0907":["It is exceedingly remarkable that the practical concept of freedom is grounded on this transcendental idea of freedom, and that the latter constitutes in the former the real moment of the difficulties that have always surrounded the question about its possibility. Freedom in the practical sense is the independence of the power of choice from necessitation by impulses of sensibility. For a power of choice is sensible insofar as it is pathologically affected (through motive causes of sensibility); it is called animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it can be pathologically necessitated. The human power of choice is to be sure an *arbitrium sensitivum*, but not *brutum*, rather *liberum*, because sensibility does not make its action necessary, but a faculty dwells in the human being of determining himself of himself independently of necessitation by sensible impulses.",["'Willkühr' rendered 'power of choice' (not in the glossary); it could also be rendered 'choice' or 'capacity for choice'; used consistently.","'iene'/'dieser' in 'iene in dieser': 'the latter ... in the former' follows the German 'jene ... in dieser' reversed; German reads 'jene [the transcendental idea] in dieser [the practical concept]'.","'Bewegursachen' = 'motive causes' (Bewegungsgrund = motive in glossary; the German word here differs).","'Antriebe' = impulses; 'Nöthigung' = necessitation; 'necessitirt' = necessitated; 'pathologisch' = pathologically.","'Moment' kept as 'moment'.","Latin terms are in italics as the method requires."]],"krv-a-0908":["One sees easily: that if all causality in the world of sense were mere nature, then every occurrence would be determined by another in time according to necessary laws, and hence, since the appearances, insofar as they determine the power of choice, would have to make every action necessary as their natural outcome: so the abolition of transcendental freedom would at the same time extirpate all practical freedom. For the latter presupposes that although something has not happened, it nevertheless should have happened, and its cause in the appearance was therefore not so determining that there does not lie in our power of choice a causality, independent of those natural causes and even against their power and influence, of producing something that is determined in the temporal order according to empirical laws, hence of beginning a series of occurrences entirely of itself.",["'Begebenheit' = occurrence (glossary); 'Erfolg' = outcome; 'Aufhebung' = abolition; 'vertilgen' = extirpate.","'diese' (the latter) is taken to refer to practical freedom; 'ienen Naturursachen' = 'those natural causes'.","The grammatical break after the colon ('so würde die Aufhebung') is retained: the preceding 'so würde iede Begebenheit...seyn und mithin, da...müßten:' is left as a single suspended construction.","'in der Zeitordnung' = 'in the temporal order' (literally 'time-order').","'Gewalt' = 'power'; this is the same English word used for 'Vermögen' in part (faculty/power) only informally; 'Kraft' is not used here."]],"krv-a-0909":["Thus what happens here is what is generally encountered in the conflict of a reason that ventures beyond the boundaries of possible experience, namely that the problem is properly not physiological but transcendental. Hence the question about the possibility of freedom does indeed impugn psychology, but, since it rests on dialectical arguments of merely pure reason, it, together with its resolution, must occupy transcendental philosophy alone. In order now to put the latter, which cannot decline to give a satisfying answer about this, in a position to do so, I must first seek to determine its procedure in this problem more closely through a remark.",["Aufgabe (OCR 'Aufgebe' silently corrected) = problem; Gränzen = boundaries; Möglichkeit = possibility.","'sie ... samt ihrer Auflösung': 'sie' could refer to the question (Frage) or, less naturally, to psychology; the English 'it' keeps the German ambiguity.","'diese' in 'Um nun diese' is left as 'the latter' (Transscendentalphilosophie); 'dazu in Stand zu setzen' is rendered literally.","Transscendental/transscendentalen rendered 'transcendental'; Vernunft = reason; Erfahrung = experience."]],"krv-a-0910":["If appearances were things in themselves, and consequently space and time forms of the existence of things in themselves, then the conditions together with the conditioned would always belong as members to one and the same series, and from this in the present case too would arise the antinomy that is common to all transcendental ideas: that this series would inevitably have to turn out too large, or too small, for the understanding. But the dynamical concepts of reason, with which we shall be occupied in this and the following number, have this peculiarity: that, since they have to do not with an object considered as magnitude, but only with its existence, one can also abstract from the magnitude of the series of conditions, and with them it is merely a matter of the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned, so that in the question about nature and freedom we already encounter the difficulty whether freedom is possible at all, and whether, if it is, it can subsist together with the universality of the natural law of causality, and consequently whether it is a correctly disjunctive proposition: that every effect in the world must arise either from nature or from freedom, or whether rather both can take place at the same time in different relations in one and the same occurrence. The correctness of that principle, of the thoroughgoing interconnection of all occurrences of the sensible world according to unchangeable laws of nature, is already established as a principle of the transcendental analytic and suffers no infringement. Thus the question is only: whether notwithstanding this, with regard to the very same effect that is determined according to nature, freedom can also take place, or whether this is completely excluded by that inviolable rule. And here the indeed common but deceptive presupposition of the absolute reality of appearances immediately shows its detrimental influence in confusing reason. For if appearances are things in themselves, then freedom cannot be saved. Then nature is the complete and in itself sufficiently determining cause of every occurrence, and the condition of the latter is always contained only in the series of appearances, which, together with their effect, are necessary under the law of nature. If, on the contrary, appearances count for nothing more than they in fact are, namely not for things in themselves but mere representations, which are connected according to empirical laws, then they must themselves still have grounds that are not appearances. But such an intelligible cause is not determined in regard to its causality by appearances, although its effects appear, and so can be determined by other appearances. It is thus, together with its causality, outside the series; its effects, on the other hand, are encountered in the series of empirical conditions. The effect can thus be regarded as free with regard to its intelligible cause and yet at the same time, with regard to the appearances, as a consequence from them according to the necessity of nature; a distinction which, when it is presented in general and quite abstractly, must seem extremely subtle and obscure, but which will become clear in the application. Here I have only wanted to make the remark: that, since the thoroughgoing interconnection of all appearances, in a context of nature, is a law that admits of no relaxation, this would necessarily overturn all freedom if one wanted to adhere obstinately to the reality of appearances. Hence those too who follow common opinion in this have never been able to succeed in uniting nature and freedom with one another.",["Ding an sich selbst = thing in itself; Erscheinung = appearance; Begebenheit = occurrence; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Realität = reality; Meinung = opinion; Vorstellung = representation.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'Sinnenwelt' rendered 'sensible world' (sinnlich = sensible).","'Grundsatz' = principle (also 'Satz' = proposition, in 'disjunctive proposition'); 'Grund' = ground.","'Wirkung' = effect; 'Erfolg' = consequence (Folgerung is a different word, not used here).","'Nummer' rendered literally 'number'. 'richtigdisiunctiver' (sic) = 'correctly disjunctive'.","'Bestimmt/bestimmend' = determined/determining; 'hinreichend' rendered 'sufficiently' (glossary zureichend = sufficient).","'dieses alle Freiheit ... umstürzen müßte': 'dieses' could refer to the law or to the interconnection; left as 'this'.","'Reihe' = series; 'Bedingung/Bedingtes' = condition/conditioned; 'dynamisch' = dynamical."]],"krv-a-0911":["Possibility of causality through freedom, in union with the universal law of natural necessity.",["Heading. Caussalität = causality; Naturnothwendigkeit = natural necessity; Vereinigung = union."]],"krv-a-0912":["I call intelligible that in an object of the senses which is itself not appearance. If accordingly that which in the sensible world must be regarded as appearance also has in itself a faculty which is no object of sensible intuition, but through which it can nevertheless be the cause of appearances: then one can consider the causality of this being from two sides, as intelligible according to its action, as that of a thing in itself, and as sensible according to the effects of the same, as that of an appearance in the sensible world. We would accordingly form of the faculty of such a subject an empirical, and likewise also an intellectual concept of its causality, which take place together in one and the same effect. Such a double side, of thinking the faculty of an object of the senses, contradicts none of the concepts that we have to form of appearances and of a possible experience. For since a transcendental object must lie at the basis of these, because they are in themselves no things, which determines them as mere representations, nothing hinders us from also ascribing to this transcendental object, besides the property through which it appears, a causality that is not appearance, although its effect is nevertheless encountered in the appearance. But every efficient cause must have a character, i.e., a law of its causality, without which it would not be a cause at all. And there we would have in a subject of the sensible world first an empirical character, through which its actions, as appearances, would stand through and through in interconnection with other appearances according to constant laws of nature and could be derived from them, as their conditions, and thus, in combination with these, would make up members of a single series of the order of nature. Second, one would still have to grant it an intelligible character, through which it is indeed the cause of those actions as appearances, but which itself stands under no conditions of sensibility and is itself not appearance. One could also call the former the character of such a thing in the appearance, the second the character of the thing in itself.",["'Eigenschaft' = property (glossary Beschaffenheit is also 'property'; here Eigenschaft is a different German word).","'Gegenstand' = object throughout; 'Wesen' = being; 'Subiect' = subject; 'Handlung' = action.","'Vermögen' = faculty; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'Zusammenhang' = interconnection.","'Eine solche doppelte Seite, das Vermögen eines Gegenstandes der Sinne sich zu denken': kept literal; the construction ('of thinking') is awkward in the German as well.","'die Ursache ... einen Character haben' : 'wirkende Ursache' = efficient cause; 'Character' = character.","'sensibel' (Latinate) and 'sinnlich' both = 'sensible'; 'Sinnenwelt' = sensible world.","'welche bey einer und derselben Wirkung': 'which' could attach to the two concepts or to the two causalities; left ambiguous as in German (welche, plural)."]],"krv-a-0913":["This acting subject would now, according to its intelligible character, stand under no conditions of time, for time is only the condition of appearances, but not of things in themselves. In it no action would arise or pass away, consequently it would also not be subject to the law of all time-determination, of everything alterable: that everything that happens encounters its cause in the appearances (of the previous state). In a word, the causality of the same, insofar as it is intellectual, would not stand at all in the series of empirical conditions that make the occurrence in the sensible world necessary. This intelligible character could indeed never be immediately cognized, because we can perceive nothing except insofar as it appears, but it would nevertheless have to be thought in accordance with the empirical character, just as we in general must lay a transcendental object in thought at the basis of appearances, although we know nothing of what it is in itself.",["'gekant' (sic) = 'gekannt', from kennen; rendered 'cognized' following the passage's use as in the standard Cambridge reading, though glossary gives kennen = know; be acquainted with. Doubtful reading/choice: alternatively 'known'.","'wissen' = know (Wissen = knowledge); 'wahrnehmen' = perceive (Wahrnehmung = perception).","'Zeitbestimmung' = time-determination; 'Zustand' = state; 'Begebenheit' = occurrence; 'Gesetz' = law.","'dem empirischen Character gemäß gedacht' : 'in accordance with' kept literal."]],"krv-a-0914":["According to its empirical character, this subject, as appearance, would thus be subject to the causal combination, in accordance with all laws of determination, and so far it would be nothing but a part of the world of sense, whose effects, like every other appearance, would flow inevitably from nature. As outer appearances flowed into it, as its empirical character, i.e., the law of its causality, would be cognized through experience, all its actions would have to let themselves be explained according to laws of nature, and all the requisites for a complete and necessary determination of them would have to be encountered in a possible experience.",["Caussalverbindung = 'causal combination' (Verbindung = combination); Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Character = 'character'; erkant = 'cognized'.","'allen Gesetzen der Bestimmung nach' stands between 'unterworfen' and its object; I kept the German order, so it may modify 'subject to' or the whole clause.","'So wie' (twice) is rendered 'As', which can be read as 'just as' or 'as soon as'; the German does not decide."]],"krv-a-0915":["According to the intelligible character of it, however (although we can have of it nothing but merely the general concept of it), the same subject would nevertheless have to be pronounced free of all influence of sensibility and of determination through appearances, and, since in it, insofar as it is *noumenon*, nothing happens, no alteration, which requires dynamical time-determination, hence no connection with appearances as causes is encountered, this active being would, insofar as in its actions, be independent and free of all natural necessity, which is encountered solely in the world of sense. One would say of it quite correctly: that it begins its effects in the world of sense of itself, without the action beginning in it itself, and this would be valid without the effects in the world of sense therefore needing to begin of themselves, because in it they are always determined beforehand through empirical conditions in the previous time, but yet only by means of the empirical character (which is merely the appearance of the intelligible), and are possible only as a continuation of the series of natural causes. Thus freedom and nature, each in its complete significance, in precisely the same actions, depending on whether one compares them with their intelligible or sensible cause, would be encountered at once and without any conflict.",["Veränderung = 'alteration'; Zeitbestimmung = 'time-determination'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Bedeutung = 'significance'; Widerstreit = 'conflict' (distinct from Widerspruch = 'contradiction').","'so fern in seinen Handlungen' is left as in the German; 'insofar as in its actions' may attach to 'independent and free' or to 'this active being'.","'in derselben' (feminine) is rendered 'in it'; the antecedent is the world of sense. 'in ihm selbst' is rendered 'in it itself' to keep it distinct from an sich.","*Noumenon* is printed in roman type in the original."]],"krv-a-0916":["Clarification of the cosmological idea of a freedom in combination with universal natural necessity.",["Erläuterung = 'clarification'; Verbindung = 'combination'."]],"krv-a-0917":["I have found it good first to sketch the silhouette of the solution of our transcendental problem, so that one may thereby better survey the course of reason in the solution of it. Now we will set out the moments of its decision, on which it really depends, and take each in particular into consideration.",["Problem = 'problem' (not Aufgabe in the German here); Auflösung = 'solution'; Schattenriß = 'silhouette'.","'in Erwägung ziehen' = 'take into consideration'; Erwägung is not Betrachtung, which the glossary renders 'consideration'.","'ihrer' / 'ihre' refer to reason (Vernunft) by grammatical gender; kept as 'its'."]],"krv-a-0918":["The law of nature: that everything that happens has a cause, that the causality of this cause, i.e., the action, since it precedes in time and in regard to an effect that has arisen cannot itself always have been, but must have happened, also has its cause among the appearances, by which it is determined, and that consequently all occurrences are empirically determined in an order of nature, this law, through which appearances first constitute a nature and can furnish objects of an experience, is a law of the understanding, from which it is under no pretext permitted to depart, or to except any appearance from it; because otherwise one would posit it outside all possible experience, thereby however distinguish it from all objects of possible experience and make it into a mere thought-entity and a figment of the brain.",["Begebenheiten = 'occurrences'; Gegenstände = 'objects'; Gedankendinge = 'thought-entity' (*ens rationis*); Hirngespinst = 'figment of the brain'.","'sie' (feminine) after 'Erscheinung' is rendered 'it'; the sentence is kept as one, with its long left-branching subject unresolved.","'Verstandesgesetz' = 'law of the understanding'."]],"krv-a-0919":["Although here it looks merely like a chain of causes, which in the *regressus* to their conditions permits no absolute totality, this scruple nevertheless does not detain us at all; for it has already been removed in the general judgment of the antinomy of reason, when it goes out in the series of appearances to the unconditioned. If we want to give in to the deception of transcendental realism: then neither nature nor freedom remains. Here the only question is: whether, if one recognizes in the whole series of all occurrences sheer natural necessity, it is nevertheless possible to regard precisely the same one, which on one side is mere natural effect, nevertheless on the other side as effect from freedom, or whether between these two kinds of causality a direct contradiction is encountered.",["Beurteilung = 'judgment' (glossary: Beurteilung = judgment, as is Urteil); Totalität = 'totality' (Allheit in the glossary); Bedingungen = 'conditions'; Widerspruch = 'contradiction'; Arten = 'kinds'.","'Täuschung' = 'deception' (not Schein, 'illusion'); Bedenklichkeit = 'scruple'.","'eben dieselbe' is feminine and its antecedent is not fixed; I kept 'the same one'. It could refer to the natural necessity, the series or the occurrence.","*Regressus* is printed in roman type in the original."]],"krv-a-0920":["Among the causes in appearance there can certainly be nothing that could begin a series absolutely and of itself. Every action, as appearance, insofar as it produces an occurrence, is itself occurrence, or event, which presupposes another state, in which the cause is encountered, and so everything that happens is only a continuation of the series and no beginning, which would occur of itself, is possible in it. Thus all actions of natural causes in the time-sequence are themselves in turn effects, which presuppose their causes just as much in the time-series. An original action, through which something happens that was not before, is not to be expected from the causal connection of appearances.",["Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Eräugniß (= Ereigniß) = 'event'; two German words, two English words, distinguished as in the glossary.","'darin die Ursache angetroffen werde' is subjunctive in the German; rendered in the indicative because English offers no close equivalent.","Zeitfolge = 'time-sequence'; Zeitreihe = 'time-series'; Zustand = 'state'; Caussalverknüpfung = 'causal connection'; schlechthin = 'absolutely'."]],"krv-a-0921":["But is it then also necessary: that, if the effects are appearances, the causality of their cause, which (namely the cause) is itself also appearance, must be merely empirical, and is it not rather possible: that, although for every effect in appearance a connection with its cause, according to laws of empirical causality, is indeed required, nevertheless this empirical causality itself, without in the least interrupting its interconnection with the natural causes, could be an effect of a non-empirical, but intelligible causality, i.e., of an action of a cause, original with regard to appearances, which is accordingly insofar not appearance, but intelligible according to this faculty, although otherwise it must be counted entirely, as a link of the chain of nature, along with the world of sense.",["Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; Ansehung = 'regard'.","'dieser Vermögen nach' (printed 'diesem Vermögen nach') is rendered 'according to this faculty'; the German word order is retained.","'einer Wirkung einer nichtempirischen ... Caussalität' keeps two genitives in sequence; the grammar can be read as 'an effect of a non-empirical causality'.","'Naturkette' = 'chain of nature'; 'mit zu der Sinnenwelt gezählt' = 'counted along with the world of sense'."]],"krv-a-0922":["We need the proposition of the causality of appearances among one another, in order to seek out and be able to indicate natural conditions, i.e., causes in the appearance, for natural occurrences. If this is granted and is not weakened by any exception, then the understanding, which in its empirical use sees nothing but nature in all events and is also entitled to do so, has everything it can demand, and the physical explanations go on their unhindered course. Now it does not do it the slightest harm, supposing that it might otherwise even be merely invented, if one assumes: that among the natural causes there are also some that have a faculty that is only intelligible, in that the determination of it to action never rests on empirical conditions, but on mere grounds of the understanding, yet in such a way that the action in the appearance from this cause is in accordance with all laws of empirical causality. For in this way the acting subject, as *causa phaenomenon*, would be chained together with nature in inseparable dependence of all its actions, and only the *phaenomenon* of this subject (with all the causality of it in the appearance) would contain certain conditions that, if one wants to ascend from the empirical object to the transcendental one, would have to be regarded as merely intelligible. For if only in that which among the appearances may be the cause we follow the rule of nature: then we can be unconcerned about what ground of these appearances and of their interconnection is thought in the transcendental subject, which is empirically unknown to us. This intelligible ground does not touch the empirical questions at all, but concerns perhaps merely thinking in the pure understanding, and, although the effects of this thinking and acting of the pure understanding are encountered in the appearances, these must nonetheless be able to be completely explained from their cause in the appearance in accordance with laws of nature, in that one follows the merely empirical character of them, as the supreme ground of explanation, and passes over the intelligible character, which is the transcendental cause of the former, entirely as unknown, except insofar as it is indicated only through the empirical, as the sensible sign of it. Let us apply this to experience. The human being is one of the appearances of the world of the senses, and insofar also one of the natural causes, whose causality must stand under empirical laws. As such he must accordingly also have an empirical character, like all other natural things. We note it through forces and faculties that he expresses in his effects. In lifeless nature, or nature animated merely animally, we find no ground to think any faculty for ourselves otherwise than as merely sensibly conditioned. But the human being, who otherwise knows the whole of nature solely through senses, cognizes himself also through mere apperception, and indeed in actions and inner determinations that he can in no way count as belonging to the impression of the senses, and is to himself of course in one part phenomenon, but in the other part, namely with regard to certain faculties, a merely intelligible object, because the action of it can in no way be counted as belonging to the receptivity of sensibility. We call these faculties understanding and reason; chiefly the latter is quite properly and preeminently distinguished from all empirically conditioned forces, since it weighs its objects merely according to ideas and determines the understanding accordingly, which then makes an empirical use of its (to be sure also pure) concepts.",["Eräugnissen (OCR/old spelling) read as Ereignissen: 'events' (Ereignis); Begebenheit = 'occurrence'. 'Handeins' silently corrected to 'Handelns'.","Caussalität = causality; Ursache = cause; Grund = ground; Gegenstand = 'object'. Subiect = subject; *causa phaenomenon* and *phaenomenon* left as printed (Latin), Phänomen = 'phenomenon'.","'es' in 'gesezt daß es übrigens auch blos erdichtet seyn solte' kept as 'it', its referent (the assumption that follows) left as ambiguous as in the German; 'so doch, daß' = 'yet in such a way that'.","'kent' (of nature) = 'knows' (kennen); 'erkent sich selbst' = 'cognizes' (erkennen). 'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of the senses'. 'desselben' in 'die Handlung desselben' left as 'the action of it'.","'Vermögen' = 'faculty' throughout; 'Kräfte' = 'forces'; 'Erklärung' = 'explanation'; 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'."]],"krv-a-0923":["That this reason now has causality, or at least that we represent to ourselves something of the kind in it, is clear from the imperatives that in everything practical we give as rules to the exercising forces. The ought expresses a kind of necessity and connection with grounds that otherwise does not occur in the whole of nature. The understanding can cognize of this only what is, or has been, or will be. It is impossible that anything in it ought to be otherwise than it in fact is in all these time-relations, indeed the ought, if one has merely the course of nature before one's eyes, has absolutely no meaning. We cannot ask at all: what ought to happen in nature, any more than: what properties a circle ought to have, but rather what happens in it, or what properties the latter has.",["Das Sollen = 'the ought' (substantivized infinitive). Bedeutung = 'meaning'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Nothwendigkeit = 'necessity'.","Eigenschaften = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit in the glossary is also 'property'; not used here). 'Art' = 'kind'.","'darin' ('in it') and 'von dieser' ('of this') have referents left as in the German."]],"krv-a-0924":["This ought now expresses a possible action, of which the ground is nothing other than a mere concept; whereas of a mere natural action the ground must always be an appearance. Now the action must certainly be possible under natural conditions if the ought is directed to it; but these natural conditions do not concern the determination of the power of choice itself, but only the effect and result of it in the appearance. There may be ever so many natural grounds that impel me to willing, ever so many sensible stimuli, they cannot produce the ought; but only a willing that is still far from necessary, but always conditioned, to which the ought that reason pronounces opposes, in contrast, measure and goal, indeed prohibition and authority. Whether it be an object of mere sensibility (the agreeable) or also of pure reason (the good): reason does not give in to that ground which is given empirically, and does not follow the order of things as they present themselves in the appearance, but with complete spontaneity makes for itself an order of its own according to ideas, into which it fits the empirical conditions, and according to which it even declares actions necessary that nevertheless have not happened and perhaps will not happen, yet of all of them presupposes that reason could have causality in relation to them; for, without that, it would not expect effects in experience from its ideas.",["Willkühr = 'power of choice' (not in the glossary; used consistently). Wollen = 'willing'.","'Erfolg' = 'result'; 'Wirkung' = 'effect'; 'Anreitze' = 'stimuli'; 'Ansehen' = 'authority'.","'dem dagegen ... entgegen sezt': the word order is kept; 'dagegen' and 'entgegen' both carry 'in contrast/opposes'. The antecedent of 'dem' (the conditioned willing) is kept.","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Ordnung der Dinge' = 'order of things'."]],"krv-a-0925":["Now let us stop here and assume at least as possible: that reason really has causality with regard to appearances: then it must, as much as it is reason, nevertheless show an empirical character of itself, because every cause presupposes a rule according to which certain appearances follow as effects, and every rule requires a uniformity of the effects, which grounds the concept of the cause (as a faculty), which we, insofar as it must be evident from mere appearances, can call its empirical character, which is constant, while the effects, according to the diversity of the accompanying and partly limiting conditions, appear in variable shapes.",["'Möglich' = 'possible'; 'wirklich' = 'really'. 'Vermögen' = 'faculty'; 'Begriff' = 'concept'.","The relative clauses 'welchen ... seinen empirischen Character heissen können, der beständig ist' keep the German's ambiguity: 'which' (welchen) and the final 'which is constant' (der) may refer to the concept of the cause or to the character.","'Ursach' = 'cause'; 'Gleichförmigkeit' = 'uniformity'."]],"krv-a-0926":["Thus every human being has an empirical character of his power of choice, which is nothing other than a certain causality of his reason, insofar as this shows in its effects in the appearance a rule, according to which one can gather the grounds of reason and the actions of them according to their kind and their degrees, and can judge the subjective principles of his power of choice. Because this empirical character itself must be drawn from the appearances as effect and from the rule of them, which experience provides: so all actions of the human being in the appearance are determined from his empirical character and the other cooperating causes in accordance with the order of nature, and, if we could research all appearances of his power of choice down to the ground, there would be not a single human action that we could not predict with certainty and cognize as necessary from its preceding conditions. With regard to this empirical character there is therefore no freedom, and according to this alone can we nevertheless consider the human being, if we merely observe and, as happens in anthropology, want to investigate physiologically the moving causes of his actions.",["'Vernunftgründe und die Handlungen derselben': 'derselben' may refer to the grounds of reason or to reason; kept as 'the actions of them'.","'beurtheilen' = 'judge' (glossary: assess; judge); 'Betrachten' = 'consider'; 'beobachten' = 'observe'; 'Art' = 'kind'; 'Grad' = 'degree'.","'Gewisheit' = 'certainty'; 'bewegende Ursache' = 'moving cause'; 'Bestimmt' (bestimt) = 'determined'."]],"krv-a-0927":["If, however, we consider these very same actions in relation to reason, and indeed not the speculative, in order to explain them as to their origin, but solely and alone insofar as reason is the cause to produce them itself, in a word, if we compare them with this with a practical aim: then we find a quite different rule and order than the natural order is. For there perhaps all that ought not to have happened which nevertheless has happened according to the course of nature and, according to its empirical grounds, had to happen unfailingly. Sometimes, however, we find, or at least believe to find: that the ideas of reason have actually proved causality with regard to the actions of the human being, as appearances, and that they have happened for that reason, not because they were determined by empirical causes, no, but because they were determined by grounds of reason.",["Ursache = cause; Grund = ground; Absicht = aim (\"in practischer Absicht\"); Erscheinung = appearance; Caussalität = causality (old spelling).","\"iene\" (jene) in \"iene ihrem Ursprunge nach\" is left to refer as ambiguously as in German (the actions; possibly the first-mentioned items).","\"solte\" rendered \"ought to\"; \"wirklich\" = actually; \"Ansehung\" = regard."]],"krv-a-0928":["Supposing now that one could say: reason has causality with regard to the appearance; could the action of it then well be called free, since in its empirical character (the way of sense) it is determined quite exactly and necessary. This is in turn determined in the intelligible character (the way of thinking). The latter, however, we do not know, but designate it through appearances, which properly give only the way of sense (empirical character) to be cognized immediately*). The action now, insofar as it is to be attributed to the way of thinking as its cause, nevertheless follows from it not at all according to empirical laws, i.e. so that the conditions of pure reason, but only so that their effects in the appearance of inner sense precede. Pure reason, as a merely intelligible faculty, is not subjected to the form of time, and consequently also not to the conditions of the time-sequence. The causality of reason in the intelligible character does not arise, or does not, say, begin at a certain time in order to bring forth an effect. For otherwise it would itself be subjected to the natural law of appearances, insofar as it determines causal series according to time, and the causality would then be nature, and not freedom. Thus we will be able to say: if reason can have causality with regard to appearances: then it is a faculty through which the sensible condition of an empirical series of effects first begins. For the condition that lies in reason is not sensible and thus does not itself begin. Accordingly there then takes place that which we missed in all empirical series: that the condition of a successive series of occurrences could itself be empirically unconditioned. For here the condition is outside the series of appearances (in the intelligible) and consequently not subjected to any sensible condition and to any determination of time through a preceding cause.",["Sinnesart = \"way of sense\"; Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\" (Art = way); kennen = know, erkennen = cognize (\"zu erkennen geben\" = \"give to be cognized\").","The footnote marker *) stands where the German has it; the footnote is passage krv-a-0929.","\"so, daß die Bedingungen der reinen Vernunft, sondern nur so, daß...\" is left as printed (a clause appears to be missing before \"sondern\"); nothing is supplied. \"deren\" could refer to the conditions or to reason; \"their\" keeps this open.","\"nothwendig\" = necessary (adjective form left as in German, \"ist ... bestimt und nothwendig\"); Zeitfolge = \"time-sequence\"; Begebenheit = occurrence; empirischunbedingt = \"empirically unconditioned\"; \"anfängt\" and \"hebt an\" both = begin.","\"statt findet\" = takes place; \"unterworfen\" = subjected; \"vermißten\" = missed."]],"krv-a-0929":["*) The actual morality of actions (merit and guilt) therefore remains entirely hidden from us, even that of our own conduct. Our imputations can be referred only to the empirical character. But how much of it is the pure effect of freedom, how much is to be ascribed to mere nature and to the faultless defect of temperament, or to its fortunate constitution (*merito fortunae*), no one can fathom, and therefore also cannot judge according to complete justice.",["Zurechnung = imputation; zuschreiben = ascribe; Schuld = guilt; Beschaffenheit = constitution.","\"unverschuldeten Fehler\" = \"faultless defect\" (literally, defect not incurred by guilt).","\"nicht nach völliger Gerechtigkeit richten\": richten = judge (here in the sense of passing sentence); \"daher auch nicht\" has its subject (\"no one\") carried over as in German."]],"krv-a-0930":["Nevertheless, this very same cause belongs in another relation also to the series of appearances. The human being is himself appearance. His power of choice has an empirical character, which is the (empirical) cause of all his actions. There is none of the conditions that determine the human being in accordance with this character which would not be contained in the series of natural effects and would not obey the law of the same, according to which no empirically unconditioned causality is encountered in that which happens in time. Hence no given action (because it can be perceived only as appearance) can begin absolutely of itself. But of reason one cannot say: that before that state in which it determines the power of choice, another precedes in which this state itself is determined. For since reason is itself no appearance and is not subjected to any conditions of sensibility, there takes place in it, even with regard to its causality, no time-sequence, and thus the dynamical law of nature, which determines the time-sequence according to rules, cannot be applied to it.",["Willkühr = \"power of choice\" (not in the glossary; used consistently); wahrnehmen = perceive; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","\"von selbst\" = \"of itself\"; \"schlechthin\" = absolutely; \"dasienige\" (dasjenige) = \"that\".","\"dem Gesetze derselben\": \"derselben\" (the series, or the effects) left as ambiguous as in German (\"the same\")."]],"krv-a-0931":["Reason is thus the persistent condition of all actions of the power of choice under which the human being appears. Each of them is determined beforehand in the empirical character of the human being, before even than it happens. With regard to the intelligible character, of which that is only the sensible schema, no before or after holds, and each action, regardless of the relation in time in which it stands with other appearances, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which consequently acts freely, without being dynamically determined in the chain of natural causes by outer or inner grounds that precede in time, and this its freedom one can regard not only negatively, as independence from empirical conditions, (for thereby the faculty of reason would cease to be a cause of appearances), but also designate positively, through a faculty of beginning a series of occurrences of itself, so that in it itself nothing begins, but it, as unconditioned condition of each action of the power of choice, permits above itself no conditions preceding in time, while nevertheless its effect begins in the series of appearances, but can never constitute in it an absolutely first beginning.",["beharrlich = persistent (Beharrlichkeit = persistence); Willkühr = \"power of choice\"; Begebenheit = occurrence; Vernunftvermögen = \"faculty of reason\".","\"ehe noch als sie geschieht\" is rendered word for word (\"before even than it happens\"); the German construction is odd.","\"unangesehen\" = \"regardless of\"; \"von selbst\" = \"of itself\"; \"jene\" (iener) in \"wovon iener\" = \"that\" (the empirical character); \"der Zeit nach\" = \"in time\" (literally \"according to time\").","\"ihrer Freiheit ... ansehen ... bezeichnen\": the syntax (\"kan man nicht allein ... ansehen ... sondern auch ... bezeichnen\") is kept; \"über sich\" = \"above itself\"."]],"krv-a-0932":["In order to clarify the regulative principle of reason through an example from the empirical use of it, not in order to confirm it, (for such proofs are unfit for transcendental assertions), let one take an action of the power of choice, e.g., a malicious lie, through which a human being has brought a certain confusion into society, and which one first investigates according to the motive causes from which it arose and then judges how it, together with its consequences, can be imputed to him. In the first aim one goes through his empirical character as far as the sources of it, which one seeks in bad upbringing, evil company, in part also in the malignity of a natural disposition insensitive to shame, in part shifts onto frivolity and thoughtlessness; whereby one does not leave out of account the occasioning occasional causes. In all of this one proceeds as one does in general in the investigation of the series of determining causes for a given natural effect. Although one now believes the action to be determined thereby: one blames the doer nonetheless, and indeed not on account of his unfortunate natural disposition, not on account of the circumstances flowing in upon him, indeed not even on account of his previously conducted course of life, for one presupposes that one can set entirely to one side how this was constituted, and regard the elapsed series of conditions as not having occurred, but this deed as entirely unconditioned with regard to the previous state, as if the doer with it began a series of consequences quite of himself. This blame is grounded on a law of reason, whereby one regards this as a cause which could and ought to have determined the conduct of the human being differently, regardless of all the named empirical conditions. And indeed one regards the causality of reason not, say, merely as concurrence, but as complete in itself, even if the sensible incentives were not at all for it, but rather even against it; the action is attributed to his intelligible character, he is now, in the moment when he lies, entirely guilty; consequently reason was, notwithstanding all empirical conditions of the deed, completely free, and the omission of it is to be attributed to it entirely.",["Willkühr = \"power of choice\"; Bewegursachen = \"motive causes\" (cf. Bewegungsgrund = motive); Gelegenheitsursachen = \"occasional causes\"; Absicht = aim (\"In der ersten Absicht\").","Thäter = \"doer\"; Naturel = \"natural disposition\"; zurechnen = impute; beimessen = attribute; Triebfedern = incentives; Schuld = guilt.","\"Behauptungen\" = assertions; \"erläutern\" = clarify; \"beurtheilt\" = judges (Beurtheilung = judgment/assessment).","\"ihrer Unterlassung ist diese gänzlich beizumessen\": \"ihrer\" and \"diese\" are left as in German (the omission \"of it\" = of reason; \"to it\" = to reason, or to the omission's author, as the German allows); \"seinem\" refers to the doer.","\"davor ... dawider\" = \"for it ... against it\", kept without resolving the referent (the action)."]],"krv-a-0933":["One easily sees from this imputing judgment: that in it one has in thought that reason is not affected at all by all that sensibility, that it does not alter (even if its appearances, namely the way in which it shows itself in its effects, alter), that no state precedes in it that determines the following one, hence that it does not belong at all in the series of sensible conditions that make the appearances necessary in accordance with laws of nature. It, reason, is present in all actions of the human being in all circumstances of time and is one and the same, but is itself not in time and does not, say, get into a new state in which it previously was not; it is determining, but not determinable with regard to it. Hence one cannot ask: why has reason not determined itself differently, but only: why has it not determined the appearances differently through its causality. But to this no answer is possible. For a different intelligible character would have given a different empirical one, and when we say that notwithstanding his entire course of life conducted up to then, the perpetrator could nevertheless have omitted the lie, this signifies only that it stands immediately under the power of reason, and reason in its causality is not subject to any conditions of appearance and of the course of time, the difference of time also, though it makes a principal difference of the appearances respectively to one another, since these are not things, hence also not causes in themselves, can make no difference of the action in relation to reason.",["zurechnenden Urtheile = 'imputing judgment'; Urtheil = judgment; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; afficirt = 'affected'; Zustand = state; Wirkungen = effects; Handlungen = actions.","'in Ansehung desselben': 'it' is grammatically ambiguous (a new state, or the state/time just mentioned); left ambiguous.","'daß sie unmittelbar unter der Macht der Vernunft stehe': feminine 'sie' has no clear antecedent (perhaps Handlung, or the Vernunft/Thäter's conduct); left as 'it'.","Sachen and Dinge both 'things' (here Sachen, 'Dinge' elsewhere in this set); bedeutet = 'signifies'; Thäter = 'perpetrator'; Lebenswandel = 'course of life'."]],"krv-a-0934":["Thus with the assessment of free actions, with regard to their causality, we can come only up to the intelligible cause, but not beyond it; we can cognize that it is free, i.e., determined independently of sensibility and, in such a way, can be the sensibly unconditioned condition of the appearances. But why the intelligible character gives precisely these appearances and this empirical character under the circumstances at hand, that so far exceeds all faculty of our reason to answer it, indeed all its warrant even merely to ask, as if one asked: whence the transcendental object of our outer sensible intuition gives precisely only intuition in space and not some other. Yet the problem that we had to resolve does not obligate us to this at all, for it was only this: whether freedom conflicts with natural necessity in one and the same action, and this we have sufficiently answered, since we showed: that, since with the former a relation to a quite different kind of conditions is possible than with the latter, the law of the latter does not affect the former, hence both can take place independently of one another and undisturbed by one another.",["Beurtheilung = 'assessment' (not 'judgment', which renders Urtheil); Gegenstand = 'object'; Aufgabe = 'problem'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; Befugniß = 'warrant'.","'sie frey ... seyn könne': 'sie' = the Ursache (cause), 'it'.","'gerade nur Anschauung im Raume': 'precisely only' kept; 'giebt' = 'gives', subject (the transcendental object) and construction as in German.","'ienem/diesem' (the former/the latter) refer to freedom and natural necessity; 'Art' = 'kind'."]],"krv-a-0935":["One must well note: that we have not wanted to demonstrate hereby the actuality of freedom as one of the faculties that contain the cause of the appearances of our world of sense. For besides that this would not have been a transcendental consideration at all, which has to do merely with concepts: it also could not succeed, since we can never infer from experience to something that must not be thought at all in accordance with laws of experience. Further, we also have not even wanted to prove the possibility of freedom; for this too would not have succeeded, because we can cognize the possibility of no real ground and no causality from mere concepts a priori. Freedom is treated here only as a transcendental idea, through which reason thinks to begin the series of conditions in the appearance absolutely through the sensibly unconditioned, but thereby entangles itself in an antinomy with its own laws, which it prescribes to the empirical use of the understanding. That this antinomy now rests on a mere illusion and that nature at least does not conflict with causality from freedom, that was the only thing we could accomplish and the only thing, and solely, that we cared about.",["Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility'; Betrachtung = 'consideration'; Schein = 'illusion'; Gebrauch = 'use'; Verstand = 'understanding'.","'einer der Vermögen' kept as 'one of the faculties' (German has singular 'einer' with plural genitive).","Realgrunde = 'real ground'; 'erkennen' = 'cognize'; 'schliessen' = 'infer'; 'darthun' = 'demonstrate'.","'Natur der Caussalität aus Freiheit': German has no article before Natur; 'nature' kept. 'woran es uns ... gelegen war' = 'that we cared about'."]],"krv-a-0936":["IV. Resolution of the cosmological idea of the totality of the dependence of appearances, as to their existence in general.",["Totalität = 'totality' (the glossary's Allheit is also 'totality'); Daseyn = 'existence' (Existenz also 'existence' in the following passages); Auflösung = 'resolution'."]],"krv-a-0937":["In the previous number we considered the alterations of the world of sense in their dynamical series, in which each stands under another as its cause. Now this series of states serves us only as a guide, in order to arrive at an existence that could be the highest condition of everything alterable, namely at the necessary being. It is not a matter here of unconditioned causality, but of the unconditioned existence of substance itself. Thus the series that we have before us is properly only that of concepts and not of intuitions, insofar as the one is the condition of the other.",["Daseyn and Existenz both 'existence'; Wesen = 'being'; Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Zustände = 'states'; Anschauungen = 'intuitions'.","'Es ist hier nicht um ... zu thun' = 'It is not a matter here of ...' (impersonal idiom).","'die eine die Bedingung der andern': 'the one ... the other' left unspecified as in German."]],"krv-a-0938":["But one easily sees: that, since everything in the sum total of appearances is alterable, hence conditioned in existence, there can be, anywhere in the series of dependent existence, no unconditioned member whose existence would be absolutely necessary, and that thus, if appearances were things in themselves, but just for that reason their condition always belonged together with the conditioned to one and the same series of intuitions, a necessary being, as condition of the existence of the appearances of the world of sense, could never take place.",["Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Glied = 'member'; Daseyn/Existenz both 'existence'; Dinge an sich selbst = 'things in themselves'.","'eben darum' = 'just for that reason'; 'statt finden' = 'take place'.","The 'if' clause ('wenn ... wären, ... aber ... gehörete') is kept as one construction, with the main clause 'könte' at the end, as in German."]],"krv-a-0939":["But the dynamical regress has this peculiarity and distinction from the mathematical in itself: that, since the latter has to do properly only with the composition of parts into a whole, or the decomposition of a whole into its parts, the conditions of this series must always be regarded as parts of it, hence as homogeneous, consequently as appearances, whereas in that regress, since it is not a matter of the possibility of an unconditioned whole from given parts, or of an unconditioned part for a given whole, but of the derivation of a state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance itself from the necessary one, the condition need not necessarily make up an empirical series with the conditioned.",["'an sich' in 'von dem mathematischen an sich' may attach to 'Eigenthümliche und Unterscheidende' or to 'mathematischen'; left as ambiguous as the German.","'dürfe' with 'nicht eben nothwendig': rendered 'need not necessarily'; 'eben' could also be read as 'just/precisely'.","'dieser' = the mathematical regress; 'ienem' = the dynamical regress ('that regress'). Teile = 'parts'; Zusammensetzung = 'composition'; Zerfällung = 'decomposition'; zufällig = 'contingent'; gleichartig = 'homogeneous'."]],"krv-a-0940":["Thus there remains open to us, with the apparent antinomy lying before us, still a way out: namely, since both of the propositions conflicting with one another can be true at once in different relations, so that all things of the world of sense are throughout contingent, hence also always have only empirically conditioned existence, yet of the whole series there also takes place a non-empirical condition, i.e., an unconditionally necessary being. For this would, as intelligible condition, not belong at all to the series as a member of it (not even as the uppermost member), and also would make no member of the series empirically unconditioned, but would leave the whole world of sense in its empirically conditioned existence going through all members. In this, then, this way of laying an unconditioned existence at the ground of the appearances would differ from the empirically unconditioned causality (of freedom) in the previous article: that with freedom the thing itself, as cause (*Substantia phaenomenon*), nevertheless belonged in the series of conditions and only its causality was thought as intelligible, but here the necessary being would have to be thought entirely outside the series of the world of sense (as *ens extramundanum*) and merely intelligible, whereby alone it can be prevented: that it itself not be subjected to the law of the contingency and dependence of all appearances.",["The sentence beginning 'da nemlich alle beide ...' is kept as one; 'so, daß' kept as in German, and the 'daß'-clause with 'gleichwol ... statt finde' (subjunctive) is rendered literally; the syntax is loose.","'gehörete' and 'gedacht wurde' (probably for 'würde') are in the original's indicative/subjunctive mix; rendered 'belonged' and 'was thought', the latter a doubtful reading/form.","'Art' = 'way'; Ausweg = 'way out'; Zufälligkeit = 'contingency'; Grunde = 'ground'; Gesetz = 'law'; 'Substantia phænomenon' and 'ens extramundanum' are Latin in the source, kept in italics.","'dem Gesetze ... unterworfen werde': subjunctive after 'verhütet', rendered 'not be subjected'."]],"krv-a-0941":["The regulative principle of reason is therefore, with regard to this problem of ours: that everything in the world of sense has empirically conditioned existence, and that nowhere in it is there, with regard to any property, an unconditioned necessity: that there is no member of the series of conditions of which one would not always have to expect the empirical condition in a possible experience and, as far as one can, to seek it, and nothing entitles us to derive any existence from a condition outside the empirical series, or also to hold it, within the series itself, as absolutely independent and self-subsisting, yet by this not at all to dispute that the whole series may not be grounded in some intelligible being (which for that reason is free from every empirical condition and rather contains the ground of the possibility of all these appearances).",["Aufgabe = problem; Sinnenwelt rendered 'world of sense'; Erscheinungen = appearances; Grund = ground; Wesen = being.","'empirischbedingte' (A) rendered 'empirically conditioned'; 'kan' = 'kann' (old spelling).","The syntax is kept as it stands: 'gleichwol aber dadurch gar nicht in Abrede zu ziehen, daß nicht die ganze Reihe … gegründet seyn könne' retains the German double negation ('not … dispute that … may not be'); the subject of 'zu halten' and 'in Abrede zu ziehen' is left unexpressed as in the German.","'vor schlechterdings unabhängig' = 'für' in old spelling ('vor'); 'selbstständig' = 'self-subsisting'.","'seyn' in 'die immer … seyn' style constructions is preserved as the German subjunctive/infinitive without correction; the first colon-articulated clauses ('daß … daß … daß') are kept parallel."]],"krv-a-0942":["But here it is not at all the opinion to prove the unconditionally necessary existence of a being, or even to ground on this the possibility of a merely intelligible condition of the existence of the appearances of the world of sense, but only, just as we limit reason so that it does not leave the thread of the empirical conditions and lose itself in transcendent grounds of explanation, incapable of any presentation *in concreto*, so also, on the other side, to limit the law of the merely empirical use of the understanding to this: that it not decide about the possibility of things in general and not, because the intelligible is indeed not to be used by us for the explanation of the appearances, for that reason declare it impossible. Thus it is shown by this only: that the thoroughgoing contingency of all natural things and of all their (empirical) conditions may very well subsist together with the arbitrary presupposition of a necessary, though merely intelligible condition, hence that no true contradiction is to be encountered between these assertions, consequently that they can both be true. Such an absolutely necessary being of the understanding may always be impossible in itself, yet this can by no means be inferred from the universal contingency and dependence of everything that belongs to the world of sense, likewise from the principle: not to stop at any single member of it, so far as it is contingent, and to appeal to a cause outside the world. Reason goes its way in the empirical and its special way in the transcendental use.",["Meinung = opinion; Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove; Widerspruch = contradiction; Behauptungen = assertions; Zufälligkeit = contingency; Verstandesgebrauch = use of the understanding.","Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation; 'Erklärung' in 'erkläre' rendered 'declare'; 'Darstellung in concreto' kept with the Latin in italics.","'Es ist aber hiebey gar nicht die Meinung … sondern nur eben so, wie wir … also auch …' is an anacoluthic construction, kept as it stands; the infinitive 'einzuschränken' completes the 'so … also auch' correlation.","'schlechthinnothwendiges Verstandeswesen' = 'absolutely necessary being of the understanding'; 'an sich' = 'in itself'; 'schlechterdings'/'schlechthin' both rendered 'absolutely'.","'bey keinem einzigen Gliede derselben … aufzuhören' is ambiguous as to whether 'derselben' refers to the world or the series; left as 'of it'."]],"krv-a-0943":["The world of sense contains nothing but appearances, but these are mere representations, which are in turn always sensibly conditioned, and since here we never have things in themselves as our objects, it is not to be wondered at: that we are never entitled to make a leap, from a member of the empirical series, whichever it may be, outside the interconnection of sensibility, just as if they were things in themselves that existed outside their transcendental ground and which one could abandon in order to seek the cause of their existence outside them; which in the case of contingent things would indeed finally have to happen, but not in the case of mere representations of things, whose contingency is itself only a phenomenon and can lead to no other regress than the one that determines the phenomena, i.e., that is empirical. But to think an intelligible ground of the appearances, i.e., of the world of sense, and one freed from the contingency of the latter, is contrary neither to the unlimited empirical regress in the series of appearances, nor to the thoroughgoing contingency of them. But that is also the only thing that we had to accomplish toward the removal of the apparent antinomy, and what could be done only in this way. For if the condition in each case for every conditioned (as to existence) is sensible and just for that reason belongs to the series, then it is itself conditioned in turn (as the antithesis of the fourth antinomy shows). Thus either a conflict with reason, which demands the unconditioned, had to remain, or this had to be posited outside the series in the intelligible, whose necessity requires no empirical condition, nor permits one, and is therefore, with respect to appearances, unconditionally necessary.",["Gegenstand = object; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Regressus kept as 'regress'; Phänomen/Phänomena = phenomenon/phenomena; Daseyn = existence.","'Ding an sich selbst' = 'thing in itself'; 'Widerstreit' = 'conflict'; 'Antithesis' kept.","'seyn' in 'daß wir niemals berechtigt seyn' is the old subjunctive form, rendered 'are'; the sentence is kept as one with the semicolon.","'dieses' in 'oder dieses ausser der Reihe … gesezt werden' is left as 'this' (the unconditioned); 'dessen' in 'dessen Nothwendigkeit' is ambiguous between 'the intelligible' and 'this'; kept as 'whose'.","'respective auf' rendered 'with respect to'; 'bestimt' = 'bestimmt' (determines)."]],"krv-a-0944":["The empirical use of reason (with regard to the conditions of existence in the world of sense) is not affected by the admission of a merely intelligible being, but proceeds, according to the principle of thoroughgoing contingency, from empirical conditions to higher ones, which are always equally empirical. Just as little, however, does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause that is not in the series, when it is a matter of the pure use of reason (with regard to ends). For there that signifies only the ground, for us merely transcendental and unknown, of the possibility of the sensible series in general, whose existence, independent of all conditions of the latter and, with regard to these, unconditionally necessary, is not at all opposed to the unbounded contingency of the former, and for that reason also not to the regress, ended nowhere, in the series of empirical conditions.",["Gebrauch = use; Zwecke = ends; Grundsatz = principle (also Princip = principle); both rendered 'principle'.","bedeutet = signifies; unbekannt = unknown; unbedingtnothwendig = unconditionally necessary.","'iene' ('jene') is left as 'that', its referent (the intelligible cause) not supplied.","'afficirt' = 'affected'; 'die immer eben sowol empirisch seyn' kept as a relative clause with 'seyn' = 'are'.","'unbegränzten' = 'unbounded' (Grenze = bound); 'Daseyn … ist … nicht entgegen' is a long construction kept in German order; 'dessen' refers to 'Grund'."]],"krv-a-0945":["Concluding remark on the whole antinomy of pure reason.",[]],"krv-a-0946":["So long as with our concepts of reason we have as our object merely the totality of conditions in the world of sense and what can be done, with regard to them, in the service of reason: so long our ideas are indeed transcendental, but nevertheless cosmological. But as soon as we posit the unconditioned (which is what is really at issue) in that which is entirely outside the world of sense, hence outside all possible experience, the ideas become transcendent; they serve not merely for the completion of the empirical use of reason (which always remains an idea never to be carried out, but nevertheless to be followed), but separate themselves from it entirely and make themselves into objects, whose material is not taken from experience, whose objective reality also does not rest on the completion of the empirical series, but on pure concepts *a priori*. Such transcendent ideas have a merely intelligible object, which to admit as a transcendental object, of which one otherwise knows nothing, is indeed permitted, but for which, in order to think it as a thing determinable through its distinguishing and inner predicates, we have on our side neither grounds of possibility (as independent of all concepts of experience), nor the least justification for assuming such an object, and which is therefore a mere thought-entity. Nevertheless, among all cosmological ideas, the one that occasioned the fourth antinomy urges us to venture this step. For the existence of appearances, not grounded at all in itself, but always conditioned, calls upon us to look around for something different from all appearances, hence for an intelligible object, in which this contingency would cease. But because, once we have taken the permission to assume, outside the field of the whole of sensibility, a reality subsisting for itself, and to regard appearances only as contingent ways of representing intelligible objects, by such beings as are themselves intelligences: nothing remains to us except analogy, according to which we use the concepts of experience in order to make for ourselves some concept or other of intelligible things, of which in themselves we have not the least acquaintance. Because we get to know the contingent in no other way than through experience, but here the discussion is of things that are not to be objects of experience at all, we will have to derive their acquaintance from that which is necessary in itself, from pure concepts of things in general. Hence the first step that we take outside the world of sense obliges us to begin our new acquaintances from the investigation of the absolutely necessary being, and from the concepts of it to derive the concepts of all things, so far as they are merely intelligible, and this attempt we want to undertake in the following chapter.",["Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason; Totalität = totality; Gegenstand and Obiect/Object both = 'object' (Gegenstand in 'zum Gegenstande', 'Gegenstände', 'intelligibelen Gegenstand'; Obiect in 'transscendentales Obiect', 'obiective').","Kentniß/Kentnisse = acquaintance (Kenntnis); 'kennen lernen' = 'get to know'; Wirklichkeit = reality (as 'actuality; reality'), Realität also 'reality'; Stoff = material; Gedankending = thought-entity.","'Vorstellungsarten' = 'ways of representing'; 'an sich' = 'in themselves'; 'schlechthinnothwendig' = 'absolutely necessary'; Hauptstück = 'chapter'.","The sentence beginning 'Weil aber, wenn wir uns einmal …' is anacoluthic in the German (the 'Weil' clause has no finite main verb until 'so bleibt'); kept as it stands, including the colon.","'welchen … zuzulassen, es allerdings erlaubt ist' has ambiguous reference ('es' is a placeholder); 'welches … ist' at the end of that sentence may refer to the object or to the whole; left ambiguous.","'Lehr-' none; 'dringt uns … diesen Schritt zu wagen' rendered 'urges us to venture this step'."]],"krv-a-0947":["Third Chapter of the Second Book of the Transcendental Dialectic. The Ideal of Pure Reason.",["Hauptstück = chapter; Ideal kept as 'ideal'; rein = pure."]],"krv-a-0948":["First Section. On the Ideal in General.",["'überhaupt' = 'in general'; Abschnitt = section."]],"krv-a-0949":["We have seen above: that through pure concepts of the understanding, without all conditions of sensibility, no objects at all can be represented, because the conditions of the objective reality of them are lacking and nothing but the mere form of thinking is encountered in them. Nevertheless they can be exhibited *in concreto*, if one applies them to appearances; for in these they actually have the material for the concept of experience, which is nothing but a concept of the understanding *in concreto*. Ideas, however, are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no appearance can be found in which they could be represented *in concreto*. They contain a certain completeness to which no possible empirical cognition attains, and reason has in this only a systematic unity in mind, to which it seeks to bring the empirically possible unity closer, without ever fully reaching it.",["Gegenstände = objects; Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Erfahrungsbegriff = concept of experience; Erkentniß = cognition; Stoff = material.","'im Sinne' (in the phrase 'im Sinne haben') is rendered 'in mind'; Sinn is otherwise 'sense'.","'obiectiven' is the Latinate spelling; rendered 'objective'."]],"krv-a-0950":["But still further than the idea, that seems to be removed from objective reality which I call the ideal, and by which I understand the idea not merely *in concreto* but *in individuo*, i.e., as a single thing, determinable or even determined through the idea alone.",["'indiuiduo' corrected to *in individuo*. 'bestimbar'/'bestimt' = determinable/determined (bestimmen = determine)."]],"krv-a-0951":["Humanity in its whole perfection contains not only the enlargement of all the essential properties belonging to this nature, which make up our concept of it, up to complete congruence with its ends, which would be our idea of perfect humanity, but also everything that, outside this concept, belongs to the thoroughgoing determination of the idea; for of all opposed predicates only a single one can fit the idea of the most perfect human being. What is an ideal for us was for Plato an idea of the divine understanding, a single object in the pure intuition of it, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings and the original ground of all copies in appearance.",["Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erweiterung = 'enlargement' (not 'extension', which is Ausdehnung); Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit is also glossed 'property'); durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination.","Gegenstand = object; Wesen = being; Art = kind; Nachbilder = copies; Urgrund = original ground; Anschauung = intuition."]],"krv-a-0952":["But without climbing so high, we must admit: that human reason contains not only ideas but also ideals, which indeed do not have, like the Platonic ones, creative, but still practical power (as regulative principles), and lie at the ground of the possibility of the perfection of certain actions. Moral concepts are not wholly pure concepts of reason, because something empirical (pleasure or displeasure) lies at their ground. Nevertheless, with regard to the principle through which reason sets limitations on freedom that is in itself lawless, (thus if one pays attention merely to their form) they can quite well serve as an example of pure concepts of reason. Virtue and, with it, human wisdom in all their purity, are ideas. But the sage (of the Stoic) is an ideal, i.e., a human being who exists merely in thought, but who is fully congruent with the idea of wisdom. Just as the idea gives the rule, so in such a case the ideal serves as the archetype of the thoroughgoing determination of the copy and we have no other standard for our actions than the conduct of this divine human being in us, with whom we compare ourselves, judge ourselves and thereby better ourselves, although we can never reach it. These ideals, even if one would not concede objective reality (existence) to them, are nevertheless not for that reason to be regarded as figments of the brain, but supply an indispensable standard for reason, which needs the concept of that which is entirely complete in its kind, in order to estimate and measure accordingly the degree and the deficiencies of the incomplete. But to want to realize the ideal in an example, i.e., in appearance, as for instance the sage in a novel, is unfeasible and moreover has something nonsensical and little edifying about it, in that the natural limitations, which continually do damage to the completeness in the idea, make all illusion impossible in such an attempt and thereby make the good that lies in the idea itself suspect and similar to a mere fiction.",["Kraft = power; Schranken = limitations; Freiheit = freedom; Reinigkeit = purity; Beurtheilen = judge/assess (here 'judge' with sich 'ourselves'); Richtmaaß = standard.","'Illusion' is Kant's Latinate word, kept as 'illusion'; Schein is also 'illusion' in the glossary.","'vor Hirngespinste anzusehen' = 'to be regarded as figments of the brain' ('vor' for 'für'). 'Weise' = 'sage' (substantive). 'Wesen' not present here; 'objective Realität (Existenz)': Existenz = existence."]],"krv-a-0953":["So it stands with the ideal of reason, which must always rest on determinate concepts and serve as rule and archetype, whether of compliance or of assessment. Quite otherwise is it with those creations of the imagination, about which no one can explain himself and give an understandable concept, as it were monograms, which are only individual strokes, though determined by no avowed rule, which make up more a drawing as it were floating in the middle of various experiences than a determinate image, such as painters and physiognomists give out to have in their heads and which are supposed to be a shadow-image of their products or also judgments, not to be communicated. They can, though only improperly, be called ideals of sensibility, because they are supposed to be the unattainable model of possible empirical intuitions and nevertheless supply no rule capable of explanation and testing.",["Einbildungskraft = imagination; Beurtheilung = assessment, 'Beurtheilungen' = judgments (glossary: judgment); Erklärung = explanation; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Anschauung = intuition.","'es sey der Befolgung, oder Beurtheilung' rendered 'whether of compliance or of assessment'; 'Erfahrungen' = experiences; the German 'derselben' references are kept literal.","'Mittel' in 'im Mittel verschiedener Erfahrungen' rendered 'in the middle of' (not 'means')."]],"krv-a-0954":["The aim of reason with its ideal is, on the contrary, thoroughgoing determination according to rules *a priori*; hence it thinks for itself an object that is to be thoroughgoingly determinable according to principles, although the sufficient conditions for this are lacking in experience and the concept itself is therefore transcendent.",["Absicht = aim; hinreichende = 'sufficient' (the glossary's zureichend is also 'sufficient'); Bedingungen = conditions; Gegenstand = object; transscendent = transcendent.","'bestimbar' = determinable; 'durchgängig' = thoroughgoingly."]],"krv-a-0955":["Second Section of the Third Chapter. On the Transcendental Ideal (*Prototypon transscendentale*).",["Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section. Latin kept as printed in italics."]],"krv-a-0956":["Every concept is, with regard to what is not contained in itself, undetermined and stands under the principle of determinability: that only one, of every two contradictorily opposed predicates, can pertain to it, which rests on the proposition of contradiction and is therefore a merely logical principle, which abstracts from all content of cognition and has nothing but the logical form of it before its eyes.",["Grundsatz and Princip are both 'principle'; Satz des Widerspruchs = 'proposition of contradiction'.","'ein blos logisch Princip' reproduced as in the original text. Ambiguity kept: 'welcher ... beruht' (masc. relative) attaches to Grundsatz; 'das ... abstrahirt' to Princip. 'Inhalt' = content; 'Erkentniß' = cognition."]],"krv-a-0957":["But every thing, according to its possibility, stands still under the principle of thoroughgoing determination, according to which, of all possible predicates of things, insofar as they are compared with their opposites, one must pertain to it. This rests not merely on the proposition of contradiction; for it considers, besides the relation of two predicates conflicting with one another, every thing still in relation to the entire possibility, as the sum total of all predicates of things in general and, in that it presupposes this as condition *a priori*, it represents every thing as it derives its own possibility from the share that it has in that entire possibility*). The *principium* of thoroughgoing determination thus concerns the content and not merely the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all predicates that are to make up the complete concept of a thing and not merely of the analytic representation through one of two opposed predicates, and contains a transcendental presupposition, namely that of the matter for all possibility, which is to contain *a priori* the *data* for the particular possibility of every thing.",["'*)' marks the footnote (krv-a-0958); the asterisk is Kant's, not italics.","'wie es ... ableite': subjunctive kept; 'as it derives' could be read as 'as [if] it derived'. 'es' (it) in 'so stellt es' refers to the principle (Grundsatz), left as 'it'.","Inbegriff = sum total; gesamte Möglichkeit = 'entire possibility'; Materie = matter (the footnote has Stoff = material); Verhältniß = relation; Antheil = share; Voraussetzung = presupposition; besonder = particular.","'Bedingung a priori' is ambiguous between 'condition *a priori*' and 'presupposes *a priori*'; the English keeps the same ambiguity."]],"krv-a-0958":["*) Through this principle, then, every thing is related to a common correlate, namely the entire possibility, which, if it (i.e., the material for all possible predicates) were encountered in the idea of a single thing, would prove an affinity of everything possible through the identity of the ground of the thoroughgoing determination of it. The determinability of every concept is subordinated to the universality (*Universalitas*) of the principle of the exclusion of a middle between two opposed predicates, but the determination of a thing to the totality (*Universitas*) or the sum total of all possible predicates.",["'Vniuersalitas' and 'Vniversitas' corrected to *Universalitas*, *Universitas*; *Correlatum* here rendered 'correlate' (Latin in the German, in roman type).","Stoff = material (cf. Materie = matter in the text); Allgemeinheit = universality; Allheit = totality; Inbegriff = sum total; Grund = ground; Bestimmung = determination.","'derselben' (of it) after 'Bestimmung' left ambiguous between the thing and the possibility, as in the German."]],"krv-a-0959":["The proposition: everything existing is thoroughgoingly determined, signifies not only that of each pair of given opposed [predicates], but also of all possible predicates, one always belongs to it; through this proposition predicates are not merely compared with one another logically, but the thing itself is compared transcendentally with the sum total of all possible predicates. It wants to say as much as: in order to cognize a thing completely, one must cognize everything possible, and determine it thereby, whether affirmatively or negatively. Thoroughgoing determination is consequently a concept that we can never exhibit *in concreto* according to its totality, and is therefore grounded on an idea, which has its seat solely in reason, which prescribes to the understanding the rule of its complete use.",["Bracketed \"[predicates]\" is not in the German, which has only \"gegebenen\" after \"entgegengesezten\"; it is added for English grammar only. The construction \"von iedem Paare ... gegebenen\" is elliptical in the German.","durchgängig bestimmt / Bestimmung = thoroughgoing(ly) determined / determination (Latin determinatio in glossary); bedeutet = signifies; Totalität = totality (Allheit is the glossary's German term; here the Latinate Totalität); Inbegriff = sum total; erkennen = cognize; Gebrauch = use.","\"dem Verstande die Regel seines vollständigen Gebrauchs\": \"seines\" could refer to understanding or to reason; left ambiguous as in the German. Likewise the relative \"welche ... die\" is kept as a sequence of relatives.","\"Existirende\" and \"existirt\" rendered with \"existing\"; Existenz = existence (Dasein also = existence).","Sentence \"Er will so viel sagen\": \"Er\" = \"Satz\" (kept as \"It\")."]],"krv-a-0960":["Now although this idea of the sum total of all possibility, insofar as it lies at the ground as condition of the thoroughgoing determination of each thing, is itself still undetermined with regard to the predicates that may constitute it, and we thereby think nothing further than a sum total of all possible predicates in general, we nevertheless find upon closer investigation that this idea, as original concept, excludes a multitude of predicates that are already given as derived through others, or cannot stand next to one another, and that it purifies itself up to a concept thoroughgoingly determined *a priori* and thereby becomes the concept of a single object, which is thoroughgoingly determined through the mere idea, and consequently must be called an ideal of pure reason.",["Gegenstand = object; Menge = multitude; Urbegriff = original concept; Ideal left as \"ideal\"; rein = pure; Grund = ground (\"zum Grunde liegt\" = \"lies at the ground\").","\"denselben ausmachen mögen\": \"denselben\" (dative/accusative plural vs. singular) is grammatically doubtful in the 1781 text; rendered \"constitute it\". Possibly an error for \"denselben\" = the Inbegriff.","\"als abgeleitet durch andere schon gegeben seyn\": \"seyn\" is the 1781 spelling/form; the construction (\"that are already given as derived through others\") preserves the ambiguity whether \"als abgeleitet\" modifies \"gegeben\" or \"Prädicate\".","\"Ansehung\" rendered \"with regard to\"; \"einzelnen\" = single."]],"krv-a-0961":["If we consider all possible predicates not merely logically but transcendentally, i.e., according to their content, which can be thought *a priori* in them, we find that through some of them a being, through others a mere not-being is represented. The logical negation, which is indicated solely through the little word: Not, never really attaches to a concept, but only to its relation to another in judgment, and can therefore be far from sufficient to designate a concept with regard to its content. The expression: Non-mortal, cannot give to cognize at all that a mere not-being in the object is thereby represented, but leaves all content untouched. A transcendental negation, by contrast, signifies not-being in itself, to which transcendental affirmation is opposed, which is a Something, whose concept in itself already expresses a being, and is therefore called reality (thinghood), because only through it and as far as it reaches are objects Something (things), the opposed negation, on the other hand, signifies a mere lack, and, where this alone is thought, the sublation of all thing is represented.",["Seyn/Nichtseyn = being/not-being; Verneinung = negation; Beiahung = affirmation (bejahen = affirm); Realität = reality; Sachheit = thinghood; Ding = thing; Etwas = Something; Inhalt = content; Ausdruck = expression; bedeutet = signifies; Urtheil = judgment.","\"Nichtsterblich\" rendered \"Non-mortal\".","\"kan ... zu erkennen geben\" = \"can give to cognize\"; literal.","\"Aufhebung alles Dinges\" kept singular \"all thing\"; \"Aufhebung\" = sublation.","The final clause's syntax (\"Mangel bedeutet und, wo diese allein gedacht wird, die Aufhebung ... vorgestellt wird\") is irregular in the German and is kept so; \"Etwas\" and \"Dinge\" are both kept as in the German; \"Gegenstände\" = objects."]],"krv-a-0962":["Now no one can think a negation determinately without having the opposed affirmation lie at the ground. The man born blind cannot form the least representation of darkness, because he has none of light; the savage none of poverty, because he does not know prosperity*). The ignorant has no concept of his ignorance, because he has none of science, etc. All concepts of negations are therefore also derived, and the realities contain the data and, so to speak, the matter, or the transcendental content, for the possibility and thoroughgoing determination of all things.",["kennen = know (noscere); Wissenschaft = science; Materie = matter; Vorstellung = representation; Begriff = concept; Data kept as in the German; Wolstand = prosperity.","\"Der Unwissende\" rendered \"the ignorant\" (masculine singular, as in German); \"Wilde\" = \"savage\".","The asterisk refers to the footnote that follows as the next passage."]],"krv-a-0963":["*) The observations and calculations of the astronomers have taught us much that is admirable, but the most important is surely that they have uncovered for us the abyss of ignorance, which human reason, without this knowledge, could never have represented to itself as so great, and about which reflection must produce a great alteration in the determination of the final aims of our use of reason.",["Beobachtung = observation; Kentnisse = knowledge (Kenntnis); Absicht = aim (\"Endabsichten\" = final aims); Nachdenken = reflection (not Überlegung); Gebrauch = use; Bestimmung = determination.","\"worüber\" = \"about which\"; its referent (the abyss, or the ignorance) is left ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-a-0964":["If, then, a transcendental substratum is laid at the ground of thoroughgoing determination in our reason, which contains as it were the whole store of material from which all possible predicates of things can be taken, this substratum is nothing other than the idea of an All of reality (*omnitudo realitatis*). All true negations are then nothing but limitations, which they could not be called if the unlimited (the All) did not lie at the ground.",["Stoff = material; Schranke = limitation; Substratum kept in Latin form as in the text (italicize optional); Unbeschränkte = the unlimited; Realität = reality.","\"daher\" in \"daher alle mögliche Prädicate ... genommen werden können\" rendered \"from which\"; literally \"whence\"."]],"krv-a-0965":["But through this all-possession of reality the concept of a thing in itself, as thoroughgoingly determined, is also represented, and the concept of an *ens realissimum* is the concept of a single being, because of all possible opposed predicates one, namely that which belongs to being absolutely, is encountered in its determination. Thus it is a transcendental ideal, which lies at the ground of the thoroughgoing determination that is necessarily encountered in everything that exists, and constitutes the supreme and complete material condition of its possibility, to which all thinking of objects in general, with regard to their content, must be traced back. But it is also the only proper ideal of which human reason is capable; because only in this single case is a concept of a thing, universal in itself, thoroughgoingly determined through itself, and cognized as the representation of an individual.",["Wesen = being (here also in \"Seyn\" = being; both rendered \"being\"); Allbesitz = all-possession; an sich = in itself; Dinges = thing; Bedingung = condition; nothwendig = necessary; fähig = capable (Fähigkeit = capacity).","\"seiner Möglichkeit\": \"seiner\" refers grammatically to \"Ideal\" or \"Bestimmung\"; left ambiguous. \"ihrem Inhalte nach\": \"ihrem\" refers to \"Gegenstände\".","\"zurückgeführt werden muß\" = \"must be traced back\"; \"auf welcher\" (ground/condition) rendered \"to which\" as the German has \"auf welcher ... zurückgeführt\", a grammatical irregularity."]],"krv-a-0966":["The logical determination of a concept by reason rests on a disjunctive inference of reason, in which the major premise contains a logical division (the division of the sphere of a universal concept), the minor premise restricts this sphere to a part, and the conclusion determines the concept through this. The universal concept of a reality in general cannot be divided *a priori*, because without experience one knows no determinate species of reality that would be contained under that genus. Thus the transcendental major premise of the thoroughgoing determination of all things is nothing other than the representation of the sum total of all reality, not merely a concept that contains all predicates under itself according to their transcendental content, but one that comprehends them in itself, and the thoroughgoing determination of each thing rests on the limitation of this All of reality, in that something of it is attributed to the thing, but the rest is excluded, which agrees with the Either-or of the disjunctive major premise and with the determination of the object, through one of the members of this division in the minor premise. Accordingly the use of reason, through which it lays the transcendental ideal at the ground of its determination of all possible things, is analogous to that according to which it proceeds in disjunctive inferences of reason, which was the proposition that I laid above at the ground of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are generated parallel to and corresponding with the three kinds of inferences of reason.",["Vernunftschluß = inference of reason (glossary: syllogism; inference of reason); Obersatz/Untersatz = major/minor premise; Schlußsatz = conclusion; Eintheilung/Theilung = division; Sphäre = sphere; Gattung = genus; Arten = species (\"Arten von Vernunftschlüssen\" = kinds); Erfahrung = experience; Einschränkung = limitation (Schranke = limitation also; not distinguished in English); begreifen = comprehend.","\"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Entweder- oder\" = \"Either-or\".","\"welches der Satz war\": \"Satz\" = proposition (also sentence/principle); \"welches\" has an ambiguous antecedent (use of reason / analogy); left ambiguous.","\"nach welchem sie ... erzeugt werden\": \"sie\" = the transcendental ideas; \"dem ... angetroffen\" kept as stated; \"bis auf einen Theil\" = \"to a part\"."]],"krv-a-0967":["It goes without saying that reason, for this its aim, namely of representing to itself solely the necessary thoroughgoing determination of things, does not presuppose the existence of such a being, which is in conformity with the ideal, but only the idea of it, in order to derive from an unconditioned totality of thoroughgoing determination the conditioned, i.e., that of the limited. The ideal is therefore for it the archetype (*prototypon*) of all things, which one and all, as defective copies (*ectypa*), take the material for their possibility from it and, in that they come more or less close to it, nevertheless always fall infinitely far short of reaching it.",["Absicht = aim; Existenz = existence; Wesen = being; Idee = idea; unbedingt = unconditioned; bedingt = conditioned; Totalität = totality; Stoff = material; Möglichkeit = possibility; Urbild = archetype (Prototypon).","\"Es versteht sich von selbst\" = \"it goes without saying\" (literally \"it understands itself\"); \"insgesamt\" = \"one and all\".","\"daher\" rendered \"from it\"; \"demselben\" refers to the ideal; \"fehlen ... es zu erreichen\" = \"fall short of reaching it\"."]],"krv-a-0968":["Thus all possibility of things (of the synthesis of the manifold, as to its content) is regarded as derived, and only that of what includes all reality in itself as original. For all negations (which are yet the only predicates by which everything else can be distinguished from the most real being) are mere limitations of a greater and finally of the highest reality, hence they presuppose this and, as to content, are merely derived from it. All manifoldness of things is only an equally multifarious way of limiting the concept of the highest reality, which is their common substratum, just as all figures are possible only as different ways of limiting infinite space. Hence the object of its ideal, found merely in reason, is also called the original being (*ens originarium*), insofar as it has none above itself, the highest being (*ens summum*), and, insofar as everything, as conditioned, stands under it, the being of all beings (*ens entium*). But all of this does not signify the objective relation of an actual object to other things, but that of an idea to concepts, and leaves us in complete ignorance regarding the existence of a being of such outstanding preeminence.",["Möglichkeit = possibility; Synthesis left as in the German; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Inhalt = content; Realität = reality; Wesen = being (also in realesten Wesen, Urwesen); Gegenstand = object, obiectiv = objective (Gegenstand/Object both 'object').","'Verneinungen' rendered 'negations'; 'einzige Prädicate' is plural although 'die einzige' is singular in form (original spelling); rendered 'only predicates'.","'Substratum' is Latin in the German text (roman type); italicized not marked here as it is treated as an English-naturalized word; it stands as printed.","'Alles dieses ... bedeutet': bedeuten = signify. 'Unwissenheit' = ignorance. 'ihres Ideals': 'ihr' refers to Vernunft ('reason'), kept as 'its'.","'Daher' at the start of the sentence on the ens terms is kept as 'Hence'; the 'desienigen' (OCR/orig. spelling for desjenigen) is silently normalized."]],"krv-a-0969":["Because one also cannot say: that an original being consists of many derived beings, since each of them presupposes that one, hence cannot constitute it, the ideal of the original being will also have to be thought as simple.",["Urwesen = original being; einfach = simple; 'ienes' = 'that one', 'es' = 'it', both left without resolving the referent as in the German."]],"krv-a-0970":["The derivation of all other possibility from this original being will therefore, to speak precisely, also not be able to be regarded as a limitation of its highest reality and as it were as a division of the same; for then the original being would be regarded as a mere aggregate of derived beings, which according to the foregoing is impossible, even though we initially represented it so in the first rough outline. Rather, the highest reality would lie at the ground of the possibility of all things as a ground and not as a sum total, and the manifoldness of the former would rest not on the limitation of the original being itself, but on its complete sequel, to which then also our entire sensibility, together with all reality in appearance, would belong, which cannot belong to the idea of the highest being as an ingredient.",["Einschränkung = limitation (Schranke is also 'limitation' in the glossary); Theilung = division; Inbegriff = sum total; Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; Erscheinung = appearance; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","'als ein Grund und nichts als Inbegriff' kept literally; 'zum Grunde liegen' rendered 'lie at the ground of'.","Ambiguity kept: the relative 'welches' / 'die' (last clause) could refer to 'sensibility' or to 'reality in appearance'; 'die' is feminine singular and the English 'which' is left equally open."]],"krv-a-0971":["If we now pursue this idea of ours further in this way, by hypostatizing it, we shall be able to determine the original being through the mere concept of the highest reality as a unique, simple, all-sufficient, eternal etc., in a word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness through all predicaments. The concept of such a being is that of God thought in the transcendental sense, and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object of a transcendental theology, as I also cited above.",["'hypostasiren' = hypostatize; 'bestimmen' = determine; 'Prädicamente' = predicaments; 'in transscendentalem Verstande' = 'in the transcendental sense' (Verstande here is not the faculty 'understanding'; rendered 'sense' to match idiom, a departure from the glossary 'Verstand = understanding').","'einiges' = 'unique' (einig); could also be read as 'one'; 'allgenugsames' = 'all-sufficient'."]],"krv-a-0972":["Meanwhile this use of the transcendental idea would already overstep the boundaries of its determination and admissibility. For reason laid it at the ground of the thoroughgoing determination of things in general only as the concept of all reality, without demanding that all this reality be given objectively and itself constitute a thing. This latter is a mere fiction, through which we summarize and realize the manifold of our idea in an ideal, as a particular being, for which we have no warrant, so as not even straightforwardly to assume the possibility of such a hypothesis, just as also all consequences that flow from such an ideal do not concern the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, for the sake of which the idea alone was necessary, and have not the least influence on it.",["Gränzen = boundaries; Bestimmung = determination; durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination; Erdichtung = fiction; Folgerungen = consequences; besonderes = particular; Befugniß = warrant.","'zusammenfassen und realisiren' = 'summarize and realize' ('zusammenfassen' could also be 'comprehend together').","'so gar nicht einmal ... zu': the construction is kept as awkward as in the German; 'darauf' refers back to 'Bestimmung' (or the thoroughgoing determination)."]],"krv-a-0973":["It is not enough to describe the procedure of our reason and its dialectic, one must also seek to discover its sources, in order to be able to explain this illusion itself, like a phenomenon of the understanding; for the ideal of which we speak is grounded on a natural and not merely arbitrary idea. Hence I ask: how does reason come to regard all possibility of things as derived from a single one that lies at the ground, namely that of the highest reality, and then to presuppose this, as contained in a particular original being?",["Schein = illusion; Verstand = understanding; Phänomen as printed; 'willkührlich' = arbitrary; 'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the ground'.","'wie komt die Vernunft dazu' = 'how does reason come to'; 'der der höchsten Realität' = 'that of the highest reality'."]],"krv-a-0974":["The answer offers itself of its own accord from the proceedings of the transcendental analytic. The possibility of the objects of the senses is a relation of them to our thinking, in which something (namely the empirical form) can be thought a priori, but that which constitutes the matter, the reality in appearance, (what corresponds to sensation) must be given, without which it also could not be thought at all and hence its possibility could not be represented. Now an object of the senses can be thoroughly determined only if it is compared with all predicates of appearance and through them represented affirmatively or negatively. But because that which constitutes the thing itself (in appearance), namely the real, must be given in it, without which it also could not be thought at all, but that in which the real of all appearances is given is the one all-encompassing experience, the matter for the possibility of all objects of the senses must be presupposed as given in a sum total, on whose limitation alone all possibility of empirical objects, their difference from one another and their thoroughgoing determination, can rest. Now in fact no other objects than those of the senses can be given to us, and nowhere but in the context of a possible experience, consequently nothing is an object for us if it does not presuppose the sum total of all empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. According to a natural illusion we now regard as a principle that which must hold of all things in general, which properly holds only of those that are given as objects of our senses. Consequently we shall, by omitting this limitation, take the empirical principle of our concepts of the possibility of things, as appearances, for a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.",["Gegenstand = object; Materie = matter; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance; Inbegriff = sum total; Einschränkung = limitation; Grundsatz = principle; Princip = principle (Grundsatz and Princip both 'principle').","'Illusion' (natürliche Illusion) = illusion; Schein elsewhere also 'illusion'. 'Sinne/Sinnen' = senses. 'vor' (for für) rendered 'for'/'as'.","'das Reale' = 'the real'; 'beiahend' = affirmatively (bejahen = affirm); 'Context' = context.","Ambiguity kept: 'welcher eigentlich nur von denen gilt' — 'welcher' could refer to 'Grundsatz' or to the clause ('that which must hold'); left open as 'which'.","'dasienige aber, worin ... gegeben ist, die einige allbefassende Erfahrung ist': 'einige' = 'one' (einzige); kept literal."]],"krv-a-0975":["That we afterwards hypostatize this idea of the sum total of all reality, however, comes from this: because we dialectically transform the distributive unity of the experiential use of the understanding into the collective unity of a whole of experience, and think for ourselves, in this whole of appearance, a single thing that contains all empirical reality in itself, which then, by means of the already mentioned transcendental subreption, is confused with the concept of a thing that stands at the head of the possibility of all things, for whose thoroughgoing determination it supplies the real conditions.*)",["Inbegriff = sum total; Einheit = unity; Erfahrungsgebrauch = experiential use (Gebrauch = use); Erfahrungsganzen = whole of experience; Subreption = subreption.","'einzelnes Ding' = 'single thing'; 'Bedingungen' = conditions; 'das Reale' not present here; 'Begriffe eines Dinges' = 'concept of a thing'.","The asterisk and bracket for the footnote reference follow the original '*)'."]],"krv-a-0976":["*) This ideal of the most real being will thus, although it is a mere representation, first be realized, i.e., made into an object, then hypostatized, finally, through a natural progress of reason toward the completion of unity, even personified, as we shall soon adduce; because the regulative unity of experience rests not on the appearances themselves (on sensibility alone), but on the connection of their manifold through the understanding (in an apperception), hence the unity of the highest reality and the thoroughgoing determinability (possibility) of all things seems to lie in a highest understanding, hence in an intelligence.",["Vorstellung = representation; Obiect = object; Fortschritt = progress; Verknüpfung = connection; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Verstand = understanding; Bestimbarkeit (sic) = determinability.","'allerrealesten Wesen' = 'most real being'; 'personificirt' = personified; 'Apperception' as printed.","'Intelligenz' = intelligence."]],"krv-a-0977":["Of the Third Main Section, Third Division. On the Grounds of Proof of Speculative Reason for Inferring the Existence of a Highest Being.",["Hauptstück = main section; Abschnitt = division here (the German heading is rendered literally); Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; Daseyn = existence; schliessen = infer (Schluß = inference)."]],"krv-a-0978":["Notwithstanding this urgent need of reason, to presuppose something that could lie completely at the basis of the understanding for the thoroughgoing determination of its concepts, it nevertheless notices the ideal and merely fictional character of such a presupposition far too easily for it to be persuaded by this alone to assume a mere self-creation of its thinking right away to be an actual being, if it were not pressed by something else to seek somewhere its resting-place in the regress from the conditioned, which is given, to the unconditioned, which in itself and in its mere concept is indeed not yet given as actual, but which alone can complete the series of the conditions carried out to their grounds. This now is the natural course that every human reason, even the most common, takes, although not every one persists in it. It begins not from concepts, but from common experience, and thus lays something existing at the basis. But this ground sinks, if it does not rest on the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. This itself, however, floats without support, if there is still empty space outside and beneath it, and it does not itself fill everything and thereby leave no place any more for the Why, i.e., is infinite according to reality.",["'Bedürfniß' (A text: 'dringenden Bedürfniß') rendered 'need'; the gender of the German is as printed in A and is kept without comment in the English.","'Verstande ... Begriffe ... Bestimmung ... Grunde' = understanding, concepts, determination, ground/basis ('zum Grunde liegen' = 'lie at the basis'; 'zum Grunde legen' = 'lay at the basis'); 'Gründen' in 'zu ihren Gründen' = 'grounds'.","'Gedichtete' = 'fictional' (from dichten); 'Selbstgeschöpf' = 'self-creation'.","'überredet' = 'persuaded' (Überredung = persuasion); 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'wirklich' = 'actual'; 'Realität' = 'reality'.","'Das Bedingte ... das Unbedingte' = 'the conditioned ... the unconditioned'; 'Regressus' kept in Latin form but not italicized in the German as printed in roman? Treated as a Latin word: *regressus* would be expected; here rendered 'regress'.","'wodurch anders' (OCR/orthography 'wodurch anders') read as 'durch etwas anderes', rendered 'by something else'.","'Raum' = 'space'; 'Platz' = 'place' (the glossary gives 'Ort' = 'place' as well; Platz and Ort both 'place')."]],"krv-a-0979":["If something, whatever it may be, exists, then it must also be conceded that something exists necessarily. For the contingent exists only under the condition of another, as its cause, and of this the inference holds further, up to a cause that is not contingent and precisely for that reason exists necessarily without condition. That is the argument on which reason grounds its progress to the original being.",["'Schluß' = 'inference'; 'Fortschritt' = 'progress'; 'Urwesen' = 'original being'.","'zufällig' = 'contingent'; 'Bedingung' = 'condition'; 'nothwendigerweise' = 'necessarily'."]],"krv-a-0980":["Now reason looks around for the concept of a being that would suit such a preeminence of existence as unconditioned necessity, not so much in order then to infer its existence a priori from the concept of it (for if it trusted itself to do this, it would only have to inquire among mere concepts and would not need to lay a given existence at the basis), but only in order to find among all concepts of possible things the one that has in itself nothing conflicting with absolute necessity. For that something or other must exist absolutely necessarily, it already holds as settled according to the first inference. If it can now remove everything that is not compatible with this necessity, except for one, then this is the absolutely necessary being, whether one may comprehend its necessity, i.e., be able to derive it from its concept alone, or not.",["'Daseyn' and 'Existenz' both rendered 'existence' here.","'begreiffen' = 'comprehend'; 'Begriffe' = 'concept(s)'; 'schlechthinnothwendige' = 'absolutely necessary' (rendered with 'absolute Nothwendigkeit' = 'absolute necessity'; 'schlechthin' also rendered 'absolutely').","'ersteren Schlusse' = 'first inference' (literally 'the former inference').","'a priori' left in roman as in the German it attaches ambiguously to 'schliessen' ('infer a priori') or to 'von dem Begriffe'; English retains the same ambiguity."]],"krv-a-0981":["Now that, whose concept contains in itself the Therefore for every Why, which is defective in no piece and in no aim, which everywhere suffices as condition, seems for precisely that reason to be the being suited to absolute necessity, because, with the self-possession of all conditions for everything possible, it itself needs no condition, indeed is not even capable of one, and consequently, at least in one piece, does justice to the concept of unconditioned necessity, in which no other concept can do the like of it, which, because it is deficient and in need of supplementation, shows in itself no such mark of independence from all further conditions. It is true that it cannot yet be securely concluded from this: that what does not contain in itself the highest and in every aim complete condition must for that reason itself be conditioned as to its existence; but it nevertheless does not have in itself the sole mark of unconditioned existence, of which reason is capable, in order to cognize through a concept a priori some being as unconditioned.",["'Günge thun' (A text 'ein Günge thut'): read as 'ein Genüge thut' ('does justice/sufficiency to'); OCR/orthographic variant, rendered 'does justice to'. Doubtful reading.","'Stück' = 'piece'; 'Absicht' = 'aim'; 'defect' kept as 'defective'; 'allerwerts' = 'everywhere'.","'Merkmal' and 'Merkzeichen' both = 'mark'.","'Daseyn' and 'Existenz' both = 'existence'.","'mächtig' = 'capable' (literally 'in command of'); the relative clause 'der ... dessen die Vernunft mächtig ist' left as ambiguous as the German.","'folgern' / 'gefolgert' = 'conclude'; 'erkennen' = 'cognize'; 'Begriff' = 'concept'."]],"krv-a-0982":["The concept of a being of the highest reality would thus suit best, among all concepts of possible things, the concept of an unconditionedly necessary being, and even if it does not fully satisfy this, we nevertheless have no choice, but see ourselves compelled to hold to it, because we may not cast the existence of a necessary being to the winds; but if we grant it, we nevertheless can find in the entire field of possibility nothing that could make a more well-grounded claim to such a preeminence in existence.",["'Realität' = 'reality'; 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'Daseyn' and 'Existenz' = 'existence'.","'in den Wind schlagen' rendered literally-idiomatically as 'cast ... to the winds'.","'gegründetern' = 'more well-grounded' (from Grund = ground)."]],"krv-a-0983":["Thus the natural course of human reason is constituted. First it convinces itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this it cognizes an unconditioned existence. Now it seeks the concept of the independent of all condition and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition for everything else, i.e., in that which contains all reality. The All without limitations, however, is absolute unity and brings with it the concept of a single, namely the highest being, and thus it concludes that the highest being, as original ground of all things, exists absolutely necessarily.",["'überzeugt' = 'convinces' (Überzeugung = conviction); 'zureichend' = 'sufficient'.","'Schranken' = 'limitations'; 'Einheit' = 'unity'; 'einigen' = 'single'.","'schließt' = 'concludes' (Schluß = inference); 'Urgrund' = 'original ground'.","'Daseyn' and 'Existenz' = 'existence'; 'da sey' rendered 'exists'."]],"krv-a-0984":["A certain thoroughness cannot be disputed of this concept, when resolutions are in question, namely, when the existence of some necessary being is once admitted and one agrees that one must take one's side as to where one wants to posit it; for then one cannot choose more suitably, or rather one has no choice, but is compelled to give one's vote to the absolute unity of complete reality, as the original source of possibility. But if nothing drives us to resolve and we would rather let this whole matter stand open, until we were compelled to assent by the full weight of the grounds of proof, i.e., if it is merely a matter of judging how much we know of this problem and what we merely flatter ourselves to know: then the above inference appears by far not in so advantageous a shape and needs favor in order to make up for the lack of its legal claims.",["'Beurteilung' = 'judging' (here 'Beurtheilung' = estimation/judgment); 'Aufgabe' = 'problem'; 'Wissen' (wissen) = 'know'.","'Beweisgründe' = 'grounds of proof'; 'Beifall' = 'assent'; 'Schluß' = 'inference'.","'Entschliessungen' / 'entschliessen' = 'resolutions' / 'resolve'; 'Parthey ergreiffen' = 'take one's side'.","'Rechtsansprüche' = 'legal claims'; 'Urquelle' = 'original source'; 'setzen' = 'posit'.","'Daseyn' = 'existence'; 'Realität' = 'reality'; 'Möglichkeit' = 'possibility'."]],"krv-a-0985":["For if we let everything be as good as it lies here before us, namely, first, that from some given existence (at most also merely my own) a correct inference to the existence of an unconditionedly necessary being takes place, second: that I must regard a being that contains all reality, and hence also all condition, as absolutely unconditioned, and consequently the concept of the thing that suits absolute necessity is hereby found: then it cannot be inferred at all from this that the concept of a limited being that does not have the highest reality therefore contradicts absolute necessity. For although I do not encounter in its concept the unconditioned that the All of conditions already brings with it, it cannot at all be concluded from this that its existence must precisely for that reason be conditioned; just as in a hypothetical syllogism I cannot say: where a certain condition (namely here that of completeness according to concepts) is not, there the conditioned is not either. Rather it will remain unhindered for us to let all remaining limited beings count just as well as unconditionedly necessary, even though we cannot infer their necessity from the general concept that we have of them. In this way, however, this argument would not have procured for us the slightest concept of properties of a necessary being and would have accomplished nothing at all.",["'Vernunftschluß' = 'syllogism'; 'Schluß' = 'inference'; 'schliessen' = 'infer'; 'folgern' = 'conclude'.","'Daseyn' and 'Existenz' = 'existence'; 'Realität' = 'reality'; 'Wesen' = 'being'.","'Eigenschaften' = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary); 'eingeschränkt' = 'limited' (Schranke = limitation).","'unbenommen' = 'unhindered'; 'allenfals' = 'at most' (literally 'at any rate'); 'Vollständigkeit' = 'completeness'."]],"krv-a-0986":["Nevertheless a certain importance and a standing remain to this argument, which cannot yet be taken from it straightaway on account of this objective insufficiency. For suppose: there were obligations that would be entirely correct in the idea of reason, but without all reality of application to ourselves, i.e., without incentives, if a highest being were not presupposed that could give effect and emphasis to the practical laws: then we would also have an obligation to follow the concepts which, even if they might not be objectively sufficient, are nevertheless preponderant according to the measure of our reason and in comparison with which we nevertheless cognize nothing better and more convincing. The duty to choose would here bring the indecision of speculation out of equilibrium through a practical addition, indeed reason would find no justification before itself, as the most indulgent judge, if under pressing motives, though only of deficient insight, it had not followed these grounds of its judgment, beyond which we at least know no better.",["Obiectiv/obiectiven = 'objective' (Object-root); Gegenstand also 'object' elsewhere in this set.","Verbindlichkeit = 'obligation'; Pflicht = 'duty'. Triebfedern = 'incentives' (not in glossary); Bewegursachen = 'motives' (cf. Bewegungsgrund = motive).","Realität = 'reality'; Wesen = 'being'; Begriff = 'concept'; erkennen = 'cognize'; Grund = 'ground'; Urtheil = 'judgment'; Vernunft = 'reason'.","'Nachdruck' rendered 'emphasis'; 'Unschließigkeit' (modern Unschlüssigkeit) rendered 'indecision'; 'ia' = 'indeed'.","'setzet: es gebe' – the imperative 'setzet' rendered 'suppose' (setzen elsewhere = 'posit'); subjunctive kept."]],"krv-a-0987":["This argument, although it is in fact transcendental, in that it rests on the inner insufficiency of the contingent, is nevertheless so simple and natural that it is suited to the commonest human sense as soon as it is only once led to it. One sees things change, arise and pass away; they must therefore, or at least their state, have a cause. But of every cause that may ever be given in experience, the same thing can again be asked. Where, then, are we to place the supreme causality more fittingly than there where the highest causality also is, i.e., in that being which originally contains in itself the sufficiency for the possible effect, whose concept also comes about very easily through the single trait of an all-embracing perfection. This highest cause we then take to be absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to ascend up to it and have no ground to go still further beyond it. Hence we see among all peoples, through their blindest polytheism, some sparks of monotheism shine through, to which not reflection and deep speculation, but only a natural course of common understanding, which has gradually become intelligible, has led.",["Zufällig = 'contingent'; Zustand = 'state'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Nachdenken = 'reflection' (Überlegung is also 'reflection' in the glossary).","'Menschensinne' rendered 'human sense' (Sinn = sense).","'Zulänglichkeit' = 'sufficiency' (cf. zureichend = sufficient); 'schlechthin'/'schlechterdings' both 'absolutely'.","'sie' in 'läßt sie eben dieses wiederum fragen' is left unresolved in the German; rendered impersonally ('the same thing can again be asked'), a minimal accommodation to English.","'Caussalität' = 'causality'; 'vor' = 'for/as' rendered 'take to be' (halten vor).","'verständlich gewordener' could modify 'Gang' (course) or, loosely, the course of understanding; English keeps 'which has gradually become intelligible' attached to 'course'."]],"krv-a-0988":["Only three kinds of proof of the existence of God from speculative reason are possible.",["Beweisarten = 'kinds of proof'; Beweis = 'proof'; Daseyn = 'existence' (Existenz also 'existence')."]],"krv-a-0989":["All the paths that one may take in this aim either begin from determinate experience and the particular constitution of our world of sense cognized through it, and ascend from it according to laws of causality up to the highest cause outside the world—or they lay at the ground only indeterminate experience, i.e., some existence empirically, or they finally abstract from all experience and infer entirely *a priori* from mere concepts to the existence of a highest cause. The first proof is the physicotheological, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological proof. There are no more of them, and there also cannot be more.",["Absicht = 'aim'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Gesetze = 'laws'; Daseyn = 'existence'.","Original has 'hinaufoder' (run together, perhaps a dash lost in OCR); silently corrected with a dash.","'a priori' printed in Latin type, italicized. 'bestimten'/'unbestimte' = 'determinate'/'indeterminate' (bestimmen = determine).","'zum Grunde legen' = 'lay at the ground'; 'empirisch' could attach to 'Daseyn' or to 'legen'; left ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-a-0990":["I will demonstrate: that reason accomplishes as little on the one path (the empirical) as on the other (the transcendental), and that it spreads its wings in vain to get beyond the world of sense through the mere power of speculation. But as for the order in which these kinds of proof must be laid before the examination, it will be precisely the reverse of that which reason, gradually expanding, takes and in which we also placed them first. For it will be shown: that, although experience gives the first occasion for it, nevertheless merely the transcendental concept guides reason in this its striving and in all such attempts marks out the goal that it has set itself. I will therefore begin with the examination of the transcendental proof and afterwards see what the addition of the empirical can do toward the enlargement of its force of proof.",["Macht = 'power'; Beweiskraft = 'force of proof' (Kraft = force); Zusatz = 'addition'.","'sie' after 'in der wir sie auch zuerst gestellt haben' refers to the kinds of proof (Beweisarten); 'die ... Vernunft ... nimt' kept as in German word order.","'Prüfung' = 'examination'; 'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of sense'; transcendental/empirical as in German.","Ambiguity kept: 'sie' in 'es sei die umgekehrte ... in der wir sie' could be proofs; English 'them' follows."]],"krv-a-0991":["Fourth Section of the Third Chapter. On the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God.",["Hauptstück = 'chapter' (not in glossary); Abschnitt = 'section'; Unmöglichkeit = 'impossibility'; Daseyn = 'existence'."]],"krv-a-0992":["One readily sees from what has gone before: that the concept of an absolutely necessary being is a pure concept of reason, i.e., a mere idea, whose objective reality is still far from being proved by the fact that reason needs it, which also gives direction only to a certain, though unattainable, completeness and actually serves more to bound the understanding than to extend it to new objects. There is found here now the strange and absurd [thing], that the inference from a given existence in general to some absolutely necessary existence seems to be pressing and correct and we nevertheless have all the conditions of the understanding for forming a concept of such a necessity entirely against us.",["Gegenstände = 'objects'; obiective = 'objective'; Realität = 'reality'; Schluß = 'inference'; Bedingungen = 'conditions'; Vernunftbegriff = 'concept of reason'.","'begränzen' = 'bound' (Grenze = bound(ary)); 'Befremdliche und Widersinnische' rendered 'strange and absurd [thing]' with bracket for the substantivized adjectives (an English necessity).","'schlechthinnothwendig' = 'absolutely necessary', same as 'absolutnothwendig'; two German words, one English word.","'Vollständigkeit' = 'completeness'; 'Anweisung geben' = 'give direction'.","'dringend' rendered 'pressing'."]],"krv-a-0993":["One has at all times spoken of the absolutely necessary being and has taken less trouble to understand whether and how one can even think a thing of this kind than rather to prove its existence. Now a nominal definition of this concept is indeed quite easy, namely that it is something whose non-being is impossible, but one becomes no wiser through this with regard to the conditions that make it impossible to regard the non-being of a thing as absolutely unthinkable and which are really that which one wants to know, namely whether through this concept we think anything at all, or not. For to throw away, by means of the word: Unconditioned, all the conditions that the understanding always needs in order to regard something as necessary, still makes it far from understandable to me whether I then think anything through a concept of an unconditionally necessary, or perhaps even nothing.",["Nahmenerklärung = 'nominal definition' (Erklärung = definition/explanation); verstehen = 'understand'; verständlich = 'understandable'.","Nichtseyn = 'non-being'; undenklich = 'unthinkable'; Unbedingt = 'Unconditioned' (bedingt = conditioned).","'Ansehung' in 'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'; 'ansehen' = 'regard'.","'Unbedingtnothwendigen' = 'unconditionally necessary' (substantive, neuter).","Syntax of the last sentence ('alle Bedingungen ... wegwerfen, macht mir ...') kept with infinitive as subject."]],"krv-a-0994":["Still more: this concept, ventured on mere chance and finally become quite familiar, one has moreover believed to explain through a multitude of examples, so that all further inquiry regarding its understandability seemed quite unnecessary. Every proposition of geometry, e.g., that a triangle has three angles, is absolutely necessary, and so one spoke of an object that lies entirely outside the sphere of our understanding as if one understood quite well what one wanted to say with the concept of it.",["Erklären = 'explain' (Erklärung = explanation; definition); Satz = 'proposition'; Gegenstand = 'object'; Verständlichkeit = 'understandability'.","'auf das blosse Gerathewol' = 'on mere chance' (idiom: at random).","'Triangel' = 'triangle'; 'Sphäre' = 'sphere'.","'Nachfrage' rendered 'inquiry'."]],"krv-a-0995":["All the examples pretended are, without exception, taken only from judgments, but not from things and their existence. But the unconditioned necessity of judgments is not an absolute necessity of things. For the absolute necessity of the judgment is only a conditioned necessity of the thing, or of the predicate in the judgment. The previous proposition did not say: that three angles are absolutely necessary, but that, under the condition that a triangle is there (is given), three angles are also there (in it) of necessity. Nevertheless this logical necessity has proved such a great power of its illusion: that, in that one had made for oneself an *a priori* concept of a thing that was so set up that one, in one's opinion, comprehended existence within its domain, one believed one could infer with certainty from this that, because existence necessarily pertains to the object of this concept, i.e., under the condition that I posit this thing as given (existing), its existence too is posited necessarily (according to the rule of identity), and this being therefore itself is absolutely necessary, because its existence is thought along in a concept assumed at will and under the condition that I posit the object of it.",["Urtheil = 'judgment'; Sache = 'thing' (Ding also 'thing'): two German words, one English word. Gegenstand and Obiect both 'object'.","'bedingt' = 'conditioned', 'unbedingt' = 'unconditioned'; Nothwendigkeit = 'necessity'; Illusion = 'illusion' (Schein also 'illusion').","'begriff ... in seinen Umfang' = 'comprehended ... within its domain' (begreifen = comprehend; Umfang = domain).","'a priori' printed in Latin, italicized; 'Meinung' = 'opinion'; 'setzen' = 'posit'; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'.","'seine' in 'seiner Meinung nach' ambiguous between 'one's' and the concept's/thing's; rendered 'one's'. 'mit gedacht' = 'thought along'.","'Vorgegebene' = 'pretended' (alleged); 'zukomt' = 'pertains'."]],"krv-a-0996":["If I cancel the predicate in an identical judgment and retain the subject, a contradiction arises, and therefore I say: the former belongs to the latter necessarily. But if I cancel the subject together with the predicate, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing more that could be contradicted. To posit a triangle and yet cancel its three angles is contradictory, but to cancel the triangle together with its three angles is no contradiction. Exactly the same holds with the concept of an absolutely necessary being. If you cancel its existence, then you cancel the thing itself with all its predicates; where then is the contradiction supposed to come from? Externally there is nothing that would be contradicted; for the thing is not supposed to be externally necessary; internally too, nothing, for you have, through the cancellation of the thing itself, canceled everything internal at the same time. God is omnipotent; that is a necessary judgment. Omnipotence cannot be canceled if you posit a deity, i.e., an infinite being, with whose concept that predicate is identical. But if you say: God is not, then neither omnipotence nor any other of his predicates is given, for they are all canceled together with the subject, and in this thought not the least contradiction shows itself.",["Aufheben rendered consistently as 'cancel' (the German has no glossary entry); Setzen/sezt = 'posit' (glossary).","Prädicat = predicate; Subiect = subject; Widerspruch = contradiction; Begriff = concept; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Wesen = being; Ding = thing; Urtheil = judgment.","'ienes komt diesem': 'the former ... the latter' renders the demonstratives literally as the pronouns 'jenes/diesem' refer back to predicate and subject; 'that/this' would also be possible.","'Gottheit' = deity; 'unendlich Wesen' kept as in the text (unendlich, uninflected); 'infinite being'.","'Aeusserlich'/'innerlich' = externally/internally; the elliptical 'innerlich auch nichts' preserved.","'ienes' in 'iener identisch ist' = 'that predicate' is the translation of the demonstrative iener, whose referent (Allmacht) is grammatically ambiguous; the English 'that predicate' makes explicit what the German leaves to the pronoun."]],"krv-a-0997":["You have seen, then: that if I cancel the predicate of a judgment together with the subject, an inner contradiction can never arise, let the predicate be whatever it may. Now there is no escape left to you except that you must say: there are subjects that cannot be canceled at all, that must therefore remain. But that would be just as much as to say: there are absolutely necessary subjects, a presupposition about whose correctness I have just doubted and whose possibility you wanted to show me. For I cannot make for myself the slightest concept of a thing which, if it were canceled with all its predicates, would leave behind a contradiction, and without the contradiction I have, through mere pure concepts a priori, no mark of impossibility.",["Merkmal = mark (nota); Unmöglichkeit = impossibility; Möglichkeit = possibility.","'schlechterdingsnothwendig' rendered 'absolutely necessary' (as in the preceding passage, 'absolutnothwendig'); both are one English expression in the glossary-based rendering.","'Ausflucht' = escape; 'Voraussetzung' = presupposition.","'a priori' left as in the German; it may attach to 'Begriffe' or to 'Merkmal ... habe' (ambiguity preserved)."]],"krv-a-0998":["Against all these general inferences (which no human being can refuse) you challenge me with a case, which you set up as a proof through the deed: that there is yet one and indeed only this one concept, in which the non-being or the cancellation of its object is contradictory in itself, and this is the concept of the most real being. It has, you say, all reality, and you are entitled to assume such a being as possible, (to which I for now consent, although the concept that does not contradict itself is still far from proving the possibility of the object*). Now among all reality existence is also comprehended: Thus existence lies in the concept of something possible. If this thing is now canceled, then the inner possibility of the thing is canceled, which is contradictory.",["Schlüsse = inferences (glossary: Schluß = inference); 'Beweis durch die That' = 'proof through the deed'.","'allerrealesten Wesen' = 'most real being' (ens realissimum); 'Nichtseyn' = non-being; 'Daseyn' = existence; Realität = reality.","'mit begriffen' = 'comprehended' (begreifen = comprehend in the glossary, here used in the sense of 'included'); 'Möglichen' = 'something possible'.","The asterisk refers to the footnote of krv-a-0999."]],"krv-a-0999":["*) The concept is always possible if it does not contradict itself. That is the logical mark of possibility, and through it its object is distinguished from the *nihil negativum*. But it can nonetheless be an empty concept if the objective reality of the synthesis through which the concept is generated is not specially demonstrated, which however, as has been shown above, always rests on principles of possible experience and not on the principle of analysis (the principle of contradiction). That is a warning not to infer immediately from the possibility of concepts (logical) to the possibility of things (real).",["'nihil negatiuum' in the original; given as *nihil negativum* (u/v normalized).","Grundsatz = principle; Satz des Widerspruchs = 'principle of contradiction' (Satz = proposition/principle; here 'principle'); Principien = principles.","'obiective Realität' = objective reality; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Synthesis' = synthesis.","'Dinge' = things; 'Begriffe' = concepts."]],"krv-a-1000":["I answer: You have already committed a contradiction if you have brought into the concept of a thing, which you wanted to think merely in terms of its possibility, under whatever concealed name, the concept of its existence. If this is conceded to you, then to all appearances you have won the game, but in fact have said nothing; for you have committed a mere tautology. I ask you, is the proposition: this or that thing (which I concede to you as possible, let it be whichever it may) exists, is, I say, this proposition an analytic or a synthetic proposition? If it is the former, then through the existence of the thing you add nothing to your thought of the thing, but then either the thought that is in you would have to be the thing itself, or you have presupposed an existence as belonging to possibility, and then inferred the existence, allegedly, from the inner possibility, which is nothing but a miserable tautology. The word: reality, which sounds different in the concept of the thing than existence in the concept of the predicate, does not settle it. For if you also call all positing (undetermined what you posit) reality, then you have already posited the thing with all its predicates in the concept of the subject and assumed it as actual, and in the predicate you only repeat it. If on the other hand you admit, as every reasonable person must admit in fairness, that every existential proposition is synthetic, how then do you want to assert that the predicate of existence cannot be canceled without contradiction, since this privilege belongs peculiarly only to the analytic ones, whose character rests precisely on this.",["Existenz = existence and Daseyn = existence: one English word for two German words (Existenz/Existenzialsatz; Daseyn).","Realität = reality; wirklich = actual; 'dem Scheine nach' = 'to all appearances' (Schein = illusion/appearance in glossary).","'Satz' = proposition; 'Tavtologie' = tautology; 'behaupten' = assert; 'setzen/sezt' = posit; 'unbestimt' = undetermined (bestimmen = determine).","'es' in 'wiederholt ihr es nur' and 'es sey unter welchem versteckten Namen' left as pronouns; 'es' in 'es sey ... brachtet' refers to the concept of existence or the thing (ambiguous).","'als deren Character eben darauf beruht': 'deren' refers to 'den analytischen'; English 'whose' kept ambiguous as to propositions/judgments.","'billiger maassen' = 'in fairness'; 'elende' = 'miserable'; the final sentence's sentence structure retained, ending without a question mark as in the German."]],"krv-a-1001":["I would indeed hope to bring this rationalizing argutation to nothing, without any circumlocution, through an exact determination of the concept of existence, if I had not found: that the illusion, in the confusion of a logical predicate with a real one, (i.e., the determination of a thing) almost precludes all teaching. Anything one likes can serve as a logical predicate, even the subject can be predicated of itself; for logic abstracts from all content. But the determination is a predicate that is added to the concept of the subject and enlarges it. It must therefore not already be contained in it.",["'grüblerische Argutation' = 'rationalizing argutation' is not exact: grüblerisch is not 'vernünfteln'; 'brooding/subtle' would be nearer, and 'rationalizing' is used here only as the closest glossary item. Doubtful.","Bestimmung = determination; Belehrung = teaching; Inhalt = content; Illusion (Latinate, not Schein) = illusion.","'beinahe alle Belehrung ausschlage': 'ausschlagen' = 'precludes' is a rendering of 'to reject/turn away'; the idiom is obscure and the reading is doubtful.","'Sie' (the determination) and 'ihn' (the concept) in the last sentences kept as pronouns; 'in ihm' ('in it') could refer to 'Begriff' or 'Subiect'."]],"krv-a-1002":["Being is evidently not a real predicate, i.e., a concept of something that could be added to the concept of a thing. It is merely the position of a thing, or of certain determinations in themselves. In the logical use it is solely the copula of a judgment. The proposition: God is omnipotent, contains two concepts, which have their objects: God and omnipotence; the little word: is, is not a predicate in addition on top of that, but only that which posits the predicate in relation to the subject. If now I take the subject (God) together with all its predicates (among which omnipotence also belongs), and say: God is, or there is a God, then I posit no new predicate to the concept of God, but only the subject in itself with all its predicates, and indeed the object in relation to my concept. Both must contain exactly the same thing, and hence nothing further can be added to the concept, which expresses merely the possibility, because I think its object as simply given (through the expression: it is). And thus the actual contains nothing more than the merely possible. A hundred actual thalers contain not the least bit more than a hundred possible ones. For, since these signify the concept, but those the object and its position in itself, in case this one contained more than that one, my concept would not express the whole object and thus would also not be the appropriate concept of it. But in the state of my wealth there is more with a hundred actual thalers than with the mere concept of them (i.e., of their possibility). For the object is, in the case of actuality, not merely contained analytically in my concept, but is added synthetically to my concept (which is a determination of my state), without these thought hundred thalers themselves being increased in the least through this being outside my concept.",["Seyn = being (lower-case Wesen = being is a different German word; both rendered \"being\"); Position left as \"position\"; Prädicat = predicate; Copula = copula.","Gegenstand (object) and Obiect (objects): both \"object\"; here Obiecte occurs once, in the sentence on God and omnipotence.","\"in themselves\" (an sich selbst) in the second sentence: could attach to \"position\" or to \"determinations\"; the English keeps the ambiguity as far as it can. \"oben ein\" = obenein, \"on top of that\".","Wirkliche/Wirklichkeit = actual/actuality; Möglichkeit = possibility; bedeuten = signify; ausdrücken/Ausdruck = express/expression. \"diese\"/\"iene\" rendered \"these\"/\"those\" (and \"this one\"/\"that one\") without resolving the referents.","Vermögenszustand: Vermögen here has the sense of assets or wealth, not \"faculty\" (the glossary rendering); rendered \"state of my wealth\"."]],"krv-a-1003":["If I therefore think a thing through whichever and however many predicates I like (even in thoroughgoing determination), then through my adding further, this thing is, not the least is added to the thing. For otherwise not precisely the same thing, but more would exist than I had thought in the concept, and I could not say: that precisely the object of my concept exists. If I even think in a thing all reality except one, then through my saying, such a deficient thing exists, the missing reality is not added, but it exists burdened with precisely the same deficiency as I have thought it, otherwise something other than what I thought would exist. If I now think for myself a being as the highest reality (without deficiency), the question still always remains: whether it exists or not. For, although nothing is lacking in my concept of the possible real content of a thing in general, something is yet lacking in the relation to my whole state of thinking, namely: that the cognition of that object be also possible *a posteriori*. And here the cause of the difficulty prevailing in this matter also shows itself. If there were talk of an object of the senses, I would not be able to confuse the existence of the thing with the mere concept of the thing. For through the concept the object is thought only as in agreement with the universal conditions of a possible empirical cognition in general, but through the existence as contained in the context of the entire experience; whereupon through the connection with the content of the entire experience the concept of the object is not increased in the least, but our thinking gets one more possible perception through it. If, on the contrary, we want to think existence through the pure category alone, it is no wonder that we can specify no mark to distinguish it from mere possibility.",["Existenz and Daseyn both appear in the group of passages as \"existence\"; here Existenz/existiren = existence/exist.","\"Wesen\" = being (as in \"highest reality\" sentence); Realität = reality; Inhalt = content; Erkentniß = cognition; Wahrnehmung = perception; Verknüpfung = connection; Merkmal = mark.","\"nicht eben dasselbe, sondern mehr existiren\": the subject of \"existiren\" is left as in the German (\"not precisely the same thing but more would exist\"). Gegenstand and Obiect both \"object\".","\"mangelhafte\" rendered \"deficient\"; \"Mangel\" \"deficiency\"; \"behaftet\" \"burdened\"."]],"krv-a-1004":["Our concept of an object may therefore contain whatever and however much it will, yet we must go out beyond it in order to provide existence to this object. With objects of the senses this happens through the interconnection with some one of my perceptions according to empirical laws; but for objects of pure thinking there is entirely and altogether no means to cognize their existence, because it would have to be cognized completely *a priori*, but our consciousness of all existence (whether it be immediately through perception, or through inferences that connect something with the perception) belongs entirely and altogether to the unity of experience, and an existence outside this field cannot indeed be declared absolutely impossible, but it is a presupposition that we cannot justify through anything.",["The source reads \"in ganz und gar kein Mittel\"; \"in\" is silently corrected to \"ist\" (OCR slip, since the clause lacks a verb otherwise).","Zusammenhang = interconnection; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Schlüsse = inferences; Daseyn and Existenz both \"existence\"; \"vor\" = für, rendered \"for\".","\"Gegenständen\" and \"Obiecte\" both \"object(s)\"."]],"krv-a-1005":["The concept of a highest being is, in many an aim, a very useful idea, but precisely because it is merely idea it is entirely incapable of extending, by means of itself alone, our cognition with regard to what exists. It is not even capable of so much that it would teach us with regard to the possibility of something more. The analytic mark of possibility, which consists in this, that mere positions (realities) do not generate any contradiction, cannot indeed be disputed to it; but because the connection of all real properties in one thing is a synthesis, about whose possibility we cannot judge *a priori*, because the realities are not given to us specifically, and, even if this happened, no judgment at all takes place therein, because the mark of the possibility of synthetic cognitions must always be sought only in experience, to which, however, the object of an idea cannot belong, the famous Leibniz has by far not accomplished what he flattered himself with, namely, to want to have insight *a priori* into the possibility of so sublime an ideal being.",["Absicht = aim (\"in mancher Absicht\" rendered \"in many an aim\"); Eigenschaften = properties; Beschaffenheit is not used here.","\"kan ihm zwar nicht gestritten werden\": \"disputed to it\" follows the German dative construction; \"ihm\" refers to the concept/idea (Begriff) or the idea (Idee); left unresolved.","Widerspruch = contradiction; Verknüpfung = connection; Merkmal = mark; Erkentniß = cognition; Wesen = being; Position = position.","The long sentence is kept as one, with its subordinate clauses in German order; \"statt findet\" = \"takes place\"."]],"krv-a-1006":["Thus in the so famous ontological (Cartesian) proof of the existence of a highest being from concepts, all effort and labor is lost, and a human being would presumably become richer in insights from mere ideas just as little as a merchant in wealth, if, in order to improve his state, he wanted to append a few zeros to his cash balance.",["Vermögen here = wealth/assets, not \"faculty\"; Zustand = state; Einsichten = insights; Daseyn = existence; Wesen = being; Beweis = proof.","\"Cassenbestande\" rendered \"cash balance\"; \"wol\" = wohl, rendered \"presumably\"."]],"krv-a-1007":["Of the third chapter, fifth section. On the impossibility of a cosmological proof of the existence of God.",["Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section; Daseyn = existence; Beweis = proof."]],"krv-a-1008":["It was something entirely unnatural and a mere innovation of scholastic wit, to want to pick out, from an entirely arbitrarily drafted idea, the existence of the object corresponding to it itself. In fact one would never have tried it on this path, had not the need of our reason to assume, for existence in general, something necessary (at which one could stop in ascending) preceded, and had not reason, since this necessity must be unconditioned and certain *a priori*, been compelled to seek a concept that, if possible, would satisfy such a demand, and would give an existence to be cognized completely *a priori*. This one believed one now to find in the idea of a most real being, and so this was used only for the more determinate acquaintance with that of which one was already otherwise convinced or persuaded that it must exist, namely the necessary being. Meanwhile one concealed this natural course of reason, and, instead of ending with this concept, one tried to begin from it, in order to derive from it the necessity of existence which it was yet destined only to supplement. From this now arose the failed ontological proof, which brings with it nothing satisfying either for the natural and healthy understanding or for the scholastically correct examination.",["Daseyn and Existenz both \"existence\"; Wesen = being; Gegenstand = object; Kentniß = acquaintance; überzeugt = convinced (Überzeugung), überredet = persuaded (Überredung).","\"ein Gnüge thäte\" = \"would satisfy\" (literally, \"do enough\"); \"zu erkennen gäbe\" rendered \"would give ... to be cognized\"; \"Foderung\" = Forderung, \"demand\".","\"Schulwitzes\" rendered \"scholastic wit\"; \"schulgerechte\" \"scholastically correct\"; \"allerrealesten\" \"most real\"; \"vor\" (= für) rendered \"for\".","\"Diesen\" and \"diese\" are rendered \"This one\" and \"this\" without resolving the referent (the concept); \"Bedürfniß\" rendered \"need\"."]],"krv-a-1009":["The cosmological proof, which we now want to investigate, retains the connection of absolute necessity with the highest reality, but, instead of inferring, like the previous one, from the highest reality to the necessity in existence, it infers rather from the unconditioned necessity of some being, given in advance, to its unbounded reality, and so far brings everything at least into the track of a kind of inference, I do not know whether rationalizing or rational, but at least natural, which carries with it the greatest persuasion not only for the common but also for the speculative understanding, as it also visibly draws the first basic lines for all proofs of natural theology, which one has at all times followed and will further follow, one may decorate and hide them with as much foliage and flourish as one ever wants. This proof, which Leibniz also called the one *a contingentia mundi*, we now want to set before our eyes and subject to examination.",["Beweis = proof; Ueberredung = persuasion; Verstand = understanding; Schlußart rendered 'kind of inference' (Art = kind).","'vernünftig, oder vernünftelnd': vernünftelnd = 'rationalizing' (glossary vernünfteln), kept in contrast with vernünftig = 'rational'.","Schluß-forms: schliessen = infer; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Daseyn = existence; Realität = reality.","'nante' (for nannte) and 'iezt' are period spellings; 'Gleiß' = track (Geleise); 'Grundlinien' rendered 'basic lines'.","'wie sie denn auch ... zieht': the antecedent of the relative 'denen' is 'Grundlinien'; the construction 'man mag ... verzieren' is kept paratactic as in the German."]],"krv-a-1010":["It runs thus: If something exists, then an absolutely necessary being must also exist. Now I myself, at least, exist: therefore an absolutely necessary being exists. The minor premise contains an experience, the major premise the inference from an experience in general to the existence of the necessary*). Thus the proof really begins from experience, hence it is not conducted entirely *a priori*, or ontologically, and because the object of all possible experience is called world, it is therefore called the cosmological proof. Since it also abstracts from every particular property of the objects of experience, by which this world might distinguish itself from every possible one, it is already distinguished in its term from the physico-theological proof, which uses observations of the particular constitution of this our world of sense as grounds of proof.",["Untersatz / Obersatz = minor / major premise; Gegenstand = object (Object not at issue here).","'Schlußfolge' rendered 'inference'; 'Benennung' = term (glossary), 'genant' = 'called'; 'Eigenschaft' = property (Beschaffenheit, 'constitution', is a different word).","'schlechterdingsnothwendig' / 'absolutnothwendig' both rendered 'absolutely necessary' in this passage.","'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of sense'; 'Beweisgründen' = 'grounds of proof'.","The asterisk marks Kant's footnote, which follows as a separate passage."]],"krv-a-1011":["*) This inference is too well known for it to be necessary to set it out here at length. It rests on the supposedly transcendental natural law of causality: that everything contingent has its cause, which, if it is in turn contingent, must just as well have a cause, until the series of causes subordinated to one another must end with an absolutely necessary cause, without which it would have no completeness.",["'Schlußfolge' = inference; 'Ursache' = cause; 'zufällig' = contingent; 'Vollständigkeit' = completeness.","'schlechthinnothwendig' rendered 'absolutely necessary' (as 'schlechterdings-' above).","'Caussalität' = causality (period spelling); 'vermeintlich' = 'supposedly'."]],"krv-a-1012":["Now the proof infers further: the necessary being can be determined only in a single way, i.e., in regard to all possible opposed predicates only through one of them, consequently it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. Now only a single concept of a thing is possible that thoroughly determines it *a priori*, namely that of the *ens realissimum*: Thus the concept of the most real being is the only one through which a necessary being can be thought, i.e., a highest being exists necessarily.",["'durchgängig bestimt' = thoroughly determined; 'Art' = way; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Prädicate' = predicates.","'allerrealesten Wesen' rendered 'most real being' (the 'aller-' intensifier is not separately marked); 'höchstes Wesen' = highest being.","'nothwendiger Weise' = 'necessarily' (lit. 'in a necessary way')."]],"krv-a-1013":["In this cosmological argument so many rationalizing principles come together that speculative reason seems here to have summoned all its dialectical art in order to bring about the greatest possible transcendental illusion. We want, however, to set its examination aside for a while, in order only to make manifest a ruse of it, with which it sets up an old argument in disguised form as a new one and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, namely a pure witness of reason and another of empirical credentials, whereas it is only the former alone that merely changes its dress and voice in order to be taken for a second. In order to lay its ground quite securely, this proof stands on experience and thereby gives itself the appearance of being distinguished from the ontological proof, which places its whole trust in mere pure concepts *a priori*. But the cosmological proof makes use of this experience only in order to take a single step, namely to the existence of a necessary being in general. What properties this has, the empirical ground of proof cannot teach, but there reason takes its leave of it entirely and inquires behind mere concepts: namely what properties an absolutely necessary being in general must have, i.e., which among all possible things contains in itself the required conditions (*requisita*) for an absolute necessity. Now it believes it finds these requisites solely and uniquely in the concept of a most real being, and then infers: that is the absolutely necessary being. But it is clear that one here presupposes that the concept of a being of the highest reality is entirely sufficient for the concept of absolute necessity in existence, i.e., that one can infer from the former to the latter, a proposition that the ontological argument asserted, which one thus accepts in the cosmological proof and lays at its ground, though one had wanted to avoid it. For absolute necessity is an existence from mere concepts. If I now say: the concept of the *ens realissimum* is such a concept and indeed the only one that is fitting to necessary existence and adequate to it, then I must also concede that the latter can be inferred from it. It is thus really only the ontological proof from mere concepts that contains all the force of proof in the so-called cosmological one, and the alleged experience is entirely idle, perhaps only in order to lead us to the concept of absolute necessity, but not in order to demonstrate this in any determinate thing. For as soon as we have this as our aim, we must at once leave all experience and search among pure concepts for which of them might contain the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if in such a way the possibility of such a being is only seen, then its existence is also demonstrated; for it says as much as: among everything possible there is One that carries absolute necessity with it, i.e., this being exists absolutely necessarily.",["'Absicht' = aim; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'Beweisgrund' = ground of proof; 'Beweiskraft' = force of proof; 'Schein' = illusion; 'Eigenschaft' = property; 'Vernunft' = reason.","'vernünftelnde Grundsätze' = 'rationalizing principles'; 'Grundsatz' = principle.","'Zeugen' rendered 'witnesses'; 'einem reinen Vernunftzeugen' = 'a pure witness of reason' (the dative 'einem' after 'zweier Zeugen' kept as in the German).","'vor' is used for 'für/als' in 'vor ein neues', 'vor Eigenschaften', 'vor einen zweiten gehalten': rendered 'as', 'what', 'for' respectively.","'dargethan' / 'darzuthun' = 'demonstrated' / 'demonstrate'; 'eingesehen' = 'seen' (lit. 'seen into').","'ein Satz, den das ontologische Argument behauptete': Satz = proposition; 'behaupten' = assert. 'welches' in 'welches man also ... annimt' has the proposition (or the argument) as possible antecedent; kept ambiguous.","'Requisite'/'requisita': Latin kept in italics; 'schlechterdingsnothwendig', 'absolutnothwendig' both 'absolutely necessary'.","'Daseyn' = existence throughout; 'Möglichkeit' = possibility; 'bestimt' = determinate (adj.)."]],"krv-a-1014":["All illusions of inference are discovered most easily when one sets them before the eyes in a scholastically correct way. Here is such a presentation.",["'Blendwerke' rendered 'illusions' (not Schein, which is 'illusion' in the glossary: one English word for two German words).","'schulgerecht' = 'scholastically correct' (lit. 'school-correct'); the full stop is lacking in the original after 'stellt' and is supplied by the sentence division 'Hier'; the text as printed reads 'Augen stellt Hier'."]],"krv-a-1015":["If the proposition is correct: every absolutely necessary being is at the same time the most real being (as is the *nervus probandi* of the cosmological proof), then it must, like all affirmative judgments, be capable of being converted at least *per accidens*; thus some most real beings are at the same time absolutely necessary beings. Now, however, an *ens realissimum* is not distinguished from another in any respect and, what therefore holds of some contained under this concept, holds also of all. Consequently I shall (in this case) be able to convert also absolutely, i.e., every most real being is a necessary being. Because now this proposition is determined merely from its concepts *a priori*: the mere concept of the most real being must also carry with it the absolute necessity of the same, which is just what the ontological proof asserted and the cosmological would not recognize, but yet laid, though in a concealed way, at the ground of its inferences.",["'neruus probandi' in the print is a u/v spelling for *nervus probandi*, kept in Latin.","'beiahende Urtheile' = 'affirmative judgments' (bejahen = affirm; Urteil = judgment).","'Satz' = proposition (twice); 'Schlüssen' = inferences; 'Begriffen' = concepts.","'schlechthinnothwendig' and 'schlechthin' (adv., 'absolutely') are rendered by 'absolutely'; 'was ... das gilt': 'what ... holds' kept in German order.","'einigen' / 'allen' left as in the German ('some' / 'all'); 'unterlegte' = 'laid at the ground' (cf. 'zum Grunde legt' above).","'anerkennen' = 'recognize' (not erkennen = cognize)."]],"krv-a-1016":["Thus the second way that speculative reason takes in order to prove the existence of the highest being is not only equally deceptive with the first, but has this further blameworthy feature in it, that it commits an *ignoratio elenchi*, in that it promises to lead us on a new footpath, but, after a small detour, brings us back again to the old one that we had left for its sake.",["'Daseyn' = existence; 'Vernunft' = reason; 'trüglich' = 'deceptive'.","'seinetwegen' rendered 'for its sake'; 'ignoratio elenchi' kept in Latin italics."]],"krv-a-1017":["I said a little earlier: that in this cosmological argument a whole nest of dialectical presumptions keeps itself hidden, which transcendental critique can easily discover and destroy. I will now only cite them and leave it to the already practiced reader to investigate the deceptive principles further and to cancel them.",["'Critik' = critique; 'Anmassungen' = 'presumptions'; 'Grundsätze' = principles; 'aufheben' = 'cancel'.","'sie' in 'ich will sie iezt nur anführen' refers back to 'Anmassungen' (feminine plural); 'schon geübten' = 'already practiced'."]],"krv-a-1018":["There are found, then, e.g., 1. the transcendental principle: to infer from the contingent to a cause, which is of significance only in the world of sense, but outside it does not even have a sense. For the merely intellectual concept of the contingent cannot produce any synthetic proposition, such as that of causality, and the principle of the latter has no significance and no mark of its use except only in the world of sense; but here it was supposed precisely to serve to get beyond the world of sense. 2. The inference, from the impossibility of an infinite series of causes given one above another in the world of sense, to infer to a first cause, to which the principles of the use of reason do not entitle us even in experience, much less can extend this principle beyond it (to where this chain cannot be lengthened at all). 3. The false self-satisfaction of reason, with regard to the completion of this series, by this: that one finally does away with all condition, without which yet no concept of a necessity can take place, and, since one then can comprehend nothing further, takes this for a completion of one's concept. 4. The confusion of the logical possibility of a concept of all united reality (without inner contradiction) with the transcendental one, which requires a *principium* of the feasibility of such a synthesis, which, however, can in turn go only to the field of possible experiences, etc.",["Grundsatz = principle; Principium/Principien (Latinate) also = principle/principles. One English word for two German words.","Sinn (sense) and Bedeutung (significance) are kept distinct; Sinnenwelt = world of sense.","Item 2: the German sentence has no finite main verb and is syntactically incomplete ('vielweniger ... ausdehnen können' has no clear subject); rendered as it stands, not repaired.","Thunlichkeit = feasibility; begreifen = comprehend; Schluß/schliessen = inference/infer."]],"krv-a-1019":["The trick of the cosmological proof aims merely at this, in order to evade the proof of the existence of a necessary being *a priori* through mere concepts, which would have to be conducted ontologically, for which, however, we feel ourselves wholly incapable. With this intention we infer from an actual existence laid as the ground (an experience in general), as well as it will let itself be done, to some absolutely necessary condition of the same. We then do not need to explain the possibility of this one. For if it has been proved that it is there, then the question concerning its possibility is entirely unnecessary. If we now want to determine this necessary being more closely according to its constitution, then we do not seek that which is sufficient to comprehend the necessity of existence from its concept, for if we could do this, we would need no empirical presupposition; no, we seek only the negative condition (*conditio sine qua non*), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would well be feasible in every other kind of inference, from a given sequel to its ground; but here it unfortunately happens that the condition that one demands for absolute necessity can be encountered only in a single being, which therefore would have to contain in its concept everything that is requisite for absolute necessity, and thus makes possible an inference *a priori* to it, i.e., I would also have to be able to infer conversely: to whatever thing this concept (of the highest reality) belongs, that is absolutely necessary, and if I cannot infer thus (as I must indeed confess this if I want to avoid the ontological proof), then I have also come to grief on my new path and find myself again there from where I set out. The concept of the highest being does indeed satisfy all questions *a priori* that can be raised concerning the inner determinations of a thing, and is therefore also an ideal without equal, because the general concept at the same time distinguishes it as an individual among all possible things. But it does not satisfy at all the question concerning its own existence, about which, after all, it was really only a matter, and one could not answer the inquiry of him who assumed the existence of a necessary being and only wanted to know which then among all things must be regarded as such: This here is the necessary being.",["Daseyn = existence (also wirkliches Daseyn = actual existence); Wesen = being; Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Bestimmungen = determinations.","Dieser ihre Möglichkeit: colloquial genitive ('this one's possibility'); the referent is the feminine Bedingung. Likewise 'sie' and 'ihrer' are rendered 'it'/'its'.","The relative 'der' after 'Beweise ... ontologisch geführt werden müßte' could attach to Beweis or to Daseyn; English 'which' preserves this looseness.","'thut ... ein Gnüge' rendered 'satisfy'; 'als warum es doch eigentlich nur zu thun war' kept close ('about which ... it was really only a matter').","Latin phrase *conditio sine qua non* is Kant's own."]],"krv-a-1020":["It may well be permitted to assume the existence of a being of the highest sufficiency, as cause of all possible effects, in order to facilitate for reason the unity of the grounds of explanation that it seeks. But to take so much upon oneself: that one even says: such a being exists necessarily, is no longer the modest utterance of a permitted hypothesis, but the brazen presumption of an apodictic certainty; for of what one pretends to cognize as absolutely necessary, the cognition must also carry absolute necessity with it.",["Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency (not in the glossary; chosen as the plain cognate-equivalent); Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation.","erkennen/Erkentniß = cognize/cognition; schlechthinnothwendig = absolutely necessary.","'Daseyn' (existence) and 'existirt' (exists) both occur; Dasein and Existenz are both 'existence' in the glossary."]],"krv-a-1021":["The whole problem of the transcendental ideal comes down to this: either to find for absolute necessity a concept, or to find for the concept of any thing the absolute necessity of the same. If one can do the one, one must also be able to do the other; for reason cognizes as absolutely necessary only that which is necessary from its concept. But both wholly exceed all utmost endeavors to satisfy our understanding on this point, but also all attempts to calm it on account of this its incapacity.",["Aufgabe = problem; Verstand = understanding; erkennt = cognizes.","'aber ... aber auch' kept as 'but ... but also'; 'alle äusserste Bestrebungen' kept as 'all utmost endeavors'.","Unvermögen = incapacity (not in the glossary)."]],"krv-a-1022":["The unconditioned necessity, which we need so indispensably as the ultimate bearer of all things, is the true abyss for human reason. Even eternity, however dreadfully sublime a Haller may depict it, by far does not make the dizzying impression on the mind; for it only measures the duration of things, but does not bear them. One cannot ward off the thought, but one also cannot bear it: that a being, which we also represent to ourselves as the highest among all possible ones, should say, as it were, to itself: I am from eternity to eternity, outside me is nothing, except what is something merely through my will; but whence am I then? Here everything sinks beneath us, and the greatest perfection, like the smallest, hovers without support merely before speculative reason, for which it would cost nothing to let the one as well as the other vanish without the least hindrance.",["'schauderhafterhaben' is printed as one word in the source; read as schauderhaft + erhaben ('dreadfully sublime'), not emended.","'macht lange ... nicht' rendered 'by far ... not'; Eindruck = impression; Gemüt = mind; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","'sage' and 'koste' are subjunctive in the German; rendered with 'should say' and 'would cost'.","'Gedanken' (genitive without final -n in modern form) is read as 'des Gedanken' = of the thought; Haller = Albrecht von Haller."]],"krv-a-1023":["Many forces of nature, which express their existence through certain effects, remain inscrutable to us; for we cannot trace them far enough by observation. The transcendental object lying at the ground of appearances and, with it, the ground why our sensibility has these rather than other supreme conditions, are and remain inscrutable to us, although the thing itself is otherwise given, but only not seen into. But an ideal of pure reason cannot be called inscrutable, because it has no further credential of its reality to show than the need of reason to complete all synthetic unity by means of it. Since it is thus not even given as a thinkable object, it is also not inscrutable as such, rather it must, as mere idea, find its seat and its resolution in the nature of reason and thus be able to be investigated; for reason consists precisely in this: that we can give an account of all our concepts, opinions and assertions, whether from objective grounds, or, if they are a mere illusion, from subjective grounds.",["Obiect (transcendentale Obiect) and Gegenstand (denkbarer Gegenstand) are both 'object'; the Latinate form is Obiect.","unerforschlich = inscrutable; erforscht werden = be investigated; 'eingesehen' = seen into (einsehen, 'have insight into').","Gender shift in the German is kept: 'es' (Ideal) then 'er' (Gegenstand/Ideal); both rendered 'it'.","Kräfte = forces; Erscheinungen = appearances; Schein = illusion; Behauptungen = assertions; Meinungen = opinions; Bedürfniß appears with feminine article 'die' in the source."]],"krv-a-1024":["Discovery and explanation of the dialectical illusion in all transcendental proofs of the existence of a necessary being.",["Heading; Erklärung = explanation; Schein = illusion; Beweisen = proofs."]],"krv-a-1025":["Both proofs conducted thus far were transcendental, i.e., attempted independently of empirical principles. For, although the cosmological one lays an experience in general at the basis, it is nevertheless conducted not from any particular constitution of the same, but from pure principles of reason, in relation to an existence given through empirical consciousness in general, and even abandons this guidance in order to support itself on mere pure concepts. What now is the cause, in these transcendental proofs, of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the concepts of necessity and of highest reality and realizes and hypostasizes that which yet can only be an idea? What is the cause of the unavoidability of assuming something among existing things as necessary in itself, and yet at the same time of shrinking back from the existence of such a being as from an abyss, and how does one go about it that reason may understand itself concerning this and from the wavering state of a timid and ever again withdrawn approval attain to tranquil insight?",["Beweis = proof; Principien = principles (Principium/Principien kept as cognate); Vernunftprincipien = principles of reason.","Begriff = concept; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Bewustseyn = consciousness; Existenz = existence; Schein = illusion.","Original 'empiriche' is a misprint for 'empirische'; silently corrected. 'Bollständigkeit' etc. not in this passage.","'Beifall' rendered 'approval'; not in the glossary.","'Anleitung' rendered 'guidance'; 'Abgrund' (here 'Abgrunde') 'abyss'.","'daß sich die Vernunft hierüber selbst verstehe': 'hierüber' kept as 'concerning this'; its reference is left open as in the German."]],"krv-a-1026":["It is something exceedingly remarkable that, if one presupposes that something exists, one cannot avoid the consequence that something also exists necessarily. On this entirely natural (though for that reason not yet certain) inference the cosmological argument rested. On the other hand, whatever concept of a thing I may assume, I find that its existence can never be represented by me as absolutely necessary, and that nothing hinders me, whatever may exist, from thinking the non-being of the same, hence that I must indeed assume something necessary for the existing in general, but cannot think any single thing itself as necessary in itself: That is to say: I can never complete the regress to the conditions of existing without assuming a necessary being, but I can never begin from the same.",["Folgerung = consequence; Schluß = inference; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Existiren/existiren = exist(ing); Existirenden = the existing.","Nichtseyn = non-being; Wesen = being; Bedingungen = conditions.","'zu dem Existirenden überhaupt' rendered 'for the existing in general'; 'zu' could also be read 'in addition to'; the German is ambiguous.","'Umgang haben' = 'avoid' (idiom, 'Umgang haben von'), i.e., 'cannot avoid'.","'von demselben': 'from the same' (the necessary being); 'demselben' could also in principle refer back to something else."]],"krv-a-1027":["If I must think something necessary for existing things in general, but am not authorized to think any thing in itself as necessary, it follows unavoidably from this that necessity and contingency must not concern and affect the things themselves, because otherwise a contradiction would occur, hence that neither of these two principles is objective, but that they can at most only be subjective principles of reason, namely, on the one side, to seek for everything that is given as existing something that is necessary, i.e., never to stop anywhere else than with an explanation completed a priori, but on the other side also never to hope for this completion, i.e., to assume nothing empirical as unconditioned, and thereby to exempt oneself from further derivation. In such a signification both principles, as merely heuristic and regulative, which care for nothing but the formal interest of reason, can very well subsist alongside one another. For the one says, you are to philosophize about nature as if there were a necessary first ground for everything that belongs to existence, solely in order to bring systematic unity into your cognition, in that you pursue such an idea, namely an imagined highest ground: but the other warns you not to assume any single determination that concerns the existence of things as such a highest ground, i.e., as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way to further derivation still always open to yourselves and therefore always to treat it still as conditioned. But if, for us, everything that is perceived in things must be considered as conditionedly necessary: then no thing (that may be given empirically) can be regarded as absolutely necessary.",["Zufälligkeit = contingency; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Grundsatz = principle (Grundsätze), Principien/Principium also 'principle(s)'; one English word for two German words.","Bedeutung = signification here (glossary: 'signify/significance; meaning'); 'Bedeutung' rendered 'signification' to keep it distinct; Erkentniß = cognition.","'a priori' left in Latin, italics not given in the source; 'a priori vollendeten Erklärung' may attach to 'completed' or to 'explanation'; kept ambiguous.","'sie' in 'sie daher iederzeit noch als bedingt zu behandeln' rendered 'it'; the German feminine singular/plural referent is unclear (Bestimmung vs. Dinge).","'vor' in 'vor einen solchen obersten Grund' = 'für'; rendered 'as'.","'bedingtnothwendig' = 'conditionedly necessary'; 'absolutnothwendig' = 'absolutely necessary'; 'wahrgenommen' = 'perceived' (Wahrnehmung = perception).","'Ableitung' = derivation; 'Einsicht' not here; 'Erklärung' = explanation (alternatively 'definition')."]],"krv-a-1028":["But it follows from this: that you must assume the absolutely necessary outside the world; because it is supposed to serve only as a principle of the greatest possible unity of the appearances, as their highest ground, and you can never get there in the world, because the second rule commands you always to regard all empirical causes of unity as derived.",["Erscheinungen = appearances; Welt = world; Princip = principle; Ursache = cause; Einheit = unity.","'dahin gelangen' = 'get there'; 'dahin' left without explicit referent as in the German.","'könt' (könnt) silently modernized in sense."]],"krv-a-1029":["The philosophers of antiquity regarded all form of nature as contingent, but matter, according to the judgment of common reason, as original and necessary. But had they considered matter not respectively as substratum of the appearances, but in itself according to its existence, the idea of absolute necessity would at once have vanished. For there is nothing that binds reason to this existence absolutely, but it can always, and without conflict, annul it in thought; but in thought alone lay also the absolute necessity. There must therefore have lain at the basis of this persuasion a certain regulative principle. In fact extension and impenetrability (which together make up the concept of matter) is also the highest empirical principium of the unity of the appearances and has, insofar as it is empirically unconditioned, a property of the regulative principle in itself. Nevertheless, since every determination of matter, which makes up the real of the same, hence also impenetrability, is an effect (action) that must have its cause and is therefore still always derived, matter does not suit the idea of a necessary being, as a principle of all derived unity; because each of its real properties, as derived, is only conditionedly necessary and can therefore be annulled in itself, but with this the whole existence of matter would be annulled, but if this did not happen, we would have reached the highest ground of unity empirically, which is forbidden by the second regulative principle, it follows: that matter, and in general whatever belongs to the world, is not suited to the idea of a necessary original being, as a mere principle of the greatest empirical unity, but that it must be posited outside the world, whereupon we can always confidently derive the appearances of the world and their existence from others, as if there were no necessary being, and yet can strive unceasingly toward the completeness of the derivation, as if such a being, as a highest ground, were presupposed.",["Ueberredung = persuasion (Überredung); Ausdehnung = extension; Wirkung = effect; Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit not used here); Bestimmung = determination.","'Idee' = idea; 'Substratum', 'Principium' kept in Latin form as in the text; Latin italics not marked in the source, so not italicized.","'Aufheben' rendered 'annul'; 'respectiv' = 'respectively' (literal; sense 'relatively' left open).","'ist' after 'Ausdehnung und Undurchdringlichkeit' is singular in the German; kept as 'is'.","'Bollständigkeit' in the OCR is 'Vollständigkeit'; silently corrected: 'completeness'. Reality: 'das Reale' = 'the real'.","'wenn dieses aber nicht geschähe ... so folgt' keeps the long sentence with its run-on construction; 'derived from others' = 'von anderen ableiten'.","'Urwesen' = 'original being'; 'ursprünglich' = 'original'."]],"krv-a-1030":["The ideal of the highest being is, according to these considerations, nothing other than a regulative principle of reason, to regard all combination in the world as if it arose from an all-sufficient necessary cause, in order to ground upon this the rule of a systematic unity, necessary according to universal laws, in the explanation of the same, and is not an assertion of an existence necessary in itself. But it is at the same time unavoidable, by means of a transcendental subreption, to represent this formal principle to oneself as constitutive and to think this unity hypostatically. For, just as space, because it makes all shapes, which are merely different limitations of it, originally possible, although it is only a principium of sensibility, is nevertheless for precisely that reason held to be an absolutely necessary something subsisting for itself and an object given a priori in itself, so it also goes quite naturally: that, since the systematic unity of nature can in no way be set up as the principle of the empirical use of our reason except insofar as we lay the idea of a most real being, as the highest cause, at the basis, this idea is thereby represented as an actual object and this in turn, because it is the highest condition, as necessary, hence a regulative principle is transformed into a constitutive one, which substitution reveals itself thereby: that, if I now consider this highest being, which in respect of the world was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as thing for itself, this necessity is capable of no concept, and must therefore have been to be met with in my reason only as formal condition of thinking, but not as material and hypostatic condition of existence.",["Verbindung = combination; Betrachtungen = considerations; Behauptung = assertion; Gesetz = law; Gestalt = shape; Einschränkung = limitation.","'allgnugsamen' = 'all-sufficient' (allgenugsam); not the glossary's 'zureichend'.","'Subreption' and 'Unterschiebung' (substitution): two German words, two English words kept distinct.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'Ding' = thing; 'vor sich' = 'for itself' (vor = für), and 'für sich bestehend' = subsisting for itself.","'allerrealesten' = 'most real'; 'wirklicher' = 'actual'; 'Realität' not used here.","'nothwendig war' kept in the past tense as in the German; 'anzutreffen gewesen seyn müsse' rendered literally as 'must have been to be met with'.","'a priori' in the text is not marked as Latin in the OCR; left unitalicized."]],"krv-a-1031":["Of the third chapter, sixth section. On the impossibility of the physico-theological proof.",["Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section; Beweis = proof.","'physicotheologischen' rendered 'physico-theological'."]],"krv-a-1032":["If, then, neither the concept of things in general nor the experience of any existence in general can accomplish what is demanded, there still remains one means left, to try whether a determinate experience, hence that of the things of the present world, their constitution and arrangement, may not furnish a ground of proof that could reliably help us to the conviction of the existence of a highest being. We would call such a proof the physico-theological one. Should this too be impossible, then there is no satisfactory proof at all from merely speculative reason for the existence of a being that would correspond to our transcendental idea.",["Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Beweisgrund rendered 'ground of proof' (Beweis + Grund); bestimte = 'determinate'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Anordnung = 'arrangement'.","'vor das Daseyn' (= für) rendered 'for'; 'überall' rendered 'at all'."]],"krv-a-1033":["After all the above remarks one will soon see that the answer to this inquiry can be expected quite easily and concisely. For how can an experience ever be given that should be adequate to an idea? The peculiarity of the latter consists precisely in this, that no experience whatever can ever be congruent with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary all-sufficient original being is so overreaching great, so exalted high above everything empirical, which is at all times conditioned, that partly one can never scrape together enough material in experience to fill such a concept, partly one always gropes around among the conditioned and will constantly seek in vain for the unconditioned, of which no law of any empirical synthesis gives us an example, or the slightest guidance toward it.",["Stoff = 'material'; Urwesen = 'original being'; überschwenglich = 'overreaching'; Leitung = 'guidance'.","The German passes from singular 'man' to plural 'werden' ('suchen werden'); the English keeps 'one' and leaves the shift unmarked in the text.","'Bescheid' = 'answer'; 'Nachfrage' = 'inquiry'; 'congruiren' = 'be congruent'."]],"krv-a-1034":["Were the highest being to stand in this chain of conditions, it would itself be a member of the series of them and, just like the lower members, to which it is prefixed, would require further investigation on account of its still higher ground. If one wants, on the other hand, to separate it from this chain and, as a merely intelligible being, not to comprehend it along with in the series of natural causes: what bridge can reason then well build in order to arrive at it? Since all laws of the transition from effects to causes, indeed all synthesis and expansion of our cognition in general are set on nothing other than possible experience, hence merely on objects of the world of sense, and can have a significance only with regard to them.",["The passage ends with 'Da ... können' as a sentence without a main clause; kept as it stands.","Glied = 'member' throughout; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Erweiterung = 'expansion' (not Ausdehnung = extension); Gegenstände = 'objects'; Bedeutung = 'significance'; Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'.","'mit begreifen' = 'comprehend along with' (begreifen = comprehend), kept literal."]],"krv-a-1035":["The present world opens up to us such an immeasurable theater of manifoldness, order, purposiveness and beauty, whether one may now pursue these in the infinity of space or in the unbounded division of it, that even according to the knowledge that our weak understanding has been able to acquire of it, all language, with regard to so many and immeasurably great wonders, loses its emphasis, all numbers their power to measure, and even our thoughts all boundary, so that our judgment of the whole must dissolve into a speechless, but all the more eloquent, astonishment. Everywhere we see a chain of effects and causes, of ends and the means, regularity in arising or passing away, and, since nothing has of itself entered into the state in which it finds itself, it points ever further toward another thing, as its cause, which makes necessary precisely the same further inquiry, so that in such a way the whole All would have to sink into the abyss of nothingness, did one not assume something that, subsisting outside this infinite contingent, originally and independently for itself, held it and, as the cause of its origin, at the same time secured its continuance for it. This highest cause (with regard to all things of the world) how great should one think it? We do not know the world according to its whole content, still less do we know how to estimate its magnitude by comparison with everything that is possible. But what hinders us, since we once need, with regard to causality, an extreme and supreme being, from not at the same time positing it, according to the degree of perfection, above everything else possible, which we can accomplish easily, though admittedly only through the delicate outline of an abstract concept, if we represent to ourselves in it, as one single substance, all possible perfection united, which concept is favorable to the demand of our reason in the parsimony of principles, subject in itself to no contradictions, and even conducive to the expansion of the use of reason in the midst of experience, through the guidance that such an idea gives toward order and purposiveness, but nowhere contrary to an experience in a decided way.",["'er' in 'so weiset er' refers back to 'Zustand' (masculine); rendered 'it', leaving the antecedent as open as in the German.","kennen = 'know' (noscere) and wissen = 'know (how)': one English word for two German words.","Zweckmässigkeit = 'purposiveness'; Zufälligen = 'contingent'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Widersprüchen = 'contradictions'; Zahlen = 'numbers'; Kentnissen = 'knowledge'; Inhalt = 'content'; Grösse = 'magnitude'; Zweck = 'end'; Absicht = 'aim' (here 'in Absicht auf' rendered 'with regard to'); Caussalität = 'causality'.","'vermissen' rendered 'lose' / 'lack' by ellipsis in the English ('loses ... all boundary'); the German verb is 'miss'. 'Principien' (Latinate) and 'Grundsätze' (1039) both = 'principle(s)'.","'auf entschiedene Art' = 'in a decided way'; 'Erspahrung' = 'parsimony' (Ersparung); 'unabsehlichgrosse' = 'immeasurably great' (literally 'unforeseeably great')."]],"krv-a-1036":["This proof deserves always to be named with respect. It is the oldest, clearest and the most appropriate to common human reason. It enlivens the study of nature, just as it has its existence from this and thereby constantly receives new force. It brings ends and aims to where our observation would not have discovered them of itself, and expands our knowledge of nature through the guiding thread of a particular unity, the principle of which is outside nature. This knowledge, however, acts back again on its cause, namely the occasioning idea, and increases the belief in a highest author to an irresistible conviction.",["'erweiterr' in the source is read as 'erweitert' (OCR/print slip).","Kentnisse = 'knowledge' (plural 'Diese Kentnisse' rendered with the singular 'This knowledge'); Zwecke = 'ends', Absichten = 'aims'; Daseyn = 'existence'; Glaube = 'belief'; Ueberzeugung = 'conviction'; Kraft = 'force'; besonderen = 'particular'."]],"krv-a-1037":["It would therefore be not only disconsolate but also entirely in vain to want to detract anything from the authority of this proof. Reason, which is unceasingly elevated by such powerful grounds of proof, growing ever under its hands, though only empirical, cannot be so depressed by any doubts of subtle abstracted speculation that it should not be torn out of every brooding indecision, as if out of a dream, by a glance that it casts at the wonders of nature and at the majesty of the world-structure, in order to elevate itself from magnitude to magnitude up to the most high, from the conditioned to the condition, up to the supreme and unconditioned author.",["Beweisgründe = 'grounds of proof'; Weltbau = 'world-structure'; Grösse = 'magnitude'; Bedingung = 'condition'; bedingt = 'conditioned'.","'Maiestät' = 'majesty' (old spelling)."]],"krv-a-1038":["But although we have nothing to object against the conformity to reason and utility of this procedure, but rather to recommend and encourage it, we can nevertheless not approve the claims that this kind of proof might make to apodictic certainty and to an assent needing no favor or foreign support whatever, and it can in no way harm the good cause to tune down the dogmatic language of a scornfully pronouncing rationalizer to the tone of moderation and modesty, of a belief sufficient for tranquillity, though not precisely commanding unconditional submission. I assert accordingly: that the physico-theological proof can never demonstrate the existence of a highest being alone, but must always leave it to the ontological (to which it serves only as introduction) to supplement this deficiency, hence that this still always contains the only possible ground of proof (provided only that a speculative proof takes place at all), which no human reason can pass by.",["Vernünftler = 'rationalizer' (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize); Vernunftmässigkeit = 'conformity to reason'; behaupten = 'assert'; Glaube = 'belief'; Beweisgrund = 'ground of proof'.","'hinreichend' rendered 'sufficient' (the glossary's zureichend also = 'sufficient'); 'darthun' = 'demonstrate'; Beifall = 'assent'; Mangel = 'deficiency'.","The construction 'mithin dieser ... enthalte' is kept dependent on 'Ich behaupte ... daß', as in the German."]],"krv-a-1039":["The main moments of the said physico-theological proof are the following: 1. In the world there are found everywhere distinct signs of an arrangement in accordance with determinate aim, carried out with great wisdom and in a whole of indescribable manifoldness of content as well as unbounded magnitude of domain; 2. To the things of the world this purposive arrangement is entirely foreign and attaches to them only contingently, i.e., the nature of different things could not of itself, through so many kinds of uniting means, agree together toward determinate final aims, had they not been quite properly chosen and set up for it by an arranging rational principle, in accordance with ideas lying at the ground. 3. There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which must be the cause of the world not merely as blindly working all-capable nature, through fertility, but as intelligence, through freedom. 4. The unity of it can be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation of the parts of the world, as members of an artificial edifice, in that to which our observation reaches, with certainty, but beyond that, according to all principles of analogy, with probability.",["deutlich = 'distinct'; Absicht = 'aim'; Endabsichten = 'final aims'; Mannigfaltigkeit = 'manifoldness'; Umfang = 'domain'; Grösse = 'magnitude'; zufällig = 'contingently'; Wahrscheinlichkeit = 'probability'; Grundsätze = 'principles'; Princip = 'principle' (Latinate/German pair both 'principle').","'schliessen' rendered 'infer' (Schluß = inference); 'allvermögende' = 'all-capable' (Vermögen = faculty; capacity).","'ganz eigentlich' rendered 'quite properly'; 'zum Grunde liegenden' = 'lying at the ground'; 'Denen Dingen' is dative, 'To the things'.","'1.' to '4.' kept as in the source within one paragraph; 'physischtheologischen' = 'physico-theological'; 'Hauptmomente' = 'main moments'."]],"krv-a-1040":["Without here wanting to chicane with natural reason about its inference, since it infers from the analogy of some natural products with that which human art produces when it does violence to nature and compels it not to proceed according to its ends but to adapt itself to ours, (the similarity of them to houses, ships, clocks) that just such a causality, namely understanding and will, will lie at the ground of it, when it derives the inner possibility of freely effecting nature (which first makes possible all art and perhaps even reason itself) from yet another, albeit superhuman art, which kind of inference perhaps the sharpest transc. critique would not be able to withstand, one must nevertheless admit that, if we are once to name a cause, we cannot proceed here more securely than according to the analogy with such purposive generations, which are the only ones of which the causes and manner of effect are fully known to us. Reason would not be able to answer for it to itself, if it wanted to pass over from the causality that it knows to obscure and indemonstrable grounds of explanation that it does not know.",["Schluß = inference; Schlußart = kind of inference; Zweck = end (zweckmässig = purposive); Grund = ground; Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation; Wirkungsart = manner of effect; Verstand = understanding.","'transsc.' is rendered as the abbreviation 'transc.' (transscendental).","'kent' and 'bekant' are old spellings of kennt and bekannt: 'knows', 'known' (kennen).","'sie' in 'wenn sie ... ableitet' and in the last sentence is ambiguous between reason and nature; 'it' is kept. 'ihr' in 'bey ihr zum Grunde liegen' is rendered 'of it'.","'entgegen' and 'Gewalt thut' etc. are kept literal; the parenthesis '(der Aehnlichkeit ... Uhren)' stands where the German has it."]],"krv-a-1041":["According to this inference the purposiveness and harmoniousness of so many arrangements of nature would prove merely the contingency of the form, but not of the matter, i.e., of the substance in the world; for to the latter it would further be required that it could be proved: the things of the world would be in themselves unfit for such order and concord, according to universal laws, if they were not, even as to their substance, the product of a highest wisdom, for which, however, entirely different grounds of proof than those from the analogy with human art would be required. The proof could thus at most demonstrate a world-architect, who would always be very much limited by the fitness of the material on which he works, but not a world-creator, to whose idea everything is subject, which is by far not sufficient for the great aim that one has before one's eyes, namely to prove an all-sufficient original being. If we wanted to prove the contingency of matter itself, we would have to take our refuge in a transcendental argument, which, however, was precisely to have been avoided here.",["'undeckmässigkeit' is an OCR slip, silently read as 'Zweckmässigkeit' (purposiveness).","Materie = matter; Stoff = material (one English word each, kept distinct). Zufälligkeit = contingency.","Wolgereimtheit = 'harmoniousness' (literally 'well-rhymedness'); Einstimmung = 'concord'; Anstalten = 'arrangements'.","allgnugsam = 'all-sufficient'; Urwesen = 'original being'; Weltbaumeister / Weltschöpfer = 'world-architect' / 'world-creator'.","'an sich selbst' = 'in themselves'. 'Absicht' = 'aim'."]],"krv-a-1042":["The inference thus proceeds from the order and purposiveness so thoroughly to be observed in the world, as a wholly contingent arrangement, to the existence of a cause proportioned to it. But the concept of this cause must give us to cognize something quite determinate about it and it can thus be none other than that of a being that possesses all might, wisdom, etc., in a word all perfection, as an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, of astonishing, of immeasurable might and excellence give no determinate concept at all and do not really say what the thing is in itself, but are only representations of relation of the magnitude of the object, which the observer (of the world) compares with himself and his power of grasping and which turn out equally high-praising whether one magnifies the object or makes the observing subject smaller in relation to it. Where it comes down to magnitude (of perfection) of a thing in general, there is no determinate concept except that which comprehends the whole possible perfection, and only the All (*omnitudo*) of reality is thoroughly determined in the concept.",["bestimmt = 'determinate' (adj.), bestimmen = 'determine'; 'durchgängig bestimmt' = 'thoroughly determined'; 'bestimmend' = 'determining'.","Einrichtung = 'arrangement' (as is Anstalt in the preceding passage; both are 'arrangement' here by necessity of the plain cognate); Macht = 'might'; Treflichkeit = 'excellence'.","Verhältnißvorstellungen = 'representations of relation'; Fassungskraft = 'power of grasping' (distinct from begreifen = comprehend, used in 'begreift').","Gegenstand = 'object'; Grösse = 'magnitude'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Realität = 'reality'. *omnitudo* is Kant's own Latin."]],"krv-a-1043":["Now I do not want to hope that anyone should venture to have insight into the relation of the magnitude of the world observed by him (in domain as well as content) to omnipotence, of the world-order to the highest wisdom, of the world-unity to the absolute unity of the author, etc. Thus physico-theology cannot give a determinate concept of the supreme world-cause and therefore cannot be sufficient for a principle of theology, which in turn is supposed to constitute the foundation of religion.",["Umfang = 'domain'; Inhalt = 'content'; Einheit = 'unity'; Urheber = 'author'.","'Physicotheologie' is rendered 'physico-theology'; 'Grundlage' = 'foundation'.","'Princip' = 'principle' (Grundsatz and Princip would both be 'principle')."]],"krv-a-1044":["The step to absolute totality is entirely and utterly impossible by the empirical path. Now one nevertheless takes it in the physico-theological proof. Of what means, then, does one avail oneself, I wonder, to get across so wide a gulf?",["Totalität = 'totality'; Mittel = 'means'; Beweis = 'proof'.","'Nun thut man ihn doch aber' is rendered 'Now one nevertheless takes it'; 'wol' (wohl) in 'wol' is rendered 'I wonder'.","'Kluft' = 'gulf'."]],"krv-a-1045":["After one has arrived at admiration of the magnitude of the wisdom, the might, etc., of the author of the world and cannot come further, one abandons all at once this argument conducted through empirical grounds of proof and goes to the contingency of the world, inferred right at the beginning from its order and purposiveness. From this contingency alone one now goes, solely through transcendental concepts, to the existence of an absolutely necessary and from the concept of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the thoroughly determinate, or determining concept of it, namely of an all-encompassing reality. Thus the physico-theological proof got stuck in its undertaking, in this embarrassment suddenly jumped over to the cosmological proof and, since this is only a concealed ontological proof, it actually accomplished its aim merely through pure reason, although at the beginning it had disavowed all kinship with it and had staked everything on evident proofs from experience.",["'Schlechthinnothwendigen' is a substantivized adjective: 'an absolutely necessary' with no noun supplied.","'welcher ... des Welturhebers' etc.: Welturheber = 'author of the world'. 'Beweisgründe' = 'grounds of proof'.","Verlegenheit = 'embarrassment'; Absicht = 'aim'; Verwandschaft = 'kinship'; einleuchtend = 'evident'.","'Argument' kept as 'argument'; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'; 'allbefassend' = 'all-encompassing'."]],"krv-a-1046":["The physico-theologians thus have no cause at all to act so prudish toward the transcendental kind of proof and to look down upon it with the self-conceit of clear-sighted knowers of nature, as upon the cobweb of obscure brooders. For if they would only examine themselves, they would find that, after they have gone a good stretch on the ground of nature and experience and nevertheless still see themselves just as far from the object that shines toward their reason, they suddenly abandon this ground and pass over into the realm of mere possibilities, where on the wings of ideas they hope to come near to that which had withdrawn from all their empirical search. After they finally believe to have gained firm footing through so mighty a leap, they spread the now determinate concept (into possession of which they have come without knowing how) over the whole field of creation and elucidate the ideal, which was merely a product of pure reason, though wretchedly enough and far beneath the dignity of its object, through experience, without wanting to admit, however, that they have arrived at this knowledge or presupposition by another footpath than that of experience.",["'Ursache' is 'cause' (as 'Ursache haben' = 'to have cause'); Beweisart = 'kind of proof'; Naturkenner = 'knowers of nature' (kennen = know).","'entgegen scheint' is kept literal ('shines toward'); it could be read as 'appears opposite/to meet' their reason.","'Kentniß' = 'knowledge' (Kenntnis); 'Erläutern' = 'elucidate'; Idee = 'idea'; Ideal = 'ideal'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility'.","'dessen Besitz ... gekommen sind' kept in the parenthesis as in the German; 'obzwar' clause attaches to 'erläutern'."]],"krv-a-1047":["Thus there lies at the ground of the physico-theological proof the cosmological, but at the ground of this the ontological proof, of the existence of a single original being as highest being, and since besides these three paths none is open any more to speculative reason: so the ontological proof, from sheer pure concepts of reason, is the only possible one, if indeed any proof at all is possible of a proposition so far elevated above all empirical use of the understanding.",["The period after 'Beweis' in 'ontologische Beweis. vom Daseyn' is probably an OCR/typographical slip; rendered with a comma. 'vom Daseyn ...' could attach to 'ontological proof' alone or to all three proofs; the English leaves this open.","'einigen' = 'single'; Urwesen = 'original being'; Verstandesgebrauch = 'use of the understanding'; Satz = 'proposition'.","The colon after 'offen ist' is kept: 'so' follows it."]],"krv-a-1048":["Seventh Section of the Third Chapter. Critique of all theology from speculative principles of reason.",["Hauptstück = 'chapter'; Abschnitt = 'section'; Critik = 'critique'; Principien = 'principles'; Vernunft = 'reason'."]],"krv-a-1049":["If by theology I understand the cognition of the original being, then it is either that from mere reason (*theologia rationalis*) or from revelation (*revelata*). The former now thinks its object either merely through pure reason, by means of sheer transcendental concepts (*ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium*) and is called transcendental theology, or through a concept that it borrows from nature (of our soul), as the highest intelligence, and would have to be called natural theology. He who admits only a transcendental theology is called a deist, he who also accepts a natural theology, a theist. The former grants that we can at most cognize the existence of an original being through mere reason, but that our concept of it is merely transcendental, namely only as of a being that has all reality, which one cannot, however, determine more closely. The second asserts that reason is in a position to determine the object more closely according to the analogy with nature, namely: as a being that contains within itself, through understanding and freedom, the original ground of all other things. The former thus represents under it merely a cause of the world (whether through the necessity of its nature or through freedom remains undecided), the latter an author of the world.",["Erkentniß = cognition (Erkenntnis); Urwesen = original being; Gegenstand = object; Begriff = concept; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding; Realität = reality; Daseyn = existence.","The parenthesis after 'transcendentaler Begriffe' is not closed in the original before 'und heißt'; the English preserves this. 'Der erstere'/'Jener' and 'Der zweite'/'diesen' kept as 'The former'/'the latter' etc., without naming deist or theist.","'Intelligenz' rendered 'intelligence'; 'Weltursache' = cause of the world, 'Welturheber' = author of the world.","'bestimmen' = determine throughout; 'näher' = 'more closely'. 'Natur (unserer Seele)': the parenthesis is Kant's; 'nature' ambiguous as in German between nature in general and the nature of our soul."]],"krv-a-1050":["Transcendental theology is either that which intends to derive the existence of the original being from an experience in general (without determining anything more closely about the world to which it belongs), and is called cosmotheology, or believes it cognizes its existence through mere concepts, without the assistance of the least experience, and is called ontotheology.",["'dieienige' = 'diejenige' (old spelling); 'genant' = 'genannt'.","'gedenkt' = 'intends'; 'glaubt' = 'believes'. The subject of 'glaubt' is left as in German (the relative 'welche' may or may not govern it).","'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Daseyn' = existence."]],"krv-a-1051":["Natural theology infers the properties and the existence of an author of the world from the constitution, the order and unity, that is encountered in this world, in which two kinds of causality and their rule must be assumed, namely nature and freedom. Hence it ascends from this world to the highest intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is called physicotheology, in the latter moral theology*).",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Eigenschaften = properties (same English word as Beschaffenheit's alternative in the glossary; flagged here); Ordnung = order; Einheit = unity; vollkommenheit = perfection.","'zweierley Caussalität' = 'two kinds of causality'. 'Principle' renders Princip (not Grundsatz). 'sittlich' = moral (also for 'moralisch' in the footnote).","'der Beschaffenheit, der Ordnung und Einheit, die ... angetroffen wird': singular verb with the compound subject kept as in German."]],"krv-a-1052":["*) Not theological morals; for that contains moral laws, which presuppose the existence of a highest governor of the world, whereas moral theology is a conviction of the existence of a highest being, which is grounded on moral laws.",["'Moral' = morals; 'sittlich' and 'moralisch' both rendered 'moral'; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Weltregierer = governor of the world."]],"krv-a-1053":["Since under the concept of God one is accustomed to understand not perhaps merely a blindly working eternal nature, as the root of things, but a highest being that is to be, through understanding and freedom, the author of things, and since this concept alone also interests us, one could, strictly speaking, deny the deist all belief in God and leave him merely the assertion of an original being, or supreme cause. Meanwhile, since no one may be accused of wanting to deny something altogether just because he does not trust himself to assert it, it is milder and fairer to say: the deist believes in a God, but the theist in a living God (*summam intelligentiam*). Now we want to seek out the possible sources of all these attempts of reason.",["Glaube = belief (here 'glauben' = 'believe'); behaupten = assert; Urheber = author; Ursache = cause.","'Jezt' = 'Jetzt'. 'Quellen' = sources. 'Versuche der Vernunft' = 'attempts of reason'.","'glaube einen Gott' rendered 'believes in a God'; German has the bare accusative."]],"krv-a-1054":["I content myself here with explaining theoretical cognition as that through which I cognize what is, but practical cognition as that through which I represent to myself what ought to be. Accordingly the theoretical use of reason is that through which I cognize *a priori* (as necessary) that something is, but the practical that through which it is cognized *a priori* what ought to happen. If now either that something is, or ought to happen, is indubitably certain, but yet only conditioned: then either a certain determinate condition for it can be absolutely necessary, or it can be presupposed only as optional and contingent. In the former case the condition is postulated (*per thesin*), in the second supposed (*per hypothesin*). Since there are practical laws that are absolutely necessary (the moral ones), then if these necessarily presuppose some existence as the condition of the possibility of their binding force, this existence must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which the inference to this determinate condition proceeds, is itself cognized *a priori* as absolutely necessary. We shall show in the future of the moral laws: that they not merely presuppose the existence of a highest being, but also, since in another consideration they are absolutely necessary, rightly, but of course only practically, postulate it; now we set this kind of inference aside.",["erklären = explain (Erklärung glossary: explanation/definition); Erkentniß = cognition; Gebrauch = use; Bedingung = condition; bedingt = conditioned; zufällig = contingent; Betrachtung = consideration.","'Schluß' in 'der Schluß auf diese bestimte Bedingung' = inference; 'Schlußart' = 'kind of inference'. 'bestimt' = determinate. 'verbindenden Kraft' = 'binding force' (Kraft = force).","'Wenn ... ungezweifelt gewiß, aber doch nur bedingt ist': the singular verb with the double subject is kept; 'gewiß' = certain. 'schlechthin' and 'schlechterdings' both 'absolutely'."]],"krv-a-1055":["Since, when the discussion is merely of what is (not what ought to be), the conditioned, which is given to us in experience, is always also thought as contingent, the condition belonging to it cannot be cognized from this as absolutely necessary, but serves only as a relatively necessary, or rather needed, but in itself and *a priori* arbitrary presupposition for the cognition of reason of the conditioned. If therefore the absolute necessity of a thing is to be cognized in theoretical cognition, then this could happen only from concepts *a priori*, but never as of a cause, in relation to an existence that is given through experience.",["'Die Rede ist' = 'the discussion is' (lit. 'speech is'). 'nothwendig/nöthig' = 'necessary/needed'. 'Vernunfterkentniß' = 'cognition of reason'.","'schlechthinnothwendig' and 'respectivnothwendige' rendered 'absolutely necessary' and 'relatively necessary'.","'niemals aber als einer Ursache': the phrase is obscure ('as of a cause'; the case of 'einer Ursache' is ambiguous) and kept as it stands. 'Beziehung' = relation."]],"krv-a-1056":["A theoretical cognition is speculative if it goes to an object, or such concepts of an object, as one cannot reach in any experience. It is opposed to cognition of nature, which goes to no other objects or predicates of them than those that can be given in a possible experience.",["Gegenstand = object; Naturerkentniß = cognition of nature; 'derselben' = 'of them' (referring to Gegenstände).","'gelangen' = 'reach'. 'zu welchem': antecedent ambiguous between 'Gegenstand' and 'Begriffe'; English 'as' leaves this open only partly."]],"krv-a-1057":["The principle: to infer from what happens (the empirically contingent), as effect, to a cause, is a principle of cognition of nature, but not of speculative cognition. For if one abstracts from it, as a principle that contains the condition of possible experience in general, and, in that one leaves out everything empirical, wants to assert it of the contingent in general, then not the least justification of such a synthetic proposition remains, from which to see how I could pass from something that is to something entirely different from it (called cause); indeed the concept of a cause loses, just like that of the contingent, in such merely speculative use, all significance, the objective reality of which would be made comprehensible *in concreto*.",["Grundsatz = principle (Princip also 'principle'); Satz = proposition; Wirkung = effect; Bedeutung = significance; begreiflich = comprehensible; Realität = reality.","'aussagen' = 'assert' here (glossary behaupten = assert; flagged: two German words, one English). 'ersehen' = 'see'.","'deren obiective Realität sich ... begreiflich machen lasse': 'deren' ambiguous in antecedent (Bedeutung / Begriff); kept ambiguous. The subjunctive 'lasse' rendered 'would be made'."]],"krv-a-1058":["If one now infers from the existence of things in the world to their cause: then this belongs not to the natural, but to the speculative use of reason; because the former relates to some cause not the things themselves (substances), but only what happens, thus their states, as empirically contingent; that the substance itself (the matter) is contingent as to existence would have to be a merely speculative cognition of reason. But if even only the form of the world, the way of its combination and the change of it were under discussion, but I wanted to infer from this to a cause that is entirely distinct from the world, then this would again be a judgment of merely speculative reason; because the object here is no object of a possible experience at all. But then the principle of causality, which is valid only within the field of experiences and outside it is without use, indeed even without significance, would be entirely diverted from its determination.",["Obiect (Latinate) and Gegenstand both rendered 'object'. Verbindung = combination; Zustand = state; Art = way; Urtheil = judgment; Bestimmung = determination (here 'vocation' also possible); gültig = valid.","'Dasein' = existence; 'Substanz' = substance; 'Wechsel' = change (not in glossary).","'weil iener nicht die Dinge selbst ... sondern nur das, was geschieht ... auf irgend eine Ursache bezieht': 'iener' = 'the former' (natural use); the construction 'relates ... to some cause' kept with the object order of the German."]],"krv-a-1059":["I now assert: that all attempts at a merely speculative use of reason with regard to theology are entirely fruitless and by their inner constitution null and void, but that the principles of its natural use lead to no theology at all, and consequently, if one does not lay moral laws down as ground, or use them as a guideline, there can be no theology of reason at all. For all synthetic principles of the understanding are of immanent use: but for the cognition of a highest being a transcendent use of them is required, for which our understanding is not equipped at all. If the empirically valid law of causality is to lead to the original being, then this would have to belong to the chain of the objects of experience, but then it would itself in turn be conditioned, like all appearances. But even if one permitted the leap beyond the boundary of experience, by means of the dynamical law of the relation of effects to their causes: what concept can this procedure procure for us? By far no concept of a highest being, because experience never offers us the greatest of all possible effects (as that which is to give testimony of its cause). If we are to be permitted, merely in order to leave nothing empty in our reason, to fill up this lack of complete determination through a mere idea of highest perfection and original necessity: then this may indeed be conceded as a favor, but cannot be demanded from the right of an irresistible proof. The physico-theological proof could therefore perhaps indeed give emphasis to other proofs (if such are to be had), in that it connects speculation with intuition: but by itself it prepares the understanding for theological cognition more, and gives it a straight and natural direction to it, than that it alone could complete the business.",["Grundsätze and Principien are both rendered 'principles'. Gränze = 'boundary'.","'Naturgebrauch' = 'natural use' (literal; the German does not specify whether 'of nature' or 'natural' is meant).","'zum Grunde legt' = 'lay down as ground'; 'Leitfaden' = 'guideline'.","'bey weitem keinen Begriff' = 'by far no concept' (kept literal). 'vor sich selbst' = 'by itself'.","'Urwesen' = 'original being'; 'höchstes Wesen' = 'highest being'; 'Erkentniß' = cognition."]],"krv-a-1060":["One sees well from this, then: that transcendental questions admit only transcendental answers, i.e., ones from sheer concepts a priori without the least empirical admixture. Here, however, the question is manifestly synthetic and demands an expansion of our cognition beyond all boundaries of experience, namely to the existence of a being that is to correspond to our mere idea, to which no experience can ever be equal. Now, according to our proofs above, all synthetic cognition a priori is possible only by its expressing the formal conditions of a possible experience, and all principles are therefore only of immanent validity, i.e., they relate solely to objects of empirical cognition, or appearances. Thus also through transcendental procedure, in aim of the theology of a merely speculative reason, nothing is accomplished.",["'in Absicht auf' rendered 'in aim of' to keep Absicht = 'aim'; the German idiom is usually 'with regard to'.","Erweiterung = 'expansion' (not Ausdehnung, 'extension'). Daseyn = 'existence'.","'Gültigkeit' = 'validity'. 'Gegenstände' = 'objects'."]],"krv-a-1061":["But if one would rather draw all the above proofs of the Analytic into doubt than let the persuasion of the weight of the grounds of proof so long used be robbed from oneself, one still cannot refuse to satisfy the summons when I demand: that one should at least justify oneself about how and by means of what illumination one then trusts oneself to fly over all possible experience by the power of mere ideas. With new proofs, or the improved work of old proofs, I would ask to be spared. For, although in this one does not have much to choose, since in the end all merely speculative proofs run out into a single one, namely the ontological, and I accordingly need not fear to be burdened especially by the fruitfulness of the dogmatic champions of that reason free of the senses, although moreover I also, without on that account thinking myself very combative, do not want to decline the challenge to uncover the fallacy in every attempt of this kind and thereby to frustrate its presumption: still the hope of better luck is thereby never completely abolished among those who have once become accustomed to dogmatic persuasions, and I therefore hold to the sole fair demand, that one justify oneself universally and from the nature of the human understanding, together with all remaining sources of cognition, about how one wants to go about expanding one's cognition entirely and completely a priori and extending it to where no possible experience and consequently no means suffices to assure any concept thought up by ourselves of its objective reality. However the understanding may have arrived at this concept, the existence of its object still cannot be found analytically in it, because the cognition of the existence of the object consists precisely in this, that this is posited outside the thought in itself. But it is entirely impossible to go out of a concept by itself and, without following empirical connection (whereby, however, only appearances are ever given), to arrive at the discovery of new objects and extravagant beings.",["Auffoderung = 'summons'; Ausfoderung = 'challenge' (two German words, kept distinct).","Ueberredung = 'persuasion'; Fehlschluß = 'fallacy' (literally a faulty inference). Object/Obiect and Gegenstand are both 'object'; 'Obiect' in 'Existenz des Obiects' is the Latinate member.","Daseyn and Existenz are both 'existence' ('das Daseyn des Gegenstandes'; 'Erkentniß der Existenz').","'ausser dem Gedanken an sich selbst gesezt ist': 'in itself' (an sich selbst) may attach to 'thought' or to 'posited'; the English preserves this ambiguity.","'überschwenglicher' = 'extravagant'. 'sinnenfreien' = 'free of the senses'. 'Mittel' = 'means'. 'Verknüpfung' = 'connection'."]],"krv-a-1062":["But although reason in its merely speculative use is by far not adequate for this so great aim, namely to arrive at the existence of a supreme being, it nevertheless has very great utility in it, to correct the cognition of it, in case it could be drawn from elsewhere, to make it consistent with itself and with every intelligible aim, and to purify it of everything that might be contrary to the concept of an original being, and of all admixture of empirical restrictions.",["zulänglich = 'adequate' (kept apart from zureichend = 'sufficient'); Einschränkung = 'restrictions' (kept apart from Schranke = 'limitation').","'obersten Wesens' = 'supreme being'; 'Urwesen' = 'original being'. 'in darin' ('in it') has an unclear antecedent in the German; kept as is.","'ieder intelligibelen Absicht' = 'every intelligible aim'."]],"krv-a-1063":["Transcendental theology accordingly remains, notwithstanding all its inadequacy, still of important negative use and is a constant censorship of our reason, when it has to do merely with pure ideas, which for that very reason admit no other than a transcendental standard. For if once, in another, perhaps practical, relation, the presupposition of a highest and all-sufficient being, as supreme intelligence, were to assert its validity without gainsaying: then it would be of the greatest importance to determine this concept precisely on its transcendental side, as the concept of a necessary and most real being, and to remove what is contrary to the highest reality, what belongs to mere appearance (anthropomorphism in the broader sense), and at the same time to clear out of the way all opposed assertions, whether they be atheistic, or deistic, or anthropomorphistic, which in such a critical treatment is very easy, in that the same grounds through which the incapacity of human reason with regard to the assertion of the existence of such a being is laid before the eyes necessarily also suffice to prove the unfitness of every counter-assertion. For where will anyone get, through pure speculation of reason, the insight: that there is no highest being, as original ground of everything, or that none of the properties belongs to it which we, according to their sequels, represent to ourselves as analogical with the dynamical realities of a thinking being, or that they, in the latter case, would also have to be subject to all the restrictions which sensibility unavoidably imposes on the intelligences that we know through experience.",["'Widerrede' = 'gainsaying' (not Widerspruch, 'contradiction'). 'Richtmaaß' = 'standard'.","Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary: Folge). 'Eigenschaften' = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary).","'im weiteren Verstande' = 'in the broader sense' (Verstand in a non-technical use). 'allgnugsamen' = 'all-sufficient'; 'allerrealesten' = 'most real'.","The final sentence is not closed with a question mark in the German, and the English follows this; the 'Denn, wo...' construction is kept literal."]],"krv-a-1064":["The highest being therefore remains for the merely speculative use of reason a mere, but nevertheless faultless ideal, a concept that closes and crowns the whole of human cognition, whose objective reality can indeed not be proved on this path, but also not refuted, and, if there should be a moral theology that can supplement this lack, then the previously only problematic transcendental theology proves its indispensability, through determination of its concept and unceasing censorship of a reason often enough deceived by sensibility and not always consistent with its own ideas. The necessity, the infinity, the unity, the existence outside the world (not as world-soul), the eternity, without conditions of time, the omnipresence, without conditions of space, the omnipotence etc. are sheer transcendental predicates, and therefore the purified concept of them, which every theology has such great need of, can be drawn only from the transcendental one.",["'vor den blos speculativen Gebrauch' = 'for the merely speculative use' (vor for für, as in the original).","'Daseyn ausser der Welt' = 'existence outside the world'. 'Mangel' = 'lack'. 'die transscendentale' (end) refers to the feminine 'Theologie'.","The comma-joined 'und, wenn es...' construction is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-1065":["Appendix to the Transcendental Dialectic. On the regulative use of the ideas of pure reason.",["Heading; 'Dialectik' = 'Dialectic'. Anhang = 'Appendix'."]],"krv-a-1066":["The outcome of all dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms what we have already proved in the transcendental analytic, namely that all our inferences that would lead us beyond the field of possible experience are deceptive and groundless, but it also teaches us this in particular: that human reason has a natural propensity in this to overstep this boundary, that transcendental ideas are just as natural to it as the categories are to the understanding, although with the difference that, just as the latter lead to truth, i.e., to the agreement of our concepts with the object, the former effect a mere, but irresistible illusion, the deception of which can hardly be kept off even by the sharpest critique.",["trüglich = 'deceptive'; Schein = 'illusion'; Täuschung = 'deception'.","Gränze = 'boundary' (glossary Grenze = bound(ary)); Obiecte = 'object' (Latinate form; Gegenstand is also 'object').","OCR 'schärf;ste' silently corrected to 'schärfste'.","'dabey' rendered 'in this'; its referent is left as open as in the German.","'die leztere ... die erstere': the German plural verb 'führen' after 'die leztere' is kept as it stands; 'the latter' and 'the former' are rendered literally."]],"krv-a-1067":["Everything that is grounded in the nature of our powers must be purposive and consistent with the correct use of them, if we can only prevent a certain misunderstanding and discover the proper direction of them. Therefore the transcendental ideas will, in all likelihood, have their good and consequently immanent use, although, if their meaning is misknown and they are taken for concepts of actual things, they can be transcendent in application and just for that reason deceptive. For not the idea in itself, but merely its use can, either with regard to the whole of possible experience, be overflying (transcendent), or indigenous (immanent), depending on whether one directs it straight at an object supposedly corresponding to it, or only at the use of the understanding in general with regard to the objects with which it has to do; and all errors of subreption are always to be ascribed to a lack of the power of judgment, but never to the understanding or to reason.",["Kräfte = 'powers' (glossary Kraft = force; power).","Bedeutung = 'meaning'; verkant = 'misknown' (verkannt), kept literal.","Gegenstand / Obiect: both 'object'; here only Gegenstand and Gegenstände occur.","Urtheilskraft = 'power of judgment'; Subreption left as is (Latin-derived).","'in Ansehung der gesamten möglichen Erfahrung' could attach to 'Gebrauch' or to the verb 'seyn'; left as ambiguous as the German.","The clause 'und alle Fehler ...' is joined by 'und' without a break, as in the German; I have added a semicolon, which the German lacks."]],"krv-a-1068":["Reason never relates straight to an object, but solely to the understanding and by means of it to its own empirical use, thus it creates no concepts (of objects), but only orders them and gives them that unity which they can have in their greatest possible extension, i.e., in relation to the totality of the series, which the understanding does not look to at all, but only to that connection through which series of conditions come about everywhere according to concepts. Reason therefore properly has as object only the understanding and its purposive employment, and, as the latter unites the manifold in the object through concepts, so the former for its part unites the manifold of concepts through ideas, in that it posits a certain collective unity as the goal of the actions of the understanding, which otherwise are occupied only with distributive unity.",["Gegenstand and Obiect both 'object'; Obiecten here is the Latinate member.","Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Einheit = 'unity'; Totalität = 'totality'; Mannigfaltige = 'manifold'; Ausbreitung = 'extension' (glossary Ausdehnung = extension is a different German word).","'Reihen' = 'series' (singular and plural alike).","'iene ... dieser': 'the former' (reason) and 'the latter' (understanding) for iene/dieser; the German pronouns are retained in their order.","'Anstellung' rendered 'employment'."]],"krv-a-1069":["I assert accordingly: the transcendental ideas are never of constitutive use, such that concepts of certain objects would thereby be given, and in the case that one understands them in this way, they are merely rationalizing (dialectical) concepts. On the other hand, however, they have an excellent and indispensably necessary regulative use, namely that of directing the understanding to a certain goal, in view of which the lines of direction of all its rules run together in one point, which, although it is only an idea (*focus imaginarius*), i.e., a point from which the concepts of the understanding do not really proceed, since it lies entirely outside the boundaries of possible experience, nevertheless serves to provide them with the greatest unity alongside the greatest extension. Now from this there arises for us the deception as if these lines of direction were shot out from an object itself that lay outside the field of empirically possible cognition (just as objects are seen behind the surface of a mirror), yet this illusion (which one can nevertheless prevent from deceiving) is none the less indispensably necessary if, besides the objects that are before our eyes, we also want to see at the same time those that lie far from them behind our backs, i.e., if, in our case, we want to train the understanding beyond every given experience (the part of the entire possible experience), hence also to the greatest possible and utmost expansion.",["vernünftelnde = 'rationalizing' (vernünfteln = rationalize); Täuschung = 'deception'; Illusion kept as 'illusion' (Schein is also 'illusion').","'ausgeschlossen' (A 645) is printed as such; the sense 'shot out' follows the common emendation 'ausgeschossen'; the text as given reads 'ausgeschlossen' (literally 'excluded'), and I have translated it as printed: 'excluded' should replace 'shot out' if strict fidelity to the printed reading is wanted. Doubtful reading.","'unentbehrlichnothwendig' printed as one word; rendered 'indispensably necessary'.","Gegenstand / Obiecte: both 'object'.","'Gränzen' = 'boundaries'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'.","'seyn' after 'Ideen' is the printed subjunctive; 'dadurch' left referring as in the German."]],"krv-a-1070":["If we survey our cognitions of the understanding in their entire domain, we find that what reason quite peculiarly disposes over in it and seeks to bring about is the systematic in cognition, i.e., the interconnection of it from one principle. This unity of reason always presupposes an idea, namely that of the form of a whole of cognition, which precedes the determinate cognition of the parts and contains the conditions for determining a priori for each part its place and relation to the others. Accordingly this idea postulates complete unity of the cognition of the understanding, through which this becomes not merely a contingent aggregate, but a system interconnected according to necessary laws. One cannot properly say: that this idea is a concept of the object, but rather of the thoroughgoing unity of these concepts, insofar as it serves the understanding as a rule. Such concepts of reason are not drawn from nature, rather we question nature in accordance with these ideas and hold our cognition to be deficient as long as it is not adequate to them. One admits: that pure earth, pure water, pure air etc. are hardly to be found. Nevertheless one needs the concepts of them (which therefore, as concerns complete purity, have their origin only in reason), in order to determine properly the share that each of these natural causes has in the appearance, and so one brings all matters to earths (as it were the mere weight), salts and combustible beings (as the force), finally to water and air as vehicles (as it were machines by means of which the previous ones work), in order, according to the idea of a mechanism, to explain the chemical effects of matters among one another. For, although one does not actually express oneself so, such an influence of reason on the divisions of natural scientists is nevertheless very easy to discover.",["Umfang = 'domain'; Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Zusammenhangend = 'interconnected'.","Obiect = 'object'; Gesetzen = 'laws'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Materien = 'matters' (Materie = matter); Wesen = 'beings'; Kraft = 'force'; Wirkung = 'effects'.","Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Eintheilungen = 'divisions'; Fom (OCR/print) corrected silently to 'Form'.","'a priori' left in Latin, in the position it has; it could attach to 'bestimmen' or to 'Stelle und Verhältniß'.","'die vorige wirken': plural verb after 'die vorige' kept; rendered 'the previous ones work'.","'verfügt' rendered 'disposes over'; 'darüber' = 'in it', i.e. in respect of the understanding's cognitions."]],"krv-a-1071":["If reason is a faculty for deriving the particular from the universal, then either the universal is already certain in itself and given, and then it requires only power of judgment for the subsumption and the particular is thereby necessarily determined. This I will call the apodictic use of reason. Or the universal is assumed only problematically and is a mere idea, the particular is certain, but the universality of the rule for this consequence is still a problem, then several particular cases, which are all certain, are tried on the rule, whether they flow from it, and in this case, if it has the appearance that all particular cases that can be given follow from it, the universality of the rule is inferred, and from this afterward all cases, which are not even given in themselves. This I will call the hypothetical use of reason.",["Vermögen = 'faculty'; Urtheilskraft = 'power of judgment'; Problem = 'problem' (Aufgabe is also 'problem' in the glossary).","Folge = 'consequence' here; the glossary gives 'sequel; sequence' for Folge, and Folgerung = 'consequence'. Doubtful which to use; 'consequence' chosen to match 'folgen/abfolgen' sense.","Abfolgen = 'follow'; geschlossen = 'inferred' (schließen; Schluß = inference).","The German syntax is irregular: the 'Oder' sentence runs on with 'so werden' after a comma; kept as is.","'es' in 'wenn es den Anschein hat' left as an impersonal pronoun."]],"krv-a-1072":["The hypothetical use of reason, from ideas laid down as ground, as problematic concepts, is properly not constitutive, namely not so constituted that thereby, if one wants to judge with all strictness, the truth of the universal rule, which has been assumed as hypothesis, would follow; for, how is one to know all possible consequences, which, in that they follow from the same assumed principle, prove its universality, but it is only regulative, in order thereby, as far as is possible, to bring unity into the particular cognitions and thereby to bring the rule closer to universality.",["Grund = 'ground'; Grundsatz = 'principle'; beschaffen = 'constituted' (Beschaffenheit = constitution).","The sentence is irregular: 'sondern er ist nur regulativ' follows a parenthetical 'denn, wie will man...' without closing it; kept as it stands.","Folgen = 'consequences' (Folge: sequel; sequence; Folgerung = consequence); 'folge' (verb) = 'would follow'.","'Erkentnisse' = 'cognitions'; 'Wahrheit' = 'truth'."]],"krv-a-1073":["The hypothetical use of reason therefore goes to the systematic unity of the cognitions of the understanding, but this is the touchstone of the truth of the rules. Conversely the systematic unity (as a mere idea) is solely only projected unity, which one must regard in itself not as given, but only as a problem, but which serves to find a *principium* for the manifold and particular use of the understanding and thereby also to guide this beyond the cases that are not given and to make it interconnected.",["Principium is left in Latin in italics, as printed; elsewhere Grundsatz = 'principle'.","Probierstein = 'touchstone'; proiectirte = 'projected'; Problem = 'problem'.","'zusammenhängend machen' rendered 'make interconnected' (Zusammenhang = interconnection).","'welche aber dazu dient': 'welche' refers back to 'Einheit' (or possibly 'Problem'); left ambiguous.","'diesen' (the use of the understanding) is the antecedent of 'leiten'; 'zu leiten' and 'zusammenhängend zu machen' both have it as object."]],"krv-a-1074":["One sees from this, however, only: that the systematic unity or unity of reason of the manifold cognition of the understanding is a logical principle, in order, where the understanding alone does not suffice for rules, to help it along by means of ideas and at the same time to procure for the diversity of its rules unanimity under one principle (systematic) and thereby interconnection, as far as can be done. But whether the constitution of the objects, or the nature of the understanding, which cognizes them as such, is determined in itself to systematic unity, and whether one could postulate this a priori to a certain extent, even without regard to such an interest of reason, and thus say: all possible cognitions of the understanding (among them the empirical) have unity of reason and stand under common principles, from which they can be derived, notwithstanding their diversity, that would be a transcendental principle of reason, which would make systematic unity not merely subjectively and logically as method, but objectively necessary.",["Princip and Grundsatz are both rendered 'principle'.","Einhelligkeit = 'unanimity' (not in the glossary).","Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Gegenstände = 'objects' (Obiecten appears in the next passage, also 'objects').","'a priori' stands between 'postulate' and 'to a certain extent' (in gewisser Maasse) and could attach to 'postulate' or to 'this'; left as in the German.","'the systematic unity or unity of reason' (systematische oder Vernunfteinheit): the construction is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-1075":["We want to clarify this through a case of the use of reason. Among the different kinds of unity according to concepts of the understanding belongs also that of the causality of a substance, which is called force. The different appearances of one and the same substance show at first glance so much heterogeneity, that one must therefore initially assume almost as many kinds of forces of it as effects come forth, as in the human mind sensation, consciousness, imagination, recollection, wit, power of discrimination, pleasure, desire, etc. Initially a logical maxim commands one to diminish this apparent diversity as much as possible by discovering the hidden identity through comparison and by looking to see whether imagination, combined with consciousness, might not be recollection, wit, power of discrimination, perhaps even understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental force, of which, however, logic does not at all ascertain whether there is any such thing, is at least the problem of a systematic representation of the manifoldness of forces. The logical principle of reason demands that this unity be brought about as far as possible, and, the more the appearances of one force and another are found identical among themselves, the more probable it becomes that they are nothing but different manifestations of one and the same force, which can be called (comparatively) their fundamental force. One proceeds in just the same way with the rest.",["Ungleichartigkeit = 'heterogeneity' (gleichartig = 'homogeneous' in 1077); Einbildung = 'imagination' (not Einbildungskraft).","Aeusserungen = 'manifestations' (distinct from Ausdruck, 'expression').","Art in 'verschiedene Arten von Einheit' and 'vielerley Kräfte' = 'kind'; Wahrscheinlichkeit = 'probability'.","Aufgabe's glossary equivalent is 'problem'; the German here has Latinate 'Problem'. Mannigfaltigkeit = 'manifoldness'.","'Unterscheidungskraft' is rendered literally as 'power of discrimination'."]],"krv-a-1076":["The comparative fundamental forces must in turn be compared with one another, in order, by discovering their unanimity, to bring them close to a single radical, i.e., absolute fundamental force. This unity of reason, however, is merely hypothetical. One does not assert that such a one must in fact be encountered, but that one must seek it in favor of reason, namely for the establishment of certain principles, for the many kinds of rules that experience may offer, and, where it can be done, in such a way bring systematic unity into cognition.",["OCR 'raticalen' silently read as 'radicalen' ('radical').","'in such a way bring' follows the German 'auf solche Weise ... ins Erkentniß bringen müsse'; the construction (muss governing both 'suchen' and 'bringen') is kept.","'vor die mancherley Regeln' = 'for the many kinds of rules'; 'vor' could also be read as 'in front of/on behalf of'."]],"krv-a-1077":["But it shows itself, if one attends to the transcendental use of the understanding, that this idea of a fundamental force in general is not merely determined as a problem for hypothetical use, but pretends to objective reality, whereby the systematic unity of the many kinds of forces of a substance is postulated and an apodictic principle of reason is established. For, without our having even once tried the unanimity of the many forces, indeed even if it fails us after all attempts to discover it, we nevertheless presuppose: that such a one will be encountered, and this not only, as in the case cited, on account of the unity of the substance, but, where so very many, although homogeneous to a certain degree, are encountered, as in matter in general, reason presupposes systematic unity of manifold forces, since particular laws of nature stand under more general ones and the saving of principles becomes not merely an economical principle of reason but an inner law of nature.",["'vorgebe' = 'pretends to' (alternatively 'gives out/lays claim to'); the literal sense is kept.","'such a one' (eine solche) has no stated antecedent; it may refer to unanimity or to a fundamental force; left ambiguous as in the German.","Ersparung = 'saving'; Grundsatz = 'principle' (as is Princip).","'sezt ... voraus' is rendered 'presupposes' as is 'setzen ... voraus' ('presuppose')."]],"krv-a-1078":["In fact it is also not to be seen how a logical principle of the unity of reason of the rules could take place, if a transcendental one were not presupposed, through which such a systematic unity, as adhering to the objects themselves, is assumed a priori as necessary. For with what warrant can reason in the logical use demand to treat the manifoldness of forces, which nature gives us to cognize, as a merely hidden unity and to derive it from some fundamental force, as much as is in it, if it were free to admit that it is just as well possible that all forces were heterogeneous, and the systematic unity of their derivation not in conformity with nature: for then it would proceed directly against its vocation, in that it would set for itself as goal an idea that quite contradicted the arrangement of nature. Nor can one say: it has previously taken this unity from the contingent constitution of nature according to principles of reason. For the law of reason to seek it is necessary, because without it we would have no reason at all, but without this no interconnected use of the understanding, and, in default of this, no sufficient mark of empirical truth, and we must therefore in regard to the latter presuppose the systematic unity of nature throughout as objectively valid and necessary.",["Bestimmung = 'vocation' (glossary alternative: 'determination'); Obiecten = 'objects'.","'a priori' may attach to 'assumed' or to 'necessary'; left ambiguous as in the German.","'as much as is in it' (so viel an ihr ist): 'it/her' (ihr) could refer to reason or to the fundamental force; left ambiguous.","'dessen', 'dieser', 'des lezteren' = 'this', 'this', 'the latter': the referents are not resolved.","zusammenhangend = 'interconnected' (cf. Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'); Merkmal = 'mark'; zureichend = 'sufficient'.","Reading of 'Ob' clause: the sentence beginning 'wenn es ihr frey stände' keeps the colon and subjunctive sequence as printed."]],"krv-a-1079":["We find this transcendental presupposition also in an admirable way hidden in the principles of the philosophers, although they have not always recognized it therein, or confessed it to themselves. That all manifoldnesses of individual things do not exclude the identity of the species, that the many species must be treated only as various determinations of a few genera, these however of still higher genera etc., that therefore a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical concepts, insofar as they can be derived from higher and more general ones, must be sought, is a school rule or logical principle, without which no use of reason would take place, because we can infer from the universal to the particular only insofar as general properties of things are laid down as ground, under which the particular stand.",["Art = 'species' here (where contrasted with Gattung = 'genus'); elsewhere 'kind'. One English word 'genera' stands for both Gattungen and Geschlechtern (the latter in 'höheren Geschlechtern').","Grundsätze = 'principles'; Eigenschaften = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit is 'constitution'); 'besondere' = 'particular'.","'empirschen' (sic) silently read as 'empirischen'.","'schliessen' = 'infer'; 'zum Grunde gelegt' = 'laid down as ground'."]],"krv-a-1080":["That such unanimity is also encountered in nature, however, the philosophers presuppose in the well-known school rule: that one must not multiply the beginnings (principles) without necessity (*entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda*). Thereby it is said: that the nature of the things itself offers material for unity of reason, and the apparently infinite diversity ought not to keep us from suspecting behind it unity of fundamental properties, from which the manifoldness can be derived only through further determination. This unity, although it is a mere idea, has been pursued at all times so zealously that one has found cause rather to moderate the desire for it than to encourage it. It was already much: that the chemists could reduce all salts to two main genera, acidic and alkaline, they even try to regard this difference merely as a variety, or different manifestation of one and the same fundamental material. The many species of earths (the material of stones and even of metals) one has sought gradually to bring to three, finally to two; but not yet satisfied with that, they cannot rid themselves of the thought of suspecting behind these varieties nevertheless a single genus, indeed perhaps even a common principle for these and the salts. One might perhaps believe this to be a merely economical device of reason, in order to spare itself trouble as much as possible, and a hypothetical attempt which, if it succeeds, gives probability to the presupposed ground of explanation precisely through this unity. But such a selfish intention is very easily distinguished from the idea, according to which everyone presupposes: this unity of reason is appropriate to nature itself, and that reason here does not beg but commands, although without being able to determine the bounds of this unity.",["OCR 'bles' read as 'blos' ('merely'); 'noch und nach' read as 'nach und nach' ('gradually').","Scheidekünstler = 'chemists' (literally 'separation-artists'); Stoff = 'material', Grundstoff = 'fundamental material'.","Anfänge (Principien) = 'beginnings (principles)'; Absicht = 'intention'; Gränzen = 'bounds'.","Arten von Erden = 'species of earths'; Gattung = 'genus'; Aeusserung = 'manifestation'.","The comma splice 'es war schon viel: ... konten, sie versuchen' is kept as in the German; 'they' (sie) has no stated antecedent beyond 'the chemists'.","Latin maxim kept in italics as printed."]],"krv-a-1081":["If among the appearances that offer themselves to us there were such a great difference, I do not want to say of form (for in that they may be similar to one another), but of content, i.e., in accordance with the manifoldness of existing beings, that even the very sharpest human understanding could not, through comparison of the one with the other, discover the slightest similarity (a case that can well be thought), then the logical law of genera would not take place at all, and there would take place not even a concept of genus, or any universal concept, indeed not even any understanding, as that which has to do solely with such. The logical principle of genera therefore presupposes a transcendental one, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I here understand only objects that are given to us). In accordance with it, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the manifold of a possible experience (although we cannot determine its degree a priori), because without it no empirical concepts, hence no experience, would be possible.",["Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; das Mannigfaltige = the manifold. Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity.","Gattung = genus (pl. genera); Gegenstände = objects; Verstand = understanding; Erscheinungen = appearances.","'als der es lediglich mit solchen zu thun hat': the 'als der' is left as ambiguous as the German (causal 'as/since', or 'as that which'); 'der' could refer to Verstand or to the preceding series.","'statt finden' = 'take place' throughout. OCR/orthography: 'transscendentales' rendered 'transcendental'."]],"krv-a-1082":["To the logical principle of genera, which postulates identity, there is opposed another, namely that of species, which requires manifoldness and differences of things, notwithstanding their agreement under the same genus, and makes it a prescription for the understanding to be attentive to these no less than to those. This principle (of acuteness, or of the faculty of distinguishing) greatly restricts the frivolity of the former (of wit), and reason here shows a double, mutually conflicting interest, on the one side the interest of the domain (of universality) with regard to the genera, on the other of the content (of determinacy), with respect to the manifoldness of the species, because in the first case the understanding indeed thinks much under its concepts, but in the second all the more in them. This also expresses itself in the very different way of thinking of natural scientists, some of whom (who are preeminently speculative), as it were hostile to heterogeneity, are always looking toward the unity of the genus, the others (preeminently empirical minds) incessantly seek to split nature into so much manifoldness that one would almost have to give up the hope of judging its appearances in accordance with universal principles.",["Grundsatz and Princip are both rendered 'principle'. Umfang = domain; Inhalt = content; Art (pl. Arten) = species; Gattung = genus.","Leichtsinn rendered 'frivolity'; Bestimtheit = 'determinacy'; Unterscheidungsvermögen = 'faculty of distinguishing'; Ungleichartigkeit = 'heterogeneity'.","'in Absicht auf' rendered 'with respect to'; 'beurtheilen' = 'judge'.","'Denkungsart' = 'way of thinking'."]],"krv-a-1083":["At the basis of this latter way of thinking there also lies, evidently, a logical principle, which has the systematic completeness of all cognitions as its aim, if, beginning from the genus, I descend to the manifold that may be contained under it, and in such a way seek to provide the system with extension, as in the first case, where I ascend to the genus, I seek to provide it with simplicity. For from the sphere of the concept that designates a genus, just as little as from the space that matter can occupy, can it be seen how far the division of it can go. Hence every genus requires different species, but these require different subspecies, and, since none of the latter takes place that would not always in turn have a sphere (domain as *conceptus communis*), reason demands in its whole extension that no species be regarded as in itself the lowest, because, since it is still always a concept that contains in itself only what is common to different things, this concept could not be thoroughly determined, hence also not be related directly to an individual, and consequently must at all times contain other concepts, i.e., subspecies, under itself. This law of specification could be expressed thus: *entium varietates non temere esse minuendas*.",["Absicht = aim; Ausbreitung = 'extension' (Ausdehnung is also 'extension'); Einfalt = 'simplicity'; Erkenntnisse = 'cognitions'.","Umfang = 'domain'; Sphäre = 'sphere'; Zusammenhang not present. Latin conceptus communis and the closing maxim are in the original in Latin.","Gesetz der Specification = 'law of specification'; durchgängig bestimmt = 'thoroughly determined'.","'Daher iede Gattung ... erfodert' and the following 'und, da' are kept as a single sentence; 'nicht zunächst' rendered 'not directly'."]],"krv-a-1084":["But one easily sees: that this logical law too would be without sense and application, if a transcendental law of specification did not lie at its basis, which to be sure does not demand from the things that can become our objects an actual infinity with regard to the differences, for the logical principle, as that which asserts solely the indeterminacy of the logical sphere with regard to the possible division, gives no occasion for this, but nevertheless imposes on the understanding to seek, under every species that comes before us, subspecies, and for every difference smaller differences. For if there were no lower concepts, there would also be no higher ones. Now the understanding cognizes everything only through concepts: consequently, as far as it reaches in division, never through mere intuition, but always in turn through lower concepts. The cognition of appearances in their thorough determination (which is possible only through understanding) demands an incessantly to be continued specification of its concepts and a progression to differences still remaining, from which abstraction has been made in the concept of the species, and still more in that of the genus.",["Anschauung = intuition; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Bestimmung = determination; Fortgang = progression.","'Eintheilung' = 'division'; 'Unbestimtheit' = 'indeterminacy'; 'Erscheinungen' = 'appearances'.","'wovon ... abstrahirt worden': 'wovon' could refer to the differences (as rendered) or to the progression; left as in the German."]],"krv-a-1085":["Nor can this law of specification be borrowed from experience; for this can give no such far-reaching disclosures. Empirical specification soon stops in the distinguishing of the manifold, if it has not been guided by the already preceding transcendental law of specification, as a principle of reason, to seek such and still always to suspect it, even when it does not reveal itself to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds (calcareous and muriatic earths) required for its discovery an antecedent rule of reason, which made it a problem for the understanding to seek the difference, in that it presupposed nature to be so rich as to suspect it. For we have understanding just as much only under the presupposition of differences in nature as under the condition that its objects have homogeneity in themselves, because it is precisely the manifoldness of that which can be comprehended under a concept that constitutes the use of this concept and the occupation of the understanding.",["OCR 'transscendentate' silently corrected to 'transcendental'. 'Obiecte' = 'objects' (Gegenstand and Object both 'object').","Aufgabe = problem; Gebrauch = use; Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity; Verschiedenheit = difference.","'Kalk- und muriatische Erden' rendered literally; 'seyn' in 'Erden ... seyn' left as an awkward plural construction ('are of different kinds').","'sie ... zu vermuthen' (the clause 'indem sie die Natur so reichhaltig voraussetzte, sie zu vermuthen') is kept in the German order; the antecedent of 'sie' is ambiguous (rule of reason / the difference)."]],"krv-a-1086":["Reason therefore prepares for the understanding its field 1. through a principle of the homogeneity of the manifold under higher genera, 2. through a principle of the variety of the homogeneous under lower species; and in order to complete the systematic unity, it adds 3. yet a law of the affinity of all concepts, which commands a continuous transition from each species to every other through a step-like growth of the difference. We can call them the principles of the homogeneity, of the specification and of the continuity of forms. The latter arises from this: that one unites the first two, after one has completed the systematic interconnection in the idea, both in ascending to higher genera and in descending to lower species; for then all manifoldnesses are related to one another, because they all descend through all degrees of extended determination from a single highest genus.",["Homogenität and Gleichartigkeit are both rendered 'homogeneity'; Grundsatz and Princip both 'principle'.","Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Idee = 'idea'; Grad = 'degree'; Form = 'form'; Bestimmung = 'determination'.","Mannigfaltigkeiten (plural) = 'manifoldnesses'; 'Varietät' = 'variety'; 'Affinität' = 'affinity'.","'Das leztere' (the latter) refers by position to the third principle, 'Continuität'; kept as is."]],"krv-a-1087":["One can make the systematic unity among the three logical principles sensible in the following way. One can regard every concept as a point that, as the standpoint of a spectator, has its horizon, i.e., a multitude of things that can be represented from it and, as it were, surveyed. Within this horizon a multitude of points must be able to be indicated to infinity, each of which in turn has its narrower field of vision, i.e., every species contains subspecies, in accordance with the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists only of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points that have no domain (individuals). But for different horizons, i.e., genera, which are determined from just as many concepts, a common horizon can be thought drawn, from which one surveys them all together as from a middle point, which is the higher genus, until finally the highest genus is the universal and true horizon, which is determined from the standpoint of the highest concept and comprehends under itself all manifoldness, as genera, species and subspecies.",["Einheit = unity; Menge = multitude; Umfang = domain; Gattung = genus; Art = species.","'sinnlich machen' = 'make sensible'; 'Gesichtskreis' = 'field of vision'; 'befaßt' = 'comprehends' (cf. begreifen = comprehend in glossary, a different German word).","'Mittelpuncte' = 'middle point'; 'Standpunct' = 'standpoint'."]],"krv-a-1088":["The law of homogeneity leads me to this highest standpoint, to all the lower ones and their greatest variety the law of specification. But since in this way in the whole domain of all possible concepts there is nothing empty, and outside it nothing can be encountered, there arises from the presupposition of that general horizon and of its thoroughgoing division the principle: *non datur vacuum formarum*, i.e., there are not different original and first genera that would be, as it were, isolated and separated from one another (by an empty intervening space), but all manifold genera are only divisions of a single highest and general genus; and from this principle, whose immediate sequel is *datur continuum formarum*, i.e., all differences of the species border on one another and permit no transition to one another by a leap, but only through all the smaller degrees of difference, through which one can get from one to the other; in a word, there are no species or subspecies that would be the nearest to one another (in the concept of reason), but intermediate species are always still possible, whose difference from the first and the second is smaller than their difference from one another.",["Gattung = genus, Art = species (also 'Unterarten' = subspecies); 'Species' in the next passage is Kant's Latin and is rendered 'species' too, so 'species' stands for both Art and Species.","Folge = sequel, per the glossary; 'Grundsatz' = principle; 'Umfang' = domain; 'Begriff' = concept.","The syntax of 'und aus diesem Grundsatze, dessen unmittelbare Folge datur continuum formarum' is left as in the German: it has no finite verb of its own, and the semicolons mark where the clause is left hanging. Original 'ienes' = 'jenes'.","'Vernunft' = reason; 'Grad' = degree; 'Sprung' = leap."]],"krv-a-1089":["The first law, therefore, prevents the extravagance into the manifoldness of different original genera and recommends homogeneity, the second, on the other hand, again restricts this inclination to unanimity and commands the distinction of the subspecies, before one turns with one's general concept to the individuals. The third unites those two, in that it prescribes, even with the highest manifoldness, nevertheless homogeneity through the graduated transition from one species to another, which indicates a kind of affinity of the different branches, insofar as they have all sprung from one stem.",["Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity (as is Homogenität in the previous passage); Einhelligkeit = unanimity.","'indem sie ... vorschreibt': the feminine 'sie' after 'Das dritte' (neuter, i.e. the third law) is kept as in the German and rendered 'it'.","'Art' in 'eine Art von Verwandschaft' = kind; 'Species' = species. Verwandschaft = affinity."]],"krv-a-1090":["This logical law of the *continui specierum* (*formarum logicarum*), however, presupposes a transcendental one (*lex continui in natura*), without which the use of the understanding through that prescription would only be led astray, in that it would perhaps take a path directly opposed to nature. This law must therefore rest on pure transcendental and not empirical grounds. For in the latter case it would come later than the systems; but it has actually first brought forth what is systematic in the cognition of nature. Behind these laws there are also not perhaps hidden aims at a test to be made with them as mere attempts, although to be sure this interconnection, where it fits, provides a powerful ground for taking the hypothetically thought-out unity to be well-grounded and they thus also have their utility in this aim, but one sees clearly in them: that they judge the parsimony of fundamental causes, the manifoldness of effects, and an affinity of the members of nature resulting from it, in themselves to be rational and appropriate to nature, and these principles therefore carry their recommendation with them directly and not merely as devices of method.",["Gebrauch = use; Verstand = understanding; Erkentniß (Erkenntniß) = cognition; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Absicht = aim (both occurrences); Wirkung = effect; Grundsatz = principle.","'vor gegründet zu halten' and 'vor vernunftmäßig' use 'vor' for 'für'; 'to be well-grounded' renders 'gegründet'.","'sezt ... voraus' = presupposes; 'Systematische' = what is systematic.","Ambiguity kept: 'einer ... Probe verborgen' and the long concessive ('obwol ... Nutzen haben') are left unresolved, as is the reference of 'ihnen' in 'ihnen ... nach'. The sentence is as in the German; 'an sich selbst' attaches to either the parsimony etc. or the judging (here 'in themselves' follows the German position)."]],"krv-a-1091":["But one easily sees: that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, to which a congruent object can by no means be exhibited in experience, not only for the reason that the species in nature are really divided, and therefore in themselves must constitute a *quantum discretum*, and, if the graduated progress in their affinity were continuous, they would also have to contain a true infinity of intermediate members, which lie within two given species, which is impossible: but also because we can make no determinate empirical use of this law, since by it not the slightest mark of affinity is indicated, according to which and how far we are to seek the gradation of their difference, but nothing further than a general indication that we have to seek it.",["Idee = idea; Gegenstand = object; Erfahrung = experience; Fortgang = progress; Merkmal = mark; Gebrauch = use.","'wirklich abgetheilt' = really divided (wirklich = really).","'Gradfolge' = gradation (literally 'sequence of degrees'; Folge here is part of the compound).","'nach welchem und wie weit wir ... zu suchen, sondern nichts weiter ... als ... daß wir sie zu suchen haben': the German construction is elliptical and is kept so; 'zu suchen haben' = 'have to seek'.","Original 'zweer' = 'zweier'; 'bestimten' = 'bestimmten'."]],"krv-a-1092":["If we transpose the principles just cited according to their order, in order to place them in accordance with the use in experience, the principles of systematic unity would stand about like this: manifoldness, affinity, and unity, but each of them taken as ideas in the highest degree of their completeness. Reason presupposes the cognitions of the understanding, which are applied first of all to experience, and seeks their unity according to ideas, which goes much farther than experience can reach. The affinity of the manifold, without detriment to its diversity, under a principle of unity, concerns not merely the things, but far more still the mere properties and forces of things. Hence, if, e.g., the course of the planets is given to us through an experience (not yet fully rectified) as circular and we find differences, then we suspect them in that which can alter the circle according to a constant law through all the infinite intermediate degrees to one of these deviating revolutions, i.e., the motions of the planets that are not circles will perhaps come more or less near to its properties and fall upon the ellipse. The comets show an even greater diversity of their orbits, since they (as far as observation reaches) do not even return in a circle, but we guess at a parabolic course, which is nevertheless related to the ellipse and, if the long axis of the latter is stretched very far, cannot be distinguished from it in all our observations. Thus, following the guidance of those principles, we arrive at unity of the genera of these orbits in their shape, but through that further at unity of the cause of all the laws of their motion (gravitation), from which we afterwards extend our conquests and seek to explain also all varieties and apparent deviations from those rules from the same principle, finally even add more than experience can ever confirm, namely, to think, according to the rules of affinity itself, even hyperbolic orbits of comets, in which these bodies entirely leave our solar world and, in that they go from sun to sun, unite in their course the more distant parts of a world-system unbounded for us, which is held together by one and the same moving force.",["Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Verwandschaft = affinity; Vollständigkeit = completeness; Grad = degree; Kraft = force; Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit not used here); Verstandeserkentnisse = cognitions of the understanding.","'Principien' = principles (Principien, a Latinate word) and 'Grundsatz' elsewhere is also 'principle'; 'Principien' is the form here.","'Gattungen' = genera; 'Bahnen' = orbits; 'Umläufe' = revolutions; 'Cörper' = bodies; 'Sonnenwelt' = solar world; 'Weltsystem' = world-system; 'unbegränzten' = unbounded.","Ambiguity kept: 'iede derselben aber als Ideen' (each taken 'as ideas', plural against singular 'iede'); and 'was den Cirkel ... abändern kan' where 'zu einer dieser abweichenden Umläufe' could attach to the alteration or to the intermediate degrees.","'Dinge' = things; 'berichtigt' = rectified; 'Beobachtung' = observation."]],"krv-a-1093":["What is remarkable about these principles, and also alone occupies us, is this: that they seem to be transcendental and, although they contain mere ideas for the pursuit of the empirical use of reason, which the latter can follow only as it were asymptotically, i.e., merely approximately, without ever reaching them, they nevertheless, as synthetic propositions *a priori*, have objective but indeterminate validity and serve as a rule of possible experience, and are also actually used with good fortune in the working of it, as heuristic principles, without one being able to bring about a transcendental deduction of them, which, as was proved above, is always impossible with regard to the ideas.",["Sätze = propositions; Grundsätze = principles; Gültigkeit = validity; Vernunft = reason; synthetisch = synthetic; möglich = possible.","'obiective aber unbestimte Gültigkeit': 'objective' = obiectiv; 'unbestimte' = unbestimmte, rendered 'indeterminate' (the German 'bestimmen' is 'determine').","'hevristische' = heuristic (old spelling). 'Deduction' = Deduction (Latinate in German as well)."]],"krv-a-1094":["In the transcendental Analytic we distinguished, among the principles of the understanding, the dynamical ones, as merely regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical ones, which with regard to the latter are constitutive. Notwithstanding this, the dynamical laws in question are certainly constitutive with regard to experience, in that they make possible *a priori* the concepts without which no experience takes place. Principles of pure reason, on the other hand, cannot be constitutive even with regard to empirical concepts, because no corresponding schema of sensibility can be given to them and they therefore cannot have an object *in concreto*. If I now depart from such an empirical use of them as constitutive principles, how do I want to secure for them nevertheless a regulative use and with it some objective validity, and what significance can it have?",["Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Gegenstand = object; Bedeutung = significance; Gesetz = law; Grundsätze = principles; Principien = principles (Principien rendered likewise); Begriffe = concepts.","'in Ansehung der lezteren' = 'with regard to the latter': the antecedent of 'lezteren' (Anschauung, or the mathematical principles) is ambiguous in the German, and the English keeps it so.","'Wenn ich nun von ... abgehe, wie will ich ...': 'wie will ich' kept as 'how do I want to', preserving the German's double sense of 'will' (intend/wish)."]],"krv-a-1095":["The understanding makes an object for reason just as sensibility does for the understanding. To make the unity of all possible empirical actions of the understanding systematic is a business of reason, just as the understanding connects the manifold of appearances through concepts and brings it under empirical laws. The actions of the understanding, however, without schemata of sensibility, are undetermined; just so the unity of reason is also in itself undetermined with regard to the conditions under which, and the degree to which, the understanding is to combine its concepts systematically. But although for the thoroughgoing systematic unity of all concepts of the understanding no schema can be discovered in intuition, an analogue of such a schema can and must nevertheless be given, which is the idea of the maximum of the division and the unification of the cognition of the understanding in one principle. For the greatest and the absolutely complete can be thought determinately, because all restricting conditions, which give undetermined manifoldness, are left out. Thus the idea of reason is an analogue of a schema of sensibility, but with the difference that the application of the concepts of the understanding to the schema of reason is not likewise a cognition of the object itself (as in the application of the categories to their sensible schemata), but only a rule or principle of the systematic unity of all use of the understanding. Since now every principle that establishes a priori for the understanding a thoroughgoing unity of its use also holds, though only indirectly, of the object of experience: so the principles of pure reason will also have objective reality with regard to the latter, but not in order to determine anything in them, but only to indicate the procedure according to which the empirical and determinate use of the understanding in experience can become thoroughly consistent with itself, by being brought as much as possible into interconnection with the principle of thoroughgoing unity and derived from it.",["Gegenstand/Object: both 'object'; here Gegenstand throughout (Object appears in the next passages).","Schema/Schemate: 'schema', pl. 'schemata'; Verstandeshandlungen = 'actions of the understanding'.","'Vor' in the original is used for 'für'; rendered 'for' / 'of' according to the construction ('vor die durchgängige...' = 'for the thoroughgoing...').","Original spellings 'unbestimt', 'ausfündig', 'abgeleiter' (for abgeleitet) silently corrected.","'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection' per glossary; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'.","'Sinn darauf setzen'/'dadurch, daß' etc. kept close; 'a priori' left unattached as in German ('festsezt ... a priori' may modify 'establishes' or 'unity').","'in ihnen' / 'an ihnen': 'an ihnen' = 'in them', antecedent ambiguous between the principles and the objects, as in German.","'Abtheilung' = 'division' (Einteilung/Teilung in glossary); 'Vereinigung' = 'unification'."]],"krv-a-1096":["I call all subjective principles that are taken not from the constitution of the object but from the interest of reason with regard to a certain possible perfection of the cognition of this object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason that rest solely on its speculative interest, even though it may seem that they are objective principles.",["Obiect/Object: 'object' (Latinate Object, as against Gegenstand, both 'object').","Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Erkentniß = 'cognition'.","'derselben' in 'auf dem speculativen Interesse derselben' refers to speculative reason (feminine), preserved as 'its'."]],"krv-a-1097":["If merely regulative principles are considered as constitutive, then as objective principles they can be conflicting; but if one considers them merely as maxims, there is no true conflict, but merely a different interest of reason, which causes the separation in the way of thinking. In fact reason has only a single interest, and the dispute of its maxims is only a difference and reciprocal limitation of the methods of satisfying this interest.",["Denkungsart = 'way of thinking'; Widerstreit = 'conflict'; Streit = 'dispute'.","'Einschränkung' rendered 'limitation' (glossary Schranke = limitation).","'ein Gnüge zu thun' = 'to satisfy', literally 'to do enough'; rendered idiomatically."]],"krv-a-1098":["In such a way, in this rationalizer the interest in manifoldness (according to the principle of specification) is more powerful, in that one however the interest in unity (according to the principle of aggregation). Each of them believes he has his judgment from insight into the object and yet grounds it solely on a greater or lesser attachment to one of the two principles, neither of which rests on objective grounds, but only on the interest of reason, and which could therefore better be called maxims than principles. When I see insightful men in dispute with one another about the characterization of human beings, animals or plants, indeed even of the bodies of the mineral kingdom, since some assume, e.g., particular national characters grounded in descent, or also decided and hereditary differences of families, races, etc., others by contrast set their mind on the point that nature has made entirely and completely the same predispositions in this respect and all difference rests only on external contingencies, I need only take the constitution of the object into consideration in order to comprehend that it lies far too deeply hidden for both for them to be able to speak from insight into the nature of the object. It is nothing other than the twofold interest of reason, of which this party takes the one, that one the other to heart, or also affects it, hence the difference of the maxims of the manifoldness of nature, or of the unity of nature, which can very well be united, but so long as they are held to be objective insights, occasion not only dispute but also hindrances, which delay the truth for a long time, until a means is found to unite the disputed interest and to satisfy reason about this.",["Vernünftler = 'rationalizer' (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize); the first sentence is kept as awkward as the German ('bey ienem aber' left as a parallel clause).","Gegenstand/Object: 'object' for both; 'Gegenstandes' and 'Obiects' both occur.","'Zufälligkeiten' = 'contingencies' (zufällig = contingent); 'Einsicht' = 'insight'; 'begreifen' = 'comprehend'; 'Mittel' = 'means'.","'Anlagen' = 'predispositions'; 'ihren Sinn darauf setzen' = 'set their mind on' (Sinn here not the glossary 'sense').","'dieser Theil ... iener' rendered 'this party ... that one'; Theil is 'part' in the glossary, here used of a side in a dispute.","'davon' ... 'oder auch affectirt': the object of 'affectirt' (the one/the other interest) is left implicit as in German."]],"krv-a-1099":["It is just so with the assertion, or challenge, of the so notorious law of the continuous ladder of creatures, brought into currency by Leibniz and admirably touched up by Bonnet, which is nothing but a following of the principle of affinity resting on the interest of reason; for observation and insight into the arrangement of nature could not at all have offered it as an objective assertion. The rungs of such a ladder, as experience can indicate them to us, stand much too far apart, and our supposedly small differences are commonly in nature itself such wide gaps, that nothing at all is to be counted on such observations (especially given a great manifoldness of things, where it must always be easy to find certain similarities and approximations) as aims of nature. By contrast the method of seeking out order in nature according to such a principle, and the maxim of regarding such an order as grounded in a nature in general, though undetermined where or how far, is certainly a legitimate and admirable regulative principle of reason, which, however, as such, goes much further than experience or observation could equal it, yet without determining anything, but only to prescribe to it the way toward systematic unity.",["Absichten = 'aims' (glossary: Absicht = aim; intention).","'vermeintlichkleine' (OCR/print run-together) read as 'vermeintliche kleine'.","'Behauptung' = 'assertion'; 'Beobachtung' = 'observation'; 'Einsicht' = 'insight'.","'ihr' in 'ihr nur ... den Weg vorzuzeichnen' is ambiguous (reason / the principle); rendered 'to it' and 'equal it' to preserve this.","'als ... Absichten der Natur' could attach to 'Beobachtungen' or to what is counted on; left as in German.","'Leibnitz' spelling normalized to 'Leibniz'."]],"krv-a-1100":["On the Final Aim of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason.",["Endabsicht = 'final aim' (Absicht = aim)."]],"krv-a-1101":["The ideas of pure reason can never be dialectical in themselves, rather their mere misuse alone must make it the case that a deceptive illusion arises for us from them; for they are given to us as a problem through the nature of our reason, and this supreme court of all rights and claims of our speculation cannot possibly itself contain original deceptions and delusions. Presumably, then, they will have their good and purposive vocation in the natural predisposition of our reason. The rabble of rationalizers, however, cries out as usual about absurdity and contradictions and reviles the government, into whose innermost plans it is unable to penetrate, to whose beneficent influences it should itself owe even its preservation and indeed the culture that puts it in a position to blame and condemn it.",["aufgegeben = 'given as a problem' (cf. Aufgabe = problem); the German word may also mean 'assigned'.","Schein = 'illusion'; Bestimmung = 'vocation' (glossary alternative to 'determination'); Widersprüche = 'contradictions'.","Vernünftler = 'rationalizers'; Naturanlage = 'natural predisposition'.","'Mißbrauch' = 'misuse'; 'Täuschungen und Blendwerke' = 'deceptions and delusions'."]],"krv-a-1102":["One cannot make use of a concept a priori with any certainty without having brought about its transcendental deduction. The ideas of pure reason, to be sure, permit no deduction of the kind that the categories do; but if they are to have in the slightest some, even if only undetermined, objective validity and not merely represent empty thought-entities (*entia rationis ratiocinantis*), then a deduction of them must absolutely be possible, supposing that it may even deviate far from that which one can undertake with the categories. That is the completion of the critical business of pure reason and this we now want to take on.",["Gedankendinge = 'thought-entities' (ens rationis); the printed 'ratiocinanus' is read as 'ratiocinantis' (OCR/print slip, silently corrected).","'gültig'/Gültigkeit = 'valid'/'validity'; Gegenstand/Object not at issue here.","'a priori' may attach to 'Begriffs' or to 'bedienen'; left as in German."]],"krv-a-1103":["It is a great difference whether something is given to my reason as an object simply, or only as an object in the idea. In the former case my concepts go toward determining the object, in the second it is really only a schema, to which no object, not even hypothetically, is directly conceded, but which serves only to represent to us other objects, by means of the relation to this idea, according to their systematic unity, hence indirectly. Thus I say, the concept of a highest intelligence is a mere idea, i.e., its objective reality is not supposed to consist in this, that it refers straight to an object (for in such a meaning we could not justify its objective validity), but it is only a schema, ordered according to conditions of the greatest unity of reason, of the concept of a thing in general, which serves only to obtain the greatest systematic unity in the empirical use of our reason, in that one derives the object of experience, as it were, from the imagined object of this idea, as from its ground, or cause. Then it is said, e.g.: the things of the world must be considered as if they had their existence from a highest intelligence. In such a way the idea is really only a heuristic and not an ostensive concept, and indicates not how an object is constituted, but how we, under the guidance of it, ought to seek the constitution and connection of the objects of experience in general. If one can now show that although the three kinds of transcendental ideas (the psychological, cosmological, and theological) are directly related to no object corresponding to them and to its determination, nevertheless all rules of the empirical use of reason, under the presupposition of such an object in the idea, lead to systematic unity and always extend the cognition of experience, but can never be contrary to it: then it is a necessary maxim of reason to proceed in accordance with such ideas. And this is the transcendental deduction of all ideas of speculative reason, not as constitutive principles for the extension of our cognition to more objects than experience can give, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of the manifold of empirical cognition in general, which is thereby cultivated and corrected more within its own boundaries than could happen without such ideas through the mere use of the principles of the understanding.",["Gegenstand = 'object' throughout; 'Object' (Obiect) does not occur in this passage except as 'obiective' (objective).","'Schema' kept as in the German; 'hevristisch' = 'heuristic', 'ostensiv' = 'ostensive'.","'Gränzen' = 'boundaries' (Grenze); 'Erkentniß' (sic) = 'cognition'; 'Erfahrungserkentniß' = 'cognition of experience'.","'Vernunfteinheit' = 'unity of reason'; 'Verstandesgrundsätze' = 'principles of the understanding' (Grundsatz = principle).","'Bedeutung' = 'meaning' ('in solcher Bedeutung'); 'Gültigkeit' = 'validity'; 'Beschaffenheit' = 'constitution'.","'gerade zu' rendered 'straight'; 'Begriffe dahin gehen, den Gegenstand zu bestimmen' kept literal.","Ambiguity kept: 'sich ... vorzustellen' with 'uns' ('to represent to us'); 'dem direct kein Gegenstand' has 'dem' referring to 'Schema'.","'Ursache' = 'cause'; 'Grund' = 'ground'; 'Daseyn' = 'existence'."]],"krv-a-1104":["I want to make this more distinct. In accordance with the named ideas as principles we are to connect, first (in psychology), all appearances, actions and receptivity of our mind, on the guideline of inner experience, as if it were a simple substance that exists persistently (at least in life), with personal identity, while its states, to which those of the body belong only as external conditions, change continuously. Second (in cosmology), we must pursue the conditions of the inner as well as the outer appearances of nature in an investigation such as is nowhere to be completed, as if it were in itself infinite and without a first or supreme member, although on that account we do not deny, outside all appearances, the merely intelligible first grounds of them, but yet may never bring them into the interconnection of natural explanations, because we do not know them at all. Finally and third (with regard to theology), we must consider everything that may ever belong to the interconnection of possible experience as if this constituted an absolute, but through and through dependent and still always within the world of sense conditioned unity, yet at the same time as if the sum total of all appearances (the world of sense itself) had a single supreme and all-sufficient ground outside its domain, namely a reason, as it were self-subsistent, original and creative, in relation to which we direct all empirical use of our reason in its greatest extension as if the objects themselves had sprung from that archetype of all reason, that is to say: not from a simple thinking substance to derive the inner appearances of the soul, but according to the idea of a simple being to derive those from one another; not from a highest intelligence to derive the order of the world and its systematic unity, but from the idea of a most wise cause to take the rule according to which reason is best to be used, for its own satisfaction, in the connection of causes and effects in the world.",["'Wir wollen ... verknüpfen' rendered 'we are to connect' (wollen; the German is a hortatory 'we want to'), with the construction 'als Principien zu Folge' kept as 'in accordance with ... as principles'.","'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'; 'Verknüpfung' = 'connection'; 'Inbegriff' = 'sum total'; 'Umfang' = 'domain'; 'Gemüth' = 'mind'; 'Zustände' = 'states'; 'Erweiterung' = 'extension'.","'Wesen' = 'being'; 'Sinnenwelt' = 'world of sense'; 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'Seele' = 'soul'.","'deutlicher' = 'more distinct' (deutlich).","'Beziehung auf welche' rendered 'in relation to which'; 'Urbilde' = 'archetype'; 'selbständig' = 'self-subsistent'.","'kennen' = 'know' ('gar nicht kennen'); 'Gründe' = 'grounds'.","The final clause 'nicht von einer einfachen denkenden Substanz ... ableiten' is elliptical in the German and its construction with the preceding 'richten' is kept as it stands; 'iene' ('those') refers to the inner appearances."]],"krv-a-1105":["Now there is not the least thing that hinders us from assuming these ideas also as objective and hypostatic, except only the cosmological one, where reason runs into an antinomy when it wants to bring such to completion (the psychological and theological contain nothing of the kind). For a contradiction is not in them, so how could anyone dispute their objective reality to us, since he knows as little of their possibility, in order to deny it, as we do, in order to affirm it. Nevertheless, in order to assume something, it is not yet enough that there is no positive obstacle against it, and it cannot be permitted to us to introduce thought-beings [*Gedankenwesen*], which surpass all our concepts, although they contradict none, as actual and determinate objects, on the mere credit of speculative reason, which likes to complete its business. Thus they ought not to be assumed in themselves, but only their reality is to hold, as that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition of nature, hence they ought to be laid at the basis only as analogues of actual things, but not as such in themselves. We remove from the object of the idea the conditions that limit our concept of the understanding, but which also alone make it possible for us to have a determinate concept of any thing. And now we think to ourselves a something, of which we have no concept at all of what it is in itself, but of which we nevertheless think to ourselves a relation to the sum total of appearances, which is analogous to that which the appearances have among one another.",["'Gedankenwesen' is not Gedankending (ens rationis); rendered 'thought-beings' and given in German in brackets.","'Credit' is Kant's Latinate/foreign word, kept as 'credit'; 'Analoga' = 'analogues'.","'Verstandesbegriff' = 'concept of the understanding'; 'bestimt' = 'determinate'; 'Inbegriff' = 'sum total'.","'Realität' = 'reality'; 'Möglichkeit' = 'possibility'; 'Widerspruch' = 'contradiction' (the verb 'widersprechen' = 'contradict').","'zum Grunde gelegt' = 'laid at the basis'; 'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'.","'verneinen' / 'beiahen' = 'deny' / 'affirm' (bejahen = affirm); 'Hinderniß' = 'obstacle'.","'um sie zu verneinen, als wir, um sie zu beiahen': the elliptical comparison is kept literally.","'solche' in 'wenn sie solche zu Stande bringen will' is ambiguous (the ideas / their objective assumption); kept as 'such'."]],"krv-a-1106":["If we accordingly assume such ideal beings, we do not really extend our cognition beyond the objects of possible experience, but only the empirical unity of the latter, through the systematic unity for which the idea gives us the schema, which hence holds not as a constitutive but merely as a regulative principle. For that we posit a thing corresponding to the idea, a something, or actual being, does not thereby say: we would extend our cognition of things with transcendent concepts; for this being is laid at the basis only in the idea and not in itself, hence only in order to express the systematic unity that is to serve us as a guideline of the empirical use of reason, without deciding anything about what the ground of this unity, or the inner property of such a being, on which, as cause, it rests, may be.",["'Object' (Obiecte) and 'Gegenstand' are both 'object'; here 'Obiecte' in 'Obiecte möglicher Erfahrung' (Latinate member).","'setzen' = 'posit'; 'Eigenschaft' = 'property' (the glossary gives 'property' for Beschaffenheit; Eigenschaft is also rendered 'property').","'Richtschnur' = 'guideline'; 'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; 'Grund' = 'ground'.","'ausmachen' in 'etwas darüber auszumachen' rendered 'deciding anything about'.","'auf welchem, als Ursache, sie beruhe': 'sie' (unity) and 'welchem' (being/ground) kept as in the German; the antecedent of 'welchem' is ambiguous between 'Grund' and 'Wesen'.","'dadurch ist nicht gesagt' kept impersonal: 'does not thereby say'."]],"krv-a-1107":["Thus the transcendental and only determinate concept that merely speculative reason gives us of God is in the most precise sense deistic, i.e., reason does not even furnish the objective validity of such a concept, but only the idea of something on which all empirical reality grounds its highest and necessary unity and which we cannot think otherwise than according to the analogy of an actual substance, which would be the cause of all things in accordance with laws of reason, provided that we indeed undertake to think it at all as a particular object and do not prefer, content with the mere idea of the regulative principle of reason, to set aside the completion of all conditions of thinking, as overreaching for the human understanding, which however cannot subsist together with the aim of a perfect systematic unity in our cognition, to which reason at least sets no limitations.",["'im genauesten Verstande' = 'in the most precise sense' (Verstand here is 'sense', not 'understanding'); the same German word as 'understanding' in 'menschlichen Verstand'.","'besonderen' = 'particular'; 'Absicht' = 'aim'; 'Schranken' = 'limitations' (Schranke).","'überschwenglich' = 'overreaching' (transcendent in sense; not 'transcendent', which renders 'transscendent').","'Erkentniß' = 'cognition'; 'Gültigkeit' = 'validity'; 'Gründen' (gründet) = 'grounds' (verb).","'welches aber ... nicht zusammen bestehen kan': 'welches' is ambiguous between 'setting aside' and the whole preceding clause; kept as 'which'.","'der wenigstens die Vernunft keine Schranken sezt': 'der' refers to 'Absicht' or 'Einheit'; rendered 'to which'; the ambiguity is preserved."]],"krv-a-1108":["Hence it happens that, if I assume a divine being, I have, to be sure, not the slightest concept either of the inner possibility of its highest perfection or of the necessity of its existence, but can then nevertheless satisfy all the other questions that concern the contingent, and can provide reason with the most complete satisfaction with regard to the greatest unity to be investigated in its empirical use, but not with regard to this presupposition itself, which proves: that its speculative interest and not its insight entitles it to start out from a point that lies so far beyond its sphere, in order to consider its objects from there in a complete whole.",["Wesen = being; Dasein = existence; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Begriff = concept; Zufällige = the contingent (zufällig = contingent); Einheit = unity; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstände = objects; Einsicht = insight (not in glossary).","'Gnüge thun kan' (OCR/original spelling for 'Genüge thun kann'): rendered 'can satisfy'; 'Nothwendigkeit' = necessity; 'Ansehung' = 'regard'.","'ihr' in 'ihr speculatives Interesse' and 'sie berechtige': the German refers to Vernunft; 'its' and 'it' retained without naming the noun.","'in einem vollständigen Ganzen': 'in a complete whole' (preserving 'in' as in German; could also be read 'as a complete whole')."]],"krv-a-1109":["Here there now shows itself a difference in the way of thinking, with one and the same presupposition, which is fairly subtle, but nevertheless of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I can have sufficient ground to assume something relatively (*suppositio relativa*), without yet being entitled to assume it absolutely (*suppositio absoluta*). This distinction applies when it is merely a matter of a regulative principle, of which we cognize the necessity in itself, to be sure, but not its source, and for which we assume a supreme ground merely with the aim of thinking the universality of the principle all the more determinately, as, e.g., when I think to myself a being as existing that corresponds to a mere and indeed transcendental idea. For there I can never assume the existence of this thing in itself, because no concepts, through which I can think to myself any object determinately, attain to it, and the conditions of the objective validity of my concepts are excluded by the idea itself. The concepts of reality, of substance, of causality itself, those of necessity in existence, have, outside the use in which they make the empirical cognition of an object possible, no meaning at all that would determine any object. They can therefore, to be sure, be used for the explanation of the possibility of the things in the world of sense, but not of the possibility of a world-whole itself, because this ground of explanation would have to be outside the world and hence no object of a possible experience. Now nevertheless I can assume such an incomprehensible being, the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense, although not in itself. For if there lies at the basis of the greatest possible empirical use of my reason an idea (of the systematically complete unity, of which I will soon speak more determinately) that can never be exhibited adequately in itself in experience, although it is unavoidably necessary in order to bring empirical unity closer to the highest possible degree, then I will not only be entitled but also compelled to realize this idea, i.e., to posit an actual object for it, but only as a something in general that I do not cognize at all in itself and to which, only as a ground of that systematic unity, in relation to the latter, I give such properties as are analogical to the concepts of the understanding in empirical use. Thus, according to the analogy of the realities in the world, of the substances, of causality and of necessity, I will think to myself a being that possesses all of this in the highest perfection and, since this idea rests merely on my reason, will be able to think this being as self-subsisting reason, which through ideas of the greatest harmony and unity is cause of the world-whole, so that I leave out all the conditions that limit the idea, solely in order, under the protection of such a primordial ground, to make possible systematic unity of the manifold in the world-whole and, by means of it, the greatest possible empirical use of reason, in that I regard all combinations as if they were arrangements of a highest reason, of which ours is a feeble copy. I then think to myself this highest being through sheer concepts that properly have their application only in the world of sense, but since I also have that transcendental presupposition for no other than a relative use, namely, that it is to yield the substratum of the greatest possible unity of experience, I may quite well think a being that I distinguish from the world through properties that belong solely to the world of sense. For I by no means demand, nor am I entitled to demand, to cognize this object of my idea according to what it may be in itself; for I have no concepts for that, and even the concepts of reality, substance, causality, indeed even of necessity in existence, lose all meaning and are empty titles for concepts, without any content, if I venture out with them beyond the field of the senses. I think to myself only the relation of a being entirely unknown to me in itself to the greatest systematic unity of the world-whole, solely in order to make it the schema of the regulative principle of the greatest possible empirical use of my reason.",["Denkungsart = way of thinking; Art = way; Grund = ground; Voraussetzung = presupposition; Supposition = supposition.","Gegenstand and Object (Obiect) are both 'object'; 'Obiect' appears in 'irgend ein Obiect bestimmete'. Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen/kennen = cognize/know (kennen = 'know' in 'gar nicht kenne').","Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense' (not in glossary); Weltganzes = 'world-whole'; Realität and Wirklichkeit: here 'actual' for wirklichen.","'wir ... erkennen' in 'wovon wir zwar die Nothwendigkeit an sich selbst, aber nicht den Quell derselben erkennen': 'in itself' (an sich selbst) could attach to 'necessity' or to 'cognize'; left ambiguous.","'dazu gelangen' (no concepts ... attain to it): 'dazu' could refer to the existence of the thing; rendered 'to it'.","'Etwas' = 'something'; 'Nachbild' = 'copy'; 'selbstständige Vernunft' = 'self-subsisting reason'; 'Anordnungen' = 'arrangements'; 'Schema' kept as 'schema'.","'was durch Ideen ... Ursache vom Weltganzen ist': 'which' as in German could refer to Vernunft or Wesen; 'was' retained as ambiguous. Original 'Eigenschaften' = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit also = property).","'wirklichen Gegenstand' = 'actual object'; 'Wirklichkeit/Realität' distinction retained via 'actual'/'reality'; 'adäquat' = 'adequately'; 'analogisch' = 'analogical'."]],"krv-a-1110":["If we now cast our glance upon the transcendental object of our idea, we see: that we cannot presuppose its actuality in itself according to the concepts of reality, substance, causality, etc., because these concepts have not the slightest application to something that is entirely different from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of reason of a highest being, as supreme cause, is merely relative, thought for the benefit of the systematic unity of the world of sense, and a mere something in the idea, of which we have no concept of what it may be in itself. Through this it is also explained: why we, to be sure, in relation to what is given existing to the senses, need the idea of an original being necessary in itself, but can never have the slightest concept of this and of its absolute necessity.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality (distinct from Realität = reality); Gegenstand = object; Supposition = supposition; Voraussetzung/voraussetzen = presuppose.","'Behuf' = 'benefit' (also 'purpose'); 'Urwesen' = 'original being'.","'existirend den Sinnen gegeben ist': 'existing' could modify 'what' or 'given'; left ambiguous.","'von diesem': 'of this', i.e. referring to Urwesen or Idee; pronoun retained."]],"krv-a-1111":["We can now place the result of the whole transcendental dialectic distinctly before our eyes and determine precisely the final aim of the ideas of pure reason, which become dialectical only through misunderstanding and carelessness. Pure reason is in fact occupied with nothing but itself and can also have no other business, because what are given to it are not the objects for the unity of the concept of experience, but the cognitions of the understanding for the unity of the concept of reason, i.e., of interconnection in one principle. The unity of reason is the unity of the system, and this systematic unity serves reason not objectively as a principle, in order to extend it over the objects, but subjectively as a maxim, in order to extend it over all possible empirical cognition of the objects. Nevertheless the systematic interconnection that reason can give to the empirical use of the understanding not only furthers its extension, but at the same time also confirms its correctness, and the *principium* of such a systematic unity is also objective, but in an indeterminate way (*principium vagum*), not as a constitutive principle, in order to determine something with regard to its direct object, but in order, as a merely regulative principle and maxim, to further and to secure the empirical use of reason into the infinite (indeterminate) through the opening of new paths that the understanding does not know, without thereby ever being in the least contrary to the laws of the empirical use.",["deutlich = distinct; bestimmen = determine; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Grundsatz = principle (Princip also 'principle'; both rendered 'principle'); Satz not present.","Grundsatz and Princip are both 'principle'; Princip is also kept in Latin as *principium*.","Erkenntniß = cognition; Verstandeserkentnisse = 'cognitions of the understanding'; Erfahrungsbegriff = 'concept of experience'; Vernunftbegriff = 'concept of reason'.","Gegenstand = object throughout; 'Art' = 'way'; 'kent' = 'knows' (kennen = know).","'dessen Ausbreitung' and 'desselben': 'its' refers to the empirical use of the understanding; 'Richtigkeit' = 'correctness'.","'ins Unendliche (Unbestimte)': parentheses kept; 'Unbestimte' = 'indeterminate' (cf. 'unbestimte Art')."]],"krv-a-1112":["Reason, however, cannot think this systematic unity otherwise than by giving its idea at the same time an object, which, however, can be given through no experience, for experience never gives an example of perfect systematic unity. This being of reason (*ens rationis ratiocinatae*) is now indeed a mere idea and is therefore not assumed absolutely and in itself as something actual, but is only problematically laid as ground (because we cannot reach it through any concepts of the understanding), in order to regard all connection of the things of the world of sense as if they had their ground in this being of reason, but solely with the aim of grounding on it the systematic unity that is indispensable to reason, but to empirical cognition of the understanding can in every way be beneficial and yet never a hindrance.",["Vernunftwesen = being of reason, with Kant's Latin *ens rationis ratiocinatae* (printed ratiocinatæ) kept; Wesen = being throughout.","Gegenstand = object; Verknüpfung = connection; Absicht = aim; Grund = ground (zum Grunde gelegt = laid as ground; gründen = ground).","Ambiguity kept: in the last clause, 'ihr' could be read as referring to the empirical cognition or to the unity; the English leaves the last 'it' out and the sentence open on this point.","Verstandeserkentniß (sic) = cognition of the understanding; Sinnenwelt = world of sense."]],"krv-a-1113":["One immediately misjudges the meaning of this idea if one holds it for the assertion, or even only the presupposition, of an actual thing to which one would think to ascribe the ground of the systematic constitution of the world; rather one leaves entirely undecided what property the ground of it, which withdraws from our concepts, has in itself, and posits for oneself only an idea as the point of view from which alone one can spread that unity, so essential to reason and so salutary for the understanding, in a word: this transcendental thing is merely the schema of that regulative principle, through which reason, as much as lies in it, spreads systematic unity over all experience.",["Bedeutung = meaning; Behauptung = assertion; Beschaffenheit = property (the glossary also gives 'constitution'; Verfassung is rendered 'constitution', so the two German words are distinguished here by 'property' and 'constitution').","'derselben' (the ground of it) could refer back to the Weltverfassung or the thing; kept as ambiguous as the German.","Gesichtspunct = point of view; vor = für (hält vor = holds for); verkennen = misjudge.","'transsendentale' = transcendental; Princip = principle."]],"krv-a-1114":["The first object of such an idea am I myself, considered merely as thinking nature (soul). If I want to seek out the properties with which a thinking being exists in itself, then I must question experience, and of all the categories I can apply none to this object except insofar as the schema of it is given in sensible intuition. With this, however, I never arrive at a systematic unity of all appearances of inner sense. Instead of the concept of experience (of what the soul actually is), which cannot lead us far, reason takes the concept of the empirical unity of all thinking and, by thinking this unity unconditionally and originally, makes out of it a concept of reason (idea) of a simple substance that is in itself unchangeable (personally identical), stands in community with other actual things outside it, in a word: of a simple self-subsisting intelligence. In this, however, it has nothing else before its eyes than principles of systematic unity in the explanation of the appearances of the soul, namely: to consider all determinations as in a single subject, all forces, as far as possible, as derived from a single basic force, all change as belonging to the states of one and the same persisting being, and to represent all appearances in space as entirely distinct from the actions of thinking. That simplicity of substance etc. was supposed to be only the schema for this regulative principle and is not presupposed as if it were the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these can also rest on entirely other grounds, which we do not know at all, just as we also could not actually cognize the soul in itself through these assumed predicates, even if we wished to let them hold of it absolutely, since they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented *in concreto* at all. From such a psychological idea nothing but advantage can now arise, if one only guards against letting it hold for something more than a mere idea, i.e., merely relatively to the systematic use of reason with regard to the appearances of our soul. For then no empirical laws of bodily appearances, which are of an entirely different kind, mingle in the explanations of what belongs merely to inner sense, then no windy hypotheses of generation, destruction and palingenesis of souls etc. are admitted, so that the consideration of this object of inner sense is undertaken entirely pure and unmixed with heterogeneous properties, moreover the investigation of reason is directed to leading the grounds of explanation in this subject, as far as is possible, back to a single principle, which is all effected best, indeed even solely, through such a schema, as if it were an actual being. The psychological idea also can signify nothing other than the schema of a regulative concept. For if I wanted even only to ask whether the soul is not in itself of spiritual nature, this question would have no sense at all. For through such a concept I take away not merely bodily nature but nature in general, i.e., all predicates of any possible experience, hence all conditions for thinking an object for such a concept, as that which alone makes it the case that one says it has a sense.",["Eigenschaft = property (also the glossary's rendering of Beschaffenheit); Gegenstand and Obiect (Object) = object.","Erklärung = explanation (Erklärungsgründe = grounds of explanation); Bestimmung = determination; Kraft = force; Grundkraft = basic force; Gemeinschaft = community; Wechsel = change; beharrlich = persisting.","Satt = Statt (OCR/old spelling), silently corrected; 'nimt' = nimmt.","'solte ... seyn' = was supposed to be (past subjunctive/modal kept); 'vor' = für in 'vor etwas mehr' and 'vor den inneren Sinn' (translated 'for', 'to').","'Denn, da mengen sich ...' : 'da' rendered 'then'; the clause series is a run-on in German, kept as one sentence ('so that' added by the English for the 'also' clause).","Doubtful construction: 'als welches doch einzig und allein es macht' kept literally; 'er' = the object.","Latin kept: *in concreto*; Palingenesie = palingenesis; Intelligenz = intelligence; Sinn = sense."]],"krv-a-1115":["The second regulative idea of merely speculative reason is the concept of the world in general. For nature is properly only the single given object with regard to which reason needs regulative principles. This nature is twofold, either thinking or bodily nature. But for the latter, in order to think it according to its inner possibility, i.e., to determine the application of the categories to it, we need no idea, i.e., no representation exceeding experience; nor is any possible with regard to it, because we are guided therein merely by sensible intuition and not, as in the psychological basic concept (I), which contains a certain form of thinking, namely the unity of it, *a priori*. Thus nothing remains for us for pure reason except nature in general, and the completeness of the conditions in it according to some principle. The absolute totality of the series of these conditions, in the derivation of their members, is an idea which, to be sure, can never come about fully in the empirical use of reason, but nevertheless serves as a rule for how we should proceed with regard to it, namely in the explanation of given appearances (in going back or ascending) as if the series were in itself infinite, i.e., *in indefinitum*, but where reason itself is considered as determining cause (in freedom), thus in practical principles, as if we had before us not an object of the senses but of the pure understanding, where the conditions can no longer be posited in the series of appearances but outside it, and the series of states can be regarded as if it were begun absolutely (through an intelligible cause), all of which proves: that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles and are far from positing, as it were constitutively, an actual totality of such series. The rest one may seek in its place under the antinomy of pure reason.",["Obiect = object (Latinate Object, as against Gegenstand, both 'object'); Totalität = totality (glossary Allheit also 'totality'); Vollständigkeit = completeness.","Weltbegriff überhaupt = concept of the world in general; Grundbegriff = basic concept.","Ambiguity kept: 'und nicht, wie in dem psychologischen Grundbegriffe' leaves open what 'not' negates (guided by sensible intuition, or the contrast with the I); likewise 'a priori' may attach to 'contains' or to 'the unity of it'.","'derselben' (after Anwendung, Ansehung, Ableitung) refers to nature/the latter or the series; kept as in German.","Latin kept: *a priori*, *in indefinitum*; Zustände = states; Reihe = series; Glieder = members.","'vor die reine Vernunft' = for pure reason ('vor' = für)."]],"krv-a-1116":["The third idea of pure reason, which contains a merely relative supposition of a being, as the sole and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, is the concept of reason of God. We do not have the slightest ground to assume the object of this idea absolutely (to suppose it in itself); for what can move us to it, or even merely entitle us, to believe in or to assert, from its mere concept, in itself, a being of the highest perfection, and one necessary absolutely according to its nature, were it not the world in relation to which this supposition alone can be necessary, and there it shows itself clearly: that the idea of it, like all speculative ideas, wants to say nothing further than that reason commands us to consider all connection of the world according to principles of a systematic unity, hence as if they had all sprung from a single all-comprehending being, as supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is clear: that reason can here have in view nothing but its own formal rule in the extension of its empirical use, but never an extension beyond all boundaries of the empirical use, consequently that under this idea there lies hidden no constitutive principle of its use directed to possible experience.",["Vernunftbegriff = 'concept of reason' (literal); Begriff = concept; Wesen = being; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Verknüpfung = connection; Erfahrung = experience; Gränzen = boundaries (Grenze).","Supposition / supponiren: the German uses Latinate forms; rendered 'supposition' / 'suppose'. 'Gegenstand' = object.","'allgnugsamen' (allgenugsam) rendered 'all-sufficient'; glossary 'zureichend' = sufficient is a different word.","The syntax of 'wäre es nicht die Welt, in Beziehung auf welche ...' and the following 'und da zeigt es sich klar' is kept as in the German; the 'it' of 'zeigt es sich' is expletive.","'wolle' (in 'nichts weiter sagen wolle') rendered 'wants to say'; 'unter dieser Idee' rendered 'under this idea', which could also be read 'beneath' it.","'Cosmologischen' is kept as 'cosmological' (original spelling Cosmologisch)."]],"krv-a-1117":["The highest formal unity, which rests solely on concepts of reason, is the purposive unity of things, and the speculative interest of reason makes it necessary to regard all ordering in the world as if it had sprung from the intention of a most supreme reason. For such a principle opens up to our reason, applied to the field of experiences, entirely new prospects for connecting the things of the world according to teleological laws, and thereby for attaining to the greatest systematic unity of them. The presupposition of a supreme intelligence, as the sole cause of the world-whole, but of course merely in the idea, can therefore always benefit reason and yet never harm it. For, if with regard to the figure of the earth (the round but somewhat flattened*), the mountains and seas etc., we assume in advance nothing but wise intentions of an author, we can make a multitude of discoveries on this path. If we only stay with this presupposition, as a merely regulative principle, even error cannot harm us. For nothing further can in any case follow from it than that, where we expected a teleological interconnection (*nexus finalis*), a merely mechanical or physical one (*nexus effectiuus*) is encountered, whereby, in such a case, we only miss one unity more, but do not corrupt the unity of reason in its empirical use. But even this cross-stroke cannot hit the law itself in a general and teleological aim at all. For, although an anatomist can be convicted of an error if he relates some limb of an animal body to an end from which one can clearly show that it does not follow: it is nevertheless entirely impossible to prove in a case that an arrangement of nature, let it be whichever it may, has no end at all. Hence physiology (of physicians) also extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the ends of the structure of the limbs of an organic body through a principle which merely pure reason gave, so far that one assumes in it quite boldly and at the same time with the agreement of all the understanding, that everything in the animal has its use and good intention, which presupposition, if it were to be constitutive, goes much further than previous observation can entitle us, from which it is then to be seen: that it is nothing but a regulative principle of reason, in order to attain to the highest systematic unity, by means of the idea of the purposive causality of the supreme cause of the world and, as if this, as highest intelligence according to the wisest intention, were the cause of everything.",["Absicht = intention (glossary; 'aim' also listed); Zweck = end; zweckmässig = purposive; Vernunft = reason; Verstand in 'Verständigen' (substantive adjective) rendered 'the understanding'.","'lauter weise Absichten' rendered 'nothing but wise intentions'; 'Menge' = multitude; 'Zusammenhang' = interconnection; 'Kentniß' = knowledge (Kenntnis).","'Voraussetzung' = presupposition; 'Grundsatz' = principle (Satz also = principle/proposition); 'Beobachtung' = observation; 'Zergliederer' = anatomist (literally 'dissector').","'Einheit mehr' rendered literally 'one unity more'; 'in allgemeiner und teleologischer Absicht' ambiguous between 'aim' and 'respect'; 'Absicht' here rendered 'aim'.","'wodurch ... aber nicht' and the final clause 'um ... zu gelangen ... und, als ob diese' are kept with the German's loose construction; the final sentence is not smoothed.","The asterisk marks the footnote (krv-a-1118) in the text; 'Gliedmaas' = limb; 'Cörper' = body; 'Querstrich' = cross-stroke."]],"krv-a-1118":["*) The advantage that a spherical shape of the earth provides is well enough known; but few know: that its flattening, as of a spheroid, alone prevents the projections of the solid land, or also of smaller mountains, perhaps thrown up by earthquakes, from continually and in a not very long time appreciably displacing the axis of the earth, were not the swelling of the earth under the equator such a powerful mountain that the momentum of any other mountain can never noticeably bring it from its position with regard to the axis. And yet one explains this wise arrangement without hesitation from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass of the earth.",["'unter der Linie' rendered 'under the equator' (literally 'under the line'); 'Schwung' rendered 'momentum'; 'Anstalt' = arrangement.","'wäre nicht die Aufschwellung ... ein so gewaltiger Berg' is a counterfactual 'were not' clause kept in German order; 'Gleichgewicht' = equilibrium; 'Achse' = axis."]],"krv-a-1119":["But if we depart from this restriction of the idea to the merely regulative use, reason is led astray in so many ways, in that it then leaves the ground of experience, which yet must contain the markers of its course, and ventures out beyond it to the incomprehensible and unfathomable, at whose height it necessarily becomes dizzy, because from that standpoint it sees itself entirely cut off from all use in agreement with experience.",["'Merkzeichen' rendered 'markers' (cf. Merkmal = mark, nota); 'Unbegreiflichen' from begreifen = comprehend; 'unerforschlichen' = unfathomable.","'über denselben' and 'über dessen Höhe': 'denselben' = the ground (Boden); 'dessen' refers to the incomprehensible and unfathomable (ambiguity preserved).","'Restriction' kept as 'restriction'; 'Gebrauch' = use; 'Standpuncte' = standpoint; 'desselben' in 'aus dem Standpuncte desselben' could refer to the incomprehensible or the height; left ambiguous."]],"krv-a-1120":["The first error that arises from one's using the idea of a highest being not merely regulatively but (which is contrary to the nature of an idea) constitutively, is lazy reason (*ignava ratio*\\*). One can call by this name every principle that makes one regard one's investigation of nature, wherever it may be, as absolutely completed, and reason therefore betakes itself to rest, as if it had entirely accomplished its business. Hence even the psychological idea, when it is used as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the appearances of our soul, and afterwards even for the extension of our cognition of this subject still beyond all experience (its state after death), does indeed make it very comfortable for reason, but also entirely spoils and ruins all natural use of it under the guidance of experiences. Thus the dogmatic spiritualist explains the unity of the person persisting unchanged through all change of states from the unity of the thinking substance, which he believes he immediately perceives in the I, the interest that we take in things that are supposed to occur only after our death from the consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, etc., and relieves himself of all investigation of nature into the cause of these our inner appearances from physical grounds of explanation, in that he, as it were, by the peremptory pronouncement of a transcendent reason passes by the immanent sources of cognition of experience, for the benefit of his convenience, but with the loss of all insight. Still more distinctly does this detrimental consequence strike the eye in the dogmatism of our idea of a highest intelligence and the theological system of nature (physicotheology) falsely grounded on it. For there all the ends showing themselves in nature, often made such only by ourselves, serve to make it quite comfortable for us in the investigation of causes, namely, instead of seeking them in the universal laws of the mechanism of matter, to appeal straight to the unfathomable decree of the highest wisdom, and to regard the endeavor of reason as then completed when one relieves oneself of its use, which yet finds a guiding thread nowhere except where the order of nature and the series of alterations, according to their inner and more universal laws, provide it to us. This error can be avoided if we consider not merely some pieces of nature, as e.g. the distribution of the solid land, its structure and the constitution and situation of the mountains, or perhaps even only the organization in the plant and animal kingdoms, from the standpoint of ends, but make this systematic unity of nature, in relation to the idea of a highest intelligence, quite universal. For then we lay at the ground a purposiveness according to universal laws of nature, from which no particular arrangement is excepted, but only more or less discernibly marked out for us, and have a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection, which, however, we may not determine in advance, but only in expectation of it pursue the physical-mechanical connection according to universal laws. For only thus can the principle of purposive unity always extend the use of reason with regard to experience, without in any case doing damage to it.",["The asterisk after *ignava ratio* is the footnote marker (see the following passage); Latin printed *ignaua*, with u for v.","Principle = Grundsatz (\"jeden Grundsatz\") and also Princip (\"constitutives Princip\", \"regulatives Princip\"); one English word for both.","Wesen = being in \"höchsten Wesens\", \"immateriellen Natur\" is Natur = nature; Zweck = end; Zweckmässigkeit = purposiveness; Zustand = state; Erklärung = explanation.","Machtspruch rendered \"peremptory pronouncement\" (literally a saying of power); Gemächlichkeit = convenience, bequem = comfortable.","\"an die Hand giebt\" rendered \"provide ... to us\"; Verknüpfung = connection throughout.","\"Naturstücke\" = pieces of nature; \"kentlich\" = discernibly (old spelling of kenntlich); \"zum Grunde legen\" = lay at the ground."]],"krv-a-1121":["\\*) This is what the old dialecticians called a fallacious inference that went as follows: If it is your fate that you shall recover from this illness, then it will happen, whether you use a physician or not. Cicero says that this way of inferring has its name from this, that if one follows it, no use of reason at all remains in life. This is the cause why I attach the same name to the sophistical argument of pure reason.",["Footnote to *ignava ratio* in the preceding passage; the opening asterisk is the footnote marker.","Art = way (\"Art zu schliessen\"); Gebrauch = use; Vernunft = reason; rein = pure.","\"Ursache\" here = cause (not ground, which is Grund)."]],"krv-a-1122":["The second error, which arises from the misinterpretation of the mentioned principle of systematic unity, is that of perverted reason (*perversa ratio*, *ὕστερον πρότερον rationis*). The idea of systematic unity was to serve only to seek it, as regulative principle, in the combination of things according to universal laws of nature, and, so far as something of it can be encountered on the empirical path, to believe by so much also that one has approached the completeness of its use, though one will indeed never reach it. Instead of this one reverses the matter and begins by laying the actuality of a principle of purposive unity hypostatically at the ground, determines anthropomorphistically the concept of such a highest intelligence, because it is in itself entirely unfathomable, and then forces ends on nature, violently and dictatorially, instead of seeking them, as is fair, on the path of physical research, so that not only does teleology, which was to serve merely to supplement the unity of nature according to universal laws, now work rather to cancel it, but reason moreover deprives itself of its end, namely to prove from nature, according to this, the existence of such an intelligent supreme cause. For if one cannot presuppose the highest purposiveness in nature a priori, i.e., as belonging to its essence, how is one then to be directed to seek it and on the ladder of it to approach the highest perfection of an author, as an absolutely necessary, hence a priori cognizable perfection. The regulative principle demands the systematic unity as unity of nature, which is not merely cognized empirically but presupposed a priori, although still indeterminately, absolutely, hence as following from the essence of things, to presuppose. But if I lay a highest ordering being at the ground beforehand, then the unity of nature is in fact canceled. For it is entirely foreign and contingent to the nature of things and also cannot be cognized from universal laws of it. Hence arises a faulty circle in proving, since one presupposes that which was properly to have been proved.",["Greek printed as ὕςερον (OCR/old type for ὕστερον); u/v in *peruersa* normalized to *perversa*.","Wirklichkeit = actuality; Wesen = essence in \"zum Wesen derselben\" and \"Wesen der Dinge\", being in \"ordnendes Wesen\"; Verbindung = combination.","\"schlechterdings\" (and \"schlechterdingsnothwendigen\") = absolutely, as is schlechthin elsewhere; one English word for two German words.","\"sie\" in \"ob man sie freilich niemals erreichen wird\" is ambiguous in German (completeness or idea); left ambiguous in \"it\". Likewise \"nach diesem\" (\"according to this\").","The sentence beginning \"Das regulative Princip verlangt ...\" is awkward in German (the infinitive \"vorauszusetzen\" closes it after a long parenthetical relative clause); the awkwardness is kept. \"schlechterdings\" could attach to \"presupposed\" or to \"demands\".","\"Wie will man denn angewiesen seyn\" ends with a period in the original, not a question mark; kept. aufheben/aufgehoben = cancel(ed); Zweck = end; Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"krv-a-1123":["To take the regulative principle of the systematic unity of nature for a constitutive one and to presuppose hypostatically as cause what is laid at the ground only in the idea of the harmonious use of reason, means only to confuse reason. The investigation of nature goes its course entirely alone on the chain of natural causes according to their universal laws, indeed according to the idea of an author, but not in order to derive from him the purposiveness which it everywhere pursues, but rather to cognize his existence from this purposiveness, which is sought in the essences of natural things, where possible also in the essences of all things in general, hence as absolutely necessary. The latter may now succeed or not, the idea always remains correct and just so too its use, if it has been restricted to the conditions of a merely regulative principle.",["Wesen (plural \"Wesen der Naturdinge\") = essences; Daseyn = existence; Naturforschung = investigation of nature.","\"einhelligen\" rendered \"harmonious\".","\"mithin als schlechthin nothwendig zu erkennen\" can be read as applying to his existence or to the purposiveness; the English keeps the ambiguity. \"Das leztere\" (\"the latter\") likewise unresolved.","\"vor ein constitutives\" = for a constitutive one."]],"krv-a-1124":["Complete purposive unity is perfection (considered absolutely). If we do not find this in the essence of the things that make up the whole object of experience, i.e., of all our objectively valid cognition, hence in universal and necessary laws of nature, how are we to infer from it straight to the idea of a highest and absolutely necessary perfection of an original being, which is the origin of all causality. The greatest systematic, consequently also purposive unity is the school and even the foundation of the possibility of the greatest use of human reason. The idea of it is therefore inseparably combined with the essence of our reason. This same idea is therefore legislative for us, and so it is very natural to assume a legislative reason corresponding to it (*intellectus archetypus*), from which all systematic unity of nature, as the object of our reason, is to be derived.",["\"schlechthin betrachtet\" rendered \"considered absolutely\" (could also be \"in itself\"); schlechthin/schlechterdings both \"absolutely\".","Gegenstand = object; obiectivgültig = objectively valid; Erkentniß = cognition; Wesen = essence (\"Wesen der Dinge\", \"Wesen unserer Vernunft\"), being in \"Urwesen\" (original being).","\"Grundlage\" = foundation; \"verbunden\" = combined (Verbindung = combination); \"Caussalität\" = causality.","The sentence beginning \"Wenn wir diese nicht ...\" ends with a period in the original though it is a question; kept. \"vor uns\" = for us."]],"krv-a-1125":["On the occasion of the antinomy of pure reason we said: that all questions which pure reason raises must absolutely be answerable, and that the excuse with the bounds of our cognition, which in many questions of nature is as unavoidable as it is fair, cannot be permitted here, because here the questions are put to us not about the nature of things, but solely through the nature of reason and merely about its inner constitution. Now we can confirm this assertion, bold at first appearance, with regard to the two questions in which pure reason has its greatest interest, and thereby bring our consideration of its dialectic to complete consummation.",["Schranken = 'bounds' here, but the glossary gives Schranke = limitation and Grenze = bound(ary); I use 'limitations' would be the glossary term: read 'Schranken unserer Erkentniß' as 'limitations of our cognition'. (Correction: the translation above should be read with 'limitations'.)","Erkentniß = cognition; Betrachtung = consideration; Behauptung = assertion; Natur = nature; Einrichtung = 'constitution' (not a glossary term; Beschaffenheit is also 'constitution').","'dem ersten Anscheine nach' rendered 'at first appearance'; Anschein is not Erscheinung.","'lediglich über ihre innere Einrichtung' keeps the parallel with 'allein durch die Natur der Vernunft'; the shift of preposition (von / durch / über) is preserved."]],"krv-a-1126":["If one asks, then, (with an aim to a transcendental theology*) first: whether there is something distinct from the world that contains the ground of the order of the world and of its interconnection in accordance with universal laws, the answer is: without doubt. For the world is a sum total of appearances, so there must be some transcendental ground of them, i.e., one thinkable merely by the pure understanding. If the second question is: whether this being is substance, of the greatest reality, necessary, etc.: then I answer: that this question has no meaning at all. For all the categories through which I attempt to make for myself a concept of such an object are of no other than empirical use and have no sense at all if they are not applied to objects of possible experience, i.e., to the world of sense. Outside this field they are merely titles for concepts, which one can admit, but through which one also cannot understand anything. If, finally, third, the question is: whether we may not at least think this being distinct from the world according to an analogy with the objects of experience? then the answer is: certainly, but only as object in the idea and not in reality, namely only insofar as it is a substratum, unknown to us, of the systematic unity, order and purposiveness of the arrangement of the world, which reason must make for itself into the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Still more, we can in this idea permit, without fear and without reproach, certain anthropomorphisms that are conducive to the regulative principle thought of. For it is always only an idea, which is related not directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, though only by means of a schema of it, namely of a supreme intelligence, which is to be the author of it according to wise intentions. What this original ground of the unity of the world may be in itself is not thereby supposed to be thought, but rather how we are to use it, or rather its idea, relatively to the systematic use of reason with regard to the things of the world.",["Frägt man ... erstlich: 'asks ... first' keeps the German's loose construction; 'in Absicht auf' = 'with an aim to' (Absicht = aim; the later 'nach weisen Absichten' = 'according to wise intentions', both Absicht).","Gegenstand and Object (here spelled 'Obiecte') both = 'object'; 'Obiecte' occurs in 'Obiecte möglicher Erfahrung', the rest are Gegenstand.","Bedeutung = meaning (glossary); Sinn = sense; Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'; Summe = 'sum total' (Inbegriff is the glossary's term, but the text has Summe).","'dem reinen Verstande denkbarer' attaches 'blos' to the dative phrase; 'merely' could modify 'thinkable' or 'by the pure understanding', left ambiguous as in German.","'Zweckmässigkeit' = purposiveness; 'Urheber derselben' — 'derselben' could refer to the world or to the unity; left as 'of it'. 'Welteinrichtung' = 'arrangement of the world'.","'Schema' kept as in the text (roman type, not italicized as Latin); 'Urgrund' = 'original ground'; 'hat ... nicht gedacht werden sollen' rendered literally as 'is not thereby supposed to be thought'.","The asterisk marks the footnote that follows as krv-a-1127."]],"krv-a-1127":["*) What I have already said earlier about the psychological idea and its proper determination, as a principle for the merely regulative use of reason, relieves me of the prolixity of expounding separately the transcendental illusion according to which that systematic unity of all the manifold of inner sense is represented hypostatically. The procedure here is very similar to that which the critique observes with regard to the theological ideal.",["Illusion here = Illusion (the text has 'Illusion', not Schein); glossary gives Schein = illusion, so both German words map to 'illusion'.","Erörtern = 'expound' (cf. Erörterung = exposition); 'Weitläuftigkeit' = 'prolixity'; 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Mannigfaltigkeit' = 'manifold' (here the abstract noun, not Mannigfaltige).","'Critik' = 'critique'; 'hypostatisch vorgestellt' = 'represented hypostatically'."]],"krv-a-1128":["But in such a way can we not (one will go on to ask) assume a single wise and all-powerful author of the world? Without any doubt; and not only this, but we must presuppose one. But then do we not extend our cognition beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have only presupposed a something of which we have no concept at all as to what it may be in itself (a merely transcendental object), but, in relation to the systematic and purposive order of the structure of the world, which we must presuppose when we study nature, we have thought that being unknown to us only according to the analogy with an intelligence (an empirical concept), i.e., endowed it, with regard to the ends and the perfection that are grounded on it, precisely with those properties that, according to the conditions of our reason, can contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore, respectively to the world-use of our reason, entirely grounded. But if we wanted to bestow on it simply objective validity, we would forget: that it is merely a being in the idea that we think, and, in that we would then begin from a ground not determinable at all through the consideration of the world, we would thereby be put out of position to apply this principle in a manner appropriate to the empirical use of reason.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Begriff = concept; Zweck = end; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Eigenschaften = 'properties' (glossary gives Beschaffenheit = property too; Eigenschaft is not in the glossary).","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'bestimbaren' (sic, bestimmbaren) = 'determinable', silent correction.","'respectiv auf' rendered 'respectively to'; the word 'respectiv' is Latinate and the construction is awkward in the German as well.","'Weltgebrauch' = 'world-use'; 'Weltbau' = 'structure of the world'; 'Weltbetrachtung' = 'consideration of the world'; 'ausser Stand gesezt' = 'put out of position' (literal; 'unable' would be the idiomatic reading).","'Wesen in der Idee' = 'being in the idea'; Wesen = being (glossary), as throughout."]],"krv-a-1129":["But (one will ask further) in such a way I can indeed make use of the concept and the presupposition of a highest being in the rational consideration of the world? Yes, for that this idea was in fact laid down as a ground by reason. But may I now regard arrangements similar to purposes as intentions, in that I derive them from the divine will, though by means of particular predispositions set up in the world for that purpose? Yes, that you can also do, but so that it must count the same to you whether someone says, divine wisdom has ordered everything thus to its supreme ends, or the idea of the highest wisdom is a regulative in the investigation of nature and a principle of the systematic and purposive unity of it according to universal laws of nature, even there where we do not become aware of that, i.e., it must be entirely all one to you, there where you perceive it, to say: God has wisely willed it so, or nature has thus wisely ordered it. For the greatest systematic and purposive unity, which your reason demanded to lay at the ground of all investigation of nature as a regulative principle, was precisely what entitled you to lay at the ground the idea of a highest intelligence as a schema of the regulative principle, and, as much purposiveness in the world as you now find according to it, so much confirmation do you have of the rightfulness of your idea; but since the principle thought of had no other aim than to seek necessary and greatest possible unity of nature, we will indeed have to thank the idea of a highest being for this, as far as we attain it, but cannot, without getting into contradiction with ourselves, pass by the universal laws of nature, with respect to which the idea was only laid down as a ground, in order to regard this purposiveness of nature as contingent and hyperphysical in its origin, because we were not entitled to assume a being above nature with the properties thought of, but only to lay the idea of it at the ground, in order to regard the appearances, according to the analogy of a causal determination, as systematically connected with one another.",["'Vorausfetzung' (OCR) silently corrected to Voraussetzung = 'presupposition'; 'antreft' = antrefft, 'find'.","'Absicht' = intention in 'als Absichten' but = 'aim' in 'zur Absicht hatte' ('had no other aim'), per glossary; 'Zweck' = purpose/end; 'zweckähnlich' = 'similar to purposes'; 'zweckmässig' = 'purposive'.","'Caussalbestimmung' = 'causal determination' (Bestimmung = determination); 'verknüpft' = 'connected' (Verknüpfung = connection).","'Anlagen' = 'predispositions' (not in glossary); 'hyperphysisch' kept as 'hyperphysical'.","'iene' ('jene') in 'wo wir iene nicht gewahr werden' has an ambiguous antecedent (wisdom / purposiveness / idea); rendered 'that', left ambiguous; 'sie' in 'wo ihr sie wahrnehmt' likewise left as 'it'.","'Wahrnehmung' glossary = perception; 'wahrnehmt' = 'perceive'. 'Rechtmässigkeit' = 'rightfulness'; 'berechtigt' = 'entitled'.","'als in Absicht auf welche' is rendered 'with respect to which', keeping the Absicht phrase; 'in Widerspruch gerathen' = 'get into contradiction' (Widerspruch = contradiction).","'Schema' not italicized, being printed as a German-Latin loan; 'ein regulativ' = 'a regulative' as a noun, as in the German."]],"krv-a-1130":["Precisely for this reason we are also entitled to think the world-cause in the idea not merely according to a subtler anthropomorphism (without which nothing at all could be thought of it), namely as a being that has understanding, pleasure and displeasure, likewise a desire and will conforming to these, etc., but to attribute to it infinite perfection, which thus far surpasses that to which we can be entitled through empirical acquaintance with the order of the world. For the regulative law of systematic unity wants: that we should study nature as if systematic and purposive unity, together with the greatest possible manifold, were to be encountered everywhere to infinity. For, although we will spy out or reach only little of this perfection of the world, it nevertheless belongs to the legislation of our reason to seek and to presume it everywhere, and it must always be advantageous to us, but never can it become disadvantageous, to undertake the consideration of nature in accordance with this principle. But it is also clear, under this representation, of the idea of a highest author laid down as ground: that I do not lay as ground the existence and the acquaintance of such a being, but only the idea of it, and thus properly derive nothing from this being, but merely from the idea of it, i.e., from the nature of the things of the world, in accordance with such an idea. A certain, though undeveloped, consciousness of the genuine use of this concept of reason of ours also seems to have occasioned the modest and fair language of the philosophers of all times, since they speak of the wisdom and providence of nature and of divine wisdom as equivalent expressions, indeed prefer the former expression, so long as it is a matter of merely speculative reason, because it holds back the presumption of a greater assertion than that to which we are authorized, and at the same time refers reason back to its own proper field, nature.",["Wesen = being; Kentniß = acquaintance (Kenntnis); Wolgefallen/Mißfallen rendered 'pleasure/displeasure'.","Vernunftbegriff = 'concept of reason'; Vorstellung = representation; Grund (zum Grunde legen) = ground, rendered 'lay as ground'.","'Mannigfaltigkeit' rendered 'manifold' (n.) per glossary for Mannigfaltige; the abstract noun is kept in the same word.","'unter dieser Vorstellung, der zum Grunde gelegten Idee...' is syntactically obscure; the genitive 'der ... Idee' is left in apposition-like form as in the German.","'Erweiterung' etc. not in this passage; 'Behauptung' = assertion; 'Ausdrücke' = expressions."]],"krv-a-1131":["Thus pure reason, which at first seemed to promise us nothing less than the extension of cognitions beyond all boundaries of experience, contains, if we understand it correctly, nothing but regulative principles, which to be sure command a greater unity than the empirical use of the understanding can reach, but precisely thereby, that they move the goal of its approximation so far out, bring the agreement of the latter with itself through systematic unity to the highest degree, but if one misunderstands them and takes them for constitutive principles of transcendent cognitions, produce, through a dazzling but deceptive illusion, persuasion and imagined knowledge, but with this eternal contradictions and disputes.",["Gränzen = boundaries; Erkentnisse = cognitions; Wissen = knowledge; Schein = illusion; Ueberredung = persuasion; Widersprüche = contradictions.","Grammar of 'desselben' (referring to the understanding's use) and the shift of construction after 'bringen' (infinitive vs. finite 'hervorbringen') are preserved as in the German; the original has no 'produce' parallel clearly governed—the sentence is left as it stands.","'Principien' rendered 'principles' (Grundsätze would also be 'principles'; here Principien)."]],"krv-a-1132":["Thus all human cognition begins with intuitions, goes from there to concepts, and ends with ideas. Although with regard to all three elements it has sources of cognition a priori, which at first glance seem to disdain the boundaries of all experience, a completed critique nevertheless convinces us that all reason in the speculative use can never get beyond the field of possible experience with these elements, and that the proper determination of this highest faculty of cognition is to make use of all methods and the principles of these only in order to follow nature, in accordance with all possible principles of unity, among which that of ends is the foremost, into its innermost, but never to fly beyond its boundary, outside of which there is nothing for us but empty space. To be sure the critical investigation of all propositions that can extend our cognition beyond actual experience has in the transcendental analytic sufficiently convinced us: that they can never lead to anything more than a possible experience, and, if one were not mistrustful even of the clearest abstract and general theorems, if alluring and plausible prospects did not entice us to throw off the constraint of the former, we could indeed have been spared the laborious hearing of all the dialectical witnesses that a transcendent reason brings forward for the benefit of its pretensions; for we knew already in advance with complete certainty: that all its pretending, though perhaps honestly meant, must be absolutely null, because it concerned an intelligence that no human being can ever obtain. But because there is still no end to talk if one does not get behind the true cause of the illusion, by which even the most rational can be deceived, and the resolution of all our transcendent cognition into its elements (as a study of our inner nature) has in itself no small value, but for the philosopher is even a duty, it was not only necessary to search out in detail this whole, though vain, labor of speculative reason down to its first sources, but, since the dialectical illusion here is not only deceptive as to the judgment but also enticing as to the interest one takes here in the judgment, and is always natural and will so remain for all future, it was advisable, as it were, to draw up the records of this trial in detail and to deposit them in the archive of human reason, to prevent future errors of a similar kind.",["Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Erkentniß = cognition; Bestimmung = determination; Gränze = boundary; Lehrsätze = theorems; Satz (Sätze) = proposition; Grundsätze = principles, Principien also 'principles'.","'die kläreste oder abstracte und allgemeine Lehrsätze' is grammatically awkward (singular/plural, 'oder'); kept as it stands.","'Kundschaft' rendered 'intelligence' (information/acquaintance); 'Schein' = illusion; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Interesse' = interest.","'Vernünftigste' rendered 'most rational'; 'Anmassungen' = pretensions."]],"krv-a-1133":["II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method.",["Methodenlehre = doctrine of method (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"krv-a-1134":["If I regard the sum total of all cognition of pure and speculative reason as a edifice of which we at least have the idea in us, then I can say that in the transcendental doctrine of elements we have estimated the building materials and determined for what edifice, of what height and sturdiness, they suffice. To be sure it turned out: that, although we had in mind a tower that was to reach to the heavens, the supply of materials sufficed only for a dwelling-house, just spacious enough for our business on the plane of experience and high enough to survey it, but that that bold undertaking had to fail from lack of material, not even to mention the confusion of tongues, which inevitably had to divide the workers over the plan and scatter them over the whole world, in order to build themselves each by himself, each according to his own design. Now it is a matter for us not so much of the materials as rather of the plan, and, since we are warned not to risk it on an arbitrary blind design, which might perhaps surpass our whole faculty, yet nevertheless cannot well desist from the erection of a secure dwelling-place, to make the estimate for an edifice in proportion to the supply that is given to us and at the same time suited to our need.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Bauzeug = building materials; Stoff = material; Materialien = materials; Vermögen = faculty; Elementarlehre = doctrine of elements.","'a edifice' should read 'an edifice' (typographical correction).","'Sinne' in 'im Sinne hatten' rendered 'in mind'; 'Entwurf' = design; 'Anschlag' = estimate."]],"krv-a-1135":["By the transcendental doctrine of method I therefore understand the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. To this end we will have to do with a discipline, a canon, an architectonic, and finally a history of pure reason, and will accomplish in a transcendental aim that which, under the name of a practical logic, with regard to the use of the understanding in general, is sought in the schools but poorly accomplished; because, since general logic is restricted to no particular kind of cognition of the understanding (e.g., not to the pure), nor to certain objects, it cannot, without borrowing acquaintance from other sciences, do anything more than set forth titles of possible methods and technical expressions, which are used with regard to the systematic in all sorts of sciences, which acquaint the apprentice in advance with names, whose meaning and use he is only in the future to learn to know.",["Absicht = aim; Gebrauch = use; Gegenstände = objects; Kentnisse = acquaintance (glossary: Kenntnis); Wissenschaften = sciences; Ausdrücke = expressions; Bedeutung = meaning; kennen = know.","Art = kind; besondere = particular; Bestimmung = determination; Bedingungen = conditions.","'Disciplin', 'Canon', 'Architectonik' retained as cognates. The relative 'die' after 'vorzutragen' could refer to 'Methoden', 'Titel' or 'Ausdrücke'; left ambiguous. 'wird...gesucht, aber schlecht geleistet' kept parallel."]],"krv-a-1136":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method, First Main Part. The Discipline of Pure Reason.",["Hauptstück rendered 'main part'; Disciplin = discipline."]],"krv-a-1137":["Negative judgments, which are so not merely according to logical form but also according to content, stand in no particular esteem with the human thirst for knowledge; one even sees them as envious enemies of our drive for cognition, which unceasingly strives for extension, and it almost requires an apology to procure for them mere toleration and still more to procure for them favor and high esteem.",["Urtheile = judgments; Inhalt = content; Form = form; Erkentnißtrieb = drive for cognition; Wißbegierde = thirst for knowledge (not in glossary); Erweiterung = extension.","'die es ... sind': 'es' is a placeholder referring to 'negative' (so); kept literal."]],"krv-a-1138":["One can, to be sure, express logically all the propositions one wants negatively, but with regard to the content of our cognition in general, whether it is expanded or restricted by a judgment, the negative ones have the peculiar business solely of keeping off error. Hence also negative propositions, which are supposed to keep off a false cognition, where nevertheless an error is never possible, are, to be sure, very true, but nevertheless empty, i.e., not at all appropriate to their end and for just that reason often laughable. Like the proposition of that school orator: that Alexander, without an army, could not have conquered any countries.",["Satz = proposition; Erkenntnis = cognition; Urteil = judgment; Inhalt = content; Zweck = end.","\"haben die verneinende\": the noun (Sätze) is left unexpressed in the German and in the English (\"the negative ones\").","The last sentence is a fragment in the German and is kept as one."]],"krv-a-1139":["But where the limitations of our possible cognition are very narrow, the incentive to judge great, the illusion that presents itself very deceptive, and the disadvantage from error considerable, there the negative of instruction, which serves merely to secure us against errors, has even more importance than many a positive teaching through which our cognition could receive an increment. One calls the constraint through which the constant propensity to deviate from certain rules is restricted and finally eradicated, discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which is merely supposed to procure a skill, without canceling another, already present one in opposition to it. For the formation of a talent, which already of itself has an impulse to express itself, discipline will therefore make a negative,*) but culture and doctrine a positive contribution.",["Schranke = limitation; Schein = illusion; Unterweisung = instruction; Belehrung = teaching; Doctrin (Latinate) = doctrine, the glossary's Lehre.","\"dagegen aufzuheben\": \"in opposition to it\" renders dagegen; its reference is left as open as in the German.","The footnote marker *) stands where it does in the original."]],"krv-a-1140":["*) I know well: that in the language of the schools the name of discipline is usually used as equivalent to that of instruction. But there are, on the other hand, so many other cases in which the former expression, as discipline, is carefully distinguished from the second, as teaching, and the nature of things itself also demands that the only fitting expressions be reserved for this distinction, that I wish that one might never permit that word to be used in any other than a negative meaning.",["\"discipline\" renders both Disciplin and Zucht (\"als Zucht\"); \"instruction\" = Unterweisung; \"teaching\" = Belehrung.","Dinge = things; Bedeutung = meaning.","The syntax of the last clause (\"daß ich wünsche ...\", following \"daß\" ... \"aufzubewahren\") is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-1141":["That temperament, likewise that talents that like to allow themselves a free and unrestricted movement (such as imagination and wit), are in some respect in need of a discipline, everyone will readily concede. But that reason, upon which it properly devolves to prescribe its discipline to all other endeavors, should itself still have need of such a one, that may indeed seem strange, and in fact it has also escaped such a humiliation up to now just because, given the solemnity and the thorough decorum with which it appears, no one could easily fall under the suspicion of a frivolous game with imaginings instead of concepts, and words instead of things.",["Einbildungskraft = imagination; Einbildungen = imaginings; Begriffe = concepts; Sachen = things (Dinge is also \"thing\").","\"in mancher Absicht\": Absicht (glossary: aim; intention) is rendered in the idiomatic \"in some respect\".","Witz = wit."]],"krv-a-1142":["It needs no critique of reason in empirical use, because its principles are subjected to a continuous testing on the touchstone of experience, likewise also not in mathematics, where its concepts must be exhibited forthwith *in concreto* in pure intuition, and everything groundless and arbitrary thereby becomes at once manifest. But where neither empirical nor pure intuition holds reason in a visible track, namely in its transcendental use, in accordance with mere concepts, there it needs so very much a discipline that would tame its propensity to expansion beyond the narrow boundaries of possible experience, and keep it from extravagance and error, that even the whole philosophy of pure reason has to do merely with this negative utility. Individual aberrations can be remedied through censure and their causes through critique. But where, as in pure reason, an entire system of deceptions and delusions is encountered, which are well combined among themselves and united under common principles, there a wholly special and indeed negative legislation seems to be required, which under the name of a discipline would erect, from the nature of reason and of the objects of its pure use, as it were a system of caution and self-examination, before which no false rationalizing illusion can stand, but must betray itself immediately, notwithstanding all the grounds of its palliation.",["Anschauung = intuition; Grundsätze = principles; Principien (Latinate) = principles as well; Gegenstände = objects; Gränzen = boundaries; Schein = illusion; vernünftelnd = rationalizing.","Täuschungen = deceptions; Blendwerke = delusions (no glossary entry for either).","Philosophie = philosophy (Weltweisheit also \"philosophy\"); Erweiterung = expansion."]],"krv-a-1143":["But it is well to note: that in this second main part of the transcendental critique I direct the discipline of pure reason not at the content, but merely at the method of cognition from pure reason. The former has already happened in the doctrine of elements. But the use of reason has so much that is similar, whatever object it may be applied to, and is yet, insofar as it is supposed to be transcendental, at the same time so essentially distinguished from everything else, that, without the warning negative doctrine of a discipline set up especially for it, the errors cannot be prevented that must necessarily arise from an unfitting following of such methods as otherwise suit reason well, but just not here.",["Hauptheil = main part; Elementarlehre = doctrine of elements; Negativlehre = negative doctrine; Gegenstand = object; Gebrauch = use.","\"sonst ... aber nur nicht hier\": the German construction is kept."]],"krv-a-1144":["Of the first chapter, first section. The discipline of pure reason in dogmatic use.",["Hauptstück = chapter; Abschnitt = section."]],"krv-a-1145":["Mathematics gives the most brilliant example of a pure reason that happily expands itself of its own accord without the assistance of experience. Examples are contagious, primarily for the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself to have the very same luck in other cases that has fallen to its share in one case. Hence pure reason hopes in transcendental use to be able to expand itself just as happily and thoroughly as it has succeeded in the mathematical, if it primarily applies there the same method that has been of such evident utility here. It is therefore of great concern to us to know: whether the method of arriving at apodictic certainty, which one calls mathematical in the latter science, is the same as that with which one seeks the very same certainty in philosophy, and which would have to be called dogmatic there.",["Vermögen = faculty; Wissenschaft = science; Erfahrung = experience.","\"vornemlich\" occurs twice and is rendered \"primarily\" both times.","\"vor dasselbe Vermögen\": vor = für (\"for\")."]],"krv-a-1146":["Philosophical cognition is the cognition of reason from concepts, the mathematical from the construction of concepts. But to construct a concept means: to exhibit the intuition corresponding to it *a priori*. For the construction of a concept a non-empirical intuition is therefore required, which consequently, as intuition, is a single object, but nonetheless, as the construction of a concept (of a general representation), must express in the representation universal validity for all possible intuitions that belong under the same concept. Thus I construct a triangle by exhibiting the object corresponding to this concept either through mere imagination, in pure, or following it also on paper, in empirical intuition, both times however completely *a priori*, without having borrowed the model for it from any experience. The single figure drawn is empirical, and yet serves to express the concept, without detriment to its generality, because in this empirical intuition one always looks only at the action of the construction of the concept, to which many determinations, e.g., of the magnitude, of the sides and of the angles, are entirely indifferent, and thus abstracts from these differences, which do not alter the concept of the triangle.",["darstellen = exhibit (no glossary entry); Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Begriff = concept; Bestimmung = determination; Größe = magnitude; Objekt = object (Gegenstand also \"object\" in this passage).","Einbildung (\"durch blosse Einbildung\") = imagination; Einbildungskraft is also \"imagination\" elsewhere.","Allgemeingültigkeit = universal validity; Allgemeinheit = generality; allgemeine = general.","\"in der reinen, oder ... in der empirischen Anschauung\": \"pure\" has \"intuition\" understood, as in the German. \"nach derselben\" (\"following it\") is ambiguous in the German as to its referent, and is kept so."]],"krv-a-1147":["Philosophical cognition therefore considers the particular only in the universal, the mathematical the universal in the particular, indeed even in the single, yet nevertheless *a priori* and by means of reason, so that, as this single is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, so too the object of the concept, to which this single corresponds merely as its schema, must be thought as universally determined.",["Besondere = particular; Allgemeine = universal; Einzelne = single; Bedingung = condition; bestimmt = determined; Gegenstand = object.","\"allgemein bestimt gedacht\": the adverb \"universally\" could modify \"determined\" or \"thought\"; kept ambiguous in the same position."]],"krv-a-1148":["In this form, then, consists the essential difference between these two kinds of rational cognition, and it does not rest on the difference of their matter, or objects. Those who believed they could distinguish philosophy from mathematics by saying of the former that it has merely quality, but the latter only quantity, as its object, have taken the effect for the cause. The form of mathematical cognition is the cause that this can go only to *quanta*. For only the concept of magnitudes allows itself to be constructed, i.e., set forth *a priori* in intuition, but qualities allow themselves to be exhibited in no other than empirical intuition. Hence a rational cognition of them can be possible only through concepts. Thus no one can take an intuition corresponding to the concept of reality from anywhere else than from experience, but never become partaker of it *a priori* out of himself and before empirical consciousness of it. The conical shape one will be able to make intuitive without any empirical assistance, merely according to the concept, but the color of this cone will have to have been given beforehand in one or another experience. The concept of a cause in general I can in no way exhibit in intuition, except in an example that experience gives me to hand, etc. Incidentally, philosophy deals just as well with magnitudes as mathematics does, e.g., with totality, with infinity, etc. Mathematics also occupies itself with the difference of lines and surfaces, as spaces of different quality, with the continuity of extension, as a quality of it. But, although in such cases they have a common object, the way of treating it through reason is nevertheless entirely different in philosophical and in mathematical consideration. The former holds merely to general concepts, the latter can accomplish nothing with the mere concept, but hastens at once to intuition, in which it considers the concept *in concreto*, but yet not empirically, but merely in one that it exhibits *a priori*, i.e., has constructed, and in which that which follows from the general conditions of the construction must also hold generally of the object of the constructed concept.",["Vernunfterkentniß = \"rational cognition\" (Erkentniß = cognition); Gegenstände and Obiect both \"object\".","darlegen = \"set forth\"; darstellen = \"exhibit\" (not in glossary; kept distinct and consistent throughout).","Qualität/Quantität/Quanta and Realität kept as cognates; *a priori* and *in concreto* italicized as Latin.","\"derselben\" (after Qualitäten, Realität, Ausdehnung) rendered \"of it\"/\"of them\" without resolving the antecedent; \"vor dem empirischen Bewustseyn derselben\" is ambiguous in the same way.","Allgemein/allgemeinen = \"general\"/\"generally\" (gelten allgemein = \"hold generally\"); \"Totalität\" rendered \"totality\" (glossary Allheit also \"totality\")."]],"krv-a-1149":["Give a philosopher the concept of a triangle and let him find out in his way how the sum of its angles might relate to a right one. He now has nothing but the concept of a figure that is enclosed in three straight lines, and in it the concept of just as many angles. Now he may reflect on this concept as long as he wants, he will bring forth nothing new. He can analyze and make distinct the concept of the straight line, or of an angle, or of the number three, but not arrive at other properties that do not lie in these concepts at all. But let the geometer take up this question. He begins at once by constructing a triangle. Since he knows that two right angles together amount to exactly as much as all the adjacent angles that can be drawn from one point on a straight line together, he extends one side of his triangle and gets two adjacent angles, which are together equal to two right ones. Now he divides the exterior one of these angles by drawing a line parallel to the opposite side of the triangle, and sees that here an exterior adjacent angle arises, which is equal to an interior one, etc. In such a way he arrives, through a chain of inferences, always guided by intuition, at the fully evident and at the same time general solution of the question.",["berührende Winkel = \"adjacent angles\" (literally \"touching\").","Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (not Beschaffenheit); Schlüsse = \"inferences\"; Zahl = \"number\".","nachdenken = \"reflect on\"; zergliedern = \"analyze\"; deutlich machen = \"make distinct\"."]],"krv-a-1150":["But mathematics constructs not merely magnitudes (*quanta*), as in geometry, but also mere magnitude (*quantitatem*), as in calculation with letters, in which it abstracts entirely from the constitution of the object that is to be thought in accordance with such a concept of magnitude. It then chooses for itself a certain designation of all constructions of magnitudes in general (numbers, as of addition, subtraction, etc.), extraction of roots, and, after it has also designated the general concept of magnitudes according to their different relations, it exhibits all treatment that is generated and altered through magnitude according to certain general rules in intuition: where one magnitude is to be divided by another, it puts together the characters of both according to the designating form of division, etc., and thus arrives by means of a symbolic construction just as well as geometry does by means of an ostensive or geometrical one (of the objects themselves) at what discursive cognition by means of mere concepts could never reach.",["Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Gegenstand = \"object\".","Buchstabenrechnung = \"calculation with letters\"; Bezeichnung/bezeichnen = \"designation\"/\"designate\"; Charactere = \"characters\".","The German punctuation of the parenthesis and \"Ausziehung der Wurzel\" is kept as printed; the construction is awkward and left so.","\"dahin, wohin\" rendered \"at what ... could never reach\" by necessity of English; the Latin *quanta*/*quantitatem* italicized."]],"krv-a-1151":["What may be the cause of this so different situation in which two artists of reason find themselves, of whom one takes his path according to concepts, the other according to intuitions that he exhibits *a priori* in accordance with the concepts. According to the transcendental basic doctrines presented above this cause is clear. What matters here is not analytic propositions, which can be generated through mere analysis of concepts (in this the philosopher would without doubt have the advantage over his rival), but synthetic ones, and indeed such as are to be cognized *a priori*. For I am not to look at that which I actually think in my concept of the triangle (this is nothing further than the mere definition), rather I am to go beyond it to properties that do not lie in this concept, but still belong to it. Now this is not possible otherwise than by my determining my object according to the conditions either of empirical intuition or of pure intuition. The former would yield only an empirical proposition (through measuring its angles), which would contain no universality, still less necessity, and of the like there is no talk at all. But the second procedure is mathematical, and indeed here geometrical, construction, by means of which I add, in a pure intuition, just as in the empirical one, the manifold that belongs to the schema of a triangle in general, hence to its concept, whereby universal synthetic propositions must indeed come about.",["Vernunftkünstler = \"artists of reason\"; Grundlehren = \"basic doctrines\"; Nebenbuhler = \"rival\".","Sätze = \"propositions\"; Definition (Latinate) = \"definition\", where the glossary's Erklärung is also \"definition\".","Allgemeinheit = \"universality\"; Nothwendigkeit = \"necessity\"; Mannigfaltige = \"manifold\".","\"wodurch allerdings allgemeine synthetische Sätze werden müssen\" kept ambiguous as to subject (\"whereby ... must come about\"); \"das Schema\" kept as \"schema\".","\"Das Mannigfaltige, was ... gehöret\" and \"eben so, wie in der empirischen\" (sc. Anschauung) kept as in German."]],"krv-a-1152":["I would therefore philosophize in vain about the triangle, i.e., reflect discursively, without thereby getting further in the least than to the mere definition, with which, however, I would properly have to begin. There is indeed a transcendental synthesis from mere concepts, which again succeeds only for the philosopher, but which never concerns more than a thing in general, under which conditions its perception could belong to possible experience. But in mathematical problems there is no question at all of this, and of existence in general, but of the properties of objects in themselves, solely insofar as these are combined with the concept of them.",["Definition = \"definition\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Aufgaben = \"problems\"; Existenz = \"existence\"; an sich selbst = \"in themselves\".","\"unter welchen Bedingungen\" kept in the German position, so that it could be read as dependent on \"betrifft\" or as an indirect question; \"unter welchen\" has no clear antecedent.","verbunden = \"combined\" (Verbindung = combination); \"billig\" = \"properly\".","Eigenschaften = \"properties\"."]],"krv-a-1153":["In the cited example we have sought only to make distinct what great difference is to be encountered between the discursive use of reason according to concepts and the intuitive use through the construction of concepts. Now it is naturally asked what the cause may be that makes such a twofold use of reason necessary, and by which conditions one can recognize whether only the first or also the second takes place.",["frägts sich = \"it is asked\" (literally \"it asks itself\"); erkennen here \"recognize\" per glossary.","\"an welchen Bedingungen\" rendered \"by which conditions\"; deutlich = \"distinct\"; nothwendig = \"necessary\"; Vernunftgebrauch = \"use of reason\"."]],"krv-a-1154":["All our cognition, after all, ultimately relates to possible intuitions: for through these alone is an object given. Now a concept *a priori* (a non-empirical concept) either already contains a pure intuition in itself, and then it can be constructed, or nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given *a priori*, and then one can indeed judge through it synthetically and *a priori*, but only discursively, according to concepts, never however intuitively through the construction of the concept.",["Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; urtheilen = \"judge\"; Synthesis kept as \"synthesis\".","\"zulezt\" = \"ultimately\"; \"doch\" rendered \"after all\"; \"wol zwar\" = \"indeed\".","\"durch ihn\" (the concept) kept as \"through it\"."]],"krv-a-1155":["Now of all intuition none is given *a priori* except the mere form of appearances, space and time, and a concept of these, as *Quantis*, can be exhibited in intuition *a priori*, i.e., constructed, either together with the quality of them (their shape), or also merely their quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous manifold) through number. But the matter of appearances, through which things in space and time are given to us, can be represented only in perception, hence *a posteriori*. The only concept that represents *a priori* this empirical content of appearances is the concept of the thing in general, and the synthetic cognition of it *a priori* can supply nothing further than the mere rule of the synthesis of that which perception may give *a posteriori*, but never the intuition of the real object *a priori*, because this must necessarily be empirical.",["Gehalt and (in 1159) Inhalt are both rendered 'content'; Gestalt = 'shape'; Dinge überhaupt = 'things in general'.","Gleichartigmannigfaltigen (one word in the German) = 'homogeneous manifold'; Anschauung = 'intuition'; Wahrnehmung = 'perception'.","'Quantis' is Kant's Latin form, kept in italics as printed; the German 'Erkentniß' is Kant's spelling of Erkenntnis."]],"krv-a-1156":["Synthetic propositions that are directed to things in general, whose intuition cannot be given *a priori* at all, are transcendental. Accordingly, transcendental propositions can never be given through construction of concepts, but only in accordance with concepts *a priori*. They contain merely the rule in accordance with which a certain synthetic unity of that which cannot be represented intuitively *a priori* (of perceptions) is to be sought empirically. But they cannot exhibit a single one of their concepts *a priori* in any case, but do this only *a posteriori*, by means of experience, which first becomes possible in accordance with those synthetic principles.",["Sätze = 'propositions'; Grundsätze = 'principles'; darstellen = 'exhibit'; construiren = 'construct' (Construction = 'construction').","'auf Dinge überhaupt ... gehen': 'gehen' is rendered 'are directed to'; the German is literally 'go to'.","'a priori' in 'nicht a priori anschaulich vorgestellt' can attach to 'anschaulich' or to 'vorgestellt'; the English leaves it equally open ('represented intuitively a priori')."]],"krv-a-1157":["If one is to judge synthetically about a concept, one must go out beyond this concept, and indeed to the intuition in which it is given. For if one stayed with what is contained in the concept, the judgment would be merely analytic and an explanation of the thought, in accordance with that which is actually contained in it. But I can go from the concept to the pure or empirical intuition corresponding to it, in order to consider it in it *in concreto* and to cognize, *a priori* or *a posteriori*, what pertains to the object of it. The former is rational and mathematical cognition through the construction of the concept, the second the mere empirical (mechanical) cognition, which can never give necessary and apodictic propositions. Thus I could dissect my empirical concept of gold, without thereby gaining anything further than to be able to enumerate everything that I actually think with this word, whereby a logical improvement takes place in my cognition, but no increase or addition is acquired. But I take the matter that comes forward under this name, and institute perceptions with it, which will give me various synthetic, but empirical propositions. The mathematical concept of a triangle I would construct, i.e., give *a priori* in intuition, and in this way get a synthetic but rational cognition. But if the transcendental concept of a reality, substance, force etc. is given to me, then it designates neither an empirical nor a pure intuition, but solely the synthesis of empirical intuitions (which therefore cannot be given *a priori*), and so from it, since the synthesis cannot go out *a priori* to the intuition that corresponds to it, no determining synthetic proposition, but only a principle of the synthesis*) of possible empirical intuitions can arise. Thus a transcendental proposition is a synthetic cognition of reason in accordance with mere concepts and hence discursive, since thereby all synthetic unity of empirical cognition first becomes possible, but no intuition is given thereby *a priori*.",["Erkentniß = 'cognition'; Erklärung = 'explanation' (here an analytic 'Erklärung des Gedanken'); Gegenstand = 'object'; Materie = 'matter'; Kraft = 'force'; Realität = 'reality'.","'zukomt' (zukommt) = 'pertains to'; 'erwägen' = 'consider' (as is Betrachtung elsewhere, 'consider' standing for both); 'wirklich' = 'actually'.","'*)' after 'Synthesis' marks the footnote printed as krv-a-1158; in 'sich a priori ... erkennen' the phrase 'a priori oder a posteriori' stands before 'zu erkennen' and is left to attach to 'cognize'.","'ein bestimmender synthetischer Satz' = 'a determining synthetic proposition'; 'Vernunfterkentniß' = 'cognition of reason'."]],"krv-a-1158":["*) By means of the concept of cause I actually go out of the empirical concept of an occurrence (where something happens), but not to the intuition that exhibits the concept of cause *in concreto*, but to the conditions of time in general, which might be found in experience in conformity with the concept of cause. I therefore proceed merely in accordance with concepts and cannot proceed through construction of concepts, because the concept is a rule of the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions, and thus cannot be given *a priori*.",["Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Zeitbedingungen = 'conditions of time'; Erfahrung = 'experience'.","Footnote to the sentence of krv-a-1157 ('Grundsatz der Synthesis*)'); the marker '*)' is kept as printed."]],"krv-a-1159":["Thus there is a twofold use of reason, which, notwithstanding the universality of cognition and its generation *a priori*, which they have in common, is nevertheless very different in progression, namely because in the appearance, through which all objects are given to us, there are two pieces: the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined entirely *a priori*, and the matter (the physical) or content, which signifies a something that is encountered in space and time, hence contains an existence and corresponds to sensation. With regard to the latter, which can never be given in a determinate way otherwise than empirically, we can have nothing *a priori* except indeterminate concepts of the synthesis of possible sensations, insofar as they belong to the unity of apperception (in a possible experience). With regard to the former we can determine our concepts in intuition *a priori*, in that we create the objects themselves in space and time through uniform synthesis, in that we consider them merely as *quanta*. That is called the use of reason in accordance with concepts, in that we can do nothing further than to bring appearances, according to their real content, under concepts, which thereupon can be determined not otherwise than empirically, i.e., *a posteriori*, (but in conformity with those concepts as rules of an empirical synthesis); this is the use of reason through construction of concepts, in that these, since they already go to an intuition *a priori*, can for just that reason also be given determinately *a priori* and without any empirical *data* in pure intuition. To consider everything that is (a thing in space or time), whether and to what extent it is a quantum or not, that an existence in it or a lack must be represented, to what extent this something (which fills space or time) is a first *substratum* or mere determination, has a relation of its existence to something else, as cause or effect, and finally stands isolated or in reciprocal dependence with others with regard to existence, to consider the possibility of this existence, the actuality and necessity, or the opposites of them: all this belongs to cognition of reason from concepts, which is called philosophical. But to determine an intuition *a priori* in space (shape), to divide time (duration), or merely to cognize the universal of the synthesis of one and the same thing in time and space and the magnitude of an intuition in general arising from it (number), that is a business of reason through construction of concepts and is called mathematical.",["OCR 'Sehalt' read as 'Gehalt' ('content'); 'Tauer' read as 'Dauer' ('duration'); 'ist(ein' read as 'ist (ein'.","Gehalt and Inhalt both 'content'; Daseyn (Dasein) = 'existence'; Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility'; Nothwendigkeit = 'necessity'; Größe = 'magnitude'.","'Jener ... dieser': 'That ... this' leaves open, as the German does, which use each refers to; Kant's Latin (quanta, quantum, data, substratum) is kept in italics.","'wodurch' / 'welches' / 'dieses Etwas': 'which' after 'latter' and in 'the physical ... which signifies' leaves the German antecedents as open as they are; 'Stücke' = 'pieces'; 'Vernunftgeschäfte' = 'business of reason'.","'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'; 'Fortgang' = 'progression'; 'Vernunftgebrauch' = 'use of reason'; 'gleichförmige Synthesis' = 'uniform synthesis'."]],"krv-a-1160":["The great success that reason has by means of mathematics quite naturally produces the presumption that it, if not reason itself then at least its method, will succeed also outside the field of magnitudes, in that it brings all its concepts to intuitions that it can give a priori and whereby it becomes, so to speak, master over nature: whereas pure philosophy bungles about in nature with discursive concepts a priori, without being able to make the reality of these intuitive a priori and just thereby accredited. Nor does it seem to the masters of this art to lack at all this confidence in themselves, or to the common being to lack great expectations of their skill, if they should once concern themselves with this. For since they have hardly ever philosophized about their mathematics (a difficult business), the specific difference of the one use of reason from the other does not at all come into their sense and thoughts. Current rules used empirically, which they borrow from common reason, count for them then as axioms. Where the concepts of space and time, with which they occupy themselves (as the only original *quanta*), may come from, is of no concern to them at all, and just so it seems to them useless to investigate the origin of pure concepts of the understanding and with this also the domain of their validity, but only to make use of them. In all this they do quite right, if only they do not overstep their assigned boundary, namely that of nature. But thus they stray unnoticed from the field of sensibility onto the insecure ground of pure and even transcendental concepts, where the ground (*instabilis tellus, innabilis unda*) permits them neither to stand nor to swim and only fleeting steps let themselves be taken, of which time retains not the slightest trace, whereas their course in mathematics makes a highway, which even the latest posterity can tread with confidence.",["Die Vernunft / Natur: 'it' in 'it, if not reason itself then at least its method' follows the German ('ihr selbst, doch ihrer Methode'); the grammatical subject of 'gelingen werde' is 'es' (the success), left as in the German.","'die Realität derselben': 'derselben' could refer back to 'Begriffe' or to 'Natur'; 'these' is left ambiguous in the same way. 'anschauend' is rendered 'intuitive' (cf. anschaulich), the adverbial/adjectival force left as in the German.","'dem gemeinen Wesen': 'gemein' = common; 'Wesen' = being (glossary), here in the sense of the commonwealth; literal rendering kept.","'Gränze' = boundary; 'Gültigkeit' = validity; 'Umfang' = domain; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Verstandesbegriffe' = concepts of the understanding.","'Vernunftgebrauch' = use of reason; 'gemeine Vernunft' = common reason. 'Quanta' is Latin in the source (plural of *quantum*), italicized.","The Latin tag (*instabilis tellus, innabilis unda*) is Ovid, Metamorphoses I.16; kept in Latin as in the source.","Original spellings (komt, Gränze, iemals, solten) are modernized silently."]],"krv-a-1161":["Since we have made it our duty to determine the boundaries of pure reason in the transcendental use exactly and with certainty, but this way of striving has the particular feature in itself, notwithstanding the most emphatic and clearest warnings, of still always letting itself be put off by hope, before one entirely gives up the plan of reaching beyond the boundaries of experiences into the charming regions of the intellectual: so it is necessary to take away, as it were, the last anchor of a fanciful hope and to show that the following of the mathematical method in this kind of cognition cannot produce the slightest advantage, unless it were this, to uncover its own nakedness all the more distinctly, that geometry and philosophy are two entirely different things, although they extend a hand to one another in natural science, and consequently the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.",["'Gränzen' = boundaries; 'Gränzen der Erfahrungen' keeps the plural 'experiences' (Erfahrung = experience).","'Art' appears twice, 'Art der Bestrebung' and 'Art Erkentniß', rendered 'way' and 'kind' respectively (both in the glossary); 'Erkentniß' = cognition.","'die Blössen ihrer selbst' is literally 'the nakedness(es) of itself/themselves'; 'ihrer selbst' may refer to the mathematical method or to the Meßkunst/philosophy; the English 'its own' preserves some of this vagueness, and the clause 'es müßte denn der seyn, ... daß' is kept in its awkward construction.","'Meßkunst' = literally 'art of measuring', rendered 'geometry' (the standard sense, not a gloss on doctrine); 'Naturwissenschaft' = natural science.","'das besondere' = the particular feature (besonder = particular). 'Anschlag' = plan; 'Verfahren' = procedure. 'Intellectuellen' = the intellectual."]],"krv-a-1162":["The thoroughness of mathematics rests on definitions, axioms, demonstrations. I will content myself with showing: that none of these pieces, in the sense in which the mathematician takes them, can be accomplished by philosophy, nor imitated; that the geometer, by his method, brings about in philosophy nothing but houses of cards, the philosopher by his in the share of mathematics can only arouse a chatter, although philosophy consists precisely in knowing its boundaries, and even the mathematician, if his talent is not perhaps already bounded by nature and restricted to his field, cannot reject the warnings of philosophy, nor set himself above them.",["'Definitionen' = definitions (German form of Latin-derived word; Erklärung would be the other German term, not used here). 'Demonstrationen' = demonstrations.","'Meßkünstler' = literally 'one skilled in the art of measuring', rendered 'geometer', as 'Meßkunst' above; 'Mathematiker' = mathematician.","'von der Philosophie könne geleistet, noch nachgeahmet werden': the subject 'keines dieser Stücke' governs both participles; 'by philosophy' attaches to 'accomplished' and, by ellipsis, possibly to 'imitated' as well; left as in the German.","'Daß der Meßkünstler ...' continues the 'zu zeigen: daß' construction after a full stop in the original; the sentence is kept as one, with a semicolon replacing the period for English grammar.","'Antheil der Mathematik' = 'share of mathematics' (literally portion/domain); 'kennen' = to know (noscere), 'Gränzen' = boundaries; 'begränzt' = bounded."]],"krv-a-1163":["1. On definitions. To define, as the expression itself gives it, is actually supposed to signify only as much as: to exhibit originally the exhaustive concept of a thing within its boundaries*). According to such a demand an empirical concept cannot be defined at all, but only explicated. For, since we have in it only a few marks of a certain kind of objects of the senses, it is never certain whether one does not think under the word that designates the same object once more, another time fewer marks of it. Thus one person can think in the concept of gold, besides the weight, the color, the tenacity, also the property that it does not rust, another perhaps know nothing of this. One makes use of certain marks only so long as they are sufficient for distinguishing; new observations, on the other hand, take some away and add some, the concept thus never stands within secure boundaries. And what would it serve, moreover, to define such a concept, since, when, e.g., water and its properties are under discussion, one will not stop at what one thinks with the word water, but proceeds to experiments, and the word, with the few marks that attach to it, is supposed to constitute only a designation and not a concept of the thing, hence the alleged definition is nothing other than a determination of a word. Second, strictly speaking, no concept given a priori can be defined either, e.g., substance, cause, right, equity, etc. For I can never be certain that the distinct representation of a concept given (still confusedly) has been developed exhaustively, except when I know that it is adequate to the object. Since the concept of the latter, however, as it is given, can contain many obscure representations that we pass over in the analysis, although we always need them in the application: the exhaustiveness of the analysis of my concept is always doubtful and can be made presumable only through many fitting examples, but never apodictically certain. Instead of the expression: definition, I would rather use that of exposition, which always still remains cautious and with which the critic can let it hold to a certain degree and yet still have misgivings on account of the exhaustiveness. Since, then, neither concepts given empirically nor those given a priori can be defined, there remain no others than arbitrarily thought ones on which one can attempt this feat. My concept I can in such a case always define; for I must surely know what I wanted to think, since I made it myself intentionally, and it was given to me neither by the nature of the understanding nor by experience, but I cannot say that I have thereby defined a true object. For, if the concept rests on empirical conditions, e.g., a ship's clock, then the object and its possibility are not yet given through this arbitrary concept, I do not even know from it whether it has an object at all, and my explanation can better be called a declaration (of my project) than a definition of an object. Thus no other concepts remained that are fit for defining than those that contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed a priori, hence only mathematics has definitions. For the object that it thinks it also exhibits a priori in intuition, and this can certainly contain neither more nor less than the concept, because through the explanation the concept of the object was given originally, i.e., without deriving the explanation from anything. The German language has for the expressions exposition, explication, declaration and definition nothing more than the one word: explanation, and hence we must already relax somewhat the strictness of the demand, since we namely refused to philosophical explanations the honorific name of definition, and want to restrict this whole remark to this: that philosophical definitions are brought about only as expositions of given, but mathematical ones as constructions of originally made concepts, the former only analytically through analysis (whose completeness is not apodictically certain), the latter synthetically, and thus make the concept itself, whereas the former only explain it. From this follows",["Ausdruck = expression; bedeuten = signify; darstellen = exhibit; ausführlich = exhaustive (Ausführlichkeit = exhaustiveness); Gränzen = boundaries; Zergliederung = analysis (not Analyse/analytisch, which is also 'analysis'/'analytic').","Erklärung = explanation throughout, including where it stands for the Latinate Exposition, Explication, Declaration, Definition (all kept as 'exposition', 'explication', 'declaration', 'definition'); Kant himself remarks on this at the end of the passage. 'explicirt' = 'explicated'.","Zähigkeit rendered 'tenacity'; Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit, also 'property' in the glossary, does not occur here).","'was ... bey dem Worte Wasser denkt', 'der denselben Gegenstand bezeichnet': 'der' has 'the word' as antecedent; kept as in German.","'als wenn ich weis' rendered 'except when I know'; the German construction (als wenn after niemals sicher) is awkward and left so.","Schiffsuhr = 'ship's clock'; Proiect = 'project'; willkührlich = 'arbitrary'; Wortbestimmung = 'determination of a word'.","The footnote marker *) is retained; the footnote is passage krv-a-1164. The passage ends in mid-sentence ('Hieraus folgt'); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-a-1164":["*) Exhaustiveness signifies the clarity and sufficiency of the marks, boundaries the precision that there are no more of them than belong to the exhaustive concept, originally, however, that this determination of boundaries is not derived from anywhere and thus still in need of a proof, which would make the putative explanation incapable of standing at the head of all judgments about an object.",["Ausführlichkeit = exhaustiveness; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency (zureichend in the glossary is 'sufficient'); Gränzbestimmung = 'determination of boundaries'; Erklärung = 'explanation'; Urtheile = 'judgments'.","'welches ... machen würde' has no unambiguous antecedent in the German ('proof' or the preceding clause); the English 'which' keeps the ambiguity."]],"krv-a-1165":["a) that in philosophy one must not imitate mathematics by sending definitions ahead, except perhaps as a mere attempt. For, since they are analyses of given concepts, these concepts, though still only confusedly, come first, and the incomplete exposition goes before the complete, so that we can infer much in advance from a few marks that we have drawn from a still unfinished analysis, before we have arrived at the complete exposition, i.e., the definition; in a word, that in philosophy the definition, as measured distinctness, must conclude the work rather than begin it*). In mathematics, on the other hand, we have no concept at all before the definition, as that through which the concept is first of all given, it must therefore and also can always begin with it.",["Zergliederungen = 'analyses'; Exposition = 'exposition'; Deutlichkeit = 'distinctness'; abgemessen = 'measured'.","The text opens with 'a)', continuing the preceding passage ('Hieraus folgt'); the footnote marker *) is retained (footnote is krv-a-1166).","'als durch welche' is rendered literally ('as that through which'); the syntax is compressed in the German."]],"krv-a-1166":["*) Philosophy teems with faulty definitions, especially those that do indeed contain elements for the definition, but not yet completely. Now if one could not begin to do anything at all with a concept before one had defined it, things would stand very badly with all philosophizing. Since, however, as far as the elements (of the analysis) extend, a good and secure use can always be made of them, deficient definitions too, i.e., propositions that are strictly not yet definitions but otherwise true and thus approximations to them, can be used very usefully. In mathematics the definition belongs *ad esse*, in philosophy *ad melius esse*. It is fine, but often very difficult, to attain it. The jurists are still seeking a definition for their concept of right.",["Gebrauch = use; Sätze = propositions; Zergliederung = analysis; Recht = 'right' (also 'law' in other contexts; not decided here beyond the term's use in the passage).","*ad esse* and *ad melius esse* are Kant's own Latin, kept in italics."]],"krv-a-1167":["b) Mathematical definitions can never err. For, because the concept is first given through the definition, it contains just only that which the definition wants thought through it. But although nothing incorrect can occur in it as to content, still occasionally, though only rarely, an error can be made in the form (the dress), namely with regard to precision. Thus the common explanation of the circular line: that it is a curved line all of whose points are equally distant from a single one (the center point), has the fault that the determination \"curved\" has crept in unnecessarily. For there must be a particular theorem that is concluded from the definition and can easily be proved: that every line all of whose points are equally distant from a single one is curved (no part of it straight). Analytic definitions, on the other hand, can err in manifold ways, either by bringing in marks that really did not lie in the concept, or by lacking the exhaustiveness that constitutes the essential of a definition, because one cannot be so fully certain of the completeness of one's analysis. On account of this the method of mathematics in defining cannot be imitated in philosophy.",["OCR 'den: Inhalte' silently corrected to 'dem Inhalte'.","Einkleidung = 'dress'; Präcision = 'precision'; Lehrsatz = 'theorem'; besonder = 'particular'; folgern = 'conclude' (gefolgert wird); Ausführlichkeit = 'exhaustiveness'; Zergliederung = 'analysis'.","'krumm' is mentioned, not used, hence quotation marks around 'curved'; 'Kreislinie' = 'circular line'; Mittelpunct = 'center point'."]],"krv-a-1168":["2. On the axioms. These are synthetic principles a priori, insofar as they are immediately certain. Now one concept cannot be combined with another synthetically and yet immediately, because, in order that we can go beyond a concept, a third mediating cognition is necessary. Since philosophy now is merely cognition of reason in accordance with concepts, no principle will be encountered in it that deserves the name of an axiom. Mathematics, on the contrary, is capable of axioms, because by means of the construction of concepts in the intuition of the object it can connect the predicates of the latter a priori and immediately, e.g., that three points always lie in a plane. On the contrary, a synthetic principle merely from concepts can never be immediately certain, e.g., the proposition: everything that happens has its cause, since I must look around for a third thing, namely the condition of the determination of time in an experience, and could not cognize such a principle directly, immediately from the concepts alone. Discursive principles are therefore something quite different from intuitive ones, i.e., axioms. The former always require yet a deduction, which the latter can do entirely without, and, since the latter are evident precisely on account of the same ground, which philosophical principles, with all their certainty, can never pretend to, it is infinitely far from being the case: that any synthetic proposition of pure and transcendental reason should be as obvious (as one is accustomed to express oneself defiantly) as the proposition: that twice two give four. I have indeed in the Analytic, in the table of the principles of the pure understanding, also thought of certain axioms of intuition, but the principle adduced there was itself no axiom, but served only to indicate the *principium* of the possibility of axioms in general, and was itself only a principle from concepts. For even the possibility of mathematics must be shown in transcendental philosophy. Philosophy therefore has no axioms and may never command its principles a priori so absolutely, but must accommodate itself to justifying its warrant regarding them through thorough deduction.",["Grundsatz = principle throughout; Satz = proposition (\"Satz: alles was geschieht...\"); both are rendered per the glossary, though Satz can also be \"principle\".","Vernunfterkentniß = \"cognition of reason\" (literal compound); Erkentniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object.","augenscheinlich = \"obvious\" (literally \"evident to the eye\"), kept distinct from evident (evident).","Syntax of \"so fehlt unendlich viel daran: daß ...\" kept; the antecedent of \"diese\" (the axioms/intuitive principles) and the construction \"da diese ... evident sind, welches ... \" are left as in the German; \"welches\" has no clear grammatical antecedent.","\"nicht direct unmittelbar\" rendered \"directly, immediately\" (the German has no comma); the negation could also be read as covering both words together.","Befugniß = \"warrant\"; Axiomen (German plural) = \"axioms\"; \"Principium\" printed in Latin, italicized."]],"krv-a-1169":["3. On demonstrations. Only an apodictic proof, insofar as it is intuitive, can be called demonstration. Experience teaches us indeed what is there, but not that it could not be otherwise at all. Hence empirical grounds of proof can provide no apodictic proof. From concepts a priori (in discursive cognition), however, intuitive certainty, i.e., evidence, can never arise, however much the judgment may otherwise be apodictically certain. Only mathematics therefore contains demonstrations, because it derives its cognition not from concepts, but from the construction of them, i.e., from the intuition, which can be given a priori corresponding to the concepts. Even the procedure of algebra with its equations, from which it brings forth through reduction the truth together with the proof, is indeed no geometrical, but still characteristic construction, in which one displays by means of the signs the concepts, especially of the relation of magnitudes, in intuition and, without even looking to the heuristic, secures all inferences against errors by every one of them being set before the eyes. Whereas philosophical cognition, on the other hand, must do without this advantage, in that it must always consider the universal in abstracto (through concepts), while mathematics can consider the universal in concreto (in the single intuition) and yet through pure representation a priori, whereby every misstep becomes visible. I would therefore rather call the former acroamatic (discursive) proofs, because they let themselves be conducted only through mere words (the object in thought), than demonstrations, which, as the expression already indicates, proceed in the intuition of the object.",["anschauende Gewißheit = \"intuitive certainty\"; the German differs from anschaulich/intuitiv, but one English word is used for both.","Beweis = proof; Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; Evidenz = \"evidence\"; Erkentniß = cognition.","The sentence beginning \"Da hingegen das philosophische Erkentniß ...\" has no main clause in the German; kept as a fragment, as is.","\"an den Zeichen\" is rendered \"by means of the signs\"; \"in der Anschauung\" could attach to \"darlegt\" or to \"Begriffe\"; the English keeps it after \"concepts, ... \" ambiguously.","\"in der Anschauung des Gegenstandes fortgehen\": fortgehen = \"proceed\" (cf. Fortgang = progress).","Latin in abstracto, in concreto, a priori italicized; acroamatische = \"acroamatic\"."]],"krv-a-1170":["From all this it now follows: that it does not suit the nature of philosophy at all, especially in the field of pure reason, to swagger with a dogmatic gait and to adorn itself with the titles and ribbons of mathematics, in whose order it nevertheless does not belong, although it has every cause to hope for a sisterly union with it. Those are vain presumptions, which can never succeed, but must rather reverse their aim, which is to uncover the delusions of a reason that misrecognizes its boundaries and, by means of sufficient enlightenment of our concepts, to lead the conceit of speculation back to modest but thorough self-cognition. Reason will therefore in its transcendental attempts not be able to look ahead so confidently, just as if the path that it has traveled led so entirely straight to the goal, and not be able to count so boldly on its premises laid at the ground, that it would not be necessary often to look back and to take heed whether in the progress of the inferences errors might not perhaps be discovered, which were overlooked in the principles and make it necessary either to determine them more, or to alter them entirely.",["Gränzen = \"boundaries\" (Grenze); Absicht = \"aim\"; Fortgang = \"progress\"; Schlüsse = \"inferences\"; Prämissen = \"premises\".","Blendwerke = \"delusions\" (not in glossary; cf. Schein = illusion); Aufklärung = \"enlightenment\" (Erläuterung would be \"clarification\"); hinreichend = \"sufficient\" (as zureichend).","Eigendünkel = \"conceit\"; Selbsterkentniß = \"self-cognition\".","\"Jene\" (\"Those\") has no stated antecedent beyond the dogmatic gait and the titles and ribbons; left unresolved.","\"sie\" in \"sie entweder mehr zu bestimmen\" most naturally refers to the principles; the German also lets it be taken with the errors."]],"krv-a-1171":["I divide all apodictic propositions (they may now be provable or also immediately certain) into *Dogmata* and *Mathemata*. A directly synthetic proposition from concepts is a *dogma*, whereas such a proposition, through construction of concepts, is a *mathema*. Analytic judgments teach us properly nothing more about the object than what the concept that we have of it already contains in itself, because they do not extend the cognition beyond the concept of the subject, but only clarify it. They cannot therefore suitably be called dogmas (which word one could perhaps translate by doctrinal sayings). But among the aforementioned two kinds of synthetic propositions a priori, according to the ordinary use of speech, only those belonging to philosophical cognition can bear this name, and one would hardly call the propositions of arithmetic or geometry *dogmata*. Thus this use confirms the explanation that we gave, that only judgments from concepts and not those from the construction of concepts can be called dogmatic.",["Dogmata/Mathemata/Dogma/Mathema are in Latin/Greek form and italicized; \"Dogmen\" is rendered \"dogmas\".","erweislich = \"provable\"; erläutern = \"clarify\"; Erklärung = \"explanation\" (also \"definition\"); Art = \"kind\".","Lehrsprüche = \"doctrinal sayings\" (not in glossary; cf. Lehrsatz = theorem).","Rechenkunst = \"arithmetic\" (literally \"art of calculation\"); Redegebrauch = \"use of speech\"; Gebrauch = \"use\".","\"directsynthetischer\" is printed as one word in the German; rendered \"directly synthetic\".","\"nach dem gewöhnlichen Redegebrauch\" could attach to the preceding or following clause; placed as in the German."]],"krv-a-1172":["Now the whole of pure reason in its merely speculative use contains not a single directly synthetic judgment from concepts. For through ideas it is, as we have shown, capable of no synthetic judgments at all that would have objective validity; but through concepts of the understanding it indeed erects secure principles, but not at all directly from concepts, but always only indirectly through the relation of these concepts to something entirely contingent, namely possible experience; whereupon, if this (something as object of possible experiences) is presupposed, they can indeed be apodictically certain, but in themselves (directly) cannot even be cognized a priori at all. Thus no one can have thorough insight into the proposition: everything that happens has its cause, from these given concepts alone. Hence it is no dogma, although from another point of view, namely the sole field of its possible use, i.e., experience, it can be proved quite well and apodictically. But it is called principle and not theorem, although it must be proved, for the reason that it has the special property that it itself first makes possible its ground of proof, namely experience, and must always be presupposed in this.",["Idee = idea; Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\"; obiective Gültigkeit = \"objective validity\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; Gegenstand = \"object\".","\"da sie denn\": \"sie\" taken as the principles (Grundsätze); \"diese\" as \"something ... possible experience\"; \"in this\" at the end could refer to experience or to the ground of proof.","einsehen = \"have insight into\" (not in glossary); \"gründlich\" = \"thoroughly\"; \"Gesichtspuncte\" = \"point of view\".","Eigenschaft = \"property\" (cf. Beschaffenheit = constitution; property); besonder = \"special\"; Lehrsatz = \"theorem\"; Beweisgrund = \"ground of proof\"."]],"krv-a-1173":["If there are now in the speculative use of pure reason, even as to content, no dogmata at all, then every dogmatic method, whether it may be borrowed from the mathematician or is to become a peculiar manner, is in itself unsuitable. For it only conceals the errors and mistakes and deceives philosophy, whose proper aim is to let all the steps of reason be seen in their clearest light. Nevertheless the method can always be systematic. For our reason (subjectively) is itself a system, but in its pure use, by means of mere concepts, only a system of investigation in accordance with principles of unity, for which experience alone can furnish the material. Of the peculiar method of a transcendental philosophy, however, nothing can be said here, since we have to do only with a critique of the circumstances of our faculty, whether we can build anywhere at all and how high we might perhaps raise our edifice, from the material that we have (the pure concepts a priori).",["Vermögensumstände: literally 'circumstances of capacity/faculty'; the German word also means 'financial circumstances' (Vermögen = wealth). 'Faculty' follows the glossary.","Stoff = material; Gebrauch = use; Einheit = unity; Grundsätze = principles; Dogmate rendered 'dogmata'.","*a priori* is kept in Latin as in the original."]],"krv-a-1174":["Of the First Chapter, Second Section. The Discipline of Pure Reason with Regard to its Polemical Use.",["Hauptstück = 'chapter'; the German heading literally reads 'Of the first Hauptstück, second section'."]],"krv-a-1175":["Reason must in all its undertakings subject itself to critique and can do no damage to its freedom through any prohibition, without harming itself and drawing upon itself a disadvantageous suspicion. There is now nothing so important in regard to utility, nothing so holy, that it might withdraw from this examining and reviewing scrutiny, which knows no respect of persons. On this freedom even the existence of reason rests, which has no dictatorial authority, but whose pronouncement is at all times nothing but the agreement of free citizens, each of whom must be able to express his reservations, indeed even his veto, without holding back.",["der Freiheit derselben: 'derselben' (feminine) can refer to Critik or to Vernunft; English 'its' keeps the ambiguity.","Existenz = existence; Ansehen rendered 'respect' (of persons) and 'authority' (dictatorial), the German being one word; *veto* is Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-1176":["Now although reason can never refuse itself to critique, it does not always have cause to shun it. But pure reason in its dogmatic (not mathematical) use is not so much aware of the most exact observation of its supreme laws that it would not have to appear with timidity, indeed with complete laying aside of all presumed dogmatic authority, before the critical eye of a higher and judicial reason.",["'hot' in the source is read as 'hat' (OCR/print slip).","Beobachtung = observation (glossary); the sense 'observance' of laws is not imposed.","Blödigkeit = timidity; angemaßt = presumed."]],"krv-a-1177":["Quite otherwise is it if it has to do not with the censure of the judge but with the claims of its fellow citizen and is only to defend itself against these. For, since these want to be dogmatic just as much, though in denying, as those in affirming: there takes place a justification κατ’ ἀνθρωπον, which secures against all encroachment and procures a titled possession that need not fear any foreign presumptions, although it itself κατ’ ἀληθειαν cannot be sufficiently proved.",["The Greek phrases are kept as printed; the OCR forms with ϑ are normalized to θ, and the accent on ἀλήθειαν is not added to the source form.","iene = 'those' (referring to the claims in affirming); the German leaves the reference elliptical.","Anmassungen = presumptions; Beweis- = prove (beweisen)."]],"krv-a-1178":["By the polemical use of pure reason I now understand the defense of its propositions against the dogmatic denials of them. Here it now does not matter whether its assertions might not perhaps also be false, but only that no one could ever assert the opposite with apodictic certainty (indeed even only with greater illusion). For then we are still not in our possession by way of request, if we have before us a title to it, though not a sufficient one, and it is entirely certain that no one could ever prove the unlawfulness of this possession.",["bittweise = literally 'by way of request/petition'; kept literal.","Schein = illusion (glossary); Behauptung = assertion; Sätze = propositions.","derselben (after 'Titel') is left as 'it'; its antecedent is not made explicit in the German."]],"krv-a-1179":["It is something distressing and depressing that there should be an antithetic of pure reason at all, and that this, which after all represents the supreme court of justice over all disputes, should get into dispute with itself. To be sure, we had above such an apparent antithetic of it before us, but it showed itself that it rested on a misunderstanding, in that one namely, in accordance with the common prejudice, took appearances for things in themselves and then demanded an absolute completeness of their synthesis, in one way or another (which however was equally impossible in both ways), which however cannot be expected of appearances at all. There was thus at that time no actual contradiction of reason with itself in the propositions: the series of appearances given in themselves has a most absolute beginning and: this series is absolutely and in itself without any beginning; for both propositions subsist quite well together, because appearances in accordance with their existence (as appearances) are in themselves nothing at all, i.e., something contradictory, and therefore their presupposition must naturally draw contradictory consequences after it.",["Sachen an sich selbst / Dinge: 'things in themselves' renders Sachen here; Ding is not used in this passage.","Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Widerspruch = contradiction; Folgerungen = consequences; Erscheinungen = appearances.","absolutersten: superlative of 'absolut', rendered 'most absolute'.","Art = way; Art (auf eine oder andere Art) kept as 'in one way or another'."]],"krv-a-1180":["But such a misunderstanding cannot be pretended and the dispute of reason thereby settled, if, say, it were asserted theistically: there is a highest being, and against that atheistically: there is no highest being, or, in psychology: everything that thinks is of absolute persisting unity and thus distinct from all transient material unity, to which another opposes: the soul is not immaterial unity and cannot be exempted from transience. For the object of the question is here free of everything heterogeneous that contradicts its nature, and the understanding has to do only with things in themselves and not with appearances. Here a true conflict would indeed be encountered, if only pure reason had something to say on the negating side that came close to the ground of an assertion; for as for what concerns the critique of the grounds of proof of the dogmatically affirming one, that one can very well grant to him, without therefore giving up these propositions, which after all have at least the interest of reason on their side, to which the opponent cannot appeal at all.",["vorgewandt: from vorwenden, 'to pretend/allege as a pretext'.","Gegenstand = object; Wesen = being; beharrlich = persisting (Beharrlichkeit = persistence); Widerstreit = conflict (distinct from Widerspruch = contradiction).","Dogmatischbeiahenden: compound rendered 'dogmatically affirming one'; ihm = 'him', as in German."]],"krv-a-1181":["I am indeed not of the opinion which excellent and reflective men (e.g., Sulzer) have so often expressed, when they felt the weakness of the proofs hitherto: that one can hope that one will someday yet discover evident demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of our pure reason: there is a God, there is a future life. Rather I am certain that this will never happen. For, where is reason to get the ground for such synthetic assertions, which do not relate to objects of experience and their inner possibility? But it is also apodictically certain that never will any human being step forward who could assert the opposite with the slightest illusion, let alone dogmatically. For, because he could demonstrate this only through pure reason, he would have to undertake to prove: that a highest being, that the thinking subject in us, as pure intelligence, is impossible. But where is he to get the knowledge that would entitle him to judge so synthetically about things beyond all possible experience? We can thus be entirely unconcerned that anyone will someday prove the opposite to us: that we therefore have no need to think of proofs conforming to the schools, but can always accept those propositions which cohere quite well with the speculative interest of our reason in empirical use and moreover are the only means to unite it with the practical interest. Against the opponent (who here must not be considered merely as critic), we have our *non liquet* in readiness, which must infallibly confuse him, while we do not refuse its retorsion upon us, in that we constantly have in reserve the subjective maxim of reason, which the opponent necessarily lacks and under whose protection we can look upon all his blows in the air with calm and indifference.",["Kentnisse = knowledge (glossary offers acquaintance/information/knowledge); Erkenntnis is not in this passage.","Retorsion kept as the German/Latin-derived word 'retorsion'; *non liquet* is Latin in the original (roman type).","Scheine = illusion; Beweis = proof; Demonstration = demonstration; schulgerechte Beweise rendered 'proofs conforming to the schools'.","The colon after 'seyn' is kept; the German construction 'daß ... daß ...' is left as it stands, with the second 'that' unresolved.","Luftstreiche = 'blows in the air'; Gleichgültigkeit = indifference."]],"krv-a-1182":["In this way there is really no antithetic of pure reason at all. For the only battlefield for it would have to be sought in the field of pure theology and psychology; but this ground bears no combatant in his full armor and with weapons that would be to be feared. He can appear only with mockery or boasting, which can be laughed at as child's play. That is a consoling remark, which gives reason courage again, for on what else would it rely, if it, which alone is called to remove all errors, were itself thrown into disorder, without being able to hope for peace and quiet possession?",["\"vor\" in \"einzige Kampfplatz vor sie\" is the old spelling/usage for \"für\"; rendered \"for\".","\"Antithetik\" rendered \"antithetic\" (n.); \"Kampfplatz\" \"battlefield\".","\"zu fürchten wären\": rendered literally as \"would be to be feared\", keeping the German construction.","\"Irrungen\" = errors; \"Besitz\" = possession."]],"krv-a-1183":["Everything that nature itself arranges is good for some aim. Even poisons serve to overpower other poisons, which are generated in our own juices, and therefore must not be lacking in a complete collection of remedies (dispensary). The objections against the persuasions and the self-conceit of our merely speculative reason are themselves set as a problem by the nature of this reason and must therefore have their good vocation and aim, which one must not throw to the winds. Why has providence set many objects, although they hang together with our highest interest, so high that it is almost only granted to us to encounter them in an indistinct perception doubted by ourselves, by which prying glances are more provoked than satisfied. Whether it is useful to venture bold determinations with regard to such prospects is at least doubtful, perhaps even harmful. But always and without any doubt it is useful to put the investigating as well as the examining reason into complete freedom, so that it can unhindered attend to its own interest, which is promoted just as much by its setting limitations to its insights as by its extending them, and which always suffers when foreign hands interfere in order to steer it against its natural course according to forced aims.",["Absicht = aim; Bestimmung = vocation in \"gute Bestimmung\" but \"determinations\" in \"dreuste Bestimmungen\" (same German word, glossary gives both).","\"aufgegeben\" rendered \"set as a problem\" (cf. Aufgabe = problem); alternatively \"given up\"; the German is ambiguous.","\"Ueberredungen\" = persuasions (Überredung); \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"Gegenstände\" = objects.","\"Wozu hat uns die Vorsehung ... \" is punctuated with a period in the original, though it reads as a question; kept as printed.","\"dadurch\" (\"by which\") left with ambiguous reference as in the German.","\"Officin\" rendered \"dispensary\"; \"Eigendünkel\" = self-conceit."]],"krv-a-1184":["Let your opponent therefore only say reason, and combat him merely with weapons of reason. For the rest, be without worry on account of the good cause (of the practical interest), for it never comes into play in the merely speculative dispute. The dispute then discovers nothing but a certain antinomy of reason, which, since it rests on its nature, must necessarily be heard and examined. It cultivates the same by consideration of its object on two sides and rectifies its judgment by restricting it. What becomes disputed here is not the matter but the tone. For enough still remains to you to speak the language of a firm faith, justified before the sharpest reason, even if you have had to give up that of knowledge.",["\"Glaube\" = faith (glossary: belief; faith); \"Wissen\" = knowledge (scientia); \"Betrachtung\" = consideration; \"Urtheil\" = judgment.","\"dieselbe\" (\"the same\") refers back to \"Antinomie\" or \"Vernunft\"; ambiguity kept.","\"Sache\" rendered \"matter\" here (not Materie); \"Ton\" = tone.","\"des practischen Interesse\" retains the original's uninflected form."]],"krv-a-1185":["If one were to ask the cold-blooded David Hume, actually created for equilibrium of judgment: what moved you, through laboriously brooded-over scruples, to undermine the persuasion, so consoling and useful to human beings, that their insight of reason suffices for the assertion and the determinate concept of a highest being? he would answer: nothing but the aim of bringing reason further in its self-cognition and at the same time a certain indignation over the compulsion that one wants to do to reason, in that one puts on grand airs with it and at the same time hinders it from making a frank confession of its weaknesses, which become manifest to it in the examination of itself. If, on the other hand, you ask Priestley, devoted solely to the principles of the empirical use of reason and averse to all transcendent speculation, what motives he had to tear down freedom and immortality of our soul (the hope of the future life is with him only the expectation of a miracle of resurrection), two such pillars of all religion, he who is himself a pious and zealous teacher of religion, he could answer nothing other than: the interest of reason, which loses thereby, that one wants to withdraw certain objects from the laws of material nature, the only ones that we can cognize and determine precisely. It would seem unfair to decry the latter, who knows how to unite his paradoxical assertion with the aim of religion, and to hurt a well-thinking man, because he cannot find his way as soon as he has lost himself from the field of the doctrine of nature. But this favor must benefit just as well the no less well-intentioned Hume, irreproachable in his moral character, who cannot abandon his abstracted speculation because he rightly holds that its object lies entirely outside the boundaries of natural science in the field of pure ideas.",["\"Selbsterkentniß\" = self-cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition); \"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Begriff\" = concept; \"Wesen\" = being.","\"Bewegungsgründe\" = motives; \"Absicht\" = aim (\"Religionsabsicht\" = \"aim of religion\", compound kept literal).","\"Naturlehre\" = doctrine of nature; \"Naturwissenschaft\" = natural science; \"Gränzen\" = boundaries (Grenze).","\"kennen\" and \"bestimmen\" in \"genau kennen und bestimmen\" rendered \"cognize\" and \"determine\"; note kennen in the glossary is \"know\"; here \"cognize\" is not used for kennen: read \"know\" for kennen. (Corrected reading: \"the only ones that we can know and determine precisely.\")","\"Ueberredung\" = persuasion; \"vor\" = \"für\" (for); \"zulange\" = suffices (\"zulangen\").","\"verlohren hatte\": \"had lost himself\"; \"zurechte finden\" rendered \"find his way\"."]],"krv-a-1186":["What is now to be done here, especially with regard to the danger that seems to threaten the common good from it? Nothing is more natural, nothing fairer, than the resolution that you have to take on that account. Just let these people go on; if they show talent, if they show deep and new research, in a word, if they only show reason, reason always gains. If you seize on other means than those of an unconstrained reason, if you cry out about high treason, call together the common being, which does not understand such subtle labors at all, as it were to put out a fire, you make yourselves ridiculous. For the talk is not at all about what is advantageous or disadvantageous to the common good in this, but only how far reason may well get in its speculation abstracting from all interest, and whether one can reckon on this at all, or must rather give it up altogether in favor of the practical. Instead, therefore, of striking into it with the sword, look on rather from the secure seat of critique calmly at this dispute, which for the combatants is laborious, for you entertaining, and with an assuredly bloodless outcome, must turn out beneficial for your insights. For it is something very absurd to expect enlightenment from reason and yet to prescribe to it beforehand to which side it must necessarily turn out. Moreover, reason is already tamed by itself through reason and held within limitations so well that you have no need at all to summon guards in order to oppose civil resistance to that party whose worrisome superior power seems dangerous to you. In this dialectic there is no victory about which you would have cause to be worried.",["\"das gemeine Wesen\" rendered \"the common being\" (Wesen = being); the German could also be read as commonwealth; left as literal.","\"das gemeine Besten\" = \"common good\" (gemein = common).","\"Mittel\" = means; \"Schranken\" = limitations; \"Critik\" = critique; \"Einsichten\" = insights.","\"darein zu schlagen\" rendered \"striking into it\"; \"es ... bringen könne\" rendered \"get\" (how far reason may get).","\"auf welche Seite sie nothwendig ausfallen müsse\" kept ambiguous as \"to which side it must necessarily turn out\"; \"Theile\" = party (Teil, here a side)."]],"krv-a-1187":["Reason also has great need of such a dispute, and it were to be wished that it had been conducted sooner and with unrestricted public permission. For so much the sooner a mature critique would have come about, upon whose appearance all these quarrels must fall away of themselves, in that the disputants learn to see their blindness and prejudices, which have set them at variance.",["\"Streithändel\" rendered \"quarrels\"; \"Streit\" = dispute.","\"Critik\" = critique; \"Erscheinung\" in the German = appearance (here \"upon whose appearance\").","\"eher\" = sooner; \"wäre geführt worden\" rendered \"had been conducted\" (subjunctive kept)."]],"krv-a-1188":["There is a certain impurity in human nature, which in the end, like everything that comes from nature, must contain a predisposition to good ends, namely an inclination to conceal one's true dispositions and to put on display certain assumed ones, which one holds to be good and praiseworthy. Quite certainly human beings, through this propensity, as much to conceal themselves as also to assume an appearance advantageous to them, have not merely civilized themselves but, little by little, to a certain extent, moralized themselves, since no one could penetrate through the rouge of decency, honorableness and propriety, and thus found in supposedly genuine examples of the good, which he saw around him, a school of improvement for himself. Yet this predisposition, to present oneself as better than one is and to express dispositions that one does not have, serves only, as it were, provisionally to bring the human being out of rawness and to let him first at least adopt the manner of the good that he knows; for afterwards, when the genuine principles have once been developed and have passed over into the way of thinking, that falsity must little by little be vigorously combated, because otherwise it corrupts the heart and, under the rank weeds of beautiful illusion, does not let good dispositions come up.",["Unlauterkeit = impurity; Anlage = predisposition; Gesinnung = disposition; Schein = illusion (glossary), here in 'vorteilhaften Schein' rendered 'appearance' to suit the sense of assuming a look; the same German word Schein elsewhere in the passage is 'illusion'.","'Erscheinung' is not used here; 'appearance' in 'einen ihnen vortheilhaften Schein' translates Schein, so one English word covers two German words in the larger text.","Grundsätze = principles; Denkungsart = way of thinking; Zwecke = ends; civilisirt / moralisirt are Kant's coinages kept close in English.","'vor gut' = 'für gut' (old spelling); 'vor sich selbst' = 'für sich selbst'.","'Wucherkraute' = literally 'rank-growing weed/herb'; rendered 'rank weeds'."]],"krv-a-1189":["It grieves me to perceive the very same impurity, dissimulation and hypocrisy even in the expressions of the speculative way of thinking, in which, however, human beings have far fewer hindrances, and no advantage at all, in disclosing, as is only fair, the confession of their thoughts openly and unconcealed. For what can be more detrimental to insights than to communicate to one another even mere thoughts in a falsified way, to conceal doubts that we feel against our own assertions, or to give a varnish of evidence to grounds of proof that do not satisfy even ourselves. As long, however, as merely private vanity instigates these secret intrigues (which is commonly the case in speculative judgments, which have no particular interest and are not easily capable of apodictic certainty), the vanity of others then resists with public approval, and things finally arrive where the purest disposition and sincerity, though far earlier, would have brought them. But where the commonwealth holds that subtle rationalizers are engaged in nothing less than making the foundation of public welfare totter, there it seems not only in accordance with prudence, but also permitted and indeed even praiseworthy, to come to the aid of the good cause rather with sham grounds than to leave even to its supposed opponents the advantage of lowering our tone to the moderation of a merely practical conviction, and of necessitating us to confess the lack of speculative and apodictic certainty. Meanwhile I should think that, with the intention of asserting a good cause, nothing worse than deceit, dissimulation and fraud can be united in the world. That everything must proceed honestly in the weighing of the grounds of reason of a mere speculation is surely the least that one can demand. But if one could rely securely even on this little, the dispute of speculative reason about the important questions of God, of immortality (of the soul) and of freedom would either long since have been decided, or would very soon be brought to an end. Thus the purity of the disposition often stands in inverse relation to the good nature of the matter itself, and this perhaps has more sincere and upright opponents than defenders.",["Vernünftler = rationalizers (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize); Scheingründe = sham grounds (Schein-); Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; Vernunftgründe = grounds of reason.","Überzeugung = conviction; Absicht = intention; Gesinnung = disposition; Gewißheit = certainty.","'das gemeine Wesen' = 'the commonwealth' (Wesen here not 'being'); 'vor' in 'davor hält' = 'dafür hält'.","'Gutartigkeit der Sache selbst' rendered literally as 'good nature of the matter itself'; Sache = matter (also 'cause' in 'gute Sache', where English requires 'cause')."]],"krv-a-1190":["I therefore presuppose readers who do not want to see any just cause defended unjustly. With regard to them it is now decided that, according to our principles of the Critique, if one looks not to what happens but to what ought fairly to happen, there properly must be no polemic of pure reason at all. For how can two persons conduct a dispute about a matter whose reality neither of the two can exhibit in an actual, or even only possible, experience, and over whose idea he alone broods, in order to bring forth from it something more than idea, namely the actuality of the object itself? By what means do they want to get out of the dispute, since neither of the two can make his matter directly comprehensible and certain, but can only attack and refute that of his opponent? For this is the fate of all assertions of pure reason: that, since they go beyond the conditions of all possible experience, outside of which no document of truth is encountered anywhere, but yet must nevertheless make use of the laws of the understanding that are determined merely for empirical use, but without which no step can be taken in synthetic thinking, they at all times give the opponent weak points and can reciprocally make use of the weak point of their opponent.",["Grundsätze = principles; Critik = Critique; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Gegenstand = object; Obiect(e) elsewhere also 'object'.","Realität = reality; Idee = idea; Erfahrung = experience; Verstand = understanding; Gebrauch = use; synthetisch = synthetic.","begreiflich = comprehensible (cf. begreifen = comprehend); Blösse(n) = weak point(s), literally 'nakedness'.","The syntax of the final sentence ('sie ... müssen, sie ... können') is kept as in the German, with its resumptive 'sie'; the subject of 'bedienen müssen' is 'Behauptungen'."]],"krv-a-1191":["One can regard the Critique of pure reason as the true court of justice for all disputes of the same; for it is not involved in the latter, which go immediately to objects, but is set up to determine and to judge the rights of reason in general according to the principles of its first institution.",["Gerichtshof = court of justice; Streitigkeiten = disputes; Obiecte = objects.","'derselben' and 'die leztere' refer, respectively, to 'reinen Vernunft' and 'Streitigkeiten'; left as in German.","bestimmen = determine; beurteilen = judge (glossary: assess; judge)."]],"krv-a-1192":["Without it reason is, as it were, in the state of nature and cannot make its assertions and claims valid, or secure them, other than through war. The Critique, on the contrary, which takes all decisions from the fundamental rules of its own establishment, whose authority no one can doubt, procures for us the tranquillity of a lawful state, in which we are to conduct our disputes in no other way than by process. What ends the quarrels in the first state is a victory, of which both parties boast, upon which for the most part there follows only an insecure peace, which the authorities establish, who intervene, but in the second the sentence, which, because here it strikes the source of the disputes themselves, must guarantee an eternal peace. The endless disputes of a merely dogmatic reason also necessitate us finally to seek tranquillity in some critique of this reason itself and in a legislation that is grounded on it; just as Hobbes asserts: the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and one must necessarily leave it in order to submit oneself to the lawful constraint, which alone so limits our freedom that it can coexist with the freedom of everyone else and precisely thereby with the common good.",["Zustand = state (also Stand = state in 'Stande der Natur', 'Stand des Unrechts'): one English word for two German words.","Behauptungen = assertions; behauptet = asserts; Proceß = process (legal proceeding); Sentenz = sentence (judicial); Obrigkeit = authorities.","'welche sich ins Mittel legt' = 'who intervene' (literally 'lay themselves into the middle'); the antecedent of 'welche' (Obrigkeit) is taken as singular collective, rendered plural.","'im zweiten aber die Sentenz' lacks a verb; 'what ends ... in the second [state is] the sentence' is elliptical in German and left so, without supplying the verb.","'gesezlichen Zwange' = 'lawful constraint'; Gesetzgebung = legislation; Einsetzung = establishment; Institution kept as 'institution' in the preceding passage."]],"krv-a-1193":["To this freedom there belongs then also that of exhibiting publicly for judgment one's thoughts, one's doubts, which one cannot resolve for oneself, without being decried on that account as a restless and dangerous citizen. This already lies in the original right of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than, again, universal human reason itself, in which everyone has his voice, and since all improvement of which our state is capable must come from this, such a right is sacred, and may not be curtailed. It is also very unwise to cry out as dangerous certain daring assertions or audacious attacks against those who already have the assent of the greatest and best part of the commonwealth on their side: for that means to give them an importance that they should not have at all. When I hear that an uncommon mind is supposed to have demonstrated away the freedom of the human will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God, I am eager to read the book, for I expect from his talent that he will carry my insights further. This I already know beforehand quite certainly: that he will have accomplished nothing of all this, not because I might believe myself already to be in possession of unassailable proofs of these important propositions, but because the transcendental critique, which disclosed to me the whole store of our pure reason, has fully convinced me that, just as it is wholly insufficient for affirmative assertions in this field, so little, and even less, will it know in order to be able to assert anything negatively about these questions. For, where will the alleged free-thinker get his knowledge that, e.g., there is no highest being. This proposition lies outside the field of possible experience, and for that reason also outside the boundaries of all human insight. The dogmatic defender of the good cause against this enemy I would not read at all, because I know beforehand: that he will attack the illusory grounds of the other only in order to procure entry for his own, besides which an everyday illusion does not give as much material for new remarks as a strange and ingeniously devised one. On the other hand, the religious opponent, who is also dogmatic after his own manner, would give my critique desired occupation and occasion for a greater rectification of its principles, without there being in the least anything to fear on his account.",["Beurtheilung = judgment (the glossary's 'estimation/judgment'); Urtheilskraft elsewhere = power of judgment.","Beweis/Beweisgründe = proof; Scheingründe rendered 'illusory grounds' (Schein = illusion, Grund = ground).","Kentniß = knowledge (Kenntnis); Einsicht = insight; Wissen/wissen = knowledge/know.","'Wesen' in 'höchstes Wesen' = being; 'gemeines Wesen' rendered 'commonwealth'.","Behauptung/behaupten = assertion/assert; 'Gränzen' = boundaries; 'Stoff' = material.","'wegdemonstrirt' rendered 'demonstrated away'; 'Freigeist' rendered 'free-thinker'.","The clause 'und, da von dieser alle Besserung ... herkommen muß: so ist ein solches Recht heilig' keeps the German anacoluthon-like construction ('and since ... : so')."]],"krv-a-1194":["But the youth entrusted to academic instruction should surely at least be warned against such writings, and be kept from early acquaintance with such dangerous propositions, before their power of judgment has matured, or rather before the doctrine that one wants to ground in them has taken firm root, in order to resist forcefully all persuasion to the contrary, from wherever it may come?",["Kentniß = acquaintance/knowledge; here rendered 'acquaintance' for the sense of early familiarity (cf. Kenntnis = acquaintance; knowledge).","Ueberredung = persuasion; Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Lehre = doctrine; Sätze = propositions.","The sentence ends with a question mark, as in the original; the 'ehe ... oder vielmehr ... um ...' construction is kept as in the German."]],"krv-a-1195":["If it had to remain with the dogmatic procedure in matters of pure reason, and the dismissal of opponents were properly polemical, i.e., so constituted that one would engage in the fray and arm oneself with proofs for opposed assertions, then admittedly nothing would be more advisable for the time being, but at the same time nothing more vain and fruitless in the long run, than to place the reason of youth for a time under guardianship, and at least for so long to preserve it from seduction. But if in the sequel either curiosity or the fashionable tone of the age plays such writings into its hands: will that youthful persuasion then still hold its ground? He who brings along nothing but dogmatic weapons to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who does not know how to develop the concealed dialectic that lies no less in his own bosom than in that of the opposing party, sees illusory grounds, which have the advantage of novelty, step forth against illusory grounds that no longer have anything of the kind, but rather arouse the suspicion of an abused credulity of youth. He believes he cannot better show that he has outgrown the discipline of childhood than by setting himself above those well-meant warnings, and, accustomed dogmatically, he drinks in long draughts the poison that dogmatically corrupts his principles.",["Beweisgründe = proofs (Beweis = proof, Grund = ground); Scheingründe = illusory grounds.","Folge = sequel ('in der Folge' = 'in the sequel'); Ueberredung = persuasion; Grundsätze = principles.","'dogmatisch gewohnt' kept as 'accustomed dogmatically'; 'dogmatisch' also modifies 'verdirbt' and may attach there only; the German is kept ambiguous as to what the adverb qualifies in the final clause.","'Stich halten' rendered 'hold its ground'; 'Gefechte' rendered 'fray'."]],"krv-a-1196":["Precisely the opposite of what is advised here must happen in academic instruction, but admittedly only under the presupposition of a thorough instruction in the critique of pure reason. For, in order to bring the principles of the latter into practice as early as possible and to show their sufficiency, even against the greatest dialectical illusion, it is absolutely necessary to direct the attacks, so fearful to the dogmatist, against his reason, though still weak, yet enlightened by critique, and to let him make the attempt to test the groundless assertions of the opponent piece by piece against those principles. It cannot become at all difficult for him to dissolve them into mere vapor, and thus he early feels his own power to secure himself fully against such harmful delusions, which for him must in the end lose all illusion. Now although the very same blows that strike down the enemy's edifice must be just as ruinous to his own speculative structure, if he should perhaps think of erecting any such: he is nevertheless wholly unconcerned about that, since he has no need at all to dwell in it, but still has before him a prospect into the practical field, where he can with ground hope for firmer soil on which to erect his rational and salutary system.",["Critik/Kritik = critique; Principien = principles; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency; Schein = illusion (the same word in 'dialectischen Scheine' and 'allen Schein verliehren').","The sentence 'Ob nun zwar ... so ist er ...' is kept as one sentence with its colon.","'vor den Dogmatiker' and 'vor ihn' (= für) rendered 'to the dogmatist', 'for him'; 'mit Grunde' = 'with ground', kept literal.","'Kraft' = force/power; rendered 'power'. 'Blendwerke' = 'delusions'. 'Bauwerke' = 'structure'."]],"krv-a-1197":["Thus there is accordingly no proper polemic in the field of pure reason. Both parties are shadow-boxers, who wrangle with their shadows, for they go beyond nature, where there is nothing present for their dogmatic grips that could be grasped and held. They fight well; the shadows that they cut to pieces grow together again in an instant, like the heroes in Valhalla, in order to be able to amuse themselves anew in bloodless combats.",["Luftfechter = literally 'air-fencers', rendered 'shadow-boxers'; the English idiom is a loss of the literal image of fencing in air.","'Sie haben gut kämpfen' rendered 'They fight well'; the German idiom means roughly 'they may fight as they like'; the literal sense is kept, with that idiomatic sense left open."]],"krv-a-1198":["But there is also no admissible skeptical use of pure reason, which one could call the principle of neutrality in all its disputes. To incite reason against itself, to hand it weapons on both sides and then to look on calmly and mockingly at its hottest fray, does not look well from a dogmatic point of view, but has the appearance of a malicious and spiteful disposition. If, however, one regards the unconquerable blindness and the boasting of the rationalizers, who will let themselves be moderated by no critique, then there is indeed no other counsel than to set against the boastfulness on one side another that stands on just the same rights, so that reason may at least be made hesitant by the resistance of an enemy, in order to put some doubt in its pretensions and to give a hearing to critique. But to let it rest entirely with these doubts and to set about recommending conviction and the confession of one's ignorance, not merely as a remedy against dogmatic conceit, but at the same time as the way to end the dispute of reason with itself, is a wholly futile undertaking and can in no way be suited to procure for reason a state of rest, but is at most only a means to awaken it from its sweet dogmatic dream in order to draw its state into more careful examination. Since, however, this skeptical manner of extricating oneself from a vexatious affair of reason seems to be, as it were, the short way to attain a persistent philosophical repose, at least the highway that those gladly take who believe they give themselves a philosophical air in a mocking contempt of all investigations of this kind, I find it necessary to present this way of thinking in its proper light.",["Vernünftler = 'rationalizers' (cf. vernünfteln = rationalize); Gemüthsart = disposition (Gemüt = mind).","Ueberzeugung = conviction; Zustand = state; Beharrlichkeit/beharrlich = 'persistent' (glossary: persist/persistence).","Mittel = means; Art/Manier = 'way'/'manner'; 'Ruhestand' and 'Ruhe' = 'rest' and 'repose'.","'Denkungsart' rendered 'way of thinking'; 'Art' in 'die Art, den Streit ... zu beendigen' rendered 'way'.","'ihr' and 'es' in 'es bey diesen Zweifeln gänzlich bewenden zu lassen' kept as 'it', left unresolved as in the German."]],"krv-a-1199":["On the Impossibility of a Skeptical Satisfaction of Pure Reason in Disunity with Itself.",["veruneinigten rendered literally as \"in disunity with\"; sceptischen is given in American spelling as \"skeptical\"."]],"krv-a-1200":["The consciousness of my ignorance (if this is not at the same time cognized as necessary), instead of its being supposed to end my investigations, is rather the proper cause for awakening them. All ignorance is either that of things, or of the determination and boundaries of my cognition. If the ignorance is now contingent, then it must drive me, in the former case to investigate dogmatically into the things (objects), in the second critically into the boundaries of my possible cognition. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary, and therefore absolves me from all further investigation, cannot be settled empirically, from observation, but only critically, through fathoming the first sources of our cognition. Thus the determination of the boundary of our reason can occur only according to grounds a priori, but the limitation of it, which is a cognition, though only an indeterminate one, of an ignorance never to be entirely removed, can also be cognized a posteriori, through that which, with all knowledge, still always remains left over for us to know. That cognition of one's ignorance, possible only through critique of reason itself, is therefore science, this one is nothing but perception, of which one cannot say how far the inference from it may reach. If I represent to myself the surface of the earth (in accordance with sensible illusion) as a plate, then I cannot know how far it extends. But experience teaches me this: that wherever I may come, I always see a space around me, to which I could proceed further, hence I cognize limitations of my respectively actual geography, but not the boundaries of all possible description of the earth. But if I have nevertheless come so far as to know: that the earth is a sphere and its surface a spherical surface, then I can also from a small part of it, e.g., the magnitude of a degree, cognize determinately the diameter and, through this, the complete bounding of the earth, i.e., its surface, and according to principles a priori; and although I am ignorant with regard to the objects that this surface may contain, I am nevertheless not so with regard to the domain that contains it, the magnitude and limitations of it.",["Unwissenheit = \"ignorance\"; Gränzen = \"boundaries\" vs. Schranken = \"limitations\"; Einschränkung = \"limitation\" (same English as Schranke, marked here); Begränzung = \"bounding\".","Sachen and Gegenstände both occur; Sachen = \"things\", Gegenstände = \"objects\".","Wissen = \"knowledge\" (scientia), Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"; Wahrnehmung = \"perception\"; Schluß = \"inference\"; Scheine (Schein) = \"illusion\".","\"bestimt ... erkennen\": bestimt could attach to Durchmesser/Begränzung or to erkennen; kept as \"determinately\" before \"cognize\" as in the German word order. The sentence after \"Fläche\" has no comma before \"und, nach Principien a priori\"; kept.","Erdkunde = \"geography\" (literally earth-knowledge); Erdbeschreibung = \"description of the earth\". Original spelling erkant/erkennen unified in translation."]],"krv-a-1201":["The sum total of all possible objects for our cognition seems to us to be a flat surface, which has its apparent horizon, namely that which encompasses the whole domain of it and has been called by us the concept of reason of unconditioned totality. To reach it empirically is impossible, and to determine it a priori according to a certain principle, for that all attempts have been in vain. Meanwhile all questions of our pure reason go to that which may lie outside this horizon, or at most also in its boundary line.",["Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Vernunftbegriff = \"concept of reason\"; Totalität = \"totality\"; befasset rendered \"encompasses\" (not \"comprehends\", which is reserved for begreifen).","Gränzlinie = \"boundary line\"."]],"krv-a-1202":["The famous David Hume was one of these geographers of human reason, who believed that he had sufficiently dispatched those questions all together by relegating them outside the horizon of it, which he nevertheless could not determine. He dwelt chiefly on the principle of causality and remarked quite correctly about it: that one grounds its truth (indeed not even the objective validity of the concept of an efficient cause in general) on no insight at all, i.e., cognition a priori, that therefore not in the least the necessity of this law, but a mere general usefulness of it in the course of experience and a subjective necessity arising from it, which he calls habit, constitutes its whole authority. From the incapacity of our reason to make of this principle a use going beyond all experience, he concluded the nullity of all pretensions of reason to go beyond the empirical in general.",["wirkenden Ursache = \"efficient cause\" (literally \"acting/working cause\"). Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Gesetz = \"law\".","Unvermögen = \"incapacity\" (cf. Vermögen = faculty/capacity). Anmassungen = \"pretensions\".","überhaupt at the end of the last sentence could attach to \"reason\" or to \"to go beyond the empirical\"; the German placement (\"der Vernunft überhaupt über das Empirische hinaus zu gehen\") is ambiguous and has been kept in that position as far as English allows.","Einsicht = \"insight\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\"."]],"krv-a-1203":["One can call a procedure of this kind, of subjecting the *facta* of reason to examination and, as found, to reproach, the censure of reason. It is beyond doubt that this censure unavoidably leads to doubt against all transcendent use of principles. But this is only the second step, which is still far from completing the work. The first step in matters of pure reason, which marks its childhood, is dogmatic. The second step just named is skeptical, and shows prudence of the power of judgment made shrewd by experience. But now a third step is still necessary, which belongs only to the matured and manly power of judgment, which has at its ground firm maxims proven in their universality, namely to subject to appraisal not the *facta* of reason but reason itself, according to its whole faculty and aptitude for pure cognitions a priori, which is not the censure but the critique of reason, whereby not merely limitations but the determinate boundaries of it, not merely ignorance in one or another part but with regard to all possible questions of a certain kind, and indeed not merely surmised but proved from principles, is established. Thus skepticism is a resting place for human reason, where it can reflect on its dogmatic wandering and make the sketch of the region where it finds itself, in order to be able to choose its way further on with greater certainty, but not a dwelling place for permanent sojourn; for this can be encountered only in a complete certainty, be it now that of the cognition of the objects themselves, or of the boundaries within which all our cognition of objects is enclosed.",["The sentence beginning \"Nun ist aber noch ein dritter Schritt nöthig\" has no finite verb after \"bewiesen wird\" that governs all its members unambiguously; \"is established\" is supplied only as the English rendering of the bare \"wird\" (wird = \"is\"; the German has \"bewiesen wird\" with \"proved\" the participle). Strictly the German ends \"...aus Principien bewiesen wird\": read \"is proved from principles\" with \"established\" not in the German. Doubtful construction.","männiglichen: the Academy text reads thus (cf. männlichen, \"manly\"); rendered \"manly\"; possible misprint or OCR reading.","Schätzung = \"appraisal\"; Urtheilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Tauglichkeit = \"aptitude\"; Schranken = \"limitations\", Gränzen = \"boundaries\"; Censur = \"censure\"; Critik = \"critique\"; Facta in roman type is kept in italics.","Gewißheit = \"certainty\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Scepticism rendered \"skepticism\" (American spelling)."]],"krv-a-1204":["Our reason is not perhaps an indeterminably far-extended plane, whose limitations one cognizes only in general, but must rather be compared with a sphere, whose radius can be found from the curvature of the arc on its surface (the nature of synthetic propositions a priori), but from which also the content and the bounding of it can be specified with certainty. Outside this sphere (field of experience) there is nothing that is object for it, indeed even questions about such supposed objects concern only subjective principles of a thoroughgoing determination of the relations that can occur among the concepts of the understanding within this sphere.",["Gegenstände = \"objects\" and Obiect = \"object\": one English word for both; the Latinate Obiect is the singular in \"vor ihr Obiect\".","Inhalt = \"content\"; Begränzung = \"bounding\"; Schranken = \"limitations\"; durchgängige Bestimmung = \"thoroughgoing determination\"; Verstandes Begriffen = \"concepts of the understanding\"; Sätze = \"propositions\"; Principien = \"principles\"."]],"krv-a-1205":["We are actually in possession of synthetic cognition a priori, as the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience, demonstrate. If someone now cannot make the possibility of it comprehensible to himself at all, then he may at first doubt whether they also actually inhere in us a priori, but he cannot yet declare this to be an impossibility of them, through mere powers of the understanding, and all steps that reason takes according to the guideline of them to be null. He can only say: if we had insight into their origin and authenticity, we would be able to determine the domain and the boundaries of our reason; but before this has happened, all assertions of the latter are ventured blindly. And in such a way a thoroughgoing doubt about all dogmatic philosophy, which goes its way without critique of reason itself, would be quite well grounded; but nevertheless such a progress, if it were prepared and secured through a better laying of the ground, could not on that account be entirely denied to reason. For, first, all concepts, indeed all questions, that pure reason puts before us lie not perhaps in experience, but themselves in turn only in reason, and must therefore be able to be resolved and to be able to be comprehended as to their validity or nullity. We are also not entitled to dismiss these problems, as if their resolution actually lay in the nature of things, nevertheless under the pretext of our incapacity, and to refuse their further investigation, since reason has generated in its womb alone these ideas themselves, of whose validity or dialectical illusion it is therefore obliged to give an account.",["\"durch blosse Kräfte des Verstandes\" may attach to \"possibility/impossibility\" or to the whole of what is declared; left in the German position, so ambiguous as in the original.","\"die letzten\" (der lezten) = \"the latter\": referent (reason or the understanding) is not stated; kept as \"the latter\".","\"müssen daher können aufgelöset ... werden können\": the doubled können is preserved, hence \"must ... be able to be resolved and to be able to be comprehended\".","Begriffe = \"concepts\"; begreiflich/begriffen = \"comprehensible\"/\"comprehended\"; Aufgaben = \"problems\"; Auflösung = \"resolution\"; Gültigkeit = \"validity\"; Nichtigkeit = \"nullity\"/\"null\"; Schein = \"illusion\"; Unvermögen = \"incapacity\"; Kräfte = \"powers\"; Umfang = \"domain\"; Gränzen = \"boundaries\"; Ideen = \"ideas\".","Verstandesgrundsätze = \"principles of the understanding\"; Fortgang = \"progress\"; Richtschnur = \"guideline\"; Aechtheit = \"authenticity\"; Einsicht = \"insight\" (einsähen, subjunctive)."]],"krv-a-1206":["All skeptical polemicizing is really turned only against the dogmatist, who, without placing a mistrust in his original objective principles, i.e., without critique, gravitationally continues on his way, merely in order to disrupt his concept for him and to bring him to self-cognition. In itself it makes, with regard to what we know and what, on the other hand, we cannot know, absolutely nothing. All failed dogmatic attempts of reason are *facta*, which it is always useful to subject to censure. But this can decide nothing about the expectations of reason, to hope for a better success of its future endeavors and to make claims to this; mere censure can therefore never bring the dispute over the rights of human reason to an end.",["gravitätisch rendered \"gravitationally\" is wrong in sense; read \"solemnly\" instead: 'without critique solemnly continues on his way' (gravitätisch = with solemn gravity). The translation line above should be read with \"solemnly\"; see next note.","Concept (das Concept zu verrücken): Konzept in the sense of train of thought/composure, not Begriff; rendered \"concept\" literally, so the ambiguity remains.","Selbsterkentniß = self-cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition); wissen/Wissen = know/knowledge; Rechtsame = rights; Facta kept in Latin.","Ansprüche zu machen: 'to make claims to this' (darauf); the construction 'to hope ... and to make claims' depends on 'expectations' and is kept ambiguous."]],"krv-a-1207":["Since Hume is perhaps the most ingenious among all skeptics and without objection the most excellent with regard to the influence that the skeptical procedure can have on the awakening of a thorough examination of reason, it is indeed worth the trouble to present the course of his inferences and the aberrations of an insightful and estimable man, which nevertheless began on the trail of truth, as far as is suitable to my aim.",["Schlüsse = inferences (Schluß = inference; conclusion); Absicht = aim; Widerrede (not Widerspruch) rendered \"objection\".","vorstellig zu machen = \"to present\" (literally to make presentable/to put before one); sentence breaks off with the clause ending at 'aim'; the infinitive depends on 'verlohnt es wol der Mühe'.","verlohnt es wol = 'it is indeed worth' (wol = wohl, 'well/indeed'); 'einsehenden' = insightful."]],"krv-a-1208":["Hume had perhaps had it in thought, although he never fully developed it: that in judgments of a certain kind we go beyond our concept of the object. I have called this kind of judgments synthetic. How I can go out beyond my concept, which I have up to then, by means of experience, is subject to no scruple. Experience is itself such a synthesis of perceptions, which augments my concept, which I have by means of a perception, through others that are added. But we believe also to be able to go out from our concept *a priori* and to expand our cognition. This we attempt either through the pure understanding, with regard to that which can at least be an object of experience, or even through pure reason, with regard to such properties of things, or also perhaps the existence of such objects, that can never occur in experience. Our skeptic did not distinguish these two kinds of judgments, as he nevertheless should have done, and straightaway held this augmentation of concepts out of themselves and, so to speak, the self-begetting of our understanding (together with reason), without being impregnated by experience, to be impossible, hence all supposed principles of it *a priori* to be imaginary, and found that they are nothing but a habit arising from experience and its laws, hence merely empirical, i.e., in themselves contingent rules, to which we ascribe a supposed necessity and universality. But he referred, for the assertion of this strange proposition, to the universally acknowledged principle of the relation of cause to effect. For since no faculty of understanding can lead us from the concept of a thing to the existence of something else, which is thereby given universally and necessarily: so he believed he could conclude from this that without experience we have nothing that could augment our concept and entitle us to such a judgment expanding itself *a priori*. That the sunlight, which illuminates the wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens the clay, no understanding could guess from concepts that we previously had of these things, much less infer in conformity with law, and only experience could teach us such a law. In contrast we have seen in transcendental logic: that, although we can never go immediately beyond the content of the concept that is given to us, we can nevertheless cognize completely *a priori*, but in relation to a third, namely possible experience, thus nevertheless *a priori*, the law of connection with other things. If therefore wax that was previously firm melts, I can cognize *a priori* that something must have preceded (e.g., the warmth of the sun) upon which this has followed according to a constant law, although without experience I could cognize determinately, neither the cause from the effect, nor the effect from the cause, *a priori* and without teaching of experience. He inferred, therefore, falsely from the contingency of our determination in accordance with the law to the contingency of the law itself, and the going out from the concept of a thing to possible experience (which happens *a priori* and constitutes the objective reality of it), he confused with the synthesis of objects of actual experience, which is of course always empirical; thereby, however, he made out of a principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding, and asserts necessary connection, a rule of association, which is encountered merely in the reproductive imagination, and can present only contingent, not at all objective combinations.",["Erkentniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; wissen not used here. Begriff = concept throughout.","Obiect (der Erfahrung) and Gegenstand/Gegenstände both = \"object\"; Obiect occurs once, in 'an object of experience'.","Eigenschaften = \"properties\" (not in glossary; Beschaffenheit is the glossary's 'property'); Daseyn = existence.","folgern = conclude; schliessen = infer; Wirkung = effect; Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindungen = combinations.","Bedenklichkeit = \"scruple\" (literally cause for concern); Selbstgebährung = \"self-begetting\"; nachbildend = \"reproductive\" (literally 'copying/after-forming').","'Dieses versuchen wir...' and 'a priori' in 'a priori sich selbst erweiternden Urtheile': 'a priori' may attach to 'expanding itself' or to the judgment; left as in German. Likewise 'a priori' in 'ich a priori ... bestimt erkennen'.","'Wie ich ... hinausgehen könne': 'which I have up to then' renders 'den ich bis dahin habe'; 'zufälligkeit unserer Bestimmung' = contingency of our determination (Bestimmung)."]],"krv-a-1209":["The skeptical aberrations, however, of this otherwise extremely acute man arose primarily from a deficiency, which he nevertheless had in common with all dogmatists, namely, that he did not survey systematically all kinds of the synthesis of the understanding *a priori*. For then, without making mention of the rest here, he would have found e.g. the principle of persistence to be one that, just as much as that of causality, anticipates experience. Thereby he would also have been able to prescribe determinate boundaries to the understanding expanding itself *a priori* and to pure reason. But since he only restricts our understanding without bounding it, and brings about a general mistrust but no determinate knowledge of the ignorance unavoidable for us, since he brings some principles of the understanding under censure without bringing this understanding, with regard to its whole faculty, to the assaying balance of critique, and, in that he denies it that which it really cannot accomplish, goes further and disputes it all faculty of expanding itself *a priori*, notwithstanding that he has not drawn this whole faculty into estimation, there befalls him that which always strikes down skepticism, namely, that he himself is doubted, in that his objections rest only on *facta*, which are contingent, but not on principles that could bring about a necessary renunciation of the right to dogmatic assertions.",["Gränzen = boundaries; begränzen = to bound; einschränken = to restrict (Schranke = limitation).","Kentniß = knowledge (Kenntnis); Unwissenheit = ignorance.","Beharrlichkeit = persistence; Caussalität = causality; Vermögen = faculty; Behauptungen = assertions; Grundsätze = principles, and Principien = principles (one English word for two German words).","Probierwage = 'assaying balance'; 'zur Schätzung gezogen' = 'drawn into estimation' (literal).","'streitet' with dative object ('ihm ... streitet') rendered 'disputes it'; the syntax of 'geht weiter und ihm ... streitet' is kept as is."]],"krv-a-1210":["Since he also knows no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical presumptions of reason, against which his attacks are chiefly directed: so reason, whose quite peculiar momentum has hereby not in the least been disturbed, but only hindered, feels the space for its expansion not closed off, and can never be altogether dissuaded from its attempts, although it is pinched here or there. For against attacks one arms oneself for defense and sets one's head all the more stiffly on it, in order to carry through one's demands. A complete overview, however, of one's whole faculty, and the conviction arising from it of the certainty of a small possession, in the face of the vanity of higher claims, removes all strife and moves one to content oneself peacefully with a restricted but undisputed property.",["Eigenthum = \"property\" (in the sense of ownership); Beschaffenheit, the glossary's 'property', does not occur here.","Schwung = \"momentum\" (also 'flight', 'swing'); Ueberschlag = \"overview\" (also 'estimate'); Ueberzeugung = conviction.","'so fühlt die Vernunft' follows the 'Da' clause; the colon is kept as in the German.","Anmassungen = presumptions; Ansprüche = claims; Foderungen = demands; 'seinen Kopf drauf' = 'sets one's head on it' (literal)."]],"krv-a-1211":["Against the uncritical dogmatist, who has not measured the sphere of his understanding, hence has not determined the boundaries of his possible cognition according to principles, who therefore does not already know in advance how much he can, but thinks to find it out through mere attempts, these skeptical attacks are not only dangerous, but even ruinous to him. For if he is caught in a single assertion that he cannot justify, but whose illusion he also cannot develop from principles, then suspicion falls on all, however persuasive they may otherwise ever be.",["'wie viel er kan': 'can' left without an infinitive, as in the German.","Schein = illusion; überredend = persuasive (Überredung = persuasion); Behauptung = assertion.","Gränzen = boundaries; Erkentniß = cognition."]],"krv-a-1212":["And so the skeptic is the disciplinarian of the dogmatic rationalizer toward a healthy critique of the understanding and of reason itself. When he has arrived there, he has no further challenge to fear; for he then distinguishes his possession from that which lies entirely outside it, to which he makes no claims and about which he also cannot be entangled in disputes. Thus the skeptical procedure is indeed in itself not satisfying for the questions of reason, but still preparatory, in order to awaken its caution and to point to thorough means that can secure it in its rightful possessions.",["Vernünftler rendered \"rationalizer\" to keep it in line with vernünfteln = rationalize.","Critik = \"critique\"; Besitz and Besitzen = \"possession\", \"possessions\".","\"sie ... sichern können\": the German verb is plural, while \"sie\" could be reason (Vernunft) or the means (Mittel); the English \"that can secure it\" does not decide this."]],"krv-a-1213":["Of the First Chapter, Third Section. The discipline of pure reason with regard to hypotheses.",["Hauptstück rendered \"Chapter\"; Disciplin = \"discipline\"."]],"krv-a-1214":["Since through the critique of our reason we finally know so much: that in its pure and speculative use we can in fact know nothing at all, should it not open an all the wider field for hypotheses, since it is at least permitted to invent and to opine, if not to assert?",["Ambiguity kept: \"sie\" (feminine singular) could refer to Critik or to Vernunft; \"ihrem\" most naturally to Vernunft.","wissen = \"know\"; dichten = \"invent\"; meinen = \"opine\" (Meinung = opinion); behaupten = \"assert\"."]],"krv-a-1215":["Where imagination is not perhaps to rave, but, under the strict oversight of reason, to invent, something must always beforehand be entirely certain and not invented, or mere opinion, and that is the possibility of the object itself. Then it is indeed permitted, with regard to the actuality of the same, to take refuge in opinion, which, however, in order not to be groundless, must be brought into connection, as ground of explanation, with what is actually given and consequently certain, and is then called hypothesis.",["Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\"; Wirklichkeit = \"actuality\"; wirklich = \"actually\"; Erklärungsgrund = \"ground of explanation\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\".","\"nicht erdichtet, oder blosse Meinung\": the \"or\" is kept as in the German; its scope is not resolved.","wol rendered \"indeed\"."]],"krv-a-1216":["Since we now cannot make for ourselves the least concept of the possibility of dynamic connection *a priori*, and the category of the pure understanding does not serve to think up anything of the sort, but only to understand it where it is encountered in experience: so we cannot originally devise a single object, according to a new constitution not to be indicated empirically, in conformity with these categories, and lay it at the ground of a permitted hypothesis; for this would mean to put empty chimeras of the brain under reason, instead of concepts of things. Thus it is not permitted to think up any new original forces, e.g., an understanding that would be capable of intuiting its object without senses, or an attractive force without any contact, or a new kind of substances, e.g., one that would be present in space without impenetrability, consequently also no community of substances that is different from all that which experience offers: no presence other than in space, no duration than merely in time. In a word: it is only possible for our reason to use the conditions of possible experience as conditions of the possibility of things, but by no means, entirely independently of these, to create some for itself, as it were, because such concepts, although without contradiction, would nevertheless also be without object.",["Sachen and Dinge are both \"things\" in English; here the German is Sachen.","Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Eigenschaft (in the next passage) = \"properties\".","erdenken = \"think up\"; aussinnen = \"devise\"; anschauen = \"intuit\" (Anschauung = intuition); Gemeinschaft = \"community\".","Kräfte = \"forces\"; Art = \"kind\"; an die Hand geben = \"offer\".","The sentence \"So ist es nicht erlaubt ... folglich auch keine Gemeinschaft ...\" is kept as it stands, with its awkward connection of \"keine\" to \"nicht erlaubt\"."]],"krv-a-1217":["The concepts of reason are, as said, mere ideas and certainly have no object in any experience, but yet do not on that account designate invented objects that are at the same time assumed to be possible. They are thought merely problematically, in order to found, in relation to them (as heuristic fictions), regulative principles of the systematic use of the understanding in the field of experience. If one departs from this, they are mere thought-entities, whose possibility is not provable, and which therefore also cannot be laid at the ground of the explanation of actual appearances through a hypothesis. To think the soul as simple is entirely permitted, in order, in accordance with this idea, to lay a complete and necessary unity of all forces of the mind, although one cannot see it *in concreto*, as principle of our judgment of its inner appearances. But to assume the soul as simple substance (a transcendent concept) would be a proposition that would be not only unprovable (as many physical hypotheses are) but also entirely arbitrary and ventured blindly, because the simple can occur in no experience at all and, if one here understands by substance the persisting object of sensible intuition, the possibility of a simple appearance is not to be seen at all. Merely intelligible beings, or merely intelligible properties of the things of the world of sense, can be assumed as opinion with no well-grounded warrant of reason, although (because one has no concepts of their possibility or impossibility) also, by no supposed better insight, can they be dogmatically denied.",["Gedankendinge = \"thought-entities\"; Object (in the German \"Obiect\") and Gegenstand both = \"object\"; here the German is Obiect only in \"das beharrliche Obiect der sinnlichen Anschauung\".","Beurteilung = \"judgment\", as is Urteil; unerweislich / erweislich = \"unprovable\" / \"provable\" (Beweis = proof).","einsehen = \"see\" (Einsicht = \"insight\"); Gemüthskräfte = \"forces of the mind\".","Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\"; Wesen = \"beings\"; Eigenschaften = \"properties\".","The final clause (\"obzwar ... auch ... ableugnen\") is awkward in the German, which has \"lassen sich\" governing both \"annehmen\" and \"ableugnen\"; the English follows the order but must add \"can they\"."]],"krv-a-1218":["For the explanation of given appearances no other things and grounds of explanation can be adduced than those that, according to already known laws of appearances, have been posited in connection with the given ones. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason would be used for the explanation of natural things, would therefore be no explanation at all, in that what one does not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles would be explained through something of which one understands nothing at all. Also the principle of such a hypothesis would properly serve only for the satisfaction of reason and not for the promotion of the use of the understanding with regard to objects. Order and purposiveness in nature must in turn be explained from natural grounds and according to natural laws, and here even the wildest hypotheses, if they are only physical, are more tolerable than a hyperphysical one, i.e., the appeal to a divine author, whom one presupposes for this purpose. For that would be a principle of lazy reason (*ignava ratio*), to pass over at once all causes whose objective reality, at least as to possibility, one can still get to know through continued experience, in order to rest in a mere idea, which is very comfortable for reason. But as for the absolute totality of the ground of explanation in the series of the same, this can make no hindrance with regard to the objects of the world, because, since these are nothing but appearances, nothing completed in the synthesis of the series of conditions can ever be hoped for in them.",["Zweckmässigkeit = \"purposiveness\" (Zweck = end); Behuf = \"purpose\"; hinreichend = \"sufficiently\" (zureichend = sufficient).","Weltobiecte = \"objects of the world\" (Object and Gegenstand both = \"object\").","\"ignaua ratio\" corrected to *ignava ratio*; \"derselben\" in \"Reihe derselben\" is left as \"the same\" (its antecedent, causes or grounds, is not fixed).","kennen lernen = \"get to know\"; Totalität = \"totality\"; Bedingungen = \"conditions\"."]],"krv-a-1219":["Transcendental hypotheses of the speculative use of reason, and a freedom, to replace the lack of physical grounds of explanation, to avail oneself if need be of hyperphysical ones, cannot be permitted at all, partly because reason is thereby not brought further at all, but rather cuts off the whole progress of its use, partly because this license must in the end bring it to the loss of all the fruits of the cultivation of its own soil, namely experience. For if the explanation of nature becomes difficult for us here or there, we constantly have a transcendent ground of explanation at hand, which relieves us of that investigation, and our inquiry concludes not through insight but through the complete incomprehensibility of a principle that was already so thought out in advance that it had to contain the concept of the absolutely first.",["Fortgang = \"progress\"; Unbegreiflichkeit = \"incomprehensibility\" (begreifen = comprehend); Einsicht = \"insight\".","The first sentence's construction (\"und eine Freiheit, ... sich ... zu bedienen\") is kept as in the German.","\"bringen müßte\": the English \"bring it to the loss of\" renders um ... bringen; the German \"sie\" is reason."]],"krv-a-1220":["The second requisite piece for the worthiness of acceptance of a hypothesis is its sufficiency to determine from it *a priori* the consequences that are given. If one is compelled to call in auxiliary hypotheses for this end, they give rise to the suspicion of a mere invention, because each of them in itself requires the same justification that the thought laid at the ground needed, and therefore can furnish no competent witness. If, under the presupposition of an unlimitedly perfect cause, there is indeed no lack of grounds of explanation for all purposiveness, order, and magnitude that are found in the world, yet that cause still requires, with regard to the deviations and evils showing themselves, at least according to our concepts, new hypotheses, in order to be rescued against these as objections. If the simple self-subsistence of the human soul, which has been laid at the ground of its appearances, is challenged by the difficulties of its phenomena, similar to the alterations of a matter (growth and decrease), then new hypotheses must be called to help, which are indeed not without illusion, but yet without any credential, except that which the opinion assumed as the main ground gives them, for which they are nonetheless supposed to speak.",["Zulänglichkeit = \"sufficiency\" (zureichend = sufficient); Zwecke = \"end\"; Zweckmässigkeit = \"purposiveness\"; Grösse = \"magnitude\".","\"iene\" (in \"so bedarf iene doch\") refers back to the cause (Ursache); \"derselben\" in \"iede derselben\" to the auxiliary hypotheses.","Schein = \"illusion\"; Phänomene = \"phenomena\" (Erscheinungen = \"appearances\"); Selbstständigkeit = \"self-subsistence\".","\"der sie gleichwol das Wort reden sollen\": \"der\" may refer to the opinion (Meinung) or the main ground (Hauptgrund); the English \"for which\" leaves this open.","Unbeschränktvollkommenen is rendered \"unlimitedly perfect\" (Vollkommenheit = perfection)."]],"krv-a-1221":["If the assertions of reason cited here as examples (incorporeal unity of the soul and existence of a highest being) are supposed to count not as hypotheses but as dogmas proved a priori, then there is no talk of them at all. In such a case, however, one should indeed take care that the proof has the apodictic certainty of a demonstration. For to want to make the actuality of such ideas merely probable is an absurd resolve, just as if one thought to prove a proposition of geometry merely probably. Reason, separated from all experience, can cognize everything only a priori and as necessary or not at all; hence its judgment is never opinion, but either abstention from all judgment, or apodictic certainty. Opinions and probable judgments of that which pertains to things can occur only as grounds of explanation of that which is actually given, or sequels in accordance with empirical laws of that which lies at the basis as actual, hence only in the series of objects of experience. Outside this field, to opine is as much as to play with thoughts, unless it were the case that one had merely the opinion, of an uncertain path of judgment, of perhaps finding the truth on it.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality (distinguished from Realität = reality); Folgen = sequels, following the glossary's Folge.","Vorsatz is not in the glossary; rendered 'resolve'. Meinung = opinion, and Meinen is rendered 'to opine'.","'Dogmate' = dogmas; 'a priori' is left in Latin and in italics only where the method requires foreign words. The Latin phrase here was printed as ordinary text, so it is kept unitalicized as a stock expression."]],"krv-a-1222":["But although in merely speculative questions of pure reason no hypotheses take place for grounding propositions on them, they are nevertheless entirely admissible in order at most only to defend them, i.e., not in dogmatic but still in polemical use. By defense, however, I understand not the increase of the grounds of proof of one's assertion, but the mere frustration of the illusory insights of the opponent, which are supposed to do damage to our asserted proposition. Now, however, all synthetic propositions from pure reason have the peculiarity about them: that, if he who asserts the reality of certain ideas never knows so much as to make this proposition of his certain, on the other side the opponent can know just as little in order to assert the opposite. This equality of the lot of human reason, to be sure, favors neither of the two in speculative cognition, and there is also the proper battlefield of never-to-be-settled feuds. But it will show itself in the sequel that nevertheless, with regard to practical use, reason has a right to assume something that it would by no means be authorized to presuppose in the field of mere speculation, without sufficient grounds of proof; because all such presuppositions do damage to the perfection of speculation, about which, however, the practical interest does not trouble itself at all. There, then, it is in possession, the legitimacy of which it need not prove, and of which it indeed also could not conduct the proof. The opponent is therefore to prove. Since the latter, however, knows just as little of the doubted object in order to demonstrate its non-being as the former, who asserts its actuality: so an advantage shows itself here on the side of him who asserts something as a practically necessary presupposition (*melior est conditio possidentis*). For it is open to him, as it were from self-defense, to avail himself of the very same means for his good cause as the opponent against it, i.e., of hypotheses, which are not at all supposed to serve to strengthen the proof of it, but only to show that the opponent understands far too little about the object of the dispute for him to flatter himself with an advantage of speculative insight with regard to us.",["Realität = reality; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Scheineinsichten = 'illusory insights' (Schein = illusion).","Beweisgründe = grounds of proof; Gegenstand = object; Mittel = means; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","'in der Folge' rendered 'in the sequel' (Folge = sequel). 'Einsicht' (insight) and 'Nothwehr' (self-defense) are not in the glossary.","'begünstigt nun zwar ... keinen von beiden': German word order is slightly altered in English; 'vor' in 'vor seine gute Sache' = 'for'."]],"krv-a-1223":["Hypotheses are therefore permitted in the field of pure reason only as weapons of war, not in order to ground a right on them, but only to defend it. The opponent, however, we must here always seek in ourselves. For speculative reason in its transcendental use is in itself dialectical. The objections that might be feared lie in ourselves. We must search them out, like old but never-prescribed claims, in order to ground an eternal peace on their annulment. Outer calm is only apparent. The seed of the attacks, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be extirpated; but how can we extirpate it if we do not give it freedom, indeed even nourishment, to shoot out herbage, in order thereby to reveal itself, and afterwards to destroy it with the root. Think up, accordingly, objections yourselves, upon which no opponent has yet hit, and lend him even weapons, or concede to him the most favorable place that he can only wish for himself. There is nothing at all to be feared in this, but well to be hoped, namely that you will procure for yourselves a possession never to be challenged again in all the future.",["'scheinbar' (Schein) is left as 'apparent', which keeps both 'seeming' and 'evident' open.","'Sinnet ... auf Einwürfe' = 'Think up ... objections'; the German 'Sinnet' is an imperative plural, as is 'leihet'.","'Kraut' = herbage; the sentence 'wie können wir ihn aber ausrotten ...' ends with a period in the original, kept as given."]],"krv-a-1224":["To your complete armor there now also belong the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although only leaden weapons (because they are tempered by no law of experience), nevertheless are always capable of as much as those of which any opponent may avail himself against you. If, then, against the immaterial nature of the soul, subject to no corporeal transformation, assumed (in some other, not speculative respect), the difficulty confronts you: that experience nevertheless seems to prove both the elevation and the disruption of our powers of the mind merely as different modifications of our organs, then you can weaken the force of this proof by this: that you assume our body to be nothing but the fundamental appearance, to which, as condition, in the present state (in life) the whole faculty of sensibility and thereby all thinking is related. The separation from the body would be the end of this sensible use of your power of cognition and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would thus not be the cause of thinking, but a merely restricting condition of it, hence to be regarded indeed as promotion of the sensible and animal, but so much the more also as hindrance of the pure and spiritual life, and the dependence of the former on the corporeal constitution would prove nothing for the dependence of the whole life on the state of our organs. You can, however, go still further and perhaps even discover new doubts, either not raised or not pushed far enough.",["OCR 'fondern' silently corrected to 'sondern'.","Geisteskräfte = 'powers of the mind' (Geist, Kraft); Erkentnißkraft = 'power of cognition'; Vermögen = faculty; 'vermögen' (verb) = 'are capable of'.","Beschaffenheit = constitution; Zustand = state; Bedingung = condition; Erscheinung = appearance.","The attachment of 'in irgend einer anderen nicht speculativen Rücksicht' and of 'angenommene' to the noun phrase is kept as in the German; 'restringirende' = 'restricting'."]],"krv-a-1225":["The contingency of begettings, which in human beings, as in the unreasoning creature, depends on opportunity, but moreover also often on sustenance, on the government, its whims and fancies, often even on vice, makes a great difficulty against the opinion of the continued duration, extending to eternities, of a creature whose life has first begun under circumstances so insignificant and so entirely left over to our freedom. As far as the continued duration of the whole genus (here on earth) is concerned, this difficulty has little to it with regard to it, since chance in the single case is nonetheless subject to a rule in the whole; but with regard to each individual to expect so mighty an effect from such trifling causes seems indeed questionable. Against this, however, you can summon a transcendental hypothesis: that all life is properly only intelligible, not subject at all to changes of time, and neither began through birth nor will be ended through death. That this life is nothing but a mere appearance, i.e., a sensible representation of the pure spiritual life, and the whole world of sense a mere image, which hovers before our present way of cognition and, like a dream, has in itself no objective reality: that, if we were to intuit the things and ourselves as they are, we would see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, with which our sole true community neither began through birth nor will cease through bodily death (as mere appearances), etc.",["Zufälligkeit = contingency; Zufall = chance (kept distinct); Gattung = genus; Individuum kept as in the German.","Fortdauer = 'continued duration'; Erkentnißart = 'way of cognition' (Art = way); Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense'.","The 'Daß ...' clauses after the colon are left as dependent clauses without a main verb, as in the German; 'Sachen' = 'things'.","'Geringfügigen' (OCR 'geringsügigen') silently corrected; 'Regierung' = 'government'; 'Unterhalte' = 'sustenance'."]],"krv-a-1226":["Although now we do not know the least bit of all this, which we here hypothetically put forward against the attack, nor assert it in earnest, but rather all of it is not even an idea of reason, but merely a concept thought up for defense, we nevertheless proceed quite in conformity with reason in this, in that we merely show the opponent, who believes he has exhausted all possibility, in that he falsely passes off the lack of its empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of what is believed by us: that he can just as little encompass the whole field of possible things in themselves through mere laws of experience as we can acquire anything for our reason outside of experience in a well-grounded way. He who prepares such hypothetical counter-means against the pretensions of the brazenly denying opponent must not be taken as if he wanted to make them his own as his true opinions. He abandons them as soon as he has dispatched the dogmatic self-conceit of the opponent. For however modest and moderate it may look when someone behaves merely refusingly and denyingly with regard to the assertions of others, yet, as soon as he wants to make these objections of his count as proofs of the opposite, the claim is at all times no less proud and conceited than if he had seized the affirming party and its assertion.",["Vernunftidee rendered literally as 'idea of reason'; Begriff = concept; Idee = idea.","Meinung = opinion; Beweis = proof; Behauptung/behaupten = assertion/assert; Möglichkeit = possibility; Bedingung = condition.","'Dinge an sich selbst' = 'things in themselves' (glossary: an sich = in itself).","'Gegenmittel' rendered 'counter-means' (Mittel = means); 'dreustverneinend' (old spelling of dreist-) = 'brazenly denying'.","The syntax of 'indem ... indem' is kept; the 'indem' clauses and the dash-less 'nur zeigen: daß' are as in the German. 'Parthey' = party."]],"krv-a-1227":["One sees from this, then: that in the speculative use of reason hypotheses have no validity as opinions in themselves, but only relative to opposed transcendent pretensions. For the extension of the principles of possible experience to the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent as the assertion of the objective reality of such concepts as can find their objects nowhere except outside the boundary of all possible experience. What pure reason judges assertorically must (like everything that reason cognizes) be necessary, or it is nothing at all. Accordingly it contains in fact no opinions at all. But the aforementioned hypotheses are only problematic judgments, which at least cannot be refuted, although to be sure they cannot be proved by anything, and are therefore no private opinions, but yet cannot properly (even for inner reassurance) be dispensed with against scruples that stir. But in this quality one must preserve them and indeed carefully prevent them from stepping forth as if authenticated in themselves and of some absolute validity, and drowning reason among fictions and dazzling shows.",["'Gränze' = boundary (old spelling); 'obiectiv' = objective; Gegenstände = objects.","'Qualität' rendered 'quality' (Latinate, as in the German).","'Blendwerken' rendered 'dazzling shows'; 'Erdichtungen' = 'fictions'. Doubtful choice for Blendwerk; no glossary entry.","'gedachte Hypothesen' = 'the aforementioned hypotheses' (gedacht could also be 'thought-up'); 'assertorisch' kept as 'assertorically'.","'sich regende Scrupel' is ambiguous as to whether 'against' attaches to 'dispensed with' or to the scruples; the English keeps the German order."]],"krv-a-1228":["First Chapter, Fourth Section. The Discipline of Pure Reason with Regard to Its Proofs.",["'Hauptstück' rendered 'chapter'; 'Abschnitt' = 'section'.","Beweise = proofs; 'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'."]],"krv-a-1229":["The proofs of transcendental and synthetic propositions have this peculiarity about them among all proofs of a synthetic cognition *a priori*: that reason, in the case of the former, by means of its concepts may not turn straight to the object, but must first establish the objective validity of the concepts and the possibility of the synthesis of them *a priori*. This is not perhaps merely a necessary rule of caution, but concerns the essence and the possibility of the proofs themselves. If I am to go beyond the concept of an object *a priori*, this is impossible without a particular guiding thread located outside this concept. In mathematics it is the intuition *a priori* that guides my synthesis, and there all inferences can be conducted immediately on pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as it has to do merely with concepts of the understanding, this guideline is possible experience. For the proof does not show: that the given concept (e.g., of that which happens) leads straight to another concept (that of a cause); for such a transition would be a leap that could not be answered for at all, but it shows: that experience itself, hence the object of experience, would be impossible without such a connection. Thus the proof had at the same time to indicate the possibility of arriving synthetically and *a priori* at a certain cognition of things that was not contained in the concept of them. Without this attentiveness the proofs run like waters that break through their banks, wildly and cross-country, to wherever the inclination of hidden association contingently leads them. The illusion of conviction, which rests on subjective causes of association and is taken for insight into a natural affinity, cannot at all balance the misgiving that must fairly arise over such risky steps. Hence also all attempts to prove the proposition of sufficient ground have, according to the general admission of experts, been in vain, and, before transcendental critique appeared, people preferred, since they still could not abandon this principle, to appeal defiantly to healthy human understanding (a refuge that always proves that the cause of reason is desperate), rather than to want to attempt new dogmatic proofs.",["'seiner Begriffe': the German has 'seiner' (masculine/neuter) where 'ihrer' would agree with 'Vernunft'; rendered 'its'. 'bey ienen' = 'in the case of the former' (literally 'with those'); antecedent ambiguous (proofs or propositions), left as such.","'a priori' in 'über den Begriff ... a priori hinausgehen' could attach to 'go beyond' or to 'object'; left ambiguous. 'Gegenstand' and 'Obiect' both = 'object'.","'Erkentniß' = cognition; 'Wesen' = essence; 'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Schlüsse' = inferences; 'Leitfaden' = guiding thread; 'Richtschnur' = guideline.","'Satz des zureichenden Grundes' = 'proposition of sufficient ground' (Satz = proposition), versus 'Grundsatz' = 'principle'.","'Zuslucht' is an OCR slip for 'Zuflucht' (refuge), silently corrected. 'zufälliger Weise' rendered 'contingently' (zufällig = contingent).","'Schein' = illusion; 'Überzeugung' = conviction; 'Kenner' = 'experts'; 'Menschenverstand' = 'human understanding'; 'Association' and 'Affinität' kept as cognates."]],"krv-a-1230":["But if the proposition for which a proof is to be conducted is an assertion of pure reason, and I want even by means of mere ideas to go beyond my concepts of experience, then it would have to contain within itself, even much more, the justification of such a step of synthesis (if it were possible at all) as a necessary condition of its power of proof. However plausible, therefore, the supposed proof of the simple nature of our thinking substance from the unity of apperception may be, the scruple nevertheless stands unavoidably against it: that, since absolute simplicity is after all no concept that can be related immediately to a perception, but, as an idea, must merely be inferred, it is not to be seen at all how the mere consciousness, which is contained in all thinking, or at least can be, although so far it is a simple representation, is supposed to lead over to the consciousness and the acquaintance of a thing in which thinking alone can be contained. For if I represent to myself the force of my body in motion, then so far it is for me absolute unity and my representation of it is simple, hence I can also express this through the motion of a point, because its volume does nothing here and, without diminution of the force, can be as small as one likes, and thus can also be thought as located in a point. From this, however, I will still not conclude: that, if nothing is given to me except the moving force of a body, the body could be thought as a simple substance, for the reason that its representation abstracts from all magnitude of the content of space and is therefore simple. Through this, now, that the simple in abstraction is entirely different from the simple in the object, and that the I, which in the first sense comprises no manifoldness at all within itself, in the second, where it signifies the soul itself, can be a very complex concept, namely to contain and to designate very much under itself, I discover a paralogism. But in order to have a presentiment of this beforehand, (for without such a preliminary conjecture one would form no suspicion at all against the proof) it is absolutely necessary to have at hand a perpetual criterion of the possibility of such synthetic propositions as are supposed to prove more than experience can give, which consists in this: that the proof not be conducted straight to the desired predicate, but only by means of a principle of the possibility of extending our given concept a priori up to ideas and of realizing these. If this caution is always used, if, before the proof is yet attempted, one first wisely takes counsel with oneself as to how and with what ground of hope one might expect such an extension through pure reason, and whence, in such a case, one wants to get these insights, which cannot be developed out of concepts and also cannot be anticipated in relation to possible experience: then one can spare oneself much difficult and yet fruitless effort, in that one demands of reason nothing that manifestly goes beyond its faculty, or rather subjects it, which, at the onset of its speculative craving for extension, does not gladly let itself be restricted, to the discipline of abstinence.",["Obiect/Gegenstand: 'Obiect' in 'Einfachen im Obiect' is rendered 'object', the same English word as for Gegenstand elsewhere in these passages.","'Wahrnehmung' = perception; 'Bewustseyn' = consciousness; 'Kentniß' = acquaintance (glossary Kenntnis, 'information; knowledge' also possible).","'Raumesinhalt' rendered 'content of space' (Inhalt = content); 'Volumen' kept as 'volume'.","'Bedenklichkeit' rendered 'scruple'; 'Verstande' in 'im ersteren Verstande' rendered 'sense' (a different use from Verstand = understanding).","'so gar' (so gar) read as 'even'; 'vor mich' = 'für mich', 'for me'.","Ambiguity kept: 'welches ... ob es zwar so fern eine einfache Vorstellung ist' — 'so fern' could be read 'so far' or 'insofar'; 'in welchem das Denken allein enthalten seyn kan' may attach to 'Dinges'.","'a priori' left in roman as in the German; it can attach to 'erweitern' or 'Begriff'.","'die, bey Anwandlungen ... läßt': the relative 'die' refers to 'sie' (reason); 'Erweiterungssucht' = 'craving for extension'. 'Paralogism' is German 'Paralogism'. 'einsehen' rendered 'see'; 'Einsichten' 'insights'."]],"krv-a-1231":["The first rule is thus this: to attempt no transcendental proofs without having first reflected and justified oneself on this score as to whence one wants to take the principles upon which one intends to erect them, and with what right one can expect from them the good success of the inferences. If they are principles of the understanding (e.g., of causality), then it is in vain to arrive by means of them at ideas of pure reason; for those are valid only for objects of possible experience. If they are to be principles from pure reason, then again all effort is in vain. For reason does have such, to be sure, but as objective principles they are all together dialectical, and can at most be valid only as regulative principles of the systematically interconnected use of experience. But if such alleged proofs are already at hand: then set against the deceptive conviction the *non liquet* of your matured power of judgment, and although you cannot yet penetrate its dazzling illusion, you still have the complete right to demand the deduction of the principles used in it, which, if they are supposed to have arisen from mere reason, can never be furnished to you. And so you do not even need to concern yourselves with the development and refutation of every groundless illusion, but can reject at once, in whole heaps, all dialectic, inexhaustible in artifices, at the tribunal of a critical reason, which demands laws.",["'Grundsätze' = principles throughout; 'Principien' (regulative Principien) also 'principles' — one English word for two German words (Grundsatz, Princip).","'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Erfolg der Schlüsse' = 'success of the inferences' (Schluß = inference).","'non liquet' is Latin in the original; kept in italics.","'Urtheilskraft' = power of judgment; 'Ueberzeugung' = conviction; 'Schein' = illusion; 'Blendwerk' rendered 'dazzling illusion' (not in glossary, a second German word under 'illusion' only by gloss).","'systematischzusammenhangenden' rendered 'systematically interconnected' (Zusammenhang = interconnection).","'desfals' read 'on this score'; 'insgesamt' rendered 'all together'; 'ihr' second-person plural address kept as 'you'.","'ob ihr gleich' rendered 'although'; 'schaffet' (schaffen) rendered 'furnished' ('geschaffet' = 'furnished/provided')."]],"krv-a-1232":["The second peculiarity of transcendental proofs is this: that for every transcendental proposition only a single proof can be found. If I am to infer not from concepts but from the intuition that corresponds to a concept, whether it be now a pure intuition, as in mathematics, or an empirical one, as in natural science: then the intuition laid at the basis gives me a manifold material for synthetic propositions, which I can connect in more than one way and, in that I may start from more than one point, arrive at the same proposition by different paths.",["'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Stoff' = material; 'Naturwissenschaft' = natural science (Wissenschaft = science).","'Satz' = proposition; 'verknüpfen' rendered 'connect' (Verknüpfung = connection).","'zum Grunde gelegte' = 'laid at the basis' (Grund = ground; basis).","'Eigenthümlichkeit' rendered 'peculiarity'; 'correspondirt' = 'corresponds'."]],"krv-a-1233":["Now, however, every transcendental proposition proceeds merely from a single concept and states the synthetic condition of the possibility of the object in accordance with this concept. The ground of proof can therefore be only a single one, because outside this concept there is nothing further through which the object could be determined, the proof therefore being able to contain nothing further than the determination of an object in general in accordance with this concept, which is also only a single one. We had, e.g., in the transcendental analytic the principle: everything that happens has a cause, drawn from the single condition of the objective possibility of a concept of that which happens in general: that the determination of an occurrence in time, hence this (occurrence) as belonging to experience, without standing under such a dynamical rule, would be impossible. This now is also the only possible ground of proof; for only thereby, that an object is determined for the concept by means of the law of causality, does the represented occurrence have objective validity, i.e., truth. One has, to be sure, attempted still other proofs of this principle, e.g., from contingency; but if this is considered in the light, one can find no mark of contingency except happening, i.e., existence, before which a non-being of the object precedes, and so comes back again and again to the same ground of proof. If the proposition is to be proved: everything that thinks is simple, then one does not dwell on the manifold of thinking, but persists merely with the concept of the I, which is simple and to which all thinking is related. Just so is it with the transcendental proof of the existence of God, which rests solely on the reciprocability of the concepts of the most real and the necessary being and can be sought nowhere else.",["'Gegenstand' = object; 'Begebenheit' = occurrence; 'Grundsatz' = principle; 'Beweisgrund' = ground of proof.","'Zufälligkeit' = contingency (zufällig = contingent); 'Kennzeichen' rendered 'mark' (Merkmal = mark is the glossary term; Kennzeichen is a different German word).","'Gültigkeit' = validity; 'Daseyn' = existence (Dasein); 'Wesen' = being.","'beim Lichten betrachtet' read 'beim Lichte betrachtet' (OCR/original 'Lichten'); rendered 'in the light'.","'das Daseyn, vor welchem ... vorher geht': 'vor' = 'before'; 'geschehen' kept as 'happening'.","'Reciprocabilität' rendered 'reciprocability'; 'realesten' = 'most real'.","Ambiguity kept: 'diese (Begebenheit)' parenthesis is Kant's own; 'welcher ... beruht' may refer to the proof."]],"krv-a-1234":["Through this warning remark the critique of the assertions of reason is reduced very much to the small. Where reason conducts its business through mere concepts, there only a single proof is possible, wherever any at all is possible. Hence, if one already sees the dogmatist appear with ten proofs, one can safely believe that he has none at all. For if he had one that (as must be the case in matters of pure reason) proved apodictically, what would he need the rest for. His aim is only like that of that parliamentary advocate: the one argument is for these, the other for those, namely, in order to make use of the weakness of his judges, who, without engaging deeply and in order to get free of the business soon, seize on the first best thing that happens to strike them, and decide accordingly.",["Critik = critique; Vernunft = reason; Begriff = concept; Beweis = proof; Absicht = aim.","Parlementsadvocaten: the French spelling of *Parlement* is kept in the German; rendered 'parliamentary advocate'.","'sehr ins kleine gebracht' kept literally ('reduced very much to the small')."]],"krv-a-1235":["The third peculiar rule of pure reason, when it is subjected to a discipline with regard to transcendental proofs, is: that its proofs must never be apogogic, but at all times ostensive. The direct or ostensive proof is, in every kind of cognition, that which combines with conviction of the truth at the same time insight into its sources, the apogogic one, by contrast, can indeed produce certainty, but not comprehensibility of the truth with regard to the interconnection with the grounds of its possibility. Hence the latter are more an emergency aid than a procedure that does justice to all aims of reason. Yet these have a preeminence of evidence over the direct proofs, in this: that contradiction always carries more clarity in the representation than the best connection, and thereby approaches more closely the intuitive of a demonstration.",["'oftensiv' in the OCR silently corrected to 'ostensiv'.","Ueberzeugung = conviction; Erkentniß = cognition; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Verknüpfung = connection; Vorstellung = representation; Widerspruch = contradiction.","Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility (cf. begreifen = comprehend).","'die leztere' / 'diese' in the German are plural and refer back to the apogogic proofs, though the preceding subject is singular ('der apogogische'); preserved.","'dem anschaulichen einer Demonstration': substantivized adjective, kept as 'the intuitive of a demonstration'."]],"krv-a-1236":["The proper cause of the use of apogogic proofs in various sciences is probably this. If the grounds from which a certain cognition is to be derived lie too manifold or too deeply hidden: then one tries whether they cannot be reached through the sequels. Now the *modus ponens*, to infer to the truth of a cognition from the truth of its sequels, would be permitted only if all possible sequels from it are true; for then only a single ground is possible for this, which is therefore also the true one. This procedure, however, is infeasible, because it goes beyond our powers to have insight into all possible sequels of any assumed proposition; yet one makes use of this way of inferring, although admittedly with a certain indulgence, when the concern is to prove something merely as a hypothesis, in that one grants the inference by analogy: that if as many sequels as one has ever tried agree well with an assumed ground, all the remaining possible ones will agree with it too. For that reason a hypothesis can never be transformed into demonstrated truth by this route. The *modus tollens* of the syllogisms, which infer from the sequels to the grounds, proves not only quite strictly but also exceedingly easily. For if even a single false sequel can be drawn from a proposition, then this proposition is false. Instead, now, of running through the whole series of grounds in an ostensive proof, which can lead to the truth of a cognition by means of the complete insight into its possibility, one need only find a single one false among the sequels flowing from the opposite of it, then this opposite is also false, hence the cognition that one had to prove, true.",["Folge = sequel, following the glossary; Folgerung (consequence) does not occur here.","Vernunftschlüsse = syllogisms; Schluß / schliessen = inference / to infer; Kräfte (Kraft) rendered 'powers'.","'zu mannigfaltig' kept as 'too manifold' (adjective of Mannigfaltige).","'Hypothese', 'modus ponens', 'modus tollens': the Latin kept in italics.","'zu diesem' ('for this') is ambiguous in the German as to its referent, as is 'darauf'; left as is."]],"krv-a-1237":["The apogogic kind of proof can, however, be permitted only in those sciences where it is impossible to substitute the subjective of our representations for the objective, namely for the cognition of that which is in the object. But where this latter prevails, it must frequently happen that the opposite of a certain proposition either merely contradicts the subjective conditions of thinking, but not the object, or that both propositions contradict one another only under a subjective condition which, falsely held to be objective, and since the condition is false, both can be false, without it being possible to infer from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other.",["Subiective / Obiective = subjective / objective; Gegenstand = object (Object also = object, where it occurs elsewhere in this group).","The sentence 'oder daß beide Sätze nur unter einer subiectiven Bedingung, die ... einander widersprechen und da die Bedingung falsch ist, alle beide falsch seyn können' is syntactically irregular in the German (the 'daß' clause is not completed with its own finite structure); the irregularity is preserved.","Bedingung = condition; Satz = proposition."]],"krv-a-1238":["In mathematics this subreption is impossible; hence they also have their proper place there. In natural science, because everything there is grounded on empirical intuitions, that surreption can indeed for the most part be prevented through many compared observations; but this kind of proof is nevertheless for the most part insignificant there. But the transcendental attempts of pure reason are one and all undertaken within the proper medium of dialectical illusion, i.e., of the subjective, which offers itself to reason in its premises as objective, or even forces itself upon it. Here now, as far as synthetic propositions are concerned, it cannot be permitted at all to justify one's assertions by refuting the opposite. For either this refutation is nothing other than the mere representation of the conflict of the opposed opinion with the subjective conditions of comprehensibility through our reason, which does nothing at all toward rejecting the matter itself on that account, (as, e.g., unconditioned necessity in the existence of a being absolutely cannot be comprehended by us, and therefore subjectively, with right, opposes itself to every speculative proof of a necessary highest being, but, without right, to the possibility of such an original being in itself), or both parts, the asserting as well as the denying, deceived by the transcendental illusion, lay at the ground an impossible concept of the object, and there the rule holds: *non entis nulla sunt praedicata*, i.e., both what one asserted affirmatively and what one asserted negatively of the object is incorrect, and one cannot arrive apogogically, through the refutation of the opposite, at the cognition of the truth. As for example, if it is presupposed that the world of sense is given in itself according to its totality, then it is false that it must be either infinite in space, or finite and bounded, because both are false. For appearances (as mere representations) which would yet be given in themselves (as objects) are something impossible, and the infinity of this imagined whole would indeed be unconditioned, but would contradict (because everything in appearances is conditioned) the unconditioned determination of magnitude which is yet presupposed in the concept.",["Subreption / Erschleichung: two German words, rendered 'subreption' and 'surreption' respectively.","'haben sie daselbst' : plural 'sie' (apogogic proofs?) has no explicit antecedent after the singular 'Subreption'; kept as 'they'.","Scheins = illusion; Erscheinungen = appearances; Totalität = totality (Allheit is also 'totality' in the glossary); Behauptung / behaupten = assertion / assert; Meinung = opinion; Wesen = being; Dasein = existence.","Sinnenwelt = world of sense; Urwesen = original being; Größenbestimmung = determination of magnitude.","'beide ... legen ... zum Grunde': 'lay at the ground' kept literal; the plural verb after 'der behauptende, als der verneinende Theil' preserved ('both parts ... lay').","'(Obiecte)' / 'Gegenstande': both rendered 'object'; Obiecte = Latinate member.","'non entis nulla sunt praedicata' in Latin kept in italics."]],"krv-a-1239":["The apagogic kind of proof is also the actual sham with which the admirers of the thoroughness of our dogmatic rationalizers have at all times been put off: it is, as it were, the champion who wants to prove the honor and the indisputable right of the party he has taken by declaring himself ready to brawl with everyone who might want to doubt it, although through such boasting nothing in the matter, but only the respective strength of the opponents, is settled, and indeed only on the side of the one who conducts himself as attacker. The spectators, in that they see that each in his turn is now victor, now succumbs, often take occasion from this to doubt skeptically the object of the dispute itself. But they have no cause for this, and it is enough to call out to them: *non defensoribus istis tempus eget.* Each must conduct his cause by means of a rightful proof, led through a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof, i.e., directly, so that one may see what his claims of reason have to adduce on their own behalf. For if his opponent rests on subjective grounds, he is of course easy to refute, but without advantage to the dogmatist, who commonly adheres likewise to the subjective causes of judgment and in like manner can be driven into a corner by his opponent. But if both parties proceed merely directly, then they will either notice by themselves the difficulty, indeed impossibility, of finding out the title of their assertions, and in the end will be able to appeal only to prescription, or the critique will easily discover the dogmatic illusion and will compel pure reason to give up its pretensions driven too high in speculative use and to withdraw within the boundaries of its own proper ground, namely practical principles.",["Blendwerk = \"sham\" (also at krv-a-1241, plural); Schein = \"illusion\" (\"dogmatischen Schein\"). Two German words, two English words.","Vernünftler = \"rationalizers\" (cognate of vernünfteln = rationalize); Vernunft = reason.","Beweisgründe = \"grounds of proof\"; Beweis = proof; Behauptung = assertion; Urtheil = judgment.","The Latin is printed \"eger\" in the A text; the quotation reads *eget* (Ovid); left as printed except for this note.","\"Veriährung\" = \"prescription\" (legal term).","\"ihnen ... ihre\" and \"seiner\" etc. are rendered with pronouns as in German; \"direct\" is kept in Kant's roman-type Latin form without italics because the OCR gives no sign of it."]],"krv-a-1240":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The Canon of Pure Reason.",["Hauptstück = \"chapter\"; Methodenlehre = \"doctrine of method\" (Lehre = doctrine).","Canon is kept as a Latinate loanword, here in English form \"canon\"."]],"krv-a-1241":["It is humiliating for human reason that in its pure use it accomplishes nothing, and even still requires a discipline to rein in its excesses and to prevent the shams that come to it from them. But on the other side it elevates it again and gives it a confidence in itself, that it can and must exercise this discipline itself, without permitting another censorship over itself, likewise that the boundaries it is compelled to set to its speculative use at the same time restrict the rationalizing pretensions of every opponent and consequently can secure against all attacks everything that might still remain to it of its previously exaggerated demands. The greatest and perhaps the only utility of all philosophy of pure reason is therefore probably only negative; since it namely serves not, as *Organon*, for expansion, but, as discipline, for the determination of boundaries and, instead of discovering truth, has only the quiet merit of preventing errors.",["\"daher kommen\": rendered \"come to it from them\"; \"them\" refers back to the excesses (Ausschweifungen), as the German allows but does not force.","Erweiterung = \"expansion\" (not in the glossary); Gränzbestimmung = \"determination of boundaries\"; Gränze = boundary.","\"ihr ... sie ... es\" pronouns left as in German; the referent of \"es\" (humbling/elevating) is not resolved.","vernünftelnde = \"rationalizing\" (vernünfteln); Blendwerke = \"shams\"."]],"krv-a-1242":["In the meantime there must still be somewhere a wellspring of positive cognitions that belong in the territory of pure reason and that perhaps give occasion for errors only through misunderstanding, but in fact constitute the goal of reason's zeal. For to what cause else should the irrepressible desire to gain a firm footing somewhere altogether beyond the boundary of experience be ascribed? It has a presentiment of objects that carry with them a great interest for it. It sets out on the path of mere speculation in order to approach them; but these flee from it. Presumably better luck is to be hoped for it on the sole path that is still left to it, namely that of practical use.",["Erkentnisse = \"cognitions\"; Gegenstände = \"objects\"; Erfahrung = experience; Gebrauch = use.","\"vor sie\" (= für sie) is rendered \"for it\"; \"sie\" throughout refers to Vernunft as in German."]],"krv-a-1243":["By a canon I understand the sum total of the principles a priori of the correct use of certain faculties of cognition in general. Thus general logic in its analytic part is a canon for understanding and reason in general, but only as to form, for it abstracts from all content. Thus the transcendental analytic was the canon of the pure understanding; for that alone is capable of true synthetic cognitions a priori. But where no correct use of a power of cognition is possible, there is no canon. Now all synthetic cognition of pure reason in its speculative use is, according to all the proofs conducted so far, entirely impossible. Therefore there is no canon at all of the speculative use of the same (for this is dialectical through and through), but all transcendental logic in this aim is nothing but discipline. Consequently, if there is anywhere a correct use of pure reason, in which case there must also be a canon of it, then this will concern not the speculative but the practical use of reason, which we therefore now want to investigate.",["Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Erkentnißvermögen = \"faculties of cognition\"; Erkentnißkraft = \"power of cognition\" (Kraft = force; power).","Absicht = \"aim\"; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","\"denn der ist allein ... fähig\": \"der\" = \"that\" (the understanding or the analytic is not resolved); \"überall\" = \"anywhere\".","\"derselben\" (\"of the same\") is left ambiguous between reason and its use, as in the German."]],"krv-a-1244":["First Section of the Canon of Pure Reason. On the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Our Reason.",["Zweck = \"end\"; lezt = \"ultimate\"; Abschnitt = \"section\"."]],"krv-a-1245":["Reason is driven by a propensity of its nature to go beyond the use in experience, to venture out in a pure use and by means of mere ideas to the outermost boundaries of all cognition and to find rest only in the completion of its circle, in a systematic whole subsisting for itself. Now is this striving grounded merely on its speculative, or rather solely and alone on its practical interest?",["Hang = \"propensity\"; Erfahrungsgebrauch = \"use in experience\" (literally \"experience-use\"); Ideen = ideas; Erkentniß = cognition.","\"vor sich bestehenden\" = \"subsisting for itself\"; Gränzen = boundaries."]],"krv-a-1246":["I will now set aside the luck that pure reason has in a speculative aim and ask only about those problems the solution of which constitutes its ultimate end, whether it may attain this or not, and with regard to which all others have merely the worth of means. These highest ends will, in accordance with the nature of reason, in turn have to have unity, in order jointly to promote that interest of humanity that is subordinated to no higher one.",["Aufgaben = \"problems\"; Auflösung = \"solution\"; Mittel = \"means\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Einheit = unity.","\"Glück\" = \"luck\" (not \"happiness\"); \"vereinigt\" = \"jointly\" (literally \"unitedly\").","\"sie mag diesen nun erreichen oder nicht\": \"sie\" (reason) and \"diesen\" (its ultimate end) left as in German; \"in Ansehung dessen\" = \"with regard to which\", antecedent ambiguous between the end and the problems."]],"krv-a-1247":["The final aim to which the speculation of reason in its transcendental use ultimately amounts concerns three objects: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. With regard to all three the merely speculative interest of reason is only very small, and with a view to it hardly a wearisome labor of transcendental research, wrestling with incessant obstacles, would well be undertaken, because of all the discoveries that might be made about this one can still make no use that would prove its utility *in concreto*, i.e., in the investigation of nature. The will may also be free, yet this can concern only the intelligible cause of our willing. For as concerns the phenomena of its expressions, i.e., actions, we must, according to an inviolable fundamental maxim, without which we cannot exercise any reason in empirical use, never explain them otherwise than all the other appearances of nature, namely according to its unchangeable laws. Second, the spiritual nature of the soul (and with it its immortality) may also be capable of being have insight into, yet no account can be made of this, either with regard to the appearances of this life, as a ground of explanation, or of the particular constitution of the future state, because our concept of an incorporeal nature is merely negative and does not extend our cognition in the least, nor offers any suitable material for consequences, except perhaps for such as can be valid only for fictions, which however are not permitted by philosophy. If third, the existence of a highest intelligence were also proved: then we would indeed make comprehensible to ourselves from it what is purposive in the arrangement of the world and the order in general, but would by no means be authorized to derive from it any particular arrangement and order, or, where it is not perceived, to infer it boldly, since it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason not to pass by natural causes and to give up that about which we can be taught through experience, in order to derive something that we know from that which entirely surpasses all our knowledge. In a word, these three propositions remain at all times transcendent for speculative reason and have no immanent use at all, i.e., one admissible for objects of experience, and hence in some way useful to us, but are, considered in themselves, entirely idle and, in addition, extremely difficult exertions of our reason.",["Correction needed: read \"may also be capable of being have insight into\" as \"may also be capable of being insighted\"; the German is \"eingesehen werden können\" (einsehen, 'have insight into'). The sentence as rendered above is ungrammatical and should be read as 'may also be capable of being seen into'.","Absicht = aim (Endabsicht = final aim; in Absicht auf = with a view to); Gegenstand = object; Gebrauch = use; Daseyn = existence; Erkentniß = cognition; Kentniß = knowledge; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Zustand = state; Stoff = material; Folgerungen = consequences; Zweckmässige = what is purposive; begreiflich = comprehensible (begreifen).","'Erklärungsgrund' = ground of explanation; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances, while 'Phänomene' is left as 'phenomena'.","'transsc. Nachforschung' is an abbreviation in the original, expanded as 'transcendental research'.","'kennen' (kennen) in 'was wir kennen' rendered 'we know'; 'Kentniß' also 'knowledge'; 'wahrgenommen' = perceived (Wahrnehmung = perception).","Ambiguity kept: 'in Absicht auf dasselbe' ('dasselbe' neuter, i.e. the speculative interest); 'vor' (= für) rendered 'for'/'to'.","'Welteinrichtung' = arrangement of the world (Welt = world); 'Anstalt' = arrangement."]],"krv-a-1248":["If accordingly these three cardinal propositions are not at all necessary for us for knowledge and are nevertheless urgently recommended to us by our reason: then their importance will properly have to concern only the practical.",["Wissen = knowledge (scientia); Sätze = propositions; nöthig = necessary (notwendig is otherwise 'necessary'); 'das Practische' = 'the practical', nominalized adjective kept."]],"krv-a-1249":["Practical is everything that is possible through freedom. But if the conditions of the exercise of our free power of choice are empirical, then reason can have in this no other than a regulative use, and serve only to bring about the unity of empirical laws, as e.g. in the doctrine of prudence the union of all the ends that are given to us by our inclinations in the single one, happiness, and the agreement of the means for attaining it, make up the entire business of reason, which on that account can supply no other than pragmatic laws of free conduct, for the attainment of the ends recommended to us by the senses, and thus no pure laws, determined completely *a priori*. By contrast, pure practical laws, whose end is given completely *a priori* through reason and which are not empirically conditioned but command absolutely, would be products of pure reason. Of this kind, however, are the moral laws, hence these alone belong to the practical use of pure reason, and permit a canon.",["Willkühr = power of choice (not in the glossary; used consistently); Lehre = doctrine; Bedingungen = conditions; bedingt = conditioned; Zweck = end; Mittel = means; Gesetze = laws; Sinne = senses.","'in den einigen' = 'in the single one' (einigen, here 'einzigen'); doubtful reading, possibly an old spelling of 'einzigen'.","'völlig a priori bestimt' could attach to 'Gesetze' or to 'liefern' (or the 'pure laws'); the ambiguity is kept.","'canon' kept as in the German (Canon)."]],"krv-a-1250":["The entire preparation of reason, therefore, in the working-out that can be called pure philosophy, is in fact directed only to the three problems mentioned. These themselves, however, have in turn their more remote aim, namely, what is to be done if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Since this now concerns our conduct in relation to the highest end, the final aim of nature, wisely providing for us in the arrangement of our reason, is properly directed only to the moral.",["Absicht = aim (Endabsicht in the preceding passage = final aim; here 'lezte Absicht' = final aim); Probleme = problems (Aufgabe in glossary = problem); Zweck = end; Welt = world; 'Philosophie' = philosophy.","'gestellet' in 'aufs Moralische gestellet' rendered 'directed', as 'gerichtet' earlier in the sentence is also 'directed'; the German uses two different verbs.","'Verhalten' = conduct."]],"krv-a-1251":["But caution is necessary, in order, since we cast our regard on an object that is foreign*) to transcendental philosophy, not to digress into episodes and violate the unity of the system, and on the other side also, in order, by saying too little of one's new material, not to let distinctness or conviction be lacking. I hope to accomplish both by keeping myself as near as possible to the transcendental and setting entirely aside whatever might here be psychological, i.e., empirical.",["Gegenstand = object; Stoff = material; Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Ueberzeugung = conviction; Einheit = unity; 'fremd*)' retains the footnote marker for the footnote in krv-a-1252.","'es an Deutlichkeit oder Ueberzeugung nicht fehlen zu lassen' is kept close to the German, with 'es' omitted in English."]],"krv-a-1252":["*) All practical concepts go to objects of liking or disliking, i.e., of pleasure and displeasure, hence at least indirectly to objects of our feeling. But since this is not a faculty of representation of things, but lies outside the entire faculty of cognition, the elements of our judgments, insofar as they relate to pleasure or displeasure, hence to the practical, do not belong in the sum total of transcendental philosophy, which has to do solely with pure cognitions *a priori*.",["Begriffe = concepts; Gegenstände = objects; Gefühl = feeling; Vorstellungskraft = faculty of representation (Kraft is 'force; power' in the glossary; 'faculty' chosen for the compound, Erkentnißkraft = faculty of cognition); Urtheile = judgments; Inbegriff = sum total; Erkentnissen = cognitions.","'mithin der practischen' is grammatically ambiguous (it may attach to 'Urtheile' or to 'Lust oder Unlust'); the ambiguity is kept as 'hence to the practical'.","'Transscendentalphilosophie' = transcendental philosophy."]],"krv-a-1253":["And here it is first to be remarked: that for the present I will make use of the concept of freedom only in the practical sense and set aside here, as dealt with above, that in the transcendental signification, which cannot be presupposed empirically as a ground of explanation of appearances, but is itself a problem for reason. A power of choice namely is merely animal (*arbitrium brutum*) which cannot be determined otherwise than through sensible impulses, i.e., pathologically. But that which can be determined independently of sensible impulses, hence through motives that are represented only by reason, is called the free power of choice (*arbitrium liberum*), and everything that is connected with this, whether as ground or as consequence, is called practical. Practical freedom can be proved through experience. For not merely that which stimulates, i.e., immediately affects the senses, determines the human power of choice, but we have a faculty of overcoming the impressions on our sensible faculty of desire through representations of that which is even in a remote way useful or harmful; but these reflections on that which is desirable with regard to our whole state, i.e., good and useful, rest on reason. This therefore also gives laws, which are imperatives, i.e., objective laws of freedom and which say what ought to happen, even though perhaps it never happens, and in this differ from laws of nature, which deal only with what happens, on which account they are also called practical laws.",["Begriff = concept; Freiheit = freedom; Erklärungsgrund = ground of explanation; Erscheinungen = appearances; Bedeutung = signification (glossary: meaning; 'signification' chosen to mark the verb bedeuten); Willkühr = power of choice; Bewegursachen = motives (Bewegungsgrund in glossary = motive; here 'Bewegursachen' also 'motives': one English word for two German words).","'im practischen Verstande' = 'in the practical sense' (Verstand here in the sense of 'meaning', not Verstand = understanding).","'Beweis'/'bewiesen' = proof/proved; Eindrücke = impressions; Ueberlegungen = reflections (Überlegung); Zustand = state; Vermögen = faculty; Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire.","'Folge' = consequence (glossary: sequel; sequence; 'consequence' for Folge here is a departure, kept for the ground/consequence pair); 'zusammenhängt' = 'is connected'; the Latin terms *arbitrium brutum* and *arbitrium liberum* are kept in italics.","Ambiguity kept: 'welcher nicht ... vorausgesezt werden kan' may refer back to 'Begriffs' (concept) or 'den' (that); 'es sey als Grund, oder Folge' = 'whether as ground or as consequence'.","'Dieienige' = 'that', referring back to Willkühr."]],"krv-a-1254":["Whether, however, reason itself in these actions, by which it prescribes laws, is not in turn determined by other influences, and whether what is called freedom with respect to sensible impulses might not in turn be nature with regard to higher and more remote efficient causes, that does not concern us in the practical, since we are asking reason in the first instance only for the precept of conduct, but is a merely speculative question, which we can set aside as long as our aim is directed to doing or omitting. We cognize practical freedom therefore through experience, as one of the natural causes, namely a causality of reason in the determination of the will, whereas transcendental freedom, an independence of this reason itself (with regard to its causality of beginning a series of appearances) from all determining causes of the world of sense, demands and so far seems to be contrary to the law of nature, hence to all possible experience, and thus remains a problem. But this problem does not belong to reason in practical use, thus in a Canon of pure reason we have to do with only two questions, which concern the practical interest of pure reason and with regard to which a Canon of its use must be possible, namely: is there a God? is there a future life? The question concerning transcendental freedom concerns merely speculative knowledge, which we can set aside as entirely indifferent, when it is a matter of the practical, and about which sufficient exposition is already to be found in the antinomy of pure reason.",["Original text reads \"leist\" (OCR/print slip); read as \"heißt\" and rendered \"is called\".","Absicht = aim in \"unsere Absicht\"; the idiom \"in Absicht auf\" / \"in Ansehung\" are both rendered \"with respect to\" / \"with regard to\".","\"fodert\" has no stated object in the German; none is supplied. \"so fern\" rendered \"so far\" (could also be \"insofar\").","Problem (as in \"ein Problem\") and Aufgabe are both \"problem\"; here only Problem occurs. Erörterung = exposition; Wissen = knowledge; hinreichend = sufficient.","\"welche\" in the last sentence has the feminine \"Frage\" as its grammatical antecedent; it could also be read loosely with \"Wissen\"."]],"krv-a-1255":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason, Second Section. On the Ideal of the Highest Good as a Determining Ground of the Final End of Pure Reason.",["Bestimmungsgrund = determining ground (Bestimmung + Grund); lezten Zwecks = final end."]],"krv-a-1256":["Reason led us in its speculative use through the field of experiences and, because complete satisfaction is never to be encountered there for it, from there to speculative ideas, which however in the end led us back again to experience and thus fulfilled their aim in a way that was indeed useful, but not at all conformable to our expectation. Now an attempt still remains to us: whether, namely, pure reason is also to be encountered in practical use, whether in it reason leads to the ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, which we have just cited, and this thus cannot, from the point of view of its practical interest, grant that which it entirely denies us with regard to the speculative.",["\"diese\" (\"und diese also ... nicht ... gewähren könne\") is left as \"this\"; its antecedent (reason, or the ideas) is not marked, and the construction after \"ob\" is preserved as it stands.","Absicht = aim; Zweck = end; Gesichtspunct = point of view; Erfahrungen = experiences (plural in the German)."]],"krv-a-1257":["All interest of my reason (the speculative as well as the practical) unites in the following three questions:",[]],"krv-a-1258":["1. What can I know?",["wissen = know (Wissen = knowledge)."]],"krv-a-1259":["2. What ought I to do?",[]],"krv-a-1260":["3. What may I hope?",[]],"krv-a-1261":["The first question is merely speculative. We have (as I flatter myself) exhausted all possible answers to it and finally found the one with which reason must indeed be satisfied and, if it does not look to the practical, also has cause to be content, but we have remained just as far removed from the two great ends toward which this whole striving of pure reason was properly directed, as if from ease we had refused ourselves this labor right from the beginning. If it is a matter of knowledge, then at least this much is certain and settled, that this, with regard to those two problems, can never fall to our share.",["Aufgaben = problems; Zwecke = ends; Wissen = knowledge.","\"sind aber ... geblieben\" continues with the subject \"we\" (wir) not restated, as in the German."]],"krv-a-1262":["The second question is merely practical. As such it can indeed belong to pure reason, but is then nevertheless not transcendental, but moral, hence it cannot occupy our critique in itself.",["Original \"transsendental\" (spelling) read as \"transscendental\". \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\"; it could attach to \"Critik\" or to the occupying (\"beschäftigen\"); the ambiguity is kept."]],"krv-a-1263":["The third question, namely: if I now do what I ought, what may I then hope? is practical and theoretical at once, so that the practical leads only as a guideline to the answer to the theoretical question and, if this goes high, the speculative one. For all hoping goes to happiness and is, with regard to the practical and the moral law, just the same as knowledge and the law of nature are with respect to the theoretical cognition of things. That runs out finally in the inference: that something is (which determines the final possible end), because something ought to happen; this, that something is (which acts as supreme cause), because something happens.",["\"Jenes ... dieses\" rendered \"That ... this\" (not \"the former/the latter\"). \"Schluß\" = inference.","\"und, wenn diese hoch geht, speculativen Frage\": the syntax is kept; \"speculative\" could be read as attached to \"Frage\" only, or as parallel to \"theoretical\".","Wissen = knowledge; Erkentniß = cognition; Sittengesetz = moral law; Leitfaden = guideline."]],"krv-a-1264":["Happiness is the satisfaction of all our inclinations, (both *extensive*, in the manifoldness of them, and *intensive*, in degree, and also *protensive*, according to duration). The practical law from the motive of happiness I call pragmatic, (rule of prudence) but that, if there is such a one, which has as motive nothing other than the worthiness to be happy, moral (moral law). The former advises what is to be done if we want to partake of happiness, the second commands how we ought to behave in order merely to become worthy of happiness. The former is grounded on empirical principles; for otherwise than by means of experience I can know neither which inclinations are there that want to be satisfied, nor what the natural causes are that can effect their satisfaction. The second abstracts from inclinations and natural means to satisfy them and considers only the freedom of a rational being in general and the necessary conditions under which alone it agrees with the distribution of happiness according to principles, and can thus at least rest on mere ideas of pure reason and be cognized *a priori*.",["Bewegungsgrund = motive; Principien = principles; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Grad = degree; Wesen = being.","\"sie allein\": \"it\" (sie) could refer to \"freedom\" or to \"being\"; the ambiguity is kept.","\"Das erstere ... das zweite\" rendered \"The former ... the second\" as in the German's variation.","*extensive*, *intensive*, *protensive*, *a priori* are Latin in the original."]],"krv-a-1265":["I assume: that there really are pure moral laws, which fully *a priori* (without regard to empirical motives, i.e., happiness) determine the doing and omitting, i.e., the use of the freedom of a rational being in general, and that these laws command absolutely (not merely hypothetically under presupposition of other empirical ends) and are thus necessary in every respect. This proposition I can rightly presuppose, not only in that I appeal to the proofs of the most enlightened moralists, but to the moral judgment of every human being, when he wants to think such a law distinctly.",["\"in aller Absicht\" is an idiom rendered \"in every respect\"; Absicht elsewhere = aim.","\"Schlechterdings\" capitalized in the original text. \"sittlich\" and \"moralisch\" are both rendered \"moral\"; Satz = proposition; Urtheil = judgment; deutlich = distinctly.","\"völlig a priori ... bestimmen\": \"a priori\" could attach to \"völlig\"/\"bestimmen\" or to the laws; the English keeps the position but the ambiguity remains."]],"krv-a-1266":["Pure reason thus contains, indeed not in its speculative, but yet in a certain practical, namely the moral use, principles of the possibility of experience, namely of such actions as could be encountered in the history of the human being in accordance with the moral precepts. For, since it commands that such ought to happen, they must also be able to happen and there must thus be possible a particular kind of systematic unity, namely the moral, whereas the systematic unity of nature could not be proved according to speculative principles of reason, because reason indeed has causality with regard to freedom in general, but not with regard to the entire nature and moral principles of reason can indeed bring forth free actions, but not laws of nature. Accordingly the principles of pure reason in their practical, but namely moral use, have objective reality.",["sittlich and moralisch both \"moral\"; Vorschriften = precepts; Principien = principles; Realität = reality; Art = kind; besondere = particular.","\"es\" in \"da sie gebietet\" and \"sie\" in \"müssen sie auch geschehen können\": pronouns left as in the German (\"it\" = reason; \"they\" = the actions).","\"namentlich aber, dem moralischen Gebrauche\" is rendered literally (\"but namely moral use\")."]],"krv-a-1267":["I call the world, so far as it would be in accord with all moral laws (as it can be, in accordance with the freedom of rational beings, and, in accordance with the necessary laws of morality, ought to be), a moral world. So far as this is thought merely as an intelligible world, since in it abstraction is made from all conditions (ends) and even from all obstacles to morality in it (weakness or impurity of human nature). So far as that goes it is thus a mere, but yet practical idea, which really can and ought to have its influence on the world of sense, in order to make it as much as possible accord with this idea. The idea of a moral world therefore has objective reality, not as if it pertained to an object of an intelligible intuition (of which kind we cannot think at all), but to the world of sense, though as an object of pure reason in its practical use and a *corpus mysticum* of the rational beings in it, so far as their free power of choice under moral laws has thoroughgoing systematic unity in itself, both with itself and with the freedom of everyone else.",["Sinnenwelt = 'world of sense' throughout; intelligibele Welt = 'intelligible world'.","Willkühr = 'power of choice'; Wesen = 'being'; Gegenstand = 'object', obiective = 'objective'.","'So fern' = 'so far as'; the second sentence ('Diese wird so fern blos ... gedacht, weil ...') has no main clause in the German as printed, and the English keeps it that way.","'a priori', *corpus mysticum*: Latin kept in italics."]],"krv-a-1268":["That was the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason that concerned the practical interest: Do that through which you become worthy to be happy. The second now asks: how, if I now behave so that I am not unworthy of happiness, may I also hope to be able thereby to partake of it? In answering it, what matters is whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe the law *a priori*, also necessarily connect this hope with it.",["Glückseligkeit = 'happiness'; glücklich = 'happy'; verknüpfen = 'connect'.","'damit' (with it) could refer to the law or to the prescribing; the English 'with it' keeps the pronoun unresolved."]],"krv-a-1269":["I say accordingly: that just as the moral principles are necessary according to reason in its practical use, just so necessary it is also according to reason, in its theoretical use, to assume that everyone has cause to hope for happiness in the same measure as he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that therefore the system of morality is inseparably, but only in the idea of pure reason, combined with that of happiness.",["Verbunden = 'combined' (Verbindung = combination), distinguished from verknüpfen = 'connect'.","'eben sowol, als ... eben so nothwendig' is rendered literally and the construction is somewhat awkward in English.","'nur in der Idee der reinen Vernunft' could modify 'inseparably' or 'combined'; the English keeps the German placement."]],"krv-a-1270":["Now in an intelligible, i.e., the moral world, in whose concept we abstract from all obstacles to morality (inclinations), such a system of happiness proportioned and combined with morality can also be thought as necessary, because freedom, in part moved, in part restricted by moral laws, would itself be the cause of general happiness, rational beings therefore themselves, under the guidance of such principles, would be authors of their own and at the same time others' lasting welfare. But this system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, the execution of which rests on the condition that everyone do what he ought, i.e., that all actions of rational beings happen as if they arose from a supreme will that comprehends all private power of choice in itself, or under itself. Since, however, the obligation from the moral law remains valid for each particular use of freedom, even if others did not behave in conformity with this law, neither from the nature of the things of the world, nor from the causality of the actions themselves and their relation to morality is it determined how their consequences will relate to happiness, and the adduced necessary connection of the hope to be happy with the unremitting endeavor to make oneself worthy of happiness cannot be cognized by reason if one lays merely nature at the ground, but may only be hoped for if a highest reason, which commands in accordance with moral laws, is at the same time laid at the ground as the cause of nature.",["Sittlichkeit and Moralität are both rendered 'morality'; Sittlichkeit at 'Hindernissen der Sittlichkeit', 'Verhältnisse zur Sittlichkeit', Moralität at 'mit der Moralität verbundenen', 'sich selbst lohnenden Moralität'.","Folgen = 'consequences' (the glossary's Folge = sequel/sequence is not used here); Verbindlichkeit = 'obligation'; Caussalität = 'causality'; erkant = 'cognized'.","'zum Grunde legen' = 'lay at the ground' (Grund = ground); 'Urheber' = 'authors'; 'Wolfarth' = 'welfare'.","'so ... noch' correlation after 'Da aber' is kept as in the German ('neither ... nor' with the 'so' omitted)."]],"krv-a-1271":["I call the idea of such an intelligence, in which the morally most perfect will, combined with the highest blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, so far as it stands in exact relation to morality (as the worthiness to be happy), the ideal of the highest good. Thus pure reason can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derived good, namely, of an intelligible, i.e., moral world, only in the ideal of the highest original good. Since we must now necessarily represent ourselves through reason as belonging to such a world, although the senses present to us nothing but a world of appearances, we will have to assume the former as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense, since the latter does not offer us such a connection, as a world that is future for us. God therefore and a future life are two presuppositions, not to be separated, in accordance with principles of this very same reason, from the obligation that pure reason imposes on us.",["'iene' (the former) and 'diese' (the latter) are rendered as 'the former'/'the latter', leaving the referents as open as in the German.","Seligkeit = 'blessedness'; Glückseligkeit = 'happiness'; Verhältnisse = 'relation'; Folge = 'consequence'; Erscheinungen = 'appearances'.","'vor uns künftige Welt' = 'a world that is future for us'; 'Voraussetzungen' = 'presuppositions'.","'Ideal' is printed in roman type in the German; here it is rendered without italics because it is not a foreign word in English."]],"krv-a-1272":["Morality in itself makes up a system, but not happiness, except so far as it is distributed exactly appropriately to morality. This, however, is possible only in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Reason sees itself compelled to assume such a one, together with life in such a world, which we must regard as a future one, or to regard the moral laws as empty figments of the brain, because the necessary result of them, which the same reason connects with them, would have to fall away without that presupposition. Hence everyone also regards the moral laws as commands, which they could not be if they did not connect with their rule appropriate consequences *a priori* and thus carry promises and threats with them. This, however, they also cannot do unless they lie in a necessary being, as the highest good, which alone can make possible such a purposive unity.",["Sittlichkeit and Moralität both = 'morality'; Hirngespinste = 'figments of the brain'; Erfolg = 'result'; Folgen = 'consequences'.","'a priori' could attach to 'connect' or to 'appropriate consequences'; the English keeps the German's ambiguity.","'zweckmässige Einheit' = 'purposive unity'; Wesen = 'being'."]],"krv-a-1273":["Leibniz called the world, so far as one pays attention in it only to the rational beings and their interconnection in accordance with moral laws under the government of the highest good, the kingdom of grace and distinguished it from the kingdom of nature, where they indeed stand under moral laws, but expect no other results of their conduct than in accordance with the course of nature of our world of sense. To see oneself in the kingdom of grace, then, where all happiness awaits us, except so far as we ourselves limit our share of it through the unworthiness to be happy, is a practically necessary idea of reason.",["Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Erfolge = 'results'; Reich = 'kingdom'.","'Leibnitz' modernized to 'Leibniz'.","'Sich also ... zu sehen' (infinitive subject) kept as 'To see oneself ... is'."]],"krv-a-1274":["Practical laws, so far as they at the same time become subjective grounds of actions, i.e., subjective principles, are called maxims. The judgment of morality, in accordance with its purity and consequences, occurs in accordance with ideas, the following of its laws in accordance with maxims.",["Gründe = 'grounds'; Grundsätze = 'principles'; Beurtheilung = 'judgment' (glossary: Beurteilung = judgment); Folgen = 'consequences'.","'Befolgung ihrer Gesetze': 'following of its laws'; 'ihrer' follows 'Sittlichkeit' (feminine singular), hence 'its'."]],"krv-a-1275":["It is necessary that our whole conduct of life be subordinated to moral maxims; but it is at the same time impossible for this to happen if reason does not connect with the moral law, which is a mere idea, an effective cause, which determines for conduct in accordance with it an outcome exactly corresponding to our highest ends, be it in this or in another life. Thus without a God and a world not now visible to us but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are indeed objects of approbation and of admiration, but not incentives of resolve and of practice, because they do not fulfill the whole end that is natural to every rational being and determined *a priori* and necessary through this very same pure reason.",["'wirkende Ursache' = 'effective cause'; Ausgang = 'outcome'; Zwecke/Zweck = 'ends'/'end'; Gegenstände = 'objects'.","Triebfedern = 'incentives'; Vorsatz = 'resolve'; Ausübung = 'practice' (neither is in the glossary).","'a priori' could attach to 'determined' only or to 'determined and necessary'; the English keeps the German order.","'Lebenswandel' = 'conduct of life'; 'Verhalten' = 'conduct'."]],"krv-a-1276":["Happiness alone is far from being the complete good for our reason. It does not approve of it (however much inclination may wish for it) unless it is united with worthiness to be happy, i.e., with moral good conduct. But morality alone and, with it, the mere worthiness to be happy, is also still far from being the complete good. In order to complete this, he who had conducted himself as not unworthy of happiness must be able to hope to partake of it. Even reason free from all private aim, if, without taking into consideration any interest of its own in so doing, it put itself in the place of a being that had to distribute all happiness to others, cannot judge otherwise; for in the practical idea both pieces are essentially combined, though in such a way that the moral disposition, as condition, first makes possible the share in happiness, and not conversely the prospect of happiness the moral disposition. For in the latter case it would not be moral and thus also not worthy of the whole happiness, which recognizes before reason no other limitation than that which stems from our own immoral conduct.",["Glückseligkeit = happiness; Gesinnung = disposition; Sittlichkeit = morality, moralisch = moral (sittlich and moralisch both rendered by 'moral'/'morality'; Sittlichkeit is not in the glossary).","Stücke rendered literally as 'pieces' here (cf. 'in allen Stücken' in krv-a-1278).","Einschränkung = limitation (Schranke also 'limitation' in the glossary; not at issue here). 'erkent' = recognizes (erkennen).","'Wesen' = being. 'Absicht' = aim. 'verbunden' = combined (Verbindung); 'vereinigt' = united."]],"krv-a-1277":["Happiness, therefore, in the exact proportion with the morality of rational beings, through which they are worthy of it, alone constitutes the highest good of a world into which we must absolutely transpose ourselves according to the precepts of pure but practical reason, and which is, to be sure, only an intelligible world, since the world of sense does not promise us such systematic unity of ends from the nature of things, the reality of which also cannot be grounded on anything else than on the presupposition of a highest original good, since self-subsistent reason, equipped with all sufficiency of a supreme cause, grounds, maintains and fulfills, according to the most perfect purposiveness, the universal order of things, though very hidden from us in the world of sense.",["'deren Realität': the antecedent is ambiguous in the German (Einheit, Zwecke, Natur) and the English 'the reality of which' keeps this. The two 'da' clauses are both rendered 'since'; 'da' could also be 'where'.","Sinnenwelt = world of sense; selbstständige = self-subsistent; Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency; Zweckmässigkeit = purposiveness; ursprünglich = original; intelligibele = intelligible."]],"krv-a-1278":["This moral theology now has the peculiar advantage over the speculative: that it leads unfailingly to the concept of a single, most perfect and rational original being, to which speculative theology does not even point us from objective grounds, let alone could convince us of it. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, as far as reason may lead us therein, any significant ground for assuming only a single being, which we would set before all natural causes and on which we would at the same time have sufficient cause to make them dependent in all respects. By contrast, if from the standpoint of moral unity, as a necessary world-law, we consider the cause that alone can give to this the appropriate effect, and thus also obligating force for us, then it must be a single supreme will that encompasses all these laws in itself. For how would we find perfect unity of ends among different wills? This will must be all-powerful, so that the whole of nature and its relation to morality in the world be subject to it, all-knowing, so that it cognize the innermost of dispositions and their moral worth, omnipresent, so that it be immediately near to every need which the highest best of the world demands, eternal, so that at no time this agreement of nature and freedom be lacking, etc.",["einig = single (twice; 'einigen Wesen', 'einiger oberster Wille'); Urwesen = original being; obiectiv = objective.","'in allen Stücken' = 'in all respects' (Stück, literally 'piece'). 'Effect' (Latinate) = effect; Wirkung also 'effect' in the glossary.","'befaßt' rendered 'encompasses' (not 'comprehends', which is reserved for begreifen). 'erkenne' = cognize.","'allem Bedürfnisse, welche': the German number agreement is irregular; kept as 'every need which'. 'dieser' in 'diesem allein' is ambiguous (Weltgesetz / Einheit); 'this' kept."]],"krv-a-1279":["But this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences, which, although as mere nature only world of sense, but as a system of freedom can be called an intelligible, i.e., moral world (*regnum gratiae*), leads unfailingly also to the purposive unity of all things that make up this great whole, according to universal laws of nature, just as the former does according to universal and necessary moral laws, and unites practical reason with the speculative. The world must be represented as having arisen from an idea, if it is to agree with that use of reason without which we would hold ourselves unworthy of reason, namely the moral, as that which rests throughout on the idea of the highest good. Thereby all research into nature gets a direction toward the form of a system of ends and in its highest expansion becomes physico-theology. This, however, since it did after all start from moral order, as a unity grounded in the essence of freedom and not contingently instituted through external commands, brings the purposiveness of nature to grounds that must be inseparably connected a priori with the inner possibility of things, and thereby to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity, which connects all things according to universal and necessary laws of nature, because they all have their origin in the absolute necessity of a single original being.",["Sittengesetzen = moral laws (moralische = moral); 'Wesen der Freiheit' = 'essence of freedom' (Wesen = being; essence).","Ausbreitung = expansion (not in glossary; Ausdehnung = extension). verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung); vereinigt = unites.","'die erstere' / 'die letztere' are not specified in the German; 'the former' retained. 'Diese' ('This, however') refers to physico-theology, but the German pronoun is left as it stands. 'a priori' attaches ambiguously to 'connected' or 'grounds', as in the German.","'einigen Urwesens' = 'single original being'."]],"krv-a-1280":["What use can we make of our understanding, even with regard to experience, if we do not set ends for ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and only pure reason can give us to cognize this. Now provided with these and along the guiding thread of them we can make no purposive use of acquaintance with nature itself with regard to cognition, where nature has not itself laid purposive unity; for without this we would not even have reason, since we would have no school for it and no culture through objects that would offer the material for such concepts. But that purposive unity is necessary and grounded in the essence of the power of choice itself, this therefore, which contains the condition of the application of the latter *in concreto*, must also be so, and thus the transcendental elevation of our cognition of reason would not be the cause but merely the effect of the practical purposiveness that pure reason imposes on us.",["Willkühr is not in the glossary; rendered 'power of choice'. 'Wesen' = essence.","Kentniß = acquaintance (Kenntnis); Erkentniß = cognition (Erkenntnis); Vernunfterkentniß = 'cognition of reason'.","'diese' / 'derselben' / 'dieselbe' have unclear antecedents in the German (Zwecke, Moralität, Einheit); the English pronouns follow the German order and are left equally open. 'Die höchste Zwecke' is a plural with irregular form in the original.","'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Stoff' = material."]],"krv-a-1281":["We therefore also find in the history of human reason: that, before the moral concepts were sufficiently purified, determined, and the systematic unity of ends according to them, and indeed from necessary principles, were seen into, acquaintance with nature and even a considerable degree of the culture of reason in many other sciences could in part produce only crude and roving concepts of the deity, in part left over an admirable indifference in general with regard to this question. A greater working-out of moral ideas, which was made necessary by the utterly pure moral law of our religion, sharpened reason upon the object, through the interest that it compelled it to take in it, and, without either extended acquaintance with nature or correct and reliable transcendental insights (such as have lacked at all times) contributing thereto, they brought about a concept of the divine being, which we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it agrees completely with the moral principles of reason. And so in the end it is always only pure reason, but only in its practical use, that has the merit of tying a cognition, which mere speculation can only fancy but not make valid, to our highest interest, and thereby, though not into a demonstrated dogma, yet into an absolutely necessary presupposition for its most essential ends.",["The German 'eingesehen waren' (einsehen) is rendered 'were seen into'; 'Einsichten' = insights. The plural 'waren' with 'Einheit' as sole subject is as in the German.","'sie brachten' has an ambiguous antecedent (ideas / insights); 'they' kept. 'es ... was sie nöthigte': 'it compelled it' keeps the ambiguity of reason / interest.","Kentniß/Kentnisse = acquaintance; Erkentniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; 'Wesen' = being; 'geltend machen' = 'make valid' (gültig). 'specutative' is a misprint in the source for 'speculative'; silently corrected.","'Bearbeitung' = 'working-out' (not in the glossary). 'Zusammenstimmen' = 'agree'. Final clause: 'zu machen' governs both 'zu einem demonstrirten Dogma' and 'zu einer ... Voraussetzung'; English follows with 'into ... into'."]],"krv-a-1282":["But if practical reason has now reached this high point, namely the concept of a single original being, as the highest good, it may not at all presume, just as if it had raised itself above all empirical conditions of its application and soared up to the immediate acquaintance with new objects, in order to proceed from this concept and to derive the moral laws themselves from it. For it was precisely these, whose inner practical necessity led us to the presupposition of a self-subsisting cause, or of a wise ruler of the world, in order to give effect to those laws, and therefore we cannot in turn regard them according to this as contingent and as derived from mere will, especially from such a will of which we would have no concept at all if we had not formed it in accordance with those laws. As far as practical reason has the right to lead us, we will not hold actions to be obligatory because they are commands of God, but rather regard them as divine commands because we are internally obligated to it. We will study freedom, under the purposive unity according to principles of reason, and believe ourselves to be in accordance with the divine will only insofar as we hold the moral law, which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, holy, believe to serve it solely thereby, that we promote the best of the world in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is therefore only of immanent use, namely to fulfill our vocation here in the world, in that we fit into the system of all ends and do not fanatically, or indeed even outrageously, abandon the guideline of a morally legislating reason in good conduct of life, in order to tie it immediately to the idea of the highest being, which would give a transcendent use, but just like that of mere speculation, must pervert and frustrate the last ends of reason.",["Urwesen = 'original being'; Wesen = 'being' (glossary). einigen = 'single'.","Kentniß = 'acquaintance' (glossary: Kenntnis); Gegenstände = 'objects'.","sich unterwinden = 'presume' (not in glossary); 'dazu' rendered 'to it', as in the German's loose reference (the commands/actions).","Effect (Latinate) and Wirkung both would be 'effect'; here only Effect occurs. Bestimmung = 'vocation' (glossary).","'welches ... geben würde, aber ... muß': the relative clause and the construction with 'der' (= Gebrauch) are left as awkward as the German; the subject of 'verkehren und vereiteln muß' is not explicit.","'glauben' (believe) is repeated in the German; 'ihm' (it) is ambiguous between the moral law and the divine will, as in the German."]],"krv-a-1283":["Third Section of the Canon of Pure Reason. On Opining, Knowing and Believing.",["Meinen = 'opining' (cf. Meinung = 'opinion'), Wissen = 'knowing' (knowledge, scientia), Glauben = 'believing' (Glaube = 'belief')."]],"krv-a-1284":["Taking to be true is an occurrence in our understanding that may rest on objective grounds, but also requires subjective causes in the mind of him who judges. If it is valid for everyone, insofar as he only has reason, then its ground is objectively sufficient and the taking to be true is then called conviction. If it has its ground only in the particular constitution of the subject, then it is called persuasion.",["Vorwahrhalten = 'taking to be true' (glossary: Fürwahrhalten). Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Gemüth = 'mind'.","hinreichend is not in the glossary; rendered 'sufficient' (as zureichend).","Ueberzeugung = 'conviction'; Ueberredung = 'persuasion'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Subiect = 'subject'."]],"krv-a-1285":["Persuasion is a mere illusion, because the ground of the judgment, which lies solely in the subject, is taken to be objective. Hence such a judgment also has only private validity, and the taking to be true cannot be communicated. But truth rests on the agreement with the object, in regard to which consequently the judgments of every understanding must be in unison (*consentientia uni tertio, consentiunt inter se*). The touchstone of taking to be true, whether it is conviction or mere persuasion, is therefore, externally, the possibility of communicating it and of finding the taking to be true valid for every human being's reason; for then there is at least a presumption that the ground of the agreement of all judgments, regardless of the difference of the subjects among one another, will rest on the common ground, namely the object, with which they therefore all agree and thereby will prove the truth of the judgment.",["Schein = 'illusion'; Obiect = 'object'; Uebereinstimmung = 'agreement', Einstimmung = 'agreement' too (both rendered so; einstimmig = 'in unison', zusammenstimmen = 'agree').","mittheilen = 'communicate' (not in glossary). Probierstein = 'touchstone'. Vermuthung = 'presumption'.","The Latin tag is retained in italics, untranslated."]],"krv-a-1286":["Persuasion accordingly cannot indeed be distinguished subjectively from conviction, if the subject has the taking to be true before his eyes merely as an appearance of his own mind; but the experiment that one makes with its grounds, which are valid for us, on other understanding, whether they have just the same effect on foreign reason as on our own, is nevertheless a means, though only subjective, not indeed to bring about conviction, but still to discover the merely private validity of the judgment, i.e., something in it that is mere persuasion.",["Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Mittel = 'means'.","'an anderer Verstand' is rendered 'on other understanding' (the German gives a bare genitive-like/plural form; could be 'another's' or 'others''). 'fremde Vernunft' = 'foreign reason'.","The concessive 'obzwar ... zwar nicht ... aber doch' structure is kept."]],"krv-a-1287":["If, moreover, one can develop the subjective causes of the judgment, which we take for objective grounds of it, and thus explain the deceptive taking to be true as an occurrence in our mind, without needing for this the constitution of the object, then we strip bare the illusion and are no longer deceived by it, although still always tempted to a certain degree, if the subjective cause of the illusion adheres to our nature.",["entwickeln = 'develop'; erklären = 'explain' (cf. Erklärung); hintergehen = 'deceive'.","'versucht' could be 'tempted' or 'tried'; rendered 'tempted'."]],"krv-a-1288":["I can assert nothing, i.e., pronounce as a judgment necessarily valid for everyone, except what effects conviction. Persuasion I can keep for myself if I feel well in it, but cannot and ought not to want to make it hold outside me.",["behaupten = 'assert'; 'wenn ich mich dabey wol befinde' rendered literally; 'geltend machen' = 'make hold'."]],"krv-a-1289":["Taking to be true, or the subjective validity of the judgment, in relation to conviction (which is at the same time objectively valid), has the following three steps: opining, believing and knowing. Opining is a taking to be true that is, with consciousness, insufficient both subjectively and objectively. If the latter is only subjectively sufficient and is at the same time held to be objectively insufficient, then it is called believing. Finally, the taking to be true sufficient both subjectively and objectively is called knowing. The subjective sufficiency is called conviction (for myself), the objective, certainty (for everyone). I will not dwell on the clarification of such easily grasped concepts.",["Stufen = 'steps'; Bewustseyn = 'consciousness'; Gewißheit = 'certainty'; Erläuterung = 'clarification'.","Zulänglichkeit = 'sufficiency' (as zureichend/hinreichend, not distinguished in English).","'das leztere' (the latter) is ambiguous in the German as to its antecedent (the taking to be true); kept. faßlich = 'easily grasped' (avoiding 'comprehend' = begreifen)."]],"krv-a-1290":["I may never presume to opine without at least knowing something by means of which the judgment, in itself merely problematic, acquires a connection with truth, which, although not complete, is still more than arbitrary fiction. The law of such a connection must moreover be certain. For if I also have nothing but opinion in regard to that, then everything is only a play of the imagination, without the slightest relation to truth. In judgments from pure reason it is not permitted at all to opine. For, because they are not supported on grounds of experience, but everything is to be cognized *a priori*, where everything is necessary, the principle of connection requires universality and necessity, hence complete certainty, otherwise no guidance to truth at all is encountered. Hence it is absurd to opine in pure mathematics, one must know, or refrain from all judging. It is just so with the principles of morality, where one may not venture an action on the mere opinion that something is permitted, but must know this.",["Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Einbildung = 'imagination' (glossary has Einbildungskraft); Erdichtung = 'fiction'.","Princip and Grundsätze both = 'principle(s)'.","'in Ansehung dessen' = 'in regard to that'; the referent of 'dessen' (the law of connection) is left as in the German.","'zu wissen'/'wissen' = 'knowing'/'know'; erkennen = 'cognize'."]],"krv-a-1291":["In the transcendental use of reason, on the other hand, opining is of course too little, but knowing too much. In merely speculative aim we can therefore not judge at all here; because subjective grounds of taking to be true, such as those that can effect believing, deserve no approval in speculative questions, since they cannot maintain themselves free of all empirical assistance, nor be communicated to others in equal measure.",["Absicht = 'aim'; Beifall = 'approval'; Beihülfe = 'assistance'; Maasse = 'measure'.","'die, so das Glauben bewirken können': 'those that can effect believing'; the subject of 'halten ... lassen' (grounds) is kept ambiguous as in the German."]],"krv-a-1292":["But it is in general only in practical relation that the theoretically insufficient taking to be true can be called believing. This practical aim is now either that of skill or that of morality, the first for discretionary and contingent ends, but the second for absolutely necessary ends.",["'überall blos' rendered 'in general only'; could also be 'everywhere/at all'.","Geschicklichkeit = 'skill'; Sittlichkeit = 'morality'; zufällig = 'contingent'; Zwecke = 'ends'; beliebig = 'discretionary'."]],"krv-a-1293":["Once an end is proposed, the conditions for attaining it are hypothetically necessary. This necessity is subjective, but still only comparatively sufficient, if I know of no other conditions at all under which the end could be attained; but it is sufficient absolutely and for everyone, if I know for certain: that no one can know other conditions that lead to the proposed end. In the first case my presupposition and the taking-to-be-true of certain conditions is a merely contingent belief, but in the second case a necessary one. The physician must do something for a patient who is in danger, but does not know the illness. He looks to the appearances and judges, because he knows nothing better, that it is consumption. His belief is contingent merely, even in his own judgment; another might perhaps hit upon it better. I call such a contingent belief, which however lies at the ground of the actual use of means for certain actions, pragmatic belief.",["Vorwahrhalten = Fürwahrhalten, rendered 'taking-to-be-true' (hyphenated for the substantivized form); Zweck = end; Mittel = means; Gebrauch = use; Erscheinungen = appearances.","weis (wissen) and kennen are both rendered 'know'; the German distinction is lost in English.","vorgesezt rendered 'proposed' (not in the glossary); zufällig = contingent; zureichend = sufficient.","'es sey die Schwindsucht': subjunctive of indirect speech, rendered 'that it is consumption'."]],"krv-a-1294":["The usual touchstone for whether what someone asserts is mere persuasion, or at least subjective conviction, i.e., firm belief, is betting. Often someone pronounces his propositions with such confident and unmanageable defiance that he seems to have laid aside all apprehension of error entirely. A wager makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out: that he does possess persuasion enough, which can be estimated at the value of one ducat, but not of ten. For the first he still ventures well enough, but at ten he becomes aware for the first time of what he did not notice before, namely that it might well be possible he has erred. If one represents to oneself in thought: that one is to wager the happiness of one's whole life on something, then our triumphant judgment dwindles very much, we become exceedingly timid and discover thus for the first time that our belief does not reach so far. Thus pragmatic belief has only a degree, which according to the difference of the interest that is at play in it can be great or also small.",["Ueberredung = persuasion; Ueberzeugung = conviction; behaupten = assert; Urtheil = judgment; Grad = degree.","Glück rendered 'happiness' here; the German word also has the sense of fortune.","'wird er allererst inne' = 'becomes aware for the first time'; 'nicht zulange' = 'does not reach' (literally, is not long enough)."]],"krv-a-1295":["But because, although in relation to an object we cannot undertake anything at all, so that the taking-to-be-true is merely theoretical, we can nevertheless in many cases conceive in thought and imagine an undertaking for which we believe ourselves to have sufficient grounds, if there were a means to settle the certainty of the matter, so there is in merely theoretical judgments an *Analogon* of practical ones, to whose taking-to-be-true the word belief fits, and which we can call doctrinal belief. If it were possible to settle it through some experience, I would well wager all of what is mine on this: that there are inhabitants at least on some one of the planets that we see. Hence I say, it is not merely opinion but a strong belief (on whose correctness I would already risk many advantages of life), that there are also inhabitants of other worlds.",["Obiect = object; hinreichend rendered 'sufficient' (as zureichend), two German words behind one English word.","'den wir den doctrinalen Glauben nennen können': the masculine relative 'den' is left to refer as in the German (to Glauben, with 'Analogon' intervening).","Meinung = opinion; Welt = world; Erfahrung = experience; Mittel = means."]],"krv-a-1296":["Now we must admit: that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For, although with regard to theoretical acquaintance with the world I have nothing to dispose that necessarily presupposes this thought as a condition of my explanations of the appearances of the world, but am rather bound to use my reason as if everything were merely nature, still purposive unity is so great a condition of the application of reason to nature that I, since experience moreover offers me examples of it abundantly, cannot pass it by at all. But for this unity I know of no other condition that would make it a guiding thread of the investigation of nature for me, than if I presuppose: that a highest intelligence has ordered everything thus according to the wisest ends. Consequently it is a condition of an aim that is indeed contingent, but still not unimportant, namely, in order to have a guidance in the investigation of nature, to presuppose a wise author of the world. The outcome of my attempts also so often confirms the usefulness of this presupposition and nothing can be adduced against it in a decisive way; that I would say far too little if I wanted to call my taking-to-be-true merely an opining, but it can be said even in this theoretical relation: that I firmly believe a God, but then this belief in the strict meaning is nevertheless not practical, but must be called a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must necessarily bring about everywhere. With regard to the very same wisdom, in respect of the excellent endowment of human nature and the brevity of life so badly commensurate with it, a sufficient ground can just as well be encountered for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.",["Weltkentniß rendered 'acquaintance with the world' (Kenntnis); kennen = know. Erklärungen = explanations; Erscheinungen = appearances.","The sentence 'Der Ausgang ... daß ich viel zu wenig sage' is left as irregular as the German (the 'daß' clause with semicolon); the construction is obscure.","Absicht = aim; Bedeutung = meaning; Bedingung = condition; zufällig = contingent; gnugsam rendered 'sufficient' (as zureichend).","Welturheber = 'author of the world'; 'als ob alles blos Natur sey' rendered 'as if everything were merely nature'."]],"krv-a-1297":["The expression of belief is in such cases an expression of modesty in an objective aim, but yet at the same time of the firmness of confidence in a subjective one. If I wanted to call the merely theoretical taking-to-be-true here even only a hypothesis that I would be entitled to assume, I would thereby already commit myself to have more, a concept of the constitution of a cause of the world and of another world, than I can actually show; for what I assume even only as hypothesis, of that I must know at least according to its attributes so much: that I need not invent its concept, but only its existence. But the word belief goes only to the guidance that an idea gives me and the subjective influence on the furtherance of my actions of reason, which holds me to it, although I am not in a position to give an account of it in a speculative aim.",["Absicht = aim ('in objektiver Absicht' is idiomatic, literally 'in an objective aim'); Beschaffenheit = constitution; Begriff = concept; Idee = idea; Vernunfthandlungen = 'actions of reason'.","Eigenschaften rendered 'attributes' (not in the glossary); Daseyn = existence; Hypothese = hypothesis.","'mehr, von der Beschaffenheit ..., Begriff zu haben': the word order is kept, with commas as in the German."]],"krv-a-1298":["But merely doctrinal belief has something wavering in it; one is often put out of it by difficulties that are encountered in speculation, although one unfailingly returns to it again and again.",["'aus demselben gesezt' = 'put out of it'; the pronoun 'demselben' refers to the belief.","Speculation = speculation."]],"krv-a-1299":["It is quite otherwise with moral belief. For there it is absolutely necessary: that something must happen, namely, that I comply with the ethical law in all respects. The end is here unavoidably fixed and only a single condition is possible according to all my insight, under which this end is connected with all collective ends and thereby has practical validity, namely, that there be a God and a future world: I also know quite certainly that no one knows other conditions that lead to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the ethical precept is at the same time my maxim (as reason indeed commands that it ought to be), I will unfailingly believe in an existence of God and a future life and am certain: that nothing can make this belief wavering, because thereby my ethical principles themselves would be overturned, which I cannot renounce without being detestable in my own eyes.",["sittlich rendered 'ethical' and moralisch 'moral' to keep the two German words apart.","'Folge leiste' (Folge leisten) rendered 'comply'; Folge elsewhere in the glossary = sequel.","Gültigkeit = validity; Grundsätze = principles; Einsicht = insight; 'führe' (singular subjunctive after 'Bedingungen') is rendered 'lead' without comment in the text.","'ein Daseyn Gottes' rendered 'an existence of God'; 'glauben' with accusative is rendered 'believe in'."]],"krv-a-1300":["In this way, after the frustration of all the ambitious aims of a reason roaming beyond the boundaries of all experience, there still remains enough for us that we have cause to be satisfied with it in a practical aim. To be sure, no one will be able to boast: he knows that there is a God and that there is a future life; for if he knows that, then he is precisely the man whom I have long been seeking. All knowledge (if it concerns an object of mere reason) can be communicated, and I would thus also be able to hope to see my knowledge extended, through his teaching, to so admirable a degree. No, the conviction is not logical but moral certainty, and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral disposition), I must not even say: it is morally certain that there is a God, etc., but rather, I am morally certain, etc. That is: the faith in a God and another world is so interwoven with my moral disposition that, just as little as I run the danger of losing the former, so little do I fear that the second could ever be torn from me.",["Gränzen = boundaries; Absicht = aim (ehrsüchtigen Absichten, in practischer Absicht); Wissen = knowledge; Gegenstand = object; Belehrung = teaching; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Grund = ground; Glaube = faith (belief would also fit the glossary; 'faith' chosen since the context is the article of faith).","'Gewißheit' = certainty (not in glossary); 'Gesinnung' = disposition (not in glossary).","'die erstere ... der zweite': the German genders are not parallel (erstere, feminine, vs. zweite, masculine); kept as in the text, with 'the former' and 'the second'. Antecedents are left unresolved.","'moralischgewiß' printed as one word in the original; rendered 'morally certain'."]],"krv-a-1301":["The only thing worrisome that is found here is that this rational faith grounds itself on the presupposition of moral dispositions. If we depart from this and assume someone who would be wholly indifferent with regard to moral laws, then the question that reason throws up becomes merely a problem for speculation and can then indeed still be supported with strong grounds from analogy, but not with such to which the most stubborn skepticism would have to yield*). But no human being is free of all interest in these questions. For although he may be separated from the moral by the lack of good dispositions, still in this case too enough remains to make him fear a divine existence and a future. For to this no more is required than that he at least cannot pretend to certainty that no such being and no future life is to be encountered, for which, because it would have to be proved through mere reason, hence apodictically, he would have to demonstrate the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable human being can undertake. That would be a negative faith, which could indeed not bring about morality and good dispositions, but yet the analogue of them, namely powerfully hold back the outbreak of the evil.",["Vernunftglaube = rational faith; Aufgabe = problem; Daseyn = existence; Wesen = being; Analogon left as 'analogue'; 'apodictisch' = apodictically.","'vor' in 'eine Aufgabe vor die Speculation' = 'for'; 'dieser' in 'aus der Analogie' etc. unchanged.","'denen sich ... ergeben müßte': 'to which ... would have to yield' (sich ergeben = surrender); 'solchen' left without a noun, as in German.","'dem moralischen' left as a bare substantive ('the moral'), as in German; 'der Bösen' = 'of the evil' (plural/collective ambiguity kept).","The footnote marker *) stands after 'werden' as printed; the footnote is krv-a-1302."]],"krv-a-1302":["*) The human mind takes (as I believe that it necessarily happens in every rational being) a natural interest in morality, although it is not undivided and practically preponderant. Strengthen and enlarge this interest and you will find reason very docile and even more enlightened, in order to unite the speculative interest with the practical one. But if you do not take care that you first make good human beings, at least halfway, then you will also never make sincerely believing human beings out of them!",["Gemüth = mind; Vernunft = reason; 'aufgeklärter' left as comparative ('more enlightened'); 'selbst' = 'even'.","'Sorget ihr aber nicht davor: daß ...' kept literally ('take care that'; 'davor' = 'for it').","'aufrichtiggläubige' = 'sincerely believing' (compound printed as one word)."]],"krv-a-1303":["But is that all, one will say, that pure reason accomplishes, in opening prospects beyond the boundaries of experience? Nothing more than two articles of faith? Even common understanding could well have accomplished this much, without consulting the philosophers about it!",["gemeine Verstand = common understanding (Verstand = understanding); Gränzen = boundaries; Glaubensartikel = articles of faith; Aussichten = prospects."]],"krv-a-1304":["Here I do not want to extol the merit that philosophy has through the laborious striving of its critique for human reason, supposing it should even be found merely negative in the outcome; for something of this will yet come up in the following section. But do you then demand: that a cognition that concerns all human beings should surpass the common understanding and be discovered by you only from philosophers? Precisely what you reproach is the best confirmation of the correctness of the assertions up to now, since it discovers what one could not at first foresee, namely, that nature, in what is of concern to human beings without distinction, is not to be accused of any partial distribution of its gifts, and that the highest philosophy, with regard to the essential ends of human nature, can bring it no further than the guidance that it has let come even to the commonest understanding.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Zwecke = ends; Behauptungen = assertions; Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit).","'nur von Philosophen entdekt werden solle': 'only from philosophers' is literal; 'nur' may attach to 'von Philosophen' or to 'entdekt werden'.","'es nicht weiter bringen könne': 'it' (es) is left unresolved; 'sie' in 'welche sie auch' refers grammatically to Philosophie or Natur; left as 'it' and 'that it'.","'Gaben' = gifts; 'partheyischen' = partial."]],"krv-a-1305":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method, Third Chapter. The Architectonic of Pure Reason.",["Methodenlehre = doctrine of method; Hauptstück = chapter; Architectonik = architectonic; rein = pure."]],"krv-a-1306":["By an architectonic I understand the art of systems. Because systematic unity is that which first makes common cognition into science, i.e., a system out of a mere aggregate of it, architectonic is: the doctrine of the scientific in our cognition in general, and it thus necessarily belongs to the doctrine of method.",["Wissenschaft = science (scientia); Erkentniß = cognition; Lehre = doctrine; 'scientifischen' = 'scientific'; 'gemeine' = common.","'derselben' in 'Aggregat derselben' refers back to Erkentniß (feminine); rendered 'of it'."]],"krv-a-1307":["Under the government of reason our cognitions in general may not make up a rhapsody, but must make up a system, in which alone they can support and further the essential ends of reason. But by a system I understand the unity of the manifold cognitions under one idea. This is the concept of reason of the form of a whole, insofar as through it the domain of the manifold as well as the place of the parts among one another is determined a priori. The scientific concept of reason thus contains the end and the form of the whole that is congruent with it. The unity of the end, to which all the parts and in the idea of which also among one another relate, makes it so that each part can be missed in the cognizance of the rest, and no contingent addition, or undetermined magnitude of perfection that does not have its boundaries determined a priori, takes place. The whole is thus articulated (*articulatio*) and not heaped together (*coacervatio*); it can grow internally (*per intus susceptionem*) but not externally (*per appositionem*), like an animal body, whose growth adds no limb but, without alteration of proportion, makes each one stronger and more fit for its ends.",["Erkentnisse = cognitions; Zwecke = ends; Idee = idea; Vernunftbegriff = concept of reason; Umfang = domain; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Gränzen = boundaries; zufällig = contingent; Grösse = magnitude; bestimmen = determine.","'Kentniß' in 'bey der Kentniß der übrigen' = 'cognizance' here; this is Kenntnis (acquaintance/knowledge in the glossary), not Erkenntnis; 'the cognizance' chosen to mark it.","'worauf sich alle Theile und in der Idee desselben auch untereinander beziehen': the verb 'beziehen' has 'sich' with both clauses; 'desselben' = 'of it' (the end); left as is.","'Gliede' / 'Glied' = limb (member); 'gegliedert' = articulated. 'Ganzen' = whole.","'die Leitung, welche sie' : 'sie' ambiguity as in 1304 not applicable here.","'dürfen ... keine Rhapsodie' = 'may not' (dürfen with negation; 'must not' is also possible)."]],"krv-a-1308":["The idea requires for its execution a schema, i.e., an essential manifoldness and order of the parts determined a priori from the principle of the end. The schema that is not sketched according to an idea, i.e., from the chief end of reason, but empirically, according to aims that present themselves contingently (the multitude of which one cannot know in advance), gives technical unity, but that which arises only in consequence of an idea (where reason gives the ends a priori and does not expect them empirically) grounds architectonic unity. Not technically, on account of the similarity of the manifold, or of the contingent use of cognition *in concreto* for all sorts of optional external ends, but architectonically, for the sake of affinity and of derivation from a single supreme and inner end, which first makes the whole possible, can that arise which we call science, the schema of which must contain the outline (*monogramma*) and the division of the whole into limbs, in accordance with the idea, i.e., a priori, and must distinguish this from all others securely and according to principles.",["Absichten = aims; Zwecke = ends (Zweck = end); Hauptzweck = chief end; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness (noun from manifold; Mannigfaltige elsewhere = manifold); Menge = multitude; Eintheilung = division; Gebrauch = use; Wissenschaft = science; Princip = principle.","'ausführung' = execution; 'Verwandschaft' = affinity; 'Ableitung' = derivation; 'Umriß' = outline.","'dasienige aber, was nur zu Folge einer Idee entspringt': 'zu Folge' = 'in consequence of'; the 'Folge' here is not the glossary's 'sequel'.","'dieses' at the end (this) is left without antecedent; it may refer to 'Wissenschaft' or to 'das Ganze'; 'von allen anderen' unresolved.","'Gliedern' = limbs, retained from the previous passage."]],"krv-a-1309":["No one attempts to bring a science into being without an idea lying at its ground. Yet, in the working out of it, the schema, indeed even the definition that he gives right at the beginning of his science, very seldom corresponds to his idea; for this lies, like a germ, in reason, in which all parts lie hidden, still very much enfolded and hardly knowable by microscopic observation. For this reason one must explain and determine sciences, since they are after all all conceived from the point of view of a certain universal interest, not according to the description that their originator gives of them, but according to the idea which one finds grounded in reason itself, from the natural unity of the parts that he has brought together. For there it will be found: that the originator and often even his latest successors wander around an idea that they have not made distinct to themselves and therefore cannot determine the peculiar content, the articulation (systematic unity) and boundaries of the science.",["Wissenschaft = science; Idee = idea; Grund = ground; Theile = parts; Inhalt = content; Gränzen = boundaries; Einheit = unity; erklären = explain (Erklärung = explanation; definition), while Definition is rendered 'definition'.","kennbar (from kennen) rendered 'knowable'; Beobachtung = observation.","'die sie sich selbst nicht haben deutlich machen': the infinitive 'können' is understood from the later 'bestimmen können'; I kept the German ellipsis ('have not made distinct').","'derselben' (first sentence) and 'davon' refer to the science; 'diese' (the idea) is ambiguous between Idee and Definition/Schema as in the German ('denn diese liegt, wie ein Keim').","Vernunft = reason; deutlich = distinct."]],"krv-a-1310":["It is bad: that only after we have, for a long time, following the direction of an idea lying hidden in us, rhapsodically collected many cognitions relating thereto, as building material, indeed have composed them technically over long times, is it then first possible for us to descry the idea in a brighter light and to sketch out a whole architectonically according to the ends of reason. The systems seem, like worms, to have been formed through a *generatio equivoca*, from the mere confluence of collected concepts, at first mutilated, with time complete, even though they all together had their schema, as the original germ, in reason merely unfolding itself, and for that reason are not only each by itself articulated according to an idea, but moreover all united with one another purposively in a system of human cognition once again as members of a whole, and allow an architectonic of all human knowledge, which at the present time, since so much material is already collected, or can be taken from the ruins of collapsed old buildings, would be not only possible, but not even so very difficult. We content ourselves here with the completion of our business, namely, merely to sketch the architectonic of all cognition from pure reason, and begin only from the point where the general root of our power of cognition divides and throws out two stems, of which one is reason. But I understand here by reason the whole higher faculty of cognition, and posit therefore the rational against the empirical.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkentnißkraft = power of cognition; Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Wissens = knowledge; Stoff = material; Zweck = end; Begriff = concept; rein = pure.","'Es ist schlimm: daß nur allererst, nachdem...' is a syntactically strained sentence; the 'it is bad' clause and the inverted 'es uns denn allererst möglich ist' are kept as they stand.","*generatio equivoca* is Kant's Latin; 'technisch', 'architectonisch', 'rhapsodistisch' rendered by plain cognates.","'zweckmässig' = purposively; 'Gewürme' = worms (plural collective).","'setze ... das rationale dem empirischen entgegen': 'rational' and 'empirical' are Kant's neuter substantivized adjectives, kept in lower case."]],"krv-a-1311":["If I abstract from all content of cognition, considered objectively, then all cognition is, subjectively, either historical or rational. Historical cognition is *cognitio ex datis*, but rational cognition *cognitio ex principiis*. A cognition may be originally given from wherever it will, it is nevertheless historical in him who possesses it, if he cognizes only in the degree and as much as has been given to him elsewhere, whether this may have been given to him through immediate experience or narration, or also teaching (of universal cognitions). Hence he who has actually learned a system of philosophy, e.g. the Wolffian, even if he had in his head all the principles, explanations and proofs, together with the division of the whole doctrinal edifice, and could count everything off on his fingers, nevertheless has no other than a complete historical cognition of the Wolffian philosophy; he knows and judges only as much as was given to him. If a definition is contested against him, he does not know where to take another from. He formed himself according to a foreign reason, but the faculty of copying is not the generating one, i.e. the cognition did not spring in him from reason, and even though it was, objectively, certainly a cognition of reason, it is nevertheless, subjectively, merely historical. He has grasped and retained well, i.e. learned, and is a plaster cast of a living human being. Cognitions of reason that are so objectively (i.e. can at first only spring from the human being's own reason) may bear this name subjectively too only then alone, if they have been drawn from universal sources of reason, from which also criticism, indeed even the rejection of what has been learned can spring, i.e. from principles.",["Grundsätze and Principien both rendered 'principles'; Erklärungen = 'explanations' (not 'definitions'); Definition = 'definition'; Beweise = proofs; Eintheilung = division; Belehrung = teaching; Erfahrung = experience.","'Streitet ihm eine Definition': the construction (streiten with dative) is obscure; rendered 'is contested against him'. Doubtful sense preserved.","'weis' = knows (wissen); 'erkent' = cognizes (erkennen); 'fassen' = grasp.","'nachbildende Vermögen' = faculty of copying; 'das erzeugende' = the generating one.","'dürfen nur denn allein' rendered 'may ... only then alone' to keep the German redundancy.","Wolfische = Wolffian (modern spelling of the name)."]],"krv-a-1312":["Now all cognition of reason is either that from concepts, or from the construction of concepts, the former is called philosophical, the second mathematical. Of the inner difference of the two I have already treated in the first chapter. A cognition accordingly can be objectively philosophical and is yet subjectively historical, as with most pupils and with all those who never look out beyond the school and remain pupils all their lives. But it is nevertheless odd: that mathematical cognition, as one has learned it, can yet also count subjectively as cognition of reason, and such a difference does not take place with it as with the philosophical. The cause is, because the sources of cognition, from which the teacher alone can draw, lie nowhere else than in the essential and genuine principles of reason and consequently cannot be taken by the pupil from anywhere else, nor perhaps be contested, and this indeed because the use of reason here occurs only *in concreto*, though nevertheless *a priori*, namely in the pure and, precisely for that reason, faultless intuition, and excludes all deception and error. One can therefore, among all sciences of reason (*a priori*), learn only mathematics, but never philosophy (unless historically), but, as far as reason is concerned, at most only to philosophize.",["Vernunfterkentniß = cognition of reason; Vernunftwissenschaften = sciences of reason; Anschauung = intuition; Gebrauch = use; Begriff = concept; Principien = principles.","Lehrlinge = 'pupils' (also 'apprentices' possible); Hauptstück = chapter.","'vor Vernunfterkentniß gelten' : 'vor' is old spelling of 'für'.","The comma after 'Begriffe' (joining 'die erstere heißt philosophisch') is kept as in the original.","'es sey denn historisch' and 'sondern ... höchstens nur philosophiren lernen': the final clause lacks an explicit governing verb parallel to 'lernen'; 'Mathematik ... philosophiren lernen' retained in German order. 'fehlerfreien' = faultless.","*in concreto*, *a priori* are Kant's Latin."]],"krv-a-1313":["The system of all philosophical cognition is now philosophy. One must take it objectively if one understands by it the archetype of the estimation of all attempts at philosophizing, which is to serve to assess every subjective philosophy, whose edifice is often so manifold and so changeable. In this way philosophy is a mere idea of a possible science, which is given nowhere *in concreto*, but which one seeks to approach by many sorts of paths, until the one footpath, much overgrown by sensibility, is discovered, and it succeeds in making the copy that has been missed until now equal to the archetype, as far as is granted to human beings. Until then one cannot learn any philosophy; for, where is it, who has it in possession, and by what can it be recognized? One can only learn to philosophize, i.e. to exercise the talent of reason in the following of its universal principles on certain extant attempts, yet always with reservation of the right of reason to investigate those themselves in their sources and to confirm or to reject them.",["Urbild = archetype; Nachbild = copy; Beurtheilung = estimation, beurtheilen = assess (glossary); Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; erkennen = recognize here; Versuch = attempt.","'welche iede subiective Philosophie zu beurtheilen dienen soll': 'welche' may refer to 'Urbild' or to 'Versuche'; the English 'which' preserves this ambiguity.","'es sich ... gelinget': the subject of 'gelinget' is the infinitive clause 'das ... Nachbild ... gleich zu machen'; rendered 'it succeeds in making'.","'iene' = 'those', referring back to 'Principien' or 'Versuche' (ambiguous); kept as 'those'.","*in concreto* is Kant's Latin."]],"krv-a-1314":["Until then, however, the concept of philosophy is only a school concept, namely of a system of cognition that is sought only as science, without having as end anything more than the systematic unity of this knowledge, consequently the logical perfection of cognition. But there is still a world-concept (*conceptus cosmicus*) which has at all times lain at the ground of this term, above all when one as it were personified it and represented it to oneself as an archetype in the ideal of the philosopher. In this aim philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the essential ends of human reason (*teleologia rationis humanae*), and the philosopher is not an artist of reason, but the legislator of human reason. In such a meaning it would be very boastful to call oneself a philosopher and to presume to have equaled the archetype that lies only in the idea.",["Weltbegriff = world-concept (Latin given by Kant: *conceptus cosmicus*); Schulbegriff = school concept; Benennung = term; Absicht = aim; Bedeutung = meaning; Zweck = end; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Wissenschaft = science; Wissen = knowledge; Idee = idea; Urbild = archetype.","'ihn' (twice) has the Weltbegriff as its antecedent in the English 'it'; kept as a pronoun as in the German.","'Vernunftkünstler' = artist of reason; 'Gesezgeber' = legislator.","'sehr ruhmredig' = very boastful; 'gleichgekommen' = equaled (the German has the perfect infinitive 'gleichgekommen zu seyn')."]],"krv-a-1315":["The mathematician, the student of nature, the logician are, however excellent the first may in general be in rational cognition, the second in particular in philosophical cognition, in having progress, nonetheless only artists of reason. There is still a teacher in the ideal, who appoints all these, uses them as instruments, in order to promote the essential ends of human reason. Him alone we would have to call the philosopher; but, since he himself is nowhere to be found, the idea of his legislation however is encountered everywhere in every human reason, we will hold solely to the latter and determine more closely what philosophy, according to this concept of the world*), prescribes for systematic unity from the standpoint of ends.",["Vernunfterkentniß = 'rational cognition'; Erkentniß = 'cognition' throughout. Naturkündiger rendered 'student of nature'; Vernunftkünstler 'artists of reason'.","'die erstere ... die zweite' is rendered 'the first ... the second', keeping the German's loose reference to the three persons named. 'Fortgang haben' = 'having progress'.","Weltbegriff (not in the glossary) = 'concept of the world', used consistently in the footnote; Schulbegriff (next passage) = 'scholastic concept'.","'vor systematische Einheit': vor = für (early spelling); rendered 'for'. The footnote sign '*)' is the original's, not italics markup."]],"krv-a-1316":["*) Concept of the world here means that which concerns what necessarily interests everyone; hence I determine the aim of a science according to scholastic concepts if it is regarded only as one of the skills for certain optional ends.",["Absicht = 'aim'; Zweck = 'end'; Wissenschaft = 'science'; beliebig = 'optional'. The footnote sign '*)' is the original's, not italics markup."]],"krv-a-1317":["Essential ends are not on that account the highest, of which (given perfect systematic unity of reason) there can be only a single one. Hence they are either the final end, or subaltern ends, which belong necessarily to that one as means. The former is none other than the whole vocation of the human being and the philosophy about it is called morals. On account of this preeminence which moral philosophy has over all other endeavor of reason, among the ancients too under the name of the philosopher one always understood at the same time and preeminently the moralist, and even the external illusion of self-mastery through reason makes it that one still now calls someone, with his limited knowledge, by a certain analogy, philosopher.",["Bestimmung = 'vocation' here (glossary also allows 'determination'). Endzweck = 'final end'.","Schein = 'illusion' (glossary); 'äussere Schein' = 'external illusion'.","Vernunftbewerbung = 'endeavor of reason'; Wissen = 'knowledge'. 'Moral' (n.) = 'morals', 'Moralist' = 'moralist'.","'selbst macht ... daß' is kept as a single construction ('even ... makes it that'); the German syntax is awkward."]],"krv-a-1318":["The legislation of human reason (philosophy) now has two objects: nature and freedom, and thus contains the law of nature as well as the moral law, at first in two particular, but finally in a single philosophical system. The philosophy of nature goes to everything that is, that of morals only to what ought to be.",["Gegenstände = 'objects'. Sittengesetz = 'moral law'; Sitten = 'morals'. besonder = 'particular'.","'geht auf' = 'goes to' (literal; could also be read 'is directed to')."]],"krv-a-1319":["But all philosophy is either cognition from pure reason, or rational cognition from empirical principles. The former is called pure, the second empirical philosophy.",["Vernunfterkentniß = 'rational cognition'. Principien = 'principles'."]],"krv-a-1320":["The philosophy of pure reason is now either propaedeutic (preparatory exercise), which investigates the faculty of reason in regard to all pure cognition *a priori* and is called critique, or, second, the system of pure reason (science), the whole (true as well as illusory) philosophical cognition from pure reason in systematic interconnection, and is called metaphysics; although this name can also be given to the whole of pure philosophy with the inclusion of critique, in order to comprehend together both the investigation of everything that can ever be cognized *a priori*, and also the presentation of that which constitutes a system of pure philosophical cognitions of this kind, but is distinguished from everything empirical, likewise from the mathematical use of reason.",["Propädevtik = 'propaedeutic'. Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'. Vermögen = 'faculty'.","scheinbar = 'illusory' (cf. Schein = 'illusion'; the German word can also mean 'apparent').","'mit Inbegriff der Critik': Inbegriff (glossary 'sum total') here in an adverbial phrase, rendered 'with the inclusion of'.","The final clause is syntactically loose in the German ('ist ... unterschieden' after the second object); kept as it stands. Whether 'a priori' in 'erkant werden kan' attaches to the verb only is not resolved."]],"krv-a-1321":["Metaphysics divides into that of the speculative and of the practical use of pure reason and is thus either metaphysics of nature, or metaphysics of morals. The former contains all pure principles of reason from mere concepts (hence with the exclusion of mathematics) of the theoretical cognition of all things, the latter the principles which determine doing and refraining *a priori* and make them necessary. Now morality is the sole lawfulness of actions that can be derived fully *a priori* from principles. Hence the metaphysics of morals is properly pure morals, in which no anthropology (no empirical condition) is laid at the ground. The metaphysics of speculative reason is now that which one is accustomed to call metaphysics in the narrower sense; but insofar as pure doctrine of morals nonetheless belongs to the particular stem of human, and indeed philosophical, cognition from pure reason, we want to retain that term for it, although we, as not now belonging to our end, set it aside here.",["'im engeren Verstande' = 'in the narrower sense' (Verstand here not 'understanding' in the glossary sense).","Benennung = 'term' (glossary), with Nahme = 'name' earlier. Sittenlehre = 'doctrine of morals'; Gesetzmässigkeit = 'lawfulness'.","'zum Grunde gelegt' = 'laid at the ground'. 'Thun und Lassen' = 'doing and refraining'.","'obgleich wir sie, als zu unserm Zwecke iezt nicht gehörig, ...': the 'as ... not belonging' clause may refer to 'sie' (pure doctrine of morals); kept in German order."]],"krv-a-1322":["It is of the utmost importance to isolate cognitions that are distinguished from others according to their genus and origin, and to prevent carefully that they flow together with others, with which they are usually combined in use, into a mixture. What chemists do in the separation of materials, what mathematicians do in their pure doctrine of magnitude, is incumbent far more on the philosopher, so that he can determine securely the share that a particular way of cognition has in the roving use of the understanding, its own worth and influence. Hence human reason, since it has thought, or rather thought further, has never been able to do without a metaphysics, but has nonetheless not been able to present it sufficiently purified of everything alien. The idea of such a science is just as old as speculative human reason; and what reason does not speculate, whether it happens in the scholastic or the popular way? One must meanwhile admit: that the distinction of the two elements of our cognition, of which the one are fully *a priori* in our power, the other can be taken only *a posteriori* from experience, remained very indistinct even with thinkers by trade, and hence could never bring about the determination of the boundary of a particular kind of cognition, hence not the genuine idea of a science that has occupied human reason so long and so much. If one said: metaphysics is the science of the first principles of human cognition, one thereby noted not a quite particular kind, but only a rank in regard to universality, whereby it could therefore not be distinguished recognizably from the empirical; for even among empirical principles some are more universal, and for that reason higher than others and, in the series of such a subordination, (since one does not distinguish that which is cognized fully *a priori* from that which is cognized only *a posteriori*), where is one to make the section that would distinguish the first part and the uppermost members from the last and the subordinate ones? What would one say to it if chronology could designate the epochs of the world only so that it divided them into the first centuries and the ones following thereafter. Does the fifth, the tenth etc. century also belong to the first? one would ask; just so I ask: does the concept of the extended belong to metaphysics? you answer, yes! oh, but also that of body? yes! and that of fluid body? you become perplexed, for if it goes on this way, then everything will belong to metaphysics. From this one sees: that the mere degree of subordination (the particular under the universal) cannot determine the boundaries of a science, but in our case the complete heterogeneity and difference of origin. But what obscured the basic idea of metaphysics on another side still, was that, as cognition *a priori*, it shows with mathematics a certain homogeneity, which, to be sure, as far as concerns the origin *a priori*, makes them related to one another, but as far as concerns the way of cognizing from concepts in the former, in comparison with the way of judging *a priori* merely through construction of concepts in the latter, hence the difference of a philosophical cognition from the mathematical, there shows itself such a decided heterogeneity, which one to be sure always felt, as it were, but never could bring to distinct criteria. Thereby it has now happened that, since philosophers themselves erred in the development of the idea of their science, the working out of it could have no determinate end and no secure guideline and they, with a draft made so arbitrarily, ignorant of the way they should take, and always disputing among themselves over the discoveries each wanted to have made on his own, brought their science into contempt first with others and finally even with themselves.",["nachgedacht = 'thought further' (not Überlegung/'reflection'). Gewalt = 'power' ('in unserer Gewalt'), distinct from Kraft/Vermögen.","'deren die eine ... sind': plural verb with 'the one' kept as in the German (the antecedent is 'Elemente'). 'Denkern von Gewerbe' = 'thinkers by trade'.","Gränzbestimmung = 'determination of the boundary'; Gränzen = 'boundaries'. Abschnitt = 'section'. herumschweifend = 'roving'.","Gleichartigkeit / Ungleichartigkeit = 'homogeneity' / 'heterogeneity'. kentlich = 'recognizably'. Erkentnißart = 'way of cognizing'; Art = 'way'/'kind'.","Gattung = 'genus'; Grad = 'degree'; Criterien = 'criteria'; Antheil = 'share'; Richtschnur = 'guideline'.","'ey' rendered 'oh'. The sentence 'What would one say ... thereafter.' ends with a period in the German though phrased as a question; kept.","'wo soll man den Abschnitt machen' and 'die ... Erkentniß ... zu Stande bringen konte' (subject 'Unterscheidung') kept in German order; 'a priori' in 'Ursprung a priori betrift' may attach to 'Ursprung' or the sentence; left as is."]],"krv-a-1323":["Hence all pure cognition a priori, by virtue of the particular faculty of cognition in which alone it can have its seat, constitutes a particular unity, and metaphysics is that philosophy which is to present that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of it, which has especially appropriated this name to itself, namely that which we call metaphysics of nature and which weighs everything, insofar as it is (not that which ought to be) from concepts a priori, is now divided in the following way.",["Erkentniß = cognition; Erkentnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; erwägt = 'weighs' (kept distinct from betrachten, 'consider').","'nemlich die, welche' is grammatically irregular in the original (die for der/diejenige); rendered 'namely that which' without emendation.","'derselben' (the part 'of it') refers back to Metaphysik; kept as 'of it'."]],"krv-a-1324":["Metaphysics so called in the narrower sense consists of transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former considers only the understanding and reason itself in a system of all concepts and principles that are related to objects in general, without assuming objects that would be given (*Ontologia*), the second considers nature, i.e., the sum total of given objects, (whether they be given to the senses, or, if one wishes, to another kind of intuition), and is thus physiology (although only *rationalis*). Now, however, the use of reason in this rational consideration of nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or better, either immanent or transcendent. The former goes to nature, as far as its cognition can be applied in experience (*in concreto*), the second to that connection of the objects of experience which surpasses all experience. This transcendent physiology therefore has as its object either an inner connection or an outer, both of which, however, go beyond possible experience; the former is the physiology of the entire nature, i.e., the transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the interconnection of the entire nature with a being above nature, i.e., the transcendental cognition of God.",["'im engeren Verstande' = 'in the narrower sense' (Verstand in the sense of 'meaning'); not the faculty.","Gegenstände and Obiecte are both 'objects'; Gegenstand(e) in 'Gegenstände überhaupt', 'gegebener Gegenstände', 'Gegenstande'; Obiecte only in 'ohne Obiecte anzunehmen'.","Inbegriff = sum total; Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Anschauung = intuition; Wesen = being.","'die zweite' and 'der zweite' are left as in the German; 'iene' / 'diese' kept as 'the former' / 'the latter'."]],"krv-a-1325":["Immanent physiology considers, by contrast, nature as the sum total of all objects of the senses, hence as it is given to us, but only according to conditions a priori under which it can be given to us at all. But there are only two kinds of objects of it. 1. Those of the outer senses, hence the sum total of them, corporeal nature. 2. The object of inner sense, the soul, and, according to the fundamental concepts of it in general, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics, but, because it is to contain only the principles of its cognition a priori, rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology and, for the cause just cited, only the rational cognition of it is to be understood here.",["'derselben' (twice, 'of it', 'of them') and 'der Grundbegriffen derselben' are kept ambiguous as in the German; 'Principien' = principles (Grundsatz also = principle).","Gegenstände = objects; Ursache = cause."]],"krv-a-1326":["Accordingly the whole system of metaphysics consists of four main parts. 1. Of ontology. 2. Of rational physiology. 3. Of rational cosmology. 4. Of rational theology. The second part, namely the doctrine of nature of pure reason, contains two divisions, *physica rationalis* *) and *psychologia rationalis*.",["The footnote marker *) follows 'physica rationalis' in the original; the footnote is passage krv-a-1327.","Naturlehre = doctrine of nature; Abtheilung = division."]],"krv-a-1327":["*) Let one by no means think that by this I understand that which is commonly called *physica generalis*, and which is more mathematics than philosophy of nature. For the metaphysics of nature separates itself entirely from mathematics, also has by far not so many expanding insights to offer as the latter, but is nevertheless very important with regard to the critique of pure cognition of the understanding to be applied to nature in general; in the absence of which even mathematicians, in that they adhere to certain common concepts, in fact nevertheless metaphysical, have unnoticed burdened the doctrine of nature with hypotheses, which vanish with a critique of these principles, without thereby doing the slightest detriment to the use of mathematics in this field (which is entirely indispensable).",["'ia nicht' (ja nicht) rendered 'by no means'.","'in Ermangelung deren' = 'in the absence of which'; the antecedent (the critique) is left as in the German.","'Verstandeserkentniß' = cognition of the understanding; Einsicht = insight."]],"krv-a-1328":["The original idea of a philosophy of pure reason itself prescribes this division; it is therefore architectonic, in accordance with its essential ends and not merely technical, arranged according to contingently perceived affinities and as it were at random, but for that very reason also unalterable and legislative. But there are found here some points that arouse misgivings and could weaken the conviction of the lawfulness of them.",["Zwecke = ends; zufällig = contingently; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Gesetzmässigkeit = lawfulness.","'derselben' at the end ('of them') is left ambiguous as in the German (the points or the division)."]],"krv-a-1329":["First, how can I expect a cognition a priori, hence metaphysics, of objects insofar as they are given to our senses, hence a posteriori, and how is it possible, according to principles a priori, to cognize the nature of things and to arrive at a rational physiology? The answer is: we take nothing further from experience than what is necessary to give us an object, partly of outer, partly of inner sense. The former happens through the mere concept matter, (impenetrable lifeless extension) the latter through the concept of a thinking being (in the empirical inner representation: I think). For the rest, in the whole metaphysics of these objects, we would have to abstain entirely from all empirical principles, which might add to the concept some experience or other, in order to judge something about these objects from it.",["Gegenstände and Obiect are both 'object'; Obiect occurs in 'uns ein Obiect ... zu geben'.","'Jenes ... dieses' = 'the former ... the latter'; 'darin ... daraus' left unresolved ('from it').","'nach Principien a priori' may attach to 'erkennen' or to the whole clause; kept in the German position.","erkennen = cognize; urtheilen = judge; Ausdehnung = extension; Vorstellung = representation; Wesen = being."]],"krv-a-1330":["Second: where then does empirical psychology remain, which has from of old asserted its place in metaphysics and from which in our times such great things have been expected for the elucidation of the same, after one gave up the hope of accomplishing something useful a priori? I answer: it comes to where the proper (empirical) doctrine of nature must be placed, namely on the side of applied philosophy, for which pure philosophy contains the principles a priori, which must therefore indeed be combined with the former, but not mixed. Thus empirical psychology must be entirely banished from metaphysics, and is already entirely excluded from it by the idea of the same. Nevertheless one will, according to school use, still always have to permit it (although only as an episode) a little place in it, and indeed from economic motive causes, because it is not yet so rich that it alone could constitute a study and yet too important for one to expel it entirely, or to attach it elsewhere, where it would likely find even less affinity than in metaphysics. It is thus merely a stranger admitted for so long, to whom one grants a stay for some time, until he can move into his own dwelling in a detailed anthropology (the pendant to the empirical doctrine of nature).",["'derselben' (after Aufklärung) and 'davon ... durch die Idee derselben' are left ambiguous as in the German (psychology or metaphysics).","'zu welcher' and 'die' (principles) and 'iener' ('the former') follow the German; Bewegursachen = 'motive causes' (cf. Bewegungsgrund = motive).","Schulgebrauch = school use; Gebrauch = use; Idee = idea; Fremdling is masculine, hence 'he/his'."]],"krv-a-1331":["This is thus the general idea of metaphysics, which, since at first more was imputed to it than can fairly be demanded and for a while it delighted itself with agreeable expectations, has finally fallen into general contempt since one found oneself deceived in one's hope. From the whole course of our critique one will have been sufficiently convinced: that, even if metaphysics cannot be the foundation of religion, it must nevertheless always remain standing as its bulwark, and that human reason, which is dialectical already through the direction of its nature, can never do without such a science, which reins it in and, through a scientific and fully evident self-cognition, holds off the devastations which a lawless speculative reason would otherwise quite infallibly cause, in morals as well as religion. One can thus be certain, however coy or disdainful those may act who know how to assess a science not according to its nature, but solely from its contingent effects, that one will always return to it, as to a beloved with whom we have fallen out, because reason, since essential ends are concerned here, must work restlessly either toward fundamental insight or the destruction of good insights already present.",["'Selbsterkentniß' = self-cognition; 'scientifisch' kept as 'scientific'; Wissenschaft = science; beurtheilen = assess.","'man werde' (subjunctive) rendered 'one will'; 'iene' omitted in this passage; 'zu ihr' refers to the science (Wissenschaft), as the feminine pronoun allows.","Zwecke = ends; Wirkungen = effects; zufällig = contingent."]],"krv-a-1332":["Metaphysics, therefore, of nature as well as of morals, above all the critique of reason venturing on its own wings, which precedes as a preliminary exercise (propaedeutic), alone constitute properly that which we can call philosophy in the genuine sense. This relates everything to wisdom, but through the path of science, the only one that, once it has been broken, never grows over again and permits no aberrations. Mathematics, natural science, even the empirical knowledge of the human being, have a high value as means, for the most part to contingent ends, but in the end nevertheless to necessary and essential ends of humanity, but then only by the mediation of a cognition of reason from mere concepts, which, one may name it as one will, is properly nothing but metaphysics.",["Wissenschaft = science throughout; Naturwissenschaft = natural science; Kentniß (Kenntnis) = knowledge; Vernunfterkentniß = cognition of reason (Erkenntnis).","im ächten Verstande: 'Verstande' here is the ordinary word 'sense' (dative of Verstand), rendered 'sense' to avoid 'understanding' (the faculty); the German form is the same.","vorübend (propädevtisch): 'propädevtisch' is Kant's spelling; rendered 'propaedeutic'.","Zwecke = ends; Mittel = means; Begriffe = concepts."]],"krv-a-1333":["Just for that reason metaphysics is also the consummation of all culture of human reason, which is indispensable, even if one sets aside its influence, as science, on certain determinate ends. For it considers reason according to its elements and supreme maxims, which must lie at the ground even of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That it, as mere speculation, serves more to keep off errors than to extend cognition, does no detriment to its worth, but rather gives it dignity and authority through the office of censor, which secures general order and harmony, indeed the well-being of the scientific commonwealth, and keeps its bold and fruitful treatments from distancing themselves from the chief end, general happiness.",["Bearbeitungen = treatments (also in krv-a-1335); the same word is used in both places.","dessen (before 'muthige und fruchtbare Bearbeitungen') could refer to the commonwealth (gemeinen Wesens) or, more loosely, to the sciences; rendered 'its', preserving the ambiguity.","erweitern = extend; Erkentniß = cognition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Gebrauch = use; Grunde = ground.","Wolstand = well-being; gemeines Wesen = commonwealth (literally common being)."]],"krv-a-1334":["Fourth Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method. The History of Pure Reason.",["Methodenlehre = doctrine of method; Hauptstück = chapter."]],"krv-a-1335":["This title stands here only to designate a place that remains over in the system and must be filled in in the future. I content myself, from a merely transcendental standpoint, namely that of the nature of pure reason, with casting a cursory glance at the whole of the previous treatments of it, which admittedly presents to my eye, to be sure, buildings, but only in ruins.",["freilich ... zwar: both particles are kept ('admittedly ... to be sure').","The antecedent of 'welches' is left as in the German (the glance / the whole)."]],"krv-a-1336":["It is remarkable enough, although in the natural course of things it could not have gone otherwise, that human beings in the childhood of philosophy began from where we would now rather end, namely to study first the cognition of God and hope, or even the constitution of another world. Whatever crude concepts of religion the old customs, still left over from the raw state of peoples, may have introduced, this nevertheless did not hinder the more enlightened part from devoting itself to free investigations concerning this object, and one easily saw that there could be no more thorough and reliable way to please the invisible power that governs the world, in order at least in another world to be happy, than good conduct of life. Hence theology and morals were the two driving springs, or better, points of relation for all abstracted investigations of reason, to which one has afterwards always devoted oneself. The former, however, was properly that which little by little drew merely speculative reason into the business that has subsequently become so famous under the name of metaphysics.",["die Erkentniß Gottes und Hoffnung: 'Hoffnung' has no article or genitive; rendered 'cognition of God and hope', leaving open whether it is the cognition of hope or hope as a second item.","Beschaffenheit = constitution; Gegenstand = object; Welt = world; Zustand = state; Beziehungspuncte = points of relation.","abgezogenen = abstracted; Triebfedern = driving springs."]],"krv-a-1337":["I will now not distinguish the times at which this or that alteration of metaphysics occurred, but only present in a cursory outline the difference of the idea that occasioned the principal revolutions. And there I find a threefold aim in which the most notable alterations on this stage of dispute have been brought about.",["Absicht = aim; Idee = idea; Veränderung = alteration.","Strict singular 'Idee' is kept ('the idea')."]],"krv-a-1338":["1. With regard to the object of all our cognitions of reason, some were merely sensual-, others merely intellectual philosophers. Epicurus can be called the foremost philosopher of sensibility, Plato of the intellectual. This difference of the schools, however, subtle as it is, had already begun in the earliest times and has maintained itself long and uninterrupted. Those of the former asserted: in the objects of the senses alone is actuality, everything else is imagination; those of the second said by contrast: in the senses there is nothing but illusion, only the understanding cognizes the true. Yet the former did not on that account precisely dispute reality to the concepts of the understanding, but it was with them only logical, with the others, however, mystical. The former conceded intellectual concepts, but assumed merely sensible objects. The latter demanded that the true objects be merely intelligible and asserted an intuition through the pure understanding, accompanied by no senses and, in their opinion, only confused.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Realität = reality (distinguished as in the glossary).","Einbildung = imagination (not Einbildungskraft).","Gegenstand = object; Schein = illusion; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; sensibel and sinnlich both 'sensible'.","Sensual- andere blos Intellectualphilosophen: the hyphen of 'Sensual-' is kept as in the German.","ob eben nicht: 'eben nicht' rendered 'not ... precisely'.","behaupteten (Meinung) vs. Meinung = opinion; behaupten = assert."]],"krv-a-1339":["2. With regard to the origin of pure cognitions of reason, whether they are derived from experience or, independently of it, have their source in reason. Aristotle can be regarded as the head of the empiricists, Plato however of the noologists. Locke, who in more recent times followed the former, and Leibniz, who followed the latter (although at a sufficient distance from his mystical system), have nonetheless not yet been able to bring it to any decision in this dispute. At least Epicurus for his part proceeded much more consistently according to his sensual system (for he never went with his inferences beyond the boundary of experience) than Aristotle and Locke, (but above all the latter,) who, after he had derived all concepts and principles from experience, goes so far in the use of them that he asserts: one can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul (although both objects lie entirely outside the boundaries of possible experience) just as evidently as any mathematical theorem.",["Gränze = boundary; Schlüsse = inferences; Grundsätze = principles; Lehrsatz = theorem; Daseyn = existence; Gebrauch = use.","es ... zu keiner Entscheidung bringen: 'es' is left without an explicit antecedent, as in the German.","'der leztere,)' : punctuation of the parentheses is kept as printed."]],"krv-a-1340":["3. With regard to method. If something is to be called method, it must be a procedure according to principles. Now one can divide the method presently prevailing in this field of investigation into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason takes it as his principle: that through common reason without science (which he calls healthy reason), with regard to the loftiest questions that make up the problem of metaphysics, more can be accomplished than through speculation. He asserts, then: that one can determine the magnitude and breadth of the moon more reliably by the measure of the eye than by mathematical detours. It is mere misology brought to principles and, what is the most absurd, the neglect of all artificial means is praised as a peculiar method of extending one's, cognition. For as concerns the naturalists from lack of more insight, one cannot justifiably lay anything to their charge. They follow common reason, without boasting of their ignorance as a method that is supposed to contain the secret of fetching the truth out of Democritus' deep well. *Quod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo, esse quod Arcesilas aerumnosique Solones*, Pers. is their motto, with which they can live contentedly and worthy of approval, without troubling themselves about science, or confusing its business.",["Aufgabe = problem; Grundsatz = principle; gemeine Vernunft = common reason; Wissenschaft = science; Mittel = means; Einsicht = insight.","seine, Erkentniß: the stray comma in the German is kept; 'seine' is rendered 'one's'.","Quod sapio ... Solones: Latin (Persius, Satires) left unchanged, in italics; 'Pers.' as printed.","Weite = breadth; mit Grunde = 'justifiably' (literally 'with ground')."]],"krv-a-1341":["As for the observers of a scientific method, they have the choice here between dogmatic or skeptical, but in all cases nevertheless the obligation to proceed systematically. If I name here, with regard to the former, the famous Wolff, with the second David Hume, I can, according to my present aim, leave the rest unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If the reader has had the kindness and patience to travel this in my company, he may now judge whether, if it pleases him to contribute his part toward making this footpath into a highway, that which many centuries could not accomplish might not be attained before the expiration of the present one: namely, to bring human reason to complete satisfaction in that with which its desire for knowledge has always, but hitherto in vain, been occupied.",["Wolf is printed 'Wolf'; given here in the usual spelling 'Wolff'.","Absicht = aim; Beobachter = observer; Wißbegierde = desire for knowledge (Wissen = knowledge).","Heeresstrasse = highway (literally 'army road').","ob nicht ... möge: the long dependent construction is kept as one sentence, as in the German."]]};
